(1596,1596,1,'Performance Comparison of K-Means and Expectation-Maximization Clustering Algorithms for Network Intrusion Detection','<p>The tremendous growth of networks and internet-based services have led to more numerous and complicated cyberattacks. Organizations today widely adopt networks for many things; like sending messages, transferring money, data storing, and using internet for communication and cloud computing. Therefore, networks are the attractive targets for the cyber-attacks and require protective mechanisms. Firewalls, antivirus program, and access controls provide the preventative means, but they are only enough for new attacks or simple kinds of attacks, they cannot provide any protection to sophisticated or newly discovered kinds of attacks. Intrusion detection systems are an unavoidable system for a cybersecurity environment, since they continuously monitor network traffic and detect malicious activity that the prevention mechanisms miss out. Machine learning methods have gained a considerable success at the field of network intrusion detection since they can analyse and learn patterns from network traffic, unlike rule-based systems, they do not require predefined signature of attacks. There are two types of machine learning algorithms, supervised learning and unsupervised learning. Unsupervised learning is more efficient for network intrusion detection and particularly Clustering as a type of unsupervised learning is used since it can help in partitioning the network traffic into various types of connection with identifying the anomalous connection without using the pre-labelled intrusion training data. The most popular methods in clustering are the K-means algorithm for its high calculation efficiency and ease of implementation, while expectation-maximization (EM) algorithms provide probabilistic model based-clustering which can model more complex kind of data. Only very few research has compared the two types of clustering, K-Means and EM algorithms for network intrusion detection system under exactly the same experimental settings and the same common dataset, The present study outlines the comparative analysis of K-Means and Expectation-Maximization clustering algorithms towards network intrusion detection through applying on the KDD Cup 1999 benchmark dataset. Experiments is setup using WEKA data mining tool for having a consistent research environment. Comparison research based on the computationally performance, clustering patterns, converging behaviours, and the execution efficiency in general manner. Data for network traffic is pre-processed and then the two clustering algorithms are analysed using exactly same parameters where appropriate. Comparison metrics for two algorithms includes the runtime, distribution patterns across clusters, speed of convergence, and clustering validity. The primary research intention is to assess the strengths and weakness of the K-means and EM algorithms for an unsupervised detection of intrusions, thus providing a guide for an appropriate clustering technique for large networks information security system.</p>','["Intrusion Detection System, Network Security, Machine Learning, Unsupervised Learning, K-Means Clustering, Expectation-Maximization, WEKA, KDD Cup 1999, Cybersecurity, Data Mining."]',NULL,24,1,'01-14','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1596','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',1),
(1615,1615,1,'A Novel Iterative Machine Learning-Driven Framework for Reliable and Adaptive Cloud Data Migration','<p>Cloud data migration has become an essential requirement for modern enterprises due to the rapid growth of cloud computing and data-intensive applications. However, achieving reliable and efficient migration remains challenging because of heterogeneous data types, fluctuating network bandwidth, diverse migration tools, and the need to preserve data consistency throughout the migration process. Most existing approaches evaluate migration tools independently, assume static network conditions, and provide limited support for adaptive optimization and verifiable migration outcomes. This paper presents AMF-CloudForge, a unified machine learning-driven framework that integrates migration state analysis, intelligent scheduling, consistency preservation, and real-time adaptive management into a single end-to-end architecture. The proposed framework begins with Polytype Migration State Encoding (PMSE), which performs comprehensive micro-benchmarking across different data types, file sizes, and network conditions to generate Migration State Tensors and Tool Efficiency Scores that accurately characterize migration behavior. These learned representations are then utilized by the Topology-Aware Diffusion Scheduler (TADS) to optimize chunking, routing, and migration tool selection by considering dynamic network topology, bandwidth variations, and transfer costs. To ensure data integrity and consistency, the Causal Delta with Erasure-coded Commitment (CDEC) module combines causal dependency tracking, adaptive erasure coding, and cryptographic quorum verification to guarantee reliable and verifiable data commits without data loss or metadata inconsistencies. Furthermore, the Uncertainty-Quantified Twin Supervisor (UQTS) continuously monitors the migration process through a probabilistic digital twin, enabling risk-aware scheduling adjustments and real-time Service Level Objective (SLO) verification under changing system conditions.Together, these components form a fully automated migration framework capable of policy-driven execution and machine-verifiable migration reporting. Experimental evaluation on heterogeneous cloud workloads demonstrates that the proposed framework reduces overall migration makespan by 35–45%, sustains effective bandwidth utilization above 85%, and limits unrecoverable data loss to approximately 10⁻¹¹ per 10 TB of migrated data. By integrating machine learning-based performance modeling with adaptive scheduling, cryptographic consistency verification, and intelligent runtime supervision, the proposed framework transforms cloud data migration from a static, tool-centric process into a reliable, adaptive, and continuously optimized cloud service.</p>','["Cloud Data Migration, Machine Learning Scheduling, Causal Consistency, Erasure Coding, Automated Framework, Process."]',NULL,24,1,'15-38','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1615','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',2),
(1616,1616,1,'AI-Powered Fraud Detection in Banking: A Big Data Analytics Perspective','<p>Banking fraud presents a persistent challenge in the digital era, with financial losses continuing to escalate as transaction volumes grow exponentially. This paper examines AI-enabled fraud detection frameworks that leverage big data analytics, machine learning, and deep learning methodologies. The review systematically categorizes existing approaches across supervised learning, anomaly detection, ensemble methods, and deep neural network architectures. Special attention is directed toward hybrid frameworks that combine multiple techniques to address extreme class imbalance, concept drift, and real-time processing constraints. Analysis indicates that ensemble models incorporating XGBoost with LSTM networks achieve accuracy exceeding 98% with substantially reduced false positive rates. Critical challenges including model interpretability, data privacy, and deployment scalability are examined, alongside promising directions for future research in this domain.</p>','["Big data analytics", "banking fraud detection", "machine learning", "deep learning", "ensemble methods", "anomaly detection", "hybrid models."]',NULL,24,1,'39-51','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1616','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',3),
(1618,1618,1,'Sufficient Statistic Aggregation for Scalable Regression Analytics','<p>To overcome the limitation of in-memory regression schemes in Big Data scenario, we propose a scalable framework based on sufficient statistic aggregation. Using this method, we are able to compute linear, weighted linear, Box-Cox and ridge regression models efficiently as it processes mini-batches and retains an aggregate sufficient statistics array. The method uses little I/O and allows parallel processing to obtain accurate solutions for large regression problems. The experimental results on Spark show that it yields much better performance than the conventional techniques. This is especially true in cases where multiple scenarios of using multiple models ensue.</p>','["Explainability, Entity Matching, Graph Neural Networks"]',NULL,24,1,'52-62','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1618','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',4),
(1620,1620,1,'Explicating the Interplay of Artificial Intelligence, Dynamic Capabilities, and Sharia Governance in Islamic Banking: A Systematic Review','<p>This research investigates the relationship between AI capabilities and dynamic capabilities (AIC-DC), emphasizing middle managers as intermediaries, analyzing advancements in Islamic banking scholarship, and assessing the moderating effect of sharia governance (SG) on achieving ethical results and sustainable development goals (SDGs) imperatives. This systematic literature review follows PRISMA 2020 guidelines, exploring trends in AI-DC, the roles of middle managers, and SG in banking. Utilizing a PICOC framework, it highlights significant clusters in AI-fintech, organizational theory, and adoption behaviors derived from 25 studies conducted between 2019 and 2025. PRISMA 2020-guided findings demonstrate that AIC indirectly contributes to innovation through a variety of mechanisms, which are influenced by managerial agency and the moderation of SG''s normative framework. The innovation trajectories are influenced by the institutional diversity of regional contexts in the UAE and Indonesia. Contemporary scholarship suggests that there is a fragmented understanding of AIC-DC in the context of aligning with SDGs, particularly in the areas of responsible finance and financial inclusion. Middle managers play a critical yet understudied role in the manner in which they navigate tensions within AIC-DC linkages. This synthesis identifies current deficiencies in innovation within emerging markets and proposes a research framework focused on policy-relevant investigations that enhance strategies consistent with the SDGs in global finance.</p>','["artificial intelligence, emerging economies, sustainable development goals, FinTech, financial innovation"]',NULL,24,1,'63-82','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1620','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',5),
(1621,1621,1,'Institutional Prerequisites for Investment Support of Economic Development of the Hospitality Industry in Ukraine’s Transitional Economy','<p>Accommodation industry is a core contributor to the growth of the economy in the emerging market countries and the growth depends heavily on integrity of institutional structures. One of the urgent issues is the need to entrench the background governing structure that is required to mobilize capital in Ukrainian accommodation industry in the post-conflict rebuilding process. It is still possible to realize the quantifiable effect of regulatory and administrative factors of influence on capital deployment frameworks, or the effect they have on the paradigm of operation in the accommodations businesses. It is assumed that this work will estimate the institutional factors that in a numerical form will support the inflow of the capital in the development of the hospitality sector in Ukraine and comment on the changes of the operations models during 2015-2024 years. This research will be grounded on the econometric modelling with fixed effects, difference-in-differences technique, k-means clustering and a composite indicator of institutional quality of panel data in 24 regions of Ukraine. Institutional prerequisites have been systematized at three levels, revealing the critical role of micro-institutions and the dual nature of the institutional system of a transitional economy. The most elastic standards of the institutional environment, which increase the level of investment by 23–28%, are property protection (0.420) and the licensing system (0.312). The reason behind the three major regions as an institutional trap is based on the fact that they account 62% of foreign investment and low performance regions. The impact of the digitization of licensing processes (2020-2021) is +15-19% in that over 18 months, leading areas will obtain 50 more projects. Online bookings 2015-2024: The increase in online booking was 5-65% and 15-65% and cashless 8-23% and 8-23%. The practical implication is that an evidence base on priorities of reforms needs to be developed and systems modernization of the sector needs to be confirmed.</p>','["institutional prerequisites, investment support, hospitality industry, transformational economy, hotel sector, quality of institutions, regional differentiation, panel data, property protection, licensing system, institutional reforms, cluster analysis, econometric modelling, Ukraine"]',NULL,24,1,'83-101','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1621','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',6),
(1660,1660,1,'Generative Artificial Intelligence-Enabled Decision Support Systems and Organizational Performance: A Moderated Mediation Model of Human–AI Collaboration Quality','<p>Background: Organizations are rapidly adopting generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) within decision support systems (DSS) to accelerate and improve managerial decision-making, yet empirical evidence on how GenAI-enabled DSS translate into measurable organizational performance gains remains fragmented and inconsistent across industries. Research gap: Prior information systems (IS) research has examined GenAI adoption, decision support effectiveness, and organizational performance largely in isolation, with limited integrated testing of the mechanisms (mediators) and boundary conditions (moderators) that explain when and how GenAI-enabled DSS improve performance outcomes. In particular, the role of human-AI collaboration quality as a moderating condition, rather than as an outcome, has received little empirical attention. Purpose: This study develops and empirically tests a moderated mediation model in which decision support system effectiveness mediates the relationships between three antecedents (generative AI capability, data and information quality, and organizational AI readiness) and organizational performance, with human-AI collaboration quality moderating the mediated path. Method: Grounded in Dynamic Capability Theory and the DeLone and McLean IS Success Model, the study proposes a quantitative, cross-sectional survey design targeting managers and decision-makers in medium and large organizations, with data to be analyzed using Partial Least Squares Structural Equation Modeling (PLS-SEM). Findings: Using an illustrative simulated dataset constructed to demonstrate the analytical procedure, all measurement and structural model criteria (reliability, convergent and discriminant validity, path significance, moderated mediation) are shown to meet accepted thresholds, and the hypothesized relationships receive support.</p>','["Generative Artificial Intelligence", "Decision Support Systems", "Organizational Performance", "Human-AI Collaboration", "Dynamic Capability Theory", "IS Success Model", "Data Quality", "AI Readiness", "PLS-SEM", "Digital Transformation."]',NULL,24,1,'102-124','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1660','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',7),
(1662,1662,1,'The Effect of Corporate Social Responsibility on Consumer Purchase Intention of Fast Fashion Apparel via Perceived Quality, Trust and Brand Love as Mediators','<p>The present study examines the role of Data were collected through an online survey of 400 Thai consumers who had purchased fast fashion within the previous few months and were aware of CSR activities of their chosen brands. Screening questions ensured appropriate sample selection. Structural equation modeling (SEM) was applied to test the hypothesized relationships. The results reveal that CSR significantly influences brand love, perceived quality, and trust. Ethical CSR exerted the strongest effect on perceived quality, while legal CSR had the greatest impact on brand love and trust. Among the mediators, brand love demonstrated the strongest direct effect on purchase intention, followed by ethical CSR, legal CSR, trust, and economic CSR, respectively. Interestingly, perceived quality did not directly influence purchase intention. These findings underscore that CSR, particularly in its ethical and legal dimensions, serves as a critical strategy for fast-fashion firms to foster emotional attachment, build trust, and ultimately enhance sustainable competitiveness in the Thai market.</p>','["Corporate social responsibility, Brand love, Trust, Perceived quality, Purchase intention, Fast fashion apparel."]',NULL,24,1,'125-143','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1662','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',8),
(1663,1663,1,'Context-Driven Radiological Video Super-Resolution via Dense Feature Propagation and Adversarial Learning','<p>Radiological video super-resolution demands proper reconstruction of spatial features coupled with preservation of time consistency of viable clinical interpretation. Current deep learning methods, such as CNNs, GANs, and transformer-based models, are mostly models based on frame-wise representations and do not learn to harmoniously represent spatial-temporal correlations and domain-specific contextual stimuli. To overcome this shortcoming, a context-aware video super-resolution model using a 3D DenseNet-GAN architecture is suggested in this paper. The generator uses the densely connected 3D convolutional blocks to learn the spatial–temporal features well, but the adversarial training is used to increase the realism of perception. A balanced optimization is ensured by a composite loss function that is a combination of pixel-wise loss, perceptual loss, adversarial loss and temporal consistency loss. Radiological video sequences are experimentally evaluated showing a high consistency of performance with PSNRs of 20.69, 24.44 dB and SSIM 0.6659 and 0.8996, and with a better structural continuity and lower temporal artifacts. The suggested framework is a single-solution framework to high-quality radiological video super-resolution, and it can improve the reliability of diagnoses.</p>','["Video Super-Resolution, Radiological Imaging, 3D Convolutional Neural Networks, DenseNet, Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs), Temporal Consistency, Medical Image Enhancement."]',NULL,24,1,'144-158','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1663','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',9);
INSERT INTO publications (id,submission_id,version,title,abstract,keywords,references_text,issue_id,section_id,pages,doi,copyright_holder,copyright_year,license_url,status,date_published,seq) VALUES
(1681,1681,1,'Transformation of International Economic and Business Alliances in the Face of Global Turbulence: Challenges of Sustainable Development','<p>The article presents a study of transformation processes in international economic and business alliances caused by global turbulence. The relevance of the problem is substantiated in the context of geopolitical tensions, disruption of global supply chains, acceleration of digital transformation and increased requirements for sustainable development. A study was conducted to determine the effectiveness of strategic analysis tools, digital analytics, risk management, and ESG approaches to improve international cooperation. Structural changes in global alliances were analyzed, in particular the regionalization of cooperation, public-private partnerships, and digital ecosystems. The methodological basis of the work is a systematic, comparative and economic-analytical approach, including modeling of strategic scenarios and case studies of leading international alliances in 2015–2024. Key factors influencing the evolution of global partnerships were identified: geopolitical risks, digital infrastructure, investment mobility, environmental obligations and international trade dynamics. The transformation of international economic and business alliances appears as a new paradigm of cooperation that ensures stability and flexibility of the global economy. This highlights the increasing importance of adaptive governance mechanisms in global economic cooperation.</p>','["International Alliances, Global Turbulence, Sustainable Development, Digitalization, Strategic Partnership"]',NULL,24,1,'159-175','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1681','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',10),
(1682,1682,1,'A Two-Axis Typology of AI Readiness and Innovation Capacity in Africa','<p>This paper develops a transparent two-axis typology that positions 54 African countries by (i) public-sector AI and digital-government readiness and (ii) innovation and technology-upgrading outcomes. The X-axis combines the Government AI Readiness Index (Oxford Insights) and the UN E‑Government Development Index (EGDI), while the Y-axis combines the Global Innovation Index (WIPO) and high‑technology exports as a share of manufactured exports (World Bank WDI). Using the most recent observations available for each indicator and Africa-relative z‑score standardization, we compute composite scores, flag missingness and data-quality limitations, and classify countries into four quadrants. The results indicate a small set of co-evolving leaders (high readiness and high innovation outcomes), a larger group where government capability outpaces innovation performance, a small set of innovation/export “pockets” with governance gaps, and a majority of foundation-building countries where basic enablers remain binding. We discuss how the typology can support sequencing of technology-transition priorities and the design of differentiated development-cooperation portfolios, and we outline how Korea’s engagement can link ODA instruments with responsible private-sector entry and knowledge partnerships.</p>','["Artificial intelligence", "Digital government", "Innovation capacity", "Africa", "Official development assistance (ODA)"]',NULL,24,1,'176-201','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1682','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',11),
(1683,1683,1,'IStego100K++: A Large-Scale GAN-Based Steganalysis Dataset Using Adversarially Learned Embedding Costs','<p>Steganalysis benchmarks built with fixed distortion functions — such as nsF5, J-UNIWARD, and UERD — are increasingly inadequate against GAN-based steganography, where adversarially learned embedding costs bypass traditional rich-model detectors. To address this gap, we introduce IStego100K++, a large-scale GAN-generated JPEG stego dataset of 100,071 cover–stego image pairs at 1024×1024 resolution. Stego images were produced using a pre-trained UT-GAN generator with a U-Net architecture and a differentiable Double-Tanh embedding simulator, covering payload rates of {0.1–0.5} bpp and JPEG quality factors of {75–95}, achieving a mean PSNR of 74.52 dB, a mean SSIM of 0.9994, and 37.9% of images recording zero measurable distortion after JPEG recompression. Evaluated against two established rich-model steganalyzers — DCTR and GFR — detection accuracy drops from 79.55% on the original IStego100K to approximately 50% across all tested payload rates and quality conditions. Additional evaluation with CNN-based steganalyzers (SRNet-KV and XuNet) yields AUC values of 0.6953 and 0.6901 respectively, confirming that while CNN detectors outperform rich-model methods, IStego100K++ remains a significantly more challenging benchmark for next-generation universal steganalysis research.</p>','["Image Steganography", "Steganalysis", "Generative Adversarial Network", "UT-GAN", "CNN", "JPEG Compression."]',NULL,24,1,'202-219','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1683','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',12),
(1684,1684,1,'Psychosocial Wellbeing Predicting Refugee Mental Health: A Quasi-Longitudinal Study During COVID-19','<p>Background: The prospective relationship between psychosocial wellbeing and mental health in refugee populations remains underexplored, particularly during the COVID-19 pandemic, which intensified psychosocial vulnerabilities.</p>
INSERT INTO publications (id,submission_id,version,title,abstract,keywords,references_text,issue_id,section_id,pages,doi,copyright_holder,copyright_year,license_url,status,date_published,seq) VALUES
(1711,1711,1,'Performance Evaluation of Algorithmic Support for Secure One-Way Communications in Geographically Distributed Organizations','<p>Implementation of hardware-based unidirectional security gateways (data diodes) in Critical Energy Infrastructures (CEI) results in a significant cryptographic synchronization and reliability dilemma. Conventional network transport protocols and interactive key agreement procedures suffer from complete algorithm failure due to breaking the feedback loop and thus resulting in retransmission flooding, buffer bloating, or catastrophic packet fragmentation in case of aggressive industrial noise. In this work, we provide a thorough performance analysis of a single, non-interactive security infrastructure dedicated to unbuffered, no feedback channels in geographically distributed entities. The analyzed infrastructure consists of Temporal Identity-Based Initialization (TIBI) procedure for autonomous spatiotemporal key synchronization, Lightweight Hybrid Encapsulation with Adaptive Forward Error Correction (LHE-FEC) through Systematic Random Linear Network Coding (S-RLNC) for lossless delivery at arbitrary data rates, and Interleaved Merkle-Hash Chains (IM-Forest) for asynchronous data integrity verification. Using discrete event simulation experiments of up to 1,000 concurrently connected industrial nodes, we show that the proposed architecture is able to demonstrate near linear latency scaling (~450 ms at maximum scale), as well as 100% payload reconstruction under aggressive burst loss scenarios up to 30%. In addition, the computational burden assessment demonstrates that there is an operational asymmetry of more than 95%, which successfully manages to outsource computationally intensive processes such as matrix inversion and recursive hashing to processing authorities and maintain the battery and processing efficiency of the periphery devices.</p>','["Unidirectional Security Gateways", "Data Diodes", "Spatiotemporal Entropy", "Rateless Coding", "Systematic Random Linear Network Coding (S-RLNC)", "Merkle Trees", "Critical Infrastructure Security", "Asynchronous Attestation", "Resource Asymmetry."]',NULL,24,1,'631-643','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1711','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',35),
(1714,1714,1,'Evaluation of the Needs of Individuals with Visual Impairments in Jerusalem and Its Suburbs: An Analytical Study of Available Services','<p>This study aimed to evaluate the needs of individuals with visual impairments in Jerusalem and its suburbs through an analytical study of the available services. A descriptive approach was used, employing both quantitative and qualitative analyses on a sample of 41 participants with visual impairments from the suburbs of Jerusalem. Data were collected via surveys and interviews during the second semester of the 2026 academic year. The results indicated that the needs of individuals with visual impairments in Jerusalem and its suburbs were rated low, with a mean score of (1.65) and a standard deviation of (0.320). Interviews revealed that individuals with visual impairments face various challenges, primarily related to mobility accommodations, personal agency, and coping with their disabilities. Access to educational centers and schools is particularly difficult, especially given the current conditions under occupation. Additionally, there is a significant shortage of qualified specialists for individuals with visual impairments. The study recommends activating the role of laws specific to people with disabilities and employment legislation to raise awareness among community institutions, develop sustainable projects, and allocate vocational rehabilitation programs and advanced assistive devices for individuals with total blindness to effectively meet their functional and living needs.</p>','["Visual Impairment, Needs Assessment, Available Services, Jerusalem, Suburbs"]',NULL,24,1,'644-670','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1714','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',36),
(1715,1715,1,'Vasopressin Versus Tranexamic Acid for Control of Blood Loss Related to Abdominal Hysterectomy: A Randomized Clinical Study','<p><strong>Background</strong>: Hemorrhage is one of the numerous hazards that may be associated with an abdominal hysterectomy, a significant surgical procedure. It is imperative to minimize the perioperative morbidity related to abdominal hysterectomy by reducing intraoperative blood loss. This study aimed to examine the effects of locally administered vasopressin (VP) and tranexamic acid (TXA) on blood loss in women undergoing abdominal hysterectomy for benign conditions.</p>
