(1791,1791,1,'Intelligent Predictive Maintenance Using Hybrid Artificial Intelligence Models for Sustainable Industrial Automation in Industry 4.0','<p>The rapid advancement of Industry 4.0 has transformed conventional industrial automation by integrating intelligent sensing, cyber-physical systems, industrial Internet of Things (IIoT), cloud computing, and advanced artificial intelligence technologies into manufacturing environments. Among these innovations, predictive maintenance has emerged as a strategic approach for improving equipment reliability, minimizing unexpected failures, reducing maintenance costs, and enhancing production efficiency. However, the increasing complexity of industrial systems requires more robust and adaptive prediction mechanisms than those offered by conventional machine learning models. Hybrid artificial intelligence models, combining deep learning, ensemble learning, optimization algorithms, and explainable decision-making techniques, provide improved fault diagnosis, remaining useful life estimation, and maintenance scheduling under dynamic operating conditions. Furthermore, intelligent predictive maintenance contributes significantly to sustainable industrial automation by reducing energy consumption, minimizing material waste, extending equipment lifespan, and supporting environmentally responsible manufacturing practices. This study proposes a comprehensive research framework that investigates the integration of hybrid artificial intelligence models into predictive maintenance systems for Industry 4.0. The proposed framework aims to improve prediction accuracy, operational reliability, resource utilization, and sustainability while enabling autonomous maintenance decisions in smart factories. The study further establishes performance evaluation metrics and implementation strategies for next-generation intelligent industrial maintenance systems.</p>','["Predictive Maintenance, Hybrid Artificial Intelligence, Industry 4.0, Sustainable Manufacturing, Industrial Internet of Things (IIoT), Smart Industrial Automation."]',NULL,24,1,'1099-1125','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1791','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',60),
(1796,1796,1,'Artificial Intelligence in Infection Control: A Comprehensive Review of Applications and Protocols','<p>Hospital-acquired infections (HAIs) continue to pose significant challenges to healthcare systems worldwide, contributing to increased morbidity, mortality, prolonged hospitalization, and healthcare costs. Conventional infection prevention and control (IPC) strategies often rely on labor-intensive surveillance and delayed decision-making, limiting their effectiveness in rapidly evolving clinical environments. Artificial intelligence (AI) has emerged as a transformative technology capable of enhancing infection control through advanced data analytics, machine learning, deep learning, natural language processing, and computer vision. This comprehensive review examines current AI applications in IPC, including early infection detection, healthcare-associated infection prediction, antimicrobial resistance forecasting, automated surveillance, hand hygiene and personal protective equipment compliance monitoring, environmental decontamination, antimicrobial stewardship, and microbiology laboratory support. The review also discusses major implementation challenges, including data quality, interoperability, model interpretability, ethical concerns, organizational readiness, workforce training, and infrastructure limitations, particularly in low-resource settings. Emerging trends such as federated learning, explainable AI, Internet of Things integration, robotics, genomics, and personalized infection prevention are also explored. Overall, AI has considerable potential to strengthen infection prevention by enabling proactive surveillance, improving clinical decision-making, optimizing resource utilization, and supporting real-time interventions. However, successful implementation requires rigorous validation, transparent governance, multidisciplinary collaboration, and integration into existing clinical workflows to ensure safe, equitable, and sustainable adoption.</p>','["Artificial intelligence", "Infection prevention and control", "Hospital-acquired infections", "Healthcare-associated infections", "Machine learning", "Deep learning", "Antimicrobial resistance", "Surveillance", "Predictive analytics", "Antimicrobial stewardship."]',NULL,24,1,'1126-1136','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1796','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',61),
(1797,1797,1,'Predictive Detection and Resolution of SQL Server Performance Bottlenecks Using Generative AI And Dynamic Management Views (DMVS)','<p>Resource pressure is a recurring issue in SQL Server performance management because users only notice it when they experience slow response times, blocking, timeouts, or service instability. Manual interpretation of Dynamic Management Views (DMVs) is typically reactive and requires specialist database-administration expertise to interpret the details of the telemetry, which can be accessed via DMVs This study proposes and tests a predictive model to identify and resolve SQL Server performance issues based on the DMVs telemetry, supervised machine learning, and guarded Generative AI suggestions. The records were gathered from a balanced SQL Server 2025 Developer environment in five different operational states: normal, CPU bottleneck, I/O bottleneck, blocking bottleneck, and missing-index bottleneck with the AdventureWorks2022 database. Logistic Regression, Random Forest, Gradient Boosting and XGBoost classifiers were built using Encoded DMV features. Random Forest and Gradient Boosting obtained the highest test accuracy (83.64%) and macro F1-score (83.89%), and the five-fold cross-validation showed good stability. The blocking session count, the number of tasks waiting, wait time, elapsed time, number of logical reads, worker time, and physical reads were the most important features identified by the feature-importance analysis. The GenAI recommendation layer generated root cause explanations and safe resolution actions that are understandable to the DBA. Contribution is a proactive and explainable framework for SQL Server performance management.</p>','["SQL Server performance management", "Dynamic Management Views", "bottleneck prediction", "machine learning", "generative AI", "systems management"]',NULL,24,1,'1137-1155','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1797','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',62),
(1798,1798,1,'Influence of Plasmonic Metal Surfaces on Photoelectric Effect Electron Energies: An Experimental Quantum Study','<p>The photoelectric effect under plasmonic excitation has emerged as a promising approach to enhance electron emission beyond conventional limits. Nanostructured plasmonic films of gold (Au) and silver (Ag) were investigated for their influence on electron energy enhancement in the photoelectric effect using a combined experimental and theoretical approach. The films were irradiated using a tunable laser source operating in the 400–800 nm wavelength range at power densities ranging from 10 to 100 mW/cm². Photoelectron spectroscopy measurements reveal a significant increase in emitted electron energies under plasmon-resonant excitation conditions. To analyze the observed phenomena, a combined Finite-Difference Time-Domain (FDTD) simulation and Time-Dependent Density Functional Theory (TD-DFT) framework was employed. FDTD was used to model Localized Surface Plasmon Resonance (LSPR) and electromagnetic field enhancement near the metal surface, showing strong field amplification of up to ~20–35. TD-DFT calculations were utilized to capture electron excitation dynamics and energy redistribution at the quantum level. Eigenmode and Fourier spectral analyses were performed to identify resonance peaks and describe wave particle interaction effects associated with damping behavior. A resonant regime was observed at ~520 nm (Au) and ~450 nm (Ag), where electron emission intensity increased by approximately 2.5. Additionally, a critical coupling parameter range demonstrated reduced energy loss, enabling efficient hot-electron generation with nanometer-scale confinement (~1–5 nm). The results confirm the presence of a plasmon-enhanced photoelectric effect, providing improved performance over conventional semiconductor systems. This research provides valuable insights for the development of high-performance plasmonic solar cells, nanoscale electron emitters, and advanced optoelectronic devices.</p>','["Plasmonic Metal Surfaces, Photoelectric Effect, Hot Electron Generation, Localized Surface Plasmon Resonance (LSPR), Finite-Difference Time-Domain (FDTD), Electron Energy Enhancement, Quantum Electron Dynamics."]',NULL,24,1,'1156-1175','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1798','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',63),
(1800,1800,1,'Design of a Scalable Control Plane for Large-Scale SDNs','<p>Software-Defined Networking (SDN) has become a major facilitator to flexible and programmable network operation; nevertheless, the extension of the control plane to handle big networks with heterogeneous networks has remained a core issue. The proposal presented in this paper is a design of a scalable control plane in a large SDN that can overcome the constraints of the centralized and fixed multi-controller designs. The suggested architecture takes the form of hierarchical and multi-controller structure with dynamically clustering and federation techniques to provide elasticity, fault tolerance, and effective use of resources. An assignment-based strategy to load-aware controllers is presented to dynamically assign switches to controllers in regard to real-time traffic intensity, control workload, and latency constraints whereas seamless controller migration ensures continuity of services in the face of network dynamics. The control plane also uses flow rule partitioning and aggregation to decrease state overhead and minimize redundant control operations in order to further ensure scalability. There is control message batching, adaptive poll, event based update and they are combined to reduce signaling overhead and enhance responsiveness by a large margin. A latency-aware controllerswitch mapping algorithm is also used to minimize control path delays to get rule installation and network convergence on time. Extensive performance analysis shows that the proposed design is almost linearly scalable as the network size is increased, and control latency is significantly lower and throughput is much higher than traditional SDN control plane designs. These findings affirm that the suggested scalable control plane is practical in supporting large scale SDN implementation and is therefore applicable in future carrier grade, data center and wide area network deployments at realistic workloads with varying topological setups in the modern programmable networks in the world.</p>','["Software-Defined Networking", "Scalable Control Plane", "Multi-Controller Architecture", "Load-Aware Controller Assignment", "Control Plane Latency", "Network Scalability"]',NULL,24,1,'1176-1189','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1800','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',64),
(1801,1801,1,'An Intelligent Machine Learning Classifier System for Class Imbalance Learning and Improved Prediction Accuracy','<p>One of the major problems with machine learning is class imbalance, which occurs when the majority class is much larger than the minority one and the classifiers tend to learn the majority class rather than the minority ones, and hence make poor predictions in the latter scenario. This paper introduces an Intelligent Machine Learning Classifier System for Class Imbalance Learning and Improved Prediction Accuracy in a single framework that combines adaptive class balancing (ACCB), dynamic feature selection (DFS), hybrid ensemble learning (HEL) and confidence-based decision fusion (CFDF). Initially, data preprocessing is used to remove inconsistencies from the data and to normalize the distribution of features. To obtain a balanced learning, an Intelligent Adaptive Class Balancing Algorithm is used to generate representative minority samples and to reduce majority instances to be redundant. After that, a Dynamic Feature Importance Selection Algorithm determines the attributes that are discriminative and improve classification accuracy and decrease computational complexity. A Hybrid Ensemble Classification Algorithm using several machine learning classifiers is used to process the selected features to improve the robustness and generalization. Finally, a Confidence-Based Intelligent Prediction Algorithm is used to generate reliable prediction with the estimation of confidence. Experimental results on standard imbalanced datasets show that the accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, G-Mean and ROC-AUC are always improved than the traditional machine learning methods. The proposed framework is able to effectively deal with the class imbalance, reduce the prediction bias, improve minority class detection and offer a scalable and interpretable solution for various real world intelligent decision support applications in different domains.</p>','["Class Imbalance Learning", "Machine Learning Classification", "Hybrid Ensemble Learning", "Adaptive Feature Selection", "Intelligent Decision Fusion", "Prediction Accuracy."]',NULL,24,1,'1190-1205','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1801','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',65),
(1802,1802,1,'STARC-POM: An Integrated Security Trust-aware Adaptive Routing with Cross Layer and Path Optimization Mechanism for Mobile Wireless Sensor Networks','<p>Mobile wireless sensor networks application requires secure and efficient communication that is possible only through the operation of Mobile Wireless Sensor Networks (MWSNs). MWSNs possess certain special problems due to the fact that it is mobile, energy-constrained, dynamical topological and vulnerable to security problems. The existing routing systems like cluster and ad hoc routing systems are typified by high energy consumption, unsuitability to address misbehavior by nodes, inability to engage in trust-based routing and inefficient protection against malicious attacks that renders networks inefficient and less reliable. In order to address these deficiencies, this paper proposes a security aware and energy efficient routing scheme as a combination of Path Optimization Mechanism in MWSN (POM-MWSN) and the Security Trust-aware Adaptive Routing with Cross Layer (STARC) model. POM-MWSN takes into account the node connectivity, energy and mobility and constructs the optimization route where STARC takes into account the trust assessment, cross layer routing and selection of hybrid cluster head to attain the secure and reliable communication. The proposed solution will be a special hybrid of energy-aware way optimization and faith-based routing and attack detection, which will render the network working strong and effective. Numerous simulations demonstrate that the given framework outcompetes the existing ones and achieves end-to-end delay of 0.005 ms, higher throughput of 80,000–84,000 bytes, packet delivery ratio of 237 packets, residual energy of 99.7 J, and overhead 12,450 packets and cost of communication 2985 bits. The results validate the utility of the proposed approach in the provision of trust-based, energy-efficient, and secure routing in the dynamic MWSNs.</p>','["Mobile Wireless Sensor Networks (MWSNs), Security, Path Optimization, Clustering, Energy, and Routing."]',NULL,24,1,'1206-1232','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1802','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',66),
(1803,1803,1,'The Role of Digital Marketing in Enhancing Brand Awareness: Analysing the Impact of Social Media, SEO, and Personalisation on Consumer Engagement and Brand Equity','<p>This study examines the impact of digital marketing strategies including Social Media Marketing (SMM), Search Engine Optimisation (SEO), and Personalisation on consumer engagement and brand equity in digital environments. This research aims to analyse the contribution of each of these strategies towards an enhanced customer interaction and a heightened customer-perceived brand value. A quantitative method was undertaken using a self-administered online questionnaire, which was sent through convenience sampling. Statistical analyses on the collected data were performed to identify the strength as well as the direction of the relationship between variables. Personalisation emerged as the most important driver for consumer engagement, followed by SEO, which facilitated improved consumer engagement through building brand visibility and credibility. As compared to SMM, this study found no statistically significant direct effect of SMM on either the level of consumer engagement or the level of brand equity. Given its limitations, such as exploratory study and a geographically narrow sample, its findings cannot be applied to the general population. In practice, marketers are encouraged to tailor the personalised digital experience and SEO techniques for better engagement as well as brand trust. The study makes original contributions by comparing how effective these strategies are relative to each other and suggests actionable recommendations on how to better align a digital brand position.</p>','["Digital Marketing", "Social Media Marketing", "SEO", "Personalization", "Consumer Engagement", "Brand Equity", "Quantitative Research"]',NULL,24,1,'1233-1248','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1803','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',67),
(1804,1804,1,'The Cultural Heritage of Omani Ports in Islamic eras from the Perspective of Omani University Students','<p>This study aims to reveal the extent of Omani university students'' awareness of the cultural heritage of Omani ports during the Islamic era, and to examine their perceptions of the role of these ports in the commercial, cultural, and scientific exchange between Oman and the Islamic world. To achieve this, the study sample was selected using convenience random sampling, comprising (500) male and female students from five Omani universities. The study employed a descriptive-analytical approach, and data were collected using an awareness scale consisting of (4) axes followed by (40) items, after ensuring the necessary validity and reliability. The results of this study revealed that the overall average across all axes was high, and that many students are aware of the historical and commercial importance of Omani ports. However, only a limited percentage possess detailed knowledge of the cultural and civilizational role that these ports played during the Islamic era. The study also revealed a need to enhance academic and societal awareness of the Sultanate of Oman''s maritime heritage through university curricula, cultural programs, and research activities. This aligns with the higher education objectives in Oman, which aim to foster a deeper understanding of the role of maritime ports in Omani civilization. The study recommends integrating topics related to maritime heritage and historical ports into university curricula and encouraging students to conduct field studies at historical coastal sites. This will contribute to strengthening national and cultural identity and deepening understanding of the Sultanate of Oman''s pioneering role in Islamic history.</p>','["Omani ports, cultural heritage, Islamic eras, cultural exchange, Omani university students."]',NULL,24,1,'1249-1267','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1804','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',68),
(1805,1805,1,'Transforming Education Through ICT-Driven Pedagogical Practices','<p>The use of Information and Communication Technology (ICT) is becoming an important catalyst in changing the nature of educational practice, as educational institutions have shifted from teacher-centred to more learner-centred, flexible and interactive learning environments. The influence of ICTs on teaching and learning in the classroom, introducing digital resources, virtual learning environments, the use of multimedia, collaborative platforms, educational applications and emerging new intelligent technologies to the classroom, will be discussed. Special attention is given to the role of technology-supported pedagogy in assisting with the development of active learning, personalised learning, collaborative knowledge building, critical thinking, creativity and learner autonomy. The paper also demonstrates the movement of the role of the teacher from information provider to facilitator, mentor and designer of technology-supported learning experiences. At the same time, individual teachers'' digital competences, the institutional infrastructure, the availability of ICTs, pedagogical readiness, and the capacities of students in responsible use of digital resources are important for successful incorporation. Factors which could limit the educational potential of ICT are: pedagogical resistance, information overload, insufficient technological infrastructure and the digital divide. The study highlights the importance of ensuring the integration of technology in the context of sound pedagogical practices, and not technology for technology''s sake. It asserts that to achieve meaningful integration of ICTs, there is a need for ongoing teacher development, enabling digital infrastructure, effective instructional design, and institutional support. The authors conclude that pedagogical practices and learning approaches that are supported by Information and Communication Technologies (ICTs) and strategically implemented can help build more engaging, inclusive, adaptive and future-oriented education systems and reinforce the learning competencies to participate in an increasingly digital society.</p>','["Information and Communication Technology, ICT in Education, Digital Pedagogy, Technology-Enhanced Learning, Digital Learning, Teacher Competence, Educational Transformation"]',NULL,24,1,'1268-1285','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1805','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',69),
(1806,1806,1,'An Explainable Artificial Intelligence-Driven Blockchain Architecture for Secure Healthcare Data Exchange and Clinical Decision Support','<p>The digitization of healthcare records has improved clinical efficiency but has simultaneously introduced significant challenges around data security, interoperability, patient consent management, and the transparency of AI-assisted clinical decisions. This paper proposes a Hybrid Explainable Artificial Intelligence-Driven Blockchain Architecture that integrates a permissioned blockchain network for secure, auditable healthcare data exchange with an ensemble machine learning clinical decision support model whose predictions are made transparent through an integrated explainability layer combining SHAP, LIME, and attention-based rationale. The architecture employs a permissioned Hyperledger Fabric network in which hospitals, insurers, and regulatory auditors participate as distinct peer types, governed by smart contracts that enforce patient consent, fine-grained access control, and an immutable audit trail, while bulk clinical data is stored off-chain in an encrypted vault referenced by on-chain hashes to preserve scalability. The clinical decision support module was evaluated on a multi-institutional patient dataset comprising 186,420 records across five chronic disease prediction tasks. Experimental results show that the proposed model achieves 97.3% accuracy, 95.8% precision, 94.6% recall, and a 95.2% F1-score, outperforming the strongest baseline (deep neural network) by 4.4 percentage points in F1-score, while blockchain performance evaluation shows sustained throughput of up to 858 transactions per second at 24 peer nodes with average transaction latency remaining below 420 milliseconds.</p>','["Explainable Artificial Intelligence, Blockchain, Healthcare Data Exchange, Clinical Decision Support, Hyperledger Fabric, SHAP, Smart Contracts, Data Interoperability, Patient Consent Management, Health Informatics"]',NULL,24,1,'1286-1301','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1806','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',70),
(1807,1807,1,'Unveiling Financial Challenges: RSETI''s Role in Rural Entrepreneurship','<p>The RSETI (Rural Self Employment Training Institute) program has a strategy of providing entrepreneurs with short-term training and long-term support. RSETI is an initiative by the Government of India to help rural people start their businesses by providing proper training facilities for educating them and inculcating the necessary skills required for starting a business. Young people aged between 18 to 45 years are eligible to join the training program. The RSETIs have established themselves as pioneers in capturing rural youth ambitions and turning them into prosperous entrepreneurs by teaching underprivileged rural youth and giving them training about various skills which can be used to start their businesses. This case discusses rural people''s entrepreneurial training and examines the finance-related complications trainees face after completing the training program. In order to understand the problems faced by trainees while starting their business, 15 trainees were interviewed who completed their training from RSETI. The study contributes to the extant literature of rural entrepreneurship by analyzing the financial dilemmas that are faced by trainees after completing the training program and the measures that can be taken to overcome these problems.</p>','["Entrepreneurship, Finance, Training, Rural Entrepreneurs, RSETI."]',NULL,24,1,'1302-1313','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1807','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',71),
(1808,1808,1,'A Study on the Impact of Technology Acceptance on Visitor Participation Behaviour in Sichuan Digital Museums - Based on SEM Structural Equation Modeling Analysis','<p>With the rapid development of digital technology and the in-depth promotion of the "Internet Plus" strategy, digital museums have become an important carrier for the innovative dissemination of traditional cultural heritage. However, how to enhance the interactive experience design of digital museums in order to promote the in-depth participation of tourists is still facing many challenges. Based on the theoretical framework of Technology Acceptance Model (TAM), this study explores the influence mechanism of interaction design on visitors'' participation in digital museums. Taking the key digital museums in Sichuan Province as the research field, 458 valid samples were collected through questionnaires, and structural equation modelling (SEM) was used to verify the relationship between interaction design features, technology acceptance and visitor engagement behaviours. It was found that: (1) the ease of use and functionality of the interaction design had a significant positive effect on visitors'' technology acceptance; (2) technology acceptance had a mediating effect on visitors'' actual participation behaviours through the attitude of use and the willingness to participate; and (3) Sichuan cultural identity played a significant moderating role in the relationship between technology acceptance and participation behaviours. This study enriches the theoretical research in the field of digital museums, and provides empirical evidence for the practice of interaction design in cultural heritage digital display.</p>','["Interaction Design", "Digital Museum", "Visitor Engagement", "Sichuan Culture", "Technological Influence"]',NULL,24,1,'1314-1330','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1808','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',72),
(1809,1809,1,'Social Media Dynamics and Urban Tourism: The Role of Digital Engagement in Driving Cultural Tourism to Chengdu, China','<p>This study takes Chengdu cultural tourism as the research object, and builds an integrated theoretical model on the impact of social media participation on cultural tourism intention based on social cognitive theory and technology acceptance model. This study adopted a quantitative research approach, utilizing 600 valid questionnaires and employing structural equation modeling (SEM) and other statistical analysis methods to systematically explore the mediating role of digital engagement and the moderating effect of demographic characteristics.Empirical analysis found that: (1) Social media participation has a significant positive impact on cultural tourism intention (β=0.435, p&lt;0.001), and this impact is more significant among high-frequency social media user groups ( β= 0.512, p&lt;0.001); (2) Digital participation plays a significant partial mediating role between social media participation and cultural tourism intention. The mediating effect accounts for 30.1% of the total effect, among which the cognitive participation path (13.2 %) has the largest contribution; (3) Educational level has a significant moderating effect on the relationship between social media participation and cultural tourism intention, and the highly educated group shows a stronger influence (β=0.489 vs. β=0.376). The research results reveal the key role of digital marketing strategies in improving the attractiveness of Chengdu''s cultural tourism, which not only enriches the theoretical framework of cultural tourism marketing, but also provides practical guidance for developing precise marketing strategies for cultural tourism destinations.</p>','["Social media engagement , cultural tourism intention , digital engagement , Chengdu tourism , destination marketing , tourist behavior."]',NULL,24,1,'1331-1342','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1809','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',73),
(1826,1826,1,'An Intelligent HG-GWO Framework for Performance Optimization of Grid-Connected Solar–Wind Hybrid Energy Generation','<p>Robust frameworks are needed to make the transition to sustainable energy, that make use of several renewable resources at once. This paper proposes a comprehensive study of a solar-wind hybrid power generation system optimized by Hybrid Grid-Based Grey Wolf Optimization (HG-GWO) algorithm which is integrated in the grid. The proposed architecture is organized by connecting a PV array subsystem, a wind turbine subsystem and power electronic converters to a utility grid, and a bidirectional DC-DC converter is used for seamless energy transfer. The Maximum Power Point Tracking (MPPT) algorithm integrated into HG-GWO framework ensures the PV subsystem always produces maximum output power under different irradiance and partial shading conditions. The controller is also regulating the active and reactive power to maintain the desired operating point of the power factor and provides protection of the grid interface from the disturbances from the external network. Combining Opposition Based Learning (OBL) with HG-GWO further increases the rate of convergence towards the Global Maximum Power Point (GMPP). The simulation results obtained in MATLAB validate the energy efficiency, grid stability and power quality gains over the conventional Perturbation and Observation (P&amp;O) method.</p>','["Renewable Energy, Photovoltaic System, MPPT, HG-GWO, Grid Interface Protection, Hybrid DC Converters, Opposition-Based Learning."]',NULL,24,1,'1343-1358','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1826','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',74),
(1827,1827,1,'Precision Glaucoma Screening via BWO-Guided Instance Segmentation in an SRResNet-RCNN Framework','<p>Glaucoma is still one of the most common, irreversible cause of blindness in the world with the worldwide patient burden estimated to be over 110 million by 2040. Automated and objective screening tools are clinically essential as structural deterioration of the optic nerve is asymptomatic in early disease. In this article a novel three-stage computational pipeline, named BWO-RCNN, is introduced which combines bio-inspired parameters optimization with deep-learning segmentation for high-accuracy glaucoma detection of retinal fundus photographs. In the initial stage, a hybrid pre-processing module using Median Filtering and Contrast Limited Adaptive Histogram Equalization (CLAHE) is used to reduce noise and retain the edge detail that is important for diagnosis. The second stage is to optimise the downstream Region-based Convolutional Neural Network (RCNN) using a Black Widow Optimisation (BWO) algorithm to learn the best hyper-parameter settings. The third stage is that the SRResNet architecture is employed as a backbone for feature extraction, and RCNN is applied for joint instance segmentation of the optic-disc (OD) and the optic-cup (OC). Evaluation of the publicly available Kaggle dataset of 650 fundus images shows that BWO-RCNN has a classification accuracy of 97 %, precision of 98 %, recall of 95 % and an F1-score of 96 %, outperforming the benchmark methods CNN, RF-DNN and FCN in all cases. The results highlight the importance of the synergy between swarm-intelligence optimisation and residual super-resolution networks in the field of clinical image analysis.</p>','["Black Widow Optimisation", "CLAHE", "Glaucoma Screening", "Instance Segmentation", "RCNN", "SRResNet", "Retinal Fundus Imaging."]',NULL,24,1,'1359-1371','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1827','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',75),
(1829,1829,1,'Corporate Credit Rating Solutions - A Combined Study of Quantitative Modeling and S&P Methodology','<p>Credit risk is a type of risk that all commercial banks always face to face, especially with the fierce competition in monetary business sector is increasing. It is therefore essential to build an effective tool to support banks limit risks in commercial lending. Based on the urgency of the actual situation, the paper proposes a credit risk early warning model based on the analysis include both quantitative and qualitative factors. The logistic regression analysis method was used to analyze quantitative factors with 152 observed samples and credit ranking method used to analyze qualitative factors. Moreover, to provide a total solution, the paper presents an mixed method by combined quantitative and qualitative analysis to best support commercial banks in the decision-making process for loans to minimize risks.</p>','["Warning model", "Credit risk", "Ranking model", "Financial coefficients", "Binary regression."]',NULL,24,1,'1372-1385','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1829','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',76),
(1830,1830,1,'Does Geopolitical Conflict Disrupt Bilateral Trade? Evidence from India-China HS-2 Sectoral Trade Dynamics','<p>A geopolitical conflict can typically have a negative impact on international trade, leading to higher levels of uncertainty, reduced economic cooperation, growing regional insecurity and eventually disrupting the global supply chain. But it is an empirical question whether bilateral trade will deteriorate as competition continues. The effects of the border dispute between China and India in Depsang (2013), Doklam (2017) and Galwan (2020) on Sino-Indian bilateral trade is discussed in this paper using HS-2 commodity-level data. The comparative analysis of the sectors is based on macroeconomic indicators and econometric modelling and  bilateral trade dynamics before and after each conflict. Findings show that bilateral trade decreased during periods of geopolitical conflict, but not very substantially. They found that Global Value Chains, HS-2 Sectoral Analysis... bilateral trade shifted instead in terms of the sectors involved, suggesting that geopolitical conflicts mostly shifted the composition of bilateral trade. Production networks and global value chains continue to play a significant role, with technology-intensive industries such as machinery, electrical equipment, organic chemicals and industrial intermediate goods being very resilient. Raw exports, however, were more volatile, especially after the Galwan conflict. Further, this shows the persistence of a widening bilateral trade deficit, thereby deepening India''s structural reliance on Chinese manufacturing and intermediate goods, even amid rising geopolitical tensions. The study offers valuable insights into the literature on geo-economic interdependence, economic interdependence, and trade resilience by showing that geopolitical shocks are more likely to cause sector-level trade reconfiguration than economic decoupling. The results offer key policy relevance for measures to boost domestic production, diversify import supply sources, improve export competitiveness and develop resilient supply chains in the face of strategic uncertainty.</p>','["Geopolitical conflict, Trade assesses changes in export performance, import dependence, trade composition, and Resilience,Economic interdependence,"]',NULL,24,1,'1386 - 1404','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1830','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',77),
(1837,1837,1,'HIRA-DGA: Hierarchical Invariant Representations and Abstention for Cross-Source Power Transformer Fault Diagnosis','<p>High within-dataset accuracy does not establish that a dissolved-gas analysis model will remain valid in an independently sourced transformer population, especially when gas magnitudes, case mix, and label structures differ. This study proposes HIRA-DGA, a Hierarchical Invariant-Representation and Abstention framework, and evaluates it using two public real-data sources: 200 Malaysian development records and 25 Ukrainian cases, of which 19 represented single mechanisms and six were mixed or composite. Five common gases were harmonized. HIRA-DGA separates locally calibrated normal/fault screening from portable fault typing: Stage 0 uses centered log-ratio composition plus total gas magnitude; Stage 1 uses three log gas-ratios for thermal-versus-discharge recognition; Stage 2 uses centered log-ratio composition for discharge severity; and Stage 3 returns HOLD for unsupported label structures. Duplicate-aware 10-by-5 grouped validation, and a one-way, no-target-tuning external challenge were used. Median total gas magnitude differed by 65 to 1,322 times across harmonized classes. With logistic regression, direct four-class absolute-log and ratio models achieved 2/19 and 7/19 external accuracy, whereas HIRA-DGA achieved 18/19 (94.7%; Wilson 95% CI 75.4-99.1%). Stage 2 centered log-ratio features classified 11/11 discharge-severity cases versus 5/11 for its absolute-log ablation. A fixed LightGBM robustness check showed the same architectural direction, improving from 5/19 for direct ratio classification to 13/19 for the hierarchical path. All six mixed cases were assigned HOLD. The contribution is a representation-by-decision technology architecture, not a new classifier: it localizes magnitude-sensitive screening, transfers scale-reduced chemistry and makes abstention an explicit engineering output.</p>','["Abstention", "Dissolved gas analysis", "Domain shift", "Power transformer", "Transferability"]',NULL,24,1,'1405-1421','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1837','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',78),
(1838,1838,1,'Scientific Variables in Media Theories in Light of Technological Developments A Field Study','<p>Modern and advanced communication technologies are among the most important factors that have influenced the field of media research, as they have led to fundamental changes in methods of investigation and analysis. This study aims to explore the impact of technical developments on the quality of media research, and the extent of its compatibility with current media theories. By applying a field study to a sample of experts and professors of communication and media, and collecting their opinions and attitudes about how the research and analysis process changes as a result of benefiting from modern technologies.</p>
INSERT INTO publications (id,submission_id,version,title,abstract,keywords,references_text,issue_id,section_id,pages,doi,copyright_holder,copyright_year,license_url,status,date_published,seq) VALUES
(1848,1848,1,'The Impact of Leadership Training on Nurse Managers’ Effectiveness','<p>Developing leadership and management competencies for nursing managers is critical to the effective leadership of others and driving team and organizational performance. This is a quantitative study aimed to evaluate the impact of a system-wide nursing leadership quality improvement initiative in a network of four public hospitals and one specialized outpatient center in Saudi Arabia. The initiative was designed to enhance nursing middle managers’ leadership and managerial competencies. Secondary Data analysis of a pre- and post-course self-assessment for 105 middle nursing managers who attended a nursing leadership quality improvement training program between November 2022 to March 2024. Following participation in this quality improvement initiative, the paired sample t-test analysis demonstrated a statistically significant difference between the pre- and post-assessments total and individual leadership domains mean scores. Attending well-structured nursing leadership quality improvement programs positively enhances nurse managers’ professional abilities and perception of their management and leadership competencies. Leadership development programs should equip managers with the skills and tools to achieve their professional goals effectively and support their transition to becoming expert nurse leaders. Healthcare institutions’ ethical obligation is to provide them with the necessary resources and training to achieve this goal.</p>','["Nurse Managers, Leadership Development, Healthcare Management, Professional Development, Quality Improvement, Competency Assessment, Leadership Training, Management Skills, Healthcare Leadership."]',NULL,24,1,'1559-1570','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1848','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',85),
(1850,1850,1,'Scheduling Delay Project Risk Management by Integrating Artificial Intelligence Governance','<p>Ongoing scheduling setbacks in intricate oil and gas projects expose fundamental flaws in traditional approaches to project risk management, which frequently lack the predictive insight and governance structures necessary to ensure transparency and accountability in data-informed decision making. This review investigates how the governance of Artificial Intelligence (AI) can be incorporated into project risk management frameworks to improve schedule dependability and address delay-related risks. Through a thematic synthesis of recent research, seven interconnected areas were identified: AI governance and ethics, AI-enhanced risk assessment, risk maturity, delay causes and prevention, integration with project information systems, data transparency, and sustainability.<br/>The results demonstrate how predictive analytics methods, such as fuzzy modeling, Bayesian networks, and reinforcement learning, enhance the early identification of high-impact issues when integrated into governed socio-technical systems. Governance frameworks make fairness, accountability, and explainability possible, which transforms AI from a technical tool into a reliable decision-support system. The suggested integrative framework combines probabilistic and fuzzy models with AI governance concepts to produce an auditable, flexible, and open risk management procedure. This integration improves delay prediction, maximizes mitigation resources, and builds organizational trust in automated insights in oil and gas facilities, where operational unpredictability, regulatory complexity, and safety expectations are high.<br/>The assessment concludes that a major step toward implementing ethical, resilient, and data-driven projects is aligning AI governance with risk management maturity. Incorporating governance standards into analytical workflows strengthens compliance, accountability, and sustainable performance in large-scale infrastructure projects, in addition to improving prediction accuracy.</p>','["Project Scheduling Delay, Risk Management, Artificial Intelligence Governance, Fuzzy Modeling, Bayesian Networks, Reinforcement Learning, Project Risk Maturity, Data Transparency, Ethical AI, Oil and Gas Projects."]',NULL,24,1,'1571-1584','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1850','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',86),
(1851,1851,1,'Effectiveness of Portable Electronic Low Vision Aids for Improving Visual Function in Patients with Visual Impairment in Jordan: A Prospective Crossover Comparative Study','<p>Purpose: To compare the effectiveness of portable electronic low vision aids and conventional optical low vision aids in improving visual performance and patient satisfaction among individuals with irreversible visual impairment undergoing low vision rehabilitation.<br/>Patients and Methods: This prospective crossover comparative study included 74 consecutive patients attending specialized low vision rehabilitation services in Jordan. Following a comprehensive ophthalmic and low vision assessment, each participant was evaluated using both conventional optical low vision aids and portable electronic low vision aids. Outcome measures included near visual acuity (logMAR), contrast sensitivity (log contrast sensitivity units), reading speed (words/minute), reading accuracy (%), patient satisfaction using the Quebec User Evaluation of Satisfaction with Assistive Technology (QUEST-2.0), and device preference. Paired comparisons were performed using paired-samples t-tests. Mean differences, 95% confidence intervals (CIs), and Cohen''s dz effect sizes were calculated.<br/>Results: The mean participant age was 44.5 ± 17.0 years, and 63.5% were female. Portable electronic low vision aids demonstrated significantly better visual and functional outcomes than conventional optical low vision aids across all evaluated measures (p &lt; 0.001). Mean near visual acuity improved by 0.22 logMAR (95% CI, 0.20–0.24; Cohen''s dz = 2.39), contrast sensitivity increased by 0.15 log units (95% CI, 0.13–0.18; Cohen''s dz = 1.41), reading speed improved by 25.8 words/minute (95% CI, 22.45–29.14; Cohen''s dz = 1.79), and reading accuracy increased by 5.77 percentage points (95% CI, 4.95–6.58; Cohen''s dz = 1.64). QUEST-2.0 satisfaction scores were significantly higher with portable electronic low vision aids (mean difference 0.80 points; 95% CI, 0.64–0.96; Cohen''s dz = 1.17). Overall, 67.6% of participants preferred portable electronic low vision aids, whereas 31.1% reported no preference and 1.4% preferred conventional optical low vision aids.<br/>Conclusion: Portable electronic low vision aids provided clinically meaningful improvements in visual function, reading performance, contrast sensitivity, and patient satisfaction compared with conventional optical low vision aids. The high level of patient preference further supports their incorporation into individualized low vision rehabilitation programs as complementary assistive technologies rather than replacements for conventional optical devices.</p>','["Low vision rehabilitation", "portable electronic low vision aids", "digital assistive technology", "visual impairment", "electronic magnification", "reading performance", "contrast sensitivity", "patient satisfaction."]',NULL,24,1,'1585-1613','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1851','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',87),
(1852,1852,1,'Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning Applications Across Core Laboratory Medicine Disciplines: A Systematic Review of Diagnostic Efficiency and Accuracy','<p>Background: Each day the modern clinical laboratory produces millions of data points and humans are struggling to turn them into clinically meaningful data. Artificial intelligence (AI) and machine learning (ML) have therefore emerged as tools to help pathologists and lab scientists with tasks ranging from digital microscopy and automated image analysis to predicting disease outbreaks from molecular data. The tension in the AI field: these tools often achieve superb results in laboratory setting in vitro studies, but struggle to transfer into a clinical workflow and the results cannot be always relied on in real life scenarios and there''s a struggle in terms of readiness for regulators and patient safety. <br/>Methods: We searched in major databases (PubMed, Embase, Scopus, IEEE Xplore) for clinical and analytical studies of AI/ML applied to core lab disciplines that published data on validation and/or deployment between January 2019 and December 2025.<br/>Our outcome measures included: sensitivity, specificity, AUC, turnaround time, error rates and efficiency of clinical workflows. We also extracted regulatory status, external validation data and measures of clinician uptake. We assess risk of bias and summarize findings from studies. Results: Studies included all involving image-based analysis (digital pathology and hematology) and automated morphology in hematology as areas where evidence was the most robust. Development cohort results were excellent, often &gt; 90% for AUC/accuracy, but when translated into clinical settings for external or multicenter validation performance consistently dropped between 15-30%. <br/>This applied even for the most mature applications, whereas in other disciplines like infection diseases and autoimmune serology the evidence for successful real-world deployment was still scarce and mostly anecdotal. Formal regulatory clearance was seldom reported. Conclusions: Image-based diagnostics in hematology and pathology are the most mature application areas for AI in lab medicine, but other fields need more validation before clinical adoption can be encouraged. We recommend mandatory external validation of algorithm performance, prospective clinical trials and rigorous quality control.</p>','["artificial intelligence", "machine learning", "laboratory medicine", "diagnostic accuracy", "systematic review", "digital pathology", "clinical chemistry", "hematology", "microbiology", "immunology."]',NULL,24,1,'1614-1633','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1852','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',88),
(1853,1853,1,'Epidemiology and Detection Features of Bronchial Asthma in the Republic of Karakalpakstan: Analysis for 2020-2025','<p>Background. Bronchial asthma (BA) is among the most prevalent chronic respiratory diseases globally. The Republic of Karakalpakstan (RK) presents a unique epidemiological context due to the Aral Sea ecological disaster, dust storms, and elevated aeroallergen concentrations.<br/>Objective. To analyse epidemiological indicators of BA, primary detection dynamics, hospitalisation structure, and specialist coverage in the RK for 2020–2025.<br/>Methods. Retrospective analysis of official health statistics from 17 administrative districts of the RK for 2020–2025, including primary incidence, hospitalisation rates, bed-days, mortality, and allergologist staffing data.<br/>Results. A total of 2,347 new BA cases were identified over 2020–2024, with 70% diagnosed in adults ≥18 years. The primary incidence rate per 100,000 population increased from 24.5 (2020) to 32.0 (2024). Annual hospitalisations reached 2,293 in 2025 (570 children), with a mean length of stay of 7.2 bed-days. BA mortality increased from 2 deaths (2021) to 8 deaths (2025). Dispensary coverage (88.3/100,000) remains significantly below the national average (119.7). Four of 17 districts lack a certified allergologist.<br/>Conclusions. The RK demonstrates a significant deficit in early BA diagnosis, particularly among children. The Aral Sea ecological factor is a key regional determinant of rising allergic disease burden. Strengthening specialist staffing, expanding dispensary coverage, and implementing GINA protocols at the primary care level are urgently needed.</p>','["bronchial asthma", "allergic rhinitis", "epidemiology", "Republic of Karakalpakstan", "Aral Sea", "early diagnosis", "dispensary surveillance", "GINA", "primary incidence", "specialist staffing."]',NULL,24,1,'1634-1645','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1853','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',89),
(1854,1854,1,'Detection of Sickle Cell Disease Using DenseNet121+ResNet50 Hybrid Deep Learning Model & Grad-CAM++ Explainable Artificial Intelligence Model','<p>Manual microscopic blood smear analysis of Sickle Cell Disease (SCD) clinical diagnosis has continued to pose a bottleneck in the field of hematology because of its manual nature and the level of inter-observer variability. In a bid to make this process more modern, the proposed paper presents a hybrid deep learning model that combines DenseNet121 and ResNet50 to achieve high-precision in the detection of sickle cell disease. The proposed model combines the dense connectivity of DenseNet and the residual skip connectivity of ResNet to achieve an optimal reuse of features and stabilize training to attain a synergistic combination of fine-grained textures and multifaceted spatial patterns. The pipeline used in the methodology is stringent, consisting of careful image pre-processing and large-scale data augmentation and a balanced sampling approach that uses both SMOTE and RandomUnderSampler and is confirmed by K-fold cross-validation. The final results of the experiment indicate that DenseNet121+ResNet50 ensemble has a high accuracy of 96.49, a high precision and specificity, a high sensitivity and F1-score of 97.56. These outcomes are a noteworthy performance improvement compared to the known standards like GoogleNet, ResNet18, and individual ResNet50. Moreover, the study incorporates Explainable AI (XAI) through Grad-CAM++ to deliver visual transparency so that the decision-making process of the classifier is explainable and clinically trustworthy. This strategy provides a more objective, fast, and highly accurate means of SCD diagnosis that will eventually enhance the efficiency of diagnosis and patient care in clinical environments by performing better than the conventional Grad-CAM models.</p>','["Grad-CAM++, Grad-CAM, Explainable AI, Sickle cell disease, Transfer Learning."]',NULL,24,1,'1646-1668','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1854','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',90),
(1855,1855,1,'A Time-Aware Multi-Agentic Framework for Proactive Clinical Trial Risk Monitoring and Adaptive Decision Optimization','<p>Clinical trials generate large volumes of heterogeneous clinical and operational data originating from electronic data capture systems, laboratory information systems, site performance metrics, and monitoring activities. Despite advances in digital trial management, these data streams often remain isolated, resulting in delayed detection of data quality issues, limited operational transparency, and increased reliance on manual review processes, which are insufficient for handling time-aware trial complexity.</p>
INSERT INTO publications (id,submission_id,version,title,abstract,keywords,references_text,issue_id,section_id,pages,doi,copyright_holder,copyright_year,license_url,status,date_published,seq) VALUES
(1910,1910,1,'Smart Manufacturing: Integrating IoT and AI for Learner Operations','<p>Smart manufacturing is transforming industrial production through the integration of the Internet of Things (IoT) and Artificial Intelligence (AI), enabling intelligent decision-making, predictive maintenance, real-time monitoring, and autonomous process optimization. Conventional lean manufacturing techniques primarily rely on human expertise and periodic inspections, limiting their ability to respond dynamically to changing production environments. The convergence of IoT-enabled sensing technologies with AI-driven analytics introduces a new generation of intelligent lean operations capable of minimizing waste, improving productivity, reducing operational costs, and enhancing overall equipment effectiveness (OEE). This paper proposes an integrated AI-IoT framework for smart manufacturing that continuously acquires machine data, performs real-time analytics, predicts equipment failures, optimizes production scheduling, and supports data-driven decision-making. The proposed architecture employs interconnected sensors, edge computing, cloud analytics, machine learning models, and digital dashboards to improve operational efficiency while maintaining product quality and resource sustainability. Performance evaluation demonstrates improvements in production throughput, energy efficiency, machine utilization, defect reduction, predictive maintenance accuracy, and manufacturing flexibility compared with conventional manufacturing systems. The proposed framework provides an intelligent, scalable, and sustainable solution aligned with Industry 4.0 principles and offers practical guidance for implementing AI-enabled lean manufacturing across modern industrial enterprises.</p>','["Smart Manufacturing", "Internet of Things", "Artificial Intelligence", "Lean Manufacturing", "Industry 4.0", "Predictive Maintenance"]',NULL,24,1,'2011 - 2031','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1910','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',110),
(1911,1911,1,'Hybrid Artificial Intelligence and Fuzzy Set Theory for Intelligent Decision-Making in Uncertain and Dynamic Systems','<p>Modern decision-making systems increasingly operate under conditions characterized by incomplete information, linguistic ambiguity, noisy observations, conflicting criteria, and continuously changing environments. Conventional artificial intelligence models provide strong predictive and optimization capabilities but often exhibit limited interpretability and inadequate representation of imprecise knowledge. Fuzzy set theory, in contrast, offers an effective mathematical framework for representing vagueness and gradual membership but may require substantial expert knowledge and can face difficulties in adapting to rapidly evolving data patterns. This paper develops a hybrid artificial intelligence and fuzzy set theory framework for intelligent decision-making in uncertain and dynamic systems. The proposed approach integrates machine learning-based pattern extraction, fuzzy knowledge representation, adaptive rule generation, uncertainty modelling, multi-criteria evaluation, and dynamic decision updating. The framework is designed to combine the predictive capability of AI with the interpretability and uncertainty-handling capability of fuzzy reasoning. Its applicability is considered across complex decision environments involving heterogeneous data, changing system states, conflicting objectives, and human-oriented decision criteria. The proposed framework provides a unified basis for developing adaptive, transparent, and robust intelligent decision-support systems.</p>','["Artificial Intelligence, Fuzzy Set Theory, Intelligent Decision-Making, Uncertainty Modelling, Fuzzy Inference Systems, Dynamic Systems"]',NULL,24,1,'2032 - 2065','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1911','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',111),
(1912,1912,1,'Ensemble Forest-Boosted Classification Framework For Machine Learning-Based Detection Of Fetal Anomalies From Nuchal Translucency Data','<p>In most maternity institutions, an ultrasound scan in the mid-trimester is now considered standard antenatal care. As technology advances and scanning capabilities improve, the discovery of fetal anomalies in scans increases. Foetal anomalies are developmental defects that originate in a foetus during pregnancy. Birth defects and congenital abnormalities are concepts that are linked.  have consistently reported cases of foetal abnormalities. Three out of every 1,000 pregnant women get a fetal abnormality. This work presents an ensemble forest-boosted method for assessing the risk of fetal anomalies by analyzing nuchal translucency (NT). The data was initially retrieved from here. Following that, it can be processed using the colab mean filter. The region of interest can then be delimited using the iterative coarse-grained seed algorithm (ICGSA). The ensemble forest boosted classifier (EFBC) was finally used to detect fetal abnormalities. The findings of this study show that the proposed unique method can accurately classify the anomalies related with nuchal translucency thickening. The analysis includes characteristics including accuracy, recall, precision, and F1-score. The proposed approach achieves an accuracy of 99.642%.</p>','["Fetal abnormality, iterative coarse-grained seed algorithm, ensemble forest boosted classifier."]',NULL,24,1,'2066 - 2080','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1912','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',112),
(1927,1927,1,'An Effective Centralized Admission System Adopted for All Government Universities in Saudi Arabia','<p>Over the last decade, statistics show an exponential rise of enrolment to the Saudi Arabian public universities from around 82,075 in the 1990s to 339,836 in the previous years. This has been made possible by the Saudi government which has been investing a lot of resources in the education sector. The private corporations have also participated in empowering this sector. Secondary graduates are encouraged to further their education to the University level to sharpen their skills for better job opportunities (Murphey, chap. 8, doc.13). There are about 24 public universities in the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia (KSA).</p>
INSERT INTO publications (id,submission_id,version,title,abstract,keywords,references_text,issue_id,section_id,pages,doi,copyright_holder,copyright_year,license_url,status,date_published,seq) VALUES
(1957,1957,1,'SaaS Entitlement Governance: A Reproducible Model for Detecting and Reclaiming Over-Provisioned Access at Enterprise Scale','<p>Software-as-a-Service (SaaS) applications have revolutionized information technology service consumption and delivery, garnering substantial adoption among enterprises. Nevertheless, the ease of onboarding – frequently expediting the process to oblivion – often results in excessive and obsolete entitlement provisioning. While traditional entitlement management practices—implementing governance structures, processes, and technologies—address these issues, the sheer number of applications, along with the lack of central governance bodies, recommends a detection-and-reclamation model instead. Automated SaaS entitlement governance requires only a scalable, enterprise-wide data architecture to record access requests. Such an architecture, combined with entitlement and usage metrics, provides everything necessary to operationalize the model at enterprise scale. When an application is subject to governing controls, risk and compliance teams possess the tooling and information to verify data and inform relevant stakeholders. Specifically, these teams can conduct entitlement overprovisioning checks and escalate as appropriate. Consistent overprovisioning check results can inform reclamation decisions and facilitate periodic maintenance tasks. A reproducible model, rooted in the logic of access entitlement overprovisioning, guides the detection of temporary access entitlement provisioning in SaaS applications with low-volume datasets. When access is granted for a specific task and subsequently revoked, these temporary provisions are suitable for governance board requests. The model can therefore guide the operationalization of entitlement governance within enterprise companies and likewise serve as a reference point for any critical SaaS service.</p>','["Software-as-a-Service (SaaS), Entitlement Governance, Identity and Access Management (IAM), Access Provisioning, Entitlement Reclamation, Access Governance, Compliance Monitoring, Risk Management, Usage Analytics, Enterprise Security."]',NULL,24,1,'2519-2530','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1957','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',135),
(1958,1958,1,'Evolving Policies, Effectiveness, Tools and Factors of Cybersecurity Awareness in Schools in the Context of AI: A Systematic Review, 2015-2025','<p>This study analyzes the critical success factors, tools, and effectiveness of Artificial Intelligence (AI) in cybersecurity awareness within educational institutions, covering the period 2015–2025. The research is based on a systematic review approach, collecting and evaluating empirical and theoretical studies published in international scientific databases. Findings reveal that the adoption of AI in awareness programs relies on technological infrastructure, teachers’ digital literacy, students’ acceptance, and institutional support. Regarding tools, the most prominent are chatbots, recommendation systems, immersive environments, and machine learning algorithms that enable personalized training. In terms of effectiveness, results indicate improvements in knowledge retention, changes in students’ digital habits, and an increased ability to detect threats such as phishing or malware. This article contributes to the literature by providing a synthesis of the advances and challenges in applying AI to educational cybersecurity, highlighting the importance of sustainable strategies that strengthen the digital security culture across schools and universities.</p>','["Artificial Intelligence (AI), Cybersecurity Awareness, Educational Institutions, Critical Success Factors, Machine Learning, Digital Security Culture, Chatbots and Intelligent Systems, Systematic Literature Review."]',NULL,24,1,'2531-2565','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1958','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',136),
(1959,1959,1,'Fair Trade and Small Coffee Producers: Analysis of Productive, Organizational and Sustainability Conditions','<p>International coffee market volatility and persistent asymmetries within the value chain have constrained the competitiveness and economic sustainability of smallholder coffee producers in Mexico. In this context, this study aims to design a management strategy to facilitate the integration of smallholder coffee producers from San Pedro Cafetitlán, Oaxaca, into the Fair Trade system as an alternative marketing approach incorporating social, environmental, and economic dimensions. The study adopts a quantitative approach with a correlational scope. A structured questionnaire was administered to 25 local producers, complemented by a specialized literature review on Fair Trade, sustainability, and coffee value chains. The results show that, although producers offer high-quality coffee and demonstrate willingness to pursue certification, they face limitations related to collective organization, knowledge of Fair Trade, technological development, and technical training. The study’s main academic contribution lies in the formulation of an evidence-based strategic proposal that integrates productive, organizational, and sustainability variables to facilitate the transition toward Fair Trade schemes. This proposal contributes to the literature on sustainable territorial development and inclusive value chains in highly vulnerable rural contexts.</p>','["Fair Trade, sustainable development, management strategy, smallholder coffee producers, territorial development."]',NULL,24,1,'2566-2588','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1959','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',137),
(1961,1961,1,'Typology of Female Entrepreneurship in Panama: A Machine Learning Approach','<p>This study investigates the diversity of female entrepreneurship in Panama using hierarchical cluster analysis, aiming to identify differentiated profiles among women entrepreneurs. Drawing on a survey of 328 participants, the research uncovers four distinct clusters—consolidated, aspirational, vulnerable, and disengaged—based on motivations, self-efficacy, perceived barriers, and business development. The findings challenge the prevailing notion of female entrepreneurs as a homogeneous group and highlight the heterogeneous nature of their trajectories and needs. Consolidated entrepreneurs demonstrate high levels of entrepreneurial identity, resilience, and growth orientation, whereas aspirational entrepreneurs show strong intentions but lack experience and resources. Vulnerable and disengaged profiles, in contrast, are marked by necessity-driven motivations and limited personal or institutional support. The study contributes both theoretically and methodologically by applying a robust multivariate approach to a Latin American context, where empirical research on this topic remains scarce. It also provides practical insights for designing more inclusive and targeted public policies and support programs. By advancing a replicable analytical framework, this research lays the groundwork for future comparative studies and reinforces the need for gender-sensitive entrepreneurship ecosystems in emerging economies.</p>','["Female Entrepreneurship, Panam\\u00e1, Cluster Analysis, Entrepreneurial Segmentation"]',NULL,24,1,'2589-2600','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1961','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',138),
(1962,1962,1,'Social Networks and Body Self-Perception in Adolescent Women in a Peruvian Region-2025','<p>The study “Social Media and Body Image Among Adolescent Girls in a Peruvian Region 2025” aimed to determine the correlation between social media use and body image among adolescent girls. The study was grounded in the positivist paradigm using a quantitative approach, employing a non-experimental, correlational, cross-sectional design. The sample consisted of 718 female students from the region. Data were collected using the Social Media Addiction Questionnaire (ARS) by Escurra and Salas (2014) and the Body Shape Questionnaire (BSQ) by Cooper et al. (1987). The findings showed that 48.3% of the adolescents exhibited a moderate level of social media use, while 48.1% did not report significant body concerns. Furthermore, the joint analysis revealed that 20.6% exhibited a moderate level of social media use and no body concerns. The Spearman’s Rho coefficient obtained (ρ = .200; p = .000 &lt; .05) indicated the existence of a low but statistically significant positive correlation between the two variables. It is concluded that higher levels of social media use are associated with higher levels of body concern, confirming that social media is a factor associated with body self-perception in the studied population.</p>','["Adolescents, body self-perception, female students, social networks."]',NULL,24,1,'2601-2618','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1962','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',139),
(1964,1964,1,'Analysis of Dengue-Related Deaths in Colombia During the 2024 Outbreak','<p>Background: Dengue is a viral disease transmitted by vectors, mainly by Aedes mosquitoes.</p>
INSERT INTO publications (id,submission_id,version,title,abstract,keywords,references_text,issue_id,section_id,pages,doi,copyright_holder,copyright_year,license_url,status,date_published,seq) VALUES
(1388,1388,1,'Getting Metaverse-Ready: The mediating effect of Avatar-Based Experience on Intention to Use Metaverse','<p>Our study aims to explores the impact of Metaverse landscape on customer engagement (CE) and the mediating effect of avatar-based experience (ABE) on intention to use Metaverse (IUM). The study developed a conceptual model, measurement tools, questionnaires, and collected 377 valid responses from Indian users using snowball sampling technique. We utilized SPSS-AMOS to construct a structural equation model (SEM) for data analysis. The results of the study revealed that the Metaverse, including perceived enjoyment, perceived interactivity, and perceived innovation significantly influences CE. ABE in metaverse exhibits a significant mediation effect between CE and IUM. The study highlights the explanatory power of ABE in practical scenarios, highlighting the shift from passive digital consumption to active and immersive user engagements within the Metaverse. This convergence of digital simulation and the Metaverse offers profound insights for academia and industry which will enrich our understanding of avatars'' role within this new digital landscape.</p>','["Metaverse", "Perceived enjoyment", "Perceived interactivity", "Perceived innovation", "Customer engagement", "Avatar experience", "Intention to use metaverse."]',NULL,23,1,'208-227','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1388','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',17),
(1389,1389,1,'AI and Fuzzy Multi-Criteria Decision-Making for Organizational Performance and Strategic Management','<p>Multi-Criteria Decision-Making (MCDM) has become an indispensable analytical approach for supporting organizational performance evaluation and strategic management in increasingly complex and uncertain business environments. Modern organizations operate under multiple, often conflicting objectives involving financial performance, operational efficiency, sustainability, innovation, stakeholder satisfaction, risk mitigation, and competitive positioning. Conventional single-criterion evaluation techniques frequently fail to capture these multidimensional relationships, necessitating structured decision-making frameworks capable of integrating qualitative and quantitative information. MCDM methodologies enable systematic prioritization of strategic alternatives by incorporating diverse evaluation criteria, expert judgments, uncertainty considerations, and organizational objectives into a unified decision-support process. Recent developments integrating hybrid optimization techniques, fuzzy environments, and data-driven analytics have significantly enhanced decision transparency, robustness, and adaptability across industries. This paper investigates the role of contemporary MCDM approaches in improving organizational performance assessment and strategic decision-making by examining methodological developments, practical implementation frameworks, evaluation indicators, and performance outcomes. The study further proposes an integrated conceptual framework that facilitates objective strategic prioritization, efficient resource allocation, and sustainable organizational competitiveness. The findings are expected to provide valuable theoretical insights and practical guidance for researchers, policymakers, and organizational leaders seeking evidence-based strategic management solutions.</p>','["Multi-Criteria Decision-Making (MCDM), Strategic Management, Organizational Performance, Decision Support Systems, Performance Evaluation, Sustainable Competitiveness."]',NULL,23,1,'228-249','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1389','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',18),
(1390,1390,1,'Predictive Analytics and Consumer Buying Behaviour for Personal-Use Four-Wheelers: A Quantitative Study of Eastern Maharashtra','<p>The Indian automobile market has grown increasingly complex as rising incomes, urbanization, and digital information access reshape how consumers evaluate personal-use four-wheelers. This study applies predictive analytics to model consumer buying behaviour in Eastern Maharashtra (Vidarbha region), an under-studied semi-urban market. Using a cross-sectional survey of 384 respondents selected through simple random sampling and Cochran''s (1977) formula, three behavioural constructs — economic value, brand perception, and service assurance — were measured on five-point Likert scales and analysed through exploratory factor analysis (EFA), multiple linear regression, logistic regression, and a decision-tree classifier. The three-factor structure explained 60.2% of common variance (KMO = .80; Bartlett''s χ² = 1315.06, p &lt; .001), and reliability exceeded Nunnally and Bernstein''s (1994) threshold for all constructs (α = .81–.83). The regression model explained 58.2% of variance in purchase-intention scores (R² = .582, F(3, 380) = 176.69, p &lt; .001), with brand perception (β = .428) emerging as the strongest predictor, followed by economic value (β = .391) and service assurance (β = .196). The logistic model classified purchase likelihood with 73.4% accuracy (AUC = .816), outperforming the decision-tree classifier (63.8% accuracy, AUC = .683). Findings indicate that brand-related perceptions have overtaken purely economic considerations as the dominant driver of automobile purchase intention in this regional market, offering manufacturers a validated, data-driven framework for demand forecasting and segmentation.</p>','["predictive analytics, consumer buying behaviour, automobile industry, four-wheelers, logistic regression, Eastern Maharashtra."]',NULL,23,1,'250-262','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1390','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',19),
(1391,1391,1,'Integrated Capacity Planning for AI Data Centers Using Machine Learning and Supply Chain Analytics','<p>With the emergence of artificial intelligence (AI) data centers, capacity planning has become a more complex process, especially with dynamic computational workloads, varying resource needs, the need for scalability, and the growing energy consumption. This paper introduces an integrated capacity planning framework that integrates the capacity planning of AI data centers using machine learning and supply chain analytics to facilitate intelligent and data-driven infrastructure planning for AI data centers. The proposed framework is assessed based on the Google Cluster Data 2019 which includes workload traces from the past, resource utilization information and task scheduling information. A temporal workload forecasting is used by Long Short-Term Memory and Extreme Gradient Boosting is used for predicting future capacity demand. The results of the experiments show that the proposed framework provides an overall capacity planning accuracy of 96.35%, boosts the resource utilization by 24.58%, decreases the capacity provisioning time by 29.84%, reduces energy consumption by 18.76%, lowers the operational cost by 21.93% and enhances the capacity planning efficiency by 25.71% compared to the traditional statistical forecasting and rule-based capacity planning methods. The forecasting accuracy, the use of resources, the efficiency of provisioning, the efficiency of energy usage and the operational cost are used for the comparative analysis, which proves the effectiveness of the proposed framework. This research is novel because it combines the concepts of AI-driven workload forecasting, multi-objective supply chain analytics and integrates them in a single capacity planning framework towards future scalable, intelligent and sustainable decision support solution for next-generation AI data center management.</p>','["AI Data Centers", "Capacity Planning", "Machine Learning", "Long Short-Term Memory", "XGBoost", "Supply Chain Analytics."]',NULL,23,1,'263-280','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1391','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',20),
(1392,1392,1,'An Integrated Model of Financial Advertisement Antecedents, Behavioural Determinants and Financial Literacy Determinants Influencing Investors Intention Towards Exchange Traded Funds','<p>Exchange Traded Funds (ETFs) have emerged as an attractive investment avenue; however, limited research has examined the combined influence of financial advertisement, behavioural determinants and financial literacy on investors intention towards ETFs. This study develops and validates an integrated framework based on the Extended Theory of Planned Behaviour (E-TPB) and the Stimulus–Organism–Response (S–O–R) model to explain ETF investment intention. A quantitative cross-sectional research design was employed and data were collected from 507 retail investors in India. Covariance-Based Structural Equation Modelling (CB-SEM) was used to evaluate the proposed model. The results indicate that the model demonstrates excellent fit and substantial explanatory power, accounting for 86% of the variance in investor intention. Among the significant predictors, attitude, informativeness, truthfulness, perceived behavioural control and social media influence exerted the strongest positive effects, while financial awareness and financial experience also positively influenced investment intention. Conversely, perceived risk negatively affected investor intention, whereas financial skills and saving habit were not significant predictors. The findings highlight that ETF investment intention is shaped by the combined effects of financial communication, behavioural factors, and financial capability. The study extends the behavioural finance literature by integrating E-TPB and the S–O–R framework within a CB-SEM approach and offers practical insights for policymakers, asset management companies and financial institutions to enhance ETF awareness and investor participation.</p>','["Exchange Traded Funds (ETFs), Investor Intention, Financial Advertisement, Behavioural Determinants, Financial Literacy, Covariance-Based Structural Equation Modelling (CB-SEM)."]',NULL,23,1,'281-294','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1392','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',21),
(1393,1393,1,'Image Denoising Using Wavelet Transform and Thresholding Techniques: An Advanced Approach to Improving Image Quality','<p>Image denoising is a vital process in the field of digital image processing, which aims to improve the quality of images by eliminating unwanted components resulting from various noise sources. This article presents an innovative approach based on wavelet transform and thresholding techniques to effectively remove noise while preserving important details and features in the image. In this article, grayscale images are analyzed using wavelet transform, which allows the image to be divided into multiple frequency components. This technique enables the important information to be distinguished from the noise that is often concentrated in the high-frequency components. After the analysis, carefully designed dynamic thresholds are applied to remove noise from the high-frequency components while preserving the main image signals. Finally, the image is reconstructed from the components that have been modified to be free of noise. The implementation was done using MATLAB to achieve high efficiency and accuracy. The results demonstrate that the method offers an excellent balance between noise removal and image detail preservation. This paper highlights the potential of wavelet transform as a powerful tool for image analysis and noise removal. In addition, the use of intelligent thresholding techniques enhances the accuracy of the process, making this approach suitable for applications requiring high-quality images such as medical imaging, computer vision systems, and satellite image processing. This work provides an advanced path to image quality improvement, making it a promising option for modern applications in digital image processing.</p>','["Wavelet Transform, Thresholding Techniques, Image Denoising, Frequency Decomposition, Digital Image Processing."]',NULL,23,1,'295-306','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1393','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',22),
(1395,1395,1,'Exploring the Experiences of Pre-Service Mathematics Teachers Using Artificial Intelligence for LET Preparation','<p>Artificial Intelligence (AI) has increasingly influenced educational practices by providing innovative learning support for students preparing for professional examinations. This study explored the experiences of pre-service Mathematics teachers in utilizing Artificial Intelligence for preparing for the Licensure Examination for Teachers (LET). A qualitative phenomenological research design was employed, involving 12 purposively selected pre-service Mathematics teachers who had used AI during their LET preparation. Data were collected through semi-structured interviews and analyzed using Braun and Clarke’s thematic analysis. Four overarching themes emerged: (1) Artificial Intelligence as an Accessible Learning Companion, (2) Artificial Intelligence as a Personalized Learning Support Tool, (3) Educational Benefits of Artificial Intelligence in LET Preparation, and (4) Responsible and Critical Use of Artificial Intelligence. The findings revealed that AI enhanced conceptual understanding, promoted independent learning, improved review efficiency, and enabled personalized learning experiences. However, participants also emphasized the importance of critically evaluating AI-generated information and integrating AI responsibly with traditional learning resources to support meaningful and ethical examination preparation. The study highlights the potential of Artificial Intelligence to complement LET preparation while underscoring the need to foster digital literacy, critical thinking, and responsible AI use in teacher education.</p>','["Artificial Intelligence, pre-service Mathematics teachers, Licensure Examination for Teachers, qualitative research, teacher education, AI-assisted learning"]',NULL,23,1,'307-316','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1395','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-28',23),
(1396,1396,1,'Non-Contact Vital Signs Estimation Ml and Dl Techniques: A Comprehensive Survey','<p>In the past decade,health care monitoring has emerged as the most promising area of research in the field of medicine.Vital sign signals have rapidly become a dominant factor with the recent advancement of healthcare applications. The Human Vital Signs (HVS), which are utilized for predicting medical and physical health issues earlier, are the essential ones that reveals the actual health status of the patients.Recent research works utilizes techniques like Electroencephalogram (EEG), Photoplethysmography (PPG), wireless sensors, Internet of Things (IoT), Electrocardiogram (ECG), and Remote-PPG (RPPG) However, a prominent role is played by the RPPG signal in HVS estimation. This paper aims to explore contactless HVS monitoring utilizing RPPG signals and also investigate the numerous Machine Learning (ML) and Deep Learning (DL) approaches that are employed for HVS prediction. Likewise, by evaluating certain quality metrics, the performance of the different techniques is validated. Further the survey examines the related models and  the best techniques for non-contact HVS measurement. The key objective of this study is to provide a  detailed insight into the purpose of HVS prediction.</p>','["Contactless Vital Signs Monitoring (CVSM), RPPG, Wireless Sensor Systems (WSS), Machine Learning (ML), Deep Learning (DL), medical field, and Health Care (HC)."]',NULL,23,1,'317-345','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1396','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',24),
(1397,1397,1,'Market Access Liberalization, Accounting Information Quality and Trading-Activity Reallocation in Tadawul: Evidence from the Abolition of the Qualified Foreign Investor Framework','<p>This paper examines whether the Saudi Capital Market Authority''s January 2026 abolition of the Qualified Foreign Investor (QFI) framework coincided with a measurable redistribution of trading activity across listed firms in Tadawul. Using official Saudi Exchange reports, we construct a near-population panel of 267 to 269 firms observed at annual, quarterly, monthly, and daily frequencies spanning 2025 and Q1 2026. The empirical design is framed as event-based evidence around a major market-access reform rather than as a clean natural experiment. We combine cross-sectional OLS, liquidity-sorted portfolio tests, quantile regression, event-window difference-style comparisons, placebo tests, and heterogeneity analyses to trace how activity and returns evolved around the reform window.</p>
INSERT INTO publications (id,submission_id,version,title,abstract,keywords,references_text,issue_id,section_id,pages,doi,copyright_holder,copyright_year,license_url,status,date_published,seq) VALUES
(1423,1423,1,'Adversarial Machine Unlearning for Privacy-Preserving Threat Intelligence Sharing','<p>In the era of collaborative cybersecurity, the sharing of threat intelligence models across organizations has become critical for proactive defence. However, such sharing raises significant privacy concerns, as machine learning models trained on proprietary or sensitive security data can inadvertently leak information through model inversion or membership inference attacks. This paper introduces a novel framework for adversarial machine unlearning to enable privacy-preserving threat intelligence sharing. Our approach integrates unlearning mechanisms with adversarial training to ensure that specific data—such as organization-specific attack traces or internal system logs—can be selectively forgotten from threat detection models without requiring full retraining. We define a robust threat model to evaluate potential privacy leakage post-unlearning and introduce novel metrics for quantifying forgetting efficacy and utility retention. Experiments on benchmark cybersecurity datasets, including network traffic, malware behaviour, and intrusion detection logs, demonstrate that our method effectively mitigates data leakage risks while maintaining high detection performance. This work lays the foundation for secure and compliant knowledge transfer in federated security operations and collaborative defence ecosystems.</p>','["Unlearning, Privacy", "Model", "Federated", "Data", "Accuracy", "Detection", "Adversarial", "Performance", "Compliance."]',NULL,23,1,'685-698','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1423','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',42),
(1426,1426,1,'Adaptive Threat Intelligence Framework for Real-Time Cyberattack Detection Using Behavior-Based Analytics','<p>The rapid growth of interconnected digital infrastructures, cloud computing environments, Internet of Things devices, and enterprise networking systems has significantly increased the frequency, complexity, and sophistication of cyberattacks targeting organizational information assets. Traditional cybersecurity mechanisms based primarily on signature detection and static rule-based monitoring are becoming increasingly ineffective against modern attack strategies such as zero-day exploits, advanced persistent threats, insider attacks, ransomware campaigns, and polymorphic malware. In this context, adaptive threat intelligence frameworks integrated with behavior-based analytics have emerged as a promising approach for enhancing real-time cyberattack detection and proactive security response capabilities. This research investigates the design and implementation of an adaptive threat intelligence framework capable of identifying malicious activities through continuous behavioral analysis, anomaly detection, and dynamic threat assessment techniques. The study focuses on how behavioral analytics can improve cybersecurity resilience by monitoring user activities, network communication patterns, system interactions, application behavior, and endpoint activities to identify deviations from established normal operational baselines. Unlike traditional detection approaches that depend heavily on predefined signatures, behavior-based analytics enables the identification of previously unknown threats and evolving attack vectors through machine learning algorithms, predictive analytics, and intelligent pattern recognition models. The proposed framework integrates adaptive learning mechanisms that continuously update threat intelligence repositories based on real-time attack behaviors, thereby improving detection accuracy and minimizing response delays. The research further examines the role of artificial intelligence, big data analytics, and automated incident response systems in strengthening cyber defense infrastructures across enterprise environments. In addition to operational advantages, the study critically evaluates challenges associated with implementing adaptive threat intelligence systems, including false-positive generation, data privacy concerns, computational complexity, adversarial machine learning attacks, scalability limitations, and integration difficulties within heterogeneous network architectures. The research methodology incorporates quantitative analysis, simulated attack scenarios, case study evaluations, and expert assessments to measure the effectiveness of behavior-based threat detection techniques in identifying malicious activities across dynamic cybersecurity environments. Findings from the study indicate that adaptive threat intelligence frameworks significantly enhance threat visibility, accelerate incident response, reduce detection latency, and improve organizational preparedness against sophisticated cyber threats when compared to conventional security monitoring systems. The research also emphasizes the importance of continuous learning models, human oversight, ethical cybersecurity governance, and secure data management practices to ensure sustainable and reliable implementation of intelligent threat detection systems. The study concludes that behavior-based adaptive cybersecurity frameworks represent a critical advancement in modern cyber defense strategies by enabling organizations to detect, analyze, and respond to emerging cyber threats in real time while maintaining operational continuity, information security, and digital infrastructure resilience in increasingly hostile cyber environments.</p>','["Adaptive Threat Intelligence, Behavior-Based Analytics, Real-Time Cyberattack Detection, Cybersecurity Frameworks, Machine Learning Security Systems"]',NULL,23,1,'699-715','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1426','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',43),
(1427,1427,1,'Drivers of Fashion Leadership in the Digital Age : An Empirical Study of Young Female Consumers in Tunisia','<p><strong>Purpose</strong>: this research aims to examine the relationship between specific individual characteristics of Tunisian adolescent girls (Internet innovativeness and familiarity, fashion involvement and attitude toward fashion sites) and fashion leadership.</p>
INSERT INTO publications (id,submission_id,version,title,abstract,keywords,references_text,issue_id,section_id,pages,doi,copyright_holder,copyright_year,license_url,status,date_published,seq) VALUES
(1490,1490,1,'Artificial Intelligence–Driven Solutions in Environmental Engineering: Advancing Sustainable Urban Air Quality Through PM2.5 Prediction Using Machine Learning','<p>Air pollution prediction is an integral component of environmental engineering, and effective management of air quality demands timely, accurate and explainable air quality forecasting to manage urban pollution. The concept of this research was to use artificial intelligence to predict the PM2.5 level using the required predictors PM10 and NO₂ as well as other pollutant, meteorological, temporal, wind-direction and site-specific variables from the Beijing Multi-Site Air Quality dataset. A quantitative supervised regression approach was followed, covering data preprocessing techniques such as data cleaning, handling of missing values, temporal feature engineering, encoding of categorical variables, selection of models, hyperparameter tuning, cross-validation, residual analysis, error analysis for various categories of pollution, feature importance analysis, and explainability using SHAP. A common data mining pipeline was used to evaluate the performance of five models (Linear Regression, Decision Tree, Random Forest, Gradient Boosting, XGBoost). Random Forest Regressor achieved maximum predictive performance in terms of high accuracy, low validation error, low residual bias and acceptable performance in all the pollution categories. The importance of PM10 was identified by the explainability analysis, while the other environmental factors also provided valuable information. The results indicate that explainable ensemble learning can provide a solid basis for AI-based PM2.5 prediction and assist in air-quality early warning, environmental monitoring, pollution-control and sustainable urban management.</p>','["PM2.5 prediction, artificial intelligence, machine learning, Random Forest, air quality management, sustainable urban development"]',NULL,23,1,'1162-1181','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1490','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',67),
(1491,1491,1,'Visual Stimulus Reconstruction from EEG Signals Using Masked Variational Autoencoders and Stable Diffusion','<p>Electroencephalography (EEG)-based Brain–Computer Interfaces (BCIs) have emerged as a promising paradigm for decoding neural activity and translating brain signals into meaningful outputs. Among the various applications of BCIs, reconstructing or generating visual stimuli from neural signals represents a challenging and rapidly growing research direction. Recent advances in deep generative models, particularly diffusion-based image generation frameworks, have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in synthesizing realistic images from high-level semantic representations. Leveraging these developments, this work presents an EEG-guided image generation framework that combines EEG signal processing, latent representation learning, multimodal embedding alignment, and diffusion-based image synthesis. EEG data were collected from participants exposed to multiple categories of visual stimuli. The recorded signals were preprocessed using band-pass filtering, Artifact Subspace Reconstruction (ASR), Independent Component Analysis (ICA), and average re-referencing to remove physiological and environmental artifacts. A Masked Variational Autoencoder (VAE) with a Vision Transformer backbone was employed to learn robust latent EEG representations while preserving contextual neural information. To bridge the modality gap between EEG signals and visual representations, a CLIP-based embedding alignment strategy was introduced to project EEG features into a semantically meaningful image embedding space. These aligned EEG embeddings were subsequently used to condition a pretrained Stable Diffusion model through cross-attention mechanisms, enabling image synthesis directly from neural activity. Experimental results demonstrate that EEG signals contain sufficient information to recover coarse semantic characteristics of perceived visual stimuli. The proposed framework successfully generates images that preserve category-level visual information and exhibit meaningful correspondence with the observed stimuli. The study highlights the potential of integrating EEG-based neural decoding with modern generative AI models for future applications in assistive communication, cognitive state visualization, neurofeedback systems, and next-generation brain–computer interfaces.</p>','["Electroencephalography (EEG), Brain\\u2013Computer Interface (BCI), Image Generation, Image Reconstruction, Variational Autoencoder (VAE), Stable Diffusion, CLIP, Deep Learning, Neural Decoding"]',NULL,23,1,'1182-1196','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1491','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',68),
(1492,1492,1,'A Digital Twin-Driven IoT Smart Aquaculture System with Explainable AI for Water Quality Prediction, Fish Health Monitoring, and Sustainable Aquaculture Management','<p>Aquaculture supplies more than 52% of global fish consumption yet remains beleaguered by water quality deterioration, epidemic disease outbreaks, feed inefficiency, and escalating environmental impact. Conventional monitoring relies on periodic manual sampling, which provides insufficient temporal resolution to detect rapid deterioration events that unfold within minutes to hours. This paper presents the Digital Twin-Driven IoT Smart Aquaculture System (DT-ISAS), the first operationally validated platform to unify five transformative technologies within a single architecture: a heterogeneous real-time IoT sensor network, a live-synchronised digital twin, an ensemble deep learning prediction engine, multi-modal fish health monitoring, and an integrated Explainable AI (XAI) layer. Seven water quality parameters dissolved oxygen (DO), pH, temperature, turbidity, ammonia, nitrite, and salinity are monitored continuously by 284 underwater sensor nodes across six commercial farms. A cloud-hosted digital twin synchronises with physical pond state at sub-800-millisecond latency, enabling real-time simulation and what-if scenario evaluation. An ensemble of Bidirectional LSTM, LightGBM, and Graph Neural Network models achieves 97.3% prediction accuracy. Multi-modal fish health classification fusing underwater computer vision (YOLOv8 + ResNet-50), behavioural acoustic analysis, and near-infrared spectroscopy achieves 94.8% accuracy with 89.6% early disease detection. SHAP and LIME-based XAI explanations, validated in a user study with 48 practitioners, influenced management decisions in 73.8% of cases. Reinforcement learning-based management optimisation delivers a 32.1% feed waste reduction and 47.8% decline in chemical treatment events. Eighteen-month validation across farms in India, Norway, Thailand, and Brazil confirms statistically significant improvements over eight state-of-the-art baselines. The system advances UN Sustainable Development Goals 2, 13, and 14 through quantified reductions in mortality, energy consumption, and ecological discharge.</p>','["Digital twin", "Internet of Things", "Aquaculture intelligence", "Explainable AI", "SHAP", "LSTM", "Graph neural networks", "Fish health monitoring", "Sustainable aquaculture", "Edge computing", "Precision management"]',NULL,23,1,'1197-1218','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1492','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',69),
(1493,1493,1,'Revisiting Functional and SLA Theories: A Qualitative Study of Theory-Informed Exam-Based Language Assessment','<p>While Functional Linguistics and Second Language Acquisition (SLA) theory continue to occupy an important place in Applied Linguistics, their role in language assessment has received comparatively less attention. This study examines how concepts from these two traditions can be incorporated into university-level examination tasks and how students draw on them when analyzing language data. A qualitative case study was conducted with 20 students enrolled in an Applied Linguistics course. Data were collected from a theory-informed examination and analyzed using deductive thematic analysis. The findings showed that students frequently moved beyond identifying linguistic forms and used theoretical concepts to interpret language and learner performance. Many responses discussed how responsibility is constructed through discourse, how grammatical choices influence agency and accountability, and how learner language can be understood through interlanguage, transfer, and developmental stages. The analysis also revealed differences in the extent to which students were able to connect theoretical concepts to linguistic evidence. The study shows that theory-based assessment can help teachers understand how students apply linguistic knowledge and strengthen the link between theory, assessment, and language learning.</p>','["Applied Linguistics", "Functional Linguistics", "Second Language Acquisition", "Language Assessment", "Theory-Informed Assessment", "Interlanguage", "Discourse Analysis", "Higher Education."]',NULL,23,1,'1219-1240','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1493','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',70),
(1494,1494,1,'Smart Decision-Making Frameworks for SME Internationalization Using Machine Learning, Artificial Intelligence, Communication Strategies, Information Systems, and Engineering Applications','<p>The rapid advancement of digital technologies has fundamentally transformed how small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs) make strategic decisions and expand into international markets. This review synthesizes current evidence on the role of artificial intelligence (AI), machine learning (ML), information systems, engineering applications, Industry 4.0 technologies, and big data analytics in supporting smart decision-making for SME internationalization. The article examines the theoretical foundations of SME internationalization, the evolution of AI-enabled decision support, digital transformation strategies, intelligent manufacturing, supply chain digitalization, and integrated decision-making frameworks that enhance organizational agility and competitiveness. It further evaluates the organizational factors influencing technology adoption, including AI readiness, data quality, knowledge management, and dynamic capabilities, while discussing key implementation challenges related to governance, resource constraints, and technological integration. A comparative synthesis of recent studies highlights how integrated digital capabilities improve strategic planning, market intelligence, operational efficiency, risk management, and innovation performance. The review concludes that smart decision-making frameworks provide SMEs with a comprehensive foundation for sustainable digital transformation and international growth by combining intelligent technologies with organizational capabilities. The findings offer a consolidated perspective for researchers, practitioners, and policymakers seeking to strengthen evidence-based managerial decision-making and enhance the global competitiveness of SMEs.</p>','["Artificial intelligence, Machine learning, Smart decision-making, SME internationalization, Big data analytics"]',NULL,23,1,'1241-1261','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1494','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',71),
(1498,1498,1,'From Access to Usage: Digital Payments, Regulatory Quality, and Financial Inclusion','<p>This study examines the relationship between digital finance and financial inclusion using a multidimensional framework that distinguishes between digital access and digital usage, as well as between financial access and financial usage. Using a cross-country panel dataset for 2014, 2017, and 2021, the analysis employs fixed effects estimation to control for unobserved country heterogeneity. The results indicate that digital usage, proxied by digital payments, is more strongly and consistently associated with both financial access and financial usage than digital access, while digital access has a positive but more limited association, suggesting that the availability of digital infrastructure alone may be insufficient to achieve meaningful financial inclusion. The findings further show that regulatory quality strengthens the contemporaneous association between digital usage and financial inclusion, highlighting the enabling role of institutional conditions in facilitating the adoption of digital financial services. However, this moderating effect does not persist in the lagged specification, suggesting that institutional quality is more important for initial adoption than for sustaining long-term financial behavior. Overall, the study suggests that financial inclusion in the digital era is associated not only with access to digital infrastructure but more fundamentally with the effective use of digital financial services within an enabling institutional environment.</p>','["Digital finance, Financial inclusion, Digital access, Digital usage, Institutional quality."]',NULL,23,1,'1262-1285','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1498','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',72),
(1499,1499,1,'The Expansion of Self-defence in Contemporary International Law: Targeted Killings, Sovereignty, and the Iranian Leaders Strike','<p>The scope of the right to self-defence in contemporary international law needs to be is being re-evaluated, specifically, following the recent attacks on leaders in Iran culminating in the reported execution of Ayatollah Ali Khamenei. These events mark a continuation of the process of transforming how we interpret the law on the use of force as specified in Article 51 of the United Nations Charter and provide further evidence of states increasingly relying on an expanded interpretation of their right to self-defence through anticipatory self-defence, the targeting of state officials, and extraterritorial exercise of force without the permission of the host state. The article employs an approach based on both a doctrinal (traditional) and constructivist socio-legal perspective to assess the evolving meanings attached to essential legal terms such as armed attack, imminent and necessity. The analysis evaluates the manner in which states have become more reliant on targeted killings, particularly with respect to their political leaders, and how doing so erodes the line between legally using self-defence and using force unlawfully, thereby eroding the normative structure of jus ad bellum. The paper concludes that the expansion of self-defence is representative of deeper geopolitical imbalances in the world today and runs the risk of diminishing the strength of the global order as defined by international law.</p>','["Self-defence, Sovereignty, Use of force, Targeted killings, Imminence, International law"]',NULL,23,1,'1286-1300','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1499','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',73),
(1500,1500,1,'The Right to Withdraw the Work from Circulation in the Interactive Digital Novel','<p>This study addresses the right to withdraw an interactive novel from circulation under the literary copyright laws of several countries through a descriptive comparative approach. It examines the issues imposed by rapid technological advancements on the right to withdraw interactive novels from circulation. The study reveals the nature of the right to withdraw digital interactive novels, in addition to the nature and components of interactive novels themselves. It also highlights the legislative shortcomings in the right to withdraw interactive novels amidst rapid technological advancements. In addition, the study discusses the legal issues arising from these technological developments concerning the right to withdraw interactive novels from circulation. This analysis is based on understanding the nature of the right to withdraw interactive novels, the characteristics of interactive novels, and the challenges posed by the fusion of interactive novels and the right to withdraw them due to technological progress</p>','["Interaction", "Right", "Withdrawal", "Novel", "Work", "Technology."]',NULL,23,1,'1301-1312','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1500','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',74),
(1501,1501,1,'Crime, Justice, Administration, and the Transformation of Society in the Digital Era','<p>The rapid advancement of digital and communication technologies—particularly the widespread use of social media—has inspired a growing body of interdisciplinary research seeking to understand how technological innovation reshapes social life, institutional practices, and administrative processes. Rather than viewing technology as separate from society, the concept of the digital society positions digital infrastructures, media, and networks as foundational elements of contemporary social organization. Within this perspective, technological systems are understood as deeply embedded in everyday routines, shaping experiences of crime, patterns of victimization, and the ways justice systems operate and are administered. Building on this understanding, the present study examines how the digital society can serve as a conceptual and analytical framework for rethinking criminological inquiry in the twenty-first century. By drawing on insights from sociology, communication studies, administrative sciences, and cultural theory, we argue for an expanded and more adaptive field of digital criminology—one capable of addressing the increasingly complex intersections between technology, deviance, justice, and administration. Adopting this lens allows scholars to move beyond the conventional boundaries of cybercrime research and engage with the wider social, organizational, and political implications of digital transformation.</p>','["Digital Society, Digital Criminology, Administration, Cybercrime, Cyber Culture, Digital Transformation."]',NULL,23,1,'1313-1322','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1501','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',75),
(1502,1502,1,'An Anomaly-Based Deep Learning Model for Detecting Known and Zero-Day Attacks in Network Traffic','<p>The rapid growth of digital communication, cloud computing, Internet of Things (IoT), software-defined networking, and edge computing has significantly increased the complexity and volume of network traffic, creating new opportunities for sophisticated cyberattacks. Traditional signature-based intrusion detection systems are highly effective against previously identified threats but often fail to recognize emerging zero-day attacks whose behavioral characteristics have not been previously observed. Consequently, anomaly-based deep learning approaches have gained considerable attention because of their capability to automatically learn complex traffic patterns and identify deviations from legitimate network behavior. This study proposes an anomaly-based deep learning model for detecting both known and zero-day attacks in heterogeneous network environments. The proposed framework integrates advanced traffic preprocessing, automated feature extraction, deep neural representation learning, adaptive anomaly scoring, and intelligent attack classification to enhance detection accuracy while minimizing false alarms. The model is designed to capture nonlinear relationships among network traffic attributes, enabling effective identification of sophisticated intrusion attempts that evade conventional security mechanisms. Furthermore, the proposed architecture emphasizes scalability, robustness, and real-time applicability for modern enterprise networks. The anticipated outcomes demonstrate improved detection performance, reduced false positive rates, enhanced generalization capability for unseen attacks, and strengthened network resilience, thereby providing an effective intelligent cybersecurity solution for next-generation network intrusion detection systems.</p>','["Anomaly Detection, Deep Learning, Network Intrusion Detection System, Zero-Day Attacks, Network Traffic Analysis, Cybersecurity"]',NULL,23,1,'1323-1355','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1502','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',76),
(1503,1503,1,'Artificial Intelligence-Assisted Sociolinguistic Analysis of the Representation of the “East” and the “West” in English and Uzbek Media Discourse','<p>The conceptual dichotomy of “East” and “West” continues to shape cultural identities, media narratives, and geopolitical discourse globally. This paper employs advanced artificial intelligence techniques combined with sociolinguistic frameworks to analyze the representations of “East” and “West” within English and Uzbek language media. Using a sizable multilingual corpus extracted from major newspapers, news portals, and broadcast transcripts from 2018–2023, the study applies natural language processing methods—sentiment analysis, topic modeling, and metaphor identification—to uncover semantic patterns, attitudinal orientations, and ideological frameworks embedded in these representations. Results reveal divergent discursive strategies: English media tend to reproduce traditional orientalist dichotomies, while Uzbek media negotiate post-Soviet identity and cultural restoration through a nuanced framing of “East” and “West.” This interdisciplinary study enriches both computational sociolinguistics and cross-cultural media research by demonstrating how AI tools can dissect complex ideology-laden concepts across linguistic contexts.</p>','["Artificial Intelligence, Sociolinguistics, Media Discourse, East-West Representation, Natural Language Processing, English Media, Uzbek Media, Sentiment Analysis, Discourse Analysis"]',NULL,23,1,'1356-1361','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1503','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',77),
(1504,1504,1,'Patient Movement Recognition using Body-Part Adaptive Spatial-Temporal Graph Network (BP-ASTGN)','<p>Automated patient monitoring in Intensive Care Units (ICUs) represents one of the most pressing challenges in modern healthcare delivery. This paper introduces BP-ASTGN (Body-Part Adaptive Spatial-Temporal Graph Network), a novel architecture designed specifically for identifying medically critical patient actions in bed. Our approach rests on four innovations. First, Multi-Scale Body-Part Graph Decomposition (MS-BPGD) partitions the 25-joint skeleton into four anatomically grounded sub-graphs. Second, Cross-Part Attention Fusion (CPAF) employs multi-head self-attention for adaptive feature aggregation across body parts. Third, Temporal Pyramid Attention (TPA) models multi-scale temporal dynamics through hierarchical dilated convolutions. Fourth, Class-Adaptive Focal Loss with Learnable Centroids (CAFL) addresses inherent class imbalance.</p>
INSERT INTO publications (id,submission_id,version,title,abstract,keywords,references_text,issue_id,section_id,pages,doi,copyright_holder,copyright_year,license_url,status,date_published,seq) VALUES
(1539,1539,1,'Modernizing CAD-Based Infrastructure Design Workflows Through Internal Automation, Data Standards, and Process Modelling','<p>To improve the current practices in modernizing CAD workflows within infrastructure projects, there is a need for transitioning from traditional practices characterized by inefficiencies towards intelligent automation and standardization of processes. In this regard, a three-stage framework consisting of intelligent dependency graph generation, optimization of design processes and automation of tasks within processes is proposed in this study. The first stage consists of the creation of the intelligent dependency graph utilizing knowledge graph transformers, which use graph neural networks along with transformer attention. The knowledge graph transformer learns the relationship between various CAD entities, layers, design standards and components of infrastructure to generate an intelligent dependency graph. Subsequently, the Process Mining Evolution Engine makes use of process discovery algorithms to detect inefficiencies, repetitious engineering activities, and approval processes to come up with an optimized workflow. Lastly, the Reinforcement Automation Orchestrator uses reinforcement learning to suggest the appropriate automation, task ordering, and validation timing of tasks within the workflow. As a result, the framework transforms CAD models, design standards, and process records into actionable recommendations. Consequently, the generated optimized infrastructure design workflow has an accuracy of 97.4%.</p>','["Streamlining of CAD processes, Infrastructure Design Automation, Design Knowledge Graph Transformer, Graph Neural Networks, Attention in Transformers, Entities in CAD, Intelligent Dependency Graph, Process Mining Evolution Engine, Workflow Discovery, Bottenneck Detection, Workflow Optimization, Reinforcement Automation Orchestrator, Reinforcement Learning, Sequencing of Tasks, Automation Orchestration, Resource Allocation, Optimized Workflow Model, Integration of CAD files, Compliance with Design Standards, Analysis of Workflow Logs."]',NULL,23,1,'1585-1610','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1539','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',92),
(1540,1540,1,'A Performance Comparison of QUIC vs. TCP for Web Content Delivery','<p>The high rate of web applications and multimedia services has increased the pressure on transport protocols with low latency, high reliability and good performance across various network conditions. Web delivery is based on an ancient protocol known as Transmission Control Protocol (TCP) and has issues of head-of-line blocking, multi-round handshakes, and little flexibility in the current heterogeneous networks. As an answer, Quick UDP Internet Connections (QUIC), formalised as the transport-layer of HTTP/3, incorporates transport security, stream multiplexing and congestion control on top of UDP to avoid these restrictions. In this paper, the performance of QUIC versus TCP used in delivering web content is compared thoroughly over a controlled testbed of an experimental environment. The most crucial variables such as the page load time, end to end latency, throughput, packet loss sensitivity and connection establishment delay are tested in different bandwidth, delay, and loss conditions. The findings indicate that QUIC has been effective in minimizing the handshake latency as well as enhancing page loads, especially in high-latency and lossy networks, because of zero-round-trip connection setup and stream-level loss-recovery. TCP, though, is stable throughput in case of stable wires as well as has the advantage of established congestion control implementations. The analysis also illustrates the effect of protocol tuning as well as browser implementation on measured performance.</p>','["QUIC", "TCP", "HTTP/3", "Web Content Delivery", "Latency Analysis", "Transport Protocols."]',NULL,23,1,'1611-1622','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1540','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',93),
(1542,1542,1,'Analyzing the Impact of Bufferbloat on Low-Latency Internet Traffic','<p>Bufferbloat has become a severe degradation to performance in contemporary IP networks, especially to low-latency Internet traffic that has strong delay and jitter requirements. Over buffering in network equipments which initially is supposed to eliminate the loss of packets and enhance the throughput, tend to create continuously long queues, and consequently cause swelling end-to end latency and subpar Quality-of-Experience (QoE). In this paper, the author examines the effects of bufferbloat on the application performance of cars relying on latency sensitive applications: Voice over IP (VoIP), online gaming, augmented and virtual reality (AR/VR) and real-time teleoperation. We initial discuss the underlying causes of bufferbloat, namely buffer size, conservative drop policy and the interaction between transport layer congestion control and transport layer congestion control. The paper subsequently defines low-latency traffic needs and points out that it is susceptible to queueing and jitter delays, as well as bursty packet loss. A controlled testbed based on access-network conditions with mixed traffic loads is used as an experimental evaluation. The drop-tail buffering is contrasted with Active Queue Management (AQM) methods to measure their impacts in terms of latency, throughput, fairness and coexistence with the legacy TCP flows. Findings show that uncontrolled buffers have a significant negative effect on the performance of low-latency traffic in congestion but that AQM mechanisms can be carefully tuned to achieve a substantial reduction in queueing delay without seriously impacting throughput. Lastly, the paper explains the open issues regarding bufferbloat in wireless networks and mobile networks, new applications based on ultra-low-latency, and the correct measurement and detection of operational networks. The results offer empirical evidence to the network designers and researchers whose goal is to develop latency-sensitive Internet infrastructures.</p>','["Bufferbloat, Low-latency traffic, Queueing delay, Active Queue Management, Quality of Experience."]',NULL,23,1,'1623-1634','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1542','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',94),
(1543,1543,1,'Designing Resilient Overlay Networks for Peer-to-Peer Applications','<p>Peer-to-peer (P2P) overlay networks are the basis of numerous large-scale distributed applications since they provide the ability to share resources, distribute content, and offer collaborative services without a central authority. Nonetheless, due to the openness and dynamism nature of P2P systems, they are very susceptible to failures, node churning, network partitions and maliciousness. Creation of robust overlay network is hence critical in ensuring reliability, availability and performance during unfavorable conditions. In this paper, the design principles, threat models and protocol mechanisms necessary to create fault-tolerant and secure P2P overlays are investigated. It initially examines the main properties of structured and unstructured overlay networks with emphasis made to the topology construction of the networks, their maintenance mechanisms, and their routing mechanism. This paper will then examine some of the most critical failure and attack models such as dynamic membership changes, link failures, Byzantine nodes and coordinated attacks, which worsen overlay performance. Based on this discussion, the paper provides resilient protocol architecture design of join, leave, and reconfiguration procedures, and fault-tolerant routing and look-up algorithms, including Chord successor list routing, k-redundant path routing, and self-healing overlay routing. The issue of security conscious resilience mechanisms is also discussed to reduce malicious behavior without compromising scalability. As an analytical discussion and comparative assessment results in, the findings indicate that redundancy, adaptability and self-healing properties can enhance overlay robustness without incurring too much overhead. At the end of the paper, design trade-offs and future directions of resilient P2P overlay networks in new decentralized applications have been highlighted.</p>','["Peer-to-peer networks, overlay networks, fault tolerance, resilience, self-healing routing, network security."]',NULL,23,1,'1635-1646','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1543','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',95),
(1545,1545,1,'The Impact of Knowledge Management Governance as an Approach to Enhancing Organizational Effectiveness: An Exploratory Analytical Study of Managers'' Perspectives in the Greater Amman Municipality','<p>This study aims to examine the potential impact of knowledge governance as a regulatory mechanism through which organizations can enhance their organizational effectiveness. Three key variables interact to form the conceptual framework for this study: knowledge governance mechanisms, knowledge management processes, and organizational effectiveness. The study emerged from a well-defined problem, developed through a preliminary investigation to identify the challenges facing the Greater Amman Municipality (GAM). It sought to explore the views of a sample group regarding the applicability of knowledge governance as a management approach to addressing operational disruptions and performance inefficiencies. The study sample included 39 administrative managers, and data were collected using a structured questionnaire. Using a set of statistical tools, the study concluded that the main and sub-null hypotheses were statistically rejected, indicating a significant relationship between the study variables. Among the key recommendations was the need to build on this research with further studies, starting with the development of a detailed plan for institutionalizing knowledge governance within the GAM, ensuring its continued development and implementation as a practical regulatory mechanism to address existing challenges.</p>','["Knowledge Governance, Effectiveness, Knowledge Management, Public Sector Management, Greater Amman Municipality."]',NULL,23,1,'1647-1669','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1545','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',96),
(1546,1546,1,'Explaining IPO Stock Return Volatility: The Moderating Role of Warrants in Indonesian IPO Firms','<p>Purpose, This study aims to analyze the effects of firm value, free float, and risk-taking on IPO stock return volatility, and to examine the moderating role of warrants in companies undertaking an Initial Public Offering (IPO).</p>
INSERT INTO publications (id,submission_id,version,title,abstract,keywords,references_text,issue_id,section_id,pages,doi,copyright_holder,copyright_year,license_url,status,date_published,seq) VALUES
(1591,1591,1,'A Hybrid Machine Learning Framework with Metaheuristic Feature Selection for Robust Breast Cancer Detection','<p>Breast cancer is one of the most prevalent malignancies worldwide, posing a significant health threat, particularly to women. Early detection is crucial for improving survival rates, yet conventional diagnostic methods often suffer from inaccuracies and delays. Advances in artificial intelligence and machine learning have introduced promising solutions for early and precise detection. This study proposes an advanced multimodal machine learning system integrating radiological and genomic data for improved breast cancer detection and prognosis. The Wisconsin Diagnostic Breast Cancer (WDBC) dataset, comprising 569 samples with 30 numerical features representing tumor characteristics, is utilized for training and evaluation. A novel real-time hybrid machine learning approach combining Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) and Logistic Regression (LR) is developed to enhance classification accuracy while ensuring model stability and clinical applicability. Feature extraction techniques, including Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO) and Genetic Algorithm (GA), are employed to select the most relevant attributes, optimizing predictive performance and reducing computational complexity. Comparative analysis highlights the superiority of machine learning over traditional diagnostic methods. The ensemble model achieves an accuracy of 96.49%, recall of 99%, and precision of 98%, demonstrating its robustness in real-world applications.</p>','["Breast Cancer Detection, Machine Learning, Feature Extraction, Ensemble Model, Early Diagnosis."]',NULL,23,1,'2026-2047','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1591','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',117),
(1592,1592,1,'Teachers'' Awareness and Utilization of Educational Technology Tools: Implications for Effective Teaching in Contemporary Classrooms','<p>Bringing technological tools into schools has been a huge change in the way teachers have taught and the way students have learned these days, but the role of teachers in these new technologies and how mindfully they use them have been dramatically altered. Today, our study’s goal was to get to the bottom of what influences teachers’ thoughtfulness and use of classroom technologies. We employed the technology adoption model (TAM) to lay a groundwork for understanding how the belief-system in teachers has influenced their attitudes regarding and acceptance of technology in classrooms. By   comparing the average scores of educational technology usage, we learned more about how teachers are integrating it into practice. In general, the aspects considered tended to score with closely related average values. Noted were exceptionally high scores of an assumed trait-perceived value and, perceived usefulness. The average of presumed use for the given topics was 4.58; there was a low degree of spread on this average, with the standard deviation being only 0.5. This means instructors very strongly perceive the use of these technologies would be of significance to the subject matter concerned. Among other averages noted, there was a presumed ease-of-use average, with a figure of 4.22 and standard deviation of 0.69, suggesting general favorability on these subjects to being relatively uncomplicated to use. The intentions toward actual use gained a high score with the median standing at 4.39. With a standard deviation of 0.57, this means the intent to use these technologies for some time will remain considerably high. Those deemed "external variables"-which is any attribute found not relevant for direct study-received a respectable mean of 3.65 (or standard deviation of 0.72); scores were all good, but none was incredibly high.</p>
INSERT INTO publications (id,submission_id,version,title,abstract,keywords,references_text,issue_id,section_id,pages,doi,copyright_holder,copyright_year,license_url,status,date_published,seq) VALUES
(1455,1455,1,'Intelligent Stock Market Recommender Systems: A Review of Predictive Models, LLMs, and Decision Frameworks','<p>The evolution of approaches to predicting trends in financial markets has involved several stages, starting from autoregressive statistics, going further to deep sequences, graphs, fusion of sentiment with LLMs, and finally arriving at large language model (LLM) reasoning layers. One area where there has been a lack is the gap between prediction and recommendation, however, because features such as transaction costs, diversification, and investor risk profile are not taken into consideration, a prediction model may indicate a reasonable directionality for held-out samples but be uneconomical to apply when including those factors. This paper presents a survey of the current state of the art for financial trend prediction for the 2025–2026 horizon and eight methodological families, including the classical machine learning methods, recurrent sequences, attention and transformers, graph neural networks, sentiment and LLM-based fusion, explainable AI, reinforcement-learning-based trading agents, and recommender-system-specific approaches. The review shows that predictive accuracy gains have become incremental with respect to the complexity of the architecture needed to achieve them and that the evaluation methodology is now only just catching up with what is important economically and that the number of systems for which the explicit intent is that of a recommendation is still a minority compared to those that are only forecast. In this space, we motivate a design framework for a stock recommender system where prediction is not an end goal, but is one of many inputs, and we outline the open challenges which such a system must still address before it can be trusted with real money.</p>','["stock market prediction", "recommender systems", "explainable AI", "sentiment analysis", "financial technology", "."]',NULL,22,1,'401-414','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1455','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',22),
(1456,1456,1,'The Role of VIX In Neural Network–Based Short-Horizon Volatility Forecasting: Evidence from Global Equity Markets','<p>Volatility forecasts play an important role in financial markets. They are used for setting position limits, determining margin requirements, and guiding short-term trading decisions. Financial volatility is known to exhibit persistence and regime dependence, which makes short-horizon forecasting challenging. In addition to historical volatility measures, implied volatility indicators such as the Volatility Index (VIX) are often used as forward-looking proxies for market risk expectations. However, it is not clear whether the predictive usefulness of the VIX is consistent across different neural network architectures. This study investigates the role of the VIX in one-day-ahead realized volatility forecasting and examines whether its contribution varies across alternative neural network models. The empirical analysis focuses on major global equity markets, including the S&amp;P 500, DAX, FTSE 100, Nikkei 225, and Hang Seng Index. Using market data from these indices, the study evaluates the forecasting performance of artificial neural networks (ANN), long short-term memory networks (LSTM), and gated recurrent unit (GRU) models. All models are estimated within a rolling out-of-sample framework and are tested with and without the VIX variable. Forecast accuracy is evaluated using Mean Squared Error (MSE), Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE), Quasi-Likelihood (QLIKE) loss functions, and Diebold–Mariano tests. The results show that memory-based models outperform feedforward ANN models, while the contribution of the VIX varies across models and markets.</p>','["Volatility forecasting", "VIX", "Neural networks", "Rolling out-of-sample evaluation", "Global equity markets."]',NULL,22,1,'415-432','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1456','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',23),
(1572,1572,1,'Adaptive Reliability-Guided Multimodal Learning Framework for Robust Emotion and Sentiment Classification Under Noisy and Missing Modalities','<p>Intelligent human–computer interaction, healthcare monitoring, social media analytics, and conversational AI rely on emotion recognition and sentiment analysis. However, multimodal learning systems frequently presume equal dependability between text, audio, and visual modalities, rendering them susceptible to environmental noise, missing information, and modality-specific errors. An Adaptive Reliability-Guided Multimodal Artificial Intelligence (ARGMAI) system for robust multimodal emotion and sentiment classification addresses these problems. In a single end-to-end architecture, reliability-aware preprocessing, adaptive cross-modal attention, dynamic feature fusion, contrastive representation learning, noise suppression, and cross-modal consistency optimisation are combined. Missing modality reconstruction allows the framework to preserve discriminative representations when one or more modalities are lacking. In model is tested using the public CMU-MOSEI and IEMOCAP datasets. Experimental results show that ARGMAI achieves 90.36% accuracy, 90.04% precision, 89.78% recall, 89.91% F1-score, and 96.08% AUC on the CMU-MOSEI dataset and 88.14% accuracy, 87.88% precision, 87.53% recall, 87.70% F1-score, and 88.41% weighted accuracy on the IEMOCAP The approach outperforms existing state-of-the-art multimodal learning algorithms under noisy and missing modality situations and provides consistent convergence and enhanced generalisation for real-world affective computing applications.</p>','["Multimodal Emotion Recognition, Sentiment Analysis, Reliability-Guided Learning, Cross-Modal Attention, Missing Modality Reconstruction, Contrastive Representation Learning and Artificial Intelligence."]',NULL,22,1,'433-460','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1572','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',24),
(1613,1613,1,'Cog Test-Green: An Artificial Intelligence-Based Cognitive Decision Support Framework for Resource-Optimized Sustainable Software Testing in Small and Medium-Sized Enterprises','<p>Software testing is vital to the software development lifecycle, but conventional methods run lengthy test suites without addressing computing efficiency, resource optimisation, or environmental sustainability. SMEs'' limited testing resources, infrastructure, and operational budgets make these limits particularly difficult. CogTest-Green, an AI-based Cognitive Decision Support Framework, uses intelligent test case prioritisation, adaptive workload modelling, multi-objective sustainable optimisation, and continuous feedback learning to improve software testing efficiency while reducing energy consumption and carbon emissions. Heterogeneous computer vision and natural language processing software applications are evaluated using CIFAR-10 and AG News benchmark datasets. Cognitive decision support prioritises test cases based on software complexity, enterprise resources, fault prediction, and sustainability. The suggested framework achieves 98.27% test coverage, 97.42% fault detection rate, 72.81% test suite reduction, and 98.63% APFD in 31.56 minutes, according to experiments. The framework achieves 96.45% resource efficiency, 223.57 Wh energy consumption, 0.112 kgCO₂e carbon emissions, 98.84% recommendation accuracy, and a TDQI of 0.987. These findings demonstrate that CogTest-Green enhances software quality, computational efficiency, and environmental sustainability, making it an ideal intelligent testing option for resource-constrained SMEs.</p>','["Sustainable Software Testing, Artificial Intelligence, Cognitive Decision Support System, Test Case Prioritization, Green Software Engineering, Resource Optimization and Small and Medium-Sized Enterprises (SMEs)."]',NULL,22,1,'461-488','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1613','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',25),
(1718,1718,1,'Impact of VIDA electronic medical record on teamwork among Peri operative nursing in Saudi Arabia','<p>Background: The perioperative setting demands high levels of teamwork to ensure patient safety, yet communication breakdowns contribute to over 70% of sentinel events. Traditional paper-based records introduce inefficiencies, such as outdated information and documentation errors, particularly in resource-constrained environments like Jazan Health Cluster, Saudi Arabia. Electronic medical records (EMRs) offer potential solutions by enabling real-time data sharing, but evidence on mobile EMRs in perioperative nursing is limited. This study evaluated the Vida Mobile Software Application, a novel EMR tool, for its impact on teamwork perceptions and patient safety culture.</p>
INSERT INTO publications (id,submission_id,version,title,abstract,keywords,references_text,issue_id,section_id,pages,doi,copyright_holder,copyright_year,license_url,status,date_published,seq) VALUES
(1741,1741,1,'An Explainable Heterogeneous Stacking Ensemble Framework for Fish Health Prediction in Intelligent Aquaculture','<p>Aquaculture is a vital element of global food security and economic sustainability. The changing environment from time to time and the rapid spread of diseases in aquaculture leading to huge economic losses, still pose a major challenge in the maintenance of healthy fish. Timely intervention for rapid and accurate fish health forecasting and diagnosis will decrease the fish mortality and increase the efficiency of fish production. In the current paper, a stacking model using heterogenous ensemble learning will be developed using three machine learning algorithms including the Random Forest algorithm, Support Vector Machine (SVM) and XGBoost together with Logistic Regression as the meta-classifier to predict fish health. Data preprocessing stage should be conducted prior to developing a model which will include missing value treatment, feature scaling, feature encoding and irrelevant feature elimination. The efficiency and generality of the proposed model will be estimated via using stratified 10-fold cross-validation technique. The quality of the model will be estimated by using the criteria including accuracy, precision, recall, F1 Score, confusion matrix and ROC curve analysis. In addition, SHapley Additive exPlanations will be applied to increase the explainability of the proposed model by showing the contribution of each environmental and biological factor into predicting fish health. The experimental results reveal that the heterogeneous stacking model can outperform the individual base learners and provide a transparent decision explanation. The framework is proposed to be utilized for intelligent fish health monitoring and contributing to sustainable aquaculture through predicting diseases in time and managing the aquaculture farms effectively, interpretable and scalable way.</p>','["Fish health, Aquaculture, SHAP (Shapley Additive exPlanations), Heterogeneous stacking model"]',NULL,22,1,'920-928','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1741','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',47),
(1742,1742,1,'Developing Students'' Intrapreneurial Mindset Through the Integration of Entrepreneurship Education in Mathematics Learning at Junior High Schools in West Aceh','<p>This study aims to describe efforts to foster students'' intrapreneurial spirit through entrepreneurship material in mathematics learning in junior high schools. The study used a qualitative approach with descriptive methods. Data were collected through observation, interviews, and documentation, then analyzed using data reduction, data presentation, and conclusion drawing techniques. The results showed that the integration of entrepreneurship material in mathematics learning can foster students'' intrapreneurial spirit, which is characterized by increased creative thinking skills, critical thinking, problem solving, decision-making, as well as an independent and responsible attitude. In addition, learning becomes more meaningful because students can relate mathematical concepts to real situations related to entrepreneurial activities. Thus, entrepreneurship material in mathematics learning can be one strategy to develop students'' intrapreneurial spirit in junior high schools.</p>','["intrapreneurial spirit, entrepreneurship, mathematics learning."]',NULL,22,1,'929-934','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1742','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',48),
(1744,1744,1,'Transformational Leadership and Organizational Resilience in the Era of Industry 5.0: The Mediating Role of Knowledge Management Capability and Employee Innovative Behavior','<p>The transition toward Industry 5.0 has fundamentally reshaped organizational priorities by emphasizing human-centricity, sustainability, resilience, and intelligent collaboration between advanced technologies and human capabilities. Within this context, transformational leadership has emerged as a strategic driver of organizational adaptability and long-term competitiveness. This study investigates the direct and indirect effects of transformational leadership on organizational resilience by examining the mediating roles of knowledge management capability and employee innovative behavior. A quantitative research design was employed using a survey of employees from organizations undergoing digital transformation across multiple industrial sectors. Data were analyzed using Partial Least Squares Structural Equation Modeling (PLS-SEM) to evaluate both measurement and structural models. The findings reveal that transformational leadership significantly enhances knowledge management capability, stimulates employee innovative behavior, and directly strengthens organizational resilience. Knowledge management capability also exerts a significant positive influence on employee innovative behavior and organizational resilience, confirming its strategic role in facilitating organizational learning and adaptive capacity. Furthermore, employee innovative behavior significantly contributes to organizational resilience by enabling continuous improvement, creative problem-solving, and organizational flexibility. Mediation analysis demonstrates that knowledge management capability and employee innovative behavior partially mediate the relationship between transformational leadership and organizational resilience, indicating that leadership effectiveness is reinforced through organizational knowledge processes and innovation-oriented employee behavior. These findings contribute to the growing Industry 5.0 literature by integrating leadership, knowledge management, innovation, and resilience into a comprehensive conceptual framework. The study offers practical implications for managers seeking to develop resilient organizations by fostering transformational leadership, strengthening knowledge management systems, and encouraging employee innovation to achieve sustainable organizational performance in increasingly dynamic and digitally driven business environments.</p>','["Transformational Leadership", "Knowledge Management Capability", "Employee Innovative Behavior", "Organizational Resilience."]',NULL,22,1,'935-956','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1744','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',49),
(1745,1745,1,'Intelligent Multi-Objective Optimization for UAV Mission Decision Support','<p>Solar-powered Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs) and High-Altitude Pseudo-Satellites (HAPS) offer significant potential for persistent intelligence, surveillance and reconnaissance operations, but their endurance remains constrained by variable solar irradiance, atmospheric turbulence, battery limitations and payload power demand. This review examines the role of Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Machine Learning (ML) in improving UAV decision-support optimization through predictive solar forecasting, reinforcement-learning-based flight-path optimization, adaptive Maximum Power Point Tracking (MPPT), battery state estimation and intelligent payload load balancing. The paper synthesizes current approaches using Long Short-Term Memory networks, gradient-boosting models, convolutional neural networks, graph neural networks and reinforcement learning architectures for autonomous energy-aware flight. Simulation-based analyses suggest that AI-assisted control can improve energy utilization by 15-25% and extend mission persistence by 30-40%, particularly under variable irradiance and wind conditions. However, practical deployment requires robust validation, certifiable AI architectures, adversarial resilience and reliable edge-computing implementation. The strategic deployment of AI-enabled autonomous UAVs directly supports Saudi Arabia''s Vision 2030 objectives for indigenous defence technology development, artificial intelligence research, and advanced aerospace systems. By establishing domestic expertise in AI-driven decision-support optimization, the Kingdom advances its defence industrial sovereignty while creating high-value technical employment in autonomous systems engineering and aerospace artificial intelligence.</p>','["Artificial Intelligence", "UAV Energy Management", "Machine Learning", "Solar-Powered UAV", "Reinforcement Learning", "Maximum Power Point Tracking", "Stratospheric Flight", "Predictive Algorithms", "Autonomous Systems", "Defence Technology"]',NULL,22,1,'957-980','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1745','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',50),
(1746,1746,1,'Establishment of New Flower Seed Images Dataset and Classification Via AlexNet Feature-Level Fusion Approach','<p>Flower Seed Images for Indian flower species recognition is established and shared to the public (Mendeley Data: sethy, PRABIRA (2026), “Flower Seed Images”, Mendeley Data, V2, doi: 10.17632/7ywfg2nxrf.1). It comprises curated color images of flowers categorized into hierarchical classes, intended for use in supervised learning and reproducible research. We establish a baseline transfer-learning experiment on Flowers Dataset that nears perfect accuracy (validation 99.94%, test 99.87%), showing that the dataset is useful for training and testing computer-vision models. Public release of the dataset and baseline scores should help spur further research into automated flower-identification and biodiversity informatics in general.</p>','["transfer learning, AlexNet, deep learning, image classification, Flower Seed."]',NULL,22,1,'981-986','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1746','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',51),
(1747,1747,1,'A Perspective of Sustainable Circular Economy through Technology Integration','<p>Traditional industrial manufacturing remains constrained by linear "take-make-consume-dispose" workflows, generating resource depletion, price volatility, and massive material waste. While transitioning to a sustainable circular economy through closed-loop supply chains offers a restorative solution, implementation introduces severe hurdles, including high supply uncertainty, a multi-tier trust deficit, and multi-criteria logistics coordination challenges. To resolve these vulnerabilities, this paper proposes a vertically integrated, multi-tier digital framework that orchestrates Industry 4.0 technologies into an infrastructure loop</p>','["Circular Economy", "Reverse Logistics", "Generative Artificial Intelligence", "Digital Closed-Loop Supply Chain."]',NULL,22,1,'987-995','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1747','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',52),
(1748,1748,1,'Requirement Prioritization Using Chi-Square Test of Independence','<p>In software projects, requirements engineering is considered one of the most important phases of the system development life cycle. Since these requirements primarily depend on stakeholders’ needs and constraints, requirements prioritization aims to determine the order in which requirements should be implemented. In this paper, we leverage the chi-square test, a statistical test of independence, to assess the dependency between variables, and we apply chi-square scores in the ranking process to assign a targeted weight to each requirement. The most important contributions are the novel statistical prioritization framework, which can be used to objectively weight the features of the requirement with a chi-square analysis, and the reduction of the subjectivity inherent in traditional methods that are stakeholder-dependent.</p>','["software engineering, requirements engineering, requirement prioritization, chi-squared test."]',NULL,22,1,'996-1014','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1748','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',53),
(1749,1749,1,'A Novel Adaptive Xgboost-Based Deep Neural Network for Agricultural Yield Forecasting','<p>The prediction of crop yield is a very important component in smart agriculture to enable farmers and policy makers to optimize the use of resources, improve food production planning, and enhance agricultural sustainability. Yet, conventional machine learning models are unable to capture the intricate nature of the nonlinear relationships that exist between climatic, soil, and cultivation factors and are important to crop productivity. To solve this problem, this paper introduces an Adaptive XGBoost-Guided Deep Neural Network (AXG-DNN) model to improve the crop yield prediction performance. The proposed framework combines the capability of feature selection of Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost) and the nonlinear learning power of Deep Neural Networks (DNN). Agriculture data with climatic, soil and crop management parameters is pre-processed and normalized first. Then XGBoost is used to assess the importance of the features and find the most significant features impacting the yield of the crops. After selecting these features, the selected features are fed to a multi-layer DNN optimized using the Adam algorithm for yield prediction. The proposed model can prune away unnecessary features and keep significant features, thus decreasing computational complexity and enhancing prediction results. The results of the experiments show that the proposed model is more accurate in prediction, robust and generalized as compared to other machine learning and deep learning models such as Multiple Linear Regression (MLR), Artificial Neural Network (ANN), Support vector regression (SVR), K-Nearest Neighbour (KNN) and Random Forest (RF). The proposed approach would enable scalable and efficient precision agriculture, enabling intelligent decision-making and sustainable crop management.</p>','["Crop Yield Prediction", "Deep Neural Network", "Machine Learning", "XGBoost", "Precision Agriculture"]',NULL,22,1,'1015-1030','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1749','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',54),
(1750,1750,1,'An Empirical Study on Consumer Interaction and Emotional Response in Digital Marketing Contexts','<p>Digital marketing environments increasingly rely on interactive, personalized, and content-rich interfaces that shape consumers’ emotional experiences and subsequent behavioural responses. Although prior studies highlight the importance of consumer engagement in online contexts, limited empirical work integrates interaction attributes, emotional processing, and behavioural intention within a unified structural framework. This study investigates how three core digital interaction features—interactivity, personalization, and content quality-shape emotional responses and influence purchase intention. Using survey data from 412 active digital media users, we employed covariance-based structural equation modelling (AMOS 24) and variance-based modelling (SmartPLS-4) to test the robustness of the proposed model. Results demonstrate that interactivity, personalization, and content quality significantly enhance positive emotional responses, which in turn exert a strong influence on purchase intention. Mediation analyses confirm that emotional response partially mediates the relationships between interaction attributes and behavioural intention. Additional multi-group and interaction analyses reveal moderation effects of gender and age, indicating demographic heterogeneity in emotional and behavioural pathways. The findings advance understanding of emotion-driven digital behaviour and offer data-driven insights for designing emotionally resonant digital marketing strategies.</p>','["Digital consumer behaviour", "Emotional response", "Interactivity", "Personalization", "Content quality", "SEM", "Purchase intention."]',NULL,22,1,'1031-1051','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1750','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',55),
(1751,1751,1,'Enhancing Decision-Making in Digital Asset Markets Through Hybrid Machine Learning and Deterministic Optimization','<p>The paper presents a new hybrid approach (LSTM-RF-SLSQP) that acts as an advanced decision-support tool for institutional crypto portfolio management. The methodology includes the use of LSTM neural networks to detect non-linear temporal patterns and RF algorithms to detect structural market noise. The ability to predict future prices based on LSTM-RF model has been extensively verified out-of-sample using Mean Absolute Error (MAE) and Root Mean Square Error (RMSE), and shown to have smaller prediction error than standalone algorithms on very volatile assets. Then, based on robust return and risk forecasts, SLSQP optimization algorithm allocates asset weights aiming at maximizing Sharpe ratio under specific institutional constraints, applying buy-and-hold approach with quarterly rebalance. The empirical study is performed for a portfolio of ten major cryptocurrencies (BTC, ETH, SOL, BNB, XRP, ADA, DOGE, TRX, LINK, DOT) using data provided by KuCoin exchange from January 2024 to January 2026. The numerical experiments demonstrated the significant superiority of the suggested framework compared to all benchmarks. Namely, the LSTM-RF-SLSQP approach provides the impressive annual return of 107.40%, Sharpe ratio of 2.04, with maximum drawdown equal to -3.80%.</p>','["Artificial Intelligence", "Cryptocurrency", "LSTM", "Portfolio Optimization", "Random Forest", "SLSQP"]',NULL,22,1,'1052-1068','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1751','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',56),
(1752,1752,1,'MSTF-Net: A Multi-Scale Transformer-Guided Feature Fusion Framework for Accurate Lung Cancer Detection from CT Images','<p>Lung cancer is one of the most common causes of cancer death worldwide, and early and accurate diagnosis is crucial to upsurge patient survival. Deep learning-based computer-aided diagnosis systems have demonstrated promising results, however they are difficult to recognise fine-grained local lesion traits and long-range contextual connections from CT scans. To overcome these limitations, to propose a Multi-Scale Transformer-Guided Feature Fusion Network for lung cancer diagnosis using the LIDC-IDRI dataset, named MSTF-Net. The proposed framework consists of CT image preprocessing, attention-guided lung nodule segmentation, multi-scale convolutional feature extraction, dual attention augmentation, Transformer-based contextual learning, adaptive cross-scale feature fusion and optimised classification. Multi-scale branches capture pulmonary nodules of various sizes and Transformer encoder models global contextual interactions of pulmonary structures. Experiments indicate the effectiveness of the framework. MSTF-Net outperforms Shuffle-DNN, CCHCO-DenResFT-Net, CHC-VO DCNN, FVCM-Net, and C-Swin with 99.14% accuracy, 98.96% precision, 98.72% recall, 98.84% F1-score, and 99.31% specificity. The lung nodule segmentation model reached Dice coefficient 97.42% and IoU 94.98%. The results suggest that MSTF-Net is a robust framework for automated lung cancer identification and can assist radiologists to make clinical decisions.</p>','["Lung Cancer Detection", "Computed Tomography", "Multi-Scale CNN", "Transformer Network", "Feature Fusion", "Attention Mechanism", "LIDC-IDRI Dataset."]',NULL,22,1,'1069-1086','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1752','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',57),
(1753,1753,1,'Blockchain-Assisted Lightweight Authentication Protocol for Resource-Constrained IoT Devices in 5G Smart Environments','<p>The ubiquity of lightweight resource-constrained Internet-of-Things (IoT) devices in 5G smart environments necessitates authentication protocols with the conflicting goals of being lightweight, highly secure, and having a decentralised credential management structure. Existing schemes use trusted third-party key distributors or heavyweight cryptographic primitives infeasible to IoT embedded hardware; they also fail to anchor device credentials on a permissioned blockchain ledger for tamper-evident credential revocation. In this work, we introduce BLAP-IoT: a Blockchain-Assisted Lightweight Authentication Protocol over live Hyperledger Fabric 2.5.9 that leverages elliptic-curve Diffie–Hellman over P-256 curve, keyed MACs, and a three-message challenge-response protocol to provide injective mutual authentication with device key confirmation. Device credential commitments are stored on-chain to facilitate decentralised and efficient device revocation without revealing secrets on-chain. A formal security verification of the protocol in ProVerif 2.05 shows session-key secrecy, injective mutual authentication, and perfect forward secrecy in the presence of the Dolev-Yao attacker. The empirical evaluation of BLAP-IoT on measured P-256 primitives reports that the scheme achieves a total computation cost of 0.303 ms on constrained devices — up to 52% less than compared schemes, 1920-bit two-way communication overhead, and 0.218 mJ device energy consumption. The underlying blockchain layer sustains up to 277 transactions per second (TPS) in peak throughput, with end-to-end authentication latency less than 13 ms at 1000 concurrent devices.</p>','["IoT authentication", "5G security", "blockchain", "Hyperledger Fabric", "elliptic-curve cryptography", "ProVerif", "lightweight protocol", "formal verification", "key agreement", "resource-constrained devices"]',NULL,22,1,'1087-1105','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1753','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',58),
(1754,1754,1,'A Hybrid-Based Generative Adversarial Network Pattern For Diabetic Retinopathy Prediction','<p>Diabetic retinopathy (DR), a primary cause of lack of vision, can be detected early with comprehensive examination of fundus visuals of the retina that can be averted worldwide. Several noise sources frequently damage fundus visuals, reducing visual quality and making it more difficult to see fragile macular structures like tiny blood vessels and microaneurysms. On tiny medical information sets, noise reduction methods powered by deep training are computationally challenging and prone to excessive fitting. To address these issues, the current study suggests a hybrid–classical construction called the QGAN, which employs hybrid circuits in latent space in conjunction with convolutional component coding. By enhancing the latent representations through hybrid superposition and entanglement, the recommended QGAN enhances noise reduction and macular detail maintenance. In tests on the DR-based Kaggle information set, QGAN outperforms better than conventional noise reduction systems like CAE, ResNet, and DnCNN thanks to a PSNR of 39 dB, SSIM of 0.97, and AMI of 0.89. Although there is a little computing cost related to shallow hybrid circuits, the method maintains sensitive macular patterns and consistency in intensity. The aforementioned findings show that QGAN is a viable pretreatment method in early diabetic retinopathy and a promising hybrid-aided construction for noise reduction macular visuals.</p>','["Diabetic retinopathy, Prediction, Deep training, Visual quality, Adversarial pattern"]',NULL,22,1,'1106-1127','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1754','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',59),
(1755,1755,1,'Ring Signature with Multi Designated Verifier Zero Knowledge Proof for Privacy-Preserving Blockchain Platforms','<p>Blockchain data is immutable and publicly visible a sensitive signature is placed on-chain, anyone can attempt to verify it. This openness may lead to unintended information exposure. The standard ring signature cannot fully address all the privacy, selective-verification and time -controlled disclosure requirements that arise in modern secure systems. The proposed algorithm presents a Blockchain-based Ring Signature with Multi-Designated Verifier and Zero-Knowledge Proof (BRSMDV-ZKP) enables a signer anonymously authenticate a transaction with a group of public keys while ensuring that only designated verifiers can verify the signature, The scheme incorporates a challenge–response mechanism, randomized commitments, and encrypted verifiers specific data to ensure signer anonymity, trace resistance, and verifier exclusivity. A Zero-Knowledge Proof (ZKP) is employed to prove correct decryption of the signature without revealing the verifier′s private key. The time-lock puzzle enforces a predefined delay, preventing early verification and enabling reward–penalty mechanisms for verifier compliance. This approach reduces the risk of key leakage, preserves privacy in decentralized systems and multi-party environments, and supports secure applications such as confidential e-voting, sealed-bid auctions, and legal document verification on blockchain platforms.</p>','["Blockchain, Ring Signature, Multi Designated Verifier Proof, Zero knowledge Proof, Privacy"]',NULL,22,1,'1128-1150','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1755','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',60),
(1756,1756,1,'Lack of Digital Infrastructure Undermines SDG 3 and 4: Why Personality Traits Are Not Enough to Drive Tech Adoption among India’s Elderly','<p>This paper investigates the factors limiting digital technology use among senior citizens in India, focusing on the relative influence of personality traits versus structural barriers. While existing studies highlight psychological predispositions, the interaction between individual traits and external constraints remains underexplored, particularly in the Indian context. This study addresses this gap by evaluating whether personality traits meaningfully predict digital engagement or if structural factors dominate.</p>
