(2210,2210,1,'The role of Tourism Governance in mediating the relationship between Traditional Villages and Tourism Organization Performance is strengthened by Local Leadership as a moderating variable','<p>Purpose: This study aims to explain that the role of Tourism Governance in mediating the relationship between Traditional Villages and Tourism Organization Performance is strengthened by Local Leadership as a moderating variable.<br/>Design/methodology/approach: This research utilized a quantitative approach with SEM-PLS, analyzed with SmartPLS 4.1. The data examination encompassed predictive relevance (Q²), common method bias, structural path coefficients via bootstrapping, confirmatory factor analysis, correlation analysis, multicollinearity evaluation, and construct reliability measurement. A cumulative of 53 participants engaged in the research, comprising traditional village leaders, destination supervisors, and regional tourism entities throughout several tourist regions in Bali. Data were gathered via in-person and digital surveys from February to April 2026.<br/>Findings: The empirical results indicate that Traditional Village Institutions positively and significantly influence Tourism Governance and Organizational Performance. Tourism Governance has no significant influence on Organizational Performance. Further, the mediating role of Tourism Governance is not confirmed. Also, the data suggest that increased local leadership can, in certain cases, diminish the link between traditional village institutions and tourism governance. The results emphasize the necessity of local leadership, human resource systems and sustainable knowledge management.<br/>Originality/value: The novelty of this research is in treating traditional villages as dynamic institutional actors and in incorporating institutional theory, local governance and organizational effectiveness in a single empirical framework.</p>','["Traditional Villages", "Tourism Governance", "Local Leadership", "Local Institutions", "Tourism Organization Performance."]',NULL,22,1,'1387 - 1412','10.59543/jidmis.v3.2210','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',72),
(2211,2211,1,'Perceived Training Utility of Artificial Intelligence in Virtual Reality Taekwondo Sparring: A Repeated-Measures Study','<p>Virtual reality (VR) can provide repeatable sport-specific practice, but the training value athletes assign to AI-controlled opponents remains uncertain. This study compared perceived training utility between AI-controlled and human-controlled VR Taekwondo sparring and examined whether bout duration modified this response. Thirty-one collegiate Taekwondo athletes completed four conditions in a 2 × 2 within-subject design: AI-controlled 30-s, AI-controlled 60-s, human-controlled 30-s, and human-controlled 60-s sparring. Perceived training utility was the primary outcome; condition-specific measures of presence, realism, opponent perception, biomechanical perception, usability, enjoyment, satisfaction, behavioral intention, perceived exertion, and cybersickness were also collected. Repeated-measures ANOVA showed higher perceived training utility for AI-controlled than human-controlled sparring, F(1, 30) = 6.32, p = .018, ηp² = .174, whereas the duration effect and opponent × duration interaction were not significant. No significant opponent-type or duration effects were detected for the other condition-specific outcomes. In an exploratory linear mixed-effects model, realism, opponent perception, and biomechanical perception were positively associated with perceived training utility. Within this VR system and brief exposure protocol, athletes assigned somewhat greater training utility to the AI-controlled opponent. This finding concerns perceived usefulness rather than demonstrated performance gains and supports further testing of whether realistic opponent behavior and movement fidelity translate into objective training benefits.</p>','["virtual reality", "taekwondo", "artificial intelligence", "training utility", "combat sports."]',NULL,22,1,'1413 - 1429','10.59543/jidmis.v3.2211','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',73),
(2212,2212,1,'Deep Learning-Based Detection of Deceptive UI Patterns in Web Applications Using Convolutional Neural Networks','<p>The growth of web applications has been accompanied by a parallel growth in the variety of deceptive user interface (UI) patterns or, alternatively referred to as, dark patterns that manipulate their users by taking advantage of human behavior by manipulating their visual presentation. The privacy, freedom, and economic wellbeing of consumers are at a high risk due to these trends, e.g., disguised advertisements, unnoticed subscription traps, misleading sense of urgency, and confirm shaming elements.  Although increased regulatory interest has led to an increase in regulatory focus, scale-based automated detection of such patterns is an ongoing research problem. Manual auditing is labor intensive, not consistent and cannot be used to monitor the web continuously. The paper suggests a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN)-based system to identify and classify fake UI patterns in web application screenshots. The systematic data collection and strict inter-annotator annotation created a curated, annotated set of 4,800 web screenshots in six categories of deception and a non-deceptive baseline. It has been compared to five state-of-the-art baseline models, such as VGG16, ResNet50, EfficientNetB3, a Support Vector Machine (SVM) and a Random Forest classifier. Gradient-weighted Class Activation Mapping (Grad-CAM) was used to explain the activation of salient regions of the UI that used the activation to make classification decisions. The proposed CNN obtained a macro-averaged F1-score of 0.914 and AUC of 0.971, which is higher than any baselines. Grad-CAM analyses provided support that the model pays attention to semantically meaningful areas including call-to-action buttons, pre-ticked checkboxes, and obfuscated pricing items. The suggested framework provides a scalable base of browser based auditing tools, regulatory compliance pipelines and ethical UI design assessment</p>','["dark patterns, deceptive UI, convolutional neural networks, screenshot classification, Grad-CAM, web usability, ethical AI."]',NULL,22,1,'1430 - 1446','10.59543/jidmis.v3.2212','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',74),
(2213,2213,1,'The Role of Green Business Strategy in Mediating the Relationship between Environmental Orientation and SME Performance, Moderated by Environmental Performance','<p>Purpose: SMEs are very important to the economies of developing countries, especially Indonesia. However, This study seeks to investigate the mediating function of green business strategies in the correlation between environmental orientation and SME performance, with environmental performance acting as a moderating variable.<br/>Design/methodology/approach: The study population was the SME representatives that participated in the survey, with a sample of 151 responses. The number of participants was established based on the SMEs registered in East Java Province, Indonesia. The present study was conducted utilising a quantitative approach by means of questionnaires given online and in person. Data analysis was carried out using the SEM-PLS technique supported by SmartPLS software. Data investigation comprised predictive relevance (Q²), common method bias, structural path coefficients via bootstrapping, confirmatory factor analysis, correlation analysis, multicollinearity assessment, and construct reliability measurement.<br/>Findings: The results reveal that environmental orientation positively effects green business strategies and SME performance. Environmental performance also strongly affects the performance of SMEs. Green business strategies also considerably affect the performance of SMEs. The indirect effect of environmental orientation on SME performance via green business practices is also significant. However, the moderating influence of environmental performance in the link between environmental orientation and SME performance is statistically insignificant. The results demonstrate the significant significance of green business strategies in facilitating the relationship between environmental orientation and SME performance.<br/>Originality/value: This study provides novelty by confirming the mediation of green business strategy between environmental orientation and SME performance. Furthermore, it contributes to the literature by studying the moderating influence of environmental performance in the Indonesian SME setting, thereby offering a more particular understanding of the relationship between sustainability and company performance.</p>','["Environmental Orientation, Environmental Performance, Green Business, Strategy, SMEs Performance, Moderating, mediation, Indonesua, SEM-PLS, SmartPLS."]',NULL,22,1,'1447 - 1474','10.59543/jidmis.v3.2213','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',75),
(2214,2214,1,'DMRI-Net: A Federated Multimodal Explainable Intelligence Framework for Dynamic Molting Risk Assessment and Predictive Water Quality Analytics in Smart Vertical Crab House Aquaculture','<p>Existing smart aquaculture systems are mostly based on reactive monitoring and unimodal analytics, which are inadequate for predicting environmental deterioration and biological responses. Based on these issues, this study proposes a federated multimodal explainable intelligence framework for predictive wwater-quality analytics and dynamic molting-risk assessment in a Smart vertical Crab House aquaculture system, named ater quality analytics and dynamic molting-risk assessment in Smart vertical Crab House aquaculture system, named as DMRI-Net. It combines a Temporal Transformer module for environmental encoding, a Vision Transformer module for behavioral encoding, cross-modal fusion, explainable AI (SHAP and Grad-CAM), and federated edge learning to model the environmental dynamics and the crab behavioral patterns. Also, a new Dynamic Molting Risk Index (DMRI) is provided to represent the probability of imminent molting as a continuous risk value, and a Predictive Water Quality Intelligence (PWQI) module is added to provide the multi-horizon forecasting of critical water-quality parameters. Experiments with the Scylla serrata IoT Aquaculture Dataset (18,144 environmental observations and 12,480 annotated behavioral images) show that DMRI-Net significantly outperforms classical machine learning, deep learning, and state-of-the-art multimodal baselines. The proposed framework has achieved the accuracy of 96.0%, F1 score of 95.0%, AUC of 97.0%, the R² of 0.94, the MAE of 0.12 and the RMSE of 0.18, all of which are good. The findings provide strong evidence of the potential benefits of integrating multimodal learning, explainable intelligence, and federated analytics for intelligent, proactive, interpretable, and scalable aquaculture management, setting a new benchmark for intelligent crustacean farming systems.</p>','["Federated Multimodal, Dynamic Molting Risk Index (DMRI), Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI), Predictive Water Quality Intelligence, Scylla serrata."]',NULL,22,1,'1475 - 1500','10.59543/jidmis.v3.2214','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',76),
(2215,2215,1,'Firms'' Financial Performance in Indonesia Based on the CEO''s Face Recognition','<p>The decline in the COVID-19 pandemic has prompted many companies to recover and strive to achieve performance goals. Corporate performance has become a compelling issue, drawing significant attention both internally and from stakeholders. The purpose of this study is to analyze corporate performance in Indonesia through leverage ratios, profitability, and market value. This study will investigate the relationship between CEO characteristics and facial shape, using it to demonstrate how company performance and market value are influenced, a relationship that has not been previously studied. This study will use data from the financial reports of all Indonesian companies listed on the Indonesia Stock Exchange (IDX) for the years 2022 and 2023. The results indicate differences in the Debt-to-Asset Ratio (DAR) and Return on Equity (ROE) across CEOs with different facial features. This study offers implications for investors, encouraging them to consider the characteristics of company leaders before investing carefully.</p>','["Financial performance", "Leverage", "Profitability", "Market value", "CEO facial shape."]',NULL,22,1,'1501 - 1511','10.59543/jidmis.v3.2215','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',77),
(2216,2216,1,'A Correlation-Based Feature Selection and Weighted XGBoost Framework for Minority IoT Attack Detection','<p>The rapid expansion of Internet of Things (IoT) environments has increased exposure to diverse cyberattacks, while severe class imbalance in network traffic continues to limit the effectiveness of intrusion detection systems (IDS), particularly for rare but security-critical attacks. This study proposes a Correlation-Based Feature Selection (CFS)–Weighted XGBoost framework that combines redundancy-aware feature selection with an embedded class-weighted learning classifier to improve minority-attack detection. The framework was evaluated on an attack-aware sampled subset of the CICIoT2023 dataset containing all 34 attack classes while preserving the original attack-frequency hierarchy. Using only the top 25 selected features, the proposed framework achieved 93.7% accuracy, 87.0% macro-F1, and 93.8% weighted F1, outperforming both full-featured baseline models and the recent Attack-aware Feature Aggregation Model (AFAM) while reducing the feature set by 37.5%. The proposed framework improved detection of minority web-based and reconnaissance attacks while maintaining strong performance on majority attack classes without relying on over-sampling or synthetic data generation. These findings demonstrate that integrating redundancy-aware feature selection with embedded class-weighted learning enables accurate, computationally efficient, and reliable intrusion detection for highly imbalanced IoT environments.</p>','["Internet of Things (IoT) Security, Intrusion Detection System (IDS), Class Imbalance, Feature Selection, XGBoost."]',NULL,22,1,'1512 - 1534','10.59543/jidmis.v3.2216','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',78),
(2217,2217,1,'Business Engineering as a Foundation for Sustainable AI Transformation: The ADAIEM Framework','<p>Despite the strategic priority of digital transformation and Artificial Intelligence (AI), many organizational initiatives fail to achieve sustainable outcomes due to insufficient institutional readiness and fragmented governance. To address this gap, this paper introduces the Abuhaimed Digital &amp; AI Excellence Model (ADAIEM), a comprehensive conceptual framework designed to foster institutional readiness and guide enduring transformation. The framework integrates three interdependent pillars: Institutional Foundation: Governance, strategy, organizational structure, processes, knowledge management, and talent development, Digital Enablement: Core digital systems, data infrastructure, automation, analytics, and platforms, and AI Enablement: AI governance, intelligent agents, decision-support mechanisms, and enterprise-wide adoption. Central to the framework is the ADAIEM Conditional Transformation Logic (ACTL), which utilizes capability gates to enforce progression only when prerequisite maturity levels are met. Unlike traditional static maturity models, ACTL actively facilitates continuous capability development, mitigates execution risks, and reinforces operational sustainability. Grounded in Business Engineering and organizational capability theory, ADAIEM advances the literature on digital transformation and AI governance by offering a structured, risk-mitigated pathway toward high-maturity, AI-enabled enterprise operations. Building on the concepts of Business Engineering and based on a variety of organizational and transformation theories, ADAIEM brings together governance, organizational design, knowledge management, talent development, digital capabilities and AI enablement under a single transformation architecture. The proposed framework offers a real-world action plan for sustainable AI transformation and a theoretical understanding of the phenomenon of AI transformation.</p>','["Business Engineering", "Digital Transformation", "Artificial Intelligence", "Institutional Readiness", "AI Governance", "Organizational Capability", "Sustainable Transformation.."]',NULL,22,1,'1535 - 1564','10.59543/jidmis.v3.2217','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',79),
(2218,2218,1,'A Cognitive-Inspired Scanning Framework for Topic Modeling and Document Classification','<p>As digital textual data continues to grow exponentially, it becomes progressively harder to extract information from large collections of documents. Traditional full-text analysis methods often require substantial time and computational resources. This challenge is particularly relevant for massive corpora where important information is dispersed across different documents. This dispersion makes rapid comprehension difficult. Therefore, we propose an innovative method that combines scanning (skimming) reading techniques with Latent Dirichlet Allocation (LDA). This method enables an efficient exploration and an overview analysis of massive text corpora. Our approach leverages scanning as a rapid information extraction strategy in press articles. It identifies key and representative textual segments without the need for exhaustive reading. This preliminary filtering mechanism reduces processing complexity while preserving essential semantic content. To complement this step, we employ LDA to model latent topics and cluster documents based on their thematic structures. Unlike surface-level analysis, LDA exposes hidden topic distributions within the corpus, enabling the identification of both dominant topics and latent subtopics. Through the integration of scanning with LDA, we provide a structured framework to explore extensive collections of press articles. The scanning phase improves efficiency through the refinement of the textual scope. Meanwhile, LDA provides a robust probabilistic topic model and groups articles accordingly. This synergy facilitates a comprehensive understanding of the texts at both macro and micro levels.</p>','["Scanning Reading", "Latent Dirichlet Allocation (LDA)", "Topic Modeling", "Text Mining", "Document Clustering", "Information Extraction", "Probabilistic Models."]',NULL,22,1,'1565 - 1576','10.59543/jidmis.v3.2218','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',80),
(2219,2219,1,'Explaining Post-Purchase Behavior in E-commerce: The Roles of Consumer Awareness, Perceived Risk, and Satisfaction','<p>The rapid growth of e-commerce platforms has shifted managerial focus from customer acquisition to customer retention and post-purchase behavior. Although prior studies have extensively examined pre-purchase intentions, limited research explains how post-purchase evaluations are shaped by technology perceptions, perceived risk, and consumer awareness within an integrated framework. This study addresses this gap by developing and testing a research model that combines the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM), Perceived Risk Theory, and Expectation–Confirmation Theory in the context of e-commerce platforms. Survey data were collected from 410 consumers with actual online shopping experience and analyzed using partial least squares structural equation modeling (PLS-SEM). Perceived risk is conceptualized as a second-order multidimensional construct. The results show that consumer awareness significantly reduces perceived risk and directly enhances post-purchase satisfaction. Perceived ease of use positively influences perceived usefulness, while perceived usefulness and customer satisfaction significantly drive satisfaction and repurchase intention, respectively. However, the effects of perceived risk on perceived usefulness and perceived ease of use on satisfaction are not supported. This study extends TAM to the post-purchase stage, refines the role of perceived risk in e-commerce, and highlights consumer awareness as a key determinant of post-purchase satisfaction. The findings offer important implications for e-commerce platforms aiming to strengthen customer retention and long-term sustainability.</p>','["E-commerce", "Post-purchase behavior", "Consumer awareness", "Perceived risk", "Customer satisfaction", "Repurchase intention."]',NULL,22,1,'1577 - 1597','10.59543/jidmis.v3.2219','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',81),
(2220,2220,1,'Linguistic Variety as a Framework for Evaluating LLM Behavior: A Comparative Study of Irish English and General American in Large Language Model Responses','<p>While large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used across social domains, current bias evaluations often rely on demographic proxies such as names, pronouns, and social categories. Linguistic variety itself receives less attention as an evaluative variable. This study therefore uses sociolinguistic variety to examine whether three LLMs respond differently to semantically equivalent prompts in General American English and Irish English. Thirty prompt pairs were administered twice to GPT-5, Claude, and Gemini, yielding 360 responses. Across the 180 paired comparisons, 92.8% differed in word count, although the direction varied by model: Claude produced longer Irish English responses on average, whereas GPT-5 and Gemini produced shorter responses. Claude explicitly referenced Irish English features in 55.0% of its Irish English responses, compared with 0.0% for GPT-5 and 1.7% for Gemini. No explicit correction, refusal, or researcher-observed tone shift occurred in either condition. These findings show variety-conditioned differences in response behavior, but they do not by themselves establish discriminatory biasThe study supports linguistic variety as an additional dimension for LLM differential-behavior evaluation and identifies model- and feature-specific patterns that warrant further evaluation with independent raters and additional varieties.</p>','["large language models", "linguistic variety", "LLM bias evaluation", "Irish English", "enregisterment", "sociolinguistics", "prescriptivism", "General American English."]',NULL,22,1,'1598 - 1624','10.59543/jidmis.v3.2220','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',82),
(2221,2221,1,'The Role of Organizational Learning and Product Innovation as Mediation Variables in MSMEs in Indonesia','<p>Micro, Small, and Medium Enterprises (MSMEs) are the backbone of the Indonesian economy, but they still face limitations in resources, technology access, and innovation capacity in maintaining sustainable competitive advantage (SCA). One relevant strategy to overcome these challenges is open innovation (OI), namely openness to external knowledge to accelerate the innovation process and strengthen competitiveness. However, the effectiveness of OI in creating SCA does not occur automatically, but requires appropriate mediating mechanisms, such as organizational learning (OL) and product innovation (PI). The research method uses a quantitative approach with a causality design. Primary data were collected through a survey of 300 MSMEs in the manufacturing and innovation-based service sectors in Indonesia, and analyzed using Structural Equation Modeling–Partial Least Squares (SEM-PLS). The results show that OI has a significant positive effect on SCA and encourages an increase in LOXP, LOXT, and PI. However, only LOXT contributes significantly to SCA and mediates the OI–SCA relationship. In contrast, LOXP and PI are not proven to be significant on SCA. These findings confirm that MSMEs are more effective in gaining sustainable competitive advantage through exploitation, namely the utilization of existing knowledge and resources, rather than solely focusing on exploring new ideas or product innovation. This research contributes theoretically by enriching the innovation management literature in the context of MSMEs in developing countries, while also highlighting the practical importance for MSMEs to balance openness to innovation strategies with exploitation-oriented organizational learning. Further research is recommended to examine other mediating factors, such as absorptive capacity or dynamic capabilities, and to broaden the sector and regional contexts to gain a more comprehensive understanding.</p>','["open innovation, organizational learning, product innovation, sustainable competitive advantage, MSMEs."]',NULL,22,1,'1625 - 1651','10.59543/jidmis.v3.2221','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',83),
(2222,2222,1,'Explainable Artificial Intelligence, Circular Resilience and Digital Interoperability in Healthcare Supply Chains: A Systematic Review and Integrative Research Agenda','<p>Background: Healthcare supply chains determine the availability, safety and continuity of medicines, vaccines, devices and protective equipment. Explainable artificial intelligence, circular practices and digital interoperability may each strengthen these systems, but their evidence bases have developed separately. We reviewed how these three capabilities have been designed, implemented, evaluated and connected in healthcare supply chains. Methods: PubMed/MEDLINE, OpenAlex and Crossref were searched from inception to 1 August 2026 using four prespecified search families. Backward and forward citation searching supplemented database retrieval. Eligible records addressed an explained artificial-intelligence-enabled supply-chain decision, a circular practice explicitly linked to resilience, or demonstrated cross-system supply-chain data exchange. Two reviewers screened a calibrated high-sensitivity queue independently and a third adjudicated conflicts. Design-matched critical appraisal was applied. Heterogeneous evidence was synthesised narratively following reporting guidance for synthesis without meta-analysis, and cross-stream relationships were classified as directly tested, associated, theoretically argued or proposed. Results: The searches identified 2679 records, of which 438 were duplicates. Of 2241 unique records, 1702 were marked ineligible by prespecified automation-assisted rules and 539 underwent independent title and abstract screening. Eighty-five reports were assessed in full. The synthesis included 26 studies and three authoritative standards. Four studies addressed explainability, nine circular resilience and 14 interoperability, with one study contributing to two streams. Interoperability had the strongest field evidence and was associated with improved visibility and fewer vaccine stock-outs in one non-randomised implementation. Circular evidence mainly concerned controlled protective-equipment reuse during shortages. Explainability studies demonstrated technical explanations but did not test whether explanations improved human decisions. No study evaluated all three domains together. Conclusions: Interoperability is the most mature enabling layer. Circular practices can extend effective supply under controlled safety conditions, while explainable artificial intelligence remains technically promising but weakly evaluated with users. An integrated architecture should therefore be treated as a testable research proposition rather than an established intervention. Prospective multisite studies should measure availability, safety, resilience, environmental outcomes and explanation-supported decisions.</p>','["Explainable artificial intelligence", "circular economy", "resilience", "interoperability", "healthcare supply chain", "medical products", "systematic review", "evidence synthesis."]',NULL,22,1,'1652 - 1675','10.59543/jidmis.v3.2222','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',84),
(2223,2223,1,'An Integrated Big Data and Predictive Analytics Framework for Supply Chain Resilience, International Trade and Economic Growth','<p>Global supply networks face compound disruptions arising from geopolitical conflict, climate hazards, cyber incidents, logistics bottlenecks, demand volatility, and policy uncertainty. Existing studies demonstrate that big data analytics can improve forecasting and supply chain performance, but they seldom explain the full conversion mechanism through which data resources become actionable resilience or how firm-level resilience contributes to trade continuity and economic performance. This article develops a multilevel capability to outcome framework that links six stages: multi-source data acquisition, governed data integration, predictive intelligence, resilience orchestration, international trade continuity, and productivity-oriented growth outcomes. The theoretical novelty lies in three mechanisms. First, it distinguishes predictive accuracy from decision actionability and identifies resilience orchestration as the dynamic capability that converts forecasts into coordinated operational responses. Second, it specifies cross-level transmission from firm capability to supply-network stability and then to trade continuity. Third, it defines explicit boundary conditions, including shock observability, response discretion, input substitutability, network concentration, cyber exposure, and institutional digital capacity. To address feasibility concerns, the paper proposes a focused first-stage validation in export-oriented automotive-component manufacturing using a three-wave, 24-month panel that combines survey measures with operational and shipment records. Longitudinal structural equation modelling and firm fixed-effects estimation are designated as the primary methods; machine-learning comparison and macroeconomic aggregation are retained as secondary extensions rather than simultaneous requirements. Economic growth is treated as a distal outcome mediated by resilient trade and productivity, not as a direct consequence of technology adoption. The revised framework offers a more parsimonious, falsifiable, and practically implementable research program for firms and policy institutions.</p>','["Big Data Analytics", "Predictive Intelligence", "Supply Chain Resilience", "International Trade", "Global Value Chains", "Productivity", "Dynamic Capabilities", "Data Governance", "Automotive Supply Networks."]',NULL,22,1,'1676 - 1694','10.59543/jidmis.v3.2223','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',85),
(2224,2224,1,'Determinants of AI Adoption Behaviour Among Lecturers: The Moderating Role of Institutional Support in Jordanian Higher Education','<p>This study examines the determinants influencing lecturers’ intention to use and adopt artificial intelligence (AI) in higher education. As AI technologies become increasingly integrated into teaching and learning, understanding lecturers’ perceptions is critical for successful adoption. Drawing on an extended Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (UTAUT) framework, this study investigates the roles of AI self-efficacy, perceived usefulness, relative advantage, and institutional support.<br/>A quantitative research design was employed using a systematic random sampling approach. Data were collected from 371 lecturers in public universities in Jordan through a structured questionnaire. All constructs were measured using validated items on a five-point Likert scale, and the data were analysed using Partial Least Squares Structural Equation Modelling (PLS-SEM).<br/>The results show that AI self-efficacy, perceived usefulness, and relative advantage have significant positive effects on lecturers’ intention to use AI. Furthermore, institutional support plays a pivotal moderating role, strengthening the relationship between intention to use AI and actual adoption behaviour. The findings also confirm that intention is a significant predictor of AI adoption behaviour.<br/>Theoretically, this study extends the UTAUT model by incorporating AI-specific determinants and emphasising the moderating role of institutional support, providing deeper insights into technology adoption in educational contexts. Practically, the findings highlight the importance of institutional support in developing lecturers’ AI-related skills and competencies. Targeted training programs and supportive environments can facilitate the effective and sustained use of AI tools in higher education.</p>','["Artificial intelligence (AI)", "AI adoption", "AI self-efficacy", "Perceived Usefulness", "Relative Advantage", "Institutional Support", "Higher Education."]',NULL,22,1,'1695 - 1719','10.59543/jidmis.v3.2224','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',86),
(2225,2225,1,'Transforming SMEs through AI-Driven Digital Ecosystems to Improve Sustainable Performance: The Roles of Organizational Resilience, Sustainable Value Co-Creation, and Digital Maturity','<p>This research investigates the relationship between artificial intelligence-integrated digital ecosystem, digital maturity, organisational resilience, and the mechanism of sustainable value co-creation and performance results in small and medium-sized firms in East Java, Indonesia. It aims to elucidate how these five factors collectively foster long-term resilience and sustainability in increasingly information-dense business environments.<br/>The Quantitative Method was applied with the SEM-PLS approach in the SmartPLS 3.2 program. The survey included 151 SMEs with different demographic characteristics. The model was evaluated for predictive relevance (Q2), common method bias, bootstrapped path coefficients, confirmatory factor analysis, correlation analysis, multicollinearity, and construct reliability. Data were obtained between December 2025 and April 2026.<br/>The results reveal that AI-enabled digital ecosystems have a remarkable and favourable effect on organisational resilience and sustainable value co-creation, hence improving sustainable performance. Digital maturity serves as an important enabler and mediator, further enhancing the benefits of these AI-enabled solutions. Moreover, sustainable value co-creation is found as the main predictor of sustainable performance, and the link between resilience and co-creation is reciprocal, showing a reinforcing dynamic.<br/>This study contributes to the current literature by extending the conceptualisation of SME success to include economic, social and environmental factors. Moreover, it provides a solid basis for understanding the role of AI ecosystems and digital maturity in supporting resilience and sustainability, allowing resource-constrained SMEs to retain their competitive advantage, while also supporting international sustainability agendas such as the Sustainable Development Goals and Environmental, Social, and Governance standards.</p>','["SMEs, AI-driven digital ecosystems, digital maturity, organizational resilience, Indonesia, sustainable performance, sustainable value co-creation, and digital transformation."]',NULL,22,1,'1720 - 1746','10.59543/jidmis.v3.2225','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',87),
(2226,2226,1,'User Experience, Direct Preferences, and Condition Rankings across AI- and Human-Controlled Virtual Reality Taekwondo Sparring','<p>Virtual reality (VR) sparring can be delivered with AI-controlled or human-controlled opponents, but athletes may evaluate these modes differently when judging each bout in isolation versus comparing them directly. This study examined condition-specific user experience and post-experiment preferences in 31 collegiate Taekwondo athletes. Each participant completed four VR sparring conditions: AI-controlled 30 s, AI-controlled 60 s, human-controlled 30 s, and human-controlled 60 s. Condition-specific outcomes included cybersickness, perceived exertion, presence, enjoyment, satisfaction, behavioral intention, single-item training value, opponent perception, usability, realism, and perceived training utility. After all four conditions, participants indicated their opponent-mode and duration preferences and ranked the conditions. Most condition-specific measures did not differ significantly by opponent type or duration, and cybersickness was low in all conditions. In direct comparisons, 71.0% favored human-controlled sparring for enjoyment, 61.3% for immersion, and 51.6% for suitability for actual training; judgments of exercise effect were nearly evenly divided. Sixty-second bouts were preferred by 74.2% of participants, and the human-controlled 60-s condition received the most favorable rankings across several domains. Perceived training utility was an exception among the condition-specific measures, favoring AI-controlled sparring (p = .018). The contrast between isolated ratings and direct comparative judgments indicates that these approaches capture different aspects of user experience. Evaluation of VR combat-sport systems may therefore benefit from combining condition-specific responses with comparative preference measures.</p>','["user experience", "user preference", "virtual reality", "Taekwondo", "artificial intelligence."]',NULL,22,1,'1747 - 1761','10.59543/jidmis.v3.2226','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',88),
(2227,2227,1,'A Self-Reflective Agentic Framework for Adaptive Pronunciation Assessment and Multimodal Feedback','<p>Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR) has seen tremendous advancements in recent times with the introduction of agentic frameworks for computer assisted pronunciation training (CAPT). The primary modules of a CAPT system are pronunciation assessment and feedback. The existing approaches often lack stability and an inability to adapt to learner-specific pronunciation parameters. The proposed work proposes a reinforcement learning (RL) induced self-reflective diagnostics (SRD) based agentic framework. This paper proposes a multi-stage, Wasserstein Generative Adversarial Networks with Gradient Penalty based agentic pronunciation assessment model. The proposed system models the canonical phoneme embeddings and the pronunciation deviation estimates using a Wasserstein distance–based metric, enabling fine-grained and stable phoneme-level assessment. A closed-loop optimization mechanism enables autonomous reinforcement learning by generating adaptive feedback for phoneme generation and score assessment. The self-reflective diagnostic module ensures clarity and pedagogical quality by monitoring the feedback quality. The current state-of-the-art models like GAN, LLM based diagnosis fail to deliver context awareness in the corrective feedback for phoneme deviations. The multimodal large language model generates the context aware dynamic feedback. Experimental results show noticeable in the word-level mean accuracy from 98.9% for Whisper based GAN model to 99.4% for WGAN-GP based framework with RL and SRD. Compared with representative state-of-the-art baselines, including GOP-based scoring, self-supervised mispronunciation detection, and LLM-based diagnosis, the proposed approach achieves higher sentence-, word-, and letter-level accuracy of about 99% in each criteria , significantly lower phoneme deviation with mean MSE 0.14, and improved feedback consistency with 99.5% clarity. The results confirm that ensembling distribution-aware agentic optimization and self-reflective control based generative model generate a robust and adaptive pronunciation learning architecture optimized for real-world CAPT applications</p>','["Generative Adversarial Network, Multimodal, Large Language model, Agentic Model, Self-Reflective Diagnosis."]',NULL,22,1,'1762 - 1772','10.59543/jidmis.v3.2227','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',89),
(2269,2269,1,'Pre-Balancing Causal Framework for Enterprise Analytical Data Pipelines: Benchmarking Financial Planning Models for Investment Decision Systems','<p>For large scale enterprises, the analytical data pipelines are essential for their day-to-day financial planning, forecasting and investment decision systems. These pipelines can undergo changes in upstream variables, pipeline architecture, technology stacks, database schemas and the logic used to transform data, all of which can cause variations in downstream performance that are challenging to pinpoint with traditional benchmarking techniques. This research suggests a Pre-Balancing Causal Framework (PBCF) that can be used to measure the effects of these changes in enterprise analytical data pipelines. The framework focuses on defining a collection of pre-treatment variables that affect the performance of pipelines, such as workload properties, data size, processing complexity, infrastructure situations, and operational context. These differences between the different treatment and control conditions are then evened out by history or by experiment prior to comparing different pipeline configurations. This pre-balancing step ensures that benchmark groups are more comparable and contributes to the robustness of causal estimation of performance impacts of a pipeline change of interest. The study compares several financial planning pipelines in a large-scale enterprise setting, and measures their performance in terms of execution time, processing latency, resource usage, reliability, data freshness, and downstream model performance. The proposed methodology helps isolate the impact of pipeline changes from other factors that might affect the pipeline''s performance, such as operating conditions and other variables. The outcomes show that pre-balanced causal benchmarking can help to enhance the reliability and evidence-based optimization of enterprise financial data systems and investment decision infrastructure.</p>','["Causal Inference, Data Pipelines, Financial Planning, Benchmarking, Enterprise Systems"]',NULL,22,1,'1773-1792','10.59543/jidmis.v3.2269','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-15',90),
(2337,2337,1,'Impact of Organizational Culture and Marketing Communication Mix on Marketing Performance: Human Resource Competence as an Intervening Variable','<p>The objective of this study is to examine the impact of organizational culture and marketing communication mix on the performance of marketing in the hotel industry, where human resource (HR) competence plays the role of the intervening variable. Because of stiff competition in the hotel industry, there is need for management to make best use of internal synergy and communication to improve business performance. An explanatory research design has been used which involved the distribution of questionnaires among 150 respondents who include middle level managers and marketing professionals from star rated hotels in the Greater Bandung area. Analysis of data was done using Structural Equation Modeling-Partial Least Squares (SEM-PLS). From the above results, it is evident that there is a significant positive effect of organizational culture and marketing communication mix on HR competence. In addition, organizational culture, marketing communication mix, and HR competence significantly influenced the performance of marketing. According to path analysis, HR competence was revealed to have a significant mediating effect in terms of organizational culture and marketing communication mix on hotel marketing performance. The findings above therefore show that hotel marketing strategies do not only rely on external promotional activities, but they also depend on a robust work culture and development of HR capabilities that meet market demands.</p>','["Culture Organization", "Mix Marketing Communication", "HR Competence", "Marketing Performance", "Hospitality."]',NULL,22,1,'1793 - 1809',NULL,'The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-15',91),
(2451,2451,1,'Developing a Risk-Based Counterparty Due Diligence Framework for Pertamina’s Crude Sector: An Integrated Fuzzy AHP–TOPSIS Approach','<p>This paper develops and applies an integrated risk-based counterparty due diligence framework for Pertamina’s crude segment, combining a structured questionnaire with the Fuzzy Analytical Hierarchy Process (Fuzzy AHP) and the Technique for Order of Preference by Similarity to Ideal Solution (TOPSIS). The framework operationalizes four risk domains, legal compliance, corporate image, operational performance, and financial management, by converting qualitative judgments from experts and counterparties into probability–impact scores and normalized risk indices. Expert judgements gathered via a Focus Group Discussion are modeled using Fuzzy AHP to derive uncertainty-aware weights that emphasize operational (0.45) and legal compliance (0.30) risks, with financial management (0.20) and corporate image (0.05) playing supporting roles. These weights are applied to indicator-level indices from five crude counterparties (CRD-01 to CRD-05), which together account for approximately 40 per cent of Pertamina’s crude import volume, to obtain composite risk scores, which are then processed using TOPSIS to compute closeness coefficients and produce a transparent ranking relative to an ideal low-risk profile. The empirical results show that all assessed counterparties pass minimum compliance screening and fall within the Low to Moderate risk band, with composite risk scores ranging from 25.66 to 36.84 and financial management emerging as the dominant source of residual exposure, followed by operational risk. A divergence between the weight experts assign to operational risk and the empirical dominance of financial exposure is identified and interpreted as a perception–exposure gap in expert risk judgment. Comparative and robustness analysis further indicates that the discriminatory power of the framework derives principally from multi-criteria assessment rather than from the specific weights applied, and that the identification of the least and most exposed counterparties is stable under substantial weight perturbation. The resulting Counterparty Risk Ratings provide a direct link to Pertamina’s risk appetite, enabling differentiated decision rules such as standard approval, conditional approval with enhanced covenants, or restricted engagement. The study demonstrates that integrating Fuzzy AHP and TOPSIS into counterparty due diligence can enhance the rigor, transparency, and defensibility of crude supplier evaluations, while also highlighting limitations related to sample size, reliance on subjective judgments, and opportunities for incorporating longitudinal and more objective performance data in future applications.</p>','["Counterparty due diligence", "third-party risk management", "crude oil", "Fuzzy AHP", "TOPSIS", "Risk-Based Assessment", "multi-criteria decision analysis."]',NULL,22,1,'1810 - 1837',NULL,'The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-15',92),
(2452,2452,1,'Utilizing Asset Readiness Metrics to Align Operations and Maintenance in Modern Refineries','<p>Today''s modern refineries are capital intensive, highly integrated facilities, and in these systems reliability and safety are tightly coupled because the failure of one critical asset can quickly cascade into wider production losses and increased process safety risk; however, traditional reliability metrics (e.g., availability, mean time between failure) are primarily rearward facing, telling us how assets have performed but not whether those assets and their supporting systems are ready to meet future demands, and this paper introduces Asset Readiness as a leading and integrated metric for aligning Operations and Maintenance around common reliability outcomes, and Asset Readiness is defined across three interconnected dimensions: Operational Readiness, Maintenance Readiness, and Equipment Readiness, together these dimensions address not only the physical condition of equipment but also the people, procedures, information, and logistical support needed to operate safely and reliably, and the proposed framework synthesizes key reliability factors including integrity operating windows, management of change, operator care routines, maintenance strategy, execution discipline, spare parts readiness, condition monitoring, and safeguarding layers. These factors are organized into a clear scoring structure, allowing gaps to be identified and compared across functions, and a weighted aggregation method is used to account for asset criticality, while a separate confidence score captures the completeness and quality of auditable evidence to support the assessment; by combining readiness scores with confidence levels, the framework provides greater visibility of cross-functional weaknesses and enables prioritization of improvement actions on the areas of highest risk exposure, and preliminary application of the framework across the rotating, static, and instrument/electrical asset classes at company refineries has produced Asset Readiness scores of 81 85% for rotating equipment, whereas assessments of static assets have revealed significant data gaps in piping and heat exchanger documentation that were not visible under the existing maintenance focused organizational standard, therefore Asset Readiness offers a practical way to translate disconnected operational and maintenance information into a shared reliability metric, supporting stronger governance, better cross-functional coordination, and more proactive risk control in the modern refinery.</p>','["Asset Readiness Index, Operational Readiness, Maintenance Readiness, Process Safety, Composite Indicators, Refinery Reliability, Confidence Level"]',NULL,22,1,'1838 - 1861',NULL,'The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-15',93),
(2453,2453,1,'A Multi-Vector Approach to Quantifying Asset Readiness in the Oil and Gas Industry','<p>The Oil and Gas Industry is reliant on high-value complex assets that require leading indicators that can reveal operational vulnerabilities before they result in failure, but conventional lagging measures such as availability and mean time between failure are helpful for looking back on past performance, although they do not fully articulate if the assets are properly positioned to meet changing operating demands safely and reliably. This paper introduces a multi-vector approach for measuring Asset Readiness as a more encompassing metric of an asset''s ability to perform under both normal and stressed conditions, because it defines Asset Readiness as the sum of Operational Readiness, Maintenance Readiness, and Equipment Readiness. These three subcategories are further decomposed into reliability-focused vectors such as process controls and governance, human performance and surveillance, maintenance strategy and execution, technical capability and diagnostics, maintenance skills and condition monitoring through basic equipment care, and risk barriers, which include safeguards and asset health scoring. The framework uses a weighted scoring approach to reflect the criticality of each component, based on a practical 1-5 scale tied to process safety impact, production continuity, and asset integrity, and to make the results more auditable and less subjective, the framework also includes a confidence level based on the availability, quality, and completeness of supporting evidence, such as procedures, records, plans, and technical documentation. The use of both a readiness score and a confidence level enables decision makers to differentiate between real gaps in performance and gaps that are due to missing data or insufficient documentation, which facilitates more informed prioritization of interventions, and the monitoring of progress over time. Consequently, the metric offers a systematic and practical means for combining technical, organizational, and procedural factors into an integrated view of asset readiness.</p>','["asset readiness, leading indicators, reliability engineering"]',NULL,22,1,'1862 - 1886',NULL,'The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-15',94),
(2454,2454,1,'Agentic Explainable AI Framework for Intelligent Clinical Decision Support Using Large Language Models and Multimodal Healthcare Data','<p>The swift expansion of multimodal health data, such as EHRs, medical images, lab reports, physiological signals, and clinical notes, has opened up many avenues for intelligent clinical decision support systems. However, the traditional AI models are generally considered to be black-box systems, which does not equate to clinician’s confidence and ease of use in real-world health care situations. Therefore, this study presents an Agentic Explainable Artificial Intelligence (AXAI) Framework for intelligent clinical decision support by integrating Large Language Models (LLMs), multimodal healthcare data, and autonomous AI agents. The presented framework uses specialized agents intended for data collection, data processing, multimodal feature extraction, explainability generation, and validation of decisions. Structured clinical information is combined with unstructured texts and imaging information to obtain a detailed description the patients. Multimodal fusion is used to combine structured clinical data with unstructured medical text and images. This is done to ensure a full representation of the patient. The reasoning agents use LLMs to give diagnostic suggestions and treatment recommendations based on the available clinical guidelines. Explainable AI methods to provide feature attribution, reasoning based on facts, measurement of confidence, and natural language explanations backing recommendations have been added to the framework to increase reliability and trust of clinicians. In addition, there is an autonomous validation entity responsible for assessing consistency of the decisions-making process, identifying contradictory evidence, and allowing human verification of the system before making recommendations. Experimental tests show that the suggested framework produces better diagnostic accuracy, interpretability, robustness, and decision consistency than traditional clinical decision systems based on machine learning and deep learning. The agentic design also allows for more adaptation to different healthcare scenarios while cutting down on diagnostic uncertainty through the cooperative reasoning of different intelligent agents. The proposed framework introduces a reliable, scalable, and transparent method for innovative intelligent healthcare systems by employing multimodal learning, explainable AI, and autonomous decision-making with LLM to aid healthcare professionals in providing effective, personalized healthcare for patients.</p>','["Explainable AI, Human health, LLM, Multimodal health data, intelligent clinical decision"]',NULL,22,1,'1887 - 1897','10.17762/jidmis.v3.2454','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-15',95),
(2694,2694,1,'Board Size and Investment Inefficiency: Evidence from Financing Constraints and Within- and Between-Firm Differences in Chinese Firms','<p>Using a panel of 34,802 firm-year observations for non-financial A-share firms listed in Shanghai and Shenzhen from 2006 to 2024, this study examines board size, financing constraints, and investment inefficiency with particular attention to within-firm versus between-firm variation and measurement sensitivity in financing-constraint proxies. The baseline fixed-effects estimates show no stable direct association between board size and aggregate investment efficiency. A Mundlak within-between decomposition reveals a pronounced level distinction: firms with larger long-run average boards exhibit significantly lower financing constraints, whereas within-firm board-size changes do not generate a stable reduction in financing constraints. The equality of the within- and between-firm coefficients is strongly rejected. When investment deviations are separated into overinvestment and underinvestment, the primary FC measure in the fully controlled model yields no significant asymmetry, while KZ, WW, and SA measures produce materially different patterns. Firm-cluster bootstrap tests provide only conditional evidence of an indirect financing channel, and industry heterogeneity is limited. The findings suggest that the governance meaning of board size depends on the level of analysis and that empirical conclusions about financing frictions are sensitive to proxy choice. The study therefore highlights the importance of distinguishing persistent governance structures from within-firm adjustments and of treating financing-constraint measurement as a substantive construct-validity issue rather than a mechanical robustness exercise.</p>','["Board size, Financing constraints, Investment inefficiency, Within-between differences, Measurement sensitivity, China."]',NULL,22,1,'1898 - 1922',NULL,'The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-15',96);
INSERT INTO publications (id,submission_id,version,title,abstract,keywords,references_text,issue_id,section_id,pages,doi,copyright_holder,copyright_year,license_url,status,date_published,seq) VALUES
(2695,2695,1,'An Intelligent Automated Decision Support System for Diabetic Retinopathy Detection Using Deep Learning.','<p>Diabetic Retinopathy (DR) is a progressive consequence of diabetes and one of the world''s leading causes of preventable blindness. Early detection is essential to preventing irreversible vision loss, but manual diagnosis from retinal fundus images is time-consuming and requires specialized medical knowledge. This paper presents a real-time automatic DR detection method using a DenseNet201 deep learning model with mobile-based retinal imaging with a 20D lens. By using transfer learning to efficiently extract deep visual features, the model correctly classifies retinal images into five DR stages: No DR, Mild, Moderate, Severe, and Proliferative DR. Ben Graham preprocessing, which includes augmentation and normalization, is applied to mobile imaging-captured fundus images in order to enhance resilience, balance the dataset, and improve clarity. The trained DenseNet201 model is deployed via a Flask-based web application, enabling healthcare practitioners to upload retinal pictures and immediately obtain diagnostic predictions with percentage-based confidence levels. Additionally, it gives each DR class''s occurrence percentage. This inexpensive, portable technology is particularly helpful in healthcare settings with limited resources and in rural locations where access to state-of-the-art ophthalmic equipment is restricted since it facilitates the diagnosis of DR and extensive population screening..</p>','["Diabetic Retinopathy (DR), DenseNet201, Deep Learning, Transfer Learning, Fundus Imaging, Flask Web Application, Ben Graham Preprocessing, Grad-CAM, 20D Lens, Real-Time Detection"]',NULL,22,1,'1923 - 1933',NULL,'The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-15',97),
(2880,2880,1,'Development of A Digital Twin for Early Autism Spectrum Disorder Detection and Stage Classification from Multimodal Data','<p>Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) is a neurodevelopmental condition influenced by genetic, biological, and environmental factors. Early detection of ASD is crucial for effective intervention and improved developmental outcomes. However, existing studies primarily focus on ASD detection and lack emphasis on stage-wise identification and continuous progression tracking. This paper proposes a digital twin-based framework for early ASD detection and stage classification using multimodal data. A novel Sobel Switching Median (SobSMedi) filter and an improved image fusion strategy are introduced to enhance MRI feature representation. The framework operates in two phases: (i) TriSpec clustering for ASD possibility detection and (ii) a Genetic Algorithm-based Recurrent Neural Network (Gen-RNN) for confirmation. The digital twin enables continuous monitoring and updating of a child’s behavioral and neurological state. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed model achieves 97% accuracy in ASD detection, while the digital twin-based four-stage classification achieves 99.1% accuracy. The framework provides a promising solution for early diagnosis, stage classification, and real-time monitoring of ASD progression.</p>','["autism spectrum disorder, digital twin ,early diagnosis, multimodal data, stage detection, MRI, machine learning (ML), deep learning (DL), and trivial-spectral clustering (TriSpec Clustering)"]',NULL,22,1,'1934 - 1967','10.17762/jidmis.v3.2880','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-20',98),
(2943,2943,1,'Green Knowledge Sharing as a Mediating Mechanism: The Effects of Green HRM and Environmental Awareness on Environmental Performance with Green Transformational Leadership as a Moderator','<p>This study examines the effect of green human resource management (GHRM) and environmental awareness on environmental performance, with green knowledge sharing (GKS) as a mediating variable and green transformational leadership (GTL) as a moderating variable among star-rated hotel employees in five tourism regions of East Java, Indonesia. Using a quantitative approach and cross-sectional survey design, data from 271 valid respondents selected via multistage purposive sampling were analyzed. PLS-SEM analysis supported all six hypotheses. GHRM and environmental awareness positively affect GKS, which in turn enhances environmental performance. GKS successfully mediates both relationships, while GTL moderates the GHRM-GKS link, strengthening the positive effect when GTL is high. This research contributes to green management literature by developing an integrated framework demonstrating the dual role of GKS and confirming GTL as a boundary condition. Methodologically, using multidimensional measures of environmental performance spanning management, operational, and strategic aspects provides analytical advantages over aggregate measures. Ultimately, by focusing on East Java, this study offers novel empirical evidence from an under-researched hospitality context, extending green literature beyond Western and East Asian regions. These findings offer practical insights for hospitality managers seeking to enhance ecological practices.</p>','["Green human resource management", "environmental awareness, green knowledge sharing", "green transformational leadership", "environmental performance", "hospitality industry", "Indonesia"]',NULL,22,1,'1968 - 1987',NULL,'The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-15',99),
(3278,3278,1,'Predictive HR Analytics for Employee Retention and Financial Sustainability in Indian MSMEs: Integrating Employee Work Experience Quality, Binary Logistic Regression and Decision Tree Analysis','<p>Employee turnover is a vital challenge faced by MSMEs in India; it has an adverse affect on organizational productivity, continuity and long-term sustainability of business. This study researches the influence of variables Employee Work Experience Quality, Perceived Regulatory Support, Supervisor Support, and Digital Adoption on Employee Turnover Intention among employees working in MSMEs in India. A quantitative research design was implemented on a primary data collected from 110 employees using a structured questionnaire. Cronbach''s alpha and exploratory factor analysis were used to establish the reliability and validity of the measurement scales. To identify the significant predictors of employee turnover intention Binary Logistic Regression was employed. To evaluate predictor importance and enhance the predictive capability of the model, Decision Tree Analysis was employed. The findings show that Employee Work Experience Quality is the strongest predictor of employee turnover intention, while Supervisor Support, Perceived Regulatory Support, and Digital Adoption exhibited weaker predictive effects on employees'' intentions to remain with or leave their organizations. The Decision Tree model further demonstrated the predominance of Employee Work Experience Quality as the most effective classification variable, highlighting its importance in employee retention strategies. The findings supports to the growing field of HR analytics by indicating the applicability of predictive techniques for employee turnover analysis in MSME settings. This study presents practical insights for small scale business owners and HR practitioners to design evidence-based framework aimed at strengthen employees'' work experiences, organisational commitment and reducing turnover intention. Future research should expand the model by incorporating additional behavioural and organisational variables and comparing advanced machine learning algorithms to improve predictive accuracy, model robustness and generalise across different organisational contexts.</p>','["Employee Work Experience Quality, Employee Turnover Intention, Binary Logistic Regression, Decision Tree Analysis, HR Analytics, MSMEs in India"]',NULL,22,1,'1988 - 2000','10.17762/jidmis.v3.3278','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-15',100),
(3284,3284,1,'Beyond Course Administration: A Documentary Case Review of Learning Management System Utilization-Supported Teaching at Universitas Kristen Indonesia','<p>Learning management systems (LMS) can support both routine course administration and richer forms of active learning, but institutional claims about utilization are often stronger than the available evidence. This study examines how the UKI LMS is represented and pedagogically positioned at Universitas Kristen Indonesia (UKI). A qualitative documentary case-review design was applied to a fixed corpus of eight user-nominated sources: an instructor LMS tutorial, an official institutional video channel, two recorded lectures, two teaching slide decks, a peer-reviewed LMS benchmark article, and a curriculum monitoring and supervision article. Directed content analysis coded explicitly demonstrated platform functions, active-learning design elements, and governance requirements. Claims were classified as directly observed, triangulated interpretations, not evidenced, or recommendations. The instructor tutorial explicitly demonstrated 18 distinct functions: course administration (7), content and resource delivery (2), communication (1), and assignment management (8). No reporting or learning-analytics function was demonstrated. The inquiry and problem-based learning materials showed a strong conceptual fit with topic structuring, multimodal resources, forums, and controlled assignment submission; however, the corpus did not verify student participation, feedback quality, satisfaction, achievement, or causal learning effects. Cross-disciplinary lecture artifacts indicated institutional capacity to produce and circulate digital learning content in education and health-law contexts. The main implication is that UKI has documented functional foundations for LMS-supported learning, while the next maturity step is evidence-governed pedagogical orchestration: standard course design patterns, instructor support, accessible content, and privacy-conscious engagement analytics. The study offers an auditable case-review method and a measurement roadmap without fabricating primary data.</p>','["learning management system", "higher education", "documentary analysis", "active learning", "inquiry learning"]',NULL,22,1,'2001 - 2015',NULL,'The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-15',101),
(3290,3290,1,'EM3F: An Emotion-Aware Multi-modal Multi-lingual Movie Trailer Generation Framework','<p>A movie trailer highlights key scenes, characters, and themes to generate excitement and attract audience interest before release. The existing studies have not sufficiently addressed the unified preservation of diverse cinematic content, such as comedy, romance, action, and sentiment, within a single automated trailer-generation framework. So, this research proposes an emotion-aware multi-modal multi-lingual deep learning framework for movie trailer generation. Firstly, the input movie is taken, and frames are extracted through which the keyframes are selected. Emotions are then identified using trained SLDCNN system. Similar emotion frames are grouped by clustering using LRBDSS-KM, and features are extracted. Simultaneously, texts and audio are extracted from the input movie, and a language dictionary is constructed using BERT. Pre-processing is performed for the extracted texts followed by feature extraction. From the extracted audio, features are extracted. Dimensionality reduction is applied using CWPCA for all extracted features from the clustered frames, pre-processed texts, and audio. The turning point is identified using CSELU-DLSTM with correlation. Next, the best-quality shot is selected using OLGRO. Finally, the frames are rearranged, and the final trailer is generated. The proposed model achieved an accuracy 98.44821954% and 98.65568083% in emotion detection using SLDCNN and turning point identification using CSELU-DLSTM respectively. Then, LRBDSS-KM was used to cluster frames with a clustering time of 3000ms and dimensionality reduction was applied to all extracted features using CWPCA, with an EVR of 0.7913. Then, with a shot selection time of 8479ms, important shots were selected.</p>','["Correlation Weighted Principal Component Analysis (CWPCA)", "Logistic Regression Bernoulli Distribution with Sorensen Similarity-KMeans (LRBDSS-KM)", "Opposition Learned Gold Rush Optimization (OLGRO)", "Spatial Layered Deep Convolutional Neural Network (SLDCNN)", "Chaotic with Scaled Exponential Linear Unit-based Deep Long Short Term Memory (CSELU-DLSTM)", "Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers (BERT)", "Movie Trailer System"]',NULL,22,1,'2016 - 2042','10.17762/jidmis.v3.3290','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-15',102),
(3384,3384,1,'Interactive Learning Media for Enhancing Peer Interaction in Elementary Science','<p>This study examines the limited use of interactive media to facilitate peer interaction in elementary science classrooms, which often results in low student engagement and suboptimal academic achievement. Grounded in an interpersonal learning perspective, this research aims to develop and evaluate interactive science learning media using Adobe Animate to strengthen peer interaction and improve learning outcomes among Grade V students. The study employed a Research and Development (R&amp;D) approach based on the ADDIE model, encompassing analysis, design, development, implementation, and evaluation stages. The participants were Grade V elementary school students, and data were collected through observation sheets to assess peer interaction, science achievement tests, expert validation instruments, and student response questionnaires. The results indicated that the developed media met validity criteria in terms of content, design, and usability. Implementation findings showed a significant improvement in students'' peer interaction, reflected in increased collaboration, communication, and active participation during learning activities. In addition, students'' science learning achievement improved, as evidenced by higher posttest scores and positive N-gain results. Student responses also revealed high levels of engagement, motivation, and ease of use when interacting with the media. In conclusion, interactive learning media created in Adobe Animate are effective in fostering interpersonal interaction and enhancing science learning outcomes in elementary classrooms. These findings highlight the importance of integrating interactive and socially engaging digital tools to support meaningful learning experiences.</p>','["Interactive media", "Adobe Animate", "science achievement", "elementary education"]',NULL,22,1,'2043 - 2054',NULL,'The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-15',103),
(3448,3448,1,'Comparison of Social Attitude, Self-Confidence, and Problem-Solving Ability Using The Reciprocal Learning Model Assisted by Hypnoteaching with Conventional Learning at Iakn Tarutung','<p>This study aimed to analyze differences in students’ social attitudes, self-confidence, and problem-solving abilities between students taught using Reciprocal Learning assisted by Hypnoteaching and those taught using conventional learning. The study employed an experimental approach involving fourth-semester students of the Undergraduate Christian Educational Management Study Program at IAKN Tarutung in the 2023/2024 academic year. The population consisted of 61 students in two groups: Group A with 31 students and Group B with 32 students. Group A served as the control class, while Group B served as the experimental class. The intervention was conducted in three meetings of 3 × 50 minutes each, covering Research Methodology topics consisting of descriptive, ex-post facto, and experimental research. Data were collected through social attitude and self-confidence questionnaires and an essay-based problem-solving test. Data analysis included descriptive statistics, normality testing, homogeneity testing, and a two-mean difference test. The results showed that the experimental class had a higher mean social attitude score (84.63) than the control class (76.48), with t = 5.32 &gt; 1.999 and Sig. = 0.000. The mean self-confidence score was 85.47 in the experimental class and 74.94 in the control class, with t = 6.45 &gt; 1.999 and Sig. = 0.000. The mean problem-solving score was 85.91 in the experimental class and 73.81 in the control class, with t = 6.52 &gt; 1.999 and Sig. = 0.000. All datasets met the normality and homogeneity assumptions. Therefore, significant differences were found in all three variables, with higher outcomes in the Reciprocal Learning assisted by Hypnoteaching class.</p>','["Reciprocal Learning, Hypnoteaching, social attitude, self-confidence, problem solving, conventional learning"]',NULL,22,1,'2055 - 2063',NULL,'The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-15',104),
(3475,3475,1,'Improving WSN Performance and ensuring Cyber Trust with Hybrid Optimization Algorithms','<p>Recent research on wireless sensor networks has shown that energy efficiency is a top priority. Sensor networks have a finite lifespan since they are powered by batteries. Consequently, increasing the data dissipation in an energy-efficient manner is becoming an increasingly difficult task to solve the life of sensor devices. This study proposes a safe CH selection method that uses hybrid optimization techniques to determine the weight of each node in order to handle secure selection with minimal energy consumption. The algorithms in discussion are PSO-EHO, PSO-FF, and FF-EHO. An optimization approach known as PSO is used in the PSO: FF algorithm. PSO is based on swarm optimization. For efficient local search, FF incorporates exploitation features. One way to get a better balance between exploration and exploitation is to combine PSO and FF algorithms. A hybridization of PSO with EHO proposes an enhancement to conventional EHO. Additionally, EHO''s worst elephants have a strong PSO characteristic. Decreasing energy consumption and balancing load leads to enhanced network durability, which is the trust with which FF-EHO proposes the routing approach. When compared to Trust PSO-EHO and Trust PSO-FF, the results reveal that Trust FF-EHO has a longer network duration, fewer clusters formed, lower average packet loss rate, and shorter average end-to-end latency. The following core contributions are noted for hybridization in the incremental model:</p>
INSERT INTO publications (id,submission_id,version,title,abstract,keywords,references_text,issue_id,section_id,pages,doi,copyright_holder,copyright_year,license_url,status,date_published,seq) VALUES
(4285,4285,1,'A Study of Cybersecurity Issues and Their Emerging Trends in Modern Technologies','<p>As digital technology has evolved, its influence on and reliance on interconnected systems has grown, exposing people, organisations and governments to cyber threats. This study gives the basic idea of the main cyber security problems and trends in the current technologies. It looks at the nature and rising prevalence of cybercrime, focusing on the rising number of reported cybercrime cases and the demand for increased preventive action. The paper looks at issues of security on web servers, cloud computing, advanced persistent threats, mobile networks, IPv6, as well as data encryption. It also examines some of the most frequently used cyber security methods such as access control, passwords, data authentication, malware scanning, firewall and antivirus software. Moreover, the research highlights the need for cyber ethics and responsible online conduct to mitigate security threats. The results show that technology protection measures are insufficient in combating the continually emerging cyber threats. Being able to defend against cyber security threats effectively means implementing a multi-layered approach that includes advanced security technology, regular system monitoring, user awareness, ethical Internet practices, timely incident reporting and cooperation between individuals, organisations, governments and cyber security professionals.</p>','["Cybersecurity, Cybercrime, Cyber Threats, Emerging Technologies, Cloud Computing, Data Security, Cyber Ethics."]',NULL,22,1,'1796 - 1806',NULL,'The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-15',122),
(1068,1068,1,'Reproducible Performance Evaluation of a Hybrid Hadoop–Spark Big Data Architecture Using Urban Mobility and Global Event Datasets','<p>This study presents a reproducible framework for evaluating the performance of Big Data workflows inspired by Hadoop–Spark processing logic using two open datasets with distinct workload characteristics. The first dataset, the NYC TLC Yellow Taxi Trip Record Data for January 2023, represents an urban mobility scenario and contains temporal and spatial trip attributes. The second dataset, the GDELT Event Daily Export for 1 January 2023, represents global event data with temporal, geographic, categorical, and quantitative characteristics. Three reproducible processing configurations are compared: a disk-based batch baseline, a Spark-only workflow, and a Prepared-storage + Spark workflow, which serves as a storage-oriented approximation of a hybrid Hadoop–Spark processing pipeline. The experimental design includes batch aggregation, spatial–temporal analysis, stream-like processing, scalability testing, and task restart-based fault recovery. Performance is evaluated using execution time, throughput, latency, scalability behavior, CPU utilization, RAM delta, I/O-related behavior, and recovery time metrics. To ensure reproducibility, a unified preprocessing procedure was applied across all configurations. The TLC dataset was reduced from 3,066,766 to 2,883,546 valid records, while the GDELT dataset was reduced from 43,575 to 42,053 valid records. The study does not claim universal superiority of the storage-oriented workflow. Instead, its primary contribution lies in providing a transparent and reproducible methodology for comparing storage-oriented and Spark-based data processing workflows using heterogeneous open datasets under identical preprocessing rules and comparable workload execution conditions.</p>','["Big Data", "Hadoop", "Apache Spark", "HDFS", "hybrid architecture", "performance evaluation", "urban mobility", "GDELT", "reproducible benchmarking", "scalability."]',NULL,21,1,'01 - 41','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1068','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',1),
(1069,1069,1,'Behavioral Segmentation in Digital Payment Platforms Using Temporal-Adaptive Attentive Fuzzy Clustering','<p>Digital payment platforms record a wealth of transaction data, capturing intricate and dynamic patterns of user behavior. Clustering users according to their behavioral patterns presents unique challenges due to overlapping behavior sets, dynamicity, and unlabeled data. In this paper, we propose a temporal-adaptive attentive fuzzy clustering method for behavioral user segmentation in digital payment platforms. We construct a behavioral representation for each user based on transaction records using a temporal decay function to emphasize recent behaviors. An attention mechanism is incorporated into the fuzzy clustering objective to adaptively assess the importance of each behavior feature on the clustering results. We employ a soft assignment approach that allows each user to have varying degrees of membership across multiple clusters to capture the gradual transition of payment behaviors. We evaluate our approach on a publicly available digital wallet transaction dataset. Our results show that the proposed clustering method achieves better clustering quality and temporal consistency compared to traditional clustering algorithms. The experimental results show that incorporating temporal adaptivity and attention provides a promising and effective basis for behavioral clustering in digital payment platforms.</p>','["Behavioral segmentation", "digital payments", "fuzzy clustering", "temporal decay", "attention mechanism", "unsupervised learning."]',NULL,21,1,'42 - 52','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1069','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',2),
(1070,1070,1,'An Adaptive Cybersecurity Framework Integrating Machine Learning, Zero Trust Policy, and Blockchain for Academic Cloud Environments','<p>Cloud-based academic environments such as Learning Management Systems (LMS), Open Journal Systems (OJS), institutional repositories, and web applications face increasing cybersecurity challenges due to heterogeneous users, distributed services, and extensive exposure to public networks. Existing security approaches remain fragmented, where machine learning focuses on threat detection, Zero Trust Architecture (ZTA) emphasizes access control, and blockchain is primarily used for secure logging. The lack of integration among these components limits the ability of security systems to adapt dynamically to evolving cyber threats. This study proposes an Adaptive Cybersecurity Framework (ACF) that integrates unsupervised machine learning-based anomaly detection, a risk-based Zero Trust Policy Engine, and blockchain-based immutable audit logging within a continuous adaptive feedback loop. The framework was evaluated using 450,000 anonymized HTTP and Web Application Firewall (WAF) events collected from a multi-domain academic cloud environment consisting of LMS, OJS, repositories, and supporting web applications. The analysis revealed structured and repetitive attack behaviors dominated by automated endpoint probing and cross-domain propagation patterns, indicating ecosystem-level security threats. The proposed risk assessment mechanism demonstrated effective alignment between anomaly detection and policy-based decision making. Experimental results achieved an AUROC of 0.7296 for risk-based threat detection while maintaining an average decision latency of approximately 11 ms, indicating suitability for real-time deployment. Blockchain integration further provided verifiable, tamper-resistant audit trails for mitigation actions and policy enforcement activities. This study contributes an ecosystem-aware adaptive cybersecurity paradigm that bridges threat detection, policy enforcement, and auditability through a unified security architecture for Academic Cloud Environments.</p>','["cybersecurity", "machine learning", "zero trust", "blockchain", "academic cloud", "anomaly detection."]',NULL,21,1,'53 - 93','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1070','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',3),
(1071,1071,1,'A Secure and Scalable Post-Quantum Cross-Blockchain Data Exchange Model Based on Hierarchical Attribute Encryption','<p>The exponential emergence of cross-chain data sharing in blockchain-enabled IoT and cloud systems creates vital challenges in the scalability, privacy, and post-quantum security landscapes. To tackle these problems, we propose a hierarchical attribute clustering-attribute-based encryption (HAC-ABE) scheme in this paper, which offers a secure post-quantum cross-blockchain data exchange framework. The method utilizes hierarchical attribute clustering and lattice-based encryption to reduce the computation overhead while supporting fine-grained access control. It utilizes IPFS decentralized storage and smart contracts to achieve a transparent data exchange across chains. Experimental results show 9.7% faster computation time and much lower communication overheads than state-of-the-art ABE-based approaches, verifying its effectiveness and scalability for practical decentralized environments.</p>','["HAC-ABE, Post-Quantum Security, Cross-Blockchain, Attribute-Based Encryption, IPFS, Smart Contracts, Data Privacy."]',NULL,21,1,'94 - 117','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1071','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',4),
(1072,1072,1,'Energy-Efficient and Secure Anomaly Detection Using Neural Auto Encoder In Wireless Sensor Networks','<p>The study proposes an energy-efficient and secure anomaly detection framework for wireless sensor networks with a lightweight neural auto encoder that enhances precision and accuracy. A five-layer auto encoder with Mean Absolute Error (MAE) reconstruction loss and optimization, input normalization, and anomaly detection using reconstruction error thresholding was implemented. The neural auto encoder was lightweight and used to identify WSN anomalies based on reconstruction error thresholding. The performance was evaluated in terms of false alarm rate, energy consumption, latency, and network lifetime. The presented neural auto encoder had a high accuracy of 98.36%, reduced false alarms, reduced energy consumption, and enhanced detection reliability. The improvement was statistically significant with p = 0.001. The lightweight auto encoder guarantees secure anomaly detection, low power consumption, adaptive thresholding, reduced overhead, extended network lifetime, and improves the overall performance of wireless sensor networks.</p>','["Five-Layer Deep auto encoder: false alarm: Anomaly detection: Internet of Things: Machine learning.."]',NULL,21,1,'118 - 130','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1072','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',5),
(1073,1073,1,'Designing a Scalable and Adaptive Cyber Incident Response (DACIR) Framework for SMEs','<p>Small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs), due to resource constraints, lack optimal decision-making mechanisms to respond to cyber incidents. Common frameworks model the process as linear cycles and lack explicit decision-making under uncertainty. This paper presents an optimized framework as a partially observable Markov decision process (POMDP), in which the system''s true state is not fully observable and the decision-maker must act based on a belief distribution. Risk is dynamically estimated on the asset dependency graph, and action selection is performed by minimizing expected loss, cost, downtime, and uncertainty penalty. The proposed framework (DACIR) provides lightweight, executable orchestration for SMEs using micro-playbooks and updates policy parameters through post-event learning. Simulation-based evaluation shows that the proposed method, compared to static procedures, achieves significant improvement in metrics such as MTTR, business loss, and regret.</p>','["Incident Response, SMEs, Optimized Framework, Risk, Cyber Attacks"]',NULL,21,1,'131 - 141','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1073','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',6),
(1074,1074,1,'Artificial Intelligence as an Educational Decision Support System in Problem-Based Learning: Critical Thinking, Decision-Making, and Human–AI Collaboration in Higher Education','<p>Artificial Intelligence (AI) is increasingly being integrated into higher education to support learning, problem solving, and decision making. Despite growing interest in generative AI technologies, limited empirical evidence exists regarding the use of AI as an Educational Decision Support System (EDSS) within Problem-Based Learning (PBL) environments. This study examined the association between AI-supported PBL and students’ critical thinking competencies, decision-making competencies, and perceptions of Human–AI collaboration in higher education. A mixed-methods quasi-experimental design employing a one-group pretest–posttest approach was adopted. The study involved 32 undergraduate students enrolled in an Optimization Analysis course at a public university in Thailand. Over an eight-week intervention, ChatGPT was integrated as a decision-support partner to assist students in information analysis, alternative generation, evidence evaluation, and decision justification during problem-solving activities. Quantitative data were collected using critical thinking and decision-making competency assessments and analyzed using descriptive statistics and paired-samples t-tests. Qualitative data were obtained through semi-structured interviews and analyzed using thematic analysis. The findings indicated statistically significant improvements in critical thinking competencies (t = 4.16, p = .001, d = 0.61) and decision-making competencies (t = 3.92, p = .001, d = 0.56). Students also reported positive perceptions of AI-supported decision making, particularly in information analysis, evidence evaluation, alternative exploration, and decision justification. Qualitative findings identified four interconnected Human–AI decision-making processes: information analysis, alternative generation, critical evaluation, and evidence-based decision making. The study contributes to the emerging literature on Educational Decision Support Systems by providing evidence that AI-supported Problem-Based Learning may serve as a promising pedagogical approach for supporting higher-order cognitive competencies and responsible Human–AI collaboration in higher education. The findings offer theoretical and practical implications for the design of AI-enhanced learning environments that promote evidence-based reasoning while preserving human judgment and accountability.</p>','["Artificial Intelligence", "Decision Support Systems", "Problem-Based Learning", "Human\\u2013AI Collaboration", "Critical Thinking", "Decision-Making Competency."]',NULL,21,1,'142 - 167','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1074','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',7),
(1075,1075,1,'Intelligent Decision Making for Digital Equity: A Data-Driven Legal and Business Analysis of the UAE Metaverse Ecosystem','<p>This research adopts a critical lens to explore the overlay of commercial innovation and legal regulation in nascent Metaverse manifestations in the UAE. Even as efforts like the Dubai Metaverse Strategy enhance the U.A.E.’s claim to digital leadership, questions remain about inclusive participation and the rule of law. Building on the Capability Approach, Human Capital Theory, and Network Economics, it discusses how all types of virtual economies are influenced by income, education, and digital skills. Mixed-method research involving 200 participants shows that digital literacy, income, and perceived benefit are all strongly related and positively associated (R² = 0.59, p &lt; 0.001), but inequality prevents participation. These findings highlight the importance of a comprehensive approach to governance and regulation of VA and a developed business law system (i.e., IP, contracts, data protection). “Providing affordable XR access for more targeted literacy programmes and an inclusive policy to support universal Metaverse engagement would be an action of restoring digital justice and the sustainability of innovation for the creative ambitions of the UAE.</p>','["Capability Approach", "Digital Inequality", "Human Capital Theory", "Metaverse", "UAE Digital Strategy", "Virtual Economy."]',NULL,21,1,'168 - 186','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1075','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',8),
(1076,1076,1,'Digital Promotional Practices and Consumer Buying Behaviour in Fruit-Based Beverages Retailing: A Conceptual Framework and Systematic Literature Review','<p>The fruit-based beverage (FBB) segment is a fast-growing sector of the non-alcoholic beverage market, as driven by the soaring consumer demand in the market. Online promotional cues are very important in assessing the authenticity, nutritional value and brand credibility of the product in digitally mediated retail settings, which makes digital marketing a particularly effective influence in this category. Nevertheless, the current literature on digital promotional practices is not unified, but rather fragmented and often generalized across the food and FMCG industries, without a focused and theory-integrated understanding of consumer buying behaviour in the specific context of fruit-based beverages. To fill this gap, the current study is a systematic literature review, with evidence synthesized based on the PRISMA 2020 guidelines, including 47 peer-reviewed studies. The results indicate that the digital promotional practices, such as social media marketing, influencer marketing, content marketing, and electronic word-of-mouth, are interactive stimuli of retail service, which influence consumer decision making through thinking and feeling processes. Attitudes, brand trust, perceived usefulness, and emotional engagement are the key mediating constructs, with brand trust appearing to be especially crucial in the context of health-sensitive beverages. Based on these insights, the study constructs a sector-specific conceptual framework built around the Stimulus-Organism-Response (SOR) model, which is supported by the Theory of Planned Behaviour (TPB), Technology Acceptance Model (TAM) and Hierarchy of Effects Model (HEM). The research will make its contribution to the multi-theoretical analysis of digital consumer behaviour, which is context-specific, and present practical implications to marketers and policymakers.</p>','["Digital promotion", "Consumer behaviour", "Fruit-based beverages", "Retail service experience", "Systematic literature review", "SOR model."]',NULL,21,1,'187 - 204','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1076','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',9),
(1077,1077,1,'Deep Learning for Scholarship Allocation: Hybrid CNN-FFNN Architecture','<p>In this paper, we explore the application of different neural network architectures to a classification problem related to the allocation of graduate scholarships. Classical predictive models face limitations when it comes to simultaneously capturing the multidimensional complexity of data and managing class imbalance. We use classical models and artificial neural networks. We compare performance of classical models and artificial neural networks applied to predicting student eligibility for scholarships, in order to identify the most robust model best suited to the context. This comparison makes it possible to evaluate the suitability of different neural architectures for a hybrid dataset. To address these challenges, we propose an optimization approach by combining a CNN model, capable of efficiently exploiting sequential data, and a FFNN, adapted to static tabular data. The integration of the SMOTE technique also makes it possible to rebalance the classes, ensuring better robustness and generalizable performance. Experimental results show that this hybrid approach significantly improves the stability and accuracy of predictions compared to traditional models. This research contributes to increasing the accuracy and robustness some neural network model within the framework of an optimized decision support system.</p>','["Feed Forward Neural Network", "Convolutional Neural Network", "Long Short Time Memory", "Scholarships", "Performance", "Optimization."]',NULL,21,1,'205 - 223','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1077','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',10),
(1078,1078,1,'Management Practices of General Secondary Education Principals in Conditions of Uncertainty: the Ukrainian Context','<p>The aim of the study is to research and systematize the management practices of general secondary school principals in Ukraine under conditions of uncertainty, and to identify the key features of a new managerial culture in the field of education. The study applies a quantitative research design based on standardized survey data collected from heads of general secondary education institutions. Empirical information is processed using descriptive statistics and an economic-mathematical modelling framework. Survey responses are operationalized through nominal, ordinal, and binary scales and subsequently transformed into normalized indicators. An integral assessment model is constructed by aggregating partial indices of management style, managerial decisions, and leadership models using a weighted linear approach. The study showed the consolidation of the administrative community around the values of safety, adaptability, trust, partnership, and the adoption of flexible, dialogic management. Priority models of leadership point to a new paradigm of educational management based on human-centeredness, partnership, and ethical responsibility. The profile of a modern educational leader was outlined. The features of effective practices were summarized. Relevant directions for further development of management practices in Ukraine under conditions of uncertainty are identified. The results can contribute to improving management practices, developing professional training programs, and formulating policies aimed at strengthening the resilience and adaptability of educational institutions. The article offers a comprehensive vision of the managerial culture of Ukrainian general secondary education institutions in the context of uncertainty and substantiates conceptual recommendations for its development.</p>','["General Secondary Education Institutions", "Uncertainty", "Education", "Educational Leadership", "Management Practices", "Managerial Strategies."]',NULL,21,1,'224 - 242','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1078','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',11),
(1079,1079,1,'A Clustering-Based Descriptive Analysis of Urban Road Traffic Accidents','<p>Road traffic accidents are a leading cause of death and disability worldwide, and their burden falls disproportionately on low- and middle-income countries, yet data-driven analyses remain scarce for Sudan. This study presents a descriptive and unsupervised-learning analysis of 2,218 road traffic accidents recorded across the localities of Khartoum State. After cleaning, translating, and feature-engineering the records, an exploratory analysis is conducted through a series of descriptive visualizations spanning the spatial, temporal, vehicular, demographic, and severity dimensions of the data. K-means clustering is then applied within a principal-component space and validated using the elbow method, silhouette analysis, the Calinski–Harabasz and Davies–Bouldin indices, and a hierarchical-clustering cross-check, to derive latent accident typologies. A five-cluster solution separates a dominant high-volume low-severity pedestrian and motorcycle stratum from a smaller but critical high-fatality cluster, a bus-and-collision cluster, and an outlier group, with fatality rates ranging from 1.0% to 99.0%. The analysis reveals that the pedestrian and the afternoon/early evening hours account for almost half of all accidents, and that an interpretable risk segmentation can be derived, with direct consequences for the road-safety policy in Khartoum.</p>','["Road traffic accidents", "descriptive analytics", "K-means clustering", "unsupervised learning", "road data visualization", "safety", "Khartoum", "Sudan."]',NULL,21,1,'243 - 258','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1079','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',12),
(1080,1080,1,'A Hybrid Machine Learning Framework for Predictive Analytics in Large-Scale Information Systems','<p>In today’s world of big data, massive information systems are being targeted by cyber threats which are becoming more and more sophisticated and, therefore, security and operational reliability have become a great challenge. A single, model predictive analytics approach cannot usually detect the complex and non, linear patterns that reveal the nature of heterogeneous network traffic. In this research paper, a new Hybrid Machine Learning Framework (HMLF) is suggested, which combines several base learners such as Decision Trees, Random Forests, Gradient Boosting, and K, Nearest Neighbors using ensemble methods like soft voting and meta-stacking. <br/>By using a thoroughly balanced UNSW, NB15 dataset, which represents various attack vectors in the modern world, the framework applies a multi, stage feature selection and standardization preprocessing pipeline. The experiments confirm that the combination of the proposed methods significantly exceeds the performance of the individual models, with a record of 98.42% accuracy and a high F1-score, while still being time, efficient enough for on, line operations. Moreover, the strength of the suggested model is assured through extensive cross, validation and statistical hypothesis testing, thus it can be regarded as an ultra, modern solution to the problem of predictive analytics in large, scale information environments.</p>','["Hybrid Machine Learning Framework , Predictive Analytics , Network Intrusion Detection , Ensemble Learning , UNSW-NB15 Dataset."]',NULL,21,1,'259 - 268','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1080','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',13),
(1081,1081,1,'From Creative Expression to Psychological Resilience: The Role of Digital Media Art in Mental Health Interventions for International Students in China','<p>This study examined how digital media art engagement supports psychological resilience among international students in China. Digital media art was conceptualized as participation in digital drawing, collage, animation, photography, short video, immersive art, and online creative sharing. A cross-sectional quantitative design was used with 326 international students enrolled in Chinese universities. The model included four reflective constructs: digital media art engagement, emotion regulation through creative expression, intercultural social connectedness, and psychological resilience. Data were analyzed using PLS-SEM with reliability, validity, predictive relevance, mediation, moderation, and bootstrapped path tests. Results showed that digital media art engagement positively predicted psychological resilience and strongly enhanced emotion regulation through creative expression. Emotion regulation mediated the relationship between digital media art engagement and resilience, while intercultural social connectedness directly improved resilience and strengthened the engagement-resilience pathway. The findings suggest that digitally mediated creative expression can support resilience-oriented mental health interventions for international students.</p>','["Digital media art", "art therapy", "emotion regulation", "psychological resilience", "international students", "mental health intervention", "social connectedness."]',NULL,21,1,'269 - 290','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1081','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',14),
(1082,1082,1,'A Deep Learning-Enabled Privacy-Preserving Framework for Secure Medical Image Storage in Cloud Systems Using Advanced Convolutional Architectures','<p>With the rapid adoption of cloud computing for healthcare data storage, ensuring the privacy and security of sensitive medical images has become a critical challenge. Recent advancements in deep learning, particularly convolutional neural networks (CNNs), offer new possibilities for enhancing encryption and data protection without compromising image quality or diagnostic value. This research aims to develop a state-of-the-art, CNN-based privacy-preserving framework that leverages feature extraction and adaptive encryption methods to securely manage medical imaging data in cloud environments, aligning with emerging trends in AI-driven healthcare security and regulatory compliance. With the increasing reliance on cloud-based platforms for storing and sharing medical imaging data, privacy and security concerns have become paramount. Medical images such as CT scans, MRI, and X-rays contain sensitive patient information that must be protected from unauthorized access while ensuring usability for clinical diagnosis. This research proposes a Deep Learning-Enabled Privacy-Preserving Framework that integrates advanced convolutional neural network (CNN) architectures with adaptive encryption techniques to enhance the confidentiality of medical images stored in cloud environments. The framework extracts essential features from images while applying encryption algorithms that maintain data integrity and diagnostic value. Experimental results on diverse medical image datasets demonstrate the efficiency, robustness, and scalability of the proposed approach, making it suitable for modern healthcare applications where secure, cloud-based data management is essential. The proposed method aligns with current trends in artificial intelligence, cybersecurity, and regulatory standards for medical data protection.</p>','["Privacy Protection, Medical Image Security, Cloud Computing, Convolutional Neural Networks (CNN), Data Encryption."]',NULL,21,1,'291 - 313','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1082','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',15),
(1083,1083,1,'RK4-Assisted Computational Modeling of MHD CuO–Water Nanofluid Flow and Thermal Transport over a Porous Stretching Surface','<p>RK4-Assisted Computational Modeling of MHD CuO–Water Nanofluid Flow and Thermal Transport over a Porous Stretching Surface<br/>The present work explores the coupled hydrodynamic, thermal, and nanoparticle transport mechanisms arising in magnetohydrodynamic (MHD) CuO–H₂O nanofluid flow over a porous stretching surface. The formulation is established within Buongiorno’s two-component nanofluid framework, with particular emphasis on the combined contributions of Brownian diffusion and thermophoretic particle migration. By employing suitable similarity transformations, the governing conservation equations for momentum, energy, and nanoparticle concentration are reduced from their original nonlinear form to a coupled system of ordinary differential equations. The resulting nonlinear boundary-value problem is addressed through an efficient shooting strategy in conjunction with the classical fourth-order Runge–Kutta (RK4) integration scheme. The unknown wall gradients corresponding to the velocity, temperature, and nanoparticle concentration fields are estimated through an iterative procedure until the prescribed far-field boundary conditions are satisfied.<br/>The computational investigation examines the sensitivity of the flow and transport characteristics to key dimensionless parameters, particularly the magnetic interaction parameter, Brownian motion parameter, thermophoresis parameter, and Lewis number. The associated wall transport rates, quantified through the skin-friction coefficient, Nusselt number, and Sherwood number, are evaluated numerically and their variations are illustrated using MATLAB-based graphical analysis. To establish the reliability of the proposed computational framework, the numerical predictions are cross-checked against benchmark results reported in the literature and independently verified using the MATLAB BVP4C solver. The results reveal a notable improvement in thermal transport when CuO nanoparticles are suspended in the conventional water-based fluid, highlighting their potential for enhanced heat-transfer applications. Moreover, intensification of the magnetic field produces a damping effect on the fluid motion, leading to a reduction in the velocity boundary-layer thickness, while simultaneously increasing the thermal and nanoparticle concentration boundary-layer thicknesses. These findings provide useful insight into the regulation of coupled momentum, heat, and mass transport in MHD nanofluid systems involving porous stretching surfaces.</p>','["Copper Oxide (CuO)-Water Nanofluid", "Buongiorno Nanofluid Model", "Porous Stretching Surface", "Brownian Motion", "Thermophoresis", "Runge\\u2013Kutta Method", "Shooting Technique", "Heat and Mass Transfer", "MATLAB."]',NULL,21,1,'314 - 325','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1083','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',16),
(1084,1084,1,'A Hyperparameter-Optimized Bi-Directional LSTM Framework for Smart Grid Stability under Integrated Renewable Energy and Electric Vehicle Charging Dynamics','<p>The integration of renewable energy sources (RES) and electric vehicle (EV) management in the smart grids has significantly introduced new challenges, because of the intermittent nature of the RES and stochastic charging behavior. Existing studies highlight that they focus on modeling either the renewable generation or EV charging demand but not focused on a combined approach effect on the smart grid stability. To address this research gap, this study has proposed a Bi-Directional Long Short-Term Memory (Bi-LSTM) for the grid stability classification under the integrated RES-EV dynamics. The research proposes a Hyperparameter-Optimized Bi-Directional Long Short-Term Memory (HO-Bi-LSTM) framework to improve the classification performance and learning efficiency under dynamic grid operating conditions. Furthermore, this research has used the dataset which is obtained from the Kaggle repository, and this dataset comprises nearly 60000 records with the synthetically generated data samples for the EV charging features to simulate the realistic grid conditions. Based on the experimental evaluation, the HO-Bi-LSTM demonstrated significant performance. Accuracy of 96.34%, Precision of 98.27%, Recall of 95.97%, F1-score of 97.11%. The proposed HO-Bi-LSTM framework can provide the better solutions for improved grid resilience and ensuring the efficient energy distribution and supporting the smart grid operations. The HO-Bi-LSTM framework effectively captures bidirectional temporal dependencies in integrated RES-EV dynamics. Ablation studies confirm the value of synthetic features and hyperparameter optimization.</p>','["Smart Grid Stability, Hyperparameter Optimized Bi-Directional Long Short-Term Memory (HO-Bi-LST), Integrated RES-EV Dynamics Synthetically Generated Data, Machine Learning."]',NULL,21,1,'326 - 352','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1084','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',17),
(1085,1085,1,'XRAI: A Deep Learning Approach to Chest Disease Detection','<p>Given the growing challenges in healthcare diagnostics, the proposed research study aims to transform how we identify lung diseases. We aim to achieve this by integrating AI, Large Language Models (LLMs), and Image Processing with medical expertise. The main driving force behind the initiative is the need for more accurate diagnoses. In this regard, the primary focus is on using lung X-ray images to harness healthcare through image processing. Additionally, the research introduces a dimension in which medical specialists can have real-time conversations with a trained LLM model that leverages knowledge from a medical encyclopedia. This interactive feature enhances collaboration between AI and medical professionals, creating a platform for exchanging knowledge. A carefully designed convolutional neural network (CNN) was investigated, achieving an Area Under the Curve-Receiver Operating Characteristic (AUC-ROC) of 90%. By doing so, the study not only aligns with Saudi Arabia''s vision for technological advancement in healthcare by 2030 but also aims to alleviate burdens on the healthcare sector and enhance overall quality of life. The proposed approach represents a collaboration that promises groundbreaking advancements in the diagnostic process.</p>','["Deep Learning, Artificial Intelligence, Object Detection, Large language Model."]',NULL,21,1,'353 - 376','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1085','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',18),
(1087,1087,1,'Camouflaged Object Detection in Multispectral Imagery for Surveillance Applications Using YOLO Variants','<p>The proposed study investigates the efficacy of advanced YOLO (You Only Look Once) variants, specifically YOLOv5, v7, v8, and v9, in detecting concealed camouflaged objects within multispectral imagery. Utilizing the multispectral camera, “Altum PT,” a comprehensive dataset of multispectral images was captured and preprocessed through various techniques, including fusion, false coloring, and pan sharpening. The performance of these YOLO models was evaluated through extensive experiments in various environmental contexts. The results demonstrate that YOLO variants, especially when combined with Slicing Aided Hyper Inference (SAHI), exhibit robust detection capabilities, with precision ranging from 93.9% to 98.2% and recall varying from 80.2% to 98.1%. It was found that although performance metrics are affected by architectural and training modalities, these models are promising for surveillance and security purposes. The proposed SAHI implementation helped overcome the problem of false alarms and thus improved the prediction results. The proposed research is an improvement over current methods used to detect concealed objects and indicates the capability of deep learning to improve surveillance. Resources: https://github.com/iamabhi1007/YOLO-Variants-Multispectral-Imagery-Enhanced-Camouflage-Object-Detection.git.</p>','["Deep learning", "Aerial imagery", "Surveillance", "Reconnaissance", "Multispectral", "Camouflaged objects", "Altum-PT", "SAHI", "YOLO", "False colouring."]',NULL,21,1,'377 - 394','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1087','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',19),
(1088,1088,1,'From Reactive to Resilient: The Role of AI in Crisis Management and Organizational Resilience Post Cyberattack','<p>The research paper explores how artificial intelligence (AI) would transform the organizational management of crises through reactive strategies to resilient and proactive models, especially during post-cyberattack recovery. The modern challenge is reflected in the fact that the number and complexity of cyberattacks are increasing and put under threat the continuity of operations within organizations because of the fundamental vulnerability of their systems. The study hypothesizes to understand the role of AI-based capabilities of crisis management in improving organizational resilience by mediating through the intelligent threat detection capabilities, predictive analytics capabilities, and automated response capabilities in the Jordanian industrial and service industries. The research methodology adopted in this research was quantitative in nature whereby a structured survey questionnaire was used to deliver the survey to an IT security managers, crisis management officer and business continuity professional personnel of a sample of Jordanian organizations. The hypotheses that were tested were based on the direct and indirect relationship between AI-enabled crisis management capabilities and organizational resilience outcomes. The method used to test the hypotheses was a partial least squares structural equation modelling (PLS-SEM). The review has confirmed that the presence of AI-based systems of crisis management affects organizational resilience and rate of recovery after a cyberattack significantly. Predictive threat intelligence and automated incident response have a significant mediating effect, i.e., AI capabilities do not only enhance immediate reaction to a crisis but radically change the capacity of the organization to respond to, resist, and recover faster after cyber-attacks. This study proves that the use of AI in crisis management guidelines can contribute significantly to organizational resilience with the help of strategic preparedness frameworks. The results give practical implications to the security officers, crisis management team, and policymakers to enable them to develop resilient, evidence-based and adaptive organizational systems that will succeed in a highly hostile digital environment.</p>','["Artificial Intelligence, Crisis Management, Organizational Resilience, Cyberattack Recovery, Predictive Analytics, Threat Intelligence, Incident Response Automation, Digital Transformation."]',NULL,21,1,'395 - 413','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1088','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',20),
(1183,1183,1,'Exploring Digital Transformation and Organizational Capabilities Through Bibliometric Insights','<p>Over the last decade, scholarship on digital transformation, digital capabilities, and organizational performance has expanded rapidly. However, a holistic map of their intellectual structure within highly regulated micro-financial contexts has been missing. Drawing on a comprehensive corpus of 12,557 peer-reviewed articles indexed in the Scopus database, this study applies a Bibliometric-Systematic Literature Review (B-SLR) framework and VOSviewer techniques, including co-citation, bibliographic coupling, country collaboration, and keyword co-occurrence, to illuminate how the field has evolved, who drives it, and where the next frontiers lie. Results reveal an accelerating publication trajectory after 2024 and a concentrated output across interdisciplinary management, strategy, and financial stability journals. While developed Western economies and major East Asian hubs lead in volume, distinct regional ecosystems emerge with variable structural efficiencies. Multiple keyword clusters are unveiled, ranging from the micro-foundations of dynamic technological capabilities and strategic agility to systems-level regulatory environment compliance and localized banking intermediation. Co-authorship and institutional networks expose fragmented collaboration patterns with prominent gatekeepers but limited cross-regional integration. Synthesizing these structural trends, the paper highlights three urgent research frontiers: integrating capabilities across the structural adoption-continuance cycle, contextualising dynamic strategies under stringent institutional bounds in lower-middle-income emerging settings (such as the Bank Perekonomian Rakyat or BPR sector in Indonesia under the UU P2SK omnibus mandate), and embedding granular technological risk parameters into macro-economic banking models.</p>','["Digital Transformation, Digital Capabilities, Competitive Advantage, Regulatory Environment, Rural Banks, Bibliometric Analysis."]',NULL,21,1,'414 - 431','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1183','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',21),
(1185,1185,1,'Asymmetric Impacts of Urbanization and Industrialization on Indonesian Energy Intensity','<p>This study addresses the urbanization-energy paradox by evaluating whether structural shifts in Indonesia can facilitate a systemic decoupling of energy intensity from economic growth. While industrialization is traditionally associated with rising resource demand, we test the hypothesis that urban agglomeration functions as a latent efficiency mechanism. Utilizing an Error Correction Model (ECM) on national time-series data (2000–2021), this study models the long-term equilibrium and short-term stochastic shocks that define the energy-economy nexus. The empirical results uncover a strategic duality: industrialization remains a primary driver of energy intensity, yet urbanization exerts a statistically significant downward pressure. This inverse relationship suggests that agglomeration economies enhanced by density-driven infrastructure and optimized resource distribution effectively neutralize the energy burdens of industrial scaling. The Error Correction Term (ECT) provides robust evidence of an adjustment mechanism, indicating that the energy system consistently reverts to a long-term efficiency trajectory. The findings move the discourse beyond reactive conservation toward systemic decarbonization. We conclude that leveraging urban density is critical for Indonesia’s Carbon Neutrality mandates. Policy recommendations focus on the institutionalization of Energy Service Companies (ESCOs) and polycentric urban planning as essential frameworks for sustaining structural energy efficiency.</p>','["Energy Intensity, Urbanization, Industrialization, Indonesia, Agglomeration Economies."]',NULL,21,1,'432 - 445','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1185','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',22),
(1186,1186,1,'Sustainable Hand Lay_up Fabrication and Experimental Evaluation of Kenaf Fibre Composite Tubes for Energy Absorption Applications','<p>This study presents a comprehensive evaluation of composite impact boxes reinforced with natural fiber epoxy and internal support plates, focusing on enhancing automotive safety and sustainability. The study investigated energy absorption, load-bearing performance, and fracture modes of square composite tubes exposed to quasi-static loading conditions. Various natural fibers, including kenaf, flax, bamboo, sugarcane residue, and wool, were used as reinforcements within the polymer matrix. Key mechanical properties such as tensile strength, flexural strength, and impact resistance were analyzed, and the effects of fiber content, orientation, and processing techniques on overall performance were investigated. The results demonstrate that natural fiber composites can achieve mechanical performance comparable to conventional synthetic materials while providing significant environmental benefits, such as biodegradability, reduced carbon emissions, and the use of renewable resources. These findings suggest the potential for natural fiber reinforced composites to be utilized in the development of sustainable, lightweight, and eco-friendly automotive structures that align with circular economy principles.</p>','["Sustainable materials, Natural fiber composites, Eco-friendly engineering, Biodegradable composites, Renewable resources and Circular economy."]',NULL,21,1,'446 - 455','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1186','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',23),
(1187,1187,1,'Project Risk Management in the Execution of RUPTL Programs','<p>The Electricity Supply Business Plan (RUPTL) is a 10-year strategic document prepared by PT PLN (Persero) together with the Ministry of Energy and Mineral Resources as the main guideline for the development of electricity generation, transmission, and distribution. To answer the needs of the Indonesian people, the 2025-2034 RUPTL was prepared with a very challenging target, namely with a very large volume of activities that involve complex activities, require massive efforts, and involve large funds. The implementation of this RUPTL certainly contains extensive risks that can have a very significant impact on the Company. This paper focuses on identifying risks, analyzing risks, mitigating risks and formulating 10 top risks so that the Company can manage risks well. The approach used is a combination of RBS and FMEA that allows identifying risks comprehensively with RBS, analyzing risks more deeply by including severity, occurrence, and detection which are used as considerations in determining priorities or Risk Priority Number (RPN) and mitigating dangerous risks with RPNs above 12. This analysis also proposes 10 top risks in accordance with the PMBOK approach which is taken from the highest residual risk (residual RPN) to be monitored more closely. Based on the results of the top risk, the company can continue the implementation of the RUPTL with acceptable risk.</p>','["project risk management, RUPTL, PLN governance, infrastructure delivery."]',NULL,21,1,'456 - 481','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1187','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',24);
INSERT INTO publications (id,submission_id,version,title,abstract,keywords,references_text,issue_id,section_id,pages,doi,copyright_holder,copyright_year,license_url,status,date_published,seq) VALUES
(1188,1188,1,'Innovative Power Purchase Agreement (PPA) Models for Independent Power Producers (IPP): A Risk-Sharing Approach for PLN','<p>Independent Power Producers (IPPs) have become a strategic pillar in Indonesia''s efforts to expand generation capacity and accelerate the energy transition. However, the structure of conventional Power Purchase Agreements (PPAs) often results in an unbalanced allocation of risk—either overburdening PLN or hindering private investment that is not fully addressed. This paper focuses on the development of innovative models and risk-based decision-making frameworks to design a PPA model between PLN and IPPs that provide solutions to the risks faced. This study used risk identification, risk analysis and risk treatment to model alternative PPA structures. Using Strategic Assumption Surfacing and Testing (SAST), the proposed framework identifies important (hazardous) risks, proposes treatments that have a high level of certainty of success and that balance the interests of stakeholders. Case simulations were conducted to assess model performance in different project scenarios, including renewable energy projects with variable generation profiles. Ultimately, this study provides a systematic approach for PLN to implement an adaptive, transparent, and risk-based PPA model that increases investor confidence, accelerates project realization, and improves the resilience of Indonesia''s electricity sector as a whole.</p>','["Independent Power Producers (IPPs), Power Purchase Agreements (PPAs), Risk Management, Risk-Based Decision Making, Strategic Assumption Surfacing and Testing (SAST), Renewable Energy, Energy."]',NULL,21,1,'482 - 499','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1188','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',25),
(1189,1189,1,'Growth Model for New Business Entity','<p>Indonesia’s ambitious revenue growth target of 8% per year for the 2024-2029 period places significant demands on domestic companies to expand and innovate. PT XYZ, operating in the gas industry, has encountered both external and internal challenges that have constrained its ability to monetize and manage its business scope, thereby limiting its contribution to national growth objectives. This paper presents a comprehensive study aimed at overcoming these constraints and enabling sustainable growth for PT XYZ as a new business entity. The study follows a structured approach: first, by developing a robust business idea and concept through an analysis of potential future business fields; second, by conducting a SWOT analysis to identify strengths, weaknesses, opportunities, and threats, and formulating suitable strategies and business models; third, by establishing appropriate institutional frameworks to support the new entity; and fourth, by designing growth models tailored to the company’s context. The results highlight business strategies that align with the company’s capabilities and market opportunities, as well as growth models that balance aggressive expansion with prudent risk management. Based on these findings, the paper provides actionable recommendations for PT XYZ to achieve and sustain high growth rates, thereby supporting Indonesia’s broader economic objectives. This study offers valuable insights for other companies facing similar growth challenges in dynamic and competitive environments.</p>','["growth model, business strategy, SWOT analysis."]',NULL,21,1,'500 - 521','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1189','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',26),
(1190,1190,1,'Developing Revenue Model for Innovation-based Company','<p>Innovation-based companies face unique challenges in designing effective revenue models that support both growth and sustainability. Successful revenue models must not only generate income but also deliver meaningful benefits to customers and stakeholders. Despite growing interest in this domain, a clear gap remains in the literature regarding how innovation-based companies can systematically select and adapt revenue models across different stages of technological and market evolution. This paper addresses that gap through a conceptual analysis and structured literature review, examining the critical factors involved in developing revenue models tailored to innovation-driven enterprises. The study explores various revenue generation strategies, including licensing, subscription, freemium, and platform-based models, and analyzes how these align with a company''s value proposition, customer segments, and technological capabilities. Special attention is given to the dynamic nature of innovation markets, where rapid technological change and evolving customer needs require adaptable and scalable revenue strategies. The paper also discusses the role of intellectual property, strategic partnerships, and ecosystem development in enhancing competitive positioning and revenue potential. Key findings indicate that no single revenue model is universally optimal; rather, the most effective approach depends on the firm''s innovation type and its position within the industry life cycle—for instance, freemium models suit early-stage market penetration, while licensing is more appropriate during maturity or decline. The findings provide an integrated framework—centered on value alignment, scalability, and ecosystem integration—for innovation-based companies to evaluate and select appropriate revenue models. This framework serves as a practical decision-support tool for leaders and managers seeking to align monetization strategies with their firm''s innovation trajectory, enabling more informed and adaptive revenue planning in fast-paced, innovation-driven environments.</p>','["revenue model, innovation, business strategy, technology company."]',NULL,21,1,'522 - 535','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1190','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',27),
(1191,1191,1,'Adaptability Dilemmas and Innovation Paths of China’s Art Education and Industry Development in the AI Era: From the Interactive Perspective of Education, Technology, and Occupation','<p>The rapid development of AI technology has profoundly reshaped artistic creation models and industry ecology, and China’s art education system is facing adaptability challenges under the triple interaction of “technological change - industry demand - education supply”. Based on multi-dimensional frameworks such as Career Construction Theory and School Engagement Theory, this paper integrates more than 20 Chinese and English empirical studies and industry reports to systematically analyze the disconnection manifestations, causes, and solutions of art education and industry development in the AI era. The research finds that current art education has core problems including outdated curriculum content compared to the demand for AI tool application, misalignment between students’ digital literacy and the industry’s requirement for algorithmic capabilities and mismatching between educational models and the digital transformation of the creative economy. These issues lead to art students facing dilemmas such as identity crisis, increased occupational vulnerability, and insufficient employability. Based on this, this paper proposes an optimization path with “career construction” as the core, driving the innovation of educational content through digital transformation, strengthening employment adaptability by cultivating algorithmic literacy, and bridging the gap between educational practice and industry needs through university-enterprise collaboration. It aims to provide theoretical reference and practical guidance for the high-quality development of China’s art education in the AI era.</p>','["AI Era", "Art Education", "Industry Demand", "Adaptability", "Career Construction."]',NULL,21,1,'536 - 550','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1191','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',28),
(1192,1192,1,'Ritual Communication in Practice: Identifying the Key Ritual Elements of the Majie Quyi Fair','<p>This study examines the key ritual elements embedded in the communication practices of the Majie Quyi Fair. Existing studies of folk performance events and intangible cultural heritage have generated rich insights into artistic forms, historical origins, cultural symbolism, and preservation strategies, yet have paid comparatively little attention to communication as a constitutive process that sustains such events over time. Drawing on James W. Carey’s ritual view of communication, this study approaches the Majie Quyi Fair as a ritualised communicative event. It analyses its communication practices through four interrelated dimensions: ritual participants, ritual symbols, ritual field, and ritual order. Methodologically, the study relies primarily on participant observation, supplemented by informal conversations in the field. The findings show that the Majie Quyi Fair is not merely a folk performance gathering but a recurring communicative event sustained by multi-layered participation, symbolic expression, spatial organisation, and tacit order. More broadly, the study suggests that ritual communication is not confined to highly institutionalised ceremonies or media events, but also takes shape within vernacular, distributed, and periodically repeated collective practices. In doing so, the article shifts the analytical focus from folk performance as cultural content to folk gatherings as a communicative process. It offers an empirically grounded approach to understanding how cultural continuity is maintained through communication practices in folk cultural events.</p>','["Majie Quyi Fair", "Ritual View of Communication", "Ritual Elements", "Folk Performance Event", "Participant Observation."]',NULL,21,1,'551 - 572','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1192','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',29),
(1193,1193,1,'The Concept of Uswatun Ḥasanah (Good Exemplar) and its Relevance to Leadership from the Islamic Perspective','<p>This study aims to analyze the concept of uswatun ḥasanah (UH) in the Islamic literature and its relevance to leadership principles, particularly in the context of contemporary leadership. The research employs a qualitative approach with a descriptive-analytical design through content analysis techniques. The findings indicate that UH, as described in the Quran and Hadith, serves as a model of exemplary conduct, encompassing values of justice, honesty, empathy, and moral and spiritual responsibility. These values are not only aligned with Islamic leadership principles, but also correspond to modern leadership theories. The application of UH values can strengthen the integrity, effectiveness, and sustainability of leadership in organizations, communities, and governments. This study recommends that Muslim leaders integrate UH values into every aspect of their leadership, especially in terms of practicing justice, honesty, and empathy, while prioritizing consultation (shūrā) and transparency in the decision-making process. Such an approach is expected to foster a harmonious environment and support the success of the organizations or communities under Muslim leadership. Additionally, Muslim leaders are encouraged to continually enhance their understanding of Islamic leadership through formal education, training, and lifelong learning. This research contributes significantly to the development of Islamic leadership literature and practices, particularly in the context of leadership challenges in the era of globalization.</p>','["Uswatun \\u1e24asanah, Islamic Leadership, Moral and Spiritual Values, Leadership Principles, Modern Leadership Relevance."]',NULL,21,1,'573 - 594','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1193','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',30),
(1212,1212,1,'Artificial Intelligence-Driven Circular Economy Initiatives: A Global Bibliometric Analysis of Research Trends, Knowledge Structures, and Future Directions','<p>Artificial Intelligence (AI) is transforming Circular Economy (CE) initiatives, yet the field''s intellectual structure and thematic evolution remain insufficiently mapped. This study evaluates the scientific development of AI-driven CE research. Using Bibliometrix and VOSviewer, a comprehensive bibliometric analysis was conducted on 8,327 Scopus-indexed publications following an adapted PRISMA 2020 workflow. Results show scientific production surged post-2018, with logistic growth modeling indicating rapid maturation. China leads global publication output and international collaboration networks. Thematic evolution and co-occurrence mapping reveal a significant structural shift: early foundational research focused heavily on basic machine learning and recycling algorithms, whereas current frontiers emphasize system-level optimization, including intelligent manufacturing, life cycle assessment, waste valorization, and remanufacturing. Ultimately, AI-driven CE research has evolved from isolated technological applications into an interdisciplinary domain centered on data-driven sustainability. Sustaining this trajectory requires broader cross-institutional collaboration and the exploration of emerging paradigms, such as explainable and generative AI, to develop the resilient, sustainable production ecosystems necessary for global circularity goals.</p>','["Artificial intelligence", "bibliometric analysis", "circular economy", "science mapping", "sustainable development."]',NULL,21,1,'595 - 613','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1212','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',31),
(1213,1213,1,'Technological Innovations for Ocean Plastic Waste Management: A Global Bibliometric Analysis of Research Trends, Collaboration Networks, and Emerging Technologies','<p>Ocean plastic pollution has become one of the most pressing environmental challenges worldwide, driving increasing interest in technology-based solutions for monitoring, prevention, mitigation, and sustainable waste management. Despite the rapid growth of research in this field, a comprehensive understanding of its scientific development, collaboration patterns, and emerging technological directions remains limited. This study presents a global bibliometric analysis of technological innovations for ocean plastic waste management using 12,039 English-language publications retrieved from the Scopus database on 09 July 2026. Bibliographic data were analyzed using Bibliometrix (RStudio) and VOSviewer to examine publication trends, influential authors, journals, institutions, countries, collaboration networks, conceptual structures, and thematic evolution.</p>
INSERT INTO publications (id,submission_id,version,title,abstract,keywords,references_text,issue_id,section_id,pages,doi,copyright_holder,copyright_year,license_url,status,date_published,seq) VALUES
(1252,1252,1,'Enhancing Cross-Domain Recommendations Through Deep Learning: A Comprehensive Framework','<p>Recommender systems have emerged as indispensable tools for assisting users in navigating the vast array of content available on the internet. By leveraging advanced machine learning techniques, these systems filter out irrelevant information and provide tailored suggestions that align with user preferences and needs. While recommender systems have become integral to popular web platforms, their widespread adoption is attributed to recent advancements in computational capabilities and the abundance of digital data.</p>
INSERT INTO publications (id,submission_id,version,title,abstract,keywords,references_text,issue_id,section_id,pages,doi,copyright_holder,copyright_year,license_url,status,date_published,seq) VALUES
(1281,1281,1,'Data Analytics Integration in Malaysian Higher Education: Issues, Outcomes, and Organizational Readiness','<p>The adoption of data analytics in higher education institutions (HEIs) in Malaysia has become strategically important and is motivated by the Malaysia Education Blueprint (Higher Education) 2015-2025 alongside the global trend of data-driven decision making (DDDM). This study explores the relationship between organisational readiness and institutional outcomes vis-à-vis both administrative and academic performance indicators. The study employed a mixed-methods approach by collecting quantitative survey data from 212 HEIs in Malaysia and supplementing it with qualitative interviews with senior administrators. Readiness was defined through five dimensions: data literacy, technology infrastructure utilisation, decision process efficiency, data-driven culture, and change management success. Quantitative analyses performed included: descriptive analyses, independent-samples t-tests, one-way ANOVA, Pearson correlations, and robust ordinary least-squares regression. Findings from the study suggest both administrative and academic outcomes were above the scale midpoint, with notable differences based on institution type and geographic region. After accounting for institution type, region, and DDDM maturity, readiness emerged as the key predictor of institutional outcomes. Qualitative data underscored the importance of leadership commitment as well as collaboration and sustained capacity-building through cross-departmental networks. The study provides actionable insights for policymakers and institutional leaders to enhance their frameworks for readiness by strategically interweaving the human, process, and technological components to facilitate sustainable integration of analytics.</p>','["Higher Education, Data Analytics, Organizational Readiness, Malaysia, Institutional Outcomes, Data-Driven Decision-Making, Educational Technology"]',NULL,21,1,'1476 - 1491','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1281','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',75),
(1282,1282,1,'Digital Transformation of Dual Higher Education in Uzbekistan: Integrating Artificial Intelligence, Virtual Reality, and Blockchain for Inclusive and Sustainable Learning','<p>The digital transformation of higher education creates new opportunities to enhance the effectiveness, inclusiveness, and sustainability of dual education systems. However, empirical evidence on the integration of emerging technologies into dual education remains limited in developing and post-Soviet countries. This study investigates stakeholder perceptions of digital transformation in dual higher education in Uzbekistan and explores the potential of Artificial Intelligence (AI), Virtual Reality (VR), and blockchain technologies to support inclusive and sustainable learning environments. A convergent mixed-methods design was used. Quantitative data were collected from 312 students and 80 industry representatives through structured surveys, while qualitative data were obtained from semi-structured interviews with 24 academic staff members involved in dual education programmes. Descriptive statistics, correlation analysis, and thematic analysis were used to examine stakeholder readiness, implementation barriers, and future development priorities. The findings indicate strong support for digital transformation by stakeholders. Most students perceived dual education as more effective than traditional instruction (81%), and 74% expressed interest in AI- and VR-supported learning environments. Employers demonstrated a high readiness to adopt digital assessment tools (85%) and blockchain-based credential verification systems (80%). However, major challenges were identified, including insufficient digital infrastructure, limited funding, inadequate professional development opportunities, and regulatory uncertainty. Only 31% of students considered the existing digital infrastructure sufficient for advanced technology integration.<br/>Based on these findings, this study proposes an integrated framework that combines AI-driven personalized learning, VR-based experiential training, and blockchain-enabled credential verification within the principles of Universal Design for Learning (UDL) and Sustainable Development Goal 4 (SDG 4). The framework aims to enhance educational accessibility, strengthen industry–university collaboration, and support equitable participation in dual higher education. This study contributes empirical evidence from a developing country context and offers practical recommendations for policymakers and higher education institutions seeking to implement inclusive and sustainable digital transformation strategies in dual education systems.</p>','["dual education", "digital transformation", "inclusive higher education", "artificial intelligence", "virtual reality", "blockchain", "sustainability", "Uzbekistan", "SDG 4."]',NULL,21,1,'1492 - 1512','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1282','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',76),
(1283,1283,1,'Entrepreneurial Intention in the Nepalese Hydro Business Sector','<p>Business in the energy sector is a concern for many entrepreneurs worldwide as a profitable venture. The privatization and open-market policy adopted by the Nepal government encourages private-sector involvement in the hydro business. This study investigates whether Nepalese investors are interested in the hydro business and what factors affect an entrepreneur''s intention to invest in this business. Taking Psychological well-being, government role, risk-taking behavior and self-confidence as the major factors affecting entrepreneurs’ intention in the hydropower business, a research model was developed. The model was tested by using structural equation modelling. A structured questionnaire was used to collect data from 510 Nepalese entrepreneurs across different parts of the country. Psychological Wellbeing, Government Role and Self-efficacy were found to be the most influential factors affecting the intention of Nepalese entrepreneurs in the hydro power business sector.</p>','["Psychological wellbeing, Government role, Risk taking behavior, self-efficacy, entrepreneur intention."]',NULL,21,1,'1513 - 1524','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1283','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',77),
(1284,1284,1,'Human-Centric Machine Learning Frameworks for Scalable Software Quality Prediction','<p>The human-centred perspective of software quality embraces the construction of data-driven software quality prediction models, which inherently support quality governance decisions over the life cycle. While the introduced models are directly linked to software engineering quality dimensions, they lack a well-established logical relationship with stakeholder-centric software quality attributes. A conceptual framework that explicitly links quality-attribute drivers to human-centric software quality dimensional data suitable for software quality prediction has yet to be defined. The proposed framework addresses this gap and provides a structured approach for developing data sources and human-centric software quality prediction models. It supports the construction of prediction models in line with stakeholder requirements, enables the selection of quality indicators focused on business and operational objectives, and strengthens the connection between quality models and quality dimensions aimed at regulatory compliance, stakeholder trust, and attractiveness. This extended version contributes twelve numbered equations that formalise stakeholder weighting, trust, calibration, and scalability; four algorithms covering elicitation, trust-gated model routing, explanation-driven trust calibration, and governance-staged rollout; and an empirical section grounded in 2025 practitioner evidence on trust in AI-generated code.<br/>In the last two decades, machine learning and artificial intelligence have enabled new research and practical applications in various domains. However, the construction of data-driven models for predicting the quality of software products that generate scalable outcomes aligned with the requirements of those who make quality decisions throughout the life cycle remains an open issue. The necessity to develop human-centric software, recognise quality from a human-centric perspective, and comply with regulations and corporate governance principles have become important drivers for research and practice in software engineering. Accordingly, the demand for open and interpretable machine learning models is steadily growing. Yet the deep-learning nature of built models limits their direct application and restricts their operationalisation. These aspects must be considered not only in model construction or training but throughout the complete cycle of model conception, construction, and use.</p>','["Human-Centric Software Quality, Software Quality Prediction, Machine Learning, Artificial Intelligence (AI), Software Engineering, Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI), Quality Governance, Stakeholder-Centric Quality, Predictive Analytics, Software Quality Metrics."]',NULL,21,1,'1525 - 1543','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1284','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',78),
(1285,1285,1,'Agricultural Waste as a Sustainable Cement Substitute in Construction Materials: A Comprehensive Review of Properties and Future Prospects','<p>The cement industry contributes approximately 7–10% of global carbon dioxide (CO2) emissions, highlighting the need for sustainable alternatives to conventional Portland cement. Agricultural waste has emerged as a promising supplementary cementitious material because it is abundant, renewable, and rich in pozzolanic compounds. This review evaluates the potential of agricultural waste as a sustainable cement substitute by examining its material properties, treatment methods, engineering performance, and future prospects. A systematic literature review was conducted using peer-reviewed articles. The findings indicate that the performance of agricultural waste depends on its chemical composition, calcination conditions, grinding process, and pretreatment methods. Properly processed agricultural wastes, including rice husk ash, sugarcane bagasse ash, palm oil fuel ash, and corn cob ash, can partially replace Portland cement while improving compressive strength, durability, and microstructure. In addition, their use reduces agricultural waste disposal and lowers the carbon footprint of construction materials. However, differences in processing techniques, material characteristics, and testing methods remain major challenges for large-scale application. This review provides a comprehensive comparison of different agricultural wastes. It highlights the need for standardized processing methods and further research to support their wider adoption in sustainable and low-carbon construction.</p>','["agricultural waste, supplementary cementitious materials, Portland cement replacement, sustainable construction"]',NULL,21,1,'1544 - 1557','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1285','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',79),
(1286,1286,1,'Self-Evolving Analytics Pipelines for Reliable AI-Augmented Software Systems','<p>Reliable and accountable AI-augmented software systems are crucial for their successful deployment and use. For trustworthy operation at runtime, self-evolving analytics pipelines can enhance monitoring, quality control, and governance of AI applications. A systematic analysis delivers a rigorous foundation and identifies key aspects of self-evolving analytics pipelines that contribute to reliability and accountability. The resulting requirements encompass evaluation-oriented properties, techniques for continuous learning of ML pipelines, and mechanisms for runtime monitoring with data monitoring and quality dashboards. This extended version formalises those requirements in sixteen numbered equations covering reliability, availability, three distinct classes of drift, retraining economics, and accountability; specifies four algorithms governing the self-evolution control loop, joint-condition drift alerting, safe canary promotion, and accountability evidence assembly; and grounds the argument in 2023–2025 incident and transparency data.<br/>Reliable AI systems must fulfil numerous conditions, yet evidence illustrates that not all aspects are met concurrently. Empirical studies have established evaluation criteria for model quality, though mechanisms for reliable operation during production are less mature. Supply-chain principles indicate that monitoring and analytics become paramount in production. Reliable operation encompasses continual control in streaming environments, online adaptation of AI models to data drift, and external feedback to alert systems.</p>','["Self-Evolving Analytics, AI-Augmented Software Systems, Adaptive Data Pipelines, Intelligent Software Engineering, Automated Analytics Workflows, Continuous Learning Systems, Reliable Artificial Intelligence, MLOps and AIOps, Autonomous Pipeline Optimization, Software Reliability Engineering."]',NULL,21,1,'1558 - 1578','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1286','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',80),
(1304,1304,1,'The AIMEL Model: Artificial Intelligence Mediated Experiential Learning for the Formation of Sustainability and Entrepreneurial Competencies','<p>Higher education institutions have adopted artificial intelligence tools faster than they have developed the pedagogical reasoning needed to place those tools inside a coherent theory of learning. The result is a body of work that documents applications one at a time, chatbots that scaffold reflection, virtual clinics that simulate professional practice, generative tools that personalize explanation, without an account of how these applications relate to the structure of experiential learning or to the competencies that experiential learning is meant to produce. This paper develops the AIMEL framework (Artificial Intelligence Mediated Experiential Learning) in response. AIMEL integrates four bodies of theory that have so far been treated separately: Kolb''s experiential learning cycle and its later revisions, research on artificial intelligence in higher education, the sustainability competence literature, and the entrepreneurial competence literature. The framework has three layers. A mediation layer specifies four functions through which artificial intelligence can participate at each stage of the experiential cycle. A competence layer connects those stages to specific sustainability and entrepreneurial competencies rather than to generic learning gains. A governance layer sets four boundary conditions, transparency, fairness, privacy, and human agency, that determine whether a given instance of mediation is legitimate. The paper states seven propositions, offers candidate operationalization for each construct, and sets out implications for curriculum design, faculty development, and institutional policy. AIMEL treats artificial intelligence as a conditional participant in the teaching transaction whose contribution must be earned at each stage rather than assumed.</p>','["AIMEL", "artificial intelligence", "experiential learning", "sustainability competencies", "entrepreneurial competencies", "AI ethics", "higher education."]',NULL,21,1,'1579 - 1597','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1304','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',81),
(1305,1305,1,'A Multimodal EEG and fNIRS Synchronization Framework using Anatomical Channel Mapping and Temporal Alignment for Neurovascular Coupling Analysis','<p>Electroencephalography (EEG) and functional near-infrared spectroscopy (fNIRS) provide complementary information for investigating brain activity by capturing electrophysiological and hemodynamic responses respectively. However the heterogeneous sampling frequencies and differing physiological characteristics of these modalities present significant challenges for multimodal integration. This study proposes a systematic preprocessing and synchronization framework for multimodal EEG-fNIRS analysis using the recordings from the subjects. Raw fNIRS optical intensity signals acquired at dual wavelengths (780 and 850 nm) were transformed into oxygenated (HbO) and deoxygenated (HbR) hemoglobin concentration changes using the Modified Beer Lambert Law (MBLL). To overcome the disparity in acquisition frequencies a contiguous temporal-bin averaging strategy was employed to generate equal length EEG and fNIRS signals while preserving their temporal characteristics without artificial interpolation. The synchronized recordings obtained from nine participants were subsequently analysed using Pearson Correlation Coefficients to quantify the relationship between neuronal electrical activity and cerebral hemodynamic responses. The experimental results demonstrated predominantly positive correlations across the frontal regions by indicating consistent temporal coupling between EEG and HbO signals while localized variations were observed in the F3 region by reflecting regional neurovascular heterogeneity. The proposed framework effectively synchronizes multimodal brain signals and provides a reliable foundation for subsequent feature extraction, multimodal fusion and machine learning based emotion recognition. The developed preprocessing pipeline contributes toward improving the robustness and physiological interpretability of multimodal brain computer interface and affective computing applications.</p>','["EEG, fNIRS, Pearson Correlation Coefficient, Temporal alignment."]',NULL,21,1,'1598 - 1613','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1305','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',82),
(1307,1307,1,'Design and Experimental Evaluation of a Secure Cloud File-Sharing System with Expiring Tokens and Distributed Key Custody','<p>Public cloud file sharing enhances accessibility and scalability but still suffer from the same issues as bearer tokens and centralized key services: credential theft, credential replay, single point compromise, and continued key availability after credential expiration. To tackle these deficiencies, this study presents a file-sharing prototype system which is a combination of AES-256-GCM encryption, ES256 recipient-bound JSON Web Tokens, proof-of-possession signatures, nonce based replay prevention, atomic single use enforcement, and 3-of-5 Shamir threshold key sharing. A set of trials was employed to evaluate the cryptographic processing, token attacks, threshold recovery, guardian failures, expiration behaviour, and concurrent access, along with synthetic access logs for anomaly detection. The legitimate access succeeded in all trials, while most of the other types of attacks (expired, modified, replayed, stolen-token attacks) reached 0% success rate. Key reconstruction failed with less than 3 guardian failures, was still possible with a maximum of 2 guardian failures and was not possible after deletion of guardian shares. The threshold design had a small amount of extra latency across centralized baseline designs and was able to serve 100 concurrent requests with 100% success. The study concludes that with a combination of recipient bound temporal authorization with distributed key custody, auditable cryptographic expiration becomes possible. The most significant contribution it makes is the union of different features in an integrated and experimented verified workflow, which consists of secure access, replay resistance, threshold recovery, and verifiable post-expiry key unavailability.</p>','["cloud file sharing, expiring access token, threshold cryptography, proof of possession, cryptographic expiration."]',NULL,21,1,'1614 - 1633','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1307','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',83),
(1309,1309,1,'An AI-Driven Blockchain Framework for Enhancing Cybersecurity and Fraud Detection in FinTech Platforms','<p>As the growth of the FinTech platforms continues, there is an increasing demand for intelligent, secure and traceable solutions that can provide real-time detection of fraudulent transactions and shield financial records from manipulation. In this research, an Artificial Intelligence-powered blockchain framework, combining machine learning for fraud detection and permissioned blockchain for validation, was proposed. It was found that ensemble models performed better than a linear baseline. The overall best balance of precision, specificity and F1 score was obtained with the Random Forest model, and the highest precision–recall was obtained with the Extra Trees model, with fraud recall slightly better. In addition, feature-importance analysis revealed a small number of transaction attributes, which were anonymised, that most significantly affected fraud classification. The chosen model was then connected to a prototype of a chained hash blockchain that preserved the hashes of transactions, the time, the predicted probability of fraud, the validation result, and the version of the model. Through hash inconsistency, the prototype was able to detect any transaction modifications which might have been made on purpose and successfully ensured ledger integrity. The results illustrate how both AI and blockchain technologies complement each other. AI is effective in detecting fraud accurately and on time, and blockchain enhances the traceability, auditability and tamper resistance of transactions.</p>','["Artificial intelligence", "Blockchain", "Cybersecurity", "Fraud detection", "FinTech platforms", "Machine learning."]',NULL,21,1,'1634 - 1646','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1309','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',84),
(1310,1310,1,'Programmable Robotics as a Mediator of Computational Thinking: A Mixed-Methods Study on the Development of Computer Skills in the Technical Baccalaureate','<p>Ecuadorian technical secondary education continues to struggle with the gap between declarative computer-science instruction and the curricular demand that graduates leave school with operational competencies transferable to the productive sector. This article examines the extent to which the incorporation of the programmable mBot robot as a didactic resource within the computer-science subject affects the development of computing skills among twenty-eight second- and third-year technical baccalaureate students at Unidad Educativa Fiscal José Joaquín Pino Ycaza, in Guayas province, Ecuador. A mixed-methods design with purposive sampling was employed, combining pretest and posttest diagnostic instruments administered to students with semi-structured interviews conducted with teachers and analyzed through inductive coding in ATLAS.ti. The quantitative results show substantial progress in the technical mastery of peripherals: 100% of the sample correctly identified the infrared sensor as the indispensable component for line-following and the logical cause of a directional error on the posttest, while 85.7% successfully programmed the ultrasonic sensor for obstacle avoidance. Attitudinally, 92.8% of students rejected the notion that robot programming is a waste of time, and 82.1% positively valued collaborative work as a facilitator of problem-solving. Qualitative analysis confirms that teachers perceive a transition from theoretical instruction toward tangible practice, accompanied by greater autonomy and concentration, although infrastructure and instructional-time constraints persist. It is concluded that educational robotics, grounded in Papert''s constructionism, functions as an effective mediator of computational thinking in technical secondary education, and its progressive curricular institutionalization is recommended.</p>','["educational robotics, computational thinking, technical secondary education, mBot, computing skills, mixed methods."]',NULL,21,1,'1647 - 1662','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1310','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',85),
(1311,1311,1,'Municipal Composting of Forest Waste as a Circular Economy and Territorial Sustainability Strategy: Experimental Evaluation in Oaxaca, México','<p>The management of forestry waste at municipal waste management centers presents technical, environmental, and social challenges due to its accumulation, slow degradation due to its lignocellulosic composition, and limited institutional capacity to transform it into useful resources. From a circular economy and territorial sustainability perspective, this research aimed to evaluate the technical and municipal feasibility of composting alternatives for forestry waste generated at the Center for the Integrated Management of Urban Solid Waste in the municipality of San Lorenzo Cacaotepec, Oaxaca. A completely randomized block design was established with four treatments: soil, soil + lactic acid bacteria (LAB), manure, and manure + LAB. After 140 days, pH ranged from 7.77 to 8.30 and electrical conductivity from 1.19 to 5.50 mS cm⁻¹. The manure-based compost had the highest N, P, K, Mg, and Na concentrations, whereas the soil-based compost had the highest Ca, Fe, and Mn contents. The results enable evaluation of composting as an appropriate technology for the municipal management of forestry waste, with the potential to reduce pressure on disposal sites, generate organic amendments, and strengthen local waste utilization strategies. The study provides evidence applicable to municipalities with limited operational capacity and contributes to the discussion on the social valorization of residual biomass, circular waste management, and territorial sustainability in local contexts.</p>','["forest waste, municipal composting, circular economy, territorial sustainability, organic waste valorization."]',NULL,21,1,'1663 - 1675','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1311','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',86),
(1312,1312,1,'Territorial dynamics and sustainability of the mezcal system in southern Mexico: an approach from the perspective of Good Living','<p>This research aims to understand the organizational, cultural, and environmental factors that allow that the economy social and solidarity HE integrate in a mode of life further worthy, equitable and sustainable in the sector of mezcal of Sola de Vega, The largest producer of ancestral mezcal in the state of Oaxaca. development a methodology quantitative in three phases, beginning with a revision of the literature in Web Of Science . In the second phase, 110 semi-structured questionnaires were sent to mezcal producers. The third phase corresponds to the analysis quantitative, in he that HE they performed evidence of hypothesis, he alpha Cronbach''s alpha, principal component factor analysis, Pearson correlation, and linear regression simple . The results identify the key components of self-management, cooperation, and the perception of community well-being. So as a strong correlation between the practices collectives and the perception of the community well-being (r = 0.68), in addition to adequate internal consistency in the applied scales (α = 0.82). the testimonies they emphasize that the organization community is a pillar of the balance between the production, sustainability environmental and he water, This article offers evidence empirical and analytics about as The ESS and the BV are articulated in the rural contexts of southern Mexico, proposing a relational model between self-management community and he welfare collective to to start of the practice ancestral of the mezcal.</p>','["Social and solidarity economy", "Mezcal", "Rural development", "Community well-being", "Sustainable agriculture."]',NULL,21,1,'1676 - 1689','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1312','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',87),
(1313,1313,1,'Needle in a Haystack: Decamouflaging Adversarial Examples Using SBERT Embeddings','<p>Natural Language Processing models are vulnerable to adversarial perturbations which can derail the model’s classification ability. Existing works focus on correcting the training data in order to be resilient to these attacks. In this paper, we propose the idea of multi label classification for adversarial attacks. First, we introduce a new anagram based attack into the literature and second, we train our model to learn these representations by adding a novel label to the dataset which is used as a feature for training the model. Our results show that our method succeeds in separating clean samples from adversarial ones without changing the actual data in the process. We conducted our experiments on the internet movie database dataset. Results show that our method makes a model reliable and robust against character-level perturbations.</p>','["natural language processing, sentiment analysis, adversarial attacks"]',NULL,21,1,'1690 - 1704','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1313','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',88),
(1314,1314,1,'The Role of E-Learning Platforms in Improving Student Performance','<p>The development of digital skills in higher education has transformed the course of education (e.g. e-learning platforms) and is a crucial part of teaching and learning. In this study, we examine the effect of e-learning platforms in improving students'' academic performance in Jordan, Palestine, the Sultanate of Oman, and the United Arab Emirates. The study was structured in a quantitative manner and conducted over five months starting in June. Data were collected from students enrolled in higher education institutions in the four countries. The research aims to understand the impact of e-learning platforms on students'' learning in terms of accessibility, ease of use, quality of learning resources, instructor support, student engagement, and learning satisfaction. Our data can be analyzed using descriptive statistics, reliability analysis, correlation analysis, and regression methods to understand the relationship between the study variables and the most important predictors of academic performance. We expect the findings to show that effective use of e-learning platforms positively affects students'' engagement and access to educational resources and learning. This study also gives the countries in the study their common challenges and opportunities to improve digital education. The findings contribute to the growing body of knowledge on technology-enhanced learning by offering empirical evidence from the Middle East and provide practical recommendations for higher education institutions, educators, and policymakers in order to increase student productivity via the successful deployment and utilization of e-learning platfor</p>','["E-learning Platforms, Student Performance, Higher Education, Academic Achievement , Student Engagement , Digital Learning, Learning Management Systems, Educational Technology"]',NULL,21,1,'1705 - 1734','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1314','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',89),
(1316,1316,1,'Linked to the Area of Mathematics in Early Childhood Students in East Lima: A Descriptive-Comparative Study','<p>This study was aimed at comparing how well students in East Lima public schools could do math. The quantitative approach, descriptive-comparative type and non-experimental cross-sectional design were developed. The study sample was based on the census and included 218 students aged five who attended schools in the districts of San Juan de Lurigancho, Ate-Vitarte and Lurigancho. The evaluation was performed using the Spatial Orientation and Numerical Operations subtests of the LURIA-INITIAL Test. These subtests are executive functions and linguistic functions. The data were analyzed in descriptive statistics and non-parametric tests (Mann-Whitney U and Kruskal-Wallis H) with a significance of α = 0.05. The results show that all the students are performing at average or above average in neuropsychological functions related to mathematics, which is generally satisfactory. There were no statistically significant differences in the results according to sex, educational institution or district of origin. The executive and linguistic functions related to early mathematical learning develop similarly in the population studied. This shows the importance of early education in balancing neuropsychological development and promotes universal educational strategies that emphasize reinforcing these functions.</p>','["Neuropsychological Functions", "Mathematics", "Initial Education", "Executive Functions", "Linguistic Functions", "LURIA-INITIAL Test."]',NULL,21,1,'1735 - 1748','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1316','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',90),
(1317,1317,1,'Computational Thinking in Preschoolers','<p>This article addresses the idea of incorporating computational thinking into early childhood education and how to do so from the very earliest stages of learning. The study was conducted using a qualitative approach to which 17 papers were reviewed from 2015 to 2024 from journals indexed in Scielo, SCOPUS, and Google Scholar. The studies were based on the teaching methods and technologies in the preschool setting. Computational thinking (sequencing, logic, problem solving) is important in order to develop children in a digital environment. The tools and approaches used to teach these concepts at an early age have been effective at bringing them into the world, but we need to adapt educational strategies to cultural and context-specific dimensions in order to develop their effectiveness. Early integration of computational thinking in preschool education provides a structured and creative way of thinking, essential for learning at all levels. Creative and innovative strategies and use of technology in early childhood education can revolutionize the way children think. Educational approaches to learn in different cultural contexts are needed to help prepare students for a new world with the digital landscape.</p>','["computational thinking, early childhood education, sequencing, logic, problem-solving, technological tools, educational strategies, holistic development."]',NULL,21,1,'1749 - 1768','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1317','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',91),
(1318,1318,1,'Calculator-Based Emulator to Enhance Statistics Teaching: A University Case Study','<p>Statistical competence is fundamental in many fields but many students and researchers have trouble in applying statistical concepts. In this examination, we evaluate and measure the impact of the CASIO fx-570 emulator calculator in the teaching of statistics at the university level with a sample of 170 university students, divided into control and experimental groups, where the CASIO fx-570 emulator was used to integrate theory and practice and to provide a more intuitive and engaging learning experience. We conducted pre- and post-tests and evaluated the effects of the intervention on conceptual, procedural and attitudinal learning dimensions. Mann-Whitney U tests showed significant improvements (p &lt; 0.05) for the experimental group. These results suggest that the CASIO fx-570 emulator can help improve the learning of statistics in university students. Finally, we should consider the current educational requirements and take advantage of technological progress to bridge the gap between statistical theory and practice and to help people to understand statistics intuitively in research.</p>','["Education, statistical literacy, CASIO fx-570 emulator, university students"]',NULL,21,1,'1769 - 1780','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1318','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',92),
(1319,1319,1,'Neuropsychological Development and its Impact on Mathematical Learning in Childhood: A Systematic Review','<p>Neuropsychological Development and its Impact on Mathematical Learning in Childhood: A Systematic Review<br/>The present study presents a systematic review of the impact of neuropsychological development on mathematical learning in childhood based on 30 peer-reviewed scientific articles indexed in Scopus, published between 2019 and 2025. The study was done in the PRISMA 2020 protocol, with strict inclusion criteria for empirical and theoretical research in Spanish and English, for university settings in the USA, China and Spain. From a neuropsychological perspective, the results suggest that initial mathematics learning is based on the relation between specific and general cognitive processes. The quality of teacher-student learning environment is fundamental to mathematics'' learning. Adverse situations with respect to learning pathways in mathematics can be detected, such as early births, traumas in early years and sleeping problems.<br/>In addition, games, recreation activities and family involvement are the key to further mathematical reasoning development, the research found. On the other hand, it was found that initial and continuous teacher training is a crucial factor to better mathematics education from an early age. Finally, educational technology tools (e.g., digital apps and creative areas) can contribute to mathematics skills development in the early years. In conclusion, the study has led us to identify, classify, select and interpret the main studies and findings on the initial learning of mathematics based on the links between neuropsychological and cognitive factors and to emphasize executive functions -and numerical sense and language -related to mathematics.</p>','["Neuropsychological development, mathematical learning, childhood."]',NULL,21,1,'1781 - 1804','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1319','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',93),
(1326,1326,1,'UA-FGDRL: An Uncertainty-Aware Federated Multi-Objective Green Deep Reinforcement Learning Framework for Energy-Efficient Cloud Resource Allocation','<p>Cloud resource management remains a big challenge. Cloud workloads are constantly evolving. The demand of the users is frequently unclear. Large cloud systems also require a lot of energy. Data privacy is also a key consideration. These problems make it challenging to manage cloud resources effectively. There are many existing resource allocation methods that are based on heuristic or deep learning. These methods rely on workload prediction. However, prediction errors often occur. The high costs of training these models are also needed. Most of these methods are based on the central collection of data, leading to privacy and scalability issues. To overcome these problems, this paper introduces an Uncertainty-Aware Federated Green Deep Reinforcement Learning (UA-FGDRL) framework. The framework consists of three key elements. The first is a workload prediction model with uncertainty. It helps in handling demand variations. The second one is federated learning. It enables nodes in the cloud to train models without sharing raw data. This is helpful to safeguard information privacy. This also decreases the communication overhead. The third part is a green-aware deep reinforcement learning approach. It aims to increase the efficiency and decrease the energy consumption. The uncertainty-aware prediction module enhances resource provisioning. It decreases the forecasting errors in a dynamic workload. Federated learning allows for decentralized training of cloud nodes. Data is stored at local servers. The green-aware learning agent includes energy and cost factors in the reward function. This contributes to the achievement of balanced resource utilization. Real cloud workload data is used for experiments. The results indicate that the proposed framework can be used to increase the utilization of resources by 15–20%. Up to 17% decrease in SLA violation rates. The energy consumption is reduced by 13-16%. The operational cost is lowered by approximately 15% than recent deep reinforcement learning methods. The model also has a faster convergence. With varying workloads, prediction accuracy is enhanced.</p>','["Cloud Computing", "Resource Allocation", "Uncertainty-Aware Learning", "Federated Learning", "Deep Reinforcement Learning", "Energy-Efficient Scheduling", "Workload Prediction", "Green Cloud Computing."]',NULL,21,1,'1805 - 1819','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1326','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',94),
(1328,1328,1,'The Role of Strategic Planning in Promoting Sustainable Scientific Research: An Analysis of Environmental Sustainability Issues and Basic Needs in Saudi Universities: An Applied Study on Prince Sattam bin Abdulaziz University','<p>Following the Brundtland Report, sustainable research has become a cornerstone of global efforts to achieve sustainable development, with universities playing a pivotal role in promoting environmental, social, and economic sustainability. Despite growing interest in sustainability in higher education, the integration of strategic planning into sustainability-oriented research practices remains largely unexplored, particularly in developing contexts. This study investigates how to enhance strategic planning for sustainability in Saudi universities, focusing on Prince Sattam bin Abdulaziz University as a case study. It examines the direct and indirect relationships between strategic planning and sustainable research through two mediating factors: environmental sustainability issues and basic needs. Data were collected through a structured questionnaire distributed to academic leaders, quality coordinators, and department heads (187 participants) and analyzed using partial least squares structural equation modeling (PLS-SEM). The results demonstrate that strategic planning exerts a significant positive impact on environmental, social, and economic sustainability, both directly and indirectly, through environmental sustainability issues and basic needs. Specifically, effective strategic planning enhances a university’s ability to address environmental challenges and meet institutional and human needs, thereby fostering a supportive environment for sustainability-oriented research. The Environmental Sustainability Issues Index (ESI) and the Basic Needs Index (BN) were found to be powerful intermediaries between strategic planning (SP) and sustainable scientific research (SSR), underscoring their crucial role in translating strategic initiatives into measurable sustainability outcomes.<br/>Theoretically, this study enhances the understanding of how environmental sustainability functions as a mechanism for institutionalizing sustainability in higher education, proposing a novel model that integrates strategic planning, sustainability science, and socio-ecological systems theory. Practically, the research provides a replicable framework for universities to integrate environmental sustainability into their strategic agendas, allocate resources effectively, and assess socio-ecological sustainability performance through interconnected indicators of strategic planning (SP), the Environmental Sustainability Index (ESI), and the Basic Needs Index (BN).<br/>At the societal level, the study reinforces the role of universities as catalysts for sustainable development and contributors to the UN 2030 Agenda and Saudi Arabia’s Vision 2030. By aligning institutional strategies with environmental and social priorities, universities can enhance the importance of research, community engagement, and societal resilience. The findings highlight that meeting basic needs and engaging in environmental sustainability are not only prerequisites for academic excellence but also key drivers of societal transformation toward sustainability.</p>','["SP, SSR, ESI, BN, Higher Education, Saudi Arabia, PLS-SEM"]',NULL,21,1,'1820 - 1840','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1328','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',95),
(1329,1329,1,'The Role of Strategic Leadership, Knowledge Management, and Human Resource Management in Enhancing the Future Competitiveness of Saudi Business Organizations in Future Economies, Using Social Responsibility as a Moderating Variable.','<p>In an era defined by globalization, technological advancement, and sustainability imperatives, enhancing future competitiveness orientation (FCO) is critical for business survival and growth. This study investigates the role of Strategic Leadership (SL), Knowledge Management (KM), and Human Resource Management (HRM) in fostering the FCO of Saudi business organizations, with Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR) examined both as a direct contributor and as a moderating variable. Grounded in the Resource-Based View (RBV), Knowledge-Based View (KBV), and Stakeholder Theory, the study utilizes a quantitative research design, collecting data from 329 valid responses using a structured questionnaire and analysing the results via Partial Least Squares Structural Equation Modeming (PLS-SEM).<br/>Findings reveal that SL, KM, HRM, and CSR each positively and significantly influence FCO, confirming their strategic importance in achieving sustained competitive advantage. However, CSR failed to moderate the relationships between SL, KM, and HRM with FCO, suggesting that its strategic integration within Saudi firms remains underdeveloped. The rejection of these moderation hypotheses challenges assumptions of automatic synergy between CSR and other strategic functions, pointing instead to the need for deliberate CSR alignment with core business strategies.<br/>The study offers theoretical contributions by extending the RBV and KBV frameworks to include CSR as a direct strategic resource in the Saudi context. Practically, it provides actionable insights for executives and policymakers on prioritizing leadership development, knowledge systems, and human capital as key levers of competitiveness. It also calls for gradual integration of CSR into strategic domains rather than treating it as a universal moderator. On a societal level, the research supports CSR as a driver of reputation, stakeholder trust, and sustainable growth, aligning with Saudi Vision 2030 and UN Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs 8, 9, 12). </p>','["SL, KM, HRM, CSR, Saudi business organizations, PLS-SEM"]',NULL,21,1,'1841 - 1862','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1329','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',96),
(1332,1332,1,'Association Between Nurse Managerial Leadership and Nurse Retention: A Cross-Sectional Study at a Tertiary Hospital in Saudi Arabia','<p>Background: Retaining experienced nurses is a major priority for healthcare organizations because workforce stability is essential for delivering safe, effective, and high-quality patient care. Nurse managers play a critical leadership role in shaping the work environment, influencing job satisfaction, and encouraging nurses to remain within their organizations. Despite growing international evidence, research examining this association in Saudi Arabian tertiary hospitals remains limited.</p>
INSERT INTO publications (id,submission_id,version,title,abstract,keywords,references_text,issue_id,section_id,pages,doi,copyright_holder,copyright_year,license_url,status,date_published,seq) VALUES
(1335,1335,1,'Beyond Economic Efficiency: A Strategic Communication and Digital Governance Framework for Saudi Arabia’s Unified Health Insurance Reform toward Vision 2030','<p>Background: Saudi Arabia is in the process of moving towards a Unified Health Insurance (UHI) system as part of the health transformation agenda of Vision 2030. <br/>Problem: The Cooperative Health Insurance System (CHIS) has been expanding coverage but there are limited studies to date showing its economic efficiency, measured as highest health outcomes per dollar spent. This study incorporates a strategic communication and digital governance lens by asserting that the fiscal dimension is not enough to insure the success of an insurance-led health reform.<br/>Goal: To measure the economic efficiency of Saudi Arabia''s UHI system between 2018 and 2025 and see how it is progressing in relation to the Vision 2030 goals. <br/>Methods: We used publicly available data reports from the Council of Cooperative Health Insurance (CCHI), Ministry of Health (MOH), General Authority for Statistics (GASTAT), Vision 2030 dashboards, WHO Global Health Expenditure Database (GHED) and World Bank for our secondary data analysis. The key indicators were: proportion of health expenditure to GDP, out-of-pocket (OOP) payments, cost per beneficiary, claims-to-premiums ratio and coverage equity. <br/>Results: In 2024, at least ~62% of population was covered by CHIS. Health insurance premiums are the highest in the insurance market at 59% of the total insurance market premiums (Ameen, 2025). OOP payments declined from 33% (2018) to 26% (2023) (WHO GHED). It was found that the average cost per insured individual was SAR 2,720/year with a ratio of claims/premiums of 75%. However, rural coverage rate was less than 12% (GASTAT, 2024) and the average of premium inflation was 15% per year (Alzahrani et al., 2025). Said to lag behind the UAE (OOP: 22%) and Bahrain (OOP: 18%) in terms of financial protection.<br/>In summary, CHIS has improved access and alleviated fiscal pressure, but has also experienced challenges with efficiency issues, such as affordability, geographic access, and fragmentation of services. The reforms should focus on premiums that are sensitive to income, quality standards and investment in rural infrastructure. Ongoing monitoring of efficiency indicators is critical to make sure the fiscal sustainability of strengthening UHC and equity.</p>','["Unified Health Insurance", "Economic Efficiency", "Vision 2030", "Saudi Arabia", "Health Financing", "Out-of-Pocket Payments. Unified Health Insurance", "Strategic Communication", "Digital Governance", "Public Trust", "Universal Health Coverage", "Health Financing"]',NULL,21,1,'1909 - 1930','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1335','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',100),
(1336,1336,1,'Explainable AI For Social Media Opinion Analysis Using Efficient Language Modelling','<p>Social-media sentiment classification is difficult because posts are short, informal, context-dependent, and unevenly distributed across classes. It was observed that five TF-IDF classifiers (Logistic Regression, Linear SVM, Multinomial Naïve Bayes, Random Forest, and Gradient Boosting) were compared with the compact DistilBERT and MiniLM models. Fixed TweetEval consisted of 45,615 training posts, 2,000 validation posts, and 12,284 test posts. The main selection criterion was the validation macro-F1. To sum up, MiniLM outperformed the strongest classical baseline, Logistic Regression, by 11.8573 macro-F1 points on test set. This improvement was confirmed by Holm-adjusted McNemar and bootstrap analyses and was considered to be statistically and practically significant. Even though it required more resources than classical alternatives, MiniLM met the required criteria predefined, and in addition had a smaller artifact and lower energy proxy than DistilBERT. Explainability used global Logistic Regression TF-IDF coefficients and local DistilBERT Integrated Gradients. Overall, top-token masking reduced the probability more than the random masking in 30 cases, lending support to the attribution faithfulness. Findings help in the selection of practical opinion-monitoring models.</p>','["Social media opinion analysis", "Sentiment classification", "Explainable artificial intelligence", "TF-IDF", "Machine learning models"]',NULL,21,1,'1931 - 1955','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1336','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',101),
(1337,1337,1,'AI-Powered HACCP Risk Prediction System: Machine Learning Framework for Predictive Risk Assessment in HACCP-Based Food Safety Systems','<p>The health of people depends heavily on the safety of food, and the risk management of food contamination is needed to prevent foodborne diseases. Conventionally, HACCP (Hazard Analysis and Critical Control Points) systems are deployed, but they are restrictive in their ability to control food safety risks, relying on threshold levels and inspection controls. This paper presents a proposal for an AI-based HACCP risk-prediction system that uses machine learning algorithms to forecast the likelihood of food contamination from real-time sensor data. XGBoost, Random Forest, Gradient Boosting, and Logistic Regression machine learning algorithms were deployed on the food contamination dataset from the Kaggle competition to determine the risk of food contamination based on environmental factors such as temperature, humidity, and time spent. XGBoost outperformed all other models, achieving the highest accuracy (90.1%) and ROC AUC (0.92). Various measures were used to assess the accuracy of the XGBoost model''s predictions. The performance of the XGBoost model was evaluated using accuracy, precision, recall, F1 score, and ROC curves. This suggests applying predictive analytics to HACCP systems to shift the risk management paradigm from reactive to proactive, enabling food safety managers to address risks before they reach critical thresholds. This is likely to enhance the food safety management system by increasing cost reduction and providing real-time monitoring through the introduction of machine learning into HACCP frameworks. It is believed that this proposed framework will offer significant benefits in predicting food contamination through the HACCP system. To enhance the validity of the models, future studies may use deep learning methods and real-time data processing.</p>','["HACCP, AI-powered risk prediction, machine learning, XGBoost, contamination risk, food safety management, predictive analytics, real-time monitoring, Random Forest, Gradient Boosting"]',NULL,21,1,'1956 - 1978','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1337','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',102),
(1354,1354,1,'The Missing Link in Guesthouse Sustainability: Institutional Factors Beyond the Triple Bottom Line','<p>The rapid growth of the guesthouse sector has transformed the tourism landscape of the Maldives by promoting local entrepreneurship, broadening community participation, and diversifying tourism products beyond the traditional resort model. Despite its economic significance, sustaining guesthouse operations remains a pressing challenge due to increasing environmental vulnerability, socio-economic pressures, and institutional constraints. Although the Triple Bottom Line (TBL) framework has been extensively applied to examine tourism sustainability, existing studies have predominantly focused on the economic, social, and environmental dimensions, with limited attention given to the behavioural and institutional mechanisms that underpin sustainable organisational performance. Addressing this gap, this study extends the TBL framework by integrating Institutional Theory to investigate the direct effects of psychological, economic, social, and environmental factors on guesthouse sustainability and the mediating role of institutional factors in these relationships. A quantitative cross-sectional research design was adopted, and primary data were collected from registered guesthouse stakeholders across the Maldives using a structured questionnaire. The proposed conceptual model was analysed using Covariance-Based Structural Equation Modelling (CB-SEM). The findings reveal that psychological, economic, social, and environmental factors each exert significant positive effects on guesthouse sustainability. Moreover, institutional factors significantly enhance sustainability outcomes and partially mediate the relationships between the four sustainability dimensions and guesthouse sustainability, highlighting governance and institutional support as critical enablers of sustainable tourism development. By identifying institutional factors as the missing link in the sustainability framework, this study advances the theoretical application of the Triple Bottom Line and Institutional Theory within the context of small tourism enterprises. It also provides practical insights for policymakers and tourism practitioners by emphasising the need for integrated governance, effective regulatory frameworks, and collaborative stakeholder engagement to foster resilient and sustainable guesthouse development in Small Island Developing States.</p>','["Guesthouse sustainability, Triple Bottom Line, Institutional Theory, Institutional factors, Sustainable tourism", "Small Island Developing States, Maldives."]',NULL,21,1,'1979-2007','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1354','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',103),
(1570,1570,1,'An Intelligent Metaheuristic-Assisted Feedforward Neural Network Framework for Accurate Alzheimer’s Disease Classification Using Medical Diagnostic Data','<p>Alzheimer''s disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disease which represents a major burden of global health. The early and accurate classification of the stages of AD is crucial for early clinical intervention. In this paper, an intelligent hybrid system combining a Feedforward Neural Network (FNN) and a metaheuristic optimization method known as Grey Wolf Optimizer (GWO) is proposed for accurate classification of Alzheimer''s disease based on multi-modal medical diagnostic information. To overcome the drawbacks of gradient-based learning such as falling into local minima and convergence slowdown, the GWO is used to optimize the FNN''s hyperparameters and synaptic weights. The proposed GWO-FNN model is tested on two datasets: The Open Access Series of Imaging Studies (OASIS) dataset and Alzheimer''s Disease Neuroimaging Initiative (ADNI) dataset. The experimental results show that the GWO-FNN outperforms the conventional machine learning models and the standalone neural networks, with a classification accuracy of 97.4%, sensitivity of 96.8%, specificity of 97.9% and AUC of 0.987. The results highlight the effectiveness of Deep Optimization using a Metaheuristic approach in clinical neurodiagnostic system.</p>','["Alzheimer''s Disease, Feedforward Neural Network, Grey Wolf Optimizer, Metaheuristic Optimization, Medical Diagnosis, Classification, Machine Learning, OASIS, ADNI"]',NULL,21,1,'2008-2017','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1570','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-01',104),
(1571,1571,1,'A Secure Optimization-Driven Defence Strategy Against Node Capture Attacks in Wireless Sensor Networks Using Improved Flower Pollination Optimization','<p>Wireless Sensor Networks (WSNs) have been a game-changer in the field of sensor networks and form the backbone of various applications in the real world from smart homes to mission-critical military sensor networks. The dispersed and limited resources of sensor nodes introduce specific vulnerabilities, most significantly that the adversary can gain access to the cryptographic material and be able to introduce malicious nodes back into the network through a node capture attack. This paper introduces a novel bio-inspired security framework called Improved Flower Pollination Optimization Defence Strategy Algorithm (IFPO-DSA), which consists of nature-mimicking global and local pollination operators and applies Hankel matrix-based pairwise key management to simultaneously achieve optimal coverage, routing and cryptographic key distribution. The simulation results for NS-2 are presented comprehensively, indicating that the algorithm outperforms the benchmark Fruit Fly Optimization Algorithm (FFOA) and FiRAO-PG in all the analyzed metrics with maximum throughput of 458.74 kbps, Packet Delivery Ratio (PDR) of 81.56%, energy consume of 18.2%, and control overhead of 64.36%, making it an efficient and scalable defense mechanism in large-scale deployments of WSNs.</p>','["Wireless Sensor Networks, Node Capture Attack, Flower Pollination Optimization, Key Management, Hankel Matrix, Network Security, Bio-Inspired Computing"]',NULL,21,1,'2018-2032','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1571','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-01',105),
(6420,6420,1,'Hybrid Deep Learning Framework for Automated Sunspot Segmentation, Classification, and Foreshortening-Corrected Area Measurement on SDO/HMI Images','<p>Automated analysis of SDO/HMI continuum images requires sunspot masks, umbra/penumbra labels, foreshortening-corrected areas and group-level morphology, which are normally produced by separate tools. This paper presents a six-stage hybrid deep-learning framework, implemented in a browser-based workstation: limb-darkening correction and hybrid anisotropic-diffusion/non-local-means (AAD–NLM) denoising; binary sunspot segmentation with a Hybrid ConvNeXt U-Net and SegFormer-B4; model comparison; umbra/penumbra classification with Efficient Channel Attention (ECA) and area measurement; and McIntosh Zpc group classification with constraint-aware decoding. We give the mathematical model of every stage, prove that constrained decoding never lowers exact-match accuracy, and derive an error-amplification law for foreshortening correction. Results are tagged as measured, reported or simulated. In a controlled noise-injection experiment (three noise levels, ten seeds) the hybrid AAD→NLM filter was best only at the strongest noise (SSIM 0.462 against 0.442 for NLM; threshold-mask IoU 0.781 against 0.768), NLM alone was best at mild noise, and AAD→NLM beat NLM→AAD at every level. End-to-end processing of a still image gave 13 groups, 14 spots, 6165 µHem of umbra, 15957 µHem of penumbra and a Wolf number of 144, with an area chain that is internally exact, yet four of the 13 groups were image-edge artefacts, and a low-resolution video exposed limb-ring and overlay false detections. A reported ConvNeXt U-Net test IoU of 87.3% against 66.65% for SegFormer-B4 conflicts with simulated cycle-generalisation results that favour SegFormer-B4 (mean Dice 0.870 against 0.837), so the architecture ordering is left open and a matched-protocol retraining is specified.</p>','["Sunspot segmentation", "SDO/HMI", "Hybrid ConvNeXt U-Net", "SegFormer-B4", "anisotropic diffusion", "non-local means", "efficient channel attention", "foreshortening correction", "McIntosh classification"]',NULL,21,1,'2033 - 2065',NULL,'The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-28',106),
(993,993,1,'Comparative Analysis of User Sentiment towards Public Opinion on Stunting using K-Nearest Neighbor, Naive Bayes, and Decision Tree Algorithm','<p>Stunting is a critical public health issue, particularly in developing countries, with long-term impacts on physical and cognitive development due to chronic malnutrition. In the digital era, public sentiment, and opinion on stunting, as reflected on social media platforms, can provide valuable insights for policymakers and healthcare providers. However, there has been limited research combining multiple machine-learning algorithms for sentiment analysis on this topic. This study aims to conduct a comparative analysis of user sentiment on X regarding public opinion on stunting using three widely utilized text classification algorithms: K-Nearest Neighbour (KNN), Naïve Bayes, and Decision Tree. The research involves collecting and annotating sentiment data (positive, negative, neutral) from social media platforms like Twitter and X, followed by model training and evaluation using metrics such as accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score. Results from the analysis of 1227 data points (2023-2024) with an 80:20 train-test split revealed that Naïve Bayes achieved the highest accuracy at 87.18%, followed by Decision Tree at 79.83%, and KNN at 57.56%. Meanwhile, a comparison using a 70:30 train-test split yielded similar results, with Naïve Bayes achieving 86.41%, Decision Tree 78.41%, and KNN 54.48%. The study demonstrates the effectiveness of the Naïve Bayes algorithm for sentiment analysis in public health contexts, offering a novel approach to understanding public opinion on stunting. By leveraging social media data, this research provides real-time insights that can guide policy and public health interventions. Furthermore, the comparative analysis of algorithms provides valuable knowledge on the most effective models for sentiment analysis in complex, sentiment-driven public health issues. The study emphasizes the role of machine learning in addressing malnutrition and stunting through data-driven policy decisions.</p>','["Comparative Sentiment Analysis", "Public Opinion", "Stunting", "K-Nearest Neighbor", "Na\\u00efve Bayes", "Decision Tree."]',NULL,20,1,'01 - 20','10.59543/jidmis.v3.993','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',1),
(994,994,1,'The Impact of AI-Based Applications on Payroll Management and Recruitment Processes in Private Universities and Colleges in Oman','<p>Artificial Intelligence (AI) is one of the most versatile innovations to date and plays a major role within the world of business. It made major changes to organization management of daily activities in terms of tasks automation, processing large amounts of information, and the enhancement of accuracy and efficiency. Within Human Resource Management and Finance sector, AI has significantly made positive impact particularly upon recruitment and payroll management. AI as technology saves time, decreases errors and improves informed decisions of organizations.<br/>AI-based systems are common in the application of handling payroll calculations, identifying qualified candidates and ascertain compliance related to taxation and compensation policies. AI also provides comprehensive perspectives for managers to craft improved strategic decisions, increase transparency and aggregate work performance by the employees. However, organizations still face challenges despite the advantages during the application of AI, which include algorithms bias, high costs of implementation, privacy risk and resistance being shown by employees due to the setup of new systems.<br/>This study focuses on private universities and colleges in Oman, such as Majan University College, Mazoon College, Al Sharqiyah University, and Middle East College, etc., because these institutions have well-structured HR departments that manage complex recruitment, payroll, and employee management processes. Private higher education institutions in Oman are increasingly adopting digital technologies to improve efficiency, transparency, and decision-making within HR functions. Focusing on these universities and colleges allows the study to examine real-world HR practices, assess the impact of AI on recruitment and payroll activities, and understand how AI can support fairness, efficiency, and sustainable development within the work environment.</p>','["Artificial Intelligence (AI), Payroll Management, Recruitment Processes"]',NULL,20,1,'21 - 35','10.59543/jidmis.v3.994','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',2),
(995,995,1,'Municipal Greenhouse Gas Inventory as a Decision-Support Tool for Climate Governance: Evidence from an Agricultural Municipality in the Philippines','<p>Local governments play a pivotal role in achieving national climate commitments; however, many rural municipalities lack evidence-based greenhouse gas (GHG) inventories to guide climate mitigation planning. This study evaluates the greenhouse gas emission profile of Rizal, Kalinga, Philippines, and demonstrates how municipal-scale inventories can strengthen evidence-based local climate governance through the enhanced Local Climate Change Action Plan (eLCCAP). A descriptive-analytical research design was employed using primary data collected from 744 respondents across households, farms, commercial establishments, institutions, and fuel suppliers, supplemented by municipal records and national activity datasets. Emissions were quantified using the Philippine Greenhouse Gas Inventory Quantification Support Spreadsheet following IPCC-based accounting protocols for Scope 1, Scope 2, and Scope 3 emissions. Results indicate that total municipal emissions reached 7,877.66 tCO₂e, with Scope 1 emissions accounting for 63.13%, Scope 2 for 36.20%, and Scope 3 for 0.67%. Agriculture was identified as the dominant emission sector, primarily driven by livestock and agricultural soils, followed by household and transport energy use and refrigeration and air-conditioning systems. Despite limited industrial activity, indirect electricity consumption represented a substantial share of total emissions, while existing carbon sequestration partially offset agricultural emissions. The inventory further enabled the identification of priority mitigation pathways including climate-smart agriculture, renewable energy adoption, energy-efficient cooling technologies, improved waste management, and institutional strengthening. The study demonstrates that municipal greenhouse gas inventories provide an effective decision-support framework for integrating localized emission evidence into climate policy, enabling rural local governments to prioritize cost-effective mitigation strategies while enhancing resilience and contributing to national decarbonization goals. These findings provide a replicable framework for climate governance in agricultural municipalities across developing countries.</p>','["Greenhouse gas inventory", "Climate governance", "Local climate change action plan", "Municipal emissions", "Climate-smart agriculture", "Low-carbon development"]',NULL,20,1,'36 - 51','10.59543/jidmis.v3.995','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',3),
(996,996,1,'Lightweight Six-Class Personal Protective Equipment Detection in Construction Scenes Using YOLO11n','<p>Personal protective equipment (PPE) detection is a critical task in construction safety management, yet real-world construction scenes are often affected by occlusion, scale variation, complex backgrounds, and small-object interference. To evaluate the applicability of lightweight object detection for practical site monitoring, this study investigates a six-class PPE detection task in construction scenes based on YOLO11n, covering person, hard hat, vest, safety boots, gloves, and mask. Experiments were conducted on a public PPE dataset containing 1,617 images, which were divided into 1,100 training images, 304 validation images, and 213 test images. The model was trained with an input size of 640 × 640 for 50 epochs and evaluated using Precision, Recall, <a class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="462b071606766873" href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection">[email protected]</a>, <a class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="1c715d4c5c2c3229" href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection">[email protected]</a>:0.95, and inference speed. The results show that YOLO11n achieved Precision, Recall, <a class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="8fe2cedfcfbfa1ba" href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection">[email protected]</a>, and <a class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="b9d4f8e9f989978c" href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection">[email protected]</a>:0.95 values of 0.821, 0.872, 0.846, and 0.566, respectively, on the test set, while maintaining an inference speed of approximately 103 FPS on an RTX 4050 Laptop GPU. Class-wise analysis indicates that person, hard hat, and vest were detected more reliably, whereas mask, gloves, and safety boots remained more challenging because of their small scale, occlusion sensitivity, and ambiguous visual features. Overall, the results demonstrate that lightweight YOLO11n provides a reasonable balance between detection accuracy and real-time performance for six-class PPE monitoring in construction environments, and it can serve as an effective baseline for future improvement in small-object enhancement and deployment-oriented optimization.</p>','["Construction safety", "personal protective equipment detection", "YOLO11n", "object detection", "small-object detection", "real-time monitoring"]',NULL,20,1,'52 - 68','10.59543/jidmis.v3.996','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',4),
(997,997,1,'Technology, Norms, and Sustainable Value in NEV Adoption: A TAM–TPB–VBN Model of Purchase Intention among China’s Generation Y','<p>New energy vehicles (NEVs) are promoted as sustainable mobility solutions, yet purchase intention among young consumers remains uneven. This study examines how technology perceptions, value-based motives, and social-psychological mechanisms shape NEV purchase intention. Using a structured online questionnaire distributed through automotive platforms and communities, 434 Chinese Generation Y consumers (aged 29–45) in Beijing were surveyed. Structural equation modeling was employed for analysis. Results indicate that perceived ease of use is the strongest driver, exerting significant direct and total effects. Perceived usefulness strengthens attitude and subjective norm, suggesting it influences intention through evaluative and social endorsement pathways. Both attitude and subjective norm significantly predict intention, confirming their roles as proximal psychological routes in high-involvement green consumption. Sustainable value positively affects attitude and directly increases purchase intention, indicating that moral alignment remains influential alongside usability. Facilitating conditions improve attitude but show weak, non-significant total effects on intention, implying contextual support functions primarily as a feasibility cue rather than an independent motivational trigger in well-connected urban settings. This study extends sustainable mobility adoption research by clarifying differentiated cognitive, normative, and value-based pathways and offers practical guidance for manufacturers, platform marketers, ecosystem partners, and policymakers seeking to accelerate NEV uptake among digitally active young consumers.</p>','["NEVs", "purchase intention", "perceived ease of use", "sustainable value", "subjective norm"]',NULL,20,1,'69 - 90','10.59543/jidmis.v3.997','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',5),
(998,998,1,'Using Food Waste: A Review of Carbon Nanomaterials for Sustainable Food Packaging Solutions','<p>The development of sustainable, multifunctional food packaging materials has enhanced a pressing concern as traditional plastics persist in causing global environmental pollution. Carbon-based nanomaterials, especially carbon dots (CDs), present exciting possibilities due to their compatibility with living organisms, fluorescent capabilities, and ability to improve material performance. This review involved the integration of green-synthesized carbon nanomaterials into a gelatin-chitosan matrix derived from discarded fish scales to produce a multifunctional bio-nanocomposite film (Gelatin/Chitosan/CDs). The films displayed outstanding antioxidant, antibacterial, UV-blocking, pH-responsive, and fluorescent properties. Different concentration of carbon nanomaterials was assessed to observe their effect on physical, structural, mechanical, and functional attributes of the packaging film. The optimal formulation, which contained 20% CNMs, showed significant enhancements in antioxidant and antimicrobial properties, improved UV protection, and better preservation of meat by minimizing nutrient loss and prolonging shelf life. Additionally, the intense blue fluorescence emitted by the carbon nanomaterials facilitated anti-counterfeiting and smart packaging capabilities. This Review also emphasizes the vast potential of carbon nanomaterials including graphene, carbon nanotubes, and carbon nanofibers in enhancing barrier properties, mechanical strength, and sensory functions in food packaging. Despite the considerable advantages provided by the incorporation of CNMs. Challenges remain concerning toxicity, migration behavior, long-term safety, and large-scale industrial application. In summary, the results highlight the potential of carbon nanomaterials as sustainable, high-performance elements for the next generation of active and intelligent food packaging solutions.</p>','["Carbon Nanomaterial, sustainable, food packaging, application of carbon nanomaterials"]',NULL,20,1,'91 - 115','10.59543/jidmis.v3.998','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',6),
(1000,1000,1,'Modelling Classroom Co-Regulation from LMS Interaction Logs for Real-Time Classroom Orchestration','<p>Real-time classroom orchestration requires teachers to infer collective regulatory dynamics from distributed student behavior under severe time constraints, yet existing learning analytics dashboards primarily surface activity metrics rather than modelling the underlying co-regulatory processes that should inform orchestration decisions. This study develops a classroom co-regulation framework based on a tri-variate regulatory state comprising task alignment, behavioral dispersion, and temporal stability derived from 60-second windows of Moodle Learning Management System interaction logs. The framework was evaluated through a seven-layer psychometric validation process involving four-rater expert annotations of 400 windows sampled from a corpus of 1,728 windows collected from undergraduate computing courses at a public university in Oman, with data gathered from 23 course sections over a ten-week period. Results demonstrate strong reliability and construct validity for the proposed state representation, as well as strong agreement among experts regarding instructional actions associated with observed classroom states. Further analysis showed that the derived regulatory indicators systematically differentiated among teaching, independent work, problem-solving, and navigation contexts, supporting their contextual sensitivity and discriminative power. The framework is implementable using standard learning management system export data without supplementary instrumentation, providing a practical foundation for human-in-the-loop classroom orchestration and teacher decision support. The proposed approach contributes a theory-grounded, psychometrically validated representation of classroom co-regulation suitable for real-time educational analytics and future classroom orchestration systems.</p>','["Classroom co-regulation, Learning analytics, Classroom orchestration, Moodle LMS, Computer and Human behavior, Teacher decision support, Intelligent tutoring system, AI in education."]',NULL,20,1,'116 - 149','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1000','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',7),
(1001,1001,1,'Rain-Proof Laundry: An IoT-Driven, Arduino-Based Smart Clothesline That Reacts to Weather in Real Time','<p>The primary aim of this research is to explore the significance of household chores, particularly focusing on the issues linked with traditional clotheslines. Although economical, conventional clotheslines often prove impractical in unpredictable weather, causing inconveniences like sudden rain soaking the laundry. To address this problem, an Automated Clothesline Retrieval System has been designed. This innovative system utilizes various sensors, such as a humidity sensor, light dependent resistor, moisture sensor, and rain sensor, to continuously monitor weather conditions and automatically retract the clothesline when needed. Solar-powered and integrated with an Arduino platform, the system also includes a Wi-Fi application that notifies users of weather changes and allows them to manually control the clothesline via app. By employing Internet of Things (IoT) technology, this system facilitates outdoor drying without the worry of adverse weather, thus improving the efficiency and convenience of laundry tasks in contemporary households.</p>','["clothesline retrieval system, internet of things, rain sensor, light dependent resistor"]',NULL,20,1,'150 - 165','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1001','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',8),
(1002,1002,1,'Generative AI and Entrepreneurial Psychological Capital for Sustainable SME Performance: The Roles of Hope, Resilience, and Adaptive Behavior in Emerging Economies according to SDG 3 (Good Health and Well-being)','<p>This study investigates the causal relationships among Institutional and Environmental Context (IEC), Support from Government and Related Organizations (SGRO), Digital Capability and Learning Skills of SMEs (DCLS), and Adaptive and Quality-driven Entrepreneurial Outcomes (AQE) in emerging-economy SMEs according to SDG 3 (Good health and well-being). Drawing on dynamic capability and entrepreneurial psychological capital perspectives, it examines how institutional conditions and organizational support enable SMEs to use generative AI to strengthen digital capability, learning, hope, resilience, and adaptive behavior for sustainable performance. Data were collected from 450 SME owners and managers using a structured questionnaire. Confirmatory Factor Analysis (CFA) validated the measurement model, while Structural Equation Modeling (SEM) tested the hypothesized relationships. The results showed that IEC significantly affects DCLS (β = 0.910, p &lt; 0.001), and SGRO positively influences DCLS (β = 0.072, p = 0.018). DCLS significantly affects AQE (β = 0.645, p &lt; 0.001), indicating that generative AI-enabled capability and learning support hopeful goal pursuit, resilience, adaptive behavior, quality improvement, and long-term competitiveness. SGRO also has a direct positive effect on AQE (β = 0.498, p &lt; 0.001), whereas IEC has no significant direct effect on AQE (β = −0.205, p = 0.189). Mediation analysis confirmed that DCLS significantly mediates the relationships between IEC and AQE (β = 0.587, p &lt; 0.001) and between SGRO and AQE (β = 0.046, p &lt; 0.001). The findings identify DCLS as a key mechanism linking external support, generative AI adoption, entrepreneurial psychological capital, and sustainable SME performance.</p>','["Generative AI, Entrepreneurial Psychological Capital, Digital Capability, Adaptive Behavior, SDG 3 (Good health and well-being)."]',NULL,20,1,'166 - 186','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1002','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',9),
(1004,1004,1,'Developing SDG-Based Indicators for 21st-Century Physical Education Program Evaluation','<p>The transformation of education under the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) requires innovative approaches to evaluating educational programs beyond traditional academic outcomes. Physical Education (PE) plays a crucial role in promoting physical literacy, lifelong learning, health, well-being, inclusion, and sustainability. However, existing PE evaluation frameworks often fail to align with Sustainable Development Goal 4 (SDG 4) and the competencies required in the twenty-first century. This study aimed to develop and validate a comprehensive SDG-based indicator framework for evaluating 21st-century Physical Education programs in higher education. A mixed-methods sequential exploratory design was employed. Phase I involved a systematic literature review of SDG 4 indicators, UNESCO Quality Physical Education guidelines, physical literacy frameworks, and 21st-century skills literature. Phase II utilized a three-round Delphi process with 25 experts in physical education, educational evaluation, sustainability education, and curriculum development. Phase III validated the proposed indicators through a survey of 420 physical education teachers and university lecturers. Confirmatory Factor Analysis (CFA) and Structural Equation Modeling (SEM) were applied to assess construct validity and model fit. Findings: The study identified five dimensions comprising 25 indicators: (1) Physical Literacy Development, (2) Sustainable Learning Competencies, (3) Health and Well-Being Outcomes, (4) Inclusion and Equity, and (5) Global Citizenship and Sustainability Awareness. The measurement model demonstrated satisfactory validity and reliability (χ²/df = 1.96, CFI = 0.97, TLI = 0.96, RMSEA = 0.047, SRMR = 0.041). All factor loadings exceeded 0.70 and composite reliability values ranged from 0.88 to 0.95.</p>','["Sustainable Development Goal 4", "Physical Education Evaluation", "Physical Literacy", "Quality Physical Education", "21st-Century Skills", "Sustainability Education", "Indicator Development"]',NULL,20,1,'187 - 196','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1004','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',10),
(1005,1005,1,'Artificial Intelligence Applications in Educational Management: Improving Decision Quality and Organizational Performance','<p>Innovative approaches to the use of artificial intelligence (AI) in educational institutions are progressively changing administrative decision-making from retrospective reporting to predictive, explainable and evidence-guided action. We propose an Artificial Intelligence Educational Management for Decision Quality and Organizational Performance (AIEM-DQOP) framework for improving student success analytics, dropout-risk detection, faculty &amp; course analytics, resource allocation, as well as institutional dashboarding and governance accountability. The study is based on the Open University Learning Analytics Dataset (OULAD), which acts as a primary benchmark dataset, with the UCI Higher Education Students Performance Evaluation dataset used for external validation. The methodology consists of a PRISMA-style review, data preprocessing and feature engineering, the construction of machine-learning and deep learning models, explainable artificial intelligence and fairness audit paired with human-in-the-loop decision control. Comparative models are logistic regression, random forest, XGBoost, LSTM and a hybrid XAI-assisted ensemble. The predictive performance, decision-quality indicators and organizational performance indicators are used to evaluate the framework. The results demonstrate that while hybrid provides better benchmark performances relative to baseline methods, governance improves interpretability, accountability, and decision tracing. A Q2 Scopus-level educational technology and higher-education management research contribution is an integrated, reproducible and governance-oriented model.</p>','["Artificial Intelligence", "Educational Management", "Decision Quality", "Organizational Performance", "Learning Analytics", "Explainable AI", "OULAD Dataset", "Predictive Analytics", "Resource Allocation", "Higher Education Governance."]',NULL,20,1,'197 - 215','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1005','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',11),
(1006,1006,1,'Managerial and Technological Innovation''s Effects on Operational Performance at Iraqi Public Universities through the Mediating Role of Information Systems','<p>The main goals of higher educational institutions in modern times are to maintain a competitive advantage and reach the excellence of institutions. Information systems (IS) can be used by universities to improve their operational performance (OP). But implementation of information systems (IS) is not achieved in a vacuum, it is rather dependent on the evolution of supportive innovations. The extant literature has been more so concerned with the possible antecedents of IS with no knowledge about how IS mediates the relationship between the OP and the types of innovations in the higher education sector. Hence, the current paper examines the mediation role of information systems (IS) in the Iraqi context of universities in the framework of operational performance (OP), technological innovation (TEI), and managerial innovation (MEI). The quantitative cross-sectional research design was used. The sample size presented in the study was 420 academic and administrative employees, due to the convenience sampling technique, in 15 publicly owned Iraqi universities. The data was analyzed with the help of Structural Equation Modeling (SEM) by using Smart PLS. The findings indicate that both TEI and MEI positively influence OP and IS in a significant direct way. For the same reason, IS affects OP positively and significantly. The indirect mediation analysis proves that the role of an IS as a mediator is substantial in the relationships between TEI, MEI, and OP. The conclusion also has relevancy to the body of literature in higher education management and has practical implications for the university administrators and policymakers.</p>','["Iraq, Higher Education, Information Systems, Operational Performance, Technological Innovation, Managerial Innovation."]',NULL,20,1,'216 - 232','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1006','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',12),
(1020,1020,1,'Design and Implementation of an AI/ML-Enabled Azure Orchestration Framework for Regulated Enterprise Workflows','<p>This paper presents the design and implementation of an AI/ML-enabled Azure-native orchestration framework aimed at addressing the growing integration challenges of regulated enterprise environments, particularly within the financial and healthcare sectors. Rapid digital transformation and cloud adoption have intensified the need for scalable, secure, and compliant workflow orchestration across heterogeneous systems. The proposed framework leverages core Azure services, including Azure Logic Apps, Azure Data Factory, Azure Functions, Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS), and Azure Machine Learning, to enable intelligent automation, real-time data processing, and predictive decision support. AI and machine learning models are embedded within the orchestration layer to enhance adaptive workflow coordination, anomaly detection, and policy-driven execution. Security, governance, and regulatory compliance are incorporated through identity management, audit logging, encryption, and compliance-aware workflow controls aligned with industry standards. The architecture is evaluated using domain-specific use cases from financial transaction processing and healthcare data interoperability to assess performance, scalability, resilience, and compliance readiness. Experimental results demonstrate improved interoperability, reduced operational complexity, enhanced fault tolerance, and lower integration costs compared to conventional rule-based orchestration approaches. Overall, the study highlights the effectiveness of cloud-first, intelligent orchestration frameworks in modernizing enterprise workflows within data-intensive and regulation-sensitive industries.</p>','["Azure Cloud", "AI/ML", "Workflow Orchestration", "Enterprise Integration", "Financial Systems", "Healthcare Systems", "Cloud-First Architecture"]',NULL,20,1,'233 - 245','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1020','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',13),
(1027,1027,1,'Retracted','<p>Retracted</p>',NULL,NULL,20,1,'246 - 269','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1027','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',14),
(1028,1028,1,'Performance evaluation of Fuzzy and Intuitionistic fuzzy M/M/1 Jackson-Type Network Queue under Uncertain Parameters','<p>Classical queueing models usually assume that arrival and service parameters are known precisely; however, in many practical service systems these parameters are uncertain due to demand variation, service fluctuations, incomplete information, and operational ambiguity. This paper presents the performance evaluation of a finite-capacity M/M/1 Jackson-type network queue under fuzzy and intuitionistic fuzzy environments. The suggested network is composed of two service nodes and one Central Server (Head Office). Customers are coming from outside the system at nodes 1, 2 and the Head Office and if a customer needs more services at nodes 1 and 2, he may be referred to the Head Office. The transient behaviour of the system is formulated through Kolmogorov forward equations and represented using an infinitesimal generator matrix. Since closed-form solutions are difficult for the considered finite-capacity network with routing and impatience, the system is solved numerically using the fourth-order Runge–Kutta method in MATLAB. Triangular and trapezoidal fuzzy numbers are used to represent uncertain arrival and service rates, and α-cut analysis is applied to obtain interval bounds of performance measures. The model is further extended to intuitionistic fuzzy environments by applying the α-cut and the β-cut techniques which include membership, non-membership and hesitation. The numerical and graphical results indicate that the uncertainty decreases with the increase of the α-cut value and the intuitionistic fuzzy model with the β-cut gives a more compact and stable performance interval. It is observed that as per the node-wise comparison the Head Office has the highest expected system length and mean waiting time and the same can be interpreted that the Head Office is the main bottleneck of the network.</p>','["M/M/1 queue", "Jackson-type network", "fuzzy queueing model", "intuitionistic fuzzy queue", "transient analysis", "Runge\\u2013Kutta method", "expected system length", "mean waiting time"]',NULL,20,1,'270 - 294','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1028','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',15),
(1029,1029,1,'Predicting Student Performance: A Comparative Analysis of Machine Learning Algorithms on HSLC Exam Data','<p>This paper compares three machine learning algorithms—k-Nearest Neighbours (k-NN), Random Forest, as well as Support Vector Machine (SVM)—for predicting high school student performance, using actual exam data from 12,211 students in Jorhat, Assam. The most important decision was to keep all student records (109 absent students and 2 withheld results) instead of deleting them. This methodology made the models more realistic and applicable to real schools. The results demonstrate that Random Forest achieved the highest accuracy at 69.63%, followed by k-NN at 58.08%, and SVM at 57.35%. The total percentage of the grand total and minimum subject mark emerged as the most accurate predictors of student success. Current research demonstrates that it is now possible to identify struggling students with high accuracy, enabling timely interventions that support academic success.</p>','["Machine Learning, k-NN, SVM, Student Performance Prediction, Random Forest, HSLC Exam, Educational Data Mining, Assam."]',NULL,20,1,'295 - 302','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1029','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',16),
(1030,1030,1,'Toward a Semantic SCOR-VSM Framework for Supply Chain Performance Evaluation','<p>Supply chain performance assessment requires models that are capable of linking strategic, tactical, and operational data in a consistent and traceable manner. SCOR provides a standardized process and performance framework, while Value Stream Mapping (VSM) captures operational flows, waste, and machine-level data. However, SCOR remains weakly connected to shop-floor observations, and VSM often supports local improvement without aggregating data into global supply chain indicators. To address this gap, this paper proposes a conceptual architecture integrating SCOR, VSM, and ontology through a semantic, data-driven pivot layer. Rather than extending SCOR to a universal Level 4, which would be subject to industry limitations, our approach normalizes VSM data at the machine level and semantically maps it to SCOR Level 3 processes. The ontology formalizes relationships among operators, activities, resources, flows, contexts, constraints, and metrics. The proposed prototype supports traceability from shop-floor data to global performance evaluation and prepares future implementation in digital supply chain environments, including process mining, industrial IoT, and digital twin-based decision support.</p>','["SCOR model, value stream mapping, ontology, OWL/RDF, supply chain performance, semantic integration, digital supply chain"]',NULL,20,1,'303 - 312','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1030','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',17),
(1031,1031,1,'A Novel Approach to Renewable Energy Storage: Enhancing Efficiency with Advanced Lithium-Ion Battery Technologies','<p>To operate renewable-energy systems, there is a need to have efficient storage solutions to address the temporal mismatch between the variable generation and the demand of the load. This paper suggests a more advanced lithium-ion battery management system as a way of enhancing the performance of the renewable-energy storage in a solar-wind-commercial load system. A time-simulated framework of an hourly simulation was developed in Austin Texas using a normalized 1 MW commercial load profile, 1 MW photovoltaic generation, 1 MW wind generation and a 1 MW/4 MWh lithium-ion battery energy storage system. The experiment compared a traditional rule-based dispatch strategy with a state-of-the-art battery management system with efficiency-conscious operation, a protective state-of-charge window, derating power consumption based on temperature, and reserve preservation with short horizon. The annual dataset had 8,760 hourly observations with the total load demand of 3,853.72 MWh and the renewable generation of 3,127.67 MWh. The outcomes indicate that the suggested plan enhanced the efficiency of operations in terms of round-trip efficiency of 85.38 to 90.23 and annual battery energy losses of 44.00 MWh to 26.11 MWh. Final state of health improved to 99.112% and capacity decreased to 0.888%. However, the progressive approach decreased renewable curtailment of 365.04 MWh to 396.92 MWh and grid import of 1,133.89 MWh to 1,148.45 MWh, which represents a calculated trade-off between battery health maintenance and maximum renewable uptake. In general, the research shows that the high-performance Li-ion battery management can improve storage performance and decrease the stress of degradation of renewable-energy sources.</p>','["Renewable energy storage, Battery management system, Round-trip efficiency, Solar-wind microgrid, Lithium-ion battery."]',NULL,20,1,'313 - 331','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1031','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',18);
INSERT INTO publications (id,submission_id,version,title,abstract,keywords,references_text,issue_id,section_id,pages,doi,copyright_holder,copyright_year,license_url,status,date_published,seq) VALUES
(1032,1032,1,'Gamified Digital Strategies for Learning Basic Concepts in Early Childhood Education Through Virtual Environments','<p>Introduction of Information and Communication Technology (ICT) in early childhood education is one of the most important aspects of technology. This study examined the impact of digital initiatives through the use of spatial, temporal, quantitative and classified-based mathematics techniques in elementary and vocational school students. A pre-screening method has been used. The sample, consisting of 41 children, was divided into a test group (n=23), using a micromodule on the MoodleCloud platform (which combines tools such as Wordwall and Genially), and a control group (n=18). The results of this study showed significant changes in the trial group (t = 8.45; p &lt; .001), to the highest level (D = 3.12). This study shows that organized gamification not only improves brain coordination, but also acts as a catalyst for energy and activity independence in the early stages of work. The idea that technology transcends infrastructure is based on the expertise of teachers and teaching methods to address the technology gap. Thus, this approach that combines technology as a tool for intellectual equality, has shown that, when the technology of education is strong, the real environment becomes a place of learning, transcending the boundaries of analog technology in its infancy.</p>','["Early Childhood Education", "Gamification", "ICT-mediated learning", "Virtual Learning Environment", "Digital Teaching Competence."]',NULL,20,1,'332 - 344','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1032','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',19),
(1033,1033,1,'Modeling and dq-Axis Control of a Unity Power Factor and DC-Link Voltage Regulated Grid-Connected: Permanent Magnet Wind Energy Conversion System of Synchronous Generator','<p>This paper describes a full modeling and control scheme of a grid-connected Permanent Magnet Synchronous Generator (PMSG) based wind energy conversion system. The main goal is to keep a stable DC-link voltage and to work at a unity power factor. The suggested system design consists of a wind turbine coupled to a PMSG, where the PMSG variable frequency output is rectified to a DC connection and then inverted to grid compatible AC. The mathematical model of the PMSG is created in the spinning dq-reference frame. This permits decoupled torque and flux control using stator voltage dynamics. The electromagnetic torque is proportional to the q-axis current. The mechanical dynamics of the turbine-generator system is described by the swing equation. The control system is hierarchical. The outer Proportional-Integral (PI) controller controls the voltage of the DC-link and generates the reference d-axis current which determines the active power flow. The reference q-axis current is adjusted to zero in order to keep the unity power factor and avoid reactive power exchange with the grid. These reference currents are then used as inputs to inner current control loops that calculate reference voltages for the pulse width modulation inverter. A three-phase Phase Locked Loop (PLL) synchronizes the generator and is used to monitor the grid phase angle and frequency. The active and reactive power consumption is calculated using the dq components in synchronism. The main contribution of this work is the systematic inclusion of the PMSG model, the DC-link regulation and the dq-axis current control into one framework. This allows the continuous transmission of active power with small variations of reactive power. Moreover, the proposed method offers an effective framework for harmonic analysis and system optimization in wind energy applications.</p>','["Wind energy conversion system, Grid-connected inverter, PMSG, dq-axis control, PLL, Total harmonic distortion, FFT analysis."]',NULL,20,1,'345 - 356','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1033','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',20),
(1034,1034,1,'Modern Algorithmic Strategies in Cross-Domain Recommender Systems: A Comparative Review of Knowledge Transfer, Representation Learning, and Evaluation Trends (2018–2025)','<p>Cross-domain recommender systems (CDRS) have become an important research direction for addressing sparsity, cold-start, and fragmented user-behavior problems that cannot be solved effectively within a single domain. In recent years, the field has expanded rapidly from early neural transfer models into a broad landscape that includes graph-based methods, sequential recommendation, adversarial alignment, contrastive learning, disentanglement, multimodal transfer, federated recommendation, and emerging LLM-assisted approaches. This paper presents a comparative review of modern algorithmic strategies in CDRS published between 2018 and 2025. [21, 23, 32, 54] Unlike reviews that summarize the literature only chronologically, this study organizes prior work by algorithm family and compares how different methods perform cross-domain transfer, what assumptions they rely on, what types of datasets and user signals they use, and where their major strengths and limitations lie. The review integrates representative journal and conference studies from major venues and presents a taxonomy of modern CDRS methods, cross-family comparison tables, a method-evolution timeline, a dataset-and-evaluation pipeline view, and a research-gap map. [57, 59] The comparative analysis shows that graph and knowledge-graph methods are particularly strong in structurally rich and sparse settings, sequential methods are especially effective for temporally evolving behavior, contrastive and disentanglement-based approaches improve robustness and negative-transfer control, multimodal methods expand transfer under weak overlap through richer semantic information, and federated methods address growing privacy and deployment requirements. The review also finds that no single family is universally optimal, and that future progress is likely to depend on hybrid, condition-aware models that combine selective transfer, semantic enrichment, robustness, and distributed learning. [21, 23, 32, 54] The paper concludes by identifying major open challenges, including weak overlap, domain heterogeneity, benchmark inconsistency, scalability, explainability, and privacy-aware deployment. It argues that the next stage of CDRS research will be shaped by more reliable selective-transfer mechanisms, multimodal and foundation-model integration, continual adaptation, and stronger evaluation standards for real-world recommendation environments. [35, 53, 55, 56]</p>','["adversarial alignment", "robustness", "Stunting", "K-Nearest benchmark inconsistency", "contrastive approaches."]',NULL,20,1,'357 - 414','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1034','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',21),
(1035,1035,1,'Cross-Modal Attention Mechanisms in Transformer Networks for Real-Time Emotion Detection in Video Conferencing','<p>Emotion detection in video conferencing requires models that can interpret facial-expression dynamics and speech cues under variable communication conditions. This study develops a reliability-aware RA-Gated Cross-Modal Fusion framework for real-time audio-visual emotion recognition using the RAVDESS full audio-visual speech subset. The methodology includes synchronized audio-visual preprocessing, face-frame extraction, log-mel spectrogram generation, unimodal baseline training, cross-modal feature extraction, and reliability-aware gated fusion. Two evaluation protocols were used: a stratified-random split for main benchmark assessment and an actor-independent split for speaker-disjoint generalization. The RA-Gated Cross-Modal Fusion model achieved the strongest stratified-random performance, reaching 93.55% accuracy, 93.14% macro-F1, and 93.59% weighted-F1, outperforming audio-only, visual-only, and late-fusion baselines. Class-wise analysis further showed strong recognition of angry, fearful, disgust, happy, and improved performance for sad and surprised expressions. The actor-independent evaluation showed lower performance across all models, with tuned late fusion achieving the highest speaker-disjoint result and RA-Gated fusion improving over unimodal baselines. These findings indicate that reliability-aware fusion improves benchmark emotion recognition, while the 21.50 ms per-sample RA-Gated latency on an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3060 supports real-time-oriented applicability. Future work should test larger naturalistic conferencing datasets and strengthen speaker-invariant streaming transformers.</p>','["Audio-visual emotion recognition", "Cross-modal fusion", "Transformer networks", "Video conferencing", "RA-Gated fusion."]',NULL,20,1,'415 - 430','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1035','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',22),
(1036,1036,1,'A Fairness-Constrained Multimodal Adaptive Reinforcement Learning Framework for Personalized Candidate Evaluation in Virtual Interviews','<p>The surge in the use of artificial intelligence in hiring has created serious issues around algorithmic fairness and absence of bias in candidate assessment, as conventional AI-based interview systems are based on rigid rubrics that tend to reinforce demographic gaps. In order to overcome these issues, we introduce Fair- MARLTrans, a fairness-enforced multimodal adaptive reinforcement learning system for fair candidate assessment through virtual interviews. The model combines visual attributes (face landmarks, gaze, body posture), acoustic attributes (MFCC, prosody, spectral features), and textual attributes (BERT embeddings, sentiment) through a transformer-based attention mechanism to generate a single multimodal representation for end-to-end candidate profiling. Evaluation is framed as a Constrained Markov Decision Process (CMDP) with fairness-sensitive policy gradients imposing demographic parity, equalized opportunity, and individual fairness between protected groups[1]. We propose the Composite Interview Quality Index (CIQI) to simultaneously measure candidate competence and fairness compliance, with theoretical validity guarantees that provide convergence to fair policies. Experiment results on the CMU-MOSI dataset show that Fair-MARLTrans achieves better performance than static rubric baselines (15.14 ± 0.37 mean reward vs. 12.50 ± 0.85 for visual-only), standard reinforcement learning, and fairness-regularized models, with a fairness score of 0.97 and zero constraint violations. Ablation experiments show that multimodal fusion enhances performance by 18.5%, whereas attention mechanisms add 12.3% gains. Demographic sensitivity analysis for ten demographic subgroups holds firmly with fairness compliance (averaged score 0.73, p &lt; 0.01)[2], confirmed by t-tests, ANOVA, and multiple comparison adjustment. This paper provides a theoretical basis for fairness-constrained adaptive assessment and a practical approach to transparent, fair AI-powered recruitment tools, with improved state-of-the-art in algorithmic recruitment.</p>','["Reinforcement Learning, Constrained Markov Decision Process, Multimodal Fusion, Fairness-Aware AI, Algorithmic Hiring, Bias Mitigation, Virtual Interviews, Human-Computer Interaction."]',NULL,20,1,'431 - 455','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1036','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',23),
(1037,1037,1,'A Mathematical Approach for Predictive Cybersecurity in Large-Scale Networks','<p>Introduction Day-by-day, Cyberattacks are getting more and more complicated, new malware constantly changes their form, and ransomware often attacks in several stages. Hence, old-style security systems which are reactive after something bad happens are no longer good enough, as they expose the weaknesses in traditional, reactive approaches and show that current security methods are not strong. This paper introduces a new bio- inspired security method called Stateful Propagation Containment. This is inspired by how living things protect themselves. Instead of waiting for an attack to happen and then reacting, this new approach tries to predict problems early and stop them before they occur.<br/>Methods The SPC framework draws inspiration from biological self-protection mechanisms and integrates advanced computational techniques, including evolutionary computation and quantum-inspired graph analysis. The research design is theoretical and model-based, comprising five core axioms: (1) Multi Scale Behavioral Baselining for anomaly detection, (2) Evolutionary Threat Prediction to anticipate attacker movements, (3) Quantum-Inspired Correlation Engine to reveal hidden attack connections, (4) Darwinian Defense Engine for adaptive network hardening, and (5) Predictive Surgical Containment for minimally disruptive, three-step isolation of infected systems. Mathematical formulations are provided to demonstrate the reliability and efficiency of each component.<br/>Results The theoretical model demonstrates that SPC enables autonomous, self-adjusting network defense capable of preemptively identifying and containing threats before system compromise. Key findings include: the ability to baseline normal network behavior across multiple scales, predict attack propagation paths with evolutionary algorithms, correlate seemingly unrelated attack indicators via quantum-inspired methods, dynamically evolve defense strategies, and enforce containment with near-zero disruption to normal operations.<br/>Discussion This paper takes a deep dive into the mathematical formulations that support how the system works and proves that its methods are both reliable and efficient. Besides, it considers the whole system''s behavior over time; it is almost like watching how a machine self-adjusts while running like a human would. The concept of SPC is a leap in constructing computer networks that will be able to protect themselves, fix problems on their own, and stop advanced cyberattacks before they get into the network to cause infection. SPC represents a foundational advancement toward realizing autonomous, self-healing network security infrastructures capable of preempting sophisticated cyber threats.</p>','["Cybersecurity, Predictive Prevention, Zero-Day attacks, Network Segmentation, Threat Intelligence, Reinforcement Learning, Ransomware, Autonomous Defense, Stateful Security, Propagation Containment, Mathematical Modeling."]',NULL,20,1,'456 - 476','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1037','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',24),
(1038,1038,1,'Secure Collaborative Decision-Making Among Distributed Cloud Brokers Using Privacy-Preserving Federated Learning','<p>Cloud brokers play a vital role in coordinating resource-allocation decisions across a heterogeneous cloud environment. Centralised brokerage approaches are prone to single points of failure and data exposure, expose sensitive data, and have limited interoperability. There is a need for distributed cloud brokerage that enhances collaboration by enabling decision-making without exposing raw workload specifications. A shared knowledge repository in collaborative learning introduces data sovereignty and adversarial risks. This paper proposes Federated learning-based SecureBroker-FL, introducing 3 key aspects: (i) A Differential Privacy enhanced Gradient Encryption (DPGE) protocol. (ii) An Adaptive Trust scoring (ATS) mechanism, (iii) Hierarchical Secure Aggregation (HSA). The experiments are simulated and evaluated using the Google Cloud Trace 2019 and Alibaba Clustered Trace 2022 datasets, with five geographically distributed cloud brokers. It demonstrates that SecureBroker-FL achieves 94.7% decision accuracy. The framework withstands up to 40% malicious broker participation handling graceful degradation without catastrophic collapse and outperforms baseline approaches, including FedAvg, Krum, and FLTrust.</p>','["Federated Learning", "Cloud Broker", "Privacy-Preserving Collaboration", "Differential Privacy", "Secure Aggregation", "Byzantine Resilience."]',NULL,20,1,'477 - 491','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1038','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',25),
(1039,1039,1,'Grape Leaf Disease Detection through Hierarchical Features, Mask R-Convolutional Neural Network Segmentation, and Self-Attention Networks','<p>Automated grape leaf disease detection is essential for precision agriculture, enabling early diagnosis and effective disease management. This paper presents a novel deep learning-based framework to enhance the accuracy and robustness of grape disease detection by integrating advanced image pre-processing, segmentation, and feature extraction techniques. The proposed model addresses key challenges in traditional approaches by incorporating deep learning-based noise filtering, Mask R-CNN for instance segmentation, and a hybrid feature extraction strategy combining Residual Network (ResNet) and handcrafted methods such as Gray-Level Co-occurrence matrix (GLCM), shape and texture features. Additionally, an improved Mask R-CNN architecture is introduced by replacing the ResNet backbone with a Swin Transformer, leveraging hierarchical feature extraction and self-attention mechanisms for enhanced contextual understanding. Experimental results exhibit the efficacy of the proposed approach in accurately segmenting and classifying grape leaf diseases by reporting the classification accuracy as 92.75 and segmentation accuracy as 98.10%, respectively</p>','["Grape Leaf Disease, Filtering, Feature Extraction, Segmentation, Mask R-CNN, Classification"]',NULL,20,1,'492 - 513','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1039','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',26),
(1040,1040,1,'Context-Aware Stance Detection in Manipuri Editorials via FastText Embeddings and Keyword-Guided BiLSTM','<p>Although stance detection has gained substantial interest in high-resource languages, it remains largely unexplored for Manipuri, a low-resource Indo-Tibeto-Burman language with limited annotated data and linguistic resources. This paper proposes a framework for stance detection in Manipuri editorial articles. The dataset created for this research is balanced with respect to the stance categories, namely, FOR, AGAINST, and NEUTRAL. Both classical machine learning algorithms and Keyword-Guided Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory (KG-BiLSTM) are evaluated on the dataset. The KG-BiLSTM architecture proposed in the paper consists of FastText embeddings along with a label-independent keyword-guided attention network constructed from manually curated stance lexicons. The attention mechanism enables the model to focus on potentially stance-bearing words without using ground-truth class information during mask construction. With five-fold stratified cross-validation, the KG-BiLSTM obtains an average accuracy of 71.2% and a Macro-F1 score of 70.1%, outperforming logistic regression and support vector machine baselines. The model reduces confusion between FOR and NEUTRAL editorials, which is one of the most critical problems for stance identification.</p>','["FastText", "sentiment indicators", "embeddings", "KG-BiLSTM."]',NULL,20,1,'514 - 527','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1040','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',27),
(1041,1041,1,'Federated Digital Twin Reinforcement Learning Framework with Physics Informed Intelligence for Autonomous Battery Management Systems','<p>The rapid evolution of electric vehicles, smart grids, and energy storage infrastructures has exposed critical limitations in conventional Battery Management Systems (BMS), particularly in adaptive intelligence, privacy preservation, degradation awareness, and real-time decision autonomy. This paper proposes a novel Federated Physics-Informed Digital Twin Reinforcement Learning Battery Management System (FPD-RL BMS), an autonomous multi-layered framework that integrates Physics-Informed Neural Networks (PINNs), federated edge learning, deep reinforcement learning, and synchronized digital twin technology into a unified intelligent battery ecosystem. Unlike traditional AI-based BMS approaches that rely on centralized data training and isolated state estimation models, the proposed framework introduces a unique Adaptive Federated Physics-Informed Deep Reinforcement Learning (AFPIDRL) algorithm capable of jointly optimizing State-of-Charge (SOC), State-of-Health (SOH), thermal stability, charging efficiency, and battery lifespan under dynamic operating conditions. The methodology enables distributed vehicles or battery nodes to collaboratively learn degradation patterns without sharing raw data, thereby ensuring cybersecurity and privacy preservation. Additionally, a continuously evolving digital twin provides real-time electrochemical synchronization and predictive fault intelligence, while the reinforcement learning agent autonomously adapts charging and balancing strategies based on aging-aware reward functions. Experimental simulations demonstrate that the proposed architecture significantly enhances prediction accuracy, thermal safety, energy efficiency, and long-term battery sustainability compared with existing AI-driven BMS frameworks.</p>','["AI-Driven Battery Management System (BMS), Physics-Informed Neural Networks (PINNs), Federated Learning, Digital Twin Technology, Reinforcement Learning, Edge Artificial Intelligence, State of Charge (SOC)."]',NULL,20,1,'528 - 545','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1041','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',28),
(1042,1042,1,'Generativa Between Cognitive Offloading and Thinking: A Theoretical Framework of Metacognitive Tension in the Use of Generative AI','<p>Resumen<br/>The mass adoption of generative artificial intelligence in learning revives a classic question: when does the support of an external tool empower thinking and when does it replace it? This conceptual article, aimed at the construction of theory through theoretical synthesis, integrates three bodies of literature, cognitive download, desirable difficulties and metacognition, to theorize the tension between delegating cognition to AI and developing self-regulation. Its distinctive contribution in the face of close frameworks is to specify boundary conditions. The threshold model of metacognitive tension is proposed, which defines a mediating mechanism, the preservation against the suppression of the diagnostic cues of monitoring and the occupation of the metalevel, three moderators, including the learner''s expertise, and a threshold conceived as a transition band in the plane of the locus of discharge and metacognitive posture. It derives a typology of four uses and seven propositions that can be contrasted with their evidential status, and identifies implications for pedagogical, tool, and research design.<br/>Abstract<br/>The widespread adoption of generative artificial intelligence in learning revives a classic question: when does support from an external tool enhance thinking, and when does it replace it? This conceptual article, oriented toward theory building through theoretical synthesis, integrates three consolidated bodies of literature, cognitive offloading, desirable difficulties, and metacognition, to theorize the tension between delegating cognition to AI and developing self-regulation. Its distinctive contribution relative to neighboring frameworks is to specify boundary conditions. It proposes the Metacognitive Tension Threshold Model, which defines a mediating mechanism, the preservation versus suppression of the diagnostic cues of monitoring and the occupation of the metalevel, three moderators, including learner expertise, and a threshold located as a transition band within a plane of two determinants, the locus of offloading and metacognitive posture. The model derives a typology of four uses and seven testable propositions with their evidential status, and identifies implications for pedagogical design, tool design, and research.</p>','["generative artificial intelligence", "cognitive discharge", "metacognition", "desirable difficulties", "self-regulation of learning", "cognitive load", "calibration."]',NULL,20,1,'546 - 557','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1042','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',29),
(1043,1043,1,'Andamiaje Metacognitivo En Metodologías Activas: Un Modelo Integrador Para La Transferencia Del Aprendizaje','<p>Resumen<br/>Las metodologías activas se han consolidado como una vía eficaz para mejorar el aprendizaje en la educación superior, pero la evidencia muestra que sus efectos sobre la metacognición y, en particular, sobre la transferencia del aprendizaje son heterogéneos y rara vez espontáneos. Este Article propone un modelo conceptual, denominado CAMT por sus cuatro componentes (Contexto, Andamiaje, Metacognición y Transferencia), que integra cuatro corrientes de investigación habitualmente tratadas por separado: las metodologías activas como contexto habilitante, el andamiaje metacognitivo como mecanismo, la metacognición y la autorregulación como proceso, y la transferencia del aprendizaje como resultado. Siguiendo el diseño de síntesis teórica, el modelo reconcilia estas literaturas en torno a una tesis central: la transferencia no la genera el contexto activo ni el andamiaje por sí mismos, sino el desvanecimiento contingente del andamiaje metacognitivo, esto es, el traspaso progresivo de la responsabilidad regulatoria al estudiante, siempre que esa regulación se acople a estrategias concretas y se ejercite en contextos variados. A partir del modelo se derivan ocho proposiciones contrastables, entre ellas una relación de U invertida entre la persistencia del andamiaje y la transferencia, y un bucle de refuerzo entre la transferencia lograda y la metacognición. El trabajo aporta un marco que articula la práctica de las metodologías activas con los mecanismos cognitivos que sostienen el aprendizaje duradero y ofrece implicaciones para el diseño instruccional y una agenda de investigación.<br/>Palabras clave: andamiaje metacognitivo; metodologías activas; metacognición; autorregulación del aprendizaje; transferencia del aprendizaje; educación superior.</p>
INSERT INTO publications (id,submission_id,version,title,abstract,keywords,references_text,issue_id,section_id,pages,doi,copyright_holder,copyright_year,license_url,status,date_published,seq) VALUES
(1066,1066,1,'Multi-Agent AI Framework for the Early Diagnosis of Tuberculosis','<p>The World Health Organization keeps on monitoring TB-Tuberculosis affected patients every day. In recent years, researcher have developed many early prediction techniques on TB analysis which reveals that there is a significant decrease in the numbers of TB affected patients. However, there is no remarkable research work that deals with the analysis on various parameters which influences more to make such predictions. This research work focuses on early prediction of TB with an explainable AI model named Gradient-Weighted Class Activation Mapping. Grad-CAM explains why the corresponding AI model has predicted the result with respect to the given input. This is done using Multi-Agent AI framework which includes Image Quality Assessment agent, Classification agent, Explainability agent and Severity Assessment agent. Intelligent report is generated which covers predicted disease class, disease probability, predictive uncertainty, Grad-CAM heatmap, clinical risk factors along with recommended clinical action, severity level and confidence score. Performance evaluation is carried out using ResNet50 which is compared with DenseNet121, EfficientNetB3 and tinyCNN. The experimental results show that the ResNet50 outperforms other defined models with respect to various parameters such as accuracy, precision and F1 score.</p>','["Tuberculosis (TB),Explainable AI (XAI),Gradient-Weighted Class Activation , Mapping (Grad-CAM),Multi-Agent AI Framework, Early Prediction, ResNet50, Image Quality Assessment, Severity Assessment, Clinical Risk Factors, Deep Learning"]',NULL,20,1,'828 - 850','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1066','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',44),
(1132,1132,1,'The Effect of Binaural Beats Music Therapy on Sleep Quality in the Elderly','<p>Elderly people living in nursing homes experience more complex sleep disorders, evidenced by frequent nighttime awakenings, difficulty falling asleep, and waking up in the middle of the night. This study aimed to determine the effect of binaural beats music therapy on sleep quality in elderly people living in a nursing home in Surabaya. Type study This is pre experimental one group pre-post-test design. The population of this study was all elderly people with a minimum care category of 23 respondents. The sample of 23 respondents used the Purposive Sampling Technique Variables independent therapy music binaural beats , And variables dependent on the quality of sleep of the elderly in a nursing home in Surabaya. Data collection using the PSQI (Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index) questionnaire and SOP binaural beats music therapy were then analyzed using the Paired T-Test, obtained ρ = 0.0001 &lt; α 0.05. Results study obtained as big as 23 respondents (100%) own sleep quality from poor to good. The analysis results obtained ρ = 0.0001 &lt; α 0.05 which means H0 is rejected and H1 is accepted, indicating that there is an effect of binaural beats music therapy on the sleep quality of the elderly in one of the nursing homes in Surabaya. Binaural beats music therapy has an impact on sleep quality for seniors at a nursing home in Surabaya. Therefore, the nursing home is expected to implement and provide binaural beats music therapy to reduce poor sleep quality in the elderly.</p>','["Binaural Beats Music Therapy, Sleep Quality, Elderly"]',NULL,20,1,'851 - 859','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1132','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',45),
(1133,1133,1,'Evaluation of Musculoskeletal Disorders (Skeletal Muscle Disorders) in Freelancers in the Agricultural Industry','<p>Musculoskeletal Disorders (MSDs) are one of the occupational health problems commonly experienced by workers in various sectors, including the agricultural industry. Freelancers in this sector tend to face higher risks due to the nature of the work that relies on physical labor, long work durations, unergonomic posture, and lack of job protection. This study aims to evaluate the factors that affect the incidence of musculoskeletal system disorders, especially skeletal muscle disorders in freelancers in the agricultural industry. The research design used was cross-sectional with a sample of 65 respondents selected in total sampling. The variables analyzed included age, gender, working period, daily working time, work stress, physical workload, nutritional status, and level of work fatigue. Data collection was carried out through the SNI 9011:2021 questionnaire, IFRC questionnaire, interviews, and anthropometric measurements. Data analysis uses logistic regression to find out the relationships and influences between variables. The results of the study showed that the factors of age, working period, length of work, nutritional status, work fatigue, work stress, and physical workload had a significant influence on the problem of musculoskeletal disorders, in this case skeletal muscle disorders, with an overall α value of &lt;0.05. Meanwhile, gender showed no significant influence on this because the respondents'' gender was entirely male, so the data were homogeneous. Based on these results, it can be known that all factors have a statistically significant influence. These findings confirm the importance of ergonomics interventions, workload management, and occupational health education in agricultural sector freelancers to reduce the risk of MSDs.</p>','["Musculoskeletal Disorders, Skeletal Muscle Disorders, Freelancers, Agriculture, Ergonomics"]',NULL,20,1,'860 - 871','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1133','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',46),
(1134,1134,1,'The Relationship Between Nurses'' Caring Behavior and Patient Families'' Anxiety in the Intensive Care Unit (Icu) Islamic Hospital Ahmad Yani Surabaya','<p>Team model nursing care is an effort to improve the quality of nursing services at the Ahmad Yani Islamic Hospital, Surabaya.<br/>Objective: Based on the phenomenon of various issues that arise while patients are treated in the ICU, one of them is the unclear information from the medical team about the disease, which is not explained by the nurses or doctors to the patients and their families. This results in poor caring behavior from the nurses, such as poor communication, lack of empathy, slow and inadequate actions, giving an impression of unprofessionalism. The anxiety experienced by the patient''s family in the Intensive Care Unit (ICU) is due to concerns about the patient''s condition, which requires special attention from the staff. The purpose of this study is to determine the relationship between nurses'' caring behavior and the anxiety of patients'' families in the Intensive Care Unit (ICU) of RS Islam Ahmad Yani Surabaya. Method : This study uses an analytical observational method with a cross- sectional design. The sampling technique employed is simple random sampling with a total of 110 respondents. Results : The analysis results show that the Spearman rank correlation test p-value = 0.00 &lt; 0.05, thus H1 is accepted, which means there is a relationship between the nurses'' caring behavior and the anxiety of the patients'' families in the Intensive Care Unit (ICU) of RS Islam Surabaya A.Yani. According to the researchers'' assumption, negative caring behavior of nurses is related to the anxiety of the patient''s family because the family''s worry and assessment of the nurses'' caring behavior are still perceived as suboptimal. Respondents rated the caring behavior of nurses in the Intensive Care Unit (ICU) as negative (82.7%) and indicated that the majority of respondents experienced severe anxiety (70.9%). Conclusion : There is a relationship between the caring behavior of nurses and the anxiety of patients'' families in the Intensive Care Unit (ICU) of RS Islam Ahmad Yani Surabaya. This shows that the anxiety of the patient''s family is influenced by the level of care and attention provided by the nurse to the patient.</p>','["Caring Behavior, Family Anxiety, Patient"]',NULL,20,1,'872 - 880','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1134','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',47),
(1135,1135,1,'The Effect of Clothing on Thermal Stress of Workers in Confined Spaces','<p>One of the risks of working in a confined space is hot working environment. There are several conditions caused by a hot working environment, one of them is heat stress. Based on theory, one of the factors that influence heat stress is clothing. The aim of this study is to analyze the effect of clothing on heat stress complaints of workers in confined spaces. This research method is an analytical observation with a cross-sectional approach. The population of this study is all workers in the confined space of PT. X. From the results of the work environment measurements in five confined spaces, it is known that the working environment in the boiler, electrostatic precipitator, CASOX, and final absorbent tower is above Threshold Limit Value, 144 workers (80%) work at temperatures above the Threshold Limit Value. The working environment in the gas cooler is below the Threshold Limit Value, 35 workers (20%) work at temperatures below the Threshold Limit Value. There were 9 workers (5%) who experienced minor heat stress complaints in the confined space, 114 workers (63.3%) had moderate heat stress complaints, and 57 workers (31.7%) had serious heat stress complaints. The results of statistical tests using linear regression show that heat stress complaints are influenced by metabolic rate, length of work, clothing, and hot work climate. The recommendation to workers that working in confined spaces is do not wear double layers of clothing. The company should establish different temperature thresholds for each confined space.</p>','["Confined Space", "Heat Stress", "Hot Environment, Thermal Stress"]',NULL,20,1,'881 - 890','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1135','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',48),
(1136,1136,1,'Leveraging Digital Environments to Foster Critical Literacy Skills in Pre-Service Teacher Education: Insights from an Importance–Performance Analysis','<p>The rapid transformation of the digital era has significantly reshaped learning environments over the last decades. Beyond the integration of digital tools into education, the emergence of digital natives has shifted the landscape of higher education, particularly for Generation Z pre-service teachers. These future educators are expected to cultivate Critical Literacy Skills (CLS) for both their personal development and their future teaching roles, as they enter the Society 5.0. This study aims to identify and examine components of the digital learning environment (DLE) that support the enhancement of CLS among pre-service teachers during their education. This researh is quantitative non-experimental, with SERVQUAL design-based Importance-Performance Analysis (IPA). The participants were 452 pre-service teachers with prior experience in digital learning, intervention programs, and CLS assessment. Data were collected using a double-Likert Digital Learning Environment Questionnaire, which comprised 15 items on digital activities and 10 items on digital resources/media. Analyses included descriptive statistics, gap analysis, and IPA mapping. Results revealed three items in Quadrant I (“Keep Up the Good Work”), seven in Quadrant II (“Concentrate Here”), seven in Quadrant III (“Low Priority”), and eight in Quadrant IV (“Possible Overkill”). The findings suggest that digital native characteristics may confer students with greater proficiency in specific DLE components compared to their lecturers, particularly in argumentation and reasoning skills that are enhanced by active DLE use. The study recommends further in-depth or longitudinal research and highlights the need for adaptive curricula and learning designs tailored to Generation Z pre-service teachers.</p>','["critical literacy skills, digital learning environment, preservice teacher, higher education, digital native."]',NULL,20,1,'891 - 909','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1136','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',49),
(1137,1137,1,'Asymmetric Exchange-Rate Pass-Through to Inflation in Iraq: Nonlinear ARDL Evidence from Three Monetary Regimes, 1990–2023','<p>This paper examines whether depreciations and appreciations of the Iraqi dinar are transmitted to consumer-price inflation symmetrically or asymmetrically over the period 1990–2023, a sample that spans the sanctions-era hyperinflation (1990–2003), the post-reform stabilization (2003–2014), and the recent oil-shock and devaluation phase (2014–2023). Using the nonlinear autoregressive distributed lag (NARDL) framework of Shin, Yu, and Greenwood-Nimmo (2014), the nominal exchange rate is decomposed into cumulative positive (depreciation) and negative (appreciation) partial sums, allowing the long-run and short-run responses of inflation to differ by the direction of the shock. The bounds test confirms a long-run level relationship (F = 6.471, above the 5% upper critical bound), and the error-correction coefficient of −0.630 (p = 0.016) indicates that roughly 63% of any disequilibrium is eliminated within one year. Cumulative depreciations carry a statistically significant long-run coefficient (−0.140, p = 0.008), whereas cumulative appreciations are statistically indistinguishable from zero; a Wald test rejects long-run symmetry decisively (t = −4.064, p = 0.0005), while short-run symmetry cannot be rejected (F = 1.151, p = 0.335). The counter-intuitive sign of the depreciation coefficient is shown to be regime-conditioned: the structural break separating the hyperinflationary 1990s from the post-2003 stabilization dominates the full-sample average, a reading corroborated by CUSUM-of-squares instability and residual heteroskedasticity. The results imply that symmetric pass-through models misrepresent Iraqi inflation dynamics and that the Central Bank of Iraq should calibrate its response to the direction, and not merely the size, of exchange-rate movements.</p>','["asymmetric exchange-rate pass-through", "inflation", "NARDL", "cointegration", "partial-sum decomposition", "Iraq"]',NULL,20,1,'910 - 924','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1137','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',50),
(1138,1138,1,'AI-Driven Academic Success Prediction from Learning Behaviours: A Data-Centric Analysis of Student Achievement Patterns','<p>Prediction of academic success has emerged as a crucial aspect of learning analytics and intelligent educational decision support. Much of the work done to date is still model-centric, and little is done to consider data quality, behavioural meaning, leakage prevention, explainability and the usefulness of interventions. This study introduces a data-centric Artificial Intelligence pipeline to predict the students'' academic risk by analysing learning behaviour patterns. The Open University Learning Analytics Dataset (OULAD) was used, comprising records on demographics, registrations, assessments, Courses, virtual learning environments, and outcomes. The resulting analytical dataset consisted of 63 predictive features and 32,593 student observations, which had been preprocessed and engineered for the analysis. Several machine learning models were compared: Logistic Regression, Decision Tree, Random Forest, Support Vector Machine, K-Nearest Neighbours, Naïve Bayes, Gradient Boosting, XGBoost, LightGBM, CatBoost and a neural network. The Random Forest model achieved the strongest F1-score of 0.8852, with 0.8840 accuracy, 0.9272 precision, 0.8469 recall, 0.9482 ROC-AUC, and 0.9625 PR-AUC. The proposed framework translates the predicted risk probability into levels of low, moderate, high and critical, to facilitate timely academic advising, tutoring, attendance review and retention planning. In general, the study illustrates the potential of the data-oriented approach to educational decision-making and intervention, offering greater explanatory value. Overall, the study shows the power of the data-oriented approach to educational decision-making and intervention that is more explanatory.</p>','["Academic success prediction", "Learning analytics", "Data-centric AI", "Machine learning", "OULAD", "Explainable AI", "SHAP", "Educational decision support", "Student risk prediction", "Early intervention."]',NULL,20,1,'925 - 950','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1138','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',51),
(1139,1139,1,'Causal Relationships among Artificial Intelligence Capability, Organizational Innovation Climate, Digital Infrastructure Readiness and Educational Quality of Thai Private Universities','<p>This study aims to examine the causal relationships among Artificial Intelligence Capability, Organizational Innovation Climate, Digital Infrastructure Readiness, and Educational Quality (SDG 4) of Thai private universities in Bangkok, Thailand. The sample comprised 250 university personnel in private universities in Bangkok, Thailand. Data were collected using a structured questionnaire. Confirmatory Factor Analysis (CFA) was conducted to validate the multidimensional measurement models of all latent constructs. Structural Equation Modeling (SEM) was employed to find the causal relationships among variables. The SEM results revealed that artificial intelligence capability exerted significant positive effects on digital infrastructure readiness (β = 0.512, p &lt; 0.001) and educational quality (β = 0.318, p &lt; 0.001). Organizational innovation climate also had a significant positive effect on digital infrastructure readiness (β = 0.447, p &lt; 0.001) and educational quality (β = 0.276, p &lt; 0.001), while digital infrastructure readiness directly enhanced educational quality (β = 0.394, p &lt; 0.001). Mediation analysis further demonstrated that digital infrastructure readiness significantly mediated the relationships between artificial intelligence capability and Educational Quality (indirect effect β = 0.202, p &lt; 0.001) and between organizational innovation climate and Educational Quality (indirect effect β = 0.176, p &lt; 0.001). This study confirmed that technological capability, organizational climate, and digital infrastructure readiness can increase educational quality in higher education contexts.</p>','["Artificial Intelligence Capability, Organizational Innovation Climate, Digital Infrastructure Readiness, Educational Quality (SDG 4"]',NULL,20,1,'951 - 974','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1139','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',52),
(1140,1140,1,'Deep EEG-Based Video Advertisement Recommendation via Correlative Capsule Attention and Harmonic Learning','<p>This research presents the development and application of a new Brainwave Driven Advertisement Recommendation System that integrates the state-of-the-art Emotive Insight hardware, Brain-Computer Interface, and Artificial Intelligence. The primary objective of the proposed technology is to advance the state-of-the-art in both accuracy and personalization in ad recommendations through direct utilization of people''s neural responses. The brain wave frequencies that will be recorded using Emotive Insight gear include: alpha, beta, theta, delta, and gamma waves. This rich dataset is diligently stored in both edf formats for further comprehensive processing. Three different commercials are shown to study participants: Snickers, Dairy Milk, and 5-star. The effect of each advertisement is tested by measuring their brain reactions concurrently. The users then comment on their favorite ads, therefore establishing a clear relationship between subjective beliefs and brainwave patterns. In order to make sure significant insights can be extracted, careful preprocessing, removal of artifacts, and features extraction were performed during the data analysis stage. A variety of machine learning approaches were used to build a robust prediction model, and the performances were cross-validated using metrics such as accuracy, precision, recall, and F1 score.</p>','["Component Artificial Intelligence (AI), Brain-Computer Interface (BCI), Emotive Insight 5, Neural Responses Brainwave Frequencies Alpha, Beta, Theta, Delta, Gamma Machine Learning Algorithms, EEG Lab."]',NULL,20,1,'975 - 990','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1140','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',53),
(1141,1141,1,'Digital Twin–Driven Reinforcement Learning for Proactive Cyber-Financial Threat Mitigation in Smart Healthcare Systems','<p>The increasing integration of digital healthcare infrastructures with financial systems has significantly expanded the cyber-attack surface, leading to complex threats that simultaneously compromise patient safety, data privacy, and financial stability. Traditional reactive cybersecurity mechanisms are insufficient to address the dynamic and interconnected nature of cyber-financial risks in smart healthcare environments. This paper proposes a Digital Twin–driven Deep Reinforcement Learning (DT-DRL) framework for proactive cyber-financial threat mitigation in smart healthcare systems. The proposed approach constructs a virtual digital twin of healthcare cyber-physical and financial ecosystems, incorporating electronic health records, Internet of Medical Things (IoMT) devices, billing platforms, and network security components. A deep reinforcement learning agent is trained within the digital twin to learn optimal defense policies that minimize financial losses, service disruption, and security breaches under diverse attack scenarios. The framework models cyber incidents such as ransomware, insider threats, and billing fraud while explicitly quantifying their financial impact. Experimental results demonstrate that the DT-DRL approach outperforms conventional static and rule-based security strategies in terms of threat response time, risk reduction, and cost efficiency. The proposed system provides a scalable and intelligent decision-support solution for enhancing cybersecurity resilience and financial risk management in next-generation smart healthcare infrastructures.</p>','["Digital Twin Security", "Deep Reinforcement Learning", "Smart Healthcare Systems Cyber-Financial Threats", "IoMT Security", "Proactive Threat Mitigation", "Ransomware Defense", "Cyber-Physical Systems", "Financial Risk Management Healthcare Cybersecurity"]',NULL,20,1,'991 - 1004','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1141','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',54),
(1142,1142,1,'Hybrid-ResNet-C-Deep: An Explainable Framework for Intelligent Dental Caries Localization and Diagnosis Decision','<p>Dental caries is one of the most prevalent oral diseases in the world. The early detection of dental caries is essential to avoid tooth loss and the complications caused by the disease. Panoramic dental radiographs are routinely used for dental caries screening, but detecting subtle signs of dental caries on these radiographs can be challenging. To improve the detection of dental caries on panoramic dental radiographs, a deep learning framework named Hybrid-ResNet-C-Deep is proposed. This deep learning framework employs residual blocks with attention mechanisms to enhance the localization of dental caries. Additionally, the framework uses Grad-CAM to provide clinicians with explainable predictions from the model. The model was trained on 4,000 panoramic dental radiographs from the OdontoAI database and additional dental caries images with annotations from dental clinicians. The model was evaluated using cross-validation and stratified sampling of the database. The proposed framework achieved an accuracy of 95.0%, a Dice score of 90.1%, an Intersection over Union score of 84.3%, a sensitivity of 93.5%, and a specificity of 96.2% on the test data. The deep learning framework can be utilized as an intelligent decision support system for dental clinicians to improve the diagnosis of dental caries. Further validation from different dental clinics is required before the framework can be deployed in dental clinics for dental caries diagnosis.</p>','["multi-criteria decision-making", "TOPSIS", "entropy weighting", "decision tree", "hotel operations management", "service quality."]',NULL,20,1,'1005 - 1018','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1142','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',55),
(1143,1143,1,'Learning Orientation as a Predictor of Behavioral, Affective, and Cognitive Engagement in Mathematics','<p>Engaging students is crucial for improving learning outcomes, especially in math, where the abstract nature of the subject often poses challenges. This study looked into how learning orientation can predict students'' engagement in math, focusing on behavioral, emotional, and cognitive aspects. Aimed to evaluate students'' learning orientation and engagement levels in math and see if learning orientation significantly influences different engagement dimensions. This study used a quantitative correlational research design, employing Partial Least Squares Structural Equation Modeling (PLS-SEM) to explore the relationships among variables. the participants were 601 undergraduates taking math courses at Kalinga State University. Data were gathered through a structured questionnaire that assessed learning orientation and the three engagement dimensions: behavioral, affective, and cognitive. The findings showed that students generally have a high level of learning orientation and engagement in math. The structural model analysis revealed that learning orientation significantly predicts behavioral and affective engagement. This suggests that students with a strong motivation to learn are more likely to actively participate in math activities and develop positive feelings toward the subject. However, learning orientation didn''t significantly predict cognitive engagement. These results highlight the importance of nurturing students'' learning orientation to boost participation and emotional involvement in math learning. The study suggests that educators should adopt teaching strategies that enhance motivation, active learning, and meaningful experiences to strengthen student engagement in math.</p>','["learning orientation, student engagement, behavioral engagement, affective engagement, cognitive engagement, mathematics education"]',NULL,20,1,'1019 - 1030','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1143','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',56),
(1144,1144,1,'Data-Driven Decision Making in Freight Forwarding: How Market Orientation and Service Digitalization Shape Competitiveness and Customer Satisfaction','<p>Background: Modern freight forwarders are increasingly reliant on digital data skills to preserve their competitive advantage. However, the specific ways in which these talents lead to decision-relevant outcomes i.e. competitiveness and customer satisfaction remain largely unexplored. This gap in the research is especially noticeable in the logistics industries of emerging nations like Indonesia.<br/>Objectives: The aim of this study is to examine the influence of market orientation and service digitalization on the general competitiveness of freight forwarders. Moreover, the paper studies the downstream impacts of this competition on consumer satisfaction. The empirical research is situated within a mid-sized freight forwarding company in Indonesia. <br/>Methods: The present study was conducted with a quantitative and cross-sectional design to examine the expressed associations. For full coverage, the study used the saturated sampling method, covering a total of 154 customer firms. Systematic primary data gathering was performed via standardized Likert-scale questionnaires. In the next step of analysis, Partial Least Squares Structural Equation Modeling (PLS-SEM) was employed with a strict bootstrapping technique of 5000 resamples to ensure the statistical significance. <br/>Results: The structural model found that market orientation is a major predictor of competitiveness (β=0.656, p&lt;0.001). In addition, the firm’s competitive position is considerably affected by service digitalization (β=0.244, p&lt;0.001). The two independent factors explain a significant percentage of the variance of competitiveness (R 2 =0.744). Moreover, service digitalization had a very large direct effect on customer happiness (β=0.380, p=0.001) and was the greatest predictive path to satisfaction in the whole model. Conversely, the direct impacts of market orientation and competitiveness on satisfaction and their proposed mediation paths failed to reach statistical significance after a rigorous bootstrap uncertainty assessment. The final cause of this reduction of significance was found to be high collinearity across the three main predictors (VIFs ranging from 2.9 to 4.4).<br/>Therefore, the results suggest that market orientation and service digitization are simultaneous and independent strategic levers and not sequential in a pipeline. Clearly, market orientation is the key mechanism driving the competitive posture of the business. Meanwhile, the digitalization of service directly effects customer happiness, without the need for competition as a mediating variable in between. The research results have important practical consequences for logistics sector. Specifically they provide freight forwarders with a very specific framework for strategic resource allocation. Digitalization and market orientation are no longer generic good projects but rather management can invest in them with specific organizational goals.</p>','["Freight Forwarding", "Market Orientation", "Service Digitalization", "Competitiveness", "Customer Satisfaction", "PLS-SEM", "Logistics Industry", "Indonesia."]',NULL,20,1,'1031 - 1041','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1144','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',57),
(1145,1145,1,'FIDIC Contracts, Risk Allocation, and Sustainability in International Construction: Comparative Lessons from Mega Projects','<p>This study examines how FIDIC contracts operate in practice within international construction projects, with particular attention to risk allocation, dispute resolution, and sustainability-related obligations. Although FIDIC forms are designed to provide standardized contractual frameworks, their practical effectiveness may vary depending on the legal, institutional, and project-specific environments in which they are applied. The study adopts a qualitative comparative case study approach based on three selected mega-project contexts: NEOM in Saudi Arabia, Crossrail in the United Kingdom, and the Noor Solar Project in Morocco. These cases were selected to reflect different legal and regulatory environments and to enable comparison across hybrid and common law contexts. The analysis draws on contractual and legal literature, selected judicial authorities, and project-related materials to examine how legal systems, contractual amendments, governance structures, and sustainability requirements influence the practical operation of FIDIC-based contracts.<br/>The findings suggest that the effectiveness of FIDIC contracts is shaped less by their formal wording alone than by the context in which they are interpreted and implemented. In particular, legal environment appears to affect the practical operation of dispute-resolution mechanisms, national amendments may significantly alter the standardized operation of FIDIC forms, and sustainability obligations may introduce additional layers of contractual and governance complexity when incorporated into major projects.<br/>The study contributes to construction law and project governance literature by offering an integrated legal-comparative discussion of how FIDIC contracts function across selected mega-project environments. It suggests that FIDIC contracts are best understood as structured but context-sensitive frameworks whose practical effectiveness depends on legal setting, institutional support, and project-specific governance conditions.</p>','["FIDIC Contracts, Risk Management, Sustainability, Dispute Resolution, Civil Law, Common Law, Mega Projects, NEOM, ESG Indicators, International Construction."]',NULL,20,1,'1042 - 1069','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1145','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',58),
(1146,1146,1,'Perceived Benefits of Quality Assurance as Predictors of Quality Culture in Higher Education Institutions','<p>Quality assurance (QA) has become an essential strategy for strengthening institutional performance and promoting continuous improvement in higher education. Despite the widespread adoption of QA systems, empirical evidence remains limited regarding how stakeholders'' perceptions of QA benefits contribute to the development of a sustainable quality culture. This study examined stakeholders'' perceived benefits of QA across the four core functions of higher education—instruction, research, extension, and production—and investigated their influence on institutional quality culture within a Philippine state university. An explanatory sequential mixed-methods design was employed, integrating survey responses from 210 stakeholders, including students, faculty members, administrative personnel, and external partners, with qualitative data obtained through focus group discussions and key informant interviews. Quantitative data were analyzed using descriptive statistics, nonparametric tests, and multiple regression analysis, while qualitative data were examined through thematic analysis to enrich and explain the quantitative findings. The results indicated consistently high levels of perceived QA benefits across stakeholder groups and institutional functions, reflecting broad recognition of QA as a mechanism for enhancing institutional effectiveness. Although perceptions of QA benefits were generally consistent across demographic characteristics, significant differences were observed among stakeholder groups in their assessments of institutional functions and quality culture. Regression analysis further revealed that perceived QA benefits significantly predicted institutional quality culture, accounting for 42.7% of its variance. Qualitative findings highlighted that QA strengthens educational quality, organizational learning, stakeholder confidence, and evidence-informed decision-making, while resource limitations, insufficient capacity-building, and communication challenges continue to constrain effective implementation. These findings demonstrate that stakeholders'' perceived benefits constitute an important pathway through which QA fosters a strong quality culture. The study extends the current understanding of QA by providing empirical evidence that institutional efforts to communicate and reinforce the tangible value of QA may enhance stakeholder engagement and support the long-term sustainability of quality culture in higher education.</p>','["quality assurance", "quality culture", "higher education", "stakeholder perception", "mixed methods", "institutional effectiveness"]',NULL,20,1,'1070 - 1091','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1146','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',59),
(1147,1147,1,'Strategic Digital Engagement and Customer Trust: Examining the Mediating Role of Customer Experience in the E-Commerce Industry','<p>This study aims to examine the effect of Strategic Digital Engagement on Customer Trust, with Customer Experience as a mediating variable in the Indonesian e-commerce industry. A quantitative explanatory research design was employed, using purposive sampling to collect data from 356 active e-commerce users in Indonesia through structured questionnaires. The data were analyzed using Structural Equation Modeling (SEM) to assess the relationships among the research variables and test the proposed hypotheses. The results show that Strategic Digital Engagement has a significant positive effect on Customer Experience (β = 0.746, p &lt; 0.001) and Customer Trust (β = 0.298, p &lt; 0.001). Customer Experience also has a significant positive effect on Customer Trust (β = 0.581, p &lt; 0.001). Furthermore, Customer Experience significantly mediates the relationship between Strategic Digital Engagement and Customer Trust, with a reported indirect effect coefficient of 0.433 (p &lt; 0.001). All four proposed hypotheses are supported by the empirical results. These findings indicate that strategic digital interactions contribute to customer trust both directly and indirectly by improving customer experience. This study contributes to digital marketing and relationship marketing literature by integrating Strategic Digital Engagement, Customer Experience, and Customer Trust into a single empirical framework within the Indonesian e-commerce context. From a managerial perspective, e-commerce companies should strengthen personalization, responsiveness, interactive communication, and customer participation to improve customer experience and build customer trust. The findings should be interpreted in light of the study''s cross-sectional design, geographical scope, and reliance on self-reported questionnaire data.</p>','["Strategic Digital Engagement", "Customer Experience", "Customer Trust", "E-Commerce", "Digital Marketing."]',NULL,20,1,'1092 - 1108','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1147','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',60),
(1148,1148,1,'Driven but Dissatisfied? Work Motivation, Career Success Satisfaction, and Job Satisfaction Among Saudi Female Employees: An Empirical Examinationpines','<p>The motivation/job satisfaction dichotomy is a key factor in Human Resource Management, but unexplored in fast changing non-Western labor markets. This research uses survey data from 187 Saudi women in public and private sectors, to examine work motivation, career success satisfaction, and job satisfaction. Using Self-Determination Theory (Deci &amp; Ryan, 1985) and the Greenhaus et al. (1990) model of subjective career success, this research will examine whether intrinsic work motivation influences career success satisfaction and job satisfaction, and whether career success satisfaction mediates the relationship between work motivation and job satisfaction. This research reflects an important paradox. This study''s Saudi women demonstrated high work motivation (M = 4.39, SD = 0.46), due to an intrinsic orientation toward personal satisfaction and pride, self-standards, and self-fulfillment. However, this high work motivation did not influence career success satisfaction (B = 0.145, p = .272) or job satisfaction (B = 0.223, p = .140). In this study, career success satisfaction, when work motivation was controlled, was the most influential factor in predicting job satisfaction (B = 0.585, p &lt; .001, R² = .261). This study also showed that the single Saudi women in this sample were more intrinsically motivated than the married women (M = 4.48 vs. 4.29, t = -2.73, p = .007), with no changes in career satisfaction and job satisfaction.<br/>These findings indicate that HRD practitioners should reconsider the approach they take in developing motivation and career advancement programs geared towards Saudi female talent. In this context, it appears that the primary form of motivation of Saudi female talent is intrinsic motivation. While there are numerous advantages for female talent, intrinsic motivation alone does not create perceptions of career advancement and progress that drive satisfaction. HRD practitioners must improve the commitment level of HR towards developing the career progression infrastructure, as well as clear developmental and advancement pathways, and fair and equitable recognition systems.</p>','["work motivation, career success satisfaction, job satisfaction, Saudi women, HRD, Self-Determination Theory, Vision 2030, career development, subjective career success, GCC"]',NULL,20,1,'1109 - 1125','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1148','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',61),
(1149,1149,1,'The Effect of HR Practices on Faculty Career Growth: The Mediating Role of Internal Mobility','<p>This study examines the impact of human resource (HR) practices, specifically training and recognition, on faculty career growth in Saudi Arabian universities, with a focus on the mediating role of internal mobility. While previous research has emphasized faculty research productivity, this study shifts the focus to career advancement mechanisms within academic institutions. Using Structural Equation Modeling (SEM), the findings reveal that training does not significantly enhance internal mobility or career success, while recognition positively influences career success but negatively affects internal mobility. Surprisingly, internal mobility demonstrates a negative relationship with promotion and salary progression, challenging traditional assumptions about career development. These results underscore the complexity of HR practices in higher education and highlight the need for institutions to reassess their career mobility structures to better support faculty progression. The study provides both theoretical contributions and practical implications for university administrators seeking to optimize HR policies for faculty career development.</p>','["Faculty Career Growth, Human Resource Practices, Internal Mobility, Higher Education, Career Success."]',NULL,20,1,'1126 - 1141','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1149','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',62),
(1150,1150,1,'Beyond Hazard Recognition: Faculty Knowledge, Safety Attitudes, and Chemical Waste Management Practices in Higher Education Laboratories','<p>Safe chemical waste management in higher education laboratories requires more than recognizing chemical hazards; it also depends on how safety knowledge is translated into routine practice. This study assessed faculty knowledge of Globally Harmonized System (GHS) hazard pictograms, safety attitudes, and self-reported chemical waste management practices in selected higher education institutions in Tabuk City, Philippines. A descriptive-correlational survey was conducted among 76 faculty members involved in laboratory instruction, supervision, or chemical handling. Descriptive statistics and thematic categorization of open-ended responses were conducted using the full sample, while exploratory complete-case analyses (n = 19) examined group differences and relationships among knowledge, attitudes, and practices. Faculty generally recognized common GHS pictograms but showed weaker comprehension of the oxidizer and exclamation mark symbols. Safety attitudes were favorable, although emergency-response confidence and perceived institutional prioritization were comparatively weaker. Respondents reported frequent chemical segregation, container labeling, and peer guidance, while participation in formal safety training was less consistent. Exploratory analysis showed a strong positive association between safety attitudes and self-reported practices (r = .849, p &lt; .001), whereas GHS knowledge was not significantly associated with attitudes or practices. Open-ended responses identified concerns related to chemical waste disposal, training, safety standards, and laboratory facilities. The findings suggest that hazard recognition alone may not ensure consistent chemical waste management practice. Strengthening laboratory safety therefore requires practical hazard communication, emergency preparedness, institutional support, and appropriate chemical waste management systems.</p>','["chemical waste management", "GHS hazard communication", "laboratory safety culture", "higher education laboratories", "environmental management"]',NULL,20,1,'1142 - 1154','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1150','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',63),
(1151,1151,1,'Civil Protection against Misleading Advertisements via Influencers on Social Media Platforms: A Comparative Study','<p>This study examines the effectiveness of civil protection mechanisms against misleading advertisements disseminated through social media influencers, adopting comparative analytical approach between the Jordanian and French legal frameworks. It argues that influencer marketing, as a technologically driven and psychologically mediated practice, has fundamentally challenged traditional doctrines of advertising, contractual consent, and civil liability by exploiting virtual trust and para-social relationships.<br/>The research demonstrates that contemporary influencer practices particularly covert advertising, the use of visual filters, and artificial intelligence tools generate significant risks of consumer deception, leading to defects in consent and structural imbalances in contractual relations. It critically evaluates the adequacy of the Jordanian legal system, including the Civil Code, Consumer Protection Law No. (7) Of 2017, and Cybercrime Law No. (17) Of 2023, revealing key deficiencies such as the absence of a clear legal definition of influencers, the rigidity of evidentiary rules, and the lack of procedural mechanisms suited to the digital environment.<br/>In contrast, the study highlights the proactive nature of the French regulatory model, particularly the Commercial Influencer Law of 2023 and the European Digital Services Act (DSA), which establish strict transparency obligations, mandatory disclosure requirements, formalized contractual frameworks, and statutory joint liability among influencers, advertisers, and platforms. The analysis further explores the adaptation of civil liability elements fault, damage, and causation within the digital context, emphasizing the growing reliance on evidentiary presumptions to alleviate the burden of proof.<br/>The study concludes that the Jordanian legal framework requires comprehensive reform to address the realities of digital markets. It recommends the adoption of specialized legislation, enhanced transparency obligations, presumptive liability rules, platform accountability mechanisms, and procedural modernization to ensure effective consumer protection and restore contractual equilibrium in cyberspace.</p>','["Jordanian Consumer Protection Law, Influencer Marketing, Misleading Advertising, Consumer Protection, Civil Liability, Digital Platforms, Covert Advertising, Transparency Obligations, Artificial Intelligence (AI)"]',NULL,20,1,'1155 - 1187','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1151','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',64),
(1152,1152,1,'Project Management of Major Construction Projects in Jordan between Contractual Standardization and Dispute Reality: An Empirical Assessment of FIDIC Contracts','<p>Standard types of construction contracts have been heavily marketed as ways of improving contractual certainty, enhancing risk sharing and contractual disputes in complex construction projects. Of these, the FIDIC contract terms are widely used in international and public works projects, including in emerging construction markets such as Jordan. This extensive standardization notwithstanding, it is evident that construction disputes still do emerge, and this begs the critical question of whether or not FIDIC contracts actually play the functional role they are supposed to in practice.<br/>This research paper empirically investigates the issue of whether the use of FIDIC contract conditions is mainly a preventative instrument of dispute or rather realistically a dispute management framework. It was decided to use a quantitative dominant mixed methods approach, relying on survey data which will be collected among 400 construction professionals engaged in major FIDIC based projects. The necessary quantitative data were analyzed with the help of the Statistical Package of the Social Sciences (SPSS) using descriptive statistics, Pearson correlation analysis, and the linear regression, and semi structured interviews were conducted to help in contextual interpretation of the statistical results. The findings show that the use of the FIDIC contract conditions is statistically related with the better results of the dispute related outcomes; still, its explanatory capacity is small when investigated in isolation. The regression analysis shows that there is a significant yet limited predictive relationship between contractual standardization alone and the occurrence of disputes. Qualitative insights also indicate that organizational behavior, contract administration practices, delayed activation of dispute adjudication mechanisms, and extensive contractual amendments have a decisive role in escalating disputes, even when formal reliance is made on FIDIC contracts.<br/>These results offer some empirical evidence to the differentiation between dispute prevention and dispute management in construction contracting. The paper finds that FIDIC contracts are used more as governance systems, which organize the processes to resolve disputes rather than as a guarantee of avoiding disputes. Combining a legal analysis with empirical engineering data, the study makes a contribution to the current scholarship in the area of construction law and dispute resolution and highlights the significance of the alignment of the contractual mechanisms with the effective project governance and institutional practice.</p>','["Construction Dispute Management", "Dispute Prevention", "Engineering Management", "Contractual Governance", "Infrastructure Projects", "Jordan", "FIDIC", "SPSS."]',NULL,20,1,'1188 - 1208','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1152','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',65),
(1153,1153,1,'The Legal Framework of Deferred Payment Contracts via Electronic Applications and the Scope of Central Bank Supervision: A Comparative Study between Jordanian and French Legislation','<p>This research examines and analyzes the legal framework governing deferred payment contracts via electronic applications and the extent of their subjection to central bank supervision, based on an objective comparative study between Jordanian and French legislation. The study concludes that "Buy Now, Pay Later" (BNPL) contracts represent an innovative, three-dimensional financing and contractual model that falls outside the scope of traditional sales contracts prescribed in the Jordanian Civil Code, falling instead within the realm of assignment of rights (assignment of debt), subrogation, and the digital consumer credit framework.<br/>Furthermore, the study highlights the varying levels of legislative response to the challenges arising from this model in cyberspace. While the Jordanian legal system relies on the general rules of contract theory to invalidate abusive clauses and counter contracts of adhesion, the French legislator has swiftly resolved the obligatory nature of these contracts by explicitly categorizing deferred payment under consumer credit, thereby subjecting it to central bank licensing and the strict requirements of absolute banking monopoly.<br/>The research also illustrates the effective procedural and regulatory tools, represented by the Financial Consumer Protection Department and the Regulatory Sandbox for FinTech innovations adopted in Jordan, contrasted with the stringent powers, mandatory prior creditworthiness assessments of beneficiaries established in France to mitigate the risks of over-indebtedness and financial default. The study recommends that the Jordanian legislator codify this digital credit model and subject it to a specialized regulatory framework to preserve monetary stability and provide a comprehensive protective environment for consumers.</p>','["Buy Now Pay Later (BNPL) Contracts, Assignment of Rights and Subrogation Rules, Financial Consumer Protection Department, Regulatory Sandbox."]',NULL,20,1,'1209 - 1230','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1153','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',66),
(1154,1154,1,'Scalable AI-Driven Framework for Intelligent Data Identification and Migration','<p><br/>Intelligent methods are becoming more necessary for enterprise data migration to recognize, verify, and move diverse data from outdated systems to contemporary platforms. Data loss, delays, and operational inefficiencies can result from manual and rule-based techniques'' frequent struggles with missing documentation, inconsistent formats, unstructured data, and restricted scalability. To identify and migrate intelligent data, this study suggests an AI-driven framework incorporating machine learning, natural language processing, optical character recognition, metadata extraction, Elasticsearch indexing, anomaly detection, and continuous learning. The 500,000 structured database records, 100,000 text documents, and 50,000 text-containing images that made up the experimental dataset represented typical enterprise migration sources. For data identification, the suggested system obtained 98.5% precision, 97.8% recall, and a 98.1% F1-score. The CPU usage was reduced from 70% to 30%, the RAM usage was reduced from 75% to 25%, and the migration time was reduced from six months to three weeks. The scalability tests showed stable accuracy for datasets of 10k to 1M records. The study''s thorough and scalable AI-powered migration process improves the accuracy, efficiency, and reliability of digital transformation in structured, unstructured, and image-based enterprise data environments.</p>','["Artificial intelligence, Data migration, Data identification, Optical character recognition, Machine learning."]',NULL,20,1,'1231 - 1247','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1154','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',67),
(1158,1158,1,'Performance Evaluation of Reliable Data Dissemination in VANETs under Varying Node Densities using NS-3','<p>VANET, an essential component of ITS, provides effective communication when prompt action is needed. However, data dissemination is an issue because VANET is known for its mobility and dynamic nature. Using ns-3 simulations, the study assesses the effectiveness of data dissemination in VANET. The procedure takes into account UDP traffic flow with a packet size of 1024 bytes and a node density of 5 to 30. Packet Delivery Ratio (PDR), delay, and throughput are a few of the crucial factors. From the result, it is clear that while dense networks suffer from congestion issues resulting in a low PDR of 11.59%, low throughput of ~120 bps, and high delay of ~8 ms, sparse networks provide outstanding reliability with a PDR of 76%, throughput of ~760 bps, and low latency/delay of ~1.3 ms. Performance and reliability are reasonably well balanced for medium node density cases. The study emphasizes the importance of using adaptive distribution systems in situations where vehicles are dynamically moving.</p>','["VANETs, Packet delivery ratio, User Datagram Protocol, NS-3 simulator, Density aware dissemination."]',NULL,20,1,'1248 - 1268','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1158','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',68);
INSERT INTO publications (id,submission_id,version,title,abstract,keywords,references_text,issue_id,section_id,pages,doi,copyright_holder,copyright_year,license_url,status,date_published,seq) VALUES
(1159,1159,1,'A Toric Statistical Decision-Support Framework for Exact Inference from Sparse Public-Health Data','<p>Sparse public-health tables are often released through margins, structural zeros, or suppression bounds. This study develops a toric statistical decision-support framework for stratified binary surveillance data and exact conditional procedures that preserve specified aggregate constraints. One quadratic move per stratum connects every fixed-margin fiber, while uncoupled cell bounds produce truncated hypergeometric factors. When the total diseased–exposed count is additionally fixed across strata, cross-stratum exchange moves connect the resulting non-factorizing fiber. A dynamic-programming recurrence evaluates the exact null distribution without enumerating all tables. In a reproducible four-stratum benchmark with 88 observations and 6,930 fixed-margin tables, the Mantel–Haenszel odds ratio is 2.540. The uncorrected Cochran–Mantel–Haenszel test gives p = 0.0401, whereas the exact probability-ordered test gives p = 0.0503. The differing decisions at the 5% significance level demonstrate that sparse-data asymptotics can alter a borderline public-health conclusion. The framework provides an interpretable computational basis for reliable decisions from sparse, structurally constrained, or partially suppressed surveillance information.</p>','["toric model", "exact conditional inference", "decision support", "public-health surveillance", "contingency table."]',NULL,20,1,'1269 - 1274','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1159','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',69),
(1160,1160,1,'Towards Data-Driven Instructional Decision-Making: A Learning Analytics Framework for Pedagogical Content Knowledge-Based Mathematics Learning','<p>Effective instructional decision-making in mathematics education requires more than evaluating learning outcomes after instruction; it demands timely, evidence-based insights that inform pedagogical interventions throughout the learning process. This study proposes a Learning Analytics Framework for Data-Driven Instructional Decision-Making grounded in Pedagogical Content Knowledge (PCK) to support the design, evaluation, and continuous improvement of learning modules for Mathematics in the Modern World. Using a design research approach, the study examined how mathematics professors in Teacher Education Institutions (TEIs) integrate PCK into instruction and translated these practices into an analytics-informed instructional framework. The framework incorporates constructivist learning principles, collaborative and discovery-based learning, contextualized mathematics instruction, and learner-centered pedagogies as key instructional indicators for evidence-based teaching decisions. The developed learning module was evaluated as acceptable across physical design, learning outcomes, mathematical content and processes, instructional design, learning activities, assessment procedures, and relevance to PCK development. Student performance analysis demonstrated significant improvement following exposure to the module, while qualitative findings indicated enhanced learner engagement, meaningful learning experiences, and greater appreciation of mathematics through interactive and authentic learning activities. Building on these findings, the proposed learning analytics framework identifies instructional indicators: including learner performance, engagement, activity completion, and PCK-based teaching strategies, this is to support continuous monitoring and data-driven instructional decision-making. The study contributes a conceptual foundation for integrating learning analytics with PCK, enabling instructors to make timely instructional adjustments that improve teaching effectiveness and student learning in higher mathematics education.</p>','["Pedagogical content knowledge (PCK), Instructional Analytics, Instructional Material, Learning module, Mathematics Education"]',NULL,20,1,'1275 - 1286','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1160','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',70),
(1162,1162,1,'How Transformational Leadership Enhances Employee Well-Being in Chinese Public Hospitals: The Mediating Role of Organizational Commitment','<p>Purpose: This study examines the impact of transformational leadership (TL) on employee well-being (EWB) in Chinese public hospitals, with a focus on the mediating role of organizational commitment (OC).Design/methodology/approach: A quantitative, cross-sectional survey was conducted among 377 employees from four tertiary public hospitals in Sichuan Province, China. Transformational leadership was measured across its four dimensions, namely Idealized Influence (II), Inspirational Motivation (IM), Intellectual Stimulation (IS), and Individualized Consideration (IC), using the Multifactor Leadership Questionnaire (MLQ-5X). Organizational commitment was assessed using the Three-Component Model scale, and employee well-being was measured using a validated three-dimensional scale. Structural equation modeling (SEM) with bootstrapping was employed to test direct and mediation effects.Findings: All four TL dimensions significantly predicted both EWB and OC. Idealized Influence had the strongest direct effect on EWB (β = 0.346), while Intellectual Stimulation was the strongest predictor of OC (β = 0.398). Organizational commitment exhibited the largest direct effect on EWB (β = 0.399) and partially mediated all four TL–EWB relationships. The structural model explained 86.9% of the variance in EWB.Originality/value: This study extends transformational leadership theory to the under-researched context of Chinese public hospitals, providing dimension-level evidence of TL''s differential effects on well-being and commitment. By integrating transformational leadership theory with evidence-based management, the study demonstrates how organizational commitment functions as a key mechanism through which leadership decisions enhance employee well-being. The findings also provide practical decision support for hospital managers seeking to improve organizational performance under resource constraints.</p>','["Transformational Leadership", "Employee Well-Being", "Organizational Commitment", "Mediation", "Public Hospitals."]',NULL,20,1,'1287 - 1309','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1162','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',71),
(1163,1163,1,'Adaptive Energy-Based Multi-Bit Audio Steganography with Cryptographic Integrity Verification for Secure Executable File Concealment','<p>Audio steganography remains a challenging area of information hiding due to the sensitivity of the Human Auditory System (HAS), where minor signal modifications may introduce perceptible distortion. Traditional Least Significant Bit (LSB)-based audio steganography methods offer high payload capacity but often suffer from fixed embedding strategies, limited adaptability to signal characteristics, and vulnerability to statistical detection. This paper presents an adaptive energy-based multi-bit audio steganography framework for securely embedding executable payloads into uncompressed WAV audio signals while maintaining perceptual transparency and computational efficiency. Unlike conventional fixed-depth LSB techniques, the proposed method dynamically adjusts the embedding depth (1, 2, or 3 bits) according to local audio sample amplitude to balance payload capacity and signal fidelity. To enhance confidentiality and data integrity, the executable payload is protected using Fernet encryption and validated through CRC32 and SHA-256 mechanisms. Furthermore, vectorized NumPy processing is employed to improve embedding and extraction efficiency for large payloads. Experimental evaluation is performed using multiple WAV audio files under varying payload sizes, and performance is assessed using Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio (PSNR), Signal-to-Noise Ratio (SNR), embedding time, and extraction time. Results indicate that the proposed adaptive strategy achieves good payload embedding capability with minimal perceptual degradation under the evaluated conditions. The study also discusses reproducibility considerations, limitations, and future directions including robustness evaluation and steganalysis resistance</p>','["Audio steganography, Adaptive multi-bit LSB embedding, Energy-based embedding, Executable payload concealment, Information hiding, Cryptographic integrity verification, Fernet encryption, SHA-256, CRC32, Secure covert communication, Stego-audio analysis, NumPy vectorization."]',NULL,20,1,'1310 - 1330','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1163','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',72),
(1164,1164,1,'Artificial Intelligence-Enabled Predictive Decision Support Systems for Smart Enterprise and Industrial Applications','<p>AI-powered predictive systems for decision support are revolutionizing the way that smart enterprises and industrial organizations are analysing data, predicting future conditions, and making operational and strategic decisions. The systems include machine learning, deep learning, predictive analytics, prescriptive analytics, real-time monitoring, and intelligent recommendation systems to enhance decision-making accuracy, efficiency, and responsiveness. They are used in business forecasting, customer and financial analytics, supply-chain and inventory management, predictive maintenance, production optimization, quality control, energy management, workplace safety and asset monitoring. The addition of new technologies like the Internet of Things, Industrial Internet of Things, digital twins, cloud and edge computing, robotics, blockchain and next generation networks further improve system connectivity, scalability and real-time performance. The successful implementation of these steps needs a structured framework for problem identification, data collection, preprocessing, feature engineering, model selection, training, validation, system integration, deployment, and continual monitoring. Despite these progressions, data quality, interoperability, scalability, algorithmic bias, explainability, privacy, cybersecurity, organizational readiness, and regulatory compliance are all important challenges that still need to be addressed. There is still a need for human oversight, especially when dealing with safety-critical and high-impact decisions. It includes the technological foundations, system architecture, implementation processes, enterprise and industrial applications, performance evaluation, governance requirements, and future directions of AI-supported predictive decision support systems. It concludes that the systems that are trustworthy, secure, transparent, sustainable and intelligent are enterprise and industrial operations.</p>','["Artificial intelligence", "predictive decision support systems", "smart enterprise", "predictive analytics", "smart manufacturing", "digital twins."]',NULL,20,1,'1331 - 1347','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1164','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',73),
(1165,1165,1,'Accelerating Linear Regression: A Performance Evaluation Using Sequential, Multicore, and Parallel Computing','<p>Linear Regression is one of the most fundamental algorithms in the field of machine learning and statistical data analysis, offering a simple yet powerful approach for predictive modeling. This paper presents an extensive study of the Linear Regression algorithm, emphasizing its theoretical foundation, implementation process, and practical applications in prediction tasks. The research explores the mathematical formulation of the model, where the relationship between dependent and independent variables is expressed through a linear equation, and parameters are estimated using the Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) method to minimize prediction errors. Experimental analysis conducted on both synthetic and real-world datasets demonstrates that Linear Regression provides reliable and interpretable predictions when the underlying relationship between variables is linear. However, its accuracy declines for complex nonlinear data patterns. Despite these limitations, Linear Regression remains a cornerstone in predictive analytics due to its computational efficiency, interpretability, and ability to serve as a foundational framework for developing more advanced regression and machine learning algorithms</p>','["Linear Regression, Ordinary Least Squares (OLS), Predictive Modeling, Supervised Learning, Statistical Analysis, Regression Techniques, Data Prediction."]',NULL,20,1,'1348 - 1362','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1165','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',74),
(1166,1166,1,'Mathematical Modeling of Secure Wireless Networks: A Biomathematical Approach to Cybercrime Detection and Prevention','<p>Wireless networks are increasingly exposed to cybercrime because of open communication channels, distributed nodes, and evolving attack strategies. Traditional cybersecurity methods may not fully capture the dynamic spread of cyber threats across connected systems. This study develops a biomathematical model inspired by biological infection and immune-response mechanisms to analyze cybercrime detection and prevention in secure wireless networks. The proposed model classifies wireless network nodes into susceptible, infected, protected, and recovered compartments. Cyberattack transmission, protection, detection, recovery, and loss of security immunity are represented using ordinary differential equations. Equilibrium analysis, stability conditions, the cybercrime reproduction number R_0, numerical and sensitivity analysis are used to evaluate attack control and network resilience. The model shows that cybercrime declines when R_0&lt;1, indicating that detection and recovery rates exceed the attack transmission rate. Numerical results further show that adaptive biomathematical defense reduces infected nodes, improves detection performance, and increases network resilience compared with non-adaptive defense. The proposed framework provides a mathematical basis for cybercrime detection and prevention in wireless networks and supports adaptive security planning through improved detection, rapid recovery, and strengthened protection mechanisms.</p>','["Biomathematical modeling", "wireless networks", "cybercrime detection", "adaptive defense", "network resilience."]',NULL,20,1,'1363 - 1374','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1166','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',75),
(1167,1167,1,'Social Media Addiction and Compulsive Buying Behaviors: Investigating the Mediating Role of Social Comparison Using Structural Equation Modeling','<p>The rapid expansion of social media platforms has fundamentally transformed consumer behavior by creating highly interactive digital environments that influence purchasing decisions and psychological well-being. While social media offers numerous benefits for communication and information sharing, excessive engagement has contributed to addictive usage patterns that may encourage impulsive and compulsive consumption behaviors. Among the psychological mechanisms underlying this relationship, social comparison has emerged as a significant cognitive process through which individuals evaluate themselves against others'' curated online lifestyles, potentially stimulating materialistic aspirations and excessive purchasing tendencies. Despite increasing scholarly attention, limited research has comprehensively examined the mediating role of social comparison in the relationship between social media addiction and compulsive buying behaviour using Structural Equation Modeling (SEM). This study aims to investigate the direct effect of social media addiction on compulsive buying behaviour and to examine whether social comparison mediates this relationship. The study proposes a conceptual model grounded in Social Comparison Theory and the Stimulus–Organism–Response (S–O–R) framework, explaining how excessive social media use stimulates psychological comparison processes that subsequently influence compulsive purchasing decisions. A quantitative research design is adopted using a structured questionnaire administered to active social media users. Data are analyzed using Structural Equation Modeling to evaluate the measurement model, structural relationships, and mediation effects among the study constructs.</p>','["Social Media Addiction, Compulsive Buying Behaviors, Social Comparison, Structural Equation Modeling, Consumer Behaviors.."]',NULL,20,1,'1375 - 1390','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1167','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',76),
(1168,1168,1,'A Multi-Agent AI Framework for Explainable and Low-Latency Investment Analytics in Structured Finance','<p>Structured finance environments generate large volumes of heterogeneous, dynamic, and time-sensitive financial data, creating significant challenges for investment analytics, risk assessment, and decision-making. Many traditional investment intelligence systems are built on a centralized architecture, which has scalability, transparency, and real-time responsiveness challenges. To overcome these problems, this paper suggests a multi-agent artificial intelligence framework for explainable and low-latency investment analytics in structured finance. Its structure is based on the concept of a community of smart agents responsible for gathering financial information, creating market knowledge, valuing risk, predicting, explaining, and optimizing investments. The orchestration layer helps to coordinate interactions between agents and enables parallel processing of agents, leading to more efficient and responsive analysis. The high-tech machine learning models are integrated with the Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) mechanisms and produce clear, interpretable, and meaningful investment tips. It is an active system that is constantly analyzing financial data and can predict market trends and patterns and give good insights into investments without sacrificing transparency of investment decisions. The performance of the proposed framework on investment analytics is validated experimentally, which shows significant improvement in the performance of the investment analytics and an investment prediction accuracy of 97.3%. Furthermore, the framework reduces the latency of processing and guarantees the transparency, scalability, and reliability of decision-making. The results indicate that the suggested solution has the potential for offering an efficient and reliable solution for future-generation investment analytics using a structured finance environment.</p>','["Multi-Agent Artificial Intelligence, Structured Finance Analytics, Explainable AI, Investment Decision Support, Financial Risk Analytics, Low-Latency Intelligence, Predictive Analytics, Reinforcement Learning, Temporal Forecasting, Intelligent Financial Systems."]',NULL,20,1,'1391 - 1419','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1168','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',77),
(1169,1169,1,'A Neural Network Approach for Health-Based Capability Classification of Mine Workers in Hazardous Environments','<p>Mine workers are frequently exposed to hazardous environments, including extreme temperatures, dust, and toxic gases, which significantly impact their health and performance. This system leverages artificial intelligence and machine learning techniques to monitor and evaluate workers’ health in real time. The proposed model utilizes key health and behavioural parameters such as age, Body Mass Index (BMI), oxygen saturation (SpO₂), alcohol consumption, and job satisfaction. A neural network-based classification algorithm processes this data to categorize workers into three capability levels: Best, Moderate, and Limited. These classifications assist supervisors in making informed decisions regarding task allocation, identifying workers who may require medical attention, and ensuring optimal workforce management. The system features a user-friendly interface that enables easy data entry and provides real-time predictions and recommendations, making it accessible to non-technical users. By enabling proactive health monitoring and data-driven decision-making, the system reduces health risks, enhances operational efficiency, and promotes a safer and more sustainable mining environment. Furthermore, its scalable design allows integration with larger datasets and supports future expansion, ensuring long-term applicability and effectiveness.</p>','["Artificial Intelligence (AI), Machine Learning (ML), Real-Time Health Monitoring, Mining Industry, Neural Networks, Data Classification, Smart Decision Support System, Industrial Safety, Predictive Analytics."]',NULL,20,1,'1420 - 1431','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1169','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',78),
(1170,1170,1,'Analysis of AI and IoT-based Innovative Urban Predictive Intelligence Applications in Smart Cities','<p>Predictive intelligence in smart cities is one of the prevalent research domain for artificial intelligence and internet of things. However, there is no sufficient study on the mapping of its scholarly growth like thematic evolution and future trajectories. This study utilized bibliometric analysis on Scopus indexed documents from 2020 to 2025, applying PRISMA workflow, multi domain Boolean search strategy and analytical tools and science mapping techniques included in bibliometrix R-package and VOSviewer. Publication trends, citation structures, keyword co-occurrence mapping, co-authorship networks, thematic evolution and conceptual structure mapping were investigated. More or less 17,557 scopus-indexed publications were analyzed showing an annual growth rate of 16.42% where India, China and the United States lead global contributions. Predominant journals were found in the IEEE Internet of Things and IEEE Transactions on Intelligent Transportation Systems. Keyword co-occurrence revealed machine learning, deep learning, IoT, edge computing and smart city as the core thematic anchors and well-developed research areas. This study confirms that Artificial Intelligence and Internet of Things based forecasting in smart cities is a rapidly advancing research field. A roadmap for scholarly inquiry and policy-relevant applications can be explored where critical gaps and opportunities in areas such as federated learning, digital twins and cross-domain predictive analytics were highlighted.</p>','["artificial intelligence", "internet of things", "smart cities", "machine learning", "urban forecasting."]',NULL,20,1,'1432 - 1445','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1170','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',79),
(1171,1171,1,'Digital Competence as a Proxy for AI Readiness and Equity Conditions for Undergraduate Research: A Cross-Sectional Student–Faculty Study in Panama','<p>Undergraduate research (UR) increasingly requires students and mentors to work in digitally mediated environments, yet digital readiness and access to support may be unevenly distributed. This cross-sectional, observational secondary analysis examined student and faculty perception data from Panama to characterize competencies relevant to technology-enhanced UR. Digital competence was treated explicitly as a proxy for, rather than a direct measure of, AI readiness. Analyses used a student domain-profile subsample (N = 96) and a student–faculty comparison subsample (N = 42), with regional distributions, high-affirmation percentages, mean scores, and dispersion examined descriptively. Students reported a comparatively strong information-management and digital-competence profile (mean regional high affirmation = 69.27%), although regional and domain-level asymmetries remained; mathematics showed the widest regional range (40.8 points). Student and faculty mean perceptions were similar (2.82 and 2.84), but student responses were more dispersed (SD = 0.664 vs. 0.418), and student high-affirmation percentages exceeded faculty estimates in every region. Faculty technology-use readiness was generally positive but varied regionally (53.3%–85.7%). The findings support a variance-sensitive diagnostic framework that distinguishes digital-readiness foundations, student–faculty perceptual alignment, and mentor-side capacity. They do not demonstrate AI literacy or AI-specific performance; instead, they identify conditions that may support equitable development of AI-ready UR ecosystems when combined with explicit integrity, verification, and mentoring practices.</p>','["Undergraduate research", "AI-readiness proxy", "Digital competence", "Equity", "Student\\u2013faculty alignment", "Faculty readiness", "Panama."]',NULL,20,1,'1446 - 1461','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1171','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',80),
(1172,1172,1,'Priority-Coefficient Feature Fusion and Explainable Character-BiLSTM for Multi-Class Malicious URL Detection','<p>Malicious URLs remain a persistent attack vector for phishing, malware delivery and web defacement, but operational filters often reduce the problem to binary benign/malicious detection. This paper presents X-URL, a mathematically grounded explainable framework for multi-class malicious URL classification. The framework combines quantitative feature analysis (QFA), Shannon-entropy and information-gain-based priority coefficients, character-level bidirectional long short-term memory (BiLSTM), Bayesian hyperparameter optimization and SHAP-based explanation. The learning objective is formulated as weighted multi-class cross-entropy with class-imbalance coefficients, while priority feature fusion maps normalized lexical and structural indicators into an information-theoretic weighted vector. X-URL classifies URLs into benign, defacement, malware and phishing classes and produces analyst-readable reason codes. Experiments were conducted on 651,191 URLs using a stratified 80/20 split; the independent test set contained 130,236 samples. X-URL achieved 97.73% accuracy, 97.71% weighted precision, 97.73% weighted recall and 97.72% weighted F1-score. Additional analysis shows a macro F1-score of 96.68%, a balanced accuracy of 96.33%, and an approximate 95% confidence interval of 97.65-97.81% for test accuracy. Ablation results indicate that BiLSTM improves accuracy by 3.26 percentage points over vanilla LSTM, and QFA adds another 3.23 points with only 0.4 ms extra latency per URL. Algorithmic analysis shows that inference complexity is linear in URL length and quadratic in the BiLSTM hidden size, O(T H(H+d)+m), making X-URL suitable for high-throughput web filtering when combined with caching and slow-path enrichment..</p>','["Malicious URL detection", "Multi-class classification", "Priority-coefficient analysis", "Information-theoretic feature fusion", "Character-BiLSTM", "Explainable AI", "."]',NULL,20,1,'1462 - 1490','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1172','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',81),
(1173,1173,1,'The Influence of Information and Communication Technology (ICT) in the Transition from Vulnerable to Viable Conditions of People and Resources in The Regencies of Demak and Pati, Central Java-Indonesia','<p>Information and Communication Technology (ICT) is increasingly recognized as a strategic enabler for strengthening the viability of small-scale fisheries, yet its effectiveness depends on the ecological and institutional contexts in which it is adopted. This study investigates how ICT influences the transition from vulnerability to livelihood viability among small-scale fishers in Demak and Pati, Central Java. A sequential explanatory mixed-methods design was employed by integrating quantitative bioeconomic analysis using the Gordon–Schaefer model and Catch per Unit Effort (CPUE) indicators with qualitative evidence from in-depth interviews, field observations, and data triangulation. The findings demonstrate that ICT does not directly improve livelihoods; instead, it operates through a pathway that reduces environmental uncertainty by providing timely weather and fishing-ground information, enabling fishers to optimize trip planning, reduce fuel consumption, improve fishing efficiency, and increase economic returns. However, these benefits vary substantially between locations. In Pati, healthier fish stocks, clearer governance of the fishing zone, and stronger institutional support enable ICT to generate greater productivity gains and livelihood improvements. In contrast, in Demak, ecological degradation and intensive resource exploitation constrain the effectiveness of ICT, limiting its contribution despite relatively higher digital literacy among fishers. These findings suggest that ICT should be understood as a complementary rather than a standalone intervention. Its contribution to livelihood viability depends on the interaction between technology adoption, ecological sustainability, institutional governance, and adaptive resource management. The study highlights that digital transformation in small-scale fisheries must therefore be accompanied by sustainable fisheries management and strengthened institutional capacity to achieve long-term livelihood resilience.</p>','["ICT", "transition", "vulnerable", "viable", "small-scale, fishers, Demak, Pati."]',NULL,20,1,'1491 - 1512','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1173','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',82),
(1174,1174,1,'Agentic Artificial Intelligence in Corporate Governance: Transforming Board Leadership through Autonomous Intelligence and Adaptive Decision-Making','<p>The study examined that how Agentic artificial intelligence (AI) can transform corporate governance by improving the quality of decision-making at the board level, governance effectiveness, and readiness of stakeholders. Its main goal is to examine the impact of autonomous intelligence and adaptive learning processes within Agentic AI systems on the strategy, risk management, compliance, and governance performance. The study used quantitative research design and used a structured questionnaire that was given to 200 corporate governance stakeholders, who are board directors, executives and compliance professionals. Analysis and interpretation of data was done by use of “descriptive statistics, correlation analysis, regression analysis, and paired sample t-tests” in SPSS. The findings indicate that Agentic AI capability has a statistically significant positive correlation with the quality of board-level decision-making, and Agentic AI capability should be considered a significant contributor to the accountability of the governance results. Additional results show that the degree of Agentic AI integration has a moderate and significant relationship with the overall corporate governance effectiveness. More so, the willingness of stakeholders to adopt Agentic AI is also high even though their exposure to practical experience is rather limited. The study concludes that Agentic AI can be viewed as a governance enhancing mechanism that enhances transparency, decreases information asymmetry and promotes adaptive, evidence-based board leadership.</p>','["Agentic Artificial Intelligence, Corporate Governance, Leadership, Board- level, Stakeholders, Autonomous."]',NULL,20,1,'1513 - 1536','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1174','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',83),
(1175,1175,1,'Intrusion Detection and Classification using Deep Learning and Nature Inspired Optimization for IOT Based Network','<p>The Internet of Things (IoT) has rapidly developed and, accordingly, opened the door for more cyber-attacks due to the increase in the number of connected devices and the amount of sensitive data transmitted over these networks. Classic Intrusion Detection Systems (IDS) are unable to recognize threats that are coming in newly created ways since they cannot analyze the different traffic patterns well. The proposed intelligent intrusion detection framework integrates Deep Learning (DL) with nature-inspired optimization for efficient attack classification. The Hybrid CNN–LSTM deep architecture is designed to extract and learn temporal and spatial features of the network traffic, while “Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO)” and “Ant Colony Optimization (ACO)” are used for optimal feature selection and dimensionality reduction on the UNSW-NB15 dataset. The results of this study show that the optimization process not only greatly improves the classification accuracy but also saves a lot of time in computations. The Hybrid CNN-LSTM model obtains a binary accuracy of 0.948, which is coupled with 0.948 for precision, recall, and F1-score. Additionally, it surpasses CNN and LSTM models used separately. In multiclass prediction, the hybrid method claims 0.720 accuracy, 0.510 precision, and 0.680 recall, thus showing a considerable improvement in the detection of varied IoT attacks.</p>','["IoT Security, Intrusion Detection System (IDS), Nature-Inspired Optimization (PSO, ACO), CNN, LSTM."]',NULL,20,1,'1537 - 1558','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1175','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',84),
(1176,1176,1,'Privileged Access Management for z/OS System Programmers: A Zero-Trust Control Framework for RACF, TSO, and SDSF Administrative Sessions','<p>The PAM in z/OS systems needs additional measures in the realm of protecting sensitive data from misuse by insiders and credential abuse. Classic approaches using authentication through static mechanisms do not provide adequate protection of administrative sessions that use RACF, TSO, and SDSF, thus leaving systems open to risks associated with administrative activities. To combat these risks, this paper proposes implementing a new control approach for PAM known as the Zero-Trust Control Framework, based on behavioural analysis, graph theory, and entropy calculations. The new tool developed as part of this framework is called Behavioural Identity Drift Analyzer (BIDA). As such, the Privilege Interaction Graph Transformer (PIGT) leverages graph neural networks and transformers to generate privilege interaction graphs that detect the escalation routes, dependencies, and propagation vectors of insider threats in near real-time. Lastly, the Entropy Adaptive Zero-Trust Orchestrator (EAZTO) analyzes uncertainties in user actions, commands, and privileges in order to apply adaptive control measures such as multi-factor authentication, privilege reduction, or even session termination. In combination, these four modules provide layers of protection and achieve continuous PAM decision-making at 90 percent accuracy for the purpose of enhancing zero trust enforcement in z/OS administration.</p>','["PAM Security, Zero-Trust Approach, z/OS Systems Security, Behavioural Identity Drift Analysis, Privilege Interactions Graph Transformation, Entropy Adaptive Zero Trust Management, RACF Administration Controls, TSO Commands, SDSF Sessions, Insider Threat Detection."]',NULL,20,1,'1559 - 1582','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1176','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',85),
(1177,1177,1,'Wave Climate Characterisation across the Coastline of North Java and Its Implications for Coastal Management','<p>Characterising the wave climate of the Pantura coast of northern Java is essential for coastal flood-risk assessment and infrastructure design, yet directionally separated, spatially resolved analyses across this densely populated corridor remain limited. This study uses 46 years (1980–2025) of WAVERYS reanalysis data to characterise the directional, seasonal, and interannual wave climate at Jakarta and Semarang, and across five offshore regions spanning the full Pantura coast. Extreme wave conditions were estimated using a Peak-Over-Threshold Generalised Pareto Distribution (POT–GPD) framework, applied separately to Easterly and North-Westerly wave populations corresponding to the two monsoon-driven regimes. Results reveal two complementary spatial and seasonal gradients, with mean significant wave height increases from west to east and is highest during the East Monsoon (June–August), while the upper tail of the wave distribution is governed by North-Westerly events that intensify eastward and reach 100-year return levels of 3.5–4.0 m in the eastern domain. ENSO and the Indian Ocean Dipole modulate the North-West Monsoon wave signal, with neutral-to-negative phases amplifying wave energy and positive phases suppressing it. A media-based inventory of 49 coastal flood events at Jakarta and Semarang (2015–2025) confirms a first-order association between North-West Monsoon wave activity and flood occurrence but reveals contrasting compound-hazard pathways at the two cities. Jakarta flooding is linked to climate-modulated wave forcing interacting with rainfall and river discharge, whereas Semarang''s primary May–June flood peak, occurring when offshore wave energy is near or below the annual average, could be more related to the seasonal sea-level anomalies and spring tides amplified by rapid land subsidence. These findings provide the first directionally separated, spatially resolved extreme-wave characterisation across the full Pantura corridor and offer practical guidance for spatially differentiated coastal infrastructure standards, monsoon-separated engineering design, and climate-informed pre-monsoon preparedness.</p>','["Coastal Flooding", "Management", "Pantura Coast", "Wave Climate."]',NULL,20,1,'1583 - 1608','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1177','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',86),
(1178,1178,1,'A Multi-Agent Autonomous Marketing System Powered by Agentic Ai: Redefining, Planning, Execution, Experimentation and Optimization Across Marketing Functions','<p>As Digital marketing ecosystems are becoming increasingly complex, intelligent, flexible, and autonomous solutions are required, which are capable of taking decisions in real-time. In order to alter the conventional marketing lifecycle, this study proposes a novel framework of a Multi-Agent Autonomous Marketing System (MAAMS) powered by Agentic Artificial Intelligence (AI). The proposed framework facilitates decentralized yet coordinated execution of marketing activities by integrating specialized agents for planning, execution, experimentation, and optimization tasks. Each of these agents employs machine learning (ML) algorithms, data-driven intelligence, and feedback mechanisms in order to increase consumer engagement and campaign success rates. The dynamic nature of the proposed framework allows strategies to be adapted in real-time in response to environmental changes and performance tracking, which is a characteristic of the closed-loop system architecture. The multi-agent coordination also introduces a novel paradigm for managing complex interdependencies of marketing activities, including budgeting, targeting, content management, and performance tracking.</p>','["Agentic AI . Optimization Frame Work, MAA Marketing System . Autonomous system . Digital Marketing . Machine Learning."]',NULL,20,1,'1609 - 1631','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1178','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',87),
(1179,1179,1,'Governance Alignment and Local Government Authority in Urban Transport Programme Performance: Evidence from Indonesia’s Buy The Service (BTS) Programme','<p>This study investigates the governance determinants influencing the performance of Indonesia’s Buy The Service (BTS) urban transport programme and its implications for sustainable mobility and environmental planning. Using a mixed-methods design that integrates Partial Least Squares Structural Equation Modelling (PLS-SEM) with qualitative institutional analysis, the study examines how Policy Alignment, Institutional Capacity, Coordination, Regulatory Design, Political Commitment, Public Participation, and Resistance to Change shape Local Government Authority and, subsequently, BTS performance. The results show that Local Government Authority is the strongest predictor of BTS outcomes (β = 0.789; R² = 0.706), underscoring the importance of coherent mandates, regulatory power, and coordinated planning in delivering effective public transport services. Resistance to Change demonstrates a significant negative influence, reflecting broader political economy constraints that undermine institutional coherence and programme implementation. Qualitative findings further reveal that governance-driven improvements contribute to perceived environmental benefits, including reduced congestion, emissions, and private vehicle dependence, linking BTS performance to broader sustainable development objectives. The study offers important policy implications by highlighting the need for stronger cross-level policy alignment, enhanced regulatory authority for local governments, institutionalized coordination mechanisms, and participatory strategies to manage stakeholder resistance. The findings also extend theoretical discourse by identifying authority as a central mediating mechanism within multi-level governance frameworks. Limitations related to perceived environmental measures and sample coverage suggest opportunities for future research incorporating longitudinal designs, direct environmental impact data, and comparative analyses across urban contexts.</p>','["BTS, governance alignment, local government authority, urban transport."]',NULL,20,1,'1632 - 1654','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1179','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',88),
(1180,1180,1,'The Effectiveness of Flipped Classroom and Think-Pair-Share in Enhancing English Proficiency and Student Engagement among EFL Learners in Higher Education','<p>This study investigates the effectiveness of a blended learning model integrating Flipped Classroom and Think-Pair-Share (TPS) in university-level English language teaching (ELT). It evaluates its impact on English proficiency and student engagement, addressing the growing need for interactive, student-centered instruction in higher education. A quasi-experimental mixed-methods design was applied to 80 first-semester university students, divided into experimental and control groups. The experimental group engaged in Flipped Classroom and TPS activities, while the control group followed conventional instruction. English proficiency was measured through pre- and post-tests, while student engagement was assessed using questionnaires, interviews, and classroom observations. Paired t-tests and thematic analysis were conducted to analyze the data. The experimental group outperformed the control group in all language skills, with the highest gains in speaking (30%) and writing (26.5%). Student engagement significantly increased, particularly in classroom discussions (+38.71%). Qualitative findings revealed that students found the blended model more engaging, interactive, and effective in fostering collaboration and confidence in language use. The study provides empirical evidence supporting the integration of Flipped Classroom and TPS in ELT, offering practical insights for curriculum development, faculty training, and policy adaptation in higher education. This study contributes to blended learning research in ELT, demonstrating its potential for scalable, adaptive, and technology-enhanced pedagogies. Future research should explore long-term retention, cross-cultural implementation, and AI-driven learning support.</p>','["Blended Learning, English Language Teaching, Flipped Classroom, Higher Education, Think-Pair-Share.."]',NULL,20,1,'1655 - 1668','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1180','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',89),
(1181,1181,1,'Effect of Oil Palm Empty Fruit Bunch-Derived Nanocellulose Loading on the Mid-to-High-Frequency Sound Absorption of Polyurethane Composites','<p>Polyurethane foams, while widely used for acoustic insulation, exhibit limited effectiveness at low frequencies and are typically derived from non-renewable sources. This study addresses both limitations by fabricating polyurethane composites reinforced with nanocellulose extracted from oil palm empty fruit bunches, an abundant agricultural waste. We prepared nanocellulose through alkali and bleaching treatments followed by mechanical grinding, then incorporated it into a polyurethane matrix at weight ratios of 3%, 5%, 10%, and 20%. Sound absorption coefficients were measured using a Brüel and Kjær impedance tube following ASTM E1050-2 across a frequency range of 1600 to 6000 Hz. The control polyurethane showed absorption coefficients below 0.3 at low frequencies, improving to approximately 0.75 near 4000 Hz. The addition of nanocellulose systematically shifted peak absorption toward higher frequencies and increased maximum coefficients. The 10% nanocellulose composite achieved the highest peak absorption coefficient of 0.78 at approximately 3500 Hz, representing a significant improvement over the control. The 5% formulation provided a well-balanced absorption profile across mid-to-high frequencies with coefficients exceeding 0.7 above 3000 Hz. However, the 20% composite exhibited a more gradual increase in absorption, indicating fiber agglomeration and reduced pore connectivity at higher loadings. Performance remained limited below 1000 Hz for all formulations. These results demonstrate that nanocellulose from oil palm empty fruit bunches can effectively enhance the mid-to-high frequency sound absorption of polyurethane composites. This work contributes to the development of sustainable acoustic materials while valorizing agricultural waste, offering a viable pathway for noise control applications where high-frequency attenuation is required.</p>','["Polyurethane composites, Nanocellulose, Oil palm empty fruit bunches (OPEFB), Acoustic insulation, Sound absorption, Sustainable materials, Agricultural waste valorization, Polyurethane foam, Impedance tube, Noise control."]',NULL,20,1,'1669 - 1686','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1181','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',90),
(1182,1182,1,'Circulo: A Mobile Safety Tracking System Using Geofencing, Peer-to-Peer Communication, and AI-Driven Mobility Insights','<p>The increasing reliance on mobile technologies has led to the need for reliable safety and location-tracking solutions, especially in the area of connectivity and emergency situations. The purpose of this study was to design, develop and evaluate Circulo, a mobile safety tracking app that is integrated with geofencing, path visualization, peer-to-peer communication, and AI-driven mobility insights to improve user safety, situational awareness and navigation confidence. The system was built under a modified Waterfall model, with a focus on ISO/IEC 25010 software quality characteristics such as functionality, reliability, security, maintainability and usability. The results showed that the application is able to provide real-time location tracking, geofence notifications, route guidance, emergency signaling and AI-generated summaries. The evaluation results show a Very Satisfactory rating for the system and were very good for user and expert opinion regarding the effectiveness, security and usability of the system. The study highlights the need to integrate safety-oriented technologies on a common mobile platform. The novelty of the study is to combine geofencing, peer-to-peer communication, path visualization and AI-driven analytics for mobile safety tracking to be resilient and intelligent, and user-centered.</p>','["Artificial intelligence analytics, geofencing, mobile tracking systems, peer-to-peer communication, user safety."]',NULL,20,1,'1687 - 1705','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1182','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',91),
(1210,1210,1,'A Study on The Challenges in The Adoption of Fintech Innovations in Financial Reporting: Stakeholders Prespective','<p>The focus of the study is to identify the perception of stakeholders regarding the challenges in the adoption of fintech innovations in financial reporting. Data has been collected from both primary and secondary sources. Primary data has been collected through a survey from different stakeholders with the help of a structured questionnaire. A sample of 200 stakeholders were selected as per convenient sampling method for this study. While selecting a respondent, due importance was given to work experience. The response of the respondents was rated by using five-point Likert scales. Proportional test and factor analysis has been used to analyses the data. To this study following hypothesis were constructed and put to test, such that H0, FinTech adoption have a significant impact on financial reporting practices. Since the calculated value, 9.62 is greater than critical value 1.645, we reject null hypothesis and the alternative hypothesis is accepted. The analysis reveals the fact that FinTech adoption have a significant impact in financial reporting practices, and the further analysis identified the challenges in the adoption of Fintech innovation and its impact on the quality of financial reporting. Based on the analysis and findings the study put forward some suggestions that can be<br/>adopted by organizations while choosing the right fintech innovations that really required for their organization and which are financially affordable, add value to their organizations, enhance accuracy, reliability, timeliness, and transparency of financial reporting, thereby strengthening stakeholder confidence.</p>','["Fintech", "Financial reporting", "accuracy", "reliability", "transparency", "timeliness", "financial reporting quality."]',NULL,20,1,'1706 - 1716','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1210','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',92),
(1211,1211,1,'Next-Generation Tomato Harvesting: An Intelligent System for Ripeness Determination and Yield Maximization','<p>Harvesting tomatoes is an important aspect of agricultural output, and contemporary farming practices place an increasing demand on precise and effective harvesting techniques. In this research, we present an intelligent system for tomato harvesting, combining machine learning and deep learning approaches for ripeness determination and production optimisation. The YOLOv5 and YOLOv8 object detection models are employed in our approach to identify tomatoes in images and classify them into five distinct maturity stages: tomato_half_ripe, tomato_overripe, tomato_ripe, tomato_rotten, and tomato_unripe. The system is trained and evaluated using a selected dataset provided from Roboflow Universe, containing annotated tomato pictures for model training and validation. Experimental results demonstrate the utility of our technique, with both YOLOv5 and YOLOv8 models getting outstanding accuracy in tomato recognition and classification tasks. Studies comparing the two systems'' performance reveal only minor differences, with YOLOv8 doing somewhat better when it comes to identifying tomatoes at more advanced stages of ripeness. Our work sheds light on the potential uses and future developments of deep learning-based precision farming techniques, helping to build intelligent agricultural systems for automated tomato harvesting. Future research may concentrate on integrating multisensor data and further optimising model architectures to increase the effectiveness and dependability of tomato harvesting systems.</p>','["Tomato leaf disease", "crop harvesting", "machine learning model", "deep learning model", "YOLO model", "ripeness selection."]',NULL,20,1,'1717 - 1728','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1211','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',93);
INSERT INTO publications (id,submission_id,version,title,abstract,keywords,references_text,issue_id,section_id,pages,doi,copyright_holder,copyright_year,license_url,status,date_published,seq) VALUES
(1244,1244,1,'Effectiveness and Safety of Giving Iron Supplement Tablets and Vitamin C on Hemoglobin Levels of Female Students at PP Putri Wahid Hasyim Bangil','<p>Female adolescents have a higher risk of anemia than male adolescents. The first reason is that menstruation occurs every month in female adolescents. Females who experience heavy menstrual bleeding for more than five days are likely to suffer from significant iron loss, necessitating a greater need for iron supplementation compared to those with menstrual durations of three days or less. The second reason is that female adolescents often maintain their appearance, the desire to stay slim or thin so they go on a diet and eat less. The purpose of this study was to analyze the effectiveness and safety of giving iron supplement tablets and vitamin C tablets on hemoglobin levels in the blood. This study was conducted at PP Putri Wahid Hasyim Bangil involving 100 respondents divided into 2 groups including group 1 (consuming iron supplements) and group 2 (consuming iron supplements + Vitamin C). The results of the study showed that hemoglobin levels in group 1 had an average increase from 10.6 g/dL to 11.4 g /dL, with the average difference of 0.8 g / dL. while hemoglobin levels in group 2 had an average increase from 10.5 g/dL to 11.8 g/dL, with the average difference was 1.3 g/dL. The Asymp. Sig. (2-tailed) value for Initial Hb is 0.491, which is greater than the probability value of 0.05, so it can be concluded that "Ha is rejected", while the Asymp. Sig. (2-tailed) value for Final Hb is 0.443, which is greater than the probability value of 0.05, so it can be concluded that “Ha is rejected". Thus, it can be said that in the group given iron tablets only and the group given iron tablets accompanied by vitamin C, the results of the examination between Initial Hb and Final Hb are said to be unrelated (p &gt; 0.05), so there is no significant difference in hemoglobin levels between the 2 groups. Therefore, there was no significant change in the average increase in hemoglobin levels between the two groups.</p>','["Vitamin C", "Anemia", "Female adolescents", "Side effect."]',NULL,20,1,'1729 - 1737','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1244','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',94),
(1245,1245,1,'Reliability-Aware Input–Output Domination in Complex and Neutrosophic Networks','<p>The present work discusses a reliability-aware variant of the prominent IO-dominating set problem through the formulation of a novel aspect termed the Reliable IO Dominating Set (RIO-dominating set) Problem. Theoretical analysis verifies the NP-completeness of the RIO-dominating set Problem, confirming its position as a tight extension of the IO-dominating set Problem. A greedy approach is drafted to advance a solution construct through the Reliability-Based IO-Dominating Set Problem and its evaluations on synthetic networks exemplifying the impact of vertex reliability on domination structures.</p>','["IO- Domination", "RIO- Domination", "Graph Theory", "Network Reliability", "Optimization."]',NULL,20,1,'1738 - 1746','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1245','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',95),
(1246,1246,1,'A New Nine-Dimensional Dynamical Chaotic System: Construction and Synchronized of Hyper-Chaotic System with Active Control with Circuit Realization','<p>Very often bedlam occurs in a nonlinear model; however, it is not always the case. This has led to extensive research into identifying which systems can display chaos under specific initial conditions or attributes. Due to their enormous dimensionality and straightforward nonlinear terms or parameter values, certain systems are particularly difficult. Thus, higher-dimensional chaotic systems'' more intricate chaotic characteristics have made their development a crucial field of study in chaos theory. This work describes and examines a 9-D hyper-chaotic model constructed with nonlinear equations. A 9-D were used to generate a hyper-chaotic model with fifteen positive coefficients. The proposed model''s The dependence on the initial state, which depends on the sensitivity of the proposed model and its Wave analysis, and Lyapunov exponents have chaotic behavior, fractal dimension and bifurcation are all examined using Mathematica simulation. A widely accepted definition of chaos states that a model, if has a positive Lyapunov exponent or satisfies in its domain, it is chaotic. The distance between the original conditions will also grow with time. It was recognized that the chaotic behavior would become exceedingly sensitive to even a small change in the starting values. More complex dynamical behaviors were demonstrated using a non-cyclical time-domain waveform with three positive Lyapunov exponents and Active Control Matching Synchronization in Hybrid Results for the new model(9-D), the hybrid synchronization is identical to the hyper-chaos and nature of the stated model is demonstrated by active control and circuit realization of hyper-chaotic model.</p>','["Nine-dimension hyper chaotic, Lyapunov exponent, Active Control Identical Synchronization in hybrid, strange attractor, bifurcation and circuit realization."]',NULL,20,1,'1747 - 1764','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1246','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',96),
(1247,1247,1,'Enhancing Trust in AI Predictions for Education: An Application of XAI with XGBoost','<p>Artificial intelligence is increasingly shaping many sectors, and education is no exception. The increasing deployment of machine learning (ML) models in education has introduced unprecedented opportunities for personalized learning, predictive analytics, and data-informed decision-making. Machine learning has started to influence the way we design and deliver education, offering tools that adapt to students’ needs and help institutions make decisions. Despite these opportunities, there are growing concerns about how such models actually make their predictions. However, the black-box nature of widely adopted models, such as XGBoost and deep learning, poses significant challenges to trust, transparency, and accountability. Black-box-type models do not provide explanations useful in high-stakes educational environments where placing or grading students on algorithmic outputs becomes a tangible reality. Educators often see the predictions but not the reasoning behind them, which makes it difficult to build trust or act confidently on the results. Explainable AI (XAI) offers a promising solution by enhancing the interpretability of AI-driven decisions, ensuring that educators can understand and trust model predictions. In this paper, we propose a framework that brings XGBoost together with SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) to make its inner workings more transparent. Our aim is not only to improve performance but also to show why certain outcomes appear and how they can be understood in practice. The framework is developed with teachers and decision-makers in mind, since accuracy alone is rarely enough in real classrooms. We suggest that this approach can open the door to more responsible and practical uses of AI in education, while also giving researchers a clearer direction for future studies. Finally, the findings will contribute to the development of transparent, ethical, and trustworthy AI systems in education, bridging the gap between advanced ML techniques and practical educational applications.</p>','["Explainable AI, ML, XGBoost, SHAP, Education, Interpretability."]',NULL,20,1,'1765 - 1779','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1247','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',97),
(1248,1248,1,'Hybrid Transformer–CNN–BiLSTM Model with Mahalanobis Embedding for Zero-Day Intrusion Detection','<p>Abstract: The increasing complexity of cyber threats and the limitations of signature-based intrusion detection systems have made the detection of zero-day attacks a critical challenge in network security. Although machine learning and deep learning methods have achieved high accuracy for known attacks, their ability to generalize to previously unseen attack families remain limited. This paper proposes a hybrid intrusion detection model that integrates a Feature Tokenization-based Transformer with a CNN–BiLSTM model to capture global feature interactions and local sequential dependencies in tabular network traffic data. The learned fusion embedding is further exploited using a Mahalanobis distance–based anomaly detector to identify zero-day intrusions in an open-set setting. Experiments were conducted on the UNSW-NB15 dataset using a strict held-out attack family protocol, where Analysis, Backdoor, and Worms were excluded from training and reserved for zero-day testing. The proposed model achieved 94.64% accuracy, 0.9610 F1-score, and 0.9902 AUC on the known test set, that indicate the strong zero-day detection capability with high family-wise true positive rates for Backdoor and Worms. Five-fold cross-validation, ablation study, and statistical significance analysis further confirmed the robustness and effectiveness of the proposed framework. The results indicate that the integration of transformer-based feature learning, sequential modeling, and covariance-aware embedding analysis provides a reliable and extensible solution for both known attack classification and zero-day intrusion detection.</p>','["Anomaly Detection, Cyber Security, Zero-day attack detection, Feature Tokenizer Transformer, Mahalanobis distance"]',NULL,20,1,'1780 - 1806','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1248','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',98),
(1249,1249,1,'IU-Net-DDPC: A Novel Integrated U-Net and Dynamic Density Peaks Clustering Framework for Real-Time Medical Image Stream Analysis','<p>The rapid expansion of Internet of Medical Things (IoMT) devices has transformed modern healthcare systems, generating continuous, unprecedented streams of unlabeled medical images in real time from multiple sources. Current medical image clustering methods suffer from important weaknesses in both computational efficiency and accuracy, and fail to adapt their decomposition parameters to the more challenging scenario of ever-changing feature distributions found in most clinical environments. Therefore, this paper proposes a novel framework termed “IU-NET-DDPC” that integrates a U-Net convolutional autoencoder for layered deep feature extraction and dynamic density peaks clustering (DDPC) to autonomously identify clusters without a predefined number of clusters. Instead of pairwise computation, it is efficient to compute for streaming batches using a KNN-based density estimation strategy. An adaptive split-and-merge approach, as well as delete procedures, in a dynamic cluster maintenance mechanism, are applied to changes caused by data distribution. Evaluation on a Cardiac Catheterization dataset at 200 training epochs and 0.2 dropout rate achieves 89.60% accuracy, NMI of 0.5182, ARI of 0.6268, Purity of 89.60%, F1-Score of 89.60%, and Jaccard index of 0.8115. IU-Net-DDPC outperformed various baseline methods across all metrics in comparative experiments. The practical applicability of the proposed framework confirms the suitability for real-time clinical IoMT deployment.</p>','["Internet of Medical Things, Medical Image Stream, U-Net Autoencoder, Dynamic Density Peaks Clustering, Unsupervised Learning, Cardiac Catheterization."]',NULL,20,1,'1807 - 1829','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1249','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',99),
(1250,1250,1,'Real-Time Spatio-Temporal Deepfake Detection for Live Biometric Authentication via EfficientNet-GRU','<p>Deepfake technology poses a critical threat to live video conferencing and biometric authentication. Existing detection models are either purely spatial—rendering them vulnerable to video compression—or rely on computationally heavy 3D-CNNs incompatible with strict real-time CPU latency constraints. We propose a highly optimized Two-Stream Spatio-Temporal architecture specifically designed for zero-latency live video evaluation. The framework extracts fine-grained spatial artifacts using a lightweight EfficientNet-B0 backbone, while a unidirectional Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU) models frame-to-frame physiological inconsistencies. Evaluated on a diverse 3,000-video corpus from FaceForensics++, Celeb-DF, and DFDC, the model achieved 98.21 cross-dataset accuracy and a 0.9978 AUC. Crucially, CPU inference requires only 125.63ms per 16-frame sequence—well below the 533ms threshold of a standard 30 fps camera—guaranteeing seamless, real-time overlay detection. Finally, the network''s decision boundaries are mathematically validated using Explainable AI (XAI) activation maps and t- SNE clustering.</p>','["Deepfake Detection, Spatio-Temporal Networks, Live Video Authentication, Explainable AI, EfficientNet, GRU."]',NULL,20,1,'1830 - 1845','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1250','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',100),
(781,781,1,'An Integrated Entropy–TOPSIS and Decision-Tree Framework for Operational Priority Setting in a University-Affiliated Hotel: Evidence from the PSU Golden Lion Hotel, Philippines','<p>The structure of a University-affiliated hotel, reflecting its dual commercial–pedagogical mandate, makes prioritising improvements to the hotel''s operational processes, based on discipline and scientific evidence, more complex and significant than in other types of hotels. The study builds on this work to create an integrated decision analytic framework to turn evidence from multiple stakeholder surveys into a defensible prioritization of operational improvements and applies this framework at the Pangasinian State University (PSU) Golden Lion Hotel in the Philippines. Eight dimensions of operation were rated by 400 respondents who were managers, staff/interns, and guests. The performance gap, derived importance (with respect to overall guest satisfaction) and the percentage of dissatisfied evaluations are used to fill a decision matrix, which is ranked objectively using Shannon entropy and classified objectively using TOPSIS, and complemented by the importance–performance analysis and a CART classification model. The dominant improvement priority (closeness coefficient 0.866) is service quality, and the CART model achieves a ten-fold cross-validated accuracy of 91.0% while using only about 98.0% of the predictive information for this priority, as compared to the modal baseline of 52.5%. The weakest performing dimensions are sustainability practices (weighted mean 1.89) and technology integration (2.17), and they are both ranked second and third, respectively, based on equal criterion weights. It provides a transparent framework for hotel decision makers with a replicable pipeline from stakeholder perceptions to an operational investment ordering, and it adds an application of objective-weight multi-criteria decision-making and machine learning in the relatively under-studied university-hotel field.</p>','["multi-criteria decision-making", "TOPSIS", "entropy weighting", "decision tree", "hotel op-erations management", "service quality."]',NULL,19,1,'01 - 19','10.59543/jidmis.v3.781','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',1),
(782,782,1,'An Ensemble Machine Learning Model for Mastitis Disease Identification in Cows','<p>The worldwide dairy sector faces a major problem from mastitis, a disease that affects dairy cows widely and has a substantial financial effect. Timely and precise diagnosis is essential for efficient treatment and to stop the impact to a larger extent. Machine learning proven to be one of the assisted technologies for the identification of mastitis in cows and other cattle in an effective and precise manner. In this paper we have presented an ensemble model for mastitis identification. First, we implement outlier elimination and data anomaly detection based on the Implement Quartile Range (IRQ). The Box-Cox transformation, which normalizes the data distribution and makes it more appropriate for algorithms that assume a normal distribution, is then used to resolve skewness in numerical characteristics. Next for feature extraction three techniques has been applied namely Principal Component Analysis (PCA), Linear Discriminant Analysis (LDA) and Recursive Feature Elimination (RFE). Also SMOTE has been used to balance the data later on. Then proposed ensemble model based on LR, DT, RF, GB, NB, Extra Tree and XGBoost has been tested on the data based on 5-fold and 3-fold cross -validation. Also, comparative evaluation has been done based on accuracy, precision, recall, F1 score, training and validation Loss for the proposed approach with respect to the existing methods.</p>','["Mastitis", "SVM", "RF", "KNN", "DT", "GB", "PCA", "ExtraTree", "XGBoost", "SDG"]',NULL,19,1,'20 - 42','10.59543/jidmis.v3.782','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',2),
(783,783,1,'Early Prediction of Neurological Disorders using Automatic Deep Feature Extraction and Machine Learning','<p>The focus of recent research has been on using advanced computer-aided diagnostic (CAD) techniques and a variety of modalities to identify neurological disorders. Important and possibly deadly conditions, neurological diseases such as Alzheimer''s disease (AD), stroke, epilepsy, Parkinson''s disease (PD), cerebral palsy, autism, and schizophrenia (SZ) often result in more serious health issues. CAD systems for neurological diseases using a variety of modalities have attracted a lot of interest due to different approaches of Artificial Intelligence (AI) like Deep Learning (DL) and Machine Learning (ML). However, the difficulty of automatically predicting many neurological disorders using neuroimaging data remains unresolved due to the lack of a comprehensive and highly accurate solution. A novel approach is presented in which an automated deep feature extraction along with machine learning classifiers were used for detection neurological diseases (for instance schizophrenia, Parkinson''s disease, and Alzheimer''s disease) from brain scans at early stages. In order to identify neurological disorders various methods use neuroimaging studies, mainly magnetic resonance imaging (MRI). Preprocessing MRI scans, data augmentation, deep feature extraction, and machine learning-based classification are all included in the suggested model. Image filtering and contrast augmentation techniques are applied to the acquired raw MRI images to improve their quality. Various methods are used to enhance the categorization accuracy of the current datasets. For automated deep feature extraction from input MRI pictures, we modified the Convolutional Neural Network''s (CNN) layers. Deep feature extraction lowers the dimensionality and computing needs of an updated CNN by extracting complex properties from its deeper levels. The effectiveness of the suggested model for classifying neurological disorders is assessed using sophisticated machine learning classifiers. The suggested method is better at detecting neurological issues, according to experimental research on neurological illnesses and imaging modalities.</p>','["Alzheimer\\u2019s Disease", "Artificial Intelligence", "Convolutional Neural Network", "Machine Learning, Neurological Disorders", "Neuroimaging"]',NULL,19,1,'43 - 69','10.59543/jidmis.v3.783','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',3),
(784,784,1,'Do Small Financial Thresholds and Merit-Based Incentives Increase Primary Students’ Perceived Value and Engagement? Evidence from a Nonprofit English Program','<p>In the context of mandatory English education in China’s primary schools, students often struggle with Foreign Language Classroom Anxiety and a lack of opportunities for oral practice. While numerous NGOs provide free resources to address this gap, many struggle with the “zero-cost effect”, where high initial interest is undermined by low behavioral engagement and high absenteeism. This study investigates how a nominal financial threshold, grounded in the sunk cost effect and perceived value theory, influences primary students’ behavioral engagement in a nonprofit educational intervention. Using a quasi-experimental mixed-methods design, the research compares two models: a Zero-Cost Model (HFI Onism Club, n=21) and an Incentivized Threshold Model (Speak&amp;Spark program, n=10) implemented in public primary schools in Kunming, China. Results showed that both models got high perceived value scores. However, the Incentivized Threshold Model (699 CNY tuition with merit-based scholarships) had much higher external accountability (6.40/7) than the Zero-Cost Model (2.48/7). Findings confirmed that the tuition fee converted the course into an “asset”, leveraging loss aversion to sustain attendance despite external distractions. The study finds that a hybrid model called “Net Positive Incentive” is a better and more long-lasting way to share resources from educational NGOs. This is because it links students’ long-term academic goals with short-term behavioral rewards.</p>','["Sunk Cost Effect", "Loss Aversion", "Behavioral Engagement", "Nonprofit education", "Incentive-based learning."]',NULL,19,1,'70 - 79','10.59543/jidmis.v3.784','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',4),
(785,785,1,'Morphology-Preserved Super-Resolution and Hierarchical Instance Segmentation Framework for Automated Day-5 Blastocyst Grading','<p>Automated embryo grading is a critical factor in enhancing the success of in vitro fertilization (IVF) since it reduces subjectivity in the manual morphological examination. The resolution and the quality of microscopic images of embryos are not high and accurate to detect the embryo features like Inner Cell Mass(ICM) and Trophectoderm (TE). This paper addresses the issues with a deep learning approach combining optimized super resolution architecture with Convolutional Neural Network architectures to automate the grading of embryos. First stage of the frame work is implemented to enhance the quality of the embryo images using Super Resolution Generative Adversarial Networks (SRGAN) that retains morphological details. A Multi-Model Mask R-CNN architecture is applied to upscaled embryo images to detect and classify embryo structures accurately. The geometric feature extraction technique is further used to isolate trophectoderm region and extracted embryo, ICM and TE features are pooled to be graded with convolutional neural networks. The suggested approach is contrasted with some of the models of super-resolution such as bicubic interpolation, EDSR, ESRGAN, and segmentation models like U-Net, Faster R-CNN, and YOLO-based detectors. It is experimentally shown that the SRGAN is optimized with 32 residual blocks and a ResNet-34 discriminator to exhibit better performance on low resolutions embryo images. The suggested framework achieves a 96.7% classification accuracy and 0.97 mIoU, which is better in comparison with the current automated embryo grading methods and offers a dependable decision-support system to embryologists during the IVF procedures</p>','["Generator, Discriminator, SRGAN, Mask R-CNN, VGG-19, Resnet-34, Image Stitching, IVF."]',NULL,19,1,'80 - 101','10.59543/jidmis.v3.785','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',5),
