(791,791,1,'A Multi-Channel Deep Feature Fusion Framework for Classification of Kannada Epigraphic Scripts',NULL,NULL,NULL,19,1,'179 - 192',NULL,'The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-15',10),
(792,792,1,'The Impact of TBC Application on the Work Performance of Community Nurses and Early Detection of Tuberculosis Recovery Stages','<p>The development of information technology (IT) in healthcare has not been fully adapted by community nurses. This study aimed to determine whether the use of an IT-based application correlates with the work performance of community nurses and the early detection of tuberculosis (TB) recovery stages. The application may help expedite the early detection of TB recovery and improve the performance of community nurses. A cross-sectional design was employed using an Android-based application called the TB-CDINI Application, which was accessible at https://tb-cdini.com/login. The independent variable (use of the TBC application) is hypothesized to have an effect on the two dependent variables (work performance and early detection). Inclusion criteria included community nurses with experience handling TB cases, while exclusion criteria were nurses without TB patient care experience. Data were collected randomly from community nurses who participated in a webinar on TB nursing (n = 248). The questionnaire gathered demographic, TB-related, and IT-related data. Data analysis included univariate and bivariate analyses conducted using SPSS software, with Pearson''s correlation test applied. The results indicated that most community nurses (83%) had experience treating TB patients. They reported that the disappearance of symptoms such as cough, dyspnea, fever, weakness, tiredness, night sweats, and weight loss could signify recovery (65.7%). Identified challenges included limited understanding of the app (10.5%), poor network coverage (3.2%), lack of training (2.4%), and varying perceptions of the app’s benefits (56.8%). The Pearson correlation test yielded a p-value of 0.018 (&lt;0.05), with a positive correlation coefficient of 0.721, indicating a strong and positive relationship between the use of the TB-CDINI application and the early detection of TB recovery stages. In conclusion, the use of the TB-CDINI application had a significant impact on the early detection of TB recovery stages. It can serve as a valuable tool for community nurses. Future research should prioritize practical applications with TB patients, covering usage protocols, data recording, reporting procedures, maintenance requirements and field-based evaluation.</p>','["Performance", "Community", "Nurses", "Recovery", "Stages"]',NULL,19,1,'193 - 203','10.59543/jidmis.v3.792','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',11),
(793,793,1,'A Dual Lens for the Digital Age: An Integrative Examination of Media Dependency Theory and Uses and Gratifications Theory','<p>The contemporary media landscape, defined by ubiquitous connectivity and algorithmic mediation, has reshaped the relationship between audiences and media, compelling a re-evaluation of foundational communication theories. This paper presents an integrative examination of two cornerstone paradigms—Media Dependency Theory (MDT), which emphasizes structural reliance on media systems, and Uses and Gratifications Theory (U&amp;G), which highlights active, goal-oriented users. Through a PRISMA-guided systematic review of 112 peer-reviewed studies published between 2015 and 2024, we argue that these paradigms are complementary rather than competing and introduce the Need–Gratification–Dependency (NGD) cycle as a unified framework. The model posits that users initially engage platforms to satisfy pre-existing needs (U&amp;G), but algorithmic personalization, variable reinforcement, and persuasive design foster psychological and behavioral dependency (MDT); dependency in turn reshapes needs, generating a self-perpetuating feedback loop. We trace the evolution of the active audience toward guided activeness—agency exercised within algorithmically structured environments—and identify the platform features (e.g., infinite scroll, variable-reward notifications, quantified validation) and psychological mechanisms (habit formation, identity integration, emotional regulation, dopaminergic reward processing) that sustain the cycle. The framework carries substantive implications for digital literacy, platform governance, and user well-being, supporting a paradigm shift toward integrated theoretical accounts of contemporary media effects.</p>','["media dependency theory", "uses and gratifications theory", "digital media", "algorithmic influence", "platform studies", "Need\\u2013Gratification\\u2013Dependency cycle", "guided activeness", "platform governance", "digital well-being", "and persuasive design/"]',NULL,19,1,'204 - 226','10.59543/jidmis.v3.793','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',12),
(794,794,1,'SPPA-MVC: Structure-Preserving Prototype Alignment with Optimal Transport for High-Dimensional Incomplete Multi-View Clustering','<p>While incomplete multi-view clustering has the objective of utilizing complementary information from multiple views with the presence of missing data, it is still hard to achieve because of the presence of view inconsistency and structural distortion. Current approaches are mainly aimed at instance-level alignment, which can be problematic when there are many missing instances or when there is dimensional collapse. In this paper, we propose a novel framework called SPPA-MVC. The main one of our contributions is an Optimal Transport-based mechanism able to solve the Prototype-Shifted Problem (PSP) through global structural calibration. PSP is a problem that occurs when samples are not present, thereby causing view-specific prototypes to be biased, which causes incorrect feature fusion. SPPA-MVC solves the problem of alignment as an Optimal Transport problem, ensuring permutation-invariant alignment by aligning semantic anchors across views in a correct manner, irrespective of their order in coordinates. Extensive experiments on benchmark datasets show that SPPA-MVC performs better than all of the other algorithms, especially when data is missing, such as the ALOI-100 dataset with its high dimensionality. The results validate that the prototype calibration using OT is essential to obtain a reliable consensus learning in complex, high-dimensional, incomplete multi-view scenario.</p>','["Cross-view Structure Preservation", "Deep Clustering", "Incomplete Multi-view Clustering", "Optimal Transport", "Prototype Alignment."]',NULL,19,1,'227 - 245','10.59543/jidmis.v3.794','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',13),
(795,795,1,'Digital Leadership in Industry 4.0 and 5.0','<p>The move from 4.0 to 5.0 is more evolutionary than revolutionary but it represents a transition away from pure automation efficiency (4.0) toward more human-centered, sustainable and resilient systems that combines advanced technology such as AI/Internet of Things with human creativity, collaboration (cobots ) &amp; mass customization to satisfy societal needs. Therefore, such a transition necessitates new leadership which can best function in the changed environment. Informed by an integrative review approach, this paper critically investigates the change of leadership paradigms and practice and how technology plays a key role in shaping new leadership practices. It can be concluded from the findings of this study that leadership in Industry 5.0 is multidimensional and multi-dimensional. It’s no longer just simply a matter of merging known leadership styles, as was arguably the case with Industry 4.0; instead it is a new synthesis-based on symbiotic leadership –flowing from big data (incorporating AI). With these conditions, leadership becomes neurodigital. From the concept of simbiotic leadership, a neurodigital triad methodological about digital strategic setting goals and planning in leadreship digital on Industrial 5.0 is proposed. This study is aligned with the development of a methodology and applied instruments to form new socio-economic, cybersocial, and neurodigital ecosystems.</p>','["digitalization", "industry 4.0", "industry 5.0", "digital transformation", "digital leadership"]',NULL,19,1,'246 - 265','10.59543/jidmis.v3.795','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',14),
(796,796,1,'Governing Digital Identity Decisions in KYC/AML Banking: A Framework for Provenance, Risk Intelligence and Supervisory Visibility','<p>Digital KYC and AML systems increasingly rely on data-driven onboarding, automated document verification, sanctions screening, risk scoring, and transaction-monitoring analytics. Yet identity-related failures continue to occur because the decisions underlying digital identity verification are often fragmented, weakly traceable, and poorly governed across systems, institutions, and supervisory boundaries. This article develops a conceptual and practice-oriented governance framework for intelligent KYC/AML decision-making by conceptualizing digital identity as shared decision infrastructure rather than a static compliance record. Supported by an anonymized implementation illustration from a regulated banking context, the article integrates insights from digital identity, blockchain governance, RegTech, and enterprise risk governance to explain how identity-related judgments can be recorded, reused, scrutinized, and supervised over time. The framework identifies four capabilities required for more intelligent and accountable KYC/AML decision-making: decision provenance, controlled reuse of verified identity decisions, identity-layer anomaly detection, and role-based supervisory visibility. These capabilities reposition digital identity governance from a fragmented compliance workflow toward a continuous decision-assurance capability. The article contributes to intelligent decision-making and information systems research by showing how provenance-enabled digital identity infrastructure can improve explainability, reduce duplicated verification, bring risk intelligence closer to the point of identity formation, and support supervisory confidence without requiring unrestricted access to sensitive customer data.</p>','["Digital identity", "KYC", "AML", "decision provenance", "risk intelligence", "supervisory visibility."]',NULL,19,1,'266 - 287','10.59543/jidmis.v3.796','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',15),
(797,797,1,'Measurement Efficiency of the Top 25 Universities in Turkey with DEA','<p>This study examines the overall technical, pure technical, and scale efficiency levels of the top 25 research-oriented Turkish universities listed in the 2018–2019 URAP ranking by applying Data Envelopment Analysis (DEA). Relative efficiency scores were obtained using both input- and output-oriented specifications of the CCR and BCC models. The CCR results indicate that only three universities—Middle East Technical University, Bilkent University, and Bezm-i Âlem Vakıf University—operate on the overall efficiency frontier, while the remaining 22 universities exhibit varying degrees of inefficiency. Among the inefficient universities, Ege University records the lowest CCR efficiency score, ranking 25th with a value of 0.6545, whereas Istanbul Technical University achieves the highest score among the inefficient group, ranking fourth with a value of 0.9808. The BCC results reveal a broader set of technically efficient institutions. Under both input- and output-oriented BCC models, Hacettepe University, Middle East Technical University, Istanbul Technical University, Bilkent University, Bezm-i Âlem Vakıf University, and Yıldız Technical University consistently emerge as technically efficient. In addition, Marmara University is found to be technically efficient under the input-oriented BCC model, although it remains marginally below the efficiency frontier under the output-oriented specification. The scale efficiency results show that only three universities achieve full scale efficiency under both orientations, while the remaining institutions experience varying degrees of scale inefficiency. Furthermore, seven universities operate under constant returns to scale, eleven under increasing returns to scale, and seven under decreasing returns to scale. The potential improvement analysis shows that inefficient universities require different adjustment paths depending on model orientation. In particular, Ege University should reduce its PHD and ÖÜY/SAY input indicators by approximately 34.54% under the input-oriented model, while increasing its research-related output indicators under the output-oriented model. Overall, the findings suggest that inefficiency among Turkish research-oriented universities is driven by both technical and scale-related factors, highlighting the need for institution-specific strategies aimed at improving resource allocation, research productivity, and scale optimization.</p>','["Efficiency, Data Envelopment Analysis, Universities of Turkey"]',NULL,19,1,'288 - 307','10.59543/jidmis.v3.797','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',16),
(798,798,1,'Competence in Intercultural Communication, Enhanced by AI-based Language Learning Systems and Media Platforms','<p>The rapid digitalisation of education and the growing use of artificial intelligence (AI) have significantly changed the development of English language proficiency and intercultural communicative competence. AI-supported platforms, media technologies, and multimodal learning environments provide new opportunities for personalised language learning and authentic intercultural interaction, requiring a reassessment of their educational impact. This study examines the relationship between the digital preparedness of education systems and the development of English language proficiency and intercultural communicative competence in AI-enhanced learning environments. A comparative analysis was conducted using international datasets from OECD (PISA 2018), UNESCO Institute of Statistics, the World Bank (EdStats and EdTech Readiness Index), and the European Commission Education and Training Monitor. Twelve national education systems with different levels of digital development were analysed using comparative and cross-country methods. The findings demonstrate a positive relationship between digital readiness, English reading literacy, and intercultural competence. Education systems with stronger digital infrastructure achieve better language and intercultural outcomes. AI-supported learning, combined with media technologies, promotes learner autonomy, reduces language anxiety, and supports authentic intercultural communication. Effective AI integration in English language education requires a balanced combination of pedagogical, technological, and communicative approaches, together with attention to ethical issues such as algorithmic bias. The results provide evidence for developing digital learning ecosystems and strategies that strengthen English language proficiency and intercultural competence in blended and online learning environments.</p>','["English language learning", "artificial intelligence", "English proficiency", "English as a global lingua franca", "general language competence", "media platforms, intercultural communication, language learning systems, digital readiness, global competencies, adaptive learning, simulation environments, language education"]',NULL,19,1,'308 - 325','10.59543/jidmis.v3.798','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',17),
(800,800,1,'ASCIOT: An Attribute-Based Smart Contract Framework for Cross-Organizational Access Control in Precision Agriculture IoT','<p>The adoption of IoT in agriculture has moved fast, probably too fast for security to keep pace. In open, distributed farming environments, we are suddenly asking soil sensors, weather stations, and actuators to trust each other, frequently across organizational boundaries, with no central authority to validate everything. That gap is where we started. This paper presents ASCIOT, a framework we built to let IoT devices and the laboratories managing them establish trust dynamically, using Hyperledger Fabric and smart contracts instead of any single point of control. The work grew out of a concrete problem: two research labs at ESTF (The Higher School of Technology) in Fez, Morocco, each running its own smart farming setup, needed to share sensor access without either lab losing authority. Our solution was to put authentication and audit logging on-chain, where tampering is practically impossible, and push heavier computation and bulk storage off-chain where it wouldn''t block the network. We implemented attribute-based access control (ABAC) with four certificate bound attributes: role, department, zone, and building, which turned out to be granular enough for real world politics but not so complex that deployment became unmanageable. The test suite of the proposed framework covers 46 cases across registration, sensor updates, authentication, and access control, all successfully passed.</p>','["Blockchain", "smart Farming", "IoT", "Hyperledger", "ABAC", "Trust management"]',NULL,19,1,'326 - 349','10.59543/jidmis.v3.800','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',18);
