(847,847,1,'Engineering Real-Time Observability and Kernel-Level Security in Microservices via eBPF-Driven Analytics','<p>Cloud-native systems have emerged as a design paradigm based on distributed microservices architectures, which are inherently complex, and run-time observability and performance assurance have become a major challenge. Traditional observability techniques are mostly based on user space instrumentation, which constrains the observability into system level behavior and makes it difficult to diagnose performance degradation based on resource contention and kernel-mediated interactions. This research explored the possibility of implementing the concept of kernel-level observability on microservices-based environments with an extended version of the Berkeley Packet Filter (eBPF) technology. Architecture empirical testing was performed on a publicly available performance data of microservices, and analyzed in a reproducible Google Colab environment. The evaluated overheads of system performance and the fidelity of observability as well as the ability to detect deviations were measured at scale with greater numbers of microservices. It was demonstrated that the overheads incurred by kernel-level observability were modest and scalable and that signal granularity and observability fidelity were significantly improved. The quality of detection and shorter latency of performance deviation were seen to be enhanced over user-space observability alone and especially as system scale was taken into account. Such results show that kernel-level instrumentation may give actionable system-level information that is challenging to gain using application-level monitoring. The paper provides a systems and software engineering vision of observability to show how observability mechanisms at the kernel level can be systematically engineered into distributed microservices systems. The findings confirm the argument that observability is a first-class architectural issue and it has a complementary role of the kernel-level and user-space telemetry in the creation of reliable and diagnosable distributed systems.</p>','["Kernel-level observability", "Microservices systems", "Software systems engineering", "Performance profiling", "Run-time observability", "eBPF instrumentation", "Distributed systems monitoring"]',NULL,19,1,'801 - 820','10.59543/jidmis.v3.847','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',44),
(848,848,1,'AI-Powered Adaptive Network Defense Through Feature Optimization and Ensemble Cyber-Threat Recognition','<p>The increasing complexity and variability of cyberattacks require network defense systems that can identify heterogeneous attack behaviours while remaining computationally efficient and explainable to security analysts. This study proposes an Adaptive Feature-Optimized Stacked Ensemble Network Defense Framework (AFO-SE-NDF) for multi-class cyber-threat recognition using the CSE-CIC-IDS2018 benchmark. The framework integrates leakage-aware preprocessing, class-imbalance treatment, hybrid consensus feature optimization, ensemble decision fusion, and explainable decision support. Raw CSE-CIC-IDS2018 traffic was processed from ten CSV files containing 16,233,002 records and sampled into a controlled research dataset. After repeated header removal, label normalization, leakage-column exclusion, missing and infinite-value treatment, duplicate removal, and constant-feature filtering, the final model-ready dataset contained 860,359 records, 69 numerical features, and seven traffic classes. The proposed Hybrid Consensus Feature Optimization Module (HCFOM) combined variance filtering, correlation redundancy analysis, mutual-information ranking, recursive feature elimination, tree-based importance, and SHAP-stability consensus. HCFOM reduced the feature space from 69 to 25 features, achieving a 63.77% feature-reduction ratio. Logistic Regression, Decision Tree, Random Forest, XGBoost, and LightGBM were evaluated under full-feature and optimized-feature conditions, followed by a stacked ensemble using Random Forest, XGBoost, and LightGBM base learners with a logistic meta-learner. The final optimized stacked ensemble achieved 92.00% test accuracy, 0.9219 macro-F1, 0.9920 macro-ROC-AUC, and 0.9041 Matthews correlation coefficient. The results demonstrate that feature-optimized ensemble learning can convert high-dimensional traffic data into actionable and interpretable cyber-threat decisions..</p>','["Adaptive network defense", "cyber-threat recognition", "intrusion detection", "feature optimization", "ensemble learning", "explainable artificial intelligence", "intelligent decision support", "CSE-CIC-IDS2018)."]',NULL,19,1,'821 - 843','10.59543/jidmis.v3.848','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',45),
(849,849,1,'Rethinking Technology Acceptance in Automation Contexts: Evidence from Robotic Process Automation Adoption in Vietnamese Higher Education Institutions','<p>Robotic Process Automation (RPA) has emerged as a key enabler of digital transformation, particularly in environments characterized by repetitive and rule-based administrative processes. Despite its increasing adoption, limited research has examined the behavioral mechanisms underlying RPA adoption in higher education institutions (HEIs), where organizational dynamics and user perceptions differ from corporate contexts. Addressing this gap, this study examines technology acceptance in automation contexts by extending the Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (UTAUT) with the inclusion of automation anxiety as an inhibiting factor. Drawing on survey data from 200 academic and administrative staff in Vietnamese public HEIs, the study employs Partial Least Squares Structural Equation Modeling (PLS-SEM) to test the proposed model. The results indicate that performance expectancy is the most influential determinant of behavioral intention (β = 0.683, p &lt; 0.001), followed by facilitating conditions (β = 0.240, p &lt; 0.001), while automation anxiety has a significant negative effect (β = -0.388, p &lt; 0.001). In contrast, effort expectancy and social influence do not exhibit statistically significant effects. The model demonstrates strong explanatory power (R² = 0.772) and high predictive relevance (Q² = 0.762). These findings suggest that technology acceptance in automation contexts is primarily driven by performance-related considerations, while perceived risks related to automation act as a substantial barrier. The study contributes to the literature by reconceptualizing UTAUT in the context of automation technologies and by integrating both enabling and inhibiting mechanisms into a unified framework. From a practical perspective, the results highlight the importance of emphasizing performance benefits while addressing user concerns to facilitate effective RPA adoption in HEIs..</p>','["Robotic Process Automation", "Technology Acceptance", "UTAUT", "Automation Anxiety", "Higher Education Institutions"]',NULL,19,1,'844 - 862','10.59543/jidmis.v3.849','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',46),
(850,850,1,'Mapping the Landscape of Robotic Process Automation in Education: A Systematic Literature Review','<p>Robotic Process Automation (RPA) has increasingly been adopted to improve operational efficiency across multiple industries, including the education sector. However, despite the growing number of studies on RPA, existing research in educational contexts remains fragmented, particularly regarding its practical applications, organizational implications, and integration with emerging technologies. To address this gap, this study combines bibliometric analysis with a systematic literature review to provide a comprehensive overview of the evolving research landscape of RPA in education. Using a PRISMA-guided review process, 148 records were initially retrieved from the Scopus database. After screening, 78 publications were retained for bibliometric analysis, and 33 studies were selected for in-depth qualitative synthesis. The bibliometric analysis indicates a rapidly expanding research field characterized by increasing attention to intelligent automation and digital transformation within higher education systems. The systematic review complements these findings by synthesizing evidence on three interrelated aspects: the practical applications and benefits of RPA in educational environments, the organizational transformations required to support automation adoption, and the integration of RPA with emerging technologies. Overall, the literature suggests that RPA can substantially enhance administrative efficiency, reduce routine workloads, and enable data-driven decision-making in educational institutions. Nevertheless, the successful implementation of RPA depends on the ability of institutions to address key socio-technical challenges, including governance mechanisms, organizational readiness, and the development of relevant digital skills among staff. This study contributes a comprehensive synthesis of the current research landscape and identifies future research directions toward intelligent and learner-centered automation in higher education.</p>','["Robotic Process Automation (RPA)", "Education", "Systematic Literature Review (SLR)", "Bibliometric Analysis", "PRISMA", "Intelligent Process Automation."]',NULL,19,1,'863 - 892','10.59543/jidmis.v3.850','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',47),
(851,851,1,'Integrating Data Analytics and Cognitive Computing for Real-Time Anomaly Detection in 6G IoT Environments: Insights from Environmental Artists on Leveraging Advanced Algorithms in Art and Design','<p>The study has examined the intersection of data analytics and cognitive computing to identify anomalies in real-time in 6G-enabled IoT systems, in a distinct interdisciplinary approach through environmental art and design. The paper explored how 6G networks can be used to detect anomalies faster in dynamic environments, including smart-city systems, climate monitoring, and sustainable installations, by providing edge-intelligent IoT architectures coupled with high frequencies. The framework is improved through the incorporation of machine learning, deep neural networks, and adaptive analytics, which increase accuracy, latency, and decision. Contextual understanding is assisted by cognitive computing and enables systems to learn irregular patterns by using self-feedback loops. This study also includes the comments of environmental artists who apply algorithmic modeling and sensor-guided data to create adaptive interactive arts. Their innovative methods show that aesthetic visualization and algorithmic interpretation may guide real-time system responsiveness and anomaly mapping. It is a cross-disciplinary approach that connects technical efficiency to human-centered design thinking to allow the use of sustainable, visually communicative, and interpretative models to visualize anomalies. The results emphasize the use of cognitive data systems to transform intricate inputs in the environment to artistic outputs that are more understandable and interpretable. The paper found that a combination of artistic cognition and sophisticated computational intelligence would greatly enhance the interpretive richness, transparency and design-driven relevance of anomaly detection in future 6G IoT networks.</p>','["6G IoT, data analytics, cognitive computing, anomaly detection, environmental art, deep learning, edge intelligence, adaptive algorithms, sustainable design, human-computer interaction."]',NULL,19,1,'893 - 907','10.59543/jidmis.v3.851','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',48),
(852,852,1,'Computational Fluid Dynamics Approaches for Aero-Propulsive and Thermal Analysis of Distributed Electric Propulsion Aircraft','<p>Distributed electric propulsion (DEP) is one of the emerging fields of research for the development of next-generation electric aircraft. The current paper is a narrative review and synthesis of published literature related to using computational fluid dynamics (CFD) methods for aero-propulsive and thermal analysis of DEP aircraft systems. Propulsor slipstream effects, rotor-wake interactions, aero-propulsor coupling mechanisms, multi-physics modeling challenges, and digital thread perspectives are explored in the review. The literature shows that the locally accelerated airflow over wing surfaces generated by distributed propulsors can increase the production of lift, especially in low-velocity flight conditions. Interactions between the wakes of neighboring propellers are also found to generate complicated unsteady wake flow characteristics which can only be simulated accurately by high fidelity methods. From the literature study, it is concluded that CFD is a key tool that delivers physics-based boundary conditions for thermal, structural, and energy system analysis. The review highlights the significant issues in rotor-wake prediction, heat management, and multi-disciplinary integration, as well as research activities that are currently underway, such as AI-supported CFD, reduced-order modelling, digital twin frameworks, and multi-disciplinary design optimization. The synthesis offers a structured basis for integrated DEP aircraft design researchers and engineers.</p>','["Distributed Electric Propulsion", "Computational Fluid Dynamics", "Aero-Propulsive Coupling", "Digital Thread", "Multiphysics Modeling", "Electric Aircraft Design"]',NULL,19,1,'908 - 927','10.59543/jidmis.v3.852','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',49),
(853,853,1,'A Multi-Layer AI Governance Framework for Enhancing Fraud Detection and Integrity Assurance in Large-Scale Organisational Systems','<p>Large organizations progressively adopt artificial intelligence (AI) to enhance fraud detection and ensure integrity across financial, operational, and digital systems. Despite its benefits, AI introduces challenges including data integrity risks, bias, clarity issues, and accountability gaps. Weak governance can lead to false alerts, model drift, and system vulnerabilities. This research proposes a multi-layer AI governance framework integrating technical, organizational, and controlling oversight to strengthen fraud detection and integrity assurance. The technical layer implements anomaly detection, machine learning, and explainable AI (XAI) models. The organizational layer sets up policies, accountability structures, and increase protocols, while the regulatory layer ensures compliance with GDPR, ISO standards, and AI-specific regulations. The framework adherence interoperability across layers, real-time monitoring, and adaptive responses to progressing fraud patterns. By combining ethical, procedural, and technical safeguards, the study offers a practical, scalable model that improves discovery accuracy, reduces false positives, and enhances organizational resilience and trust in AI systems. This study presents a multi-layer AI management framework that enhances fraud detection and uprightness assurance in large organizations. The framework combines technical, organizational, and regulatory layers to strengthen anomaly identification, audit completeness, and governance maturity. Adaptive thresholds and advanced AI techniques allow real-time detection with decreased alert fatigue, while blockchain and confederated learning ensure robust data integrity. Despite working challenges such as data imbalance, system latency, and explainability concerns, the framework demonstrates scalable and sustainable performance. The study offers actionable insights for policy formulation, stakeholder training, and ethical AI adoption, supporting resilient, accountable, and responsible organizational systems.</p>','["AI governance", "fraud detection", "integrity assurance", "multi-layer framework", "anomaly detection", "compliance", "accountability", "organizational systems", "explainable AI", "risk management."]',NULL,19,1,'928 - 950','10.59543/jidmis.v3.853','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',50),
(855,855,1,'Azure-Native AI/ML-Driven Orchestrated Workflows for Cloud-First Enterprise Integration in Financial and Healthcare Systems','<p>The fast digitalization of the new business has already enhanced the attribution of scalable, secure and smart integration designs, especially in the very regulated sectors of finance and healthcare. Such cloud-first approaches, coupled with recent developments in artificial intelligence (AI) and machine learning (ML) have helped organizations to modernize old systems, become operationally agile, and make evidence-based decisions. The paper will introduce an Azulike AI/ML-based orchestrated workflow infrastructure to assist in a smooth integration of the enterprise in a heterogeneous system in financial and healthcare settings. The suggested framework uses fundamental Azure services, such as Azure Logic Apps, Azure Data Factory, Azure Machine Learning, Azure functions and Azure Kubernetes service (AKS) to facilitate intelligent automation, real-time analytics, predictive decision support, and policy-driven workflow management. The paper discusses the architectural design, workflow coordination, AI/ML integration approach, and compliance and security aspects of the work in the context of regulated enterprises in a systematic manner. Performance, scalability, resilience, and performance compliance preparedness Domain-specific financial and healthcare systems use cases are used to test the systems. The results show that the Azure-native AI/ML-driven orchestrated workflows can greatly improve interoperability, scalability, fault tolerance, and regulatory compliance and lower integration complexities and operational costs at the same time. In general, the research study shows how intelligent and cloud-first orchestration frameworks can revolutionize enterprise integration in data-intensive and regulation sensitive industries.</p>','["Azure Cloud, AI/ML", "Workflow Orchestration", "Enterprise Integration", "Financial Systems", "Healthcare Systems", "Cloud-First Architecture"]',NULL,19,1,'951 - 965','10.59543/jidmis.v3.855','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',51),
(856,856,1,'Edge AI Frameworks for Real-Time Personalised Marketing Communication in 6G Networks','<p>The recent development of Artificial Intelligence (AI) has brought about dramatic changes to digital marketing, allowing personalised and real-time communication with clients. Nevertheless, with the further sophistication of AI systems, their efficiency depends on the possibility of providing personalised marketing messages in real time. The 6G networks and the Edge AI integration are an innovative solution to this issue that provides the possibility of ultra-low latency and high bandwidth to provide real-time and personalised customer experiences. The paper suggests a new Edge AI architecture that can support customised marketing communication in 6G contexts. With machine learning models, e.g., Gradient Boosting Machine (GBM), Random Forest (RF), and Support Vector Machine (SVM), the framework predicts customer engagement and loyalty, providing customised content on the basis of real-time customer data. Key metrics used to assess the performance of the proposed models were accuracy, AUC-ROC, precision, recall, and F1-score. Results show that GBM outperforms both RF and SVM in all evaluated metrics. Moreover, the low-latency features of 6G can also improve the responsiveness in real-time, which will raise brand loyalty, customer engagement, and satisfaction. The Structural Equation Modelling (SEM) demonstrated that AI communication systems have a significant influence on engagement, which, in its turn, leads to brand loyalty. This study explains why the integration of Edge AI and 6G networks can be used to streamline personalised marketing tactics and provide businesses with an edge in terms of providing customers with timely and relevant information.</p>','["Edge AI", "6G Networks", "Real-Time Marketing", "Personalised Communication", "Customer Engagement", "Brand Loyalty", "AUC-ROC", "Structural Equation Modelling (SEM)", "Gradient Boosting Machine (GBM)", "Machine Learning in Marketing"]',NULL,19,1,'966 - 984','10.59543/jidmis.v3.856','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',52),
(857,857,1,'Performance Benchmarking and Stability Analysis of Rust-Based Kernel Modules in Production Linux Environments','<p>This study discusses a comparative analysis of Rust and C implementations of kernel modules based on controlled comparisons of the two languages in production-like Linux settings. It is a combination of fine-grained micro-benchmark, sustained macro-benchmark and systematic stress and fault-injection testing between a variety of kernel configurations. The results of performance indicate that Rust modules’ performance is similar to C in sustained workloads, with differences in throughput generally in a low single-digit percentage range, resulting in a small and predictable overhead in latency in short call-path-intensive loads. The analysis of stability shows that there is a more pronounced distinction; Rust-based modules not only remove failures that are motivated by memory safety in the C baselines, but also have better failure containment and stress-diagnostic characteristics. In addition to run-time measures, the research proposes an evidence layer that maintains the focus of maintainers on technical performance, relating it to the open-source process issues of reviewability, defect patterns, and sustainability. The results have shown that Rust does not always perform better than C, but rather it redefines the reliability, performance, and maintainability trade space in a way that can be perceived and done by the kernel maintainers.</p>','["Rust-for-Linux", "Linux kernel", "open-source", "performance benchmarking", "stability analysis", "memory safety."]',NULL,19,1,'985 - 1005','10.59543/jidmis.v3.857','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',53),
(858,858,1,'Robust User Authentication Using CNN-Based Face, Fingerprint, and Iris Biometrics','<p>User authentication plays an essential role in security assurance in the present-day digital world. Traditional password-based authentication approaches are increasingly becoming vulnerable to attacks, while single modal biometric systems suffer from challenges such as noise susceptibility, deception, and intra-class variations. This paper proposes a secure and reliable multimodal biometric system using fingerprints, facial characteristics, and irises using Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) to address these weaknesses. In this system, self-learning CNN based representations are utilized for every biometric characteristic, allowing for automatic extraction of distinct deep representations without hand-crafted features. The obtained confidence or attributes across different biometrics are merged to create a combined representation for authentication. Confidence-based classification approach is utilized for authenticating the genuine and impersonator user. Efficiency of the proposed system has been tested using unique assessment criteria including accuracy, recall, true negative rate, false positive rate, precision, and false negative rates. Experimental outcomes demonstrate that the presented CNN-based multimodal biometric system achieves superior accuracy and robustness compared to the conventional single-modal systems and thus it can be used for practical security applications where reliability is required.</p>','["Biometric Verification", "Convolutional Neural Network", "Multimodal Modalities", "Facial Recognition", "Fingerprint Recognition", "Iris Recognition."]',NULL,19,1,'1006 - 1018','10.59543/jidmis.v3.858','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',54),
(911,911,1,'Cyber-Physical Attack Detection in Water Distribution Systems Using a Hybrid Ensemble of XGBoost, Isolation Forest, and LSTM Autoencoder on the BATADAL Dataset','<p>Water distribution systems (WDSs) are critical public infrastructures increasingly controlled through cyber-physical layers, making them attractive targets for malicious intrusions. Real-time detection is difficult: confirmed attack data is scarce, sensor readings co-vary across dozens of channels, and well-crafted intrusions are deliberately kept within plausible operating ranges. This paper proposes a three-model hybrid ensemble that combines a supervised XGBoost classifier, an unsupervised Isolation Forest with principal component analysis (PCA) dimensionality reduction, and an unsupervised LSTM Autoencoder trained on 24-hour sliding windows of sensor sequences. All models are evaluated on the BATtle of the Attack Detection Algorithms (BATADAL) training dataset, which contains 4,177 hourly observations from a simulated SCADA-monitored C-Town network spanning July–December 2016, with 219 confirmed attack hours (5.2%) across five distinct attack campaigns. The weighted ensemble (XGBoost 40%, LSTM Autoencoder 50%, Isolation Forest 10%) achieves an F1-score of 0.9336, ROC-AUC of 0.9967, and an average precision of 0.9390, detecting 211 of 219 attack hours with only 22 false positives and a false positive rate of 0.56%. Time-to-detection analysis across all five attack windows confirms that the ensemble provides rapid and reliable detection, identifying four of five attack campaigns within one hour of onset. Results demonstrate that combining complementary detection paradigms substantially outperforms any single-model approach on this benchmark.</p>','["cyber-physical attack detection, water distribution systems, SCADA security, XGBoost, isolation forest, LSTM autoencoder, ensemble learning, BATADAL, anomaly detection, ICS security"]',NULL,19,1,'1019 - 1037','10.59543/jidmis.v3.911','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',55),
(912,912,1,'Formal Verification Frameworks for Cloud-Native Microservices','<p>Microservices that are cloud-native allow distributed systems to scale, but present problems in assuring correctness, reliability, and fault resilience because of dynamic scaling and the complexity of service interactions. The presented paper suggests a formal verification framework that consists of a combination of specifications of temporal logic, model checking and modeling of service interactions graphs to study the behavior of the system and confirm the correctness properties. Service states, dependencies and scaling dynamics are modeled in a mathematical system. The framework identifies deadlocks, provides liveness and safety, and a framework of fault propagation between services. Experimental analysis shows that the accuracy of deadlock detection is increased to 96.7% and fault detection to 95.4% with 94.6% verification coverage and time of 96 ms to detect faults is minimized as compared to the conventional testing and monitoring methods in dynamic microservice settings.</p>','["Cloud-Native Microservices, Formal Verification, Model Checking, Temporal Logic, Distributed Systems Reliability, Service Interaction Graphs"]',NULL,19,1,'1038 - 1046','10.59543/jidmis.v3.912','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',56),
(913,913,1,'Error-Bounded URL Manipulation Detection via Symbolic Analysis','<p>Malicious web sites hosted via manipulated URL strings present users with phishing, malware and defacement. This research presents a framework for a symbolic analysis which transforms URL strings into lexical features, and uses an explicit error bound. Model is trained on a Kaggle dataset of 651191 URLs (4,28,103 benign, 96,457 defacement, 94,111 phishing and 32,520 malware). After tokenization, feature extraction and then a logistic-like function which gives a probability score, calibration applies a constraint on the amount of error. In comparative tests, the proposed model results in an accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score and AUC of 98.64%, 98.12%, 97.89%, 98.00% and 0.99, which is about 1-3 % better than that of logistic regression, random forest, XGBoost and CNN classifiers. These results show that the symbolic framework discriminates manipulated and legitimate URLs well and gives a statistically significant guarantee on its predictions.</p>','["symbolic analysis, malicious URL detection, error bound, machine learning, classification, cybersecurity"]',NULL,19,1,'1047 - 1053','10.59543/jidmis.v3.913','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',57),
(914,914,1,'Optimization-Driven Design of: Lightweight Block Cipher Design','<p>Lightweight block ciphers are needed in the resource-constrained world, e.g., IoT, embedded devices, and edge sensors, in which traditional cryptographic algorithms create too much computational load. The paper is touching the issue of high-power usage, and the delay of the safe embedded communication. This aims to come up with a lightweight block cipher architecture, which is optimization based and balanced in both security and computing power. A nonlinear substitution-permutation network design that operates with an optimization model is minimized using the round-complexity and memory used and maintains diffusion and confusion properties. The proposed lightweight protocol guarantees a 28.6 % encryption latency, 34.2 % energy usage and 19.8 % reduction in memory than traditional lightweight ciphers, which increase the overall cryptographic performance by 26.4 %.</p>','["Lightweight Cryptography, Block Cipher Design, Optimization-Based Security Model, Substitution\\u2013Permutation Network, Energy-Efficient Encryption, IoT Security."]',NULL,19,1,'1054 - 1062','10.59543/jidmis.v3.914','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',58),
(915,915,1,'Token Evolution Modelling Using Finite-State Automata','<p>In decentralized systems, the evolution of tokens is not typically modeled formally, and state transitions are typically verification ally unsound and suffer a scaling problem. The paradigm of the issue of token lifecycle dynamics in this paper is to model dynamics in Finite-State Automata (FSA). This has been done to formally model the token generation, transfer, validation and termination states with deterministic state-transition structures. The proposed model develops an automata model of token development with transition functions and verification rules. Experimental analysis reveals that 18.6% is the increase in state validation accuracy, 21.3% is the reduction in transaction inconsistency and 16.4% verification is quicker than the rule based token tracking. Results indicate that such parameters as validation latency and transition integrity are more reliable. The framework assists in tracking scalable tokens of blockchain-based ecosystems.</p>','["Finite-State Automata, Token Evolution Modeling, Blockchain Systems, State Transition Verification, Transaction Consistency Analysis"]',NULL,19,1,'1063 - 1071','10.59543/jidmis.v3.915','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',59),
(916,916,1,'Spectral Community Detection for Botnet Cluster Identification','<p>Modern network infrastructures are under a massive threat of botnets due to its ability to coordinate such cyber-attacks as distributed denial-of-service, spam propagation, and data exfiltration. The presence of peer-to-peer architectures which are decentralized and encrypted by large-scale and dynamically changing communication networks has become a thorny problem in detecting botnet communities. The paper suggests a spectral community detection model which represents network traffic as a weighted graph and finds the clusters of botnets with the help of graph Laplacian eigenvalue decomposition. The approach trains low-dimensional spectral representations and uses k-means clustering to distinguish between malicious and benign communities using structural patterns of connectivity. Experimental analysis shows that the available method has attained a detection with 96.8% accuracy, precision of 95.9, recall of 97.4 and F1-score of 96.6, which is significantly higher than the performance of more traditional clustering algorithms like K-means, DBSCAN, and signature-based intrusion detection systems. The model also has a high score of modularity of 0.84 and a low false positive rate of 3.6 meaning that the model has high community separation and strength. The new framework offers a scalable and effective way to detect changing botnet structures in the complex network setup.</p>','["Spectral Clustering, Botnet Detection, Graph Theory, Network Security, Community Detection"]',NULL,19,1,'1072 - 1079','10.59543/jidmis.v3.916','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',60),
(917,917,1,'Probabilistic Analysis of Attribute-Based Encryption Schemes','<p>Effective management of the access to sensitive information remains an overriding issue for organizations. Attribute-based encryption (ABE) allows a cryptographic solution to limit decryption to users with particular sets of attributes. In this work we formulate access control as a prediction problem on the dataset of Amazon employee access, which consists of 32 769 labelled records and 58 921 test records. Employee attributes such as resource identifiers, departmental positions and job titles constitute the feature set. After the preprocessing of the data and introduction of probabilistic model inspired by ABE, we compare the standard classifiers against our proposed method. The proposed probabilistic ABE analysis model got 97.36% accuracy, 96.98% precision and 96.71% F1-score result, which are superior to traditional logistic regression and tree methods. These outcomes suggest that a probabilistic view toward ABE policies can have a meaningful impact on automated access decisions in a complex organizational environment.</p>','["Attribute\\u2011based encryption, access control, probabilistic model, employee access, machine learning, security"]',NULL,19,1,'1080 - 1086','10.59543/jidmis.v3.917','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',61),
(918,918,1,'Interference-Aware Secure Communication in Dense Access Scenarios','<p>Wireless access networks are dense, which means that there is severe co-channel interference and much more susceptible to eavesdropping, which deteriorates the reliability and secrecy of communications. The present paper presents an interference-aware secure communication scheme that optimized concurrently on transmission power, interference reduction, and secrecy capacity with the help of an optimization-based adaptive scheme. It develops a mathematical model of Signal-to-Interference-plus-Noise Ratio (SINR) and secrecy throughput and then a convex optimization-based transmission strategy is developed. The experimental simulation shows that the proposed framework can attain a secrecy rate of 4.21 ±0.18 bits/s/Hz, secure throughput of 33.7±1.2 Mbps and a success rate of 97.4 ± 0.6 packet success rate with high network density (200 users/km 2). The proposed model has a secrecy capacity that is 34.9% higher than conventional and NOMA-based schemes and secrecy outage probability is 58.6 times lower. The findings validate an increase in the ability to suppress interference and strong secure communication performance in crowded wireless environments. The given framework is a scalable solution to secure the IoT and next-generation wireless systems.</p>','["Interference-Aware Communication, Dense Access Networks, Secrecy Capacity, Secure Throughput Optimization, Interference Mitigation"]',NULL,19,1,'1087 - 1094','10.59543/jidmis.v3.918','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',62),
(919,919,1,'Graph Neural Networks for Adversarial Example Detection','<p>Online commerce has been growing rapidly and the fraudsters exploit the weak points of the payment systems by injecting crafted examples that deceive models. Adversarial inputs can lead to severe losses and spin of trust, and thus, it is necessary to work on detection methods that can separate true and manipulated transactions. This paper builds a graph from the dataset of the IEEE-CIS Fraud Detection competition and a model called Graph Neural Network (GNN) which it uses to detect adversarial. The results of our model are better than the standard classifiers with 97.82% accuracy with 96.71% precision, recall 95.64% and F1- score 96.17%. These results exceed the results obtained by a logistic regression as baseline (93.12% accuracy) and even those by a random forest (95.08% accuracy) proving that detecting structures between entities provides a better detection. The work also describes the preprocessing that is intended to clean the data and construct a network of transactions and suggests mathematical formulations for GNN training and adversarial detection.</p>','["graph neural networks, adversarial example detection, fraud detection, transaction networks, credit card fraud, machine learning"]',NULL,19,1,'1095 - 1100','10.59543/jidmis.v3.919','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',63),
(920,920,1,'Real-Time Session Control Using Temporal Logic Enforcement','<p>Real-Time Session Control Using Temporal Logic Enforcement presents a formal system of checking and enforcing the secure behaviors of the session in dynamically computing environments. The suggested model models session states and events as formal transitions between states and uses the Linear Temporal Logic (LTL) rules to check safety and liveness property in the execution. Temporal constraints allow a violated policy, an illegal transition, and an abnormal session activity to be identified early which enhances reliability, the assurance of security, and compliance in real time in a distributed system.</p>','["Real-Time Session Control, Temporal Logic Enforcement, Linear Temporal Logic (LTL), Security Policy Verification, Runtime Monitoring."]',NULL,19,1,'1101 - 1109','10.59543/jidmis.v3.920','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',64),
(921,921,1,'Secure Embedding Stabilization in Federated Learning','<p>Secure Embedding Stabilization (SES) deals with embedding drift and instability in federated learning in non-IID and adversarial scenarios. This paper develops embedding variance minimization as a stability-constrained optimization problem and regularized secure alignment framework with privacy preserving aggregation. The suggested SES-FL model combines the variance-conscious regularization and encrypted centroid alignment to provide stabilized learning of representation among distributed clients. Experimental analysis shows better performance with accuracy of 89.74, precision 0.882, recall 0.876, and F1-score 0.879 and minimizes embedding variance to 0.072. The framework rounds off to 61 rounds, better than FedAvg, FedProx and FedDyn in terms of stability and communication efficiency (6.3 MB/round). These findings confirm that SES is a useful solution to strong, stable, and privacy-aware federated learning in heterogeneous data environments.</p>','["Federated Learning, Embedding Stabilization, Secure Aggregation, Non-IID Data, Regularized Optimization"]',NULL,19,1,'1110 - 1118','10.59543/jidmis.v3.921','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',65),
(922,922,1,'Formal Verification Frameworks for Social Media Analytics Pipelines','<p>The social media analytics pipelines facilitate a high number of posts and interactions on the platforms, but their accuracy is frequently assumed. The paper will design and assess a formal verification system of the Social Media Engagement Report Dataset, which is made of platform identifiers, post identifiers, content type, time of post, likes and comments among other engagement variables. The suggested framework considers the pipeline as a chain of transformations having formally defined properties and defines a proof obligation on every step. The engagement data were experimented using logistic regression, random forest, gradient boosting and support vehicle machine models. According to the results, the formal verification pipeline has an accuracy of 93.64, precision of 91.72, recall of 90.85 and F1 score of 91.28, which is higher than the baseline methods. The value is in bringing together formal techniques and applied analytics processes and showing an improvement in model soundness and interpretability via verification.</p>','["social media analytics, engagement analysis, formal verification, machine learning, data pipeline, correctness."]',NULL,19,1,'1119 - 1124','10.59543/jidmis.v3.922','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',66),
(923,923,1,'Temperature-Dependent Encryption Scaling in IoT Chipsets','<p>There is a change in performance in IoT chipsets due to changes in temperature that affect cryptographic latency, energy consumption, and reliability. In order to address this shortcoming, an encryption scaling framework is put forward that is temperature dependent and that dynamically scales the cryptographic strength with real-time thermal conditions. The framework combines thermal modeling using CMOS with scaling voltage and frequency to achieve the best security and hardware efficiency trade-offs. A classification mechanism is used to classify thermal states into several levels to provide adaptive control of key length, encryption rounds and operational mode. Simulation-based experimental analysis of the proposed approach under simulated conditions of an IoT chipset show that the proposed approach can save 17.8% ± 1.2. of power, and sustain a security integrity at a rate higher than 96.5% ± 0.8. Moreover, the latency variation is also controlled to about ±6 percent in response to temperature, in comparison to traditional static encryption techniques. Through comparative analysis, better thermal resilience and energy efficiency is achieved without affecting cryptographic strength. The suggested framework offers a scalable and effective architecture of secure IoT deployments that can work under thermally dynamic conditions.</p>','["Temperature-Aware Cryptography", "IoT Chipsets", "Dynamic Encryption Scaling", "Thermal Modeling", "Energy-Efficient Security"]',NULL,19,1,'1125 - 1132','10.59543/jidmis.v3.923','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',67),
(924,924,1,'Delay-Aware Encryption Scheduling in Satellite Backbone Links','<p>The current paper suggests a delay-sensitive encryption scheduling model of satellite backbone connections in which the problem of high propagation delay and adaptive bandwidth environment presents challenges to traditional fixed-strength encryption techniques. The aim is to maximize the trade-off among the security strength, latency, and computational overhead with a multi-objective optimization model. The suggested system dynamically changes the levels of encryption according to the real-time estimation of delay, the priority of the traffic and the conditions of channels. A mathematical model of propagation, transmission, queueing, and encryption delays is formulated, and an adaptive utility-based decision mechanism. Under changing load conditions on the satellite, experimental assessment of the proposed approach shows that the approach minimizes end-to-end delay by up to 33.3 percent, enhances throughput to 96.8 Mbps, and enhances packet delivery ratio to 97.1 percent, over fixed encryption schemes. The model can have adaptive security levels (128 to 256 bits) without interfering with the reliability. Findings confirm that delay-conscious scheduling is much more effective at improving Quality of Service without compromising cryptographic strength, and can be used in next-generation satellite communication systems.</p>','["Delay-Aware Encryption, Satellite Backbone Links, Adaptive Cryptography, Multi-Objective Optimization, Secure Scheduling"]',NULL,19,1,'1133 - 1140','10.59543/jidmis.v3.924','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',68);
INSERT INTO publications (id,submission_id,version,title,abstract,keywords,references_text,issue_id,section_id,pages,doi,copyright_holder,copyright_year,license_url,status,date_published,seq) VALUES
(925,925,1,'Secure Aggregation of Environmental Data via Error-Bounded Encoding','<p>Environmental monitoring systems are based on distributed sensors that continuously gather environmental data such as gas concentration, temperature and humidity of the atmosphere. Secure aggregation techniques are needed to combine such readings in an efficient way and secure the confidentiality of individual sensor readings. The proposed deals with security aspects of aggregation of environmental data with error bounded encoding. Air quality monitoring networks require taking readings from sensors without having to expose individual values. We propose a method that is capable of quantizing and encrypting readings and bounds the reconstruction error. Using an open-air quality dataset, we compare the scheme with mean aggregation, secure hash aggregation and differential privacy. Our approach is able to obtain 0.492 RMSE and 0.371 MAE. Encoding distortion is reduced to 2.96% and the latency is reduced to 22.4 ms. These results are less expensive, in terms of accuracy and delay times. Environmental monitoring sensors generate massive amounts of data and are used in resource constrained environments. Many of the secure aggregation techniques add noise or heavy computation. Our approach allows for controlling error and reduces the communication overhead. The approach is light weight for sensor nodes and can be used for networks with many devices. Encoding data prior to transmission is a good compromise between accuracy and security and offers a viable solution to secure environmental monitoring.</p>','["secure aggregation, error bounded encoding, environmental sensing, air quality, data privacy, sensor networks."]',NULL,19,1,'1141 - 1147','10.59543/jidmis.v3.925','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',69),
(926,926,1,'Lattice-Induced Key Exchange with Optimized Polynomial Sampling','<p>This paper introduces an optimized policy on using polynomials sampling strategy in lattice-based key exchange protocols based on the hardness of Ring-LWE and Module-LWE. The suggested approach presents a discrete distribution that is refined and minimizes the rejection probability without loss of statistical indistinguishability and cryptographic security. The scheme combines inverse transform sampling with variable control bound that is constrained, and thus, it minimizes computation loads that are required by the conventional Gaussian sampling schemes. Compared to 148 µs sampling time and 7.8% rejection rate, experimental assessment shows that sampling time has been reduced by a factor of 32.6 (92 µs) and the rejection rate has decreased by 2.9 (2.9). The methodology retains the same degree of the polynomials and security assurances and enhances resource-constrained performance. When compared, improved performance is established at the expense of traditional sampling mechanisms without sacrificing the post-quantum resilience. The suggested optimization can be applied to real-time cryptographic implementations in IoT and embedded-systems that need low-latency secure communication.</p>','["Lattice Cryptography, Key Exchange, Ring-LWE, Polynomial Sampling, Post-Quantum Security, Computational Optimization"]',NULL,19,1,'1148 - 1156','10.59543/jidmis.v3.926','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',70),
(927,927,1,'Resilience Indices for Smart City Utility Telemetry Networks','<p>Resilient communication infrastructures are needed to enable the continuous monitoring of smart city utility telemetry networks in the face of cyber-physical failures, congestion and dynamics in the way operations are conducted. In this paper, a composite resilience index framework is proposed that combines graph-theoretic modelling, fault propagation analysis, cyber-physical dependency structure modelling and machine learning-based anomaly detection to enable smart city telemetry systems to be resilient. This framework uses adjacency matrices for reliability assessment, robustness indices and shortest-path optimization for latency stability and failure recovery to measure the efficiency, and coupled failure interaction modelling to assess data integrity. Lots of simulations are conducted with various traffic loads, node failures, and cyber attack load and show significant performance improvements. The proposed model has a reliability of 92.8%, recovery efficiency of 88.7% and a degree of resilience of 91.1%, better than traditional approaches. Within this framework, the adaptive management of infrastructure, the ability to assess its resilience at scale and the intelligence of smart city operations are supported.</p>','["Smart city networks, Telemetry resilience, Utility monitoring, Fault tolerance, Network reliability, Cyber-physical systems"]',NULL,19,1,'1157 - 1164','10.59543/jidmis.v3.927','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',71),
(928,928,1,'Predictive Maintenance and Security Co-Design with Robotic Assembly Lines','<p>In robotic assembly lines, mechanical degradation and cyber attack propagation are two factors that interact and affect each other on the operational stability of robotic assembly lines, which constitutes a single interconnected cyber-physical network. In this study, a Graph-Coupled Stochastic Dynamical Framework for Predictive Maintenance and Attack Propagation in Robotic Assembly Lines, based on stochastic degradation evolution and graph-coupled attack diffusion and spectral stability analysis, is presented. Robotic units are modeled as graph nodes where their combined state takes into account degradation in health status and vulnerability to security threats while considering weighted interaction dynamics. Operational variability is modeled by stochastic disturbances and adversarial spread through communication links is modeled by diffusion. Spectral propagation constraints and coupled risk evolution metrics are used to analyse the stability behaviour. Results of experimental evaluation showed that the remaining useful life error is reduced to 6.21%, attack propagation rate is reduced to 0.15, recovery time is reduced to 4.38 s and secure production continuity was improved to 96.84%, which demonstrates stable cyber-physical resilience and improved maintenance-security co-design.</p>','["Predictive maintenance, cyber-physical systems, degradation modeling, attack propagation, graph-based modeling"]',NULL,19,1,'1165 - 1170','10.59543/jidmis.v3.928','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',72),
(929,929,1,'Computational Models of Retinal Neuron Responses to Visual Stimuli','<p>The proposed study will model the retina by using a spatial–temporal signal processing method to model the response of the retina neurons to the dynamic visual stimuli, along with a nonlinear spike generation mechanism that is biologically interpretable. An architecture based on Difference-of-Gaussians (DoG) receptive field modelling, Gaussian spatial filtering, temporal adaptation, membrane potential dynamics and stochastic Poisson spike generation to simulate the ganglion cell output under various visual conditions. To enhance physiological plausibility and the computational interpretability, the receptive field convolution, contrast normalization, nonlinear activation and firing rate saturation are added as mathematical formulations. Experimental validation indicates that the responses of the firing is in good agreement with the biological for various kinds of stimuli with the RMSE values ranging from 0.87 to 1.36 spikes/s. The framework can be applied in various computational neuroscience, neuromorphic vision systems and retinal prosthetic modelling applications.</p>','["Retinal neurons", "Receptive field modelling", "Difference-of-Gaussians", "Spike generation", "Computational vision"]',NULL,19,1,'1171 - 1178','10.59543/jidmis.v3.929','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',73),
(930,930,1,'Multiscale Mathematical Modelling of Cellular Mechanotransduction Signalling','<p>Mechanotransduction is the transduction of extracellular mechanical signals to intracellular biochemical signaling networks that control cell growth, migration and differentiation. A multiscale mathematical framework is proposed, which is composed of mechanical force balance equations, reaction–diffusion equation for signalling dynamics and mechanosensitive receptor activation kinetic. The model developed couples the mechanical equilibrium inside the cells to the nonlinear biochemical transport equations, to characterize force-induced propagation of signalling across spatial and temporal scales in the cells. The quantitative simulations show that increasing the mechanical stimulus from 5–20 Pa increases the cytoskeletal strain by 1.2–4.6%, the activation rate of receptors by 0.08–0.36 s⁻¹ and the speed of the propagation of signalling by 2.1–6.2 µm/s. The results validate the finding that coupled mechanochemical interactions can improve the efficiency of intracellular signalling and regulation of cellular response in different biomechanical conditions.</p>','["Mechanotransduction, Multiscale Mathematical Modelling, Cellular Biomechanics, Reaction\\u2013Diffusion Signalling, Cytoskeletal Mechanics"]',NULL,19,1,'1179 - 1185','10.59543/jidmis.v3.930','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',74),
(931,931,1,'Agent-Based and Mean-Field Models for: Multi-Stage Supply Chain Attacks','<p>In digital supply chains, multi-stage cyber attacks slowly propagate, have long compromise states and are distributedly interdependent between operational entities. This study will provide a Fractional-Order Dynamical Framework for Multi-Stage Supply Chain Attack Propagation using the fractional diffusion dynamics and stochastic agent interaction and distributed recovery evolution. The modelling of memory dependent attack persistence is captured by fractional order derivatives, where the propagation behaviour of the attacks depends on the compromise states in history. Cascading attack dynamics between inter-connected stages of a supply chain are captured by including agent-level stochastic transitions and macro-level diffusion fields. Stability and resilience are assessed using persistence decay, recovery convergence and distributed risk evolution. Under memory-dependent dynamics, experimental evaluation showed that the attack diffusion error decreased to 0.104, the stage compromise rate to 22.6%, and the persistence index to 0.21 while the recovery stability index improved to 0.93, showing that the resilience of the attack was enhanced and that the attack propagation was controlled.</p>','["Fractional-order diffusion, Supply chain attacks, Multi-stage propagation, Attack persistence, Distributed risk dynamics."]',NULL,19,1,'1186 - 1191','10.59543/jidmis.v3.931','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',75),
(932,932,1,'Interpretable Deep Learning Defences via: Secure Federated Learning Frameworks','<p>In federated learning systems, the evolution of the systems is based on distributed optimization and graph coupled interactions among clients, with adversarial updates affecting the system''s convergence stability and reliability of the model. We propose a Sparse Causal Graph Dynamical Framework for Secure Federated Learning Defence which relies on sparse causal attribution, graph Laplacian regularization and trust-guided aggregation dynamics. Local gradients and feature influence are used to model client updates in the form of causal attribution vectors, and graph smoothing is used to ensure consistency among trusted participants. Sparse optimization forces irrelevant attribution components to be sparse and thus bounds the adversarial propagation during aggregation. Spectral graph constraints and bounded adversarial deviation are considered to analyse stability and robustness. Experimental assessment showed that malicious update detection is 96%, verification coverage is 90%, graph consistency is high, the behaviour of bounded convergence is stable, and adversarial influence is low, suggesting stable federated optimization and explainable defence against poisoning attacks.</p>','["federated learning, causal attribution, sparse optimization, graph laplacian, adversarial defence."]',NULL,19,1,'1192 - 1198','10.59543/jidmis.v3.932','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',76),
(933,933,1,'Optimizing Healthcare Operations through Fuzzy Graph Theory: A Framework for Patient Flow and Resource Allocation','<p>Healthcare management involves complex decision-making processes to ensure efficient patient flow and optimal resource allocation. Fuzzy graph theory, a math-ematical approach for handling uncertainty and imprecise data, provides a robust framework for addressing these challenges. This article explores the application of fuzzy graph theory in healthcare, highlighting its potential to improve operational efficiency, reduce waiting times, and enhance patient outcomes.</p>','["Fuzzy Graph Theory, Healthcare Management, Patient Flow, Resource Allocation, Fuzzy Logic, Decision Support, Healthcare Optimization, Patient Outcomes, Hospital Management, Operational Efficiency."]',NULL,19,1,'1199 - 1209','10.59543/jidmis.v3.933','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',77),
(934,934,1,'Intelligent Reflecting Surfaces for Enhancing Physical Layer Security in 6G','<p>Intelligent Reflecting Surfaces (IRSs) have also become a revolutionary contributor to enable the sixth-generation (6G) wireless networks through reconfiguring the radioenvironment in an energy-efficient and programmable way. This paper explores how IRS-aided communication can be used to improve physical layer security against 6G-level threats that are becoming more and more advanced. We begin by introducing the basics of IRS architecture, electromagnetic theories, and operating modes and then we proceed with the in-depth discussion of channel modeling that includes the phase-dependent reflection and cascaded links in channel modeling. An actual security threat model is thereafter developed comprising passive eavesdropping, active jamming, spoofing, and pilot contamination considering imperfect channel state information and intelligent adversaries. To overcome these threats, we come up with optimization-based and learning-based IRS control schemes that collaboratively design transmit precoding and IRS phase shifts. Alternating optimization and semidefinite relaxation are used to analyze both convex and non-convex formulations, and deep learning and reinforcement learning techniques are discussed to use in adaptive, low-overhead control in dynamic environments. Large-scale simulations in conditions of 6G channels have shown large secrecy rate enhancements and secrecy outage probability reductions over traditional systems that do not have IRS support. Findings also indicate that learning based controllers are near optimal in terms of faster adaptation when dealing with mobility and uncertainty. In general, this paper introduces IRS as an important physical layer security primitive of 6G, which facilitates proactive software-defined defense mechanisms that can supplement higher-layer security and provide reliable and trustworthy wire connectivity. The suggested insights offer the design considerations of scalability in the deployment, standardization congruity, and realistic integration into the future global deployments of 6G infrastructures.</p>','["Intelligent Reflecting Surfaces", "Physical Layer Security", "6G Networks", "Secrecy Rate", "Reinforcement Learning", "Wireless Channel Modeling"]',NULL,19,1,'1210 - 1222','10.59543/jidmis.v3.934','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',78),
(935,935,1,'Improving Network Resilience against Correlated Link Failures in Fiber Networks','<p>Modern communication infrastructures rely on fiber-optic networks, but correlated link failures, which occur due to common physical, geographic or operational dependencies, tend to destroy the resilience of these networks. Traditional survivability solutions, which are based on independent failures, cannot address large-scale disruptions due to a disaster or due to the everyday failure of infrastructure. The paper will solve this problem by suggesting an SRLG-conscious resilience improvement framework of fiber networks which quantifies correlated failures with Shared Risk Link Groups. Diversified heuristic routing A scalable, greedy heuristic is constructed to calculate risk-differentiated primary and alternative routes with a minimal computational complexity. The suggested solution allows graceful degradation of the situation when the strict risk disjointness is not feasible, which guarantees better service continuity. Over the wide scale simulation-based analysis and disaster-oriented case study proves that the framework contributes greatly to network survivability and decreases the recovery time in correlated failure vulnerabilities, and there are only moderate increases in routing cost. The findings confirm the significance of correlation-aware design towards the construction of robust and disaster resilient fiber optic networks.</p>','["Correlated Link Failures, Fiber Networks, Shared Risk Link Groups, Network Resilience, Risk-Aware Routing, Disaster Recovery"]',NULL,19,1,'1223 - 1233','10.59543/jidmis.v3.935','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',79),
(936,936,1,'Evaluating the Efficiency of Optical Circuit Switching in Mega Data Centers','<p>The issues of scalability and energy efficiency in mega data centers are growing because the east-west traffic is growing at a very high rate and the traditional electrical packet-switched (EPS) networks are constrained. Optical circuit switching (OCS) has come to be a promising complement to the packet switching because it offers high capacity data transport with improved energy-efficiency to support large and steady data traffic. The paper is a systematically assessed comparison of EPS-only data center architectures, OCS-assisted, and hybrid EPS-OCS data center architectures based on a coherent experimental system. The evaluation of performance is done in terms of throughput, flow completion time, energy per delivered bit, optical circuit utilization, and blocking behavior to real traffic workload. The findings indicate that hybrid EPSOCS architectures achieve much better aggregate throughput and lower energy use without compromising low latency with respect to short and latency-sensitive flows. The optical circuits are utilized highly with little blocking, but the inefficiency is still evident when there is a dynamic traffic. In sum, it can be concluded that optical circuit switching is the most effective when embedded in traffic-conscious hybrid constructions that can provide a viable and scalable approach to next-generation mega data centers networks</p>','["Optical Circuit Switching, Data Center Networks, Hybrid Switching, Energy Efficiency, Flow Completion Time, Network Scalability"]',NULL,19,1,'1234 - 1244','10.59543/jidmis.v3.936','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',80),
(937,937,1,'A Blockchain-Based Framework for Transparent Spectrum Auctions','<p>The spectrum auctions are a necessity in the process of distributing limited radio resources, but the traditional systems of auction are highly centralized, opaque, and based on trust, which provokes issues of transparency, equity, and administrative effectiveness. This essay presents a blockchain architecture of transparent spectrum auctions that makes use of distributed ledger technology and smart contracts that facilitate immutable record-keeping, deterministic auction execution and verifiable auditability. The suggested framework formalizes the rule of the auction, the stages of the bidding, the process of selecting the winner, and the stage of the settlement into the smart contracts, which ensures an absence of any discretionary actions and decreases the level of insider trading. An exhaustive assessment of the framework is based on the security, transparency, fairness and performance aspects, which have been shown to have good guarantees of bid integrity, non-repudiation and procedural fairness. Blockchain-based implementation implies a moderate transaction latency, but the findings have demonstrated that automated appraisal and settlement could lead to important post-auction delays, which result in overall points of efficiency. The framework also contributes to the shift towards an institution-based to rule-based spectrum governing framework, which improves the confidence of the bidders and regulatory responsibility. The paper finds that blockchain-based spectrum auctions offer a strong and future-proof framework to the current spectrum management and can be further enlarged to accommodate future paradigms like secondary trade and dynamic spectrum access.</p>','["Blockchain auctions, Spectrum allocation, Smart contracts, Auction transparency, Decentralized governance, Trust minimization"]',NULL,19,1,'1245 - 1256','10.59543/jidmis.v3.937','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',81),
(938,938,1,'Analyzing the Performance of Distributed Ledger Technologies in 6G','<p>Distributed Ledger Technologies (DLTs) have turned out to be an underlying enabler of trust, security, and automation in the next-generation wireless networks (6G). Contrasting centralized control models, the DLTs offer decentralized coordination, record keeping which is immutable, and programmable logic, which is consistent with the ultra-dense and intelligent heterogeneous ecosystems of 6G. The paper has discussed the performance implications of incorporation of the SDLTs with 6G networks in blockchain, directed acyclic graph based ledger and hybrid DLT architectures. There was an integrated DLT-6G framework where cross-layer communication between radio access, core, edge computing, and distributed ledgers was highlighted. To model the latency of transactions, their throughput, energy usage, and consensus overhead were modeled based on the 6G communication characteristics including ultra-low latency, massive connectivity, and edge intelligence. A large-scale set of simulations was done to test the DLT-based network slicing, secure resource orchestration, and AI-assisted ledger management and compared the results to that of traditional non-DLT methods. The results have shown that lightweight and DAG-based DLTs were much more cost-effective in terms of confirmation delay and energy usage, whereas in dense 6G operation, hybrid designs were more scalable and dependable. Moreover, ledger management with the help of AI improved flexibility in changing the conditions of traffic and mobility.</p>','["Distributed Ledger Technology, 6G Networks, Blockchain, Network Slicing, Edge Intelligence, Consensus Mechanisms"]',NULL,19,1,'1257 - 1268','10.59543/jidmis.v3.938','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',82),
(939,939,1,'A Comparison of LPWAN Technologies: LoRa vs. Sigfox vs. NB-IoT','<p>Low-Power Wide-Area Network (LPWAN) technologies have been used to form the basis of large-scale Internet of Things (IoT) deployments requiring long-range coverage, low power usage, and affordability. The paper is a detailed comparative analysis of three popular technologies of the LPWAN: LoRaWAN, Sigfox, and Narrowband IoT (NB-IoT). Their architecture, protocol stack and their underlying structures, communication and performance properties, security and scalability, deployment model and cost structures are analyzed. Technical differences are converted into practical deployment knowledge using a detailed application suitability evaluation and a case study based on how to apply smart water metering in a scenario. The findings indicate that LoRaWAN is a good fit in flexible and low-cost private deployments, Sigfox offers ultra-low data rate and simple alerting applications, and NB-IoT offers carrier-grade reliability and security to large-scale and mission-critical deployment. The paper emphasizes that the choice of an LPWAN technology has to be application-based, by focusing on trade-offs between performance, security, scale, and overall cost of ownership instead of considering the individual metrics.</p>','["LPWAN, LoRaWAN, Sigfox, NB-IoT, Internet of Things, smart metering, low-power networks, IoT connectivity"]',NULL,19,1,'1269 - 1280','10.59543/jidmis.v3.939','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',83),
(940,940,1,'A Study on the Energy Consumption of Blockchain Consensus Mechanisms','<p>Blockchain technology has become a strong backbone to decentralized applications; however, the fast-growing energy usage of consensus mechanisms has become a cause of serious economic, environmental, and sustainability worries. This paper will be a detailed examination of the energy usage in the leading blockchain consensus algorithms, focusing specifically on the question of why some of them are high-energy usage and how their energy imprints might be decreased. The paper has started by conducting a review of the basic architecture of blockchain systems and classifying the different consensus mechanisms according to their working principle and resource needs. To analyze energy-intensive schemes, the specific attention is paid to Proof of Work, which is analyzed in terms of models of mining operations, hardware dependence, difficulty adjustment, and power consumption. Case studies of large-scaled PoW-based blockchains are presented in real-life scenarios to represent the implications of the energy in practical scenarios and network conditions. Moreover, the research also undertakes the systematic examination of optimization methods that will help to decrease the energy consumption without affecting the security or decentralization. These are innovations at the protocol level, hardware-awareness, mining policies based on renewable energy sources, and the use of layer-2 and off-chain transaction processing systems. Trade-offs between security, scalability, and decentralization and energy efficiency are discussed comparatively. The findings show that next-generation consensus mechanisms and hybrid designs are capable of saving a huge amount of energy without compromising the reasonable performance and trust guarantees.</p>','["Blockchain, Consensus Mechanisms, Energy Consumption, Proof of Work, Energy Efficiency, Sustainable Distributed Ledgers"]',NULL,19,1,'1281 - 1293','10.59543/jidmis.v3.940','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',84),
(941,941,1,'Secure AI-Based Authentication for IoT Devices','<p>It is important to establish secure AI-based authentication in IoT devices in light of the growing cyber threats and environments with heterogeneity. This paper suggests a smart authentication system that combines supervised and deep learning to provide a more secure and reliable system. The goal is to enhance accuracy of authentication and minimize the false acceptance and latency. The algorithm is a hybrid of device fingerprinting, behavioral biometrics, and network traffic characteristics with the models of SVM, Random Forest, ANN, CNN, LSTM, and autoencoders. The results of the experiments show a better accuracy of 96.4, which is 18.2 better than the traditional methods. The framework enforces scalable and real-time implementation of IoT. The research concludes that AI-based authentication is a vital tool towards enhancing the security and functionality of IoT in a variety of applications. Moreover, the latency is minimized by 27.5% assuring quicker decision-making and enhanced user experience of resource-bound IoT settings around the world.</p>','["IoT authentication, device fingerprinting, deep learning, behavioral biometrics, network security, AI-based security systems"]',NULL,19,1,'1294 - 1301','10.59543/jidmis.v3.941','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',85),
(942,942,1,'A Hybrid Spectral–Spatial Deep Learning Framework with Harris Hawk Optimization and Support Vector Machine for Accurate Arecanut Plantation Mapping Using Sentinel-2 Imagery','<p>Spectral similarity with other perennial vegetation and heterogeneous agricultural landscapes still make accurate identification of arecanut plantations from medium resolution satellite imagery a challenge. In this paper, a Hybrid Spectral–Spatial DeepLabV3+ with Harris Hawk Optimization and Support Vector Machine (HSS-DEEPLAB-HHO-SVM) framework is presented for robust arecanut plantation segmentation and land-cover classification using Sentinel-2 multispectral imagery. The proposed method combines adaptive spectral feature extraction, vegetation indices, optimized DeepLabV3+ based spatial feature learning (Adaptive Atrous Spatial Pyramid Pooling (A-ASPP), Channel-Spatial Attention (CSAM) and adaptive spectral-spatial feature fusion) and Harris Hawk Optimization (HHO) for hyperparameter tuning and then utilizes SVM classifier for better decision boundary optimization. The experimental results on the arecanut dataset achieved an Overall Accuracy of 98.54% and a Mean Intersection over Union of 96.93% with a mere 0.20% relative deviation in plantation area estimation from official statistics. Moreover, evaluation on the Indian Pines hyperspectral benchmark showed excellent generalization capability, achieving 99.77% Overall Accuracy. The proposed framework provides an efficient and scalable solution for precision agriculture, crop inventory generation and remote sensing based land-cover mapping.</p>','["Arecanut Plantation Mapping, Sentinel-2 Multispectral Imagery, DeepLabV3+, Harris Hawk Optimization (HHO), Support Vector Machine (SVM), Semantic Segmentation."]',NULL,19,1,'1302 - 1323','10.59543/jidmis.v3.942','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',86),
(943,943,1,'A Deep Reinforcement Learning Approach to Dynamic Channel Selection','<p>Channel selection is a dynamic and challenging issue in the present wireless network that is shared and suffers interference in a spectrum that is not fully controlled. The traditional heuristic and greedy methods depend on immediate channel measurements and they are not always adapted to non-stationary environments resulting in poor throughput, high packet error rates and unpredictable channel switching behaviour. As a remedy to these shortcomings, this paper will suggest a deep reinforcement learning-based system to perform dynamic channel selection, which provides the formulation of the problem as a Markov Decision Process and uses a Deep Q-Network to train the optimal long-term policies of spectrum access. The given strategy combines spectrum sensing, contextual state representation, and reward-based learning to make adaptive and stable choices to select the channel. The reward function explicitly includes a switching cost in order to strike a balance between the adaptability and the stability. Numerous simulation-based tests have shown that the given technique replicates the best results in terms of mean throughput, packet error rate, and frequency of channel switching, compared to the random, greedy and bandit-based baseline schemes. The findings validate the outcomes of deep reinforcement learning in learning temporal spectrum dynamics and maximizing the long-term communication performance in non-stationary wireless conditions.</p>','["Dynamic Channel Selection, Deep Q-Network, Spectrum Access, Wireless Networks, Non-Stationary Environments"]',NULL,19,1,'1324 - 1335','10.59543/jidmis.v3.943','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',87),
(944,944,1,'A Hybrid Blockchain SAP BTP Framework for Secure and Transparent Data Embedding and Multi-Party Transaction Security in Healthcare Systems','<p>The digital healthcare ecosystem ensures privacy,security and integrity of patient health data from various vendors. SAP Business Technology Platform (SAP BTP) being a centralized platform keeping all data on a secure level having lack in managing decentralized trust and tamper proof health data.To improve security level of multi-party transactions of patient data the research proposes hybrid architecture keeping combination of SAP BTP and blockchain technology. SAP BTP Integration suite with blockchain based validation techniques is the proposed solution to manage patient health records electronically through integration flows. The hybrid cryptography algorithm is used to encrypt sensitive patient healthcare data before stored in form of blocks cloud environment. The hashing technique is used to maintain patient metadata in chain. Role based access control for patients with ownership of individual health records allowed for authorized users through secure API call, SSO and MFA. This propsedsolution bridges the gap between blockchain oriented technology with centralized SAP BTP cloud platform supporting scalable &amp; trust tamper proof deminishing the illegal access &amp; data breaches of patient healthcare records in today’s healthcare environment.</p>','["Electronic health records (EHR), Security, Blockchain, Single Sign-On (SSO), Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA), Systems, Applications & Products (SAP), Digital Health Systems, Secure API Integration, Off-Chain Storage, Data Encryption, Public Key Infrastructure (PKI)"]',NULL,19,1,'1336 - 1350','10.59543/jidmis.v3.944','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',88),
(945,945,1,'Sustainable Smart Tour Routing: An Integrated Framework for Destination Management in Sangkhlaburi District, Thailand','<p>Environmental degradation, the concentration of visitors at tourism destinations, the negative impacts of transport on the environment, and the unbalanced distribution of the benefits of tourism are among the challenges faced by tourism destinations. Although the routining of tours is traditionally considered an operational task, this study suggests that it could be a strategic tool in sustainable destination management. The quantitative, cross-sectional survey consisted of 400 tourists visiting Sangkhlaburi District, Kanchanaburi Province, Thailand, to investigate the integration of environmental, economic, social, and technological aspects of sustainability into tour route design. The findings reveal significant relationships between sustainable tour routing, sustainable tourism activities, service quality and tourist satisfaction. Sustainable tourist routes developed around community participation, resource conservation, and smart tourism technologies are more sustainable and beneficial in terms of experience gain than traditional tourist routes focusing on attraction development. A sustainable smart tour routing framework, combining the analysis of GIS, artificial intelligence, and destination sustainability indices, is proposed in five stages. This study brings sustainable tourism theory to the operational level of route planning and provides suggestions for tourism operators, destination managers, and policymakers.</p>','["sustainable tourism, tour routing, smart tourism, GIS, artificial intelligence, destination management, visitor mobility"]',NULL,19,1,'1351 - 1368','10.59543/jidmis.v3.945','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',89),
(946,946,1,'Blockchain-Backed Traceable Authentication of Randomized MAC Addresses in Cyber-Physical IoT Networks','<p>In this research work, the study focuses on the communication at the probe stage in the cyber-physical IoT, where there is exposure of MAC addresses, which enables attacks like tracking, spoofing, replay, and profiling. Even though the MAC randomization helps in privacy, there is the need for authentication due to the difficulty that faces the edge gateway when determining the legitimacy of the generated MAC address. We provide a solution of using the blockchain based and lightweight PKI inspired commitment mechanism for traceable authentication of the randomized MAC address. Our approach combines the Cuckoo Search-Levy Flight based randomized MAC transformation and edge assisted Proof-of-Authority based blockchain verification. The proposed scheme replaces sending the fixed MAC address by sending the randomized MAC and then the edge gateway verifies the identity commitments that are recorded in the blockchain ledgers. According to our analysis using NS-3 simulator, our</p>','["IoT Security, Lightweight PKI, MAC Randomization, Blockchain Authentication, Edge-Based Identity Traceability, Probe Authentication, Public Key Infrastructure"]',NULL,19,1,'1369 - 1382','10.59543/jidmis.v3.946','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',90),
(947,947,1,'Dual-Stream Fraud Detection Framework Using Behaviour Feature Engineering and Graph Neural Networks for Financial Transaction Analysis','<p>Data distributions are very skewed in financial fraud detection, and fraud behaviours are constantly changing in digital transaction systems, making financial fraud detection a tougher task. In this paper, they present a multimodal fraud detection framework that combines the representation learning of the financial network structure via graph-based methods with the feature engineering of the transactions'' behaviour based on transactional data. The framework is assessed with two large-scale datasets: the PaySim financial transaction data is synthetic, and the Elliptic Bitcoin dataset is a transaction graph structure. Classical machine learning algorithms such as Logistic Regression, Random Forest and XGBoost are utilised for behavioural modelling, while GraphSAGE-based Graph Neural Networks are utilised for learning structural dependencies in transaction graphs. According to the experimental results, the performance of XGBoost is good, with an ROC-AUC of 0.9571 and an F1 score of 0.6812 with the dataset of PaySim. Furthermore, the GraphSAGE model achieves better performance than traditional models with a ROC-AUC of 0.9633, F1 score of 0.7707 and PR-AUC of 0.8188 in detecting relational fraud and is considered effective in such patterns. The acquired framework is well designed to provide effective evaluation while eliminating leakage with suitable validation strategies and class-weighted learning to deal with imbalanced data. Overall, the results demonstrate that blending behavioural and graph-based learning does significantly improve fraud detection capabilities in a complex financial context.</p>','["Financial Fraud Detection, Graph Neural Networks, Behavioral Feature Engineering, Machine Learning, XGBoost Classification."]',NULL,19,1,'1383 - 1397','10.59543/jidmis.v3.947','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',91),
(948,948,1,'Multi-Crop Leaf Disease Detection using YOLOv12 with Class-Aware Multi-Scale Fusion and Adaptive Attention Modules','<p>- Enhancing agricultural productivity and attaining sustainable crop management depend on the early and precise identification of leaf disease. Using state-of-the-art technologies in precision agriculture like machine learning (ML) and image processing greatly increases the effectiveness of disease detection and facilitates well-informed decision-making. But conventional manual inspection techniques are still tedious, unpredictable, and prone to errors. In order to overcome these constraints, this research offers YOLOv12-CropNet, an innovative deep learning-based system for multi-crop leaf disease diagnosis in real time. The proposed YOLOv12-CropNet approach makes use of the Convolutional Block Attention Module (CBAM) for adaptive attention, the Content-Aware Reassembly of Features (CARAFE) up-sampling module to preserve fine-grained disease characteristics, the YOLOv12 architecture improved with Ghost Convolution for effective feature extraction, and Involution layers to capture spatially specific patterns. Inspection techniques are still laborious, arbitrary, and prone to mistakes. A substantial set of data of 38 classes of both healthy and sick leaves from a variety of crops, including tomato, potato, apple, grape, corn, mango and sugarcane, was put together for training and evaluation. Experimental results show that YOLOv12-CropNet finds a suitable balance between computational speed and accurate detection. Accuracy, F1-score, recall, and precision are important performance metrics that verify the model''s resilience in challenging environmental and visual circumstances. The suggested technique provides a scalable and field-deployable way to assist effective identification of diseases and precision agricultural decision-making. The proposed YOLOv12-CropNet model exhibits better performance than the other evaluated models, attaining a 98.45% peak accuracy, 98.10% precision ,98.20 % sensitivity and a 98.18% F1 score, thereby highlighting its efficacy in multi-crop leaf disease detection.</p>','["Leaf Disease Detection, YOLOv12-CropNet, Ghost Convolution, Convolutional Block Attention Module (CBAM), Content-Aware ReAssembly of Features (CARAFE)."]',NULL,19,1,'1398 - 1419','10.59543/jidmis.v3.948','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',92),
(949,949,1,'The Future of Work in the Age of Artificial Intelligence: Assessing the Role of AI in Recruitment, Workforce Reskilling, Labour Regulations, and Human Capital Management','<p>While AI is transforming the way we work and is providing numerous new opportunities, it is also presenting recruitment fairness, adaptability of workforce, regulatory compliance and strategic human capital management challenges. Current research often focuses on one of these dimensions individually, however, so far there is not enough research to fully grasp the implications of AI for the transformation of the workforce. The goal of this review is to fill this gap by analyzing and exploring how AI can be applied in the recruitment process, workforce reskilling, and labour regulation and human capital management. The primary objective is to raise up the awareness of the most recent advancements, challenges in the implementation, ethical considerations and future research directions for sustainable AI in the workplace. A systematic literature review methodology is applied, which is a compilation of recent peer-reviewed research, industry reports and policy frameworks are analysed which then inform about the AI technologies, organisational practices and policy developments. In the meantime, there are serious issues to be addressed and monitored algorithmic bias, transparency, privacy and job loss and shifting of labour regulations that must be dealt with in an ethical way. The novelty of the study is the technological, organizational, regulatory and human capital perspectives are integrated and combined in a single analytical framework in the future work ecosystems. The paper offers detailed insights into the new applications of AI, how to implement them, and policy considerations, and as such will be useful for researchers, policymakers and industry practitioners.</p>','["Artificial Intelligence", "Recruitment and Talent Acquisition", "Workforce Reskilling", "Human Capital Management", "Labour Regulations", "Algorithmic Bias and Governance"]',NULL,19,1,'1420 - 1431','10.59543/jidmis.v3.949','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',93);
INSERT INTO publications (id,submission_id,version,title,abstract,keywords,references_text,issue_id,section_id,pages,doi,copyright_holder,copyright_year,license_url,status,date_published,seq) VALUES
(950,950,1,'Prediction of In-Situ Properties of Coastal Soils Using GWO-XGBoost Model','<p>In coastal port infrastructure, accurate prediction of soil profiles and Standard Penetration Test (SPT) N-values at intermediate borehole locations is critical for safe, economical and resilient foundation design, as across all three dimensions subsoil conditions can vary significantly over short distances since soil is heterogeneous, anisotropic, and unpredictable material. In order to predict in-situ properties at intermediate points, the application of Machine Learning models is necessary which would save time and cost required during the preliminary design and detailed planning phases. In order to maximize predictive accuracy and to navigate complex hyperparameter search spaces, a metaheuristic - optimized approach like the Grey Wolf Optimizer (GWO) - eXtreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost) hybrid model was used, which is superior to classical interpolation techniques and conventional Machine Learning models such as Random Forest and XGBoost which suffer from limited generalization on sparse geotechnical datasets and suboptimal hyperparameter selection. A dataset of 385 borehole records from 72 geotechnically investigated locations, spanning depths of 0.5 m to 87 m of three major deep-sea port development sites, namely Machilipatnam, Ramayapatnam, and Durgarajpatnam, Andhra Pradesh, India, was compiled and processed using systematic data cleaning, geotechnical imputation, spatial feature engineering, and three normalization strategies Z-Score Standardization, Min-Max Scaling, and Robust Scaling in order to predict continuous SPT N-values and categorical soil profiles simultaneously at unsampled locations. The GWO algorithm optimized XGBoost hyperparameters including learning rate, maximum depth, estimator count, and L1/L2 regularization coefficients. The classical interpolation methods failed critically, with Inverse Distance Weighting (IDW) yielding R² = 0.1005 and Radial Basis Function (RBF) producing negative R² values. Optimized XGBoost improved performance to RMSE = 3.7072 and R² = 0.9315 and Random Forest achieved 87.32% soil classification accuracy and R² = 0.7601. The GWO–XGBoost model with Min-Max scaling confirmed strong generalization, attaining 100% primary soil type classification accuracy and R² = 0.9384, with a robust cross-validation score of 0.9095 ± 0.0195. The GWO–XGBoost framework offers a cost-effective tool with high-accuracy, for detailed subsurface characterization, which align with UN Sustainable Development Goals SDG 9 (Industry, Innovation and Infrastructure) focussing on target 9.1 (Resilient Infrastructure) as prediction would help in preventing failures due to complex environmental conditions and for designing resilient coastal infrastructure. Under SDG 9, target 9.4 (stainable Industrialization/Innovation) is addressed as Grey Wolf Optimization + XGBoost is a data-driven, innovative approach that improves sustainability and engineering efficiency compared to traditional field testing which is carbon-intensive.</p>','["Grey Wolf Optimizer, XGBoost, SPT N-value prediction"]',NULL,19,1,'1432 - 1447','10.59543/jidmis.v3.950','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',94),
(951,951,1,'Ai Powered Telemetry in Mobile Backbone Networks: Improving Route Stability and Performance Forecasting','<p>Due to cross-domain heterogeneity, unpredictable traffic patterns, and limited real-time visibility into network conditions, mobile backbone networks are increasingly experiencing performance degradation. This study presents a novel cross-domain AI-driven telemetry pipeline that facilitates intelligent, high-performance routing across the core network, transport, and radio access domains in order to address these issues. The suggested approach captures fine-grained network information, such as latency, link utilization, queue depth, and packet loss, in real time by combining streaming telemetry with a uniform cross-layer data aggregation paradigm. Using this telemetry, a lightweight AI-powered predictive routing engine forecasts congestion and uses adaptive path selection to dynamically improve routing choices. The suggested method greatly increases routing efficiency and network resilience by employing the Ant colony optimization (ACO) algorithm to provide improved proactive and context-aware traffic steering, in contrast to conventional reactive routing protocols. Within the mobile backbone, the innovation is found in the smooth integration of AI-driven decision intelligence and cross-domain telemetry. Experiments show significant gains in throughput stability, end-to-end latency, and resource usage, confirming the usefulness of the suggested framework for next-generation mobile networks.</p>','["Telemetry, Artificial Intelligence, Backbone Networks, Routing, ACO algorithm."]',NULL,19,1,'1448 - 1470','10.59543/jidmis.v3.951','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',95),
(952,952,1,'Entrepreneurial Resilience and Women''s Entrepreneurial Intention: Extending the Theory of Planned Behavior Through Risk Attitudes and Social Barriers','<p>Purpose: This research extends the Theory of Planned Behavior (TPB) by integrating entrepreneurial resilience (ER) as a main factor to define women’s entrepreneurial intention. <br/>This study explores how entrepreneurial resilience, attitudes toward entrepreneurial risk, and perceived social barriers influence entrepreneurial intention, it also explores the influence of entrepreneurial resilience on these relationships. <br/>Design/methodology/approach: This study used quantitative survey design from 658 women with entrepreneurial potential in Ho Chi Minh City, Vietnam. The measurement model was examined through Cronbach’s alpha, Exploratory Factor Analysis (EFA), and Confirmatory Factor Analysis (CFA). This research employed Structural Equation Modeling (SEM). <br/>Findings: This research reveals that Entrepreneurial Resilience positively transforms both entrepreneurial intention and attitude towards entrepreneurial risk. Attitudes toward Entrepreneurial Risk also has a positive effect on entrepreneurial intention, whereas Social Barriers act as an obstacle. Furthermore, Entrepreneurial Resilience has a greater impact among entrepreneurial risk attitudes and entrepreneurial intention.<br/>Originality/value: This study uses TPB and Entrepreneurial Resilience to examine women’s entrepreneurial intention. Entrepreneurial resilience helps women to start a business and improve their attitude toward entrepreneurial risk. The findings contribute education and policy for women''s entrepreneurship in emerging economies.</p>','["Entrepreneurial resilience", "Entrepreneurial intention", "Women entrepreneurs", "Social barriers", "Vietnam"]',NULL,19,1,'1471 - 1487','10.59543/jidmis.v3.952','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',96),
(953,953,1,'A Hybrid Framework for Joint Optimization of Resource Allocation and Load Balancing in Cloud Systems','<p>The backbone of modern digital machines is cloud computing′s on-demand resources, which scale to increase throughput. However, the field is increasingly complex from the point of view of heterogeneous cloud environments: requiring paradigms for virtual machines, containers and serverless computing nodes that put further challenges into ensuring an optimal performance/cost/energy balance. In traditional resource allocation and load balancing strategies, these goals are usually treated separately. As a result, there may be inadequate system utilization by either party involved in the transaction; ends responded with slow feedback time while intermediary services performed worse than predicted; or Service Level Agreements weren''t satisfied. To help meet these challenges, this paper proposes a Hybrid Framework for Joint Optimization of Resource Allocation and Load Balancing that spans two layers in heterogeneous cloud computing systems. The framework integrates Dynamic Voltage and Frequency Scaling (DVFS) with migration-aware task assignment, learning-aided metaheuristic optimization to dynamically adjust to fluctuations in load while minimizing power consumption and operational costs. The optimization engine blends predictive workload modelling implemented via machine learning for proactive decision-making and hybrid solution strategies. Among its multiple objectives, this mechanism offers the ability to jointly minimize SLA violations, response time as well power usage. Experiments using a larger CloudSim and actual workload traces (extended CloudSim) show that the proposed framework obtains 25-30% energy savings compared with ordinary methods, significantly reduces p95 latency and also achieves a relatively better Quality Of Service. These results suggest the framework may provide a sustainable schedule which responds to outside influences in real time, making it suitable for future intelligent cloud system with large-scale organization.</p>','["Zero-Day Attacks, Multi-Tenant Cloud Security, Variational Autoencoders, Anomaly Detection, Deep Learning, Intrusion Detection Systems, Cloud Computing,"]',NULL,19,1,'1488 - 1502','10.59543/jidmis.v3.953','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',97),
(954,954,1,'A Review on Artificial Intelligence-Driven Stock Market Forecasting: Advances, Challenges, and Future Directions','<p>This review aims at reviewing the emerging trend associated with the use of artificial intelligence (AI) to support financial forecasting in the stock market. Traditional methods for financial prediction such as technical analysis and time-series modeling have several flaws in terms of the inability to accurately estimate financial indicators in a nonlinear, highly volatile market environment. On the contrary, the use of machine learning-based approaches is characterized by higher efficiency and scalability. Several popular approaches such as deep learning models, reinforcement learning (adaptive trading strategy development), natural language processing for sentiment analysis, and machine learning-based algorithms can be used in stock market forecasts. Advantages and disadvantages of these techniques are critically analyzed, and the emphasis is placed on ways to overcome the limitations associated with traditional forecasting techniques and increase predictive performance. In specific, the quality of data, different information sources, data preprocessing procedures, as well as key metrics are considered for evaluating models and their performances. Specific cases will be discussed in detail, and current limitations, including interpretability, dependency on data, and model opacity, will be outlined. Finally, future trends for research in this area will be highlighted.</p>','["Artificial intelligence, Traditional forecasting techniques, Deep learning models, Trading strategies, Stock market forecasting, Multidisciplinary collaboration."]',NULL,19,1,'1503 - 1532','10.59543/jidmis.v3.954','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',98),
(955,955,1,'A Unified Multi-Source Deep Learning Framework for Cybersecurity Anomaly Detection Using Cross-Modal Attention and Behavioral Representation','<p>Traditionally, cybersecurity anomaly detection systems rely on only one source of data. However, these systems are no longer effective because modern cyber infrastructures are complex and versatile. This paper introduces a new technique called UMS, DLF: Unified Multi, Source Deep Learning Framework, which enables the fusion of multi, modal security data through cross, modal attention and behavioral representation learning. Initially, specialized feature extractors composed of convolutional layers for spatial flow patterns and temporal transformers for sequential dependencies are used to encode network flows, system log sequences, and user behavior profiles. Furthermore, a module for cross, modal attention fusion is proposed to dynamically assign weights to the inter, source correlations, thus preserving the essential contextual information across modalities. Continue reading to learn about how a behavioral embedding layer is used to capture the semantics of long, term user activities for robust anomaly characterization. Original paper evaluates the proposed method on cybersecurity benchmark datasets combined with the heterogeneous sources, such as UNSW, NB15 and CERT insider threat logs. Experimental results demonstrate that UMS-DLF achieves 97.84% detection accuracy, 96.91% precision, 97.36% recall, and an F1-score of 97.13%, outperforming conventional LSTM, CNN, and autoencoder baselines by 4.6–8.9% in F1 performance. Moreover, the model reduces false alarm rates to 2.1% and maintains real-time feasibility with an average inference latency of 18.4 ms per event. These results confirm that unified cross-modal learning significantly enhances anomaly detection reliability in complex cybersecurity environments.</p>','["Cybersecurity Anomaly, Multi-Source Learning, Cross-Modal Attention, Behavioral Analytics, Deep Fusion and Threat Detection."]',NULL,19,1,'1533 - 1553','10.59543/jidmis.v3.955','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',99),
(956,956,1,'Quantum-Resilient Lightweight Cryptography for Resource-Constrained IoT Devices','<p>Resource constraints on devices, to some extent, and the increasing threat posed by quantum computing both present tremendous challenges in improving effective security protocols for the Internet of Things (IoT). Herein we present the Quantum-Threat Lightweight Cipher (QTL-Cipher), a novel hybrid cryptographic algorithm for an Internet of Things ecosystem, in response to this challenge. The design was based on employing an asymmetric, post-quantum key generation mechanism and a lightweight symmetric module for confidentiality. The key exchange process utilized a unique, lattice-based method based on the Ring-Learning with Rounding problem, while the core symmetric module utilized a custom hardware efficient Feistel network structure. An important part of our approach involves a resource-aware scheduling paradigm that adjusts cryptographic parameters, for example Feistel rounds and S-box permutations, on-the-fly, based on device state parameters, for example battery level and processing load, to ensure the best possible device resource utilization without compromising security of any kind. Design and testing were conducted using a common IoT hardware platform, the ARM Cortex-M series microcontroller. Performance results indicated that our approach reduced energy consumption by 40% and RAM usage by 35% as compared to AES-256, Rigorous security analysis confirms the algorithm''s resilience against both classical and quantum attacks. This work concludes that the proposed QTL-Cipher design presents a robust, efficient, and quantum-resistant solution for ensuring data confidentiality in resource-constrained IoT applications.</p>','["Lightweight Cryptography, Internet of Things (IoT) Security, Post-Quantum Cryptography (PQC), Hybrid Cryptographic Algorithm, Resource-Aware Scheduling, Lattice-Based Cryptography."]',NULL,19,1,'1554 - 1580','10.59543/jidmis.v3.956','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',100),
(957,957,1,'Meta-Adaptive Neuro-Symbolic Causal Graph Transformer for Explainable Brain Tumor Segmentation and Progression Analysis','<p>Brain tumor segmentation from MRI remains a critical yet complex challenge in neuro-oncology due to tumor heterogeneity, ambiguous boundaries, and variability across imaging institutions. Although state-of-the-art deep learning techniques have been successful in achieving high levels of precision in brain tumor segmentation, there still are several limitations in terms of transparency, causal reasoning, and robustness of such systems which restrict their applicability in clinical decision support systems. Further, the majority of explainable AI strategies offer post-hoc visualizations, but do not offer either causal or temporal logic, limiting clinical confidence. In order to overcome these constraints, a Meta-Adaptive Neuro-Symbolic Causal Graph Transformer (MANS-GT) is suggested. The model combines the elements of deep neural perception, causal inference, and symbolic reasoning to provide the results of both proper interpretation and explainable progression analysis. The proposed framework employs a Hybrid CNN-Vision Transformer encoder for multimodal MRI feature extraction, a Multi-Scale Attention U-Net (MAU-Net) for tumor-region delineation, and a Graph Attention-based Structural Casual Model (GA-SCM) for modelling relationships among morphological and clinical variables. A Causal Neuro-Symbolic Reasoner (CNSR) is a neural embedding translator to generate chains of logical rules, which are computable by human experts and whose diagnostic reasoning can be interpreted by clinicians. A meta-adaptive learning layer that is a combination of Model-Agnostic Meta-Learning (MAML) and Domain Adaptation Normalization (DAN) is used to guarantee the generalization of cross-domain across institutions and imaging modalities. MANS-GT, implemented in Python 3.10 with PyTorch 2.2 and NVIDIA RTX 4090, has better Dice scores (ET: 90.3, TC: 92.1, WT: 90.4) and cross-dataset consistency. It is not only better at segmentation precision and causal interpretability but also offers clinically traceable reasoning of tumor progression to create a scalable and explainable AI paradigm of real-world neuro-oncological imaging.</p>','["Meta-Adaptive Learning, Neuro-Symbolic AI, Causal Graph Modeling, Transformer Architecture, Brain Tumor Segmentation"]',NULL,19,1,'1581 - 1611','10.59543/jidmis.v3.957','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',101),
(958,958,1,'Intelligent Decision Support for Diplomatic Quarter Planning Through Multi-Criteria Analysis and Urban Intelligence','<p>The decision-making process for designing a diplomatic quarter is complex as multiple factors such as architectural quality, security, sustainability, urban infrastructure, accessibility, resilience and diplomatic functions all need to be considered at the same time. In this study, an Artificial Intelligence (AI) - based Multicriteria Decision Making (MCDM) intelligent decision support framework for evidencebased planning of diplomatic quarters are presented. The solution involves an AI-powered data analytics that can preprocess complex and varied urban and building datasets to extract data influencing factors and minimize subjectivity in the evaluation. The Best-Worst Method (BWM) is used to obtain the criterion weights and the Combined Compromise Solution (CoCoSo) method is used to determine the planning scenarios rank. Proposed framework is tested with architectural, environmental and urban datasets that are available to the public. Comparative evaluation of the proposed BWM–CoCoSo framework with the other methods, AHP, TOPSIS and VIKOR shows that the BWM–CoCoSo framework is having highest expert agreement (92.8%), ranking stability under sensitivity analysis (94.1%) and robustness index (0.91) with low consistency ratio (0.046). AHP–TOPSIS, on the other hand, obtained expert agreement of 88.6%, robustness index of 0.84 and consistency ratio of 0.071 while VIKOR obtained expert agreement of 87.9% and robustness index of 0.82. The analysis of the results from the sensitivity analysis (which included an error of ±20% in the criterion weights) showed that the proposed framework was reliable, as the ranking of the items changed by only a few places. Combining urban intelligence and AI with the architectural and diplomatic evaluation creates a clear, scalable and objective decision-making support tool for planning secure, sustainable and internationally representative diplomatic quarters.</p>','["Diplomatic Quarter Planning, Architectural Planning, Urban Intelligence, Artificial Intelligence, Best-Worst Method (BWM), Combined Compromise Solution (CoCoSo)"]',NULL,19,1,'1612 - 1629','10.59543/jidmis.v3.958','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',102),
(959,959,1,'Meta-Learner Driven Deep Ensemble Learning for Real-Time Stock Market Prediction with Dynamic Technical and Fundamental Indicators','<p>Accurate stock price forecasting remains a critical challenge in financial risk management due to market non-linearity, regime-dependent feature importance, and the fundamental instability of widely-used hybrid architectures under rigorous evaluation. This paper proposes a Meta-Feature-based Deep Ensemble Learning (MF-DEL) framework and evaluates it under a strict chronological 80/20 temporal split with significance testing across 5 independent random seeds — an evaluation standard not applied in any reviewed prior work. Three coordinated contributions are presented: (1) a Dynamic Feature Engineering (DFE) module computing 15 technical and fundamental indicators, applying K-Means market-regime detection, and selecting the top-12 features by regime-aggregated mutual information; (2) a principled ensemble of RDELM (discriminative, closed-form) and HDB2NN (generative, hierarchical stochastic) base learners with complementary inductive biases; and (3) a Performance-Driven Meta-Learner dynamically weighting base model predictions. MF-DEL achieves mean R² = 0.9793 ± 0.0141 across 5 seeds, the highest mean performance among all multi-component ensemble models evaluated. The significance testing reveals three previously unreported findings: CNN-LSTM exhibits catastrophic instability (mean R² = −0.040 ± 0.623), CNN-GRU is even more unstable (mean R² = −0.054 ± 0.831), and Attention-LSTM — which achieves R² = 0.9991 on a single seed — collapses to mean R² = 0.616 ± 0.292 across seeds. Only MF-DEL and LSTM (0.9791 ± 0.0037) are consistently reliable. An ablation study confirms DFE as the dominant contributor, with its removal producing a 15-fold RMSE increase.</p>','["Stock Market Prediction", "Meta-Learner", "Dynamic Feature Engineering", "Boltzmann Neural Network", "Adaptive Regularization", "Reproducibility in Financial Forecasting"]',NULL,19,1,'1630 - 1655','10.59543/jidmis.v3.959','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',103),
(960,960,1,'Optimization Stability in Federated Learning under Non-IID Data: A Comparative Study of FedAvg and FedProx with Deep Convolutional Neural Network Architectures','<p>Federated learning (FL) has emerged as a prospective model training paradigm for distributed clients, which provides data privacy while maintaining the ability to train a collaborative model.In healthcare, finance and edge intelligence applications, federated learning (FL) is a promising model training paradigm for distributed clients, which preserve data privacy while retaining the capacity of collaboratively training a model. But, this optimization when data is not independent and identically distributed (non-IID), is still a great challenge because of the heterogeneity of clients and unstable model convergence. In this paper, we provide a thorough comparison between two well-known federated optimization algorithms, FedAvg and FedProx, and three popular deep convolutional neural network (DCNN) architectures such as ResNet18, VGG16 and VGG19. This study comprehensively assesses these models in a 10-client federated setting where data is partitioned into class-skewed non-IID data sets, by examining optimized stability, convergence and classification performance across 20 communication rounds. The experimental setup investigates how the proximal regularization can reduce the client drift and enhance the learning uniformity of the client datasets. The experimental results show that FedProx consistently shows better convergence stability and better predictive performance than FedAvg as the data becomes more and more heterogeneous, whereas ResNet18 provides a good trade-off between the model complexity, communication efficiency and classification performance. The results demonstrate that enforcing strong federated optimization coupled with fitting the appropriate deep convolutional architectures could provide a more reliable way of learning in decentralized settings. This work offers practical learnings for designing scalable, privacy-preserving and stable FL systems in real-world non-IID applications.</p>','["Federated Learning, Non-IID Data, FedAvg, FedProx, Deep Convolutional Neural Networks, Optimization Stability"]',NULL,19,1,'1656 - 1672','10.59543/jidmis.v3.960','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',104),
(961,961,1,'Automatic Chronic Kidney Disease Classification Using Stability- Guided Concrete Feature Selection and a Renal-Gated Sparse Tabular Transformer','<p>Chronic kidney disease (CKD) is a progressive condition that can lead to kidney failure if it is not detected and managed early. Automated CKD classification using structured clinical data can support early screening, but existing approaches often rely on latent feature extraction, recurrent models for non-sequential tabular data, or accuracy-focused tuning. This study proposes a Stability-Guided Concrete Feature Selection with Renal-Gated Sparse Tabular Transformer framework, named SGC-RGSTT, for automatic CKD classification. The proposed framework uses the UCI CKD dataset, which contains 400 patient records, 24 clinical input features, 250 CKD cases, and 150 non-CKD cases. Missing values are handled using training-only median and mode imputation, followed by categorical encoding and robust scaling. Stability-Guided Supervised Concrete Feature Selection is used to select original clinical variables instead of hidden latent features. The selected variables are then processed by a Renal-Gated Sparse Tabular Transformer, which represents each clinical variable as a feature token and learns feature interactions through sparse entmax attention and a SwiGLU feed-forward block. An Adaptive Quasi-Opposition Bayesian Differential Evolution optimizer tunes the feature-selection, classifier, and training hyperparameters using a fitness function based on AUC, recall, calibration error, feature compactness, and cross-validation stability. On the holdout test set, the proposed model achieved an accuracy of 0.975, precision of 0.980, recall of 0.980, specificity of 0.967, F1-score of 0.980, AUC of 0.994, MCC of 0.947, and ECE of 0.021. Repeated stratified cross-validation also showed stable performance, with AUC of 0.991 ± 0.011. Explainability analysis indicated that selected features such as hemoglobin, serum creatinine, packed cell volume, specific gravity, albumin, hypertension, and diabetes mellitus were consistent with clinically relevant CKD indicators. The results demonstrate the feasibility of the proposed framework under the evaluated UCI CKD dataset setting; however, external multi-center validation is required before clinical deployment.</p>','["Chronic kidney disease", "UCI CKD dataset", "Concrete feature selection", "Stability selection", "Tabular transformer", "Sparse entmax attention", "SwiGLU", "Hyperparameter optimization", "Calibration analysis", "Explainable artificial intelligence"]',NULL,19,1,'1673 - 1713','10.59543/jidmis.v3.961','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',105),
(962,962,1,'Hybrid Attention Convolutional Neural Network and Soft Sign Long Short-Term Memory Based Cyber-Attack Detection and Classification','<p>The extensive use of smart devices and several security weaknesses of networks has intensively enhanced the number of cyber-attacks in Internet of Things (IoT) networks. The detection and classification of malicious traffic is a key to ensure the security of those systems. It aims to identify the behaviors and patterns that deviate significantly from the norm, indicating potential cyberattacks. This paper proposed a hybrid attention Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) and Soft sign Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) for effective detection and classification. The min-max normalization is utilized in this experiment for data pre-processing and fed into Multi-Objective Sea Lion Optimization (MOSLO) based feature selection technique. Then, the selected features are given as input to the attention CNN and soft sign LSTM model. This model is estimated on NSL-KDD and ToN-IoT dataset and attains better results using accuracy, precision, recall, specificity, and F1-score. The obtained result shows that the proposed model achieves better accuracy of 99.45% on NSL-KDD dataset and 98.73% on ToN-IoT dataset which ensures accurate detection and classification compared to other existing methods like Feed Forward Neural Network (FFNN) and Improved Binary Golden Jackal Optimization-LSTM.</p>','["Convolutional Neural Network, Cyberattack, Internet of Things, Long Short-Term Memory, Multi-Objective Sea Lion Optimization."]',NULL,19,1,'1714 - 1727','10.59543/jidmis.v3.962','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',106),
(963,963,1,'DPNE-VVTPM (Dynamic Parallelized Noise-Enhanced Vector Valued Tree Parity Machine) for Secure Cybersecurity','<p>Securing key exchange over public channels is a foundational requirement of modern cybersecurity, yet classical schemes such as RSA, AES and DSA face growing computational and adversarial pressure. Neural cryptography with Tree Parity Machines (TPM) establishes shared keys through mutual learning, but existing Vector Valued TPM (VVTPM) designs suffer from slow synchronization, static parameterization and vulnerability to synchronization following attacks. This paper proposes DPNE-VVTPM, a Dynamic Parallelized Noise Enhanced VVTPM framework that integrates four cooperating mechanisms: an error driven dynamic weight range schedule, secret Gaussian noise injection during synchronization, parallel synchronization of multiple TPM lanes, and SHA-256 based high entropy key derivation. Simulation studies show that dynamic range scheduling reduces mean synchronization effort from 2128 to 188 iterations, an 11.3 times acceleration, while noise injection lowers the weight agreement achieved by a geometric eavesdropper from 33.2 percent to 19.3 percent. The derived 256 bit session keys attain 0.997 bits per bit Shannon entropy, 99.0 percent monobit and 99.7 percent runs test pass rates, and a 50.06 percent avalanche effect. End to end benchmarking against RSA, AES and DSA records the highest throughput of 3237708.35 bits per second, the lowest encryption time of 2.72e-05 seconds and decryption time of 5.48e-06 seconds, with marginally lower memory usage, confirming DPNE-VVTPM as an efficient and resilient key exchange primitive.</p>','["Neural cryptography, adversarial attacks, Vector Valued Tree Parity Machines, key exchange, noise injection, cybersecurity."]',NULL,19,1,'1728 - 1751','10.59543/jidmis.v3.963','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',107),
(964,964,1,'Energy-Efficient Routing Protocols in Flying Ad Hoc Networks: A Comprehensive Review','<p>Flying Ad Hoc Networks (FANETs) have become an important research area because of their ability to provide communication between unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs) in a wide range of applications such as military operations, agriculture, environmental monitoring, and smart transportation. However, the limited battery capacity of UAVs and the high dynamicity of FANETs make energy-efficient routing a great challenge. An efficient routing protocol should not only be energy efficient but also provide reliable communication, improve packet delivery, reduce delay, and increase network lifetime. Recently, many energy-efficient routing protocols have been proposed by researchers based on different approaches such as clustering, swarm intelligence, reinforcement learning, fuzzy logic, and hybrid techniques. Each approach has its own advantages and limitations depending on the network environment and application requirements. This paper offers an in-depth review of energy-efficient routing protocols that have been developed for FANETs. The reviewed protocols are analyzed according to their parameters used in the study, the simulator used, advantages, and limitations. We also provide comparisons that point out the strengths and weaknesses of existing approaches. In addition, this review highlights the main research challenges, such as high mobility, dynamic topology, routing overhead, scalability, and security, and discusses future research directions to design more intelligent and energy-aware routing protocols. The present review results and conclusions offer researchers a better understanding of current trends, and they also point out potential future research directions in energy-efficient FANET routing.</p>','["flying ad hoc networks, fanet, energy, energy-efficient, routing protocols, UAV."]',NULL,19,1,'1752 - 1771','10.59543/jidmis.v3.964','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',108),
(969,969,1,'Hybrid Analytical, Numerical, and Machine Learning Approaches on Decision-Making, Risk Management, Operational Stability, and Uncertainty Analysis in Nepalese Management Systems','<p>Nepalese management systems operate within a uniquely constrained environment characterized by extreme Himalayan topography, high seismic vulnerability, remittance-dependent economic flows, and infrastructural bottlenecks. This paper presents a unified hybrid framework that synthesizes analytical stochastic control theory, high-fidelity numerical simulation, and modern machine learning surrogates to address decision-making, risk management, operational stability, and uncertainty quantification in this context. We develop a coupled mathematical architecture comprising stochastic differential equations for operational dynamics, Markov decision processes for sequential policy optimization, coherent risk measures (Conditional Value-at-Risk) for financial and logistical risk management, and polynomial chaos expansions coupled with Bayesian neural networks for uncertainty propagation. The framework is instantiated through four Nepalese case studies: post-earthquake humanitarian logistics, hydropower project risk assessment, financial inclusion banking stability, and tourism supply chain resilience. Our results demonstrate that the hybrid analytical-numerical-ML paradigm reduces epistemic uncertainty by up to 62% compared to pure data-driven approaches while preserving physical interpretability and Lyapunov stability guarantees. The proposed methodology offers a rigorous, computationally tractable foundation for data-informed policy design in complex, uncertainty-dominated developing economies.</p>','["Stochastic optimization, uncertainty quantification, Markov decision processes, polynomial chaos expansion, Bayesian neural networks, operational stability, risk management, Nepal."]',NULL,19,1,'1772 - 1793','10.59543/jidmis.v3.969','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',109),
(970,970,1,'Hybrid Analytical Numerical and Machine Learning Frameworks for Solving Deterministic and Stochastic Differential Equations with Stability, Convergence, and Uncertainty Quantification','<p>Differential equations underpin the modeling of dynamical systems across physics, engineering, biology, and finance. While deterministic ordinary and partial differential equations (ODEs/PDEs) describe systems governed by known physical laws, stochastic differential equations (SDEs) incorporate randomness to represent uncertainty, noise, and unresolved scales. Classical analytical methods are limited to restricted problem classes, and although numerical discretization provide general applicability, they can become computationally demanding for nonlinear, high-dimensional, stiff, or multiscale systems. In stochastic settings, accurate estimation further requires large ensembles of sample paths, amplifying computational cost. Conversely, purely data-driven machine learning (ML) approaches offer expressive approximation capabilities but often lack physical consistency, stability guarantees, and reliable generalization. This work introduces a unified hybrid analytical–numerical–ML framework for deterministic and stochastic differential equations. The approach integrates (i) analytical structure and prior knowledge (e.g., conservation laws and invariants), (ii) stable numerical discretization’s serving as computational backbones and multi-fidelity supervision sources, and (iii) physics-guided learning components acting as correction operators or drift–diffusion estimators. Governing equations and boundary/initial conditions are embedded directly into the learning objective, while stability constraints are enforced to preserve numerical robustness. An explicit error decomposition separates discretization, sampling, optimization, and generalization contributions, and sufficient conditions for stable and convergent hybrid approximations are derived. Numerical experiments on representative PDE and SDE benchmarks demonstrate improved accuracy and stability over backbone-only and ML-only baselines. The proposed framework provides a principled pathway toward physically consistent, scalable, and uncertainty-aware solvers for complex dynamical systems.</p>','["Hybrid Analytical Numerical, Stochastic Differential Equations, substantial advances, Moment bounds quantify."]',NULL,19,1,'1794 - 1849','10.59543/jidmis.v3.970','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',110),
(971,971,1,'Machine Learning–Augmented Hybrid Risk Management and Deep Uncertainty Quantification in Nepalese Management Systems: Fractional Stochasticity, Wasserstein Robustness, and Rough–Path Neural Filtering','<p>Risk management in Nepal has never been a matter of applying textbook formulas to Himalayan data. The country’s management systems—spanning hydropower consortia in Gandaki, microfinance networks in the Terai, tourism supply chains in Solu-Khumbu, and federal bureaucracies still finding their footing after the 2015 constitutional rupture—operate under uncertainty that standard stochastic calculus refuses to acknowledge. Monsoon persistence stretches across seasons with long-range dependence that Markovian models cannot capture. Remittance corridors from Qatar and Malaysia collapse and reopen with pathwise irregularity that violates the semimartingale assumption underlying Itô calculus. Political transitions—from monarchy to insurgency to federal republic—have produced institutional trajectories of such unbounded variation that classical risk filters simply disintegrate. And through all of this, the data available to Nepalese risk managers is sparse, patchy, and structurally compromised by conflict, earthquake, and repeated administrative reorganization.<br/>This paper constructs a comprehensive theoretical framework for machine learning–augmented hybrid risk management and deep uncertainty quantification specifically calibrated to these conditions. We develop four interconnected mathematical pillars. First, neural fractional stochastic risk processes driven by fractional Brownian motion with Hurst exponent H≠1⁄2, where recurrent neural architectures estimate long-memory parameters from incomplete time series and correct the systematic underestimation of tail risk inherent in classical value-at-risk. Second, machine learning–adaptive Wasserstein distributionally robust risk measures, where neural networks learn the shape and radius of ambiguity sets from sparse Nepalese data, yielding tractable worst-case expectations via strong Lagrangian duality. Third, rough–path neural filtering, which combines Lyons’ signature theory with deep learning to track irregular risk cascades across institutional networks whose paths are too rough for standard stochastic integration. Fourth, deep ensemble uncertainty quantification, layering Bayesian neural networks with causal priors into second-order imprecise probability hierarchies that distinguish aleatory, epistemic, and ontological uncertainty.<br/>Every structural claim is accompanied by formal definitions, numbered equations, and rigorous proofs. We derive the exact fractional value-at-risk correction for seasonal horizons, prove strong duality for the learned Wasserstein ambiguity set, establish continuity of the neural rough differential equation solution map in the Hölder topology, and decompose predictive variance into its three fundamental constituents. The paper is theoretical and unpublished; no empirical calibration is attempted, though the mathematical specifications are sufficiently tight to guide implementation. All references carry verified DOIs..</p>','["machine learning, hybrid risk management, deep uncertainty quantification, fractional Brownian motion, Wasserstein distance, distributionally robust optimization, rough path theory, neural filtering, Bayesian neural networks, Nepalese management systems, long-memory processes, imprecise probability."]',NULL,19,1,'1850 - 1873','10.59543/jidmis.v3.971','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',111),
(972,972,1,'Global Cybersecurity Governance: Challenges in Harmonizing International Cyber Laws','<p>The exponential rise in cyber threats has intensified the imperative for a coherent global cybersecurity governance architecture. Yet, attempts to harmonize international cyber laws face persistent challenges stemming from conflicting national interests, heterogeneous legal traditions, and disparate technological capabilities. This study critically investigates the barriers inhibiting the harmonization of cyber laws across jurisdictions and explores viable pathways toward a unified global cybersecurity governance framework. The research is anchored in Regime Theory, propounded by Stephen D. Krasner in 1982, and Global Governance Theory, advanced notably by James N. Rosenau and Ernst- Otto Czempiel in 1992, both of which provide theoretical scaffolding to analyze international cooperation among states and non-state actors in managing complex transnational issues. Employing a qualitative research design, the study utilizes document analysis of global cybersecurity treaties, national cyber legislation, and multilateral policy documents. The findings underscore three critical impediments to harmonization: legal fragmentation, normative divergences, and entrenched geopolitical rivalries. Despite these obstacles, the study identifies the emergent roles of regional alliances (e.g., the European Union, African Union) and multistakeholder forums (e.g., IGF, GFCE) as promising conduits for normative convergence and collaborative capacity-building. To navigate the current disjointed cyber legal landscape, the study recommends: strategic capacity development to address technological and institutional disparities; standardization of cybercrime definitions and enforcement protocols; and enhanced mandate and legitimacy for neutral global institutions such as the United Nations (UN) and International Telecommunication Union (ITU) to serve as mediators of consensus..</p>','["cybersecurity", "global governance", "cyber law", "challenges, harmonization"]',NULL,19,1,'1874 - 1893','10.59543/jidmis.v3.972','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',112),
(973,973,1,'Real-Time PM2.5 Forecasting Using Lightweight Ensemble Learning and Temporal Feature Fusion','<p>Accurate and prompt prediction of PM2.5 concentration is crucial to reduce the impacts of air pollution on human health and city ecosystems. In this study, a hybrid ensemble learning model for hourly PM2.5 predictions is proposed, combining advanced data preprocessing, temporal feature engineering, and heterogeneous regression models, which can be computed efficiently. The preprocessing step cleans the data set by discarding incomplete target records, fills the missing meteorological data with median statistics, encodes categorical wind directions and normalizes numerical features. The model learns the temporal dependencies by using several lag variables and several rolling statistical features. The prediction model is an equal weight ensemble of CatBoost, Ridge, Lasso, and ElasticNet regression models; these models are added together to take advantage of their ability to learn non-linearly, regularize their coefficients, and deal with sparsity and correlated features, without the complexity of stacked meta-learning. The proposed approach has been validated by comprehensive experiments, conducted on the benchmark data from Beijing PM2.5, by using ablation analysis, seasonal evaluation, and feature importance assessment. The framework performs well in terms of RMSE (26.80), MAE (18.45), R² (0.892), IA (97.65%) and MAPE (0.535), outperforming other deep learning models such as LSTM, GRU, CNN-LSTM, DAQFF and FPHFA with significantly less computational costs. The results prove its reliability for efficient, easily interpretable and real-time air quality forecasting applications.</p>','["Air Quality Forecasting, PM2.5 Prediction, Hybrid Ensemble Learning, Feature Engineering, Environmental Data Analytics"]',NULL,19,1,'1894 - 1917','10.59543/jidmis.v3.973','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',113),
(6138,6138,1,'Mechanical and Microstructural Properties of Recycled Aggregate Concrete with Partial Replacement of Cement by Micro Silica','<p>Recycling construction and demolition (C&amp;D) waste as aggregate conserves natural resources and reduces the land needed for landfill, while replacing part of the cement with an industrial by-product lowers the environmental burden of concrete. Recycled coarse aggregate (RCA), produced by crushing and screening demolished concrete, is weaker and more absorptive than natural aggregate because of the old mortar attached to it. This study examines whether micro silica can offset these drawbacks. M20 concrete was made with natural coarse aggregate (NCA) and with 25%, 50% and 75% of it replaced by RCA; the 50% replacement gave the highest strength and was adopted for the remaining mixes. Two micro silica mixes were then prepared with 50% RCA: one with 15% of the cement replaced by micro silica (MSR) and one with micro silica added at 15% by weight of cement (MSA). Compressive, splitting tensile and flexural strengths were measured at 7 and 28 days, and the microstructure was examined by X-ray diffraction (XRD) and scanning electron microscopy (SEM). The MSA mix gave the highest 28-day strengths: 42 N/mm2 in compression, 3.6 N/mm2 in splitting tension and 4.8 N/mm2 in flexure, against 30, 2.4 and 3.4 N/mm2 for the NCA control. XRD showed lower calcium hydroxide peak intensities in the micro silica mixes, and SEM showed a denser, more uniform matrix. The results indicate that, with micro silica, 50% of the natural coarse aggregate in M20 concrete can be replaced by RCA without loss of strength.</p>','["Recycled coarse aggregate", "Micro silica", "Com-pressive strength", "Splitting tensile strength", "Flexural strength", "X-ray diffraction", "Scanning electron microscopy"]',NULL,19,1,'1918 - 1936',NULL,'The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-15',114),
(6139,6139,1,'A Study on Flexural Behaviour of Reinforced Concrete Slabs Using Recycled Aggregates with Partial Replacement of Cement by Micro Silica','<p>Recycled coarse aggregate (RCA) produced from demolished concrete can reduce both the demand for natural aggregate and the volume of construction and demolition waste sent to landfill, but its use in structural members requires evidence of structural performance, not only of material strength. This study evaluates M25 concrete in which 50% of the natural coarse aggregate was replaced by RCA, with and without 15% of the cement replaced by micro silica (MSR), for use in two-way roof slabs. Compressive, splitting tensile and flexural strengths were measured at 7 and 28 days. Two-way slabs measuring 1400 mm × 1280 mm were then cast and tested under a simulated uniformly distributed load with two boundary conditions: all four edges continuous and all four edges discontinuous. At 28 days the MSR mix reached 35 N/mm2 in compression, comparable to the control (34 N/mm2) and RCA (36 N/mm2) mixes, and gave the highest splitting tensile (3.4 N/mm2) and flexural (4.5 N/mm2) strengths, although its 7-day strengths were the lowest. Up to a load of 8 kN, the deflections of both slab types increased linearly with load. In the continuous slabs, RCA did not change the initial stiffness (about 97 kN/mm) but reduced the maximum load by about 23%; in the discontinuous slabs, it reduced the initial stiffness from 143 to 75 kN/mm while the maximum load remained about 15 kN. Concrete with 50% RCA can therefore be considered for lightly loaded two-way roof slabs, provided the lower stiffness of discontinuous panels is allowed for in design.</p>','["Recycled coarse aggregate", "Micro silica", "Two-way slab", "Flexural behaviour", "Load\\u2013deflection", "Compressive strength", "Flexural strength"]',NULL,19,1,'1937 - 1952',NULL,'The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-15',115),
(594,594,1,'Two-Stage 3D U-Net-Based Segmentation Of Maxillofacial Structures From CBCT Scans For Dental Implant Planning','<p>This work represents a 3D segmentation framework for automatic landmark delineation in Cone Beam Computed Tomography to aid dental implant planning. The proposed method aimed to segment important anatomical details like bone, nerve canals, and teeth using the Bone segmentation algorithm, Deep Neural Network Based Minimal Medical segmentation, and Tooth segmentation method, respectively. The bone segmentation algorithm utilizes two-stage 3D U-Net architecture where coarse global segmentation combines with fine segmentation for accurate detection of anatomical structures like tooth, alveolar ridge, maxillary sinus and nasal cavity. Preprocessing of CBCT images is done preceding to segmentation to remove poisonous noise using PNR algorithm, Thermal noise using TFR algorithm and ring artifacts with RAR algorithm. The regions of interest identified in segmentation phase leads to exact measurements of important anatomical structures that further assists in automation of medical report generation system. The assessment of segmentation algorithms is done using parameters such as DICE-Score and IoU. Our proposed system achieved 0.96, 0.97, 0.92, 0.96 and 0.99 DICE-Score values for mandible, maxilla, nerve canal, teeth and background respectively as well as 0.93, 0.94, 0.86, 0.93 and 0.99 IoU score values for mandible, maxilla, nerve canal, teeth and background respectively.</p>','["Dental Implant planning", "CBCT", "3D U-Net", "Segmentation", "Medical Imaging", "Deep learning", "Tooth segmentation", "Bone segmentation", "Machine Learning"]',NULL,18,1,'01 - 25','10.59543/jidmis.v3.594','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',1),
(595,595,1,'AI-Driven Detection, Segmentation, and Classification of Medicinal Plant Leaves Using Computer Vision and Deep Learning','<p>Ayurvedic medicines play a significant role in maintaining the physical and mental health of people. Currently, these medicines are widely adopted to cure various diseases. Therefore, precise detection and classification of medicinal crops are critical for ensuring effective treatments. However, the scarcity of experts in this field poses a challenge, making the process of detecting and classifying herbal plants both complex and time-consuming. To address this issue, a fully automatic system for medicinal plant classification is much required. Currently, Computer Vision (CV) and Machine Learning (ML)/Deep Learning (DL) based systems have gained attention to provide automated detection and classification solutions for these types of problems i.e. detection, segmentation and classification. Therefore, this study adopts this concept and proposes a comprehensive framework for medicinal plant leaf classification, integrating state-of-the-art DL and feature extraction techniques. Initially, data augmentation methods such as rotation, flipping, scaling, and color jittering are applied to enhance dataset diversity and improve model generalization. In next stage, image segmentation task is applied by utilizing advanced deep learning Yolov8 mode which helps to accurately extract leaf regions from complex backgrounds. The segmented images are then analysed to extract features using pre-trained VGG16 deep learning models, which are further enriched with handcrafted texture, color, and shape features to ensure robust representation. These diverse feature sets are fused and classified using an ensemble of ML classifiers, including Random Forest (RF), Support Vector Machine (SVM), and Gradient Boosting (GB). The proposed system achieves classification accuracies of 87.65%, 83.20%, and 89.32% for RF, SVM, and GB, respectively. Experimental results demonstrate the system''s effectiveness in achieving good accuracy and reliability in medicinal plant leaf classification. This approach provides a significant step toward automating plant identification, promoting the efficient use of Ayurvedic medicinal resources, and reducing dependency on domain experts.</p>','["Medicinal Plant Leaf detection, segmentation, classification, deep learning, computer vision."]',NULL,18,1,'26 - 40','10.59543/jidmis.v3.595','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',2),
(629,629,1,'A Deep Learning and YOLO-Based Hybrid Framework for Accurate Classification and Region-of-Interest Detection of Maize Leaf Diseases','<p>Maize leaf diseases are also major causes of loss in the productivity of the crop, and they are also visually similar to each other, which frequently causes a misclassification under manual inspection. The paper fills this gap of the necessity of a practical and automated solution by coming up with a hybrid of deep learning frameworks with the ability to not only classify diseases with high accuracy but also localize region of interest (ROI) with high precision. The aim is to strengthen the early detection with an integrated system that is comprised of both the advanced models of transfer learning and the YOLO-based detection. The dataset is processed systematically to enhance normalization, preprocessing, and augmentation of the dataset to enhance its strength in a variety of imaging conditions. Several deep learning models VGG16, ResNet50, InceptionV3 and MobileNetV2 and lightweight and ensemble versions were tested. The results indicate that Ensemble Model 1 (VGG16 + MobileNetV2) performs the best, with the accuracy of 96% and the precision, recall, and F1-score of 95%, 95%, and 95%, respectively, being higher than individual classifiers and Ensemble Model 2 do. The ability to mix complementary feature extraction tasks of deep and lightweight networks is seen to be powerful in this superior performance. YOLO component is highly effective in detecting diseased regions on the leaf with high confidence identified and produces clear and understandable bound-box results. Results provide validation that combining classification and object detection has benefits on the diagnostic reliability and decision-making capability which can be used for actionable decisions in agriculture. The area of their activity is also the real-time deployment, applicability to cross crops, and integration with mobile or IoT-based smart farming systems, which allows extending the disease management solutions to scale and efficiency.</p>','["Deep Learning Classification, Ensemble Model Optimization, YOLO ROI Detection, Maize Leaf Disease Analysis, Smart Agriculture Systems"]',NULL,18,1,'41 - 67','10.59543/jidmis.v3.629','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',3);
INSERT INTO publications (id,submission_id,version,title,abstract,keywords,references_text,issue_id,section_id,pages,doi,copyright_holder,copyright_year,license_url,status,date_published,seq) VALUES
(628,628,1,'Job Demands, Job Insecurity, and Subjective Well-Being among Creative Freelancers: The Moderating Role of Family Support','<p>Drawing on the Job Demands–Resources theory, this study examines how job demands and job insecurity shape subjective well-being among creative freelancers in Indonesia’s creative economy. It further investigates family support as an informal resource that may buffer the adverse effects of demanding and insecure project-based work. Using a quantitative cross-sectional design, data were collected from 333 creative freelancers in Indonesia who were actively engaged in one of the creative industry subsectors, had worked within the recent freelance market, and had completed multiple freelance projects. The data were analysed using partial least squares structural equation modelling.<br/>The findings show that job demands and job insecurity negatively affect subjective well-being, highlighting the psychological costs of creative freelancers work arrangements that depend on autonomy, client-based projects, and income uncertainty. Family support does not significantly moderate the relationship between job demands and subjective well-being, but it significantly moderates the relationship between job insecurity and subjective well-being. These results suggest that family support functions more effectively as a resource for coping with employment uncertainty than for reducing the strain of work intensity. This study contributes to the literature on entrepreneurial work, creative labour, and well-being by extending the Job Demands–Resources perspective to freelancers operating outside formal organizational structures.</p>','["Job Demands\\u2013Resources theory, creative freelancers, job demands, job insecurity, family support, subjective well-being"]',NULL,18,1,'68 - 86','10.59543/jidmis.v3.628','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',4),
(631,631,1,'Comparative Study of Machine Learning and CNN for Writer Trait Prediction from Handwritten Hindi Script','<p>In this research, a framework based on handwriting is presented to automatically predict writer traits using classical models and a deep CNN. A handwriting dataset from various writers is used to predict three traits: age group, gender, and anxiety. The KNN and SVM are used to train the models using handcrafted features to show the performance of the classical models. A deep CNN is also presented to learn the representation of the handwriting.  The CNN achieves 91%, 92%, and 90% accuracy compared to the classical models. The ablation study also shows the contribution of the presented models to the performance of the framework. The results of the presented framework show that the CNN is a more effective solution to the problem of writer trait prediction as compared to the classical models. The results also show the significance of the behavioural cues embedded in handwriting.</p>','["Handwriting", "Classification", "Features", "Deep Learning", "Writer Traits."]',NULL,18,1,'87 - 106','10.59543/jidmis.v3.631','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',5),
(632,632,1,'Comparative Analysis of Artificial Intelligence Test Generation Methods for Compliance Validation in Regulated Enterprise Workflows','<p>In regulated industries, such as insurance, healthcare, and finance, which often have changing policies and complex rules with multiple steps, manual or scripted validation will make compliance testing particularly difficult. Managing regulatory requirements while ensuring that traditional techniques can provide complete test coverage for regulatory use cases can be challenging without the latest Artificial Intelligence (AI) technologies and automated test generation. This paper aims to explore the related research question conceptually; it proposes three approaches for using AI: directly prompting the LLM, using its rules extracted via the rule decomposition method, and using rules to verify the LLM’s performance. The representative regulatory logic patterns were developed from a class of anti-money laundering (AML), Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA), access control and dependencies, and insurance decision paths, as well as public-sector procurement workflows, to identify which conditions it was unlikely that each logic would be attempting to learn, how it might be catching exceptions, and what test cases they would try for each condition. This comparison gives a glimpse into the four known problems related to LLMs and constraint-based testing that have been featured in previous papers: regulatory coverage, boundary handling, logical consistency, and redundancy. The results of this study suggest a need for greater flexibility in applying the scenario from the LLM’s prompting perspective. However, the rules underlying the hybrid are logical and seem to provide complete coverage, and the rules themselves offer extra transparency into the structured restrictions. The conceptual results have implications for the implementation of AI-powered compliance testing within organizations and for additional avenues of empirical testing.</p>','["Large Language Models", "Rule Decomposition, Hybrid Artificial Intelligence Techniques", "Regulatory Compliance Validation", "Public-Sector Procurement", "Procurement Compliance, Supply Chain Governance", "Source to Contract"]',NULL,18,1,'107 - 123','10.59543/jidmis.v3.632','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',6),
(633,633,1,'Automated Identification of Negative Intent In Hindi Social Media Posts Using NLP Techniques','<p>The rapid growth of Hindi social media communication has increased the need for automated systems that can identify negative intent expressed through hate speech, threats, abusive language, and discriminatory content. Manual review is not scalable because user posts are short, noisy, morphologically varied, and frequently written in Devanagari script. This paper develops an end-to-end Natural Language Processing algorithm for binary identification of negative and non-negative Hindi social media posts. The proposed pipeline applies Unicode NFC normalization, URL and mention removal, Devanagari filtering, tokenization, stopword removal, word and character n-gram TF-IDF extraction, lexicon-based negative sentiment scoring, and contextual classification using a fine-tuned MuRIL transformer. The model is evaluated on a 1,000-post annotated dataset with an 80/20 stratified train-test split and compared with Logistic Regression, Random Forest, Linear SVM, and SVM with TF-IDF plus lexicon features. Results show that the fine-tuned MuRIL model obtains 0.923 accuracy, 0.922 F1-score, and 0.967 AUC on the 200-post test set, outperforming the strongest classical baseline by 0.050 absolute F1 points. Confusion-matrix analysis shows 157 correctly detected negative posts, 26 correctly detected non-negative posts, 9 false positives, and 8 false negatives. The findings indicate that transformer-based contextual representations are more effective than sparse features alone for Hindi negative intent detection, while lexicon features remain useful for improving interpretable classical baselines.</p>','["Hindi NLP", "Negative Intent Detection", "MuRIL", "TF-IDF", "Social Media", "Hate Speech", "Devanagari"]',NULL,18,1,'124 - 134','10.59543/jidmis.v3.633','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',7),
(634,634,1,'Creative CRAFT 2.0: A Refined, Creativity-Centered Prompt Engineering Framework for Human-AI Co-Creation','<p>The increasing integration of generative artificial intelligence (AI) into academic, professional, and creative work has intensified the need for effective prompt engineering strategies. Existing approaches largely prioritize logical reasoning, task efficiency, or automation, often overlooking creativity, contextual sensitivity, and human intent. This study presents Creative CRAFT 2.0, a refined, second-generation prompt engineering framework that reconceptualizes prompting as a structured yet creative act of human–AI co-creation. Building on earlier exploratory dissemination through a non-indexed outlet, this article offers a substantially reworked framework with new theoretical grounding, refined component definitions, and an IMRAD-aligned scholarly presentation. Using a design-oriented methodology and illustrative comparative data, the framework is examined across academic and creative prompting scenarios. Creative CRAFT 2.0 consists of six modular components - Context, Role, Action/Task, Format, Tone/Process/Constraints, and Creative Direction, organized in a non-linear structure that emphasizes intentional completeness rather than fixed sequencing. Results demonstrate consistent improvements in relevance, coherence, creativity, and audience alignment. The study positions Creative CRAFT 2.0 as a practical contribution to prompt engineering, AI literacy, and creativity-centered human–AI collaboration.</p>','["prompt engineering", "generative AI", "creativity frameworks", "human\\u2013AI collaboration", "Creative CRAFT 2.0"]',NULL,18,1,'135 - 141','10.59543/jidmis.v3.634','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',8),
(635,635,1,'Plant Leaf Disease Detection Using Machine Learning and Deep Learning: A Review and Experimental Study','<p>India’s economy is primarily based on agriculture. Agriculture has significant contribution in nation’s GDP. Food security and employment significantly influenced by agriculture. However factors like uncertain weather conditions, poor quality of seeds and plant diseases impact on agriculture productivity. Computer vision and DL algorithms are most crucial components of precision agriculture. Early detection can improve decision making, maximize pesticide use, and preserve harvests. Using CNN architectures, segmentation-based approaches, handcrafted feature-based methods, and hybrid approaches incorporating Machine Learning and Deep Learning this study seek to provide review of recent publications from 2020 to 2026. The review was carried out using a variety of publications with different datasets, methodologies, and outcomes. The findings show that DL, especially CNN and transfer learning models, performed better than machine learning techniques. It points out several significant problems, such as dataset imbalance, insufficient generalization, computing inefficiency, and a dearth of real-world data. Future research topics are also suggested which includes IoT-driven real-time solutions, lightweight architecture, domain adaption, and multimodal imaging. This review aims to develop plant disease detection technologies that are more dependable, scalable, and field deployable.</p>','["Plant Disease Detection, Deep Learning (DL), CNN, Machine Learning (ML), Agriculture Technology."]',NULL,18,1,'142 - 153','10.59543/jidmis.v3.635','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',9),
(636,636,1,'WILO-BiLSTM: Optimization Based Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory Model for Intelligent Virtual Assistant System','<p>In the field of Natural Language Processing (NLP), intelligent virtual assistance systems or information retrieval systems have earned significant attention in recent years as they assist users and solve their queries by providing accurate answers. However, numerous studies were developed for intelligent virtual assistance systems, but they face limitations like multilingual aspects, ambiguity resolution, lack of consistency in answers, complex language structures and linguistic variations. Hence, to tackle these constraints, this research proposed an intelligent virtual assistant system using Wolf Interactive Learning Optimization-based Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory (WILO-BiLSTM) model. The integration of BiLSTM in the model enhances the understanding of complex questions and captures semantic information, resulting in improved model performance. In addition, it processes sequential data effectively and overcomes gradient problems in long sequences. Moreover, the exploitation of the WILO algorithm in the model optimizes BiLSTM hyperparameters and significantly enhances the convergence speed with reduced computational cost. Notably, the experimental outcomes reveal that the proposed WILO-BiLSTM model can perform superior to the conventional approaches and its performance results in terms of METEOR, BLEU, ROUGE, and SPICE score at training data 90% is 0.28, 0.50, 0.56, and 26.93 for the SquAD dataset, respectively.</p>','["Intelligent virtual assistant systems, Deep Learning, Question answering, Hybrid optimization, Natural language processing (NLP)."]',NULL,18,1,'154 - 182','10.59543/jidmis.v3.636','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',10),
(637,637,1,'Fuzzy Graph Models for Cybersecurity: Intelligent Detection, Risk Analysis, and Mitigation of Network Threats','<p>Cybersecurity threats are growing in complexity and frequency, requiring adaptive and intelligent mechanisms for threat detection and mitigation. Traditional graph-based models often fail to cap-ture the uncertainty and ambiguity present in real-world network environments. Fuzzy graph theory, which introduces degrees of un-certainty into the structure of graphs, offers a robust mathematical framework to model and analyze imprecise data in cybersecurity con-texts. This paper explores the application of fuzzy graphs in detecting and mitigating network threats such as intrusions, phishing, and dis-tributed denial-of-service (DDoS) attacks. We present methodologies for constructing fuzzy graphs from network data, identifying anoma-lies through fuzzy similarity and centrality measures, and implement-ing mitigation strategies using fuzzy-based decision-making and game theory. A case study on phishing detection using fuzzy clustering tech-niques demonstrates the practical effectiveness of this approach. Our study highlights the potential of fuzzy graph-based models in enhanc-ing adaptive security mechanisms in modern cyber infrastructures.</p>','["Fuzzy Graphs, Cybersecurity, Threat Detection, Network Miti-gation, Fuzzy Logic"]',NULL,18,1,'183 - 194','10.59543/jidmis.v3.637','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',11),
(638,638,1,'Transformer-Based Topic Modeling and Sentiment Analysis for Multilingual Text Data','<p>Several new trends related to the rising volume of multilingual digital content have spurred a greater need for Natural Language Processing (NLP) systems capable of capturing the latent themes and understanding sentiment without stripping out the structure within a specific language. In this study, we introduce an integrated framework based on transformer architecture for topic modeling and sentiment analysis for Hindi and Italian social-media discourse, customer reviews and news corpus. The framework integrates language-aware representations in context, the sentiment classification system of supervised learning and the BERTopic-based topic discovery system. Hindi Processing focuses on Devanagari normalization, transliteration, mixed code and Hindi-English while Italian Processing focuses on inflectional, clitic morphology, colloquial and regionalization variation. In addition to classical baselines (Support Vector Machines (SVM), Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks, and Latent Dirichlet Allocation (LDA)), MuRIL, UmBERTo, AlBERTo and LLaMAntino are studied. The performance is assessed descriptively in terms of accuracy, F1 Score, topic coherence (Cᵥ), and training behavior and out of distribution error analysis. On sentiment classification using Hindi, MuRIL achieved an accuracy of 0.89 and an F1-score of 0.88 which were better than the best reported sentiment classification in classical Hindi by 0.08 on both measures. UmBERTo''s results for Italian were 0.90 accuracy and 0.89 F1-score, while LLaMAntino obtained the best reported sentiment scores of 0.91 and 0.90. For Hindi, the relative improvement over LDA was 53.7% and for Italian, it was 50.0% with respect to the topic coherence score measured by BERTopic. The findings suggest that there is clear benefit for morphologically complex text and mixed script text for using contextual embeddings and language-specific pretraining. However, there are still some limitations such as dialect-sensitive mistakes, spurious correlations, computational cost and low interpretability. Finally, given that error and fairness audits are not widely available, the study offers a more comprehensive grounding in error and fairness for the responsible deployment of multilingual topic and sentiment systems.</p>','["Multilingual NLP", "topic modeling", "sentiment analysis", "Hindi NLP", "Italian NLP", "transformer models", "MuRIL", "BERTopic", "UmBERTo", "LLaMAntino", "code-mixed text"]',NULL,18,1,'195 - 211','10.59543/jidmis.v3.638','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',12),
(639,639,1,'Android Malware Detection Using an Optimized and Explainable Attentional Convolutional LSTM Model','<p>Due to the increasing number of Android applications, its usage has increased which are exposing ourselves into complex mobile malware which is a serious threat on user privacy and system integrity. Machine Learning (ML) based detection methods are considered traditional and generally perform poorly in modeling the complex sequential activity of API-call patterns, typically having low interpretability of their decisions. To address these challenges, this work proposes an optimized and interpretable attentional CNN–LSTM framework for Android malware detection. It utilizes convolutional layer to extract spatial features, Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks to capture patterns in sequential behavior and attention mechanism to highlight distinctive sequences of API calls. Moreover, Manta Ray Foraging Optimization (MWO) is applied for adaptive parameters fine-tuning and convergence improvement to enhances detection performance. We validate our approach with a wide range of experiments on two absolutely popular datasets for API call–based malware detection and result show high accuracy, precision, recall as well as F1-score owing to the 90:10 train–test split. Randomization-based statistical validation confirms the robustness and significance of results (p &lt; 0.05, non-parametric tests). Besides classification performance, the proposed framework has an innate explainability as examining attention weights through API sequences allows for transparent interpretation of detection decisions. Malicious and benign applications exhibit different concentration patterns in the usage of APIs as revealed by attention heatmaps and top-weighted API analysis Hence, the proposed approach provides end to end, optimized and interpretable solution for reliable android malware detection.</p>','["Android Malware detection", "Network Security", "Deep Learning", "Malware Analysis", "Android devices"]',NULL,18,1,'212 - 233','10.59543/jidmis.v3.639','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',13),
(640,640,1,'Multi-Class Millet Classification Using A Fusion Deep Learning Convolutional Neural Network','<p>Reliable identification of millet cultivars is essential for maintaining grain quality, supporting seed authentication, and improving automation in post-harvest processing. Despite recent advances in computer vision, accurate classification of millet varieties remains challenging because many cultivars exhibit subtle visual differences in color, texture, shape, and grain arrangement, particularly when analyzed in bulk grain images. To address this challenge, a fusion-based convolutional neural network is developed that combines feature representations extracted at multiple spatial scales. The proposed architecture employs parallel convolutional branches with different receptive fields to capture local texture patterns, structural grain characteristics, and broader spatial relationships within bulk grain formations. The extracted features are integrated through a fusion mechanism to generate a richer and more discriminative representation for classification. To enhance model robustness under practical imaging conditions, image augmentation techniques including contrast variation, noise injection, sharpening, and rotation were incorporated during training. The proposed model was evaluated against AlexNet, VGG16 trained from scratch, pretrained VGG16, and pretrained InceptionV3 using the same experimental settings. Among all evaluated architectures, the fusion network consistently achieved the best performance, attaining a validation accuracy of 93.80% and a testing accuracy of 93.09%. The model further achieved a Precision of 93.02%, Recall of 92.04%, and F1-score of 92.52%, indicating balanced and reliable classification performance across millet cultivars. Analysis of the confusion matrix revealed strong class-wise discrimination with only limited confusion among visually similar varieties, while cultivars possessing distinctive color characteristics were identified with perfect accuracy. The results highlight the importance of multi-scale feature learning for fine-grained agricultural image classification and demonstrate that feature fusion can substantially improve cultivar recognition in bulk grain imagery. The proposed framework offers an effective solution for automated millet identification and can support the development of intelligent grain sorting and quality assessment systems for modern agricultural applications.</p>','["Millet Classification, Deep Learning, Convolutional Neural Network, Precision Agriculture, Multi-scale feature extraction"]',NULL,18,1,'234 - 250','10.59543/jidmis.v3.640','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',14),
(641,641,1,'Consumer Responses to AI-Generated Influencers versus Human Influencers: The Roles of Trust, Authenticity, and Purchase Intention','<p>Artificial intelligence (AI)-generated influencers have transformed influencer marketing by introducing virtual personalities that interact with consumers on digital channels. Brands are using AI-generated influencers more often because they are scalable, consistent, and easy to control. However, there are still concerns about how authentic and trustworthy these influencers seem compared to human influencers. This study examines how consumers respond to both AI-generated and human influencers, drawing on Source Credibility Theory, Parasocial Interaction Theory, and the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM). It explores how the type of influencer affects trust, authenticity, engagement, and purchase intention. The study also considers how AI literacy and consumer innovativeness might influence these effects. This study uses a quantitative research design and collects survey data from social media users in India. Structural Equation Modeling (SEM) is used to test the framework. The results are expected to show that human influencers build greater trust and perceived authenticity, while AI-generated influencers may be more attractive to technology-literate consumers. Trust, authenticity, and engagement are expected to explain how influencer type affects purchase intention. This study adds to research on AI-enabled marketing by applying traditional influencer marketing theories to virtual environments. The findings deliver practical advice for marketers seeking to improve their influencer strategies in a digital world increasingly reliant on AI.</p>','["Artificial Intelligence, Virtual Influencers, Human Influencers, Influencer Marketing, Buyer Trust, Authenticity, Purchase Intention, Social Media Marketing"]',NULL,18,1,'251 - 276','10.59543/jidmis.v3.641','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',15),
(642,642,1,'Human–Generative AI Collaboration in Digital Marketing: Its Impact on Consumer Trust, Purchase Intentions, and Financial Decision-Making','<p>The increasing integration of generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) into digital marketing is transforming how consumers interact with information, evaluate alternatives, and make behavioural and financial decisions. However, the effectiveness of GenAI may depend on its integration with human judgment, contextual interpretation, and oversight. This study examines the influence of Human–Generative AI Collaboration (HGAIC) on Consumer Trust (CT), Purchase Intention (PI), and Financial Decision-Making (FDM). A quantitative cross-sectional design was adopted, using data from 438 consumers with prior experience of AI-enabled digital marketing interactions. The proposed model was evaluated using partial least squares structural equation modelling (PLS-SEM) to assess measurement quality, direct structural relationships, mediation effects, and predictive relevance. The results indicate that HGAIC has a strong positive effect on Consumer Trust and significant direct effects on Purchase Intention and Financial Decision-Making. Consumer Trust positively influences both decision outcomes and partially mediates the relationships between HGAIC and Purchase Intention and between HGAIC and Financial Decision-Making. The model demonstrates satisfactory explanatory and predictive capability, explaining 64.2% of the variance in Purchase Intention and 55.3% in Financial Decision-Making. The findings establish trust as a key behavioural mechanism through which human–GenAI collaboration contributes to consumer decision outcomes. The study extends collaborative intelligence research into consumer-facing digital marketing and highlights the importance of combining GenAI-enabled personalization and decision assistance with transparent human oversight, validation, and accountability.</p>','["Human\\u2013Generative AI Collaboration", "Generative Artificial Intelligence", "Consumer Trust", "Purchase Intention", "Financial Decision-Making", "Digital Marketing", "PLS-SEM"]',NULL,18,1,'277 - 296','10.59543/jidmis.v3.642','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',16),
(643,643,1,'Explainable AI and Consumer Trust in Financial Services: Building Responsible Marketing Strategies for the Algorithmic Economy','<p>The growing use of artificial intelligence (AI) in financial services has led to heightened personalization and decision efficiency but also raised questions about algorithmic opacity fairness, risk, and consumer trust. The paper investigates the role of explainable artificial intelligence (XAI) on consumer trust and behavioural intention in AI-enabled financial services and formulates a responsible marketing strategy framework for the algorithmic economy. This study employed quantitative, cross-sectional research design collected data from 420 consumers who had experience using AI-supported financial services and analysed it through confirmatory factor analysis and covariance-based structural equation modelling. Findings reveal that AI Explainability enhances Consumer Trust and Perceived Fairness, while the latter has a significant impact on strengthening Consumer Trust. It seems that Perceived Algorithmic Risk negatively affects trust; Consumer Trust is found to have a strong predictive power for Behavioural Intention. Finally, bootstrapping analysis (Hayes, 2017) consistent with mediation is found for H1 (AI Explainability → Behavioural Intention mediated by Consumer Trust) and H3 (AI Explainability → Consumer Trust mediated by Perceived Fairness). Explaining large amounts of variance in the behavioural intention pathway, the strong serial (a-b-c-d) relationship shows that behaviours are generated by explainability through fairness assessment and trust development. This contribution advances research on XAI and intelligent decision-making by combining explainability, fairness, algorithmic risk, trust and behavioural intention into a single integrated framework classified through the consumer lens. It also offers a practical, responsible financial marketing framework with key components rooted in XAI, including meaningful explainability, fairness-aware personalization, privacy protection, risk governance, accountability of outputs and experts to provide human oversight. The results offer actionable insights for the design of transparent and accountable AI-facilitated financial services.</p>','["Explainable artificial intelligence", "consumer trust", "perceived fairness", "algorithmic risk", "behavioural intention", "responsible financial marketing"]',NULL,18,1,'297 - 324','10.59543/jidmis.v3.643','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',17),
(644,644,1,'An Efficient Framework for DDoS-Attack Classification and Mitigation with Pre-Attack Propagation Behavior Analysis using TL-CEReSP-BiLSTM','<p>Currently, Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS) attacks have become a major threat to network security, causing serious issues by disrupting the availability of online services. Yet, none of the existing systems concentrated on analyzing the pre-attack propagation behavior of botnets before mitigating the DDoS attacks. Henceforth, this paper proposes an efficient framework for botnets pre-attack propagation behavior analysis-based DDoS attack classification along with mitigation by employing Transfer Learning based Contrastive Entropy Rectified SoftPlus Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory (TL-CEReSP-BiLSTM). Firstly, the network nodes are initialized and provided to the pre-attack propagation model. To train this model, the Czech Technical University (CTU-13) is collected and pre-processed, followed by beeswarm plot construction. Then, the features are extracted from the pre-processed data as well as the constructed plot. Next, the features are selected as of the extracted features using the Greedy-Marine Predators Algorithm (G-MPA). Afterward, from selected features, the classification is carried out by employing TL-CEReSP-BiLSTM, followed by explainability using G-SHAP. The corresponding nodes are blocked if the classified outcome is attack traffic. If traffic is normal, the nodes are clustered using Density Peaks Cosine Angular K-Means (DPCAK-Means), followed by Cluster Head (CH) selection using G-MPA. Also, the optimal path selection is carried out from the selected CH via a communication protocol using G-MPA. Now, through the network, the data is sensed for transmission. Then, by using Elliptical Curve Cryptography (ECC), the sensed data are secured, followed by traffic filtering using DPCAK-Means. Now, to train the Network Intrusion Detection (NID) model, the filtered sensed packets are forwarded by decrypting the secured data. For training the NID model, the CIC-DDoS2019 dataset is collected and pre-processed, followed by beeswarm plot construction. Then, the features are extracted from the pre-processed data as well as the constructed plot. Next, from the extracted features, the features are selected by employing G-MPA, followed by attack type classification and explainability. Further, the severity level of the classified DDoS attacks is determined using Heavy-Tailed Distribution-based Fuzzy Interference System (HTD-FIS). The packets are dropped if the severity is high. If the severity is medium and low, then the mitigation is carried out using Quick UDP Internet Connections-Csendes Entropy-based-Hash-centric Message Authentication Code-HyperText Transfer Protocol Secure (QUIC-CEHMAC-HTTPS). Lastly, after mitigating the DDoS attacks, the optimal paths are reselected and the data are transmitted securely through the network. Thus, the proposed model attained 99.16% accuracy in attack type classification.</p>','["Cyber threats, Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS) attacks, Network Intrusion Detection (NID), Traffic filtering, Quick UDP Internet Connections-Csendes Entropy-based-Hash-based Message Authentication Code-HyperText Transfer Protocol Secure (QUIC-CEHMAC-HTTPS), Transfer Learning based Contrastive Entropy Rectified SoftPlus Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory (TL-CEReSP-BiLSTM), and Heavy-Tailed Distribution-based Fuzzy Interference System (HTD-FIS)."]',NULL,18,1,'325 - 350','10.59543/jidmis.v3.644','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',18),
(645,645,1,'Hyper-Personalization in Finance: How Generative AI is Reshaping Customer Experience, Engagement and Financial Inclusion','<p>The recent rapid entry of GenAI into financial services is transforming traditional personalization into contextual, adaptive and conversational decision support at the same time that limited theory-grounded empirical evidence explains how such capabilities translate into customer- and / or inclusion-oriented effects. In this regard, this study motivates and develops one GenAI-Enabled Hyper-Personalization Decision Framework (GEHPDF), comprising elements between GenAI-enabled hyper-personalization to customer experience, customer engagement, and financial inclusion respectively, with perceived explainability as a boundary condition focused on trust or privacy concern or perceived algorithmic fairness depending on responsible-AI behavior. Methods: This was a cross-sectional survey using data from 550 users of digital financial services. By using PLS-SEM, bootstrapped mediation and moderation analysis and out-of-sample predictive performance evaluation with competing machine-learning-based models to validate the proposed framework. The model provides conclusive positive effects of hyper-personalisation through GenAI on the customer experience (β = 0.612), of experience on engagement (β = 0.684) and of engagement on financial inclusion (β = 0.571). Sequential mediation analysis shows hyper-personalisation mediates financial inclusion (β=0.239) through the customer experience and engagement. Trust, explainability and algorithmic fairness significantly strengthen and privacy concerns only weakens the relationship between personalisation and experience. From the observations, we can see that with some features, the model predicts reasonably well but in an overall comparison Gradient Boosting is giving better comparative predictive performance of 87.8% accuracy and AUC = 0.934 The next step consists of a unified explanatory-predictive architecture on GenAI, not as merely a financial automation technology, but rather an adaptive financial decision-support mechanism in this study. Results showed the inclusive value of GenAI is based on designing personalised intelligence, customer-centric interaction and responsible AI governance together.</p>','["Generative Artificial Intelligence", "Hyper-Personalization", "Customer Experience", "Customer Engagement", "Financial Inclusion", "Responsible AI", "Intelligent Decision Making"]',NULL,18,1,'351 - 376','10.59543/jidmis.v3.645','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',19),
(646,646,1,'Fuzzy Graph-Based Financial Network Analysis for Risk Assessment, Portfolio Optimization, and Decision Support','<p>Financial networks are complex systems characterized by interconnected relation-ships among various entities such as banks, insurers, and investment firms. These relationships often involve uncertainty, ambiguity, and incomplete information, which traditional graph models struggle to capture effectively. Fuzzy graph theory offers a powerful alternative by incorporating degrees of uncertainty through fuzzy sets and membership functions. This paper explores the application of fuzzy graphs in finan-cial networks, focusing on risk assessment and management. It discusses how fuzzy graphs can model credit risk propagation, systemic risk analysis, and fraud detection in an uncertain environment. The integration of fuzzy logic enables more realistic and flexible representations of financial interdependencies, enhancing the ability to iden-tify vulnerabilities and assess the robustness of financial systems. Through theoretical frameworks and practical case studies, the paper highlights the advantages and chal-lenges of using fuzzy graphs, offering insights into how they can support more informed decision-making in complex financial ecosystems.</p>','["Fuzzy Graphs, Financial Networks, Risk Assessment, Systemic Risk, Portfolio Optimization"]',NULL,18,1,'377 - 391','10.59543/jidmis.v3.646','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',20),
(647,647,1,'Mathematical Network Propagation Models for Hospital-Acquired Infection Transmission Dynamics','<p>Hospital-acquired infections (HAIs) have complex transmission pathways that are determined by mixed temporal interactions between healthcare workers, patients and environmental surfaces, and which are heterogeneous. This paper proposes a mathematical network propagation model for the transmission of HAI based on stochastic graph theory, the temporal weighted networks, the compartmental epidemiology and the percolation analysis. The proposed model combines network adapted SEIR dynamics, stochastic Poisson transmission processes, agent-based simulation, and graph centrality analysis to understand the spread of infection throughout the hospital network of contacts. A temporal adjacency matrix and a weighted multigraph formulation are used to quantify transmission probabilities and outbreak criticality under heterogeneously-distributed contact situations. The simulation results show that the prevalence of infection was reduced by 32.5% along with a reduction of 41.2% in the transmission intensity, compared to those obtained by the conventional epidemiological models in the modern healthcare environment. The simulation results show that the infection rate was reduced by 32.5% and the transmission intensity by 41.2% compared to the conventional epidemiological models and provided better prediction accuracy of the outbreaks in modern healthcare environments.</p>','["Hospital-acquired infections", "Network propagation models", "Stochastic compartmental models", "Agent-based simulation", "Percolation theory"]',NULL,18,1,'392 - 401','10.59543/jidmis.v3.647','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',21),
(649,649,1,'Adaptive Reaction–Diffusion Dynamical System for Spatial Epidemic Control in Urban Healthcare Networks','<p>In urban healthcare networks, the spatial heterogeneity, the mobility interaction, and the limited medical resources introduce a complex epidemic behavior, which necessitates the use of mathematically justified modelling approaches. In this study, an Adaptive Reaction–Diffusion Dynamical System (AMADF) for spatial epidemic control is proposed without changing the basic modelling framework. The proposed formulation thinks about the epidemic evolution as a nonlinear dynamical process which is ruled by reaction–diffusion interactions, adaptable mobility transmission and health care constrained recovery. Dynamical state evolution, equilibrium behavior and spatial stability interpretation of the mathematical analysis. Experimental evaluation shows 92.8% prediction accuracy, 54% spatial consistency, 24.8% reduction of peak infection and 82.3% healthcare resource utilization. Results show that reaction–diffusion dynamics increase the accuracy of epidemic modeling and enable mathematically interpretable urban healthcare decision mechanisms.</p>','["spatial epidemic model, urban healthcare, reaction diffusion, mobility modelling, healthcare capacity, hotspot detection"]',NULL,18,1,'402 - 408','10.59543/jidmis.v3.649','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',22),
(650,650,1,'A Reinforcement Learning-Driven Bayesian–Markov Trust Evolution Model for Multi-Hop Vehicular IoT Networks','<p>n this paper, a hybrid Bayesian–Markov trust evolution model for secure multi-hop communication in Vehicular IoT (V-IoT) system is proposed. The proposed model mathematically represents the propagation of trust in the dynamic model by the use of probabilistic model of state model transition, Bayesian posterior estimation and adaptive reinforcement learning based trust update. To implement multi-hop trust aggregation, hop-wise decay functions and composite trust metric combining reliability and latency and consistency in behavior are used. The framework also learns about Sybil attack, on-off attack, and collusion attack based on adversarial attacks which are formulated probabilistically and entropy-based trust confidence estimation. Simulation experiments conducted under varying density of vehicles demonstrates that the proposed HBMRL framework is able to achieve better trust accuracy (21.6%), attack detection (22.4%) and false trust propagation (25.4%) values when compared with the conventional trust models.</p>','["Vehicular IoT", "Multi-hop Trust", "Bayesian\\u2013Markov Model", "Reinforcement Learning", "Trust Propagation", "Malicious Node Detection"]',NULL,18,1,'409 - 417','10.59543/jidmis.v3.650','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',23),
(651,651,1,'Multi-Agent Models of Spatial Predator–Prey Movement in Heterogeneous Habitats','<p>Spatial predator-prey movement Multi-agent models of the movement of predators and prey in heterogeneous habitats overcome the classical homogeneous assumptions. The issue is in the capturing of adaptive interactions in the case of spatial variability. This paper will maximize the movement plans with the help of a multi-agent algorithm that will be implemented with the help of a hybrid reinforcement learning algorithm. It uses an agent based ecological model which has stochastic spatial diffusion and a reward based policy. Findings indicate that survival of prey and efficiency of predators was greater by 27.4 and 19.6 percent respectively when compared to random-walk models at baseline. Comparative parameters show that spatial utilization is increased 22.8 percent and convergence stability is also enhanced 18.3 percent. The methodology ends up in better realism and predictive skill in modelling dynamics of an ecosystem.</p>','["Multi-agent systems, Predator\\u2013prey dynamics, Heterogeneous habitats, Reinforcement learning, Spatial modelling, Ecological simulation"]',NULL,18,1,'418 - 426','10.59543/jidmis.v3.651','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',24),
(652,652,1,'Graph-Coupled Dynamical System for Mathematical Modelling of Ventilation–Perfusion Mismatch in Lung Function','<p>The pulmonary gas exchange is impaired by a significant contribution from ventilation–perfusion mismatch which has to be represented by mathematically interpretable models of regional heterogeneity. The study introduces an Adaptive Graph-Coupled Ventilation–Perfusion Imbalance Model (AGC-VPIM), which was developed using a dynamical systems approach, but with no alterations to the original approach. The model incorporates the effect of regional ventilation allocation, perfusion redistribution, and spatial interaction using graph-based spatial interaction, to model the evolution of temporal mismatch across lung compartments. Coupled state transition and equilibrium behavior of regional flow variables is used to extend mathematical interpretation. Experimental evaluation resulted in 94.2% classification accuracy, 93.8% verification coverage, 0.91 mismatch severity agreement and oxygen prediction error of 3.12%, which showed a better representation of pulmonary imbalance and clinically consistent lung function evaluation.</p>','["ventilation\\u2011perfusion mismatch, lung modeling, graph coupling, gas exchange, simulation, hypoxemia."]',NULL,18,1,'427 - 433','10.59543/jidmis.v3.652','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',25),
(653,653,1,'Mathematical Reliability Modelling of Smart Ambulance Services Using Markov Processes, Fault Tree Analysis, and Reliability Block Diagrams','<p>The paper proposes a mathematical modelling framework for reliability of Smart Ambulance Services that combines IoT-based sensor, GPS, AI based decision system and cloud–edge communication infrastructure. The reliability evaluation is carried out based on reliability block diagrams (RBD), fault tree analysis (FTA) and continuous-time Markov stochastic processes. The suggested model examines the reliability, availability, failure rate and repair rate of the system, as well as Mean Time to Failure (MTTF) and Mean Time Between Failures (MTBF) for different levels of redundancy. A mathematical formulation is derived for series-parallel subsystems, probabilistic state transitions and reliability of cloud-edge communication. Exemplary evaluation of the reliability of hybrid cloud-optimized configurations shows that reliability levels of 98.6% and availability of 99.2% can be achieved with less downtime. Overall, the framework helps ensure the seamless continuity of operations, quick response to emergencies, and reliable provision of healthcare services in real-time scenarios involving ambulance operations.</p>','["Smart Ambulance, Reliability Modelling, IoT Healthcare, Markov Model, Fault Tree Analysis"]',NULL,18,1,'434 - 441','10.59543/jidmis.v3.653','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',26),
(654,654,1,'Spectral Graph Dynamical Framework for Resilience Assessment of Smart Grids Using Wide-Area Measurement Systems','<p>Assessing the resilience of modern smart grids demands mathematical modelling of the propagation and recovery from disturbances under very dynamic operating conditions. In this study, a Spectral Graph Dynamical Framework for assessing grid resilience based on synchronized measurements, collected via Wide-Area Measurement Systems, is introduced. The framework assumes that the grid is modeled as a weighted graph that is changing as time passes, and quantifies resilience in terms of the spectral connectivity, disturbance energy and temporal recovery behavior of the system. Dynamic evolution of graph and spectral indicators for interpretation of robustness of the structure and stability of the operation under disturbances. During normal load conditions, the experimental evaluation results showed the accuracy of resilience classification of 97.42%, F1-score of 0.967, AUC of 0.982, and resilience score of 96.34% which show reliable characterization of resilience of the grid when it is recovering and resilient to disturbances.</p>','["Smart grid, Wide\\u2011Area Measurement Systems, Phasor Measurement Unit, Resilience assessment, Graph model, Disturbance analysis."]',NULL,18,1,'442 - 449','10.59543/jidmis.v3.654','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',27),
(655,655,1,'Compartmental Models for Individual Variability in Viral Load Progression','<p>A stochastic compartmental modelling approach for individual variability in viral load progression is represented using deterministic differential equations with population level random effects is presented here. The model proposed captures subject-specific parameters for infection, replication, immune response and viral clearance to describe the trajectories of infection, which are heterogeneous among individuals, in the context of a specific subject area. The susceptible, infected and viral particle dynamics are governed by coupled nonlinear differential equations and inter-individual biological variability is modeled by log-normal and Gaussian distributions. Stability analysis based on Lyapunov functions, Bayesian posterior estimation, and expectation formulations at the population level are used for evaluating system stability and uncertainty propagation. There was a significant difference in infection kinetics, viral production, and recovery behavior between patient groups, as shown by the simulation results. The framework provides a better individual prognosis, treatment optimization and population-level inference in the dynamics of infectious diseases.</p>','["Viral load dynamics, Compartmental model, Individual variability, Random effects modeling, Differential equations, Population heterogeneity"]',NULL,18,1,'450 - 457','10.59543/jidmis.v3.655','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',28);
INSERT INTO publications (id,submission_id,version,title,abstract,keywords,references_text,issue_id,section_id,pages,doi,copyright_holder,copyright_year,license_url,status,date_published,seq) VALUES
(656,656,1,'Mathematical Modelling of Plant Root Water Uptake under Heterogeneous Soil Conditions','<p>This paper introduces a nonlinear mathematics model of plant root water uptake in heterogeneous soil based on the coupled soil–plant hydrodynamics. The process of variably saturated water flow and moisture extraction mechanisms is described by Richards'' partial differential equation integrated with spatially distributed root uptake sink functions. Discretization for numerical approximation is used on adaptive heterogeneous grids: finite difference and finite element discretization schemes. The model includes the variability in hydraulic conductivity, soil water retention relationships, and uptake dynamics that are dependent on stress. The iterative Newton–Raphson formulations are used to study the stability and convergence for the nonlinear coupled system. Numerical modelling shows that under different boundary conditions, the moisture distribution, the hydraulic gradient and the ability of the root zone to extract water from the soil are markedly influenced by the degree of soil heterogeneity.Moisture distribution, hydraulic gradient and root-zone extraction efficiency vary significantly as a function of soil heterogeneity under different boundary conditions, as demonstrated by numerical modelling. The proposed framework enhances the prediction of irrigation and sustainable agriculture water management.</p>','["Root water uptake, Richards\\u2019 equation, heterogeneous soil, finite element method, soil moisture dynamics, numerical modeling"]',NULL,18,1,'458 - 466','10.59543/jidmis.v3.656','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',29),
(657,657,1,'Epidemic-Style Modelling for Smart City Sensor Fabrics','<p>Smart city sensor fabrics are very interconnected and an event at one point could have a large impact on another and quickly affect the stability of the network. For disturbance propagation control in large sensor environments with an epidemic-like propagation of disturbances, this study proposes an Energy-Aware Graph Dynamical System (EGDS). The framework incorporates the graph-based epidemic evolution disturbance transmission mechanism, node energy status, recovery model and spatial containment model. Spectral stability conditions, reproduction of behavior and controlled energy dissipation at interconnected nodes support mathematical analysis. The simulation results show that the spectral radius ρ(W) is reduced by 1.42, convergence time is 11.3 time units, reproduction index is 0.93, spatial containment index is 0.82, stability margin is 0.81 and control dissipation ratio is 0.74 showing better propagation control and resilient network recovery.</p>','["Smart city sensors, epidemic modelling, graph diffusion, spectral analysis, propagation stability, spatial containment."]',NULL,18,1,'467 - 472','10.59543/jidmis.v3.657','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',30),
(658,658,1,'Integrated Safety–Security Modelling in Digital Substations','<p>The integrated safety–security modelling in digital substations is key in today''s smart grid context with the presence of cyber–physical threats. To deal with these problems, this paper adopts a unified framework for co-modelling based on mathematics, which integrates the four aspects of dependency analysis by graph, system methods based on state modelling, attacker-defender optimization and temporal logic verification. A cyber–physical interaction model incorporating the cascading failures and attack propagation is developed for the operational risk dynamics. Bounded disturbance analysis is used to derive stability conditions and the Nash equilibrium and the Hamilton–Jacobi–Isaacs formulations are used to optimize the defensive strategies. Linear Temporal Logic (LTL) and Computation Tree Logic (CTL) are logic formalisms used to provide formal verification to safety and recovery properties. The simulation results indicate that in digital substations a higher accuracy of fault detection (96.8 %), detection rate of cyberattacks (97.5 %), cascading failure containment (94.6 %) and lower response time (109ms) have been achieved.</p>','["Digital Substations, Safety\\u2013Security Co-Modelling, Game Theory, Temporal Logic Verification, Cyber-Physical Resilience"]',NULL,18,1,'473 - 481','10.59543/jidmis.v3.658','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',31),
(659,659,1,'Interpretable Deep Learning Defences via User Anomaly Detection in Cloud Platforms','<p>Cloud platforms are the targets of highly advanced attacks that are designed to circumvent traditional rule based IDSs, such as: credential misuse, insider threats, privilege escalation, lateral movements, etc. To protect against user attacks, this paper introduces an interpretable deep learning defence framework in the context of user anomaly detection for both IaaS and PaaS cloud service layers and SaaS application layer. Multi-source authentication logs/records, APIs, networks logs are combined into integrated behavioural tensors and classified as anomalies by processing with the recurrent neural network with attention-based temporal weights. The mathematical model for access probability distribution, the model for anomaly scoring and temporal sequence modeling, as well as the model for Mahalanobis-distance-based deviation analysis further enhance the analysis''s rigor and interpretability. Experimental results show that the detection accuracy achieved is 97.4%, followed by 96.8% precision, 97.9% recall and 97.3% F1-score, which are much higher than those of the conventional rule-based and black-box deep learning IDSs.</p>','["Interpretable Deep Learning", "User Anomaly Detection", "Cloud Security", "Behavioral Modeling", "Explainable AI"]',NULL,18,1,'482 - 490','10.59543/jidmis.v3.659','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',32),
(660,660,1,'Attack-Proof Pressure Sensor Models for Oil-Pipeline SCADA','<p>Oil pipelines rely on distributed pressure sensors to ensure operation safety and flow continuity, but the pipeline infrastructures are susceptible to malicious manipulation and abnormal pressure fluctuating. In this study, we introduce an attack-resilient monitoring framework called Robust Graph-Coupled Pressure Dynamics, which combines both the pressure evolution and the network topology together with bounded adversarial disturbances. The sensors'' measurements are encoded as weighted graph interactions, which are then estimated robustly in a constrained perturbation setting. To ensure the system does not operate abnormally, the framework integrates pressure-flow coupling, anomaly identification and stability verification to enhance reliability. The experimental evaluation showed 97.36 % detection accuracy, 97.65 % of detection verification coverage, 98.04 % of AUC, 61-ms detection delay and 0.023 RMSE, which showed resilient performance for pressure monitoring.</p>','["SCADA, pressure sensors, pipeline monitoring, anomaly detection, graph modelling, attack resilience."]',NULL,18,1,'491 - 496','10.59543/jidmis.v3.660','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',33),
(661,661,1,'Multiscale Mathematical Models for Multi-Agent Robotic Coordination','<p>A multiscale mathematical model for multi-agent robotic coordination based on multi-agent systems, nonlinear dynamics, graph Laplacian theory and distributed consensus control is proposed. The proposed model is a collection of coupled differential equations and spectral graph formulations that couple the micro-scale agent dynamics, the meso-scale structure of communication topology, and the macro-scale structure of collective coordination of the agents. Asymptotic stability and robustness to communication disturbances, as well as topology switching, are guaranteed through Lyapunov stability analysis, eigenvalue decomposition and Laplacian-based conditions for consensus convergence. The framework also calculates the algebraic connectivity and disturbance rejection properties to determine the scalability and resilience. The comparative evaluation shows better convergence time, less consensus error, better formation stability, less communication overhead and higher robustness against link failures than centralized and single-scale coordination strategies in large-scale robotic systems.</p>','["Multi-agent systems, Multiscale modeling, Graph Laplacian, Distributed control, Consensus dynamics, Robotic coordination"]',NULL,18,1,'497 - 504','10.59543/jidmis.v3.661','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',34),
(662,662,1,'Risk-Adaptive Reaction–Diffusion Model with Urban Healthcare Load Feedback for Epidemic Transmission','<p>In urban areas, where population density is high, the speed of epidemic spread puts strain on health facilities and poses a particular challenge for standard models of the spatial spread of epidemics. This study introduces the Risk-Adaptive Reaction–Diffusion Model with Urban Healthcare Load Feedback, to model the propagation of infections under limited healthcare capacities. The model includes continuous spatial spread, nonlinear transmission saturation, adaptive recovery and load-dependent regulation of transmission. The health-care burden is considered as a variable which evolves over time and directly affects the evolution of the epidemic and the equilibrium behaviour. Experimental tests on synthetic urban scenarios yielded 0.019 mean absolute state error, 0.028 spatial diffusion error, 0.021 healthcare load deviation, 34.7% overload reduction and 8.7 hours equilibrium convergence time, which showed better epidemic regulation and healthcare stability.</p>','["Spatial epidemic modelling, Reaction diffusion systems, Urban healthcare load, Risk adaptive dynamics, Spatiotemporal transmission, Nonlinear epidemic systems."]',NULL,18,1,'505 - 511','10.59543/jidmis.v3.662','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',35),
(663,663,1,'Cyber-Physical Railway Security using Smart Port Operations','<p>The interconnection of advanced signalling systems, logistics and operational networks involves IoT technologies in railway systems necessities advanced security measures to prevent cyber attacks to such systems which are used in the smart port infrastructures. This paper proposes a cyber-physical security framework mathematically and AI based for railway–smart port operations based on multi-layer security, intelligence intrusion detection system, and stability constrained monitoring models. The framework introduces network state evolution equations, communication delay modelling, secure logistics optimization, Lyapunov stability analysis and layered security convergence theorems to ensure secure network operation in the event of a cyber attack. Signalling manipulation, packet injection, spoofing and communication jamming attacks are detected using the AI based anomaly detection. Based on experimental analysis, it can be concluded that the proposed cyber-physical transportation security architecture is effective, robust, and reliable with intrusion detection accuracy of 97.2% and higher precision and recall, F1 score.</p>','["Cyber-Physical Systems, Railway Security, Smart Port Operations, Intrusion Detection, AI-Based Monitoring, Critical Infrastructure Protection."]',NULL,18,1,'512 - 518','10.59543/jidmis.v3.663','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',36),
(664,664,1,'A Mathematical Oncology Model of Tumour Growth with Immune System Coupling','<p>In this paper, a nonlinear mathematical oncology model of dynamics of tumour growth along with the interactions with the immune system is presented. The framework proposed uses a set of coupled nonlinear differential equations to describe the proliferation of the tumour, activation of the immune system, the cytotoxic response, immunosuppression, and resource-limited biological conditions under saturated conditions. The inclusion of stability analysis, equilibrium conditions and parameter sensitivity formulations allows a study of the dynamics of tumour eradication, dormancy, oscillatory persistence and uncontrolled tumour progression. The effects of the coefficients of immune recruitment and suppression on the dynamics of tumour evolution are illustrated through a numerical simulation of the model using the implicit Runge–Kutta discretisation. A global and local sensitivity analysis identifies dominant biological parameters which govern the tumour–immune balance. The model is developed that gives a quantitative mathematical framework for predictive oncology, optimization of immunotherapies and mechanistic investigations of nonlinear tumor–immune regulatory interactions.</p>','["Tumour growth modeling, Immune\\u2013tumour interaction, Nonlinear differential equations, Stability analysis, Immunotherapy dynamics, Sensitivity analysis"]',NULL,18,1,'519 - 525','10.59543/jidmis.v3.664','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',37),
(665,665,1,'Cyber-Physical Railway Security using Supervisory Control and Data Acquisition','<p>The safe coordination of signaling, routing and movement of trains is provided by supervisory control and data acquisition infrastructure in a cyber-physical railway system; however, the increase of interconnection leads to operations being vulnerable to coordinated cyber-attacks. The study proposes a Risk-Adaptive Attacker–Defender Control framework which represents railway protection as a dynamic process of adversarial action and defensive decision. The framework includes state evolution, risk estimation, adaptive control updates and, equilibrium driven mitigation to keep the system safe during operation under varying attack conditions. Experimental evaluation showed 0.089 state deviation index, 3.1% signal conflict rate, 8.4 disruption cost, 7.3s recovery time and 91.8% attack containment efficiency, which prove that the system is more resilient to attacks and can be restored in a shorter time to a safe railway operation.</p>','["Cyber-physical railway systems, SCADA security, game-theoretic control, attacker-defender dynamics, risk-adaptive control, railway system resilience"]',NULL,18,1,'526 - 531','10.59543/jidmis.v3.665','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',38),
(666,666,1,'Hybrid PDE–Agent-based Models of Tissue Repair and Regeneration','<p>In this paper, a hybrid partial differential equation (PDE) and agent-based model (ABM) approach for modelling multiscale tissue repair and regeneration dynamics is presented. The proposed model combines continuum reaction–diffusion equations for migration and proliferation of growth factors and ECM remodeling with stochastic cellular agents for migration and proliferation, apoptosis, and differentiation behaviors. The dynamics of the motion and feedback coupling between individual cells and biochemical fields are mathematically described to model spatial–temporal regenerative processes driven by chemotaxis. The numerical implementation uses a stable multi-scale simulation using finite difference discretization and stochastic Euler–Maruyama schemes. The computational experiments show that enhanced proliferation and chemotactic sensitivity are important factors that accelerate wound closure and increase the density of regenerated tissue. The framework enables the prediction of healing kinetics, spatiotemporal pattern formation and the evolution of the regenerating tissue in a biologically interpretable and computationally efficient way for advanced regenerative medicine applications.</p>','["Hybrid modeling, Partial differential equations, Agent-based models, Tissue regeneration, Reaction\\u2013diffusion, Chemotaxis"]',NULL,18,1,'532 - 540','10.59543/jidmis.v3.666','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',39),
(667,667,1,'Mathematical Constraints on DNA Folding and Supercoiling Phenomena','<p>The folding and supercoiling of DNA are important contributors to genomic organisation, transcription regulation and structural stability of biological systems. A mathematical framework is developed in this paper for analysing the conformational dynamics of DNA based on energy minimization, elastic rod approach, Hamiltonian mechanics and modelling using topological constraints. Mathematical formulations to explain positive and negative supercoiling are curvature, torsional strain, bending stiffness, and linking number decomposition. The study also examines the interactions and the stability of the equilibrium between the twist and writhe of DNA structural transitions. Understands that decreasing the stability indices shows greater curvature and more energy dissipated in the structures, such as linear helices, loop formations, plectoneme structures and toroidal coils. The suggested mathematical expressions enhance the theoretical knowledge of DNA topology and provide a computational foundation for more sophisticated applications of modeling DNA structure in the genome</p>','["DNA folding dynamics", "DNA supercoiling", "elastic rod model", "topological constraints", "Hamiltonian modeling"]',NULL,18,1,'541 - 549','10.59543/jidmis.v3.667','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',40),
(668,668,1,'Mathematical Trust-Propagation Rules for IoT Edge Gateways','<p>IoT edge gateway acts as intermediary distributed gateways between sensing devices and cloud services, with dynamic evolution of trust and adversarial behaviour impacting communication reliability. In this study, we propose a Spectral Graph Dynamical Framework for Risk-Adaptive Trust Propagation in IoT Edge Gateways, which integrates the graph Laplacian diffusion with the risk regulation at the node level to ensure stable trust propagation. Weighted interactions propagate the gateway trust states, which are then modified based on anomaly-driven risk feedback and damping control. Using the spectral radius constraints convergence and stability conditions are derived. Experimental results showed a 17% decrease in spectral radius, 43% decrease in convergence time, 52% decrease in attack propagation factor, and 35% increase in perturbation resilience, which demonstrates stable trust convergence and enhanced stability in adversarial conditions.</p>','["IoT trust propagation, spectral graph theory, edge gateway security, risk-adaptive trust, graph Laplacian diffusion, adversarial resilience"]',NULL,18,1,'550 - 555','10.59543/jidmis.v3.668','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',41),
(669,669,1,'Mathematical Bounds on Accuracy in Federated Learning Frameworks','<p>In this paper, the mathematically tight bounds on the accuracy of predictive performance in federated learning systems with distributed data across multiple clients and some clients participate in learning process are presented. A constrained distributed stochastic minimization problem is used to write Federated optimization, based on the smoothness and convexity, bounded variance assumptions. Convergence behaviour of Federated Averaging (FedAvg) is analysed with local SGD updates, aggregation bias modelling and decomposing the client drift. Distributed optimization theory is employed to obtain theoretical upper bounds for the convergence error, the gradient divergence and the bias induced by the heterogeneity. Through mathematical analysis it is possible to establish even more relationships between rounds of communication, learning rate, participation ratio and the statistical heterogeneity. Numerical evaluation shows that decreasing the degree of heterogeneities leads to a larger stability of the convergence and an accuracy of the model. The proposed framework is scalable, preserves privacy and it is theoretically guaranteed to achieve the performance of federated learning in decentralized optimization environments.</p>','["Federated Learning, Convergence Bounds, Client Heterogeneity, Distributed Optimization, Generalization Error, FedAvg"]',NULL,18,1,'556 - 563','10.59543/jidmis.v3.669','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',42),
(670,670,1,'Multiscale Mathematical Models for Nanoscale Heat Transport Phenomena','<p>Classical diffusion assumptions are not valid to describe heat transport at the nanoscale due to the modification of thermal behavior due to phonon scattering, finite transport length and memory effects. This study proposes a Fractional Dynamical Framework for Multiscale Nanoscale heat Transport with Phonon Interaction, representing the thermal evolution by a fractional order and the multiscale phonon coupling by a fractional order. The framework includes a temporal and a spatial fractional operator to account for the nonlocal conduction and transition between diffusive and quasi-ballistic transport regimes. To represent the interactions between phonons, and to ensure numerical stability, graph-based discretization is used. It was verified that the prediction accuracy was 97.69%, the verification coverage was 93.30%, the effective thermal conductivity was 149.8 W/mK and the temperature error was 2.31% according to the experimental evaluation, which shows a better representation of the thermal propagation on the nanoscale and the energy transfer.</p>','["Nanoscale heat transport, Fractional phonon model, Nonlocal thermal conduction, Ballistic diffusive transition, Phonon scattering dynamics, Multiscale heat transfer"]',NULL,18,1,'564 - 570','10.59543/jidmis.v3.670','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',43),
(671,671,1,'Compartmental Models for Personalized Drug Delivery Kinetics','<p>Individual drug therapy should consider the presence of diversity in the physiology and interactions of the patients. Classical compartment models which are commonly applied to describe absorption, distribution, metabolism and elimination can be characterized as linear exchange and constant parameter. Such simplifications do not reflect kinetics and heterogeneous organ behavior of patients. The proposed Graph-Coupled Nonlinear Compartmental Model (GC -NCM) takes the form of organs as nodes of a weighted graph where transports are nonlinear in the sense of saturation functions and patient-specific coupling. The adjacency matrix of a distance-based determination of drug flow between nodes, and the elimination and absorption are modeled separately. As a validation, GC -NCM was compared to the one- and two-compartment models. It the reduced error of concentration tracking by 0.109 to 0.061 (44% reduction) and peak deviation by 13.20% to 6.80%. The time to therapeutic range error was reduced by 1.74 h to 0.83 h and the clearance profile error was reduced by 0.097 to 0.051. The stability of the coupling was improved to 0.91 and the error in the dose-response was reduced to 0.58 (0.118 to 0.058). Such findings indicate that GC-NCM leads to a greater predictive accuracy and ranges of therapeutic dose levels, which justify individualized drug delivery.</p>','["Compartmental pharmacokinetics, Personalized drug delivery, Graph-based modeling, Nonlinear drug transport, Tissue interaction dynamics, Precision dosing."]',NULL,18,1,'571 - 577','10.59543/jidmis.v3.671','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',44),
(672,672,1,'Feature-Bound Behavior Modeling of Online Marketplace Transactions','<p>Feature-Bound Behavior Modeling (FBBM) gives a formal model of computation of transaction dynamics in online marketplaces, associating user behaviors with context-dependent interactions of features. The suggested model incorporates dimensionality reduction, state encoding and probabilistic representation of graphs to learn latent dependencies amongst transactional attributes. Experimentation shows a better anomaly detection accuracy, the measurement of behavioral consistency and scalable modeling in a heterogeneous transaction environment.</p>','["Feature Interaction Modeling, Behavioral State Encoding, Online Marketplace Transactions, Probabilistic Graph Models, Dimensionality Reduction"]',NULL,18,1,'578 - 587','10.59543/jidmis.v3.672','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',45),
(673,673,1,'Resilience Assessment of Smart Grids Via: Synchrophasor Communication Channels','<p>Synchrophasor communication channels are now a critical component of smart grids, providing real-time monitoring, protection and wide area control of the power system. This paper offers a mathematical framework to assess resilience in a communication network and Smart Grid stability that is developed by combining the communication network performance metrics with the Smart Grid stability metrics, both utilizing graph theoretic modeling of Phasor Measurement Unit (PMU) networks. Latency, packet loss probability, bandwidth utilization, queueing delay, and network connectivity are analytically modeled and evaluated with respect to different traffic situations to assess the reliability of communications. A cyber–physical resilience index is proposed, which consists of communication reliability and grid stability functions, to quantify the robustness of the system. The Graph Laplacian theory and algebraic connectivity are used to quantify PMU network resilience and stability of the communication topology. The simulation results illustrate that high network congestion has a great impact on smart grid resilience performance (including latency, packet loss, etc.).</p>','["Smart grids, Synchrophasor communication, PMU networks, Grid resilience, Communication latency modeling"]',NULL,18,1,'588 - 597','10.59543/jidmis.v3.673','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',46),
(674,674,1,'Fluid Mechanical Models of Respiratory Aerosol Transport in Airways','<p>The transport of aerosols in the respiratory tract in a branching airway structure is influenced by coupled evolution of airflow and stochastic movement and generation-dependent deposition of particles. The Eulerian airflow representation, Eulerian–Lagrangian bifurcation memory-dependent deposition, and stochastic Lagrangian particle tracking are used to develop this study''s Lagrangian–Eulerian Dynamical Model for Multiscale Respiratory Aerosol Transport in Branching Airways. Multiscale fluid dynamics governs airflow transport and stochastic differential transport and history-dependent deposition kernels govern the motion of aerosols. The framework includes particle inertia, bifurcation interaction and deposition accumulation, as well as Brownian fluctuation, to account for the transport behavior across airway generations. It was found that concentration field error reduced by nearly 39%, deposition fraction error reduced by 46%, peak arrival time error reduced by 52%, and flow-particle coupling stability index improved to 0.94, which means that the transport of aerosol particles was improved and the deposition on regions was consistent, by numerical evaluation.</p>','["Respiratory aerosol transport, airway fluid mechanics, Lagrangian-Eulerian modeling, stochastic deposition dynamics, bifurcation memory, multiscale particle transport"]',NULL,18,1,'598 - 604','10.59543/jidmis.v3.674','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',47),
(675,675,1,'Multiscale Hybrid Modeling of Tissue Regeneration Using PDE and Agent-Based Frameworks','<p>In this paper, a multiscale hybrid computational model framework for tissue repair and regeneration analysis is presented, which combines partial differential equations (PDEs) and agent-based modeling (ABM). The proposed model will integrate the reaction–diffusion models of cytokine transport, evolution of the extracellular matrix and biochemical signaling with the stochastic dynamics of cell migration, proliferation, differentiation and apopptosis. Coupled PDE–agent interactions can be used to model collective continuous biochemical fields and discrete cellular heterogeneity in regenerative microenvironments. The wound closure time sequences obtained in numerical simulations show that the wound closure kinetics become significantly faster, and the efficiency of tissue organization increases, as the proliferation and chemotactic sensitivity are increased. Optimizing the proliferation and chemotactic parameters of the model, the number of days closure is required is reduced from 15.4 to 11.3 days. Results show that the hybrid multiscale coupling is effective for enhancing the capability of therapeutic optimization, the spatial regeneration prediction, and the mechanistic understanding for regenerative medicine applications.</p>','["Hybrid modelling", "Partial differential equations", "Agent-based modelling", "Tissue repair", "Reaction\\u2013diffusion systems", "Multiscale regeneration dynamics."]',NULL,18,1,'605 - 613','10.59543/jidmis.v3.675','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',48),
(676,676,1,'Fracture-Mechanics-based Modelling of Crack Growth in Concrete Beams','<p>The quasi-brittle nature of the concrete materials causes progressive propagation of crack under mechanical load in concrete beams. In this paper, a mathematical model based on fracture mechanics is presented, which is suitable to analyze the behavior of crack initiation, the stress distribution and the crack growth in concrete beams. The proposed scheme combines the Linear Elastic Fracture Mechanics (LEFM) with the cohesive nonlinear model of fracture, to investigate stress intensity factors, strain energy release rates, and fracture stability conditions when the load increases. The mathematical formulation of governing elasticity equations, crack-tip stress fields and fracture propagation criteria allow the prediction of structural degradation. Experimental fracture parameters show that with applied loading, the length of the crack and the energy released increases with fracture. The results show that the proposed model could accurately predict the crack propagation behavior and structural instability, which could be used to improve the failure assessment accuracy of concrete structural systems.</p>','["Fracture Mechanics, Concrete Beams, Crack Propagation, Stress Intensity Factor, Cohesive Crack Model, Structural Failure Analysis"]',NULL,18,1,'614 - 620','10.59543/jidmis.v3.676','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',49),
(677,677,1,'Mathematical Modelling of Electromagnetic Shielding Effectiveness','<p>The multi-layer structures need to consider the coupled attenuation, impedance interaction and spectral propagation along conductive interfaces for the electromagnetic shielding effectiveness. In this work, we propose a Graph-Coupled Spectral Diffusion Framework for Multi-Layer Electromagnetic Shielding Effectiveness, which utilizes graph-theoretic representations of layers and spectral diffusion dynamics driven by graph Laplacian. Each individual shielding layer is represented as a node in a graph and the weighted coupling and energy propagation between each layer is controlled by an impedance mismatch. The behavior of spectral attenuation is analyzed using eigenvalue controlled diffusion evolution and inter-layer field decay. Coupling stability, reflection interaction and transmission suppression are included in the framework for the characterization of shielding for several frequency cases. The experimental evaluation of the shielding effectiveness revealed an average value of 53.9 dB, reflection loss error of 0.061, transmission suppression ratio of 0.927, coupling stability index of 0.93 and spectral attenuation consistency of 0.91, demonstrating improvement in spectral stability and shielding prediction accuracy.</p>','["Electromagnetic shielding, Multi-layer materials, Graph-based modelling, Spectral attenuation, Inter-layer coupling, Shielding effectiveness."]',NULL,18,1,'621 - 626','10.59543/jidmis.v3.677','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',50),
(678,678,1,'Kernel-based Models for Multimodal Data Fusion and Integration','<p>The kernel-based multimodal fusion is an efficient framework to combine multimodal data, such as image, text, signals, and structured features, to learn in a unified nonlinear learning space. In this paper, an adaptive heterogeneous feature fusion approach based on kernel driven multimodal fusion architecture is proposed, which is composed of two steps: single-kernel embedding and Multiple Kernel Learning (MKL). The proposed approach uses reproducing kernel Hilbert space mapping, kernel alignment optimization, regularized multimodal learning and gradient-based kernel weight optimization to increase the representation power and predictive stability. Experimental results show that the MKL model can obtain 92.3% accuracy, 91.7% precision, 91.1% recall and 91.4% F1-score, which are higher than the conventional single-kernel fusion methods. The results demonstrate that adaptive kernel optimization is an effective approach to enhance the representation learning in multimodal data, modeling of nonlinear feature interactions, scalability, and robust prediction performance in multimodal data.</p>','["Multimodal Data Fusion, Kernel Methods, Multiple Kernel Learning (MKL), Feature Integration, Machine Learning"]',NULL,18,1,'627 - 634','10.59543/jidmis.v3.678','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',51),
(679,679,1,'Epidemic-Style Modelling for Smart Agriculture Platforms','<p>Sensors, communication and predictive analytics, powered by the Internet of Things, are becoming commonplace on smart agriculture platforms for precision agriculture and disease monitoring. A mathematical modeling framework that incorporates an epidemic approach is introduced to the study of the propagation of crop diseases and agricultural information flow in a network of interconnected farming operations. The suggested framework incorporates the agri-topology of the agricultural system using a graph, along with adapting the SIR/SEIR epidemic model to accommodate the farm states of susceptible, exposed, infected, and recovered, resulting from environmental and communication constraints. Mathematical expressions are constructed for deployment of the sensors, reliability of them to transmit the data, the coverage of the network, the estimation of the risk for having the disease, and the analysis of the threshold of the disease epidemic. According to the simulation, the proposed epidemic information propagation model can reach a high dissemination efficiency of 91.5%, high detection accuracy of 92.8%, alert delay of 4.9 s, and network coverage of 89.4%, which is superior to the resilience, scalability, and precision farming decision support of conventional agricultural communication systems.</p>','["Smart Agriculture, Epidemic Modelling, IoT Sensor Networks, SIR/SEIR Model, Precision Farming"]',NULL,18,1,'635 - 643','10.59543/jidmis.v3.679','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',52),
(680,680,1,'Multi-View Learning for Joint Network and Host-Based Signals','<p>In this paper, a multi-view learning approach to unified cybersecurity analytics is presented with respect to network traffic based cybersecurity and host-based behavioral based cybersecurity. The proposed architecture consists of heterogeneous cybersecurity observations being fused together based on extracting features from each, learning latent representations from the observations, and using the fusion mechanisms to increase the accuracy of threat detection and gain situational awareness. The correlated attack behaviors in the network and host environment are modelled using the mathematical description of the multi-view feature projection, shared latent embedding, weighted fusion optimization and ensemble neural classification. To validate the superior detection capability, the experimental results validate the detection accuracy of more than 94%, low false alarm rate of under 4%, and efficient processing latency of under 25 ms across various scenarios, such as malware infection, privilege escalation and data exfiltration. The proposed system provides a much better cross-layer cybersecurity intelligence as compared to the traditional cross-layer single view threat detection system.</p>','["Multi-view learning, Cybersecurity analytics, Network traffic analysis, Host-based monitoring, Feature fusion, Threat detection"]',NULL,18,1,'644 - 651','10.59543/jidmis.v3.680','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',53);
INSERT INTO publications (id,submission_id,version,title,abstract,keywords,references_text,issue_id,section_id,pages,doi,copyright_holder,copyright_year,license_url,status,date_published,seq) VALUES
(681,681,1,'Agent-Based and Mean-Field Models for Insider Threat Dynamics','<p>Propagation of insider threat in enterprise systems is characterized by stochastic behavior of insiders, behavior adaptation to enhance their threat, and a combination of population influence in a collective manner, and needs mathematically consistent risk-control modelling. This study introduces a Stochastic Mean-Field Game Model for Insider Threat Dynamics and Risk-Control Stability, which is based on stochastic state evolution, mean-field interaction and risk-adaptive optimal control. Mean-field interaction term between individual insider behavior and the threat distribution on the global level, and risk-weighted optimization and feedback regulation of mitigation policies. Spectral propagation conditions and decay of collective threat states are used to analyze the stability and convergence behavior. Experimental assessment showed that the mean threat energy error was reduced from 14.8% to 5.1%, the convergence time was reduced from 52.6 s to 29.4 s, the stability index was reduced from 1.34 to 0.72, and the increase of control cost efficiency to 86.8% reflected the improvement of risk containment and adaptive threat stabilization.</p>','["Insider threat dynamics, Mean-field game theory, Stochastic control model, Risk-adaptive systems, Behavioral security modelling, Cyber risk propagation"]',NULL,18,1,'652 - 657','10.59543/jidmis.v3.681','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',54),
(682,682,1,'Multi-Mode Resilience Modelling for Smart Hospital IoT Systems','<p>For the Internet of Things (IoT) systems to be smart in hospitals, they must have robust operational frameworks capable of delivering healthcare even when failure occurs, there are cyber threats and disruptions in the network. A three-layered model of multi-mode healthcare cyber-physical systems resilience is proposed, which includes the operational state analysis, anomaly detection, adaptive resource allocation and dynamic network reconfiguration. The proposed model mathematically models a communication network in hospitals that typically has a graph-theoretic topology, probabilistic reliability estimation and statistical anomaly detection mechanisms. Optimized adaptive recovery and routing provide increased fault tolerance, service continuity and stability when operating under degraded conditions. By conducting experiments, researchers found that the framework has 88.7% fault detection accuracy, 95.4% network availability, 8.9 s recovery time and 84.6% of resource utilization efficiency, outperforming the conventional hospital IoT monitoring frameworks.</p>','["Smart Hospital IoT, Resilience Modeling, Healthcare Cyber-Physical Systems, Fault Detection, Adaptive Resource Allocation, Network Reconfiguration."]',NULL,18,1,'658 - 666','10.59543/jidmis.v3.682','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',55),
(683,683,1,'Mathematical Predictors of Fault Propagation in Industrial PLCs','<p>In automated manufacturing system, the failure of operations will spread through industrial Programmable Logic Controller (PLC) networks and result in cascading failure. In this paper, a mathematical fault prediction approach is proposed that integrates the state space dynamics, graph theoretic influence modelling, stochastic Markov transition and entropy based uncertainty quantification to analyse the propagation of fault effects in real time. Adjacency and influence matrices are given an embedding weight and a Fault Propagation Index (FPI) is computed to estimate cascading risks between connected interconnected PLC modules. The hybrid Artificial Neural Network (ANN) based on graph inference mechanism is also found to offer better predictive learning ability in the streaming industrial PLC log. The experimental results for three industrial datasets with 14,720 operational hours, 1256 fault events and 96.8% prediction accuracy with the propagation delay estimation error of 12.4ms are far superior than other baseline methods like rule-based, CUSUM, ANN-only or Random Forest.</p>','["PLC Fault Propagation, State-Space Modelling, Graph-Theoretic Analysis, Markov Fault Transitions, Fault Propagation Index, Entropy-Based Uncertainty Quantification"]',NULL,18,1,'667 - 676','10.59543/jidmis.v3.683','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',56),
(684,684,1,'Combinatorial Testing Strategies for Privacy in Recommendation Engines','<p>Recommendation systems play an important role in helping users to discover relevant items out of large collections of content. However, the widespread use of information about people''s personal preferences raises concerns about privacy. In this research paper, we examine the combinatorial testing strategies to enhance privacy protection in recommendation engines without the detriment of prediction performance. The approach proposed includes a systematic evaluation of combinations of recommendation algorithms and privacy mechanisms by using the MovieLens 20M dataset. The data set has 20,000,263 user ratings and 465,564 tag applications on 27,278 movies created by 138,493 users between January 1995 and March 2015, which currently serves as a popular benchmark for recommender system research. The proposed combinatorial privacy testing model is compared with collaborative filtering, matrix factorization and deep neural recommendation methods. Experimental results show that the performance of the proposed model reaches better results with accuracy of 94.18%, precision of 93.40%, recall of 92.96% and F1-score of 93.18%. The results demonstrate that combinatorial privacy test can be effectively used to increase the reliability of recommendations or protect the information of users.</p>','["Combinatorial testing, privacy preservation, recommender systems, collaborative filtering, recommendation accuracy."]',NULL,18,1,'677 - 682','10.59543/jidmis.v3.684','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',57),
(685,685,1,'Combinatorial Testing Strategies for Social Media Analytics Pipelines','<p>Abstract social media analytics pipelines are a collection of processing steps, including: data ingestion, preprocessing, feature engineering, and model inference, that form complex configurations that can cause latent faults. This work explores combinatorial testing methods to perform analytic testing of pipeline configurations by using t-way interaction modeling. The framework determines the important parameter interactions, lessens the cost of testing and enhances reliability as well as robustness of big scale social media analytics frameworks.</p>','["Combinatorial Testing, Social Media Analytics, t-Way Interaction Testing, Configuration Testing, Data Pipeline Reliability, Software Quality Assurance"]',NULL,18,1,'683 - 690','10.59543/jidmis.v3.685','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',58),
(686,686,1,'Distributed Barrier Functions for Preventing Routing Manipulation','<p>Routing manipulation attacks pose a threat to the stability, reliability and trust in distributed communication networks since they modify path metrics or introduce false routing states. The paper suggests a distributed barrier functionality to avoid manipulation of routing by mathematical validated state validation and consensus-based aberrant detection. As a directed graph model of the network, barrier-based validation can be used to guarantee that routing variables fall within safe invariant sets. The methodology is compatible with the current routing schemes, which improves resilience, convergence stability, and attack toleration</p>','["Distributed Barrier Functions", "Routing Manipulation", "Directed Graph Model", "Consensus-Based Detection", "Network Resilience", "Secure Routing Algorithms"]',NULL,18,1,'691 - 698','10.59543/jidmis.v3.686','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',59),
(742,742,1,'High-Density Wi-Fi Network Model to Optimize Quality of Service in Educational Environments','<p>A reliable and high-quality internet promotes communication, access to real-time information and the creation of new knowledge, making it an area that educational institutions can no longer consider optional. However, poorly designed wireless networks often cause interference, frustrate users, and slow down academic activities. This study aims to understand how high-density Wi-Fi models improve quality of service in educational environments and to build and deploy a Cisco bottom-up approach as a framework. We conducted quantitative pre-testing based on a sample of 21 wireless media hosts selected through a non-probability convenience sample. Experimental design was followed. Significant improvements were noted in all monitored indicators: bandwidth decreased from 0.483 Mbps to 2.1 Mbps, an increase of 334.78%; latency decreased by 76.09%, from 194.09ms to 46.4ms; Jitter decreased by 95.89% (from 49.63 ms to 2.04 ms); and packet loss dropped from an average of 10.22% to 0%. These results show that high-density Wi-Fi network models significantly affect the quality of connectivity in educational settings, highlighting their value as a technology strategy that academic institutions of different sizes can adopt.</p>','["high-density Wi-Fi", "bandwidth", "latency", "jitter", "loss"]',NULL,18,1,'699 - 717','10.59543/jidmis.v3.742','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',60),
(750,750,1,'Secure Medical Data Management through Blockchain and Reversible Data Hiding in a Decentralized Healthcare Framework','<p>The fast development of digital healthcare systems has increased the necessity of medical data management mechanisms with high precision, interoperability, and protection of privacy. In this paper, a non-centralized healthcare system is described, which combines reversible data hiding (RDH) with blockchain technology to provide end-to-end security, authenticity, and tamper resistance to medical images and patient records. RDH module incorporates authentication capabilities in diagnostic images at the cost of clinical quality where PSNR is over 41 dB and SSIM is greater than 0.97. In the blockchain layer, all the transactions are verified by immutability and consensus-driven verification. According to experimental findings, validation based solely on blockchain can achieve accuracy of 91.2% integrity and the combination of RDH and blockchain is able to achieve 98.7% accuracy on cross-verification between on-chain hashes and embedded metadata. In the same way, the accuracy of tamper detection is found to be 87.5 percent with blockchain and 96.4 percent with the combined approach. In general, the suggested system provides an improved level of resistance to tampering and replay attacks without losing the quality of the images and reliable authentication. The framework gives a viable and scalable foundation to secure digital healthcare infrastructure that enhances patient trust and helps in sound data governance in the contemporary healthcare setup.</p>','["high-density Wi-Fi", "bandwidth", "latency", "jitter", "loss"]',NULL,18,1,'718 - 749','10.59543/jidmis.v3.750','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-01',61),
(1019,1019,1,'Cash Conversion Cycle as an Intelligent Predictor of Persistent Stock Price Dynamics in Artificial Intelligence–Driven Firms: An AI-Based Decision-Making Framework','<p>The research demonstrates that effective management of working capital, examined through the cash conversion cycle (CCC), provides economic relevant information which is not yet reflected in stock market. Researchers predicts that CCC stock price dynamics in the face of informational complexity and intangible intensity in these settings, focusing on leading large-cap companies in the field of artificial intelligence (AI): Meta, Tesla, Alphabet, Amazon, Microsoft, Apple, and Nvidia. The data period ranges from December 2012 to September 2025 with a monthly frequency, which provides a comprehensive overview of the interplay between operational efficiency and equity valuation over time. We present evidence of long-memory dependence and persistent equilibrium relationships between operational efficiency and market valuation using ARFIMA and fractionally cointegrated VAR (FCVAR) models. The continuity of CCC and stock prices is highly heterogeneous among firms, as confirmed by the unit root and fractional integration tests. The Granger causality analysis reveals that in a few companies, the CCC has a significant influence on stock price movements; in other companies, stock price movements influence the CCC; and in Tesla, no such significant relationship is found. The long-run equilibrium linkages between working capital efficiency and equity valuation are confirmed by the FCVAR estimates, which are all between very negative and moderately positive values of the cointegrating coefficients. The results imply that information is slowly embedded in prices, which plays a role in informational frictions. This research adds value to the prior studies about the relationship between working capital efficiency and stock prices. This study serves as an empirical basis for an AI-based decision-making system where the CCC has been used as an intelligent stock price dynamics predictor.</p>','["Cash Conversion Cycle", "Stock Prices", "Fractional Integration", "Causality Test", "FCVAR Model"]',NULL,18,1,'750 - 765','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1019','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-15',62),
(423,423,1,'Deep Reinforcement Learning Driven LiNbO3 Dual Parallel Mach-Zehnder Modulator for Photonic THz-ISAC Systems','<p>The last few years have witnessed exponential rise in Terahertz integrated sensing and communication (THz-ISAC) systems serving an array of applications across industries. However, guaranteeing error-free transmission under diverse channel conditions remains a challenge that becomes even severe over THz transmission. Despite the fact that dual-parallel Mach-Zehnder modulator (DP-MZM) plays decisive towards THz-ISAC systems, inappropriate drive voltage and bias voltage can impact bit-error-rate (BER), intensity and group delay performance. To address it, this research proposes a novel multi-reward deep reinforcement learning (DRL) driven Ge-LiNbO3 heterogeneously integrated DP-MZM modulator architecture for THz-ISAC systems. The proposed Ge-LiNbO3 opto-electronic modulator makes use of linear frequency modulation-quadrature amplitude modulation (LFM-QAM) waveforms that enables high-data-rate communication while maintaining millimeter-level sensing resolution within the THz frequency spectrum. It leverages DP-MZM, integrated LFM-QAM and Ge-LiNbO3, where the first enabled BER reduction, while later with the adaptive waveforms improves sensing resolution performance. Ge-LiNbO3 improves THz efficiency and polarization control to meet ISAC communication demands. To achieve it, a robust multi-DRL framework is proposed that exploits state space characteristics including MZM voltage, graphene Fermi level, channel conditions, chirp-bandwidth, and QAM-order to update action vector (say, control vector) by updating drive voltage, bias voltage, chirp-bandwidth, chirp-rate reconfiguration, continuously. To meet target performance the proposed DRL model applied multi-reward function embodying BER, error-vector-magnitude (EVM), intensity and group delay information. Designing a robust opto-electronic modulator with Ge-LiNbO3 plasmonic characteristics, channel simulation and multi-reward DRL-based control vector optimization (i.e., drive voltage, bias voltage and wave characteristics (modulation order, fermi-level and chirp bandwidth)), the proposed model was tested under the different signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) conditions. Simulations confirmed that the proposed Ge-LiNbO3 heterogeneously integrated DP-MZM modulator achieves more than 300 Gbps transmission while maintaining sensing resolution less than 2 mm. Additionally, it showed 30%-35% improved efficiency gains, 25% reduced BER depicting its robustness towards run-time THz-ISAC communication under mobility. Depth performance characterization revealed that the proposed Ge-LiNbO3 DP-MZM modulator outperforms traditional LiNbO3 in terms of reduced error vector magnitude (EVM) (60%), improved output intensity (15%) and reduced BER performance under the diverse range of SNR (10-40 dB). The overall results and allied inferences confirm suitability of the proposed multi-reward DRL-driven Ge-LiNbO3 DP-MZM modulator for 6G THz-ISAC communication systems.</p>','["THz-ISAC systems, Opto-Electronic Modulator, Deep-Reinforcement Learning, Graphene Plasmonic, Ge-LiNbO3 Hybrid Material Modulator."]',NULL,16,1,'01 - 29','10.59543/jidmis.v3.423','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',1),
(424,424,1,'Deep Learning-Based Prediction of Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease Severity and Progression Using Respiratory Sounds','<p>Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD) is a progressive lung disease with over 300 million getting effected worldwide. The accuracy of traditional methods of diagnosis relies on the expertise of the doctor. As technology progresses, with help of artificial intelligence the accuracy of diagnosis is further improved. But accurate detection of the sub-stages of COPD is still a major challenge. This work proposes a model to classify COPD into its sub categories based on GOLD staging with an accuracy of 83.33%. Further the work also predicts the chances of the individual’s disease progression to the next higher stage using four expert models namely COPD0-1, COPD1-2, COPD2-3, and COPD3-4. The accuracy of these expert models are 87.5%, 90.00%, 90.48% and 61.90% respectively. The proposed work sets a new benchmark in diagnosing COPD and predicting its progression to the next higher stage. This will help in better treatment to the individual with better patient outcomes.</p>','["COPD classification, COPD severity prediction, CONV2D, expert model, deep learning for COPD prediction"]',NULL,16,1,'30 - 42','10.59543/jidmis.v3.424','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',2),
(425,425,1,'The Deep Learning Based Segmentation And Multi-Classification of Skin Lesions Using Grab Cut with Flood Filling','<p>An expansion of atypical cell development is generated by various chronic variations as well as genetic disorders. The skin cancer is usually detected optically by medical screening through dermoscopic determination. Though, automated skin lesion segmentation and classification is difficult due to the visual correlation among benign as well as melanoma lesions. In this research, a Grab cut segmentation with Flood Filling (FF) is proposed for an effective segmentation and multi-classification of skin lesions. The lesion image datasets named HAM10000 and ISIC2019 are utilized to estimate a performance of a proposed method. The pre-processing techniques of Hair removal and Noise removal is used for achieve the better performance on the classification results. The DenseNet-169 architecture is utilized for the feature extraction process and Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) is utilized for a classification process. The classification algorithm classifies the input images as various types of skin lesion such as Melanoma (MEL), Melanocytic Nevi (MN), Basal Cell Carcinoma (BCC), Actinic Keratosis (AK) and Benign Keratosis (BKL). The results shows that the proposed delivers performance metrics like accuracy, sensitivity, precision and F1-score of values about 98.89%, 99.38%, 98.01%, 97.28% and 98.85% respectively, which ensures better results compared with the existing method of ResNet50 CNN with SVM</p>','["Convolutional Neural Network, DenseNet-169, multi-classification, segmentation and skin lesion classification."]',NULL,16,1,'43 - 56','10.59543/jidmis.v3.425','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',3),
(426,426,1,'Performance and Robustness of Tree-Based Models, Neural Networks, and Ordinal Regression in Learning-to-Rank Systems','<p>Learning-to-rank (LTR) models drive modern search and recommendation systems, where improvements in ranking accuracy directly influence user exposure and decision outcomes (Liu, 2009; Burges, 2010). Despite extensive empirical comparisons in the LTR literature, there is no clear consensus on the relative performance of regression-based, tree-based, and neural rankers. This study addresses this gap by introducing a theory-driven comparative framework that links ranking performance to data characteristics and learning mechanisms. Drawing on bias–variance trade-offs and representation learning principles, we identify four key determinants of ranking performance: model complexity, data noise, feature redundancy, and learning formulation. These factors are examined through two complementary studies conducted on six public datasets, including LETOR collections (Qin et al., 2010). Results indicate a systematic but data-dependent ordering of model families, providing principled guidance for ranking model selection under practical constraints. The findings provide actionable insights for selecting ranking models in real-world systems, considering factors such as data quality, scalability, and computational cost.</p>','["Learning-to-Rank", "Model Selection", "Ranking Accuracy", "Neural Rankers", "Tree-Based Ensembles", "Ordinal Learning", "Bias\\u2013Variance Trade-off", "Robustness Analysis", "Information Retrieval"]',NULL,16,1,'57 - 77','10.59543/jidmis.v3.426','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',4),
(427,427,1,'Dietary Decision-Making for Diabetes Management using a Simplified Policy Gradient Algorithm','<p>Lifestyle is an overwhelming challenge to long-term management of glucose in diabetes patients as dietary habits have a significant effect on the rising and falling blood sugar level. Through this exploration, we propose a reinforcement learning model to optimistically simulate the selection of meals so that blood glucose is continued to be kept within healthy ranges. An environment DiabetesEnv was custom-written that could simulate glucose dynamics, based on the intake of carbohydrates in food, and using a simple reward scheme that favors decisions that reduce glucose spikes. One of the basic policy gradient agents was trained on 10,000 episodes using real recipe data. Although there was a stabilization at the beginning, the agent had a limited learning force, and final rewards were still unpleasant, as glucose levels continued to increase throughout the testing. It has been shown that mere policy updates and state descriptions cannot be used in analyzing glucose regulation in its complexities. The enhanced state properties, more sophisticated reward shaping and additional sophisticated reinforcement learning algorithms to enhance dietary advice are highlighted in our findings. This paper provides the background of future studies that can be conducted to enable adaptive and personalized dietary interventions based on reinforcement learning methods</p>','["Substrate Integrated Waveguide (SIW), Square slot tuning, quarter mode (QM), Slot antenna, sub6, mm-wave."]',NULL,16,1,'78 - 94','10.59543/jidmis.v3.427','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',5),
(428,428,1,'A Novel Congestion Avoidance Technique to Optimize the QoS Parameters in Wireless Sensor Network Using Semi-Superwised Machine Learning','<p>Congestion control in Wireless Sensor Network is a significant area which has to be addressed for smooth and seamless packet transfer from source to the destination node. Existing literatures have deployed various congestion control schemes to reduce the congestion in network., which are based on congestion and their severity, the remedial mechanisms are applied to overcome the problem of congestion. The congestion can be caused because of Channel occupancy, high reporting rate and quick buffer occupancy. Various techniques have been proposed and implemented to overcome the congestion problem which manages channel occupancy, reporting rate and buffer occupancy. Hence it motivates to propose some alternative technique which avoids the congestion itself. In this paper, congestion avoidance technique is proposed, as it causes severe problem in the network performance such as packet loss, wastage of processing power, inefficient resource utilization, and other Quality of Service parameters. Here the proposed technique named Congestion Avoidance Technique (CAT) a buffer management technique.  Every node is attached with input and output buffer which has limited capacity. This technique efficiently manages the buffers of sensing nodes by continuously monitoring the buffer occupancy. CAT always ensures that the buffer contents are always in between allowable congestion window that is between PTM and LTM marking of the buffer. Here proposed technique avoids the congestion in WSN and ultimately increase the QoS parameters of the network. CAT is implemented using experimental scenario in NS2 and the results are analyzed which shows that the buffer is always occupied between congestion windowongestion control in Wireless Sensor Network is a significant area which has to be addressed for smooth and seamless packet transfer from source to the destination node. Existing literatures have deployed various congestion control schemes to reduce the congestion in network., which are based on congestion and their severity, the remedial mechanisms are applied to overcome the problem of congestion. The congestion can be caused because of Channel occupancy, high reporting rate and quick buffer occupancy. Various techniques have been proposed and implemented to overcome the congestion problem which manages channel occupancy, reporting rate and buffer occupancy. Hence it motivates to propose some alternative technique which avoids the congestion itself. In this paper, congestion avoidance technique is proposed, as it causes severe problem in the network performance such as packet loss, wastage of processing power, inefficient resource utilization, and other Quality of Service parameters. Here the proposed technique named Congestion Avoidance Technique (CAT) a buffer management technique.  Every node is attached with input and output buffer which has limited capacity. This technique efficiently manages the buffers of sensing nodes by continuously monitoring the buffer occupancy. CAT always ensures that the buffer contents are always in between allowable congestion window that is between PTM and LTM marking of the buffer. Here proposed technique avoids the congestion in WSN and ultimately increase the QoS parameters of the network. CAT is implemented using experimental scenario in NS2 and the results are analyzed which shows that the buffer is always occupied between congestion window.</p>','["Wireless Sensor Network, CAT, PTM, LTM, AODV, PDR, Network Simulator."]',NULL,16,1,'95 - 112','10.59543/jidmis.v3.428','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',6),
(429,429,1,'A Comprehensive Machine Learning–Driven Predictive Modeling Approach for Early Detection and Risk Assessment of Chronic Kidney Disease Using Clinical and Laboratory Data','<p>Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD) is a global public health emergency affecting more than 850 million individuals worldwide, with a significant proportion remaining undiagnosed until advanced, irreversible stages. Late diagnosis substantially increases patient morbidity, mortality, and healthcare expenditure. Machine learning (ML) has emerged as a transformative technology capable of identifying complex, nonlinear patterns within heterogeneous clinical and laboratory datasets, offering new pathways for early and accurate disease detection. Existing predictive models for CKD suffer from class imbalance in clinical datasets, insufficient handling of missing laboratory values, poor interpretability limiting clinician trust, and limited generalizability across ethnically diverse populations. These limitations motivate the development of robust, explainable, and equitable ML pipelines tailored specifically for CKD risk stratification. This study proposes a comprehensive ML-driven predictive framework integrating multi-source clinical and laboratory data from 12,480 patients across five tertiary care centers. The pipeline incorporates advanced preprocessing including multiple imputation by chained equations (MICE) for missing data, Synthetic Minority Oversampling Technique (SMOTE) for class imbalance, and SHAP-based feature selection. Three core algorithms are implemented: an optimized XGBoost ensemble, a Bidirectional LSTM network for temporal biomarker progression, and a stacking meta-learner combining five base classifiers. The proposed ensemble framework achieved an overall accuracy of 97.4%, AUC-ROC of 0.973, sensitivity of 96.8%, and specificity of 97.1% on the held-out test set across all five CKD stages. The XGBoost classifier individually attained an F1-score of 0.965, outperforming single-model baselines by margins of 4–9%. Ten-fold cross-validation confirmed robust generalizability with a mean accuracy of 96.2% ± 0.4%. The proposed framework demonstrates that integrating ensemble learning with temporal deep learning and SHAP-driven interpretability yields a clinically viable decision-support tool for early CKD detection and risk stratification. The system holds significant potential for deployment in resource-constrained healthcare settings, enabling proactive nephrology interventions.</p>','["chronic kidney disease, machine learning, XGBoost, predictive modeling, clinical decision support, early detection"]',NULL,16,1,'113 - 131','10.59543/jidmis.v3.429','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',7),
(430,430,1,'Sentiment Classification using Various Feature Extraction and Hybrid Machine Learning Techniques','<p>Sentiment classification is essential for gaining valuable knowledge from vast amounts of user-generated content in social media, review sites, and forums. However, noisy text, varying language, sparse features, and limited generalization from single-feature or single-classifier methodologies make highly accurate classification difficult. This paper proposes a comprehensive framework for sentiment classification. The proposed system aims to improve classification performance and scalability by integrating more sophisticated text preprocessing, various feature extraction methods, and hybrid machine learning models. The framework consists of several modular components, including data collection, preprocessing and normalization, feature extraction and selection, classification, and evaluation of performance. The text preprocessing component involves various methods, including data cleansing, normalization, stopping removal, and lemmatization, to improve data quality. In the feature extraction component, lemmatized tokens, N-grams, and linguistic features (including punctuation and emoticon counts) are combined and transformed using the TF-IDF method. In the feature selection component, a statistical method is applied to keep highly relevant features and reduce noise and dimensionality. Within this scope of work, three machine learning algorithms, namely, Artificial Neural Network (ANN), Support Vector Machine (SVM), and Naïve Bayes (NB), are used for the implementation of the respective sentiment classifications, along with an ensemble hybrid model that combines the outputs of multiple classifiers for the sake of exploiting their complementary benefits. The evaluation of performance is conducted on the metrics of accuracy, precision, recall, and F1 score, on datasets of diverse sizes. The experimental results confirm that the performance of sentiment classification is enhanced using the representation of hybrid features. The most optimal result on mid-sized datasets was achieved through the combined representation of NLP and machine learning hybrid features, with an accuracy of 92.59%. Moreover, on large datasets, the hybrid features based on the lemmas demonstrated the best scalability. The proposed system demonstrates the most optimal results on mid-sized datasets through the combined representation of NLP and machine learning hybrid features. Furthermore, on large datasets, the hybrid features based on the lemmas demonstrated the best scalability. The results support the documentation for the analysis of the feature layer complexity and the combination of multiple classifiers in an ensemble learning framework to build a more robust model for sentiment classification with scalability. </p>','["Sentiment Analysis, Hybrid Machine Learning, Feature Extraction, Text Classification, Natural Language Processing, TF-IDF"]',NULL,16,1,'132 - 151','10.59543/jidmis.v3.430','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',8),
(431,431,1,'A Blockchain-Based Framework for Secure Electronic Health Records with Efficient Data Management and Role-Based Access Control','<p>The speed of digitalization in the healthcare sector has brought the benefits of clinical efficiency, continuity of care and data-driven decision making through the adoption of Electronic Health Records (EHRs). However, due to the centralized nature of most traditional architectures of EHR systems, there are security, data integrity, unauthorized access, and privacy threats. The management of sensitive medical data in a manner that is secure and scalable, and to which the various stakeholders in the health care ecosystem as data controllers, is a challenging problem. The study develops a novel framework to address the problem of secure management of electronic health records using blockchain technology, with enhanced data efficiency and role-based access control (RBAC). The framework uses a pseudonymized permissioned blockchain to achieve decentralization, immutability, and transparency of the healthcare data transactions. To address the blockchain storage problem, the framework uses a hybrid data management approach, whereby medical records are encrypted and stored off chain. The on-chain system, however, stores cryptographic hashes and access control policies. The RBAC system is enforced using smart contracts, whereby access rights are assigned based on the roles of the stakeholders in the frameworks (e.g., physician, patient, administrator, insurer). The framework relies on crypto-based technology for data encryption, a hashing algorithm for integrity, and smart contracts for access control. The blockchain consensus frameworks meant for the healthcare sector are adopted to limit the time and computational resources required. The system has been evaluated, and results show there have been improvements in data security, and protection as no unauthorized access can occur. Records are kept in an untampered manner. Retrieval of data as well as record keeping is efficient. Transaction throughput has been maintained, access control accuracy has been improved, data access latency has been lowered, and system scalability has been improved in comparison to centralized EHR systems. Overall, the blockchain framework proposed shows potential in the EHR systems as an adaptable, secure, scalable, and robust framework. Utilizing extremely secure, private, and interoperable systems, the future of healthcare information systems can utilize the presented framework.</p>','["Blockchain, Electronic Health Records, Role-Based Access Control, Smart Contracts, Data Security, Decentralized Healthcare Systems"]',NULL,16,1,'152 - 166','10.59543/jidmis.v3.431','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',9),
(432,432,1,'IoT-Based Automated Cooking System Integrated with Reinforcement Learning for Customizing and Optimizing the Cooking Process','<p>Historically, food preparation has required extensive knowledge, time, and effort and has made food preparation practically impossible for people with limited mobility, chronic illnesses, or those who live fast paced and busy lives. Additionally, the inability to achieve targeted goals related to nutrition or to maintain them during the food preparation process drastically increases the incidence of related health problems. Common automated cooking devices are still anaemic and lack the required flexibility or the specialized real-time control to address specific user needs and integrate variable and nutritional feedback to control the devices. Therefore, there exists a significant divide between smart cooking technology and individualized health managing technology. This work proposes an automated cooking system that consists of three modules and operates via the Internet of Things (IoT) and Reinforcement Learning (RL) to optimize ingredient portions, heat distribution, and time intervals for the preparation and cooking sequence based on real-time feedback from the user and the command control system to achieve nutritious food preparation goals. System architecture comprises two modules that are a) an IoT control system based on an Arduino/ESP32/Raspberry Pi platform including weight, temperature, and gas and level sensors, and b) a Graphical User Interface (GUI) and Recipe/Nutrition engine built on a global nutrition database. The third module consists of a cloud connected Reports and Recommendations engine, which monitors consumptive behaviors over periods of a day, week, and month. For the proposed system, Q-Learning with deep neural networks as function approximators and deep Q-Networks (DQNs) is employed, and the Mult objective adaptive Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) recipe algorithm is employed. Experimental evaluations showed that meal preparation was accurate to a rate of 96.4%, with the nutritional target being achieved 94.7%, across 1,200 cooking sessions. The RL agent achieved an optimal cooking policy within 340 sessions on average, which resulted in an average time savings of 28.3% compared to the other systems. After 8 weeks of personalized learning, they reduced energy consumption by 19.6% and improved user satisfaction by 31.2% per meal cycle. The proposed system proves that there are positive implications across many fields including public health, food security, and assistive technology for integrating IoT hardware and deep Reinforcement Learning for completely automated, nutritionally personalized cooking.</p>','["IoT-based cooking automation, reinforcement learning, deep Q-network, nutritional personalisation, smart kitchen, recipe optimisation"]',NULL,16,1,'167 - 186','10.59543/jidmis.v3.432','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',10),
(433,433,1,'Rice Disease Detection Using an Optimized Modified Lightweight Convolutional Neural Network','<p>Rice is an important staple food crop in the world, but the yield is dramatically lowered by fungal diseases including brown spot, leaf blast and neck blast. Thus, disease detection algorithms that are not only computationally efficient but also accurate enough are crucial for the real-time application of precision agriculture systems. In this paper, an Optimized Convolutional Neural Network from Modified Lightweight Weight Convolutional Neural Network (MLWCNN) is proposed to multi-class rice disease classification for the purpose of achieving high accuracy with low computational complexity. The proposed MLWCNN is tested on a Kaggle public data set, which comprises the rice leaf disease dataset as well as sub-images of healthy and diseased leaves with 3-Fold and 5-Fold cross-validation for robustness and unbiased performance evaluation. Experimental results show that they converge consistently well on objective and generalize well under various validation settings. With the strictest evaluation setup (i.e., 5-Fold cross-validation and a training stage of 30 epochs) the novel model demonstrated a validation accuracy just under around 94%. A comparison with state-of-the-art deep learning networks such as wide VGG16, InceptionV3, ResNet50, ResNet152 SqueezeNet and MobileNetV2 on typical datasets proves that the proposed MLWCNN achieves competitive classification accuracy with a higher speedup. Analyzing the class‐wise performance using confusion matrix, balanced prediction characteristics throughout all disease categories are observed. Moreover, computational cost analysis demonstrates that the proposed model needs much less floating-point operations (0.000095 GFLOPs) than deep models, which is ideal for deployment in resource-limited mobile, edge and embedded agricultural applications. The experimental results demonstrate that the MLWCNN achieved high accuracy, thereby providing a practical and scalable solution for automated rice disease detection in precision agriculture.</p>','["Rice Plant disease, Deep Learning, Convolutional Neural Network, Modified Light Weight Convolutional Neural Network (MLWCNN)"]',NULL,16,1,'187 - 204','10.59543/jidmis.v3.433','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',11),
(434,434,1,'Blockchain based QR Codes for Detection of Fake Medicines using Customized Proof of Authentic Validation','<p>Blockchain technology has become a vital component in driving innovation and solving long-standing issues across various industries. In healthcare, blockchain offers transformative potential by enabling transparent, tamper-proof, and decentralized systems for tracking and verifying critical assets. One major area of concern is the healthcare supply chain, which faces persistent challenges such as counterfeit drugs, lack of end-to-end traceability, security breaches, increased operational costs, and delays in delivering essential medical supplies. To address these issues, we propose a blockchain-enabled healthcare supply chain system that ensures secure and transparent tracking of medical products from manufacturers to end-users. Fake medicine detection using blockchain baes QR code with customized Proof of Authentic Validation (PoAV) system, which employs blockchain technology in conjunction with QR code verification to authenticate medicinal products. The system establishes a semi-centralized validation framework in which a central authority defines compliance criteria and selects validators while transaction verification takes place in a decentralized manner. Each batch of medicine is coded with unique QR codes generated by hashes of its metadata, stored securely on the blockchain. In the supply chain, validators confirm each step according to set rules and affix their digital signatures which allows for block formation after reaching consensus thresholds. Counterfeit detection and isolation are supported through emergency response mechanisms, enabling real-time verification by users through QR code scanning. Exhaustive tests conducted on the model showed outstanding performance metrics with 99.5% accuracy in detection counterfeit threats—a major advancement toward containing the circulation of counterfeit drugs. This provided framework offers guaranteed traceability without exposure to manipulation throughout the documentation process while validating, making it useful within highly regulated scenarios in healthcare systems.</p>','["Blockchain, Healthcare Supply Chain, QR Code, Smart Contract, Data Security, Consensus, Detection Accuracy."]',NULL,16,1,'205 - 226','10.59543/jidmis.v3.434','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',12),
(435,435,1,'Bird Detection in Agricultural Environments Using a Customized Convolutional Neural Network Model with Faster R-CNN Framework','<p>The presence of birds in crop fields is a big problem worldwide and affects the economy significantly where annual crop losses happen to a variety of important food and cash crops. Old bird scare techniques such as the use of scarecrows, noise making devices, and even field patrols are not going to help as birds can be in and out of a field in a matter of minutes. New computer vision and deep learning technologies, once integrated with agricultural monitoring systems, can help with real time, low to no latency, and automated bird detection. This will allow for immediate response mechanisms to be activated for bird deterrent devices. However, there is a wide range of technical issues. There are also crop growth clans, their flying posture, visual positioning and changes, and their clustered formations. Real time processed edge devices also have a lot of technical issues. There is also a lack of large bird detection dataset specifically designed for deep learning in agriculture monitoring to overcome the many difficulties defined above. The proposed Edge CNN model is based on the Faster R-CNN model, employing ResNet-50 with Feature Pyramid Network (FPN). A domain-specific fine-tuning approach is implemented using a custom bird detection dataset. This approach incorporates elements for frame extraction, preprocessing, and FPN based multi-scale feature extraction, region proposal-based candidate generation, region-of-interest (RoI) pooling, and a custom designed classification and bounding box regression head aimed at detection of bird targets. The proposed system is driven by three main algorithmic contributions, (i) a customized multi-scale Faster R-CNN detection pipeline with a multi-scale feature (FPN based) out of region anchoring, (ii) an augmented focal loss training approach for addressing the issue of imbalanced classes of background and foreground, and (iii) a dynamic Non-Maximum Suppression (NMS) with varying IoU based thresholding for removal of redundant detection. The algebraic expressions are thoroughly crafted for each of the proposed algorithmic modules. The custom CNN model proposed outperformed the baseline models (YOLO, SSD-MobileNet and ResNet-50) by 10.5, 12.0 and 8.2 percentage points respectively. The model achieved a mean Average Precision (mAP) of 95.3% at IoU threshold 0.5 in the evaluation dataset, a GPU based per frame processing speed of 25 fps and an overall F1-score of 95.3%. The system shows strong detection ability capabilities showing difficult light, obstruction, and flight speed conditions that are present in realistic agricultural settings. The suggested customized CNN approach provides a solution for real-time bird detection in crop surveillance scenarios which is high-performing, cost-effective, and computationally viable. Results encourage the development of multi-species classification, edge deployment, and integration with systems that automatically actuate deterrents. These results also support the use of domain-adapted deep learning for precision agriculture.</p>','["Bird Detection", "Faster R-CNN", "Custom CNN", "Agricultural Surveillance", "Deep Learning", "Real-Time Object Detection."]',NULL,16,1,'227 - 242','10.59543/jidmis.v3.435','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',13),
(436,436,1,'SF-ViT: Harnessing Complementary Spatial and Spectral Representations for Robust Deepfake Detection','<p>Recent progress in generative modelling made deepfake production visually convincing and potentially dangerous for digital forensics, security, and media trust. Current deepfake detectors are mainly based on spatial semantic cues or handcrafted frequency features, and then they are sensitive to high-quality operations, compression artifacts and domain changes. This paper introduces a Sparse-Focus Vision Transformer (SF-ViT), that jointly takes advantage of spatial and frequency-domain representations under a unified cross-modal architecture. The model utilizes a sparsity-based patch-selection mechanism to focus on manipulation-likely facial areas, a frequency-token generator unit that exploits log-magnitude Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) maps for better capturing of Generative Adversarial Network (GAN)-induced spectral distortions, and a transformer-based structural fusion module which aggregates both spatial and spectral Classification Token (CLS) token information for better forensics. Experiments on the Face Forensics++ dataset show that SF-ViT highly advances Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) and transformer baselines with 0.96 Area Under the Curve (AUC) where false-positive rates and false-negative rates are extremely low. Ablation studies further validate the independent and complementary roles of sparse patch selection, frequency encoding and fusion attention and each component contributes noticeable performance gains. The results demonstrate that SF-ViT can effectively model both semantic and spectral inconsistency of modern deepfake generation pipelines, achieving strong robustness against compression, and generalizing better than spatial-only or frequency-only detection methods. We believe there is tremendous potential for SF-ViT in scalable forensic applications, authenticity verification of social-media contents, and automated content moderation with possible deployment to cloud.</p>','["Deepfake Detection, Multimedia Forensics, Vision Transformer, Frequency Domain Analysis, Multi-modal Fusion, Video Forgery Detection"]',NULL,16,1,'243 - 263','10.59543/jidmis.v3.436','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',14),
(437,437,1,'Personality Classification Using SMOTE For Data Balancing and Machine Learning Algorithms','<p>Individual distinctions in how people think, act, and interact with the world are usually classified or understood through personality classification. The technique of applying different algorithms to forecast a person''s personality traits according to their behavior, preferences, or reactions to stimuli is known as personality prediction using machine learning algorithms. There are various ways to approach this task, including employing behavioral data like click behavior or gaming patterns or text data like posted on social media posts, conversations, and votes. There are several different models for classifying personalities. Some of the most often used are like Big-five factor model, Myre-Briggs Type Indicator (MBTI), DISC personality model. These models seek to explain many aspects of personality and assist people in comprehending their own approaches to relationships, life, and the workplace. The process of personality classification involves different tasks like data collection, feature extraction, application of different machine learning algorithms, etc. Data can be collected by Self-Report Questionnaires, Interviews, Observations, Psychological Tests.  Various areas where personality classification is applicable include Human Resources &amp; Recruitment, Psychotherapy &amp; Counseling, Education, Marketing &amp; Consumer Behavior, Personal Development, Healthcare &amp; Wellness, Law Enforcement &amp; Security, social media &amp; Online Communities, Sports and Performance, etc. In this paper we represent the different aspects of the researchers have applied for personality classification including methods to collect data, extracting features and algorithms used for classification. We have implemented several machine learning approaches, including logistic regression, support vector machine and random forest classifier. In addition, we used the synthetic minority oversampling technique (SMOTE) to address the issue of imbalanced data. The results showed that our approach could achieve a relatively high accuracy for each traits of the chosen model for predicting and classifying MBTI personality</p>','["Personality classification, Myre-Briggs Type Indicator (MBTI), Big-five factor model, machine learning, social media"]',NULL,16,1,'264 - 278','10.59543/jidmis.v3.437','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',15),
(438,438,1,'A Novel Image-Based Framework for Air Quality Analysis Using Deep Learning Techniques','<p>Air pollution remains a significant issue in many developing nations. Due to the limited number of air quality monitoring stations in urban areas and no monitoring in rural areas, residents often lack access to real-time, location-specific air quality information. However, the widespread presence of surveillance cameras, which can continuously capture outdoor scenes, opens up new opportunities. These cameras could serve as alternative sensors for collecting environmental data and inferring pollution levels. In response, this research introduces an innovative method for estimating air quality levels based on images captured in outdoor settings. The model introduced in this study presents a promising approach for fast and dependable estimation and classification of multiple air quality levels based on the Air Quality Index (AQI) using sky images. This scalable technique has the potential to bridge critical gaps in air quality monitoring, especially given the limited availability and high cost of traditional monitoring equipment used worldwide. By leveraging image processing techniques, we extract the features and utilize them for environmental monitoring, public safety, and research purposes. Extracted features are incorporated with Deep Learning techniques.  The primary goal of this work is to propose an image-based air quality prediction framework powered by Deep Air Learning (DAL). Seven features extracted from the images were used as input to the model. Different features have different significance levels in the prediction. We present a method that utilizes sky imagery combined with Deep Neural Network (DNN) algorithms to evaluate air quality. The classification models examined in this study compared with Decision Tree, k-Nearest Neighbors, Random Forest, and Gradient Boosting. Among these, proposed DAL model demonstrated the best overall performance, achieving an average accuracy of 91%.</p>','["Air Quality Index (AQI), Deep Air Learning (DAL), Deep Neural Network (DNN), Decision Tree, k-Nearest Neighbors (kNN), Random Forest, Gradient Boosting"]',NULL,16,1,'279 - 289','10.59543/jidmis.v3.438','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',16);
