(440,440,1,'Decision Tree-Based Synthetic Data Generation Framework for Privacy-Preserving Data Publishing','<p>The growing demand for data sharing in healthcare, scientific research, and public policy raises an ongoing challenge of protecting individual privacy while keeping data useful for analysis. This research introduces a Decision Tree-based synthetic data generation method, DTSDG and evaluates its performance using six well-known anonymization techniques. , namely, ‘k-Anonymity’, ‘l-Diversity’, ‘t-Closeness’, ‘Differential Privacy (DP)’,  ‘Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs)’, and ‘Copula-GAN’ The proposed method creates synthetic records step by step. Each attribute is predicted using a trained decision tree, which offers a balance of interpretability, efficiency, and accuracy. A composite scoring framework combines normalized Nearest Neighbor Distance (NND), Jensen–Shannon Divergence (JSD), and Wasserstein Distance into a unified metric is employed to assess how effectively each of these methods balance the conflicting performance measures: the privacy preservation and the data utility. Experiments were conducted using six diverse datasets, namely, ‘Adult’, ‘ATUS’, ‘FARS’, ‘Breast Cancer Wisconsin’, ‘cardiovascular disease’, ‘Heart Disease Cleveland’. The experimental results demonstrate that the proposed DTSDG method consistently achieves stable and competitive scores across all these datasets providing the best balance between privacy preservation and the data utility. The proposed DTSDG approach could be a practical and promising solution for publishing data while protecting privacy. This is especially true in situations where computational resources are limited or there is a strong need for model transparency.</p>','["Data Privacy, Anonymization, Data Utility, Decision Tree, Synthetic Data Generation."]',NULL,16,1,'290 - 308','10.59543/jidmis.v3.440','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',17),
(441,441,1,'Attention-Guided Hybrid Deep Learning for Imbalance-Robust Breast Cancer Subtype Classification','<p>Breast cancer subtype classification from histopathological images is challenging due to severe class imbalance and subtle inter-class morphological variations. While deep learning methods have shown success in binary diagnosis, robust multi-class subtype discrimination under imbalanced conditions remains insufficiently addressed. This study proposes an imbalance-aware attention-guided hybrid deep learning framework for multi-class breast cancer subtype classification using the BreakHis dataset. The proposed pipeline integrates overlapping patch extraction with brightness-based filtering, preprocessing optimization using a Bilateral–Unsharp enhancement strategy, synthetic minority augmentation via Wasserstein GAN with Gradient Penalty (WGAN-GP), controlled majority-class down sampling, and focal loss optimization. A dual-branch DenseNet–Xception architecture with Convolutional Block Attention Module (CBAM) refinement is employed to capture complementary multi-scale representations. Experimental results demonstrate progressive improvement from 73.22% testing accuracy on raw patches to 91.42% after preprocessing and further to 92.97% following imbalance-aware synthetic augmentation, achieving a macro-F1 score of 0.9314 and macro-AUC of 0.997. Comparative evaluation against pretrained, custom, and alternative hybrid models confirms the superiority of the proposed framework. The findings highlight the importance of integrating preprocessing optimization, imbalance-aware training, and hybrid attention-guided architectures for reliable multi-class breast cancer subtype classification.</p>','["Breast cancer subtype classification", "Histopathological image analysis", "Class imbalance learning", "Wasserstein GAN (WGAN-GP)", "Attention-guided hybrid networks", "DenseNet\\u2013Xception architecture", "multi-class medical image classification."]',NULL,16,1,'309 - 332','10.59543/jidmis.v3.441','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',18),
(442,442,1,'Secure Hybrid Cryptographic and Machine Learning–Driven Framework for Automated E-Invoicing Integration in Cloud-Based ERP Systems','<p>Enterprise Resource Planning (ERP) integration with government compliance systems has become a pressing need as enterprise finance rapidly turns into a digital landscape with digital taxation infrastructures. The concept of e-invoicing through the invoice Registration Portal (IRP) in India is based on the Goods and Services Tax (GST) implementation, which will involve organizations in the process of submitting invoice details for validation and generating the Invoice Reference Number (IRN). Current e-invoicing deployments are mainly based on half-automated synchronization approaches or traditional API-based communication systems that lack inherent security, synchronization issues, auditability, authorization mechanisms and heavy operational burden. In this research, a novel implementation-oriented secure e-invoicing framework for cloud-based ERP system with the implementation platform Microsoft Dynamics 365 F&amp;O is proposed. The proposed architecture features a consistent secure outbound and inbound synchronization algorithm, which includes AES-256 payload encryption, RSA key wrapping, PBKDF2 key derivation, TLS 1.2 transport security, badge-based authentication, Role-Based Access Control (RBAC), replay attack prevention, secure API orchestration, automated generation of QR-codes and audit-driven synchronization management. To boost system intelligence and adaptive security, a Machine Learning based Anomaly Detection Layer is integrated to review invoice activity from transactions, API request patterns and synchronization logs. This layer calculates dynamic risk scores to detect anomalies in normal operating behaviour, and provides proactive security enforcement in the ERP environment. This is achieved through X++ customization, REST-based API orchestration, cryptographic processing modules and native batch execution services within the ERP. Inside the ERP environment, the proposed algorithm automatically retrieves and validates invoices, issues intelligent risk ratings, encrypts them, securely transmits them, compares the content of the responses, synchronizes the acknowledgment, attaches a QR code, and logs audits. Experimental analysis shows that the proposed framework can significantly enhance the reliability of synchronization, security robustness, intelligence-driven threat detection, transparency, data integrity, scalability and operational efficiency over the traditional and existing cloud-based approach. The study presents a novel, implementation-oriented intelligent algorithmic framework for secure communication between ERP and Government portal and lays the foundation for scalable future adaptive digital taxonomies.</p>','["Cloud ERP, D365, E-Invoicing, AES-256, RSA Encryption, PBKDF2, GST Portal Integration, Secure API Synchronization, X++, RBAC."]',NULL,16,1,'333 - 347','10.59543/jidmis.v3.442','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',19),
(443,443,1,'An Interpretable Hybrid Deep Learning Framework for Speech Emotion Recognition Using Self-Supervised Representations and Bidirectional Sequence Modelling.','<p>This study presents a Wav2Vec2-BiLSTM hybrid model for speech emotion recognition on the TESS dataset, achieving 85% accuracy surpassing CNN (78%) and SVM (72%) baselines. Raw audio undergoes parallel feature extraction: frozen Wav2Vec2 provides 768-dim contextual embeddings while prosodic features (RMS energy, ZCR) add acoustic nuance, forming a 770-dim input to a 2-layer bidirectional LSTM (256 hidden units) with dual dropout regularization. Training dynamics show stable convergence (loss: 1.85→0.45; accuracy: 68%→85% over 5 epochs). Ablation studies confirm MFCC1''s dominance (0.28 attribution), with sad (92%) and disgust (91%) achieving highest F1-scores. Confusion analysis reveals neutral↔happy overlap, addressable via temporal modeling enhancements. Real-sample inference validates deployment readiness, producing Excel-formatted results with confidence scores. Attention heatmaps demonstrate interpretable feature focus, supporting trust in clinical applications. This production-ready pipeline—leveraging transfer learning efficiency and BiLSTM temporal modeling—offers scalable emotion recognition for human-computer interaction, mental health monitoring, and multimodal systems integrating your agricultural AI expertise.</p>','["BiLSTM, Canonical Correlation Analysis, Feature Attribution, LSTM, MFCC, TESS, Wav2Vec2, Zero Crossing Rate"]',NULL,16,1,'348 - 368','10.59543/jidmis.v3.443','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',20),
(444,444,1,'Fuzzy Membership-Guided Adaptive Compression and Reinforcement-Ready Cloud Tier Assignment for Brain Tumour MRI Archival Systems','<p>The exponential growth of MRI-based neurological imaging has imposed unsustainable storage and transmission costs on hospital cloud infrastructure, while conventional compression standards — JPEG, JPEG2000, and PNG — apply spatially uniform quantization that systematically degrades diagnostically critical regions such as tumour boundaries and perilesional oedema. This paper presents a five-stage, content-adaptive pipeline for cost-effective cloud archival of MRI brain tumour images, integrating Fuzzy C-Means (FCM) segmentation, Adaptive Quantization-inspired Discrete Cosine Transform (AQI-DCT) compression, cascaded entropy coding, and an intelligent quality-gated cloud tier decision engine. In Stage 1, FCM clustering assigns each pixel soft membership values across four tissue classes, producing per-cluster intensity variance vectors and a spatial label map that drive all downstream adaptive processing; the method achieves a mean Fuzzy Partition Coefficient of 0.868 across 200 MRI images, confirming well-separated and clinically meaningful segmentation. In Stage 2, AQI-DCT derives per-cluster quality factors directly from FCM variance measurements and scales the standard JPEG 8×8 luminance quantization matrix independently per block, yielding a mean PSNR of 40.94 dB and SSIM of 0.998 — near-lossless reconstruction fidelity — at an average compression ratio of 10.65× (range: 4.68×–16.55×). Stage 3 applies Run-Length Encoding followed by per-image Huffman coding to the sparse quantized DCT coefficient arrays, achieving a mean coding efficiency of 98.89%, within 1.11% of the Shannon theoretical minimum. Finally, a greedy-cheapest-first cloud tier engine evaluates each compressed image against quality thresholds for ARCHIVE ($0.00099/GB), COLD ($0.004/GB), WARM ($0.0125/GB), and HOT ($0.023/GB) tiers; across 200 images, 71.5% are assigned to WARM, 17% to COLD, 9.5% to HOT, and 2% to ARCHIVE, yielding a mean storage cost saving of 48.6% relative to uniform hot-tier assignment while sustaining a mean composite quality score of 0.891. The proposed pipeline demonstrates that content-aware compression and quality-driven cloud tiering can simultaneously satisfy diagnostic fidelity requirements and large-scale economic sustainability constraints in clinical imaging infrastructure.</p>','["Fuzzy C-Means clustering, Adaptive Quantization-Inspired DCT, MRI brain tumour compression, Huffman entropy coding, cloud storage tier optimisation, PSNR, SSIM."]',NULL,16,1,'369 - 397','10.59543/jidmis.v3.444','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',21),
(445,445,1,'ResNet-18 Convolutional Neural Network Framework for Tomato Disease Detection with Agrochemical Dosage Recommendation','<p>This study presents a deep learning-based framework for automated tomato leaf disease detection using a transfer learning approach built on ResNet18 architecture. The system is designed to classify 10 disease categories using image inputs resized to 224×224 pixels, leveraging ImageNet pre-trained weights to enhance feature extraction. The dataset consists of approximately 18,345 training samples with a batch size of 16 and trained over 10 epochs using the Adam optimizer and CrossEntropy loss function. Experimental results demonstrate strong classification performance, achieving a peak validation accuracy of 98.2% at epoch 9, with validation loss reduced to 0.64 from an initial value of approximately 1.8. The model shows high confidence predictions, with over 90% of test samples falling within the 90–100% confidence range. Per-class F1-scores range between 0.82 and 0.95, indicating consistent performance across multiple disease categories. The confusion matrix reveals strong diagonal dominance, confirming correct classification for most classes; however, specific misclassification patterns were observed. For instance, Bacterial_spot and Early_blight exhibit mutual confusion, while Septoria_leaf_spot shows complete misclassification into Tomato_mold, indicating limitations in distinguishing visually similar disease patterns. A balanced test dataset with class distribution ranging between 9% and 11% ensures unbiased evaluation. Additionally, Grad-CAM visualization confirms that the model focuses on biologically relevant regions such as lesion areas and leaf textures, improving interpretability. Despite achieving high accuracy under controlled conditions, the model’s generalization to real-world environments remains a challenge. The study highlights the need for improved robustness against variations in lighting, background, and disease severity. Overall, the proposed system demonstrates strong potential for precision agriculture applications, particularly in mobile-based disease diagnosis systems for farmers.</p>','["Bacterial spot, Convolutional neural networks, CrossEntropy loss, Deep learning, Disease classification, F1-score, Grad-CAM visualization, Precision agriculture, ResNet-18, Softmax activation, Validation accuracy."]',NULL,16,1,'398 - 420','10.59543/jidmis.v3.445','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',22),
(446,446,1,'Performance Analysis of YOLOv5 for Real-Time Weed Localization: A Study on Edge-AI Deployment in Smart Agriculture','<p>The demand for precision farming in smart agriculture is increasing day by day with the rise of techniques such as artificial intelligence and IoT. One such appli-cation is spraying, which necessitates the accurate localization of weeds in the complex crop environments. Traditional classification methods often fall short here because they do not indicate the exact spatial coordinates. In this paper, we have presented a comprehensive performance analysis of YOLOv5 architecture for real-time weed detection and localization, which is specifically optimized for deployment on edge AI robotic platforms. The dataset, consists of 3,120 images, was collected from different sources including public repositories as well as manual field visits. Image preprocessing and Data Augmentation Techniques are applied to improve the generalization capability. The Yolov5s Object Detection model was trained for over 150 epochs and achieved a mean average precision of approximately 0.86. To ensure weeds are detected effectively for spraying sys-tem even in difficult mixed field scenes, the threshold was optimized for high recall. To make the model field-ready, quantization techniques were applied to optimize it for edge deployment. This ensures it runs efficiently on the robotic platform, which integrates an AIoT-based framework and the MQTT protocol for real-time remote monitoring and autonomous spraying.</p>','["Weed Localization, YOLOv5, Edge-AI, Precision Agriculture, Real-Time Object Detection, AIoT. Etc."]',NULL,16,1,'421 - 435','10.59543/jidmis.v3.446','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',23),
(447,447,1,'A Deep Learning Approach for Multi-Stage Diabetic Retinopathy Diagnosis in Imbalanced Retinal Fundus Datasets.','<p>Diabetic retinopathy(DR, is one of the leading causes of vision loss among the diabetic patients, which necessitates the timely screening to prevent the irreversible damage. However, the manual screening of retinal fundus images is usually affected by the shortage of trained ophthalmologists and rapidly increasing diabetic population. In this paper, we have discussed an effective deep learning strategy for automated detection and class-wise severity grading for DR using APTOS 2019 dataset. Initially, we carried out exploratory data analysis on the collected dataset, which outlines the significant challenges in real-world clinical data. We could observe a high class imbalance where no DR and moderate stages dominated the dataset. In addition to this, other factors such as wide variations in image resolution, brightness and blur also did exist in the dataset. Image pre-processing techniques were applied to overcome the variations in the collected dataset with the objective to retain critical diagnostic features. In our methodology, we trained the model on simple CNN baseline, achieving an accuracy of 71.21%, but the obtained results failed to generalize on the minority classes on test data. Transfer learning techniques using InceptionV3 as base and 40% partial finetuning and deeper classification head along with drop regularization were implemented to learn complex decision boundaries. We implemented class weighted training to address class imbalance and reduce misclassification. This proposed approach achieved an overall accuracy of 81.58% and macro F1-Score of 65.82 percent which resulted in substantial improvement over baseline CNN in detecting minority classes. The results obtained demonstrate that class aware transfer learning approach can improve the classification performance of automated DR Diagnostic systems in imbalanced clinical environments.</p>','["Diabetic Retinopathy, Deep Learning, Transfer Learning, Inception V3, Class Imbalance, Severity Grading, Fundus Image Analysis, APTOS 2019 Dataset, Image Pre-processing etc."]',NULL,16,1,'436 - 454','10.59543/jidmis.v3.447','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',24),
(448,448,1,'Progressive Imaging-Based Early Detection of Retinopathy Using Hybrid CNN-RNN-SVM Architecture','<p>Diabetic retinopathy (DR) and hypertensive retinopathy (HR) remain among the leading causes of irreversible vision loss globally. Early detection is critical, yet conventional diagnostic procedures are often time-consuming, resource-heavy, and subjective. This study presents a hybrid progressive imaging-based framework for early-stage detection of DR and HR using an integrated CNN-RNN-SVM architecture. The approach leverages convolutional neural networks (CNNs) for hierarchical feature extraction from retinal fundus images, recurrent neural networks (RNNs) for sequence modelling of temporal image features, and support vector machines (SVMs) for precise classification. The system was developed and tested using MATLAB, incorporating datasets such as benchmark database. Performance evaluation indicates an accuracy of 96.3%, sensitivity of 95.1%, and specificity of 97.4%, surpassing existing single-model methods. Results confirm the synergy of spatial, temporal, and discriminative learning modules, enabling nuanced detection of retinopathic patterns at early stages. The framework demonstrates robustness against image noise and variability in retinal pathology. Key contributions include the progressive fusion of deep learning and traditional machine learning techniques and the establishment of a scalable, adaptable model suitable for clinical integration. This work underscores the potential of AI-assisted diagnostics to enhance early intervention and reduce the burden of vision loss due to systemic conditions.</p>','["Diabetic Retinopathy(DR), Hypertensive Retinopathy(HR), CNN, RNN, SVM, LSTM, MATLAB"]',NULL,16,1,'455 - 464','10.59543/jidmis.v3.448','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',25),
(449,449,1,'Design and Development of Frequency Reconfigurable Quarter Mode SIW MIMO Antenna for 5G-Sub-6 frequency range','<p>In this paper, a reconfigurable quarter mode substrate integrated Substrate Integrated waveguide antenna  has been implemented to operate at 3.5 GHz to 10 GHz band  in sub 6 regime. In novel way, Quarter Mode SIW miniaturized antenna for sub6 frequency designed in previous part of research work  is  taken as a base antenna.[13]  Same antenna is designed and simulated  with slanted  slot as well as Square slot at top left quarter.  After simulation results  is decided to go with square slot tuning as preserving symmetrical structure of base Antenna. The proposed antenna is fabricated in RT Duroid 5880 material with dimensions of 18.8 mm×18.8 mm× 2.54 mm at 3.5GHz. The slot is filled with 4 PIN diodes to see the effect of diode on-off condition on multiband  resonant behaviour of Antenna.</p>','["Substrate Integrated Waveguide (SIW), Square slot tuning, quarter mode (QM), Slot antenna, sub6, mm-wave."]',NULL,16,1,'465 - 486','10.59543/jidmis.v3.449','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',26),
(450,450,1,'Intrusion Detection Approach for Large IoT Networks with Various Feature Extraction Techniques and Machine Learning','<p>The fast growth of Internet of Things (IoT) networks has introduced new challenges as the attack surface has expanded significantly, making intrusion detection vital für ensuring the security of the networks. This study proposes an intrusion detection system for the large-scale IoT networks by integrating multiple techniques for feature extraction as well as employing several machine learning classifiers to improve detection and classification performance. The methodology consists of TF-IDF with CO-CO filtering, computation of statistical features, Weka-based attribute evaluation, and a novel hybrid feature selection approach which aims to combine sparse, distributional, and redundancy free features.  The combination of all these methods results in a streamlined feature set which enhances classifier performance while reducing noise and redundant attributes. This feature set was used with five classification models: Naïve Bayes (NB), Support Vector Machine (SVM), Artificial Neural Network (ANN), AdaBoost, and a new proposed model, Hybrid Meta-Learner (HML). The HML model is distinctive because it integrates ANN-based input mapping, RNN feedback loops for temporal sequence refinement, and an SVM distance function for output layer, decision-based similarity computation. All models benefitted from an increase in detection accuracy due to hybrid feature extraction, with NB scoring 82.6%, SVM 91.3%, ANN 92.1%, and AdaBoost 93.0%. Accuracy from the proposed HML model benchmarked 96.1%. The framework''s expressive and comprehensive evaluation shows that combining multi-perspective feature selection and a synergistic hybrid learning architecture with concentrically structured layers improves the accuracy and generalization capabilities of systems for varying attack detection. The framework achieved high adaptability for large IoT environments, solving issues of data diversity, high dimensionality, and temporal relationships within data. These observations refine the scope of the work to optimize HML architecture for edge-based real-time interfaces with IoT systems that require trust and transparency for operational processes, strengthened through automatic explainability mechanisms focused on evolving threats for IoT systems.</p>','["Intrusion Detection, Internet of Things, Machine Learning, TON-IoT Dataset, Data Preprocessing, Feature Extraction, Support Vector Machine, Cybersecurity"]',NULL,16,1,'487 - 510','10.59543/jidmis.v3.450','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',27),
(451,451,1,'A Context-Aware Text Sentiment Classification Framework Leveraging Pretrained Large Language Models and Transfer Learning for Large-Scale Opinion Mining','<p>The proliferation of user-generated digital text across social media platforms, e-commerce ecosystems, and online discourse forums has established large-scale opinion mining as a critical capability for organisations seeking actionable intelligence from unstructured data. Classical lexicon-driven and shallow machine learning approaches to sentiment classification are demonstrably insufficient for capturing the compositional, context-dependent, and often irony-laden polarity signals embedded in contemporary natural language. The advent of self-supervised pretrained transformer architectures—most notably the BERT family and its successors—has redefined the performance ceiling for sentiment analysis; however, significant gaps persist in exploiting contextual disambiguation at the document level, adapting general-purpose language representations to domain-specific opinion vocabularies, and preserving classification performance under low-resource conditions. Despite impressive benchmark scores, existing transfer-learning frameworks for sentiment classification encounter four persistent limitations: (i) shallow context integration that fails to model long-range sentiment dependencies spanning multiple clauses; (ii) domain mismatch between pretraining corpora and target-domain lexicons resulting in systematic misclassification of domain-specific sentiment expressions; (iii) poor calibration under distributional shift, manifesting as overconfident incorrect predictions on adversarial and out-of-domain inputs; and (iv) prohibitive computational cost of full fine-tuning for large-scale transformer models in resource-constrained deployment environments. This dissertation introduces the Context-Aware Transfer Language Model (CAT-LLM) framework, integrating DeBERTa-v3-Large as the contextual encoder with three complementary components: a Context-Aware Position Encoding (CAPE) module that augments disentangled attention with document-level positional signals; a parameter-efficient Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) fine-tuning strategy with layer-wise learning-rate decay; and a Monte Carlo Dropout uncertainty estimation head providing calibrated confidence scores alongside sentiment predictions. Evaluated across six benchmark datasets—SST-2, IMDb, Yelp-5, Amazon Reviews, Twitter SemEval, and SemEval 2017—the CAT-LLM framework achieved macro F1-scores of 0.962, 0.958, 0.924, 0.941, 0.887, and 0.903 respectively, surpassing all baselines including RoBERTa-Large and XLNet-Large by margins of 1.5–7.5%. AUC-ROC reached 0.984. With only 10 training examples, CAT-LLM achieved 71.2% accuracy—12.7 percentage points above BiLSTM—demonstrating exceptional few-shot transferability. The CAT-LLM framework establishes a new state of the art for large-scale opinion mining, reconciling contextual expressiveness, parameter efficiency, and uncertainty quantification within a unified, production-deployable sentiment analysis pipeline.</p>','["sentiment classification, large language models, transfer learning, DeBERTa, opinion mining, context-aware attention."]',NULL,16,1,'511 - 531','10.59543/jidmis.v3.451','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',28),
(452,452,1,'Multi-Level Ethereum Malicious Account Detection through Transaction Behavioural Modelling and Adaptive Anomaly Reclassification','<p>The growth of the usage of decentralized applications on Ethereum has seen the rise of an increasing number of bad actors that are using it to commit fraud, phishing, money laundering and financial scams. Traditional detection methods are less effective to detect accounts with more complex and changing behaviours. The paper suggests a novel multi-level framework for detecting malicious Ethereum accounts based on supervised classification and adaptive anomalous account verification using a routing based on probabilities. To get the transaction behavior features, opcode features and time-interval features from the publicly available EtherShield data set, we use the entire data set to extract the entire features. The first is a Level-1 where an XGBoost machine learning model classifies Ethereum accounts into Fair, Likely Malicious and Malicious categories, and outputs calibrated probability scores for any account. Uncertainty about malicious accounts are escalated to level-2 where anomaly verification and behavioural re-assessment are carried out by using models such as Random Forest and Isolation Forest. The final classification is obtained by decision fusion process, which combines the results obtained from both levels. The Random Forest-based verification module is evaluated in the experiments and is found to be 95% accurate, 94% macro precision, 95% macro recall and 95% macro F1-score, which is significantly better than the Isolation Forest (baseline). Besides, stratified 5-Fold Cross Validation further demonstrates that the proposed framework is robust and generalizable with a mean accuracy of 94.96% ± 0.43, mean precision of 94.40% ± 0.42, mean recall of 94.84% ± 0.47 and mean F1-Score of 94.66% ± 0.42. The proposed framework proves to be an effective solution to minimize misclassification, enhance the reliability of detection and offer a scalable answer to safeguard Ethereum blockchain ecosystems from newly emerged malicious activities.</p>','["Ethereum", "blockchain security", "malicious account detection", "XGBoost", "Random Forest", "Isolation Forest", "anomaly detection"]',NULL,16,1,'532 - 556','10.59543/jidmis.v3.452','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',29),
(453,453,1,'Ai Powered Wearable Fall Prevention Device','<p>Falls are still among the causes of injury, hospitalization, and loss of independence in older adults across the world. The traditional fall detection systems mostly involve hard-wired fall detector systems that are operated using fixed threshold-based acceleration techniques, which are not usually flexible to accommodate individual movement patterns, and therefore have a high rate of false alarms. In this paper, the algorithm design and development of an AI-enabled wearable fall prevention device will be presented which consists of multi-sensor fusion, dynamic thresholding, and machine learning algorithms to provide an accurate real-time fall detector and health monitor. The suggested system has an ESP32 microcontroller with an MPU9250 inertial movement block to track the motion accurately and an in-built blood pressure, heart rate, and pressure sensor to measure physiological and impact-related variables. The system can improve distinguishing between everyday activities and the actual falls through the combination of acceleration, angular velocity, and vital signs data. A machine learning model is used to learn actively user-specific movement features, which is more sensitive and specific compared to the conventional fixed threshold methods. At the edge, real-time processing of data is done to reduce latency, whereas cloud integration with the help of AWS guarantees scalable communication, secure storage, and remote access. Special mobile app will afford caregivers graphical representation of vital signs, fall history and real-time emergency notifications. The combination of smart analytics, active wearable sensing, and cloud-based notification will help minimize the response time, increase the safety of the elderly and the quality of life in general because of proactive fall preventive measures and continuous health tracking.</p>','["Fall Prevention", "Artificial Intelligence", "Real-Time Monitoring", "Wearable Devices", "Health Monitoring."]',NULL,16,1,'557 - 570','10.59543/jidmis.v3.453','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',30),
(454,454,1,'Framework Evaluation for Energy-Efficient Resource Allocation in Industrial IoT Using Hybrid Swarm Optimization: Comparative Analysis by Integrating Sand Cat Swarm Optimization and Moth Flame Optimization with SVM-Based Path Quality Assessment','<p>Industrial Internet of Things (IIoT) environments generate enormous volumes of data that must be routed through cloud infrastructure with strict constraints on energy consumption, network latency, and service reliability. Existing resource allocation frameworks often rely on single-objective heuristics that fail to balance competing performance objectives across large-scale virtual machine (VM) deployments. The proliferation of heterogeneous IoT sensor nodes, each with distinct energy profiles and trust characteristics, further complicates optimal path selection in multi-hop cloud networks. These challenges demand intelligent optimization frameworks capable of simultaneously minimizing routing cost, energy usage, and latency while maximizing throughput and node trustworthiness.This paper proposes a novel hybrid swarm intelligence framework for energy-efficient resource allocation in Industrial IoT networks. The framework integrates two biologically inspired metaheuristic algorithms: Sand Cat Swarm Optimization (SCSO), which mimics the vibration-based hunting behaviour of sand cats to perform global exploration across candidate routing paths, and Moth Flame Optimization (MFO), which employs logarithmic spiral movement toward optimal flame positions to execute precise local refinement. The two-phase pipeline feeds the entire SCSO candidate population directly into the MFO refinement stage, enabling the hybrid to escape local optima that afflict standalone algorithms. A Support Vector Machine (SVM) classifier acts as a quality gate, filtering paths whose average trust score falls below 0.5 or whose average network latency exceeds 60 milliseconds. The framework is evaluated on a real-world dataset comprising 500 virtual machine nodes recorded at five-minute intervals, with 16 normalized feature dimensions including CPU utilization, memory usage, network throughput, latency, power consumption, response time, and SLA violation rate. Experimental results demonstrate that the Hybrid SCSO-MFO achieves a path efficiency of 95.3% at 300 nodes, representing improvements of 6.4 percentage points over GNN-based intrusion detection methods and 10.6 percentage points over dynamic graph transformer approaches. Energy consumption along optimal paths is reduced by 18.7% compared to standalone MFO and by 26.4% compared to standalone SCSO. Convergence is achieved 34% faster than MFO alone. These results confirm that the proposed framework delivers superior energy efficiency, network reliability, and scalability for IIoT cloud deployments.</p>','["Industrial IoT, Resource Allocation, Swarm Optimization, Moth Flame Optimization, Sand Cat Swarm Optimization, Energy Efficiency"]',NULL,16,1,'571 - 585','10.59543/jidmis.v3.454','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',31),
(460,460,1,'LiDAR Point Cloud Segmentation for Automated Virtual Set Construction - using ML Classifiers to Convert Raw Scan Data into Production-Ready','<p>Virtual production and digital content creation increasingly rely on realistic three-dimensional environmental modelling for film, gaming, augmented reality, and simulation applications. However, converting raw LiDAR point cloud data into production-ready virtual sets remains a complex and time-consuming task due to the presence of noise, irregular spatial structures, and large-scale unstructured data. To address these challenges, this paper proposes an intelligent LiDAR point cloud segmentation framework for virtual set construction for automatic operation based on machine learning classifiers. The proposed system consists of point cloud acquisition, pre-processing, noise removal, feature extraction, semantic segmentation, and object classification to extract structural and environmental elements like walls, floors, props, and scene objects from the point cloud. The segmentation is enhanced by using machine learning classification methods, which boost the semantic understanding of the scanned environment and improve the accuracy of the segmentation. The segmented point cloud data is further processed for surface reconstruction and virtual scene generation, enabling the automatic creation of production-ready digital environments. The proposed framework improves reconstruction efficiency, reduces manual modelling efforts, and enhances scene generation accuracy. Experimental analysis demonstrates improved segmentation performance, semantic consistency, and faster virtual set construction suitable for modern virtual production workflows.</p>','["3D reconstruction, virtual production, point cloud processing, scene understanding, digital environment generation, automated set modelling, semantic segmentation, virtual set construction, and LiDAR point cloud."]',NULL,16,1,'586 - 617','10.59543/jidmis.v3.460','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',32),
(461,461,1,'Intelligent Traffic Path Recommendations Using Machine Learning based Temporal Feature Aggregation and Time Series Based Graph Routing','<p>Urban transportation networks generate massive volumes of spatio-temporal data, and the effective exploitation of such data to enable intelligent route recommendations has become a critical priority in smart city development. Existing systems predominantly rely on static or short-horizon prediction models that fail to capture the complex, non-stationary dynamics characterizing real-world vehicular traffic, particularly under congestion-prone conditions in densely populated urban corridors. The challenges are further compounded by high-dimensional, noisy, and heterogeneous sensor data that makes robust feature extraction and temporal modeling particularly difficult, as traditional approaches exhibit poor generalization across varying prediction horizons and traffic regimes. This research proposes an integrated framework for traffic path recommendation that combines systematic feature extraction, temporal prediction, and dynamic graph-based routing. The methodology begins with the Feature Extraction for Vehicular Traffic Time Series (FEVTTS) algorithm, which applies Mutual Information, Random Forest Regression, and Correlation Coefficient analysis to derive an optimal, low-redundancy feature set from raw sensor data. The refined features are then fed to the Temporal Traffic Feature Aggregation Algorithm (TFTAA), which integrates short-term and long-term traffic patterns using a rolling window mechanism to produce accurate multi-horizon forecasts. Finally, the predicted traffic variables are embedded as dynamic edge weights into a Time Series Based Graph (TSBG), on which Dijkstra''s algorithm computes optimal time-dependent routes. The proposed TFTAA algorithm employs a hybrid statistical and ensemble machine learning design, incorporating SARIMAX for seasonal decomposition and gradient-boosted residual correction using XGBoost, augmented by quantile regression for uncertainty quantification. The framework was validated using the London Traffic Dataset (Highways England) and a locally collected Pune city dataset comprising 26,785 and 25,920 samples, respectively, across diverse road types. TFTAA consistently outperformed SARIMAX and XGBoost baselines at all evaluated prediction horizons from 5 to 60 minutes, achieving a Mean Absolute Error (MAE) of 2.1 at the 5-minute horizon and 14.3 at the 60-minute horizon, compared to 25.1 for SARIMAX at the same horizon. The TSBG-based routing reduced average travel time by an average of 18.3% over static Dijkstra across 15 test days on the London dataset. The proposed framework demonstrates that tightly coupling predictive traffic modeling with dynamic graph routing yields measurable improvements in route efficiency over baseline approaches. The research advances the state of the art in intelligent transportation systems by providing a scalable, feature-engineered, and prediction-integrated path recommendation solution applicable to both developed and emerging urban road networks.</p>','["traffic path recommendation, time series forecasting, TFTAA algorithm, feature extraction, TSBG routing, machine learning"]',NULL,16,1,'618 - 637','10.59543/jidmis.v3.461','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',33),
(462,462,1,'Integrating Traditional Grammar Rules with AI for Enhanced Sandhi Split in Sanskrit','<p>Sanskrit is one of the oldest and heritage languages in the world, which has special difficulty in computational analysis. It has a very complex grammar, and such common phenomena as Sandhi, the fusion of sounds and their fracture at the boundaries of words, render even simple processing of the text challenging. Conventional methods are based on the exact rules that were developed by Padmani, which are strict yet impractical in case of large digital corpus. Conversely, new AI systems like deep learning and sequence models are capable of handling vast quantities of data, although occasionally will produce answers that contradict many of the rules that provide Sanskrit with its framework. This paper unites the two worlds with our framework of a hybrid that will combine the accuracy of P27 grammar with the flexibility of machine learning. Our system identifies boundaries of Sandhi by use of transformer based models, and uses layers of sound patterns in context. Rule validators guarantee that the outputs are grammatically correct and a graph based resolver is used where there is more than one valid split. The framework has been repeatedly shown to be more effective than traditional baselines when applied to a curated Sanskrit corpus. It achieved an accuracy and a bottom-level F1-score of 92.6 and 90.8 respectively. More than figures, the method gives out results not only right, but true to the spirit of Sanskrit grammar. In our opinion, such a combination of old linguistic knowledge and new AI opens new opportunities to digital Sanskrit tools, such as search, translation, and linguistic research.</p>','["Sanskrit, Sandhi", "Splitting,", "P\\u0101\\u1e47inian Grammar", "Artificial Intelligence", "Hybrid NLP Models", "Computational Linguistics"]',NULL,16,1,'638 - 663','10.59543/jidmis.v3.462','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',34),
(463,463,1,'A Systematic Review of Deep Learning Based Grape Disease Detection Models Evaluating Generalizability Dataset Diversity and Computational Efficiency Across Diverse Environments','<p>Recent developments in machine learning, deep learning and multimodal sensing have revolutionized grape leaf disease detection based on visual symptoms but there are still major issues of generalizability to new environments, diversity of data sets, and computational efficiency to be able to apply it in a real-world scenario. This review focuses specifically on grape leaf-level disease detection using visual, hyperspectral, and multimodal data. The presented review summarizes the results of the modern research concerning the classification of grape disease, its detection and localization and the degree of the disease in question, which focuses on the comparison between the classical machine learning model, the CNN-based model, the transformer model and the hybrid models. As compared, transformer-based and CNN-ViT fusion networks have good discriminative and robustness to fine-grained disease symptoms, even though their computing requirements are higher. In contrast, lightweight CNNs, optimizations of YOLO models and architectures optimized for mobile users offer efficient processing for real-time inference but have the drawback that they cannot work well under various illumination, occlusion and geographic conditions. The utilization of multi-models based on combining RGB visuals with hyperspectral fingerprints and other environmental factors show increased stability even when changing the seasons. Furthermore, GAN-based augmentation and super-resolution techniques improve the level of the detection sensitivity in the early stage symptoms and in the low quality imagery. The cross-dataset tests show that there are still issues with domain shifts, the quality of annotations and the poor representation of field conditions, which implies the need for standardized benchmarks. The journal article leads to conclude that the trade-offs between accuracy, interpretability, generalization and computation cost should be emphasized in the future research and it is needed to use the scalable data sets, powerful domain adaptation approaches, edge optimal models, and explainable AI models to guarantee the successful and consistent detection of grape disease systems in practical use in vineyards.</p>','["Grape Disease Detection", "Deep Learning", "Transformer Models", "Dataset Diversity", "Generalizability", "Computational Efficiency"]',NULL,16,1,'664 - 694','10.59543/jidmis.v3.463','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',35),
(464,464,1,'Federated Learning Driven Framework for Healthcare Data Traffic Congestion Management and Performance Enhancement','<p>As digital health tools, electronic health records, and sensor-based monitoring devices become more popular, they cause a huge increase in data traffic for healthcare systems that has never been seen before. Using traditional centralized learning methods to handle this kind of data increases privacy risks, causes delays, and uses too much bandwidth. The goal of this study is to come up with a new federated learning-based system for improving performance and managing healthcare data traffic congestion. The suggested framework combines a smart client selection strategy with federated learning methods (FedAvg and FedProx) to improve both the amount of information needed and the performance of the model as a whole. The method uses the PhysioNet/Computing in Cardiology Challenge 2016 heart sound dataset to create spectrogram-based features that are then shared among virtual clients with different needs. Clients are automatically chosen based on available resources, dataset size, and how quickly they can compute. This makes sure that everyone contributes equally to training while staying within time limits. The proposed federated method is tested in the real world and compared to standard centralised models. The results show that the framework improves speed and lowers the amount of data that gets lost during transmission. Compared to the baseline centralised model, which had an accuracy of 88.4%, the suggested model got as high as 94.8% with FedProx and 93.5% with FedAvg. The framework also cut down on communication delays by 15.2%, raised client efficiency scores by 11.7%, and kept strong convergence even when IID data wasn''t available. This study looks at healthcare networks that are scalable, safe, and aware of their resources. These networks handle a lot of medical data and need to work together to process it without compromising privacy. This work lays the groundwork for smartly managing congestion in healthcare data ecosystems. This could have positive effects on real-time, distributed clinical decision support systems.</p>','["Federated Learning, Healthcare Data Traffic, Congestion Management, Client Selection Strategy, Distributed Learning, Performance Enhancement"]',NULL,16,1,'695 - 712','10.59543/jidmis.v3.464','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',36),
(465,465,1,'A Multi-Modal Deep Learning Framework for Depression Detection Through EEG–Speech Fusion and Conformer–Mamba State Space Modeling','<p>Depression is a common psychological condition affecting millions of people globally, frequently not diagnosed, because of a subjective nature of the conventional clinical assessment. In this research, a multi-modal deep learning framework for automatic depression detection in combination with electroencephalogram (EEG) and speech signals was proposed. The framework was based on the Multi-modal Open Dataset for Mental-disorder Analysis (MODMA) that includes synchronized resting-state EEG recordings and audio samples from participants with Major Depressive Disorder and healthy controls. The Mel-Frequency Cepstral Coefficients (MFCCs) were used to extract features from the audio signal and EEG signals were normalized and time synchronized with the speech segments. To learn the dynamic relationships between the neurophysiological and acoustic representations, a cross-modal attention fusion mechanism was used to obtain discriminative fused feature embeddings. The CNN-Transformer, GNN-Transformer, and the proposed Conformer–Mamba State Space Model architectures were tested with the same setup for training. The experimental results showed that the Conformer–Mamba model outperformed the CNN-Transformer and GNN-Transformer baselines with 89%, 91%, 88%, 89.5%, and 0.97 for the classification accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, and ROC-AUC, respectively. The improvement was thought to be due to the usefulness of the Conformer modules to represent local and global context and the Mamba state space mechanism to represent long-range temporal dependencies efficiently. The results showed that attention guided EEG-speech fusion is a reliable and an objective method for the detection of depression with great potential for future intelligent mental health screening systems.</p>','["Depression detection", "EEG\\u2013speech fusion", "Conformer\\u2013Mamba", "State space model", "Cross-modal attention", "Multi-modal deep learning", "Major depressive disorder Human-AI collaboration", "Generative AI", "Socio-technical systems", "Multi-level perspective", "Cognitive fit", "Delegation of agency", "Systematic literature review"]',NULL,16,1,'713 - 731','10.59543/jidmis.v3.465','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',37),
(466,466,1,'An Energy-Performance Efficient Systolic Array-based Quantum Gates and Circuit for Quantum Neural Networks for Complex Image Classification Workflow Task','<p>The growing demand for efficient processing of complex image classification tasks has highlighted the limitations of conventional computing architectures in terms of latency, energy consumption, and scalability. To address these challenges, this paper proposes a hybrid TensorGPU (TGPU) framework that integrates the strengths of GPU and TPU architectures with a systolic array-based computation model. The proposed approach supports the execution of an Adaptive Quantum Convolutional Neural Network (AQCNN) by enabling parallel task processing and efficient data reuse. An enhanced kernel mechanism is introduced to manage task scheduling, data partitioning, and dynamic resource allocation across processing arrays. The model also incorporates energy-aware execution by adapting processing frequencies and minimizing idle states. Experimental evaluation on benchmark image datasets demonstrates improved system utilization, reduced execution time, and lower energy consumption compared to existing methods. The proposed framework offers a scalable and effective solution for handling next-generation intelligent and data-intensive applications.</p>','["Hybrid TGPU, Systolic Array, Quantum Kernel, AQCNN, Energy-Efficient Scheduling, Image Classification"]',NULL,16,1,'732 - 747','10.59543/jidmis.v3.466','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',38),
(467,467,1,'Implementation Framework for Cloud-Based ERP Integration with Third-Party Systems Using a Novel Secure, Scalable, and Machine Learning-Assisted Integration Mechanism','<p>The adoption and diffusion of cloud ERP has provided organizations with a single source for enterprise-wide business process management‚ on-demand analytics‚ and real-time interdepartmental collaboration․ It has also raised compatibility‚ security and scalability issues with integrating cloud-based ERP with external third-party applications in heterogeneous environments with multiple different systems in place‚ a variety of APIs‚ and real time transaction synchronization․ Thus‚ existing integration solutions tend to focus on connectivity and interoperability‚ failing to protect sensitive enterprise information during transmission and processing with implementation-level security mechanisms․ This paper presents a secure and practical architecture which integrates cloud-based ERP systems with external third-party applications․ The Microsoft Dynamics ERP platform is used as an implementation platform․ The solutions provided to integrate the external applications with Microsoft Dynamics ERP offer integrated models of Transport Layer Security (TLS 1․2+)‚ symmetric encryption (AES-256)‚ RSA secure key exchange‚ Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)‚ badge-based authentication‚ replay attack and staging validation․ This framework supports outbound and inbound secure messaging workflows using hybrid cryptography‚ and secure API orchestration using X++ programming language․ Proposed method provides a secured synchronization and uses D365-specific sync states including AX Only‚ AX Updated‚ and AX and Portal for reliable data exchange with traceability․ Experiments on transactional data support the effectiveness of the proposed method in various integration scenarios․ The proposed framework was evaluated for its efficiency and validity through statistical methodologies such as ANOVA‚ regression analysis‚ correlation analysis‚ and t-tests․ Additionally, the proposed framework incorporates a lightweight Machine Learning (ML)-assisted anomaly detection mechanism for monitoring integration traffic and identifying abnormal transactional behavior during inbound and outbound communication. The ML-assisted validation layer improves threat prediction capability by identifying suspicious synchronization patterns, unauthorized access attempts, unusual API request frequencies, and anomalous payload characteristics in real time. The inclusion of intelligent anomaly detection enhances proactive security monitoring while maintaining low computational overhead suitable for enterprise-grade ERP deployments. According to the model results‚ vulnerability is scored at 0․06‚ data consistency rate is 98․9%‚ error rate is 0․07% and user experience score is 0․448․ The framework has a lower integration complexity and setup time than the blockchain-based ERP integration and the IoT-based procurement integration systems discussed above․ The proposed architecture can be integrated with more than twenty third-party systems‚ and it is scalable‚ safe‚ and efficient to operate․ Results indicate that the proposed implementation framework offers a secure‚ scalable‚ and efficient solution to enterprise-grade ERP integration‚ and meaningful improvements over conventional integration methodologies․</p>','["Cloud ERP, AES-256, RSA Encryption, RBAC, TLS 1.2, Machine Learning, Anomaly Detection, Intelligent ERP Security."]',NULL,16,1,'748 - 769','10.59543/jidmis.v3.467','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',39),
(468,468,1,'A Hybrid Deep Learning Feature Extraction and Transfer Learning-Based Approach for Respiratory Disease Diagnosis','<p>Respiratory diseases, particularly paediatrics pneumonia, remain a leading cause of morbidity and mortality worldwide, demanding timely and accurate diagnosis. Traditional diagnostic methods relying on manual interpretation of chest X-rays often exhibit limited accuracy and are prone to subjective errors. Although existing deep learning approaches offer potential, they face significant limitations, including insufficient feature extraction, high-dimensional data challenges and class imbalance. To address these gaps, this study proposes the Hybrid Multi-Attention Respiratory Disease Classifier (HMARDC), designed to enhance pneumonia detection by integrating a Multi-Scale Contextual Enhancer (MSCE) for capturing local and global image features, a Dual-Attention Refinement Module (DARM) for highlighting salient patterns, a transfer-learning-based convolutional neural network backbone for robust feature representation, a compact fully connected network for non-linear feature embedding, and an optimized XGBoost classifier for accurate classification. The model was trained and evaluated on a paediatrics chest X-ray dataset comprising 5,856 images, achieving 99.96% accuracy, 99.5% precision, 99.6% recall, and 99.55% F1-score. The findings demonstrate the effectiveness of HMARDC in mitigating overfitting, improving feature discriminability, and providing reliable and interpretable predictions. The HMARDC offers significant potential for clinical adoption, supporting early and precise pneumonia detection in children.</p>','["Paediatric Pneumonia, Hybrid Deep Learning, Multi-Scale Feature Extraction, Attention Mechanism, Chest X-Ray, XGBoost, Transfer Learning"]',NULL,16,1,'770 - 787','10.59543/jidmis.v3.468','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',40),
(469,469,1,'Design and Development of a Deep Learning-Based System for Multi-Fruit Disease Classification and Severity Detection Using VGG-16 and VGG-19 Architectures on an Expert-Verified Indian Fruit Crop Dataset','<p>Fruit diseases caused by fungal, bacterial, and viral pathogens cause devastating pre- and post-harvest losses to Indian agriculture, particularly in Maharashtra where Guava, Mango, Orange, Papaya, and Pomegranate are major horticulture crops. Accurate, early-stage disease identification directly impacts farmer income, food security, and precision crop management. Conventional manual inspection by agronomists is subjective, time-consuming, and not scalable across thousands of orchard acres. Automated deep learning-based image analysis has emerged as a transformative solution, offering high accuracy, speed, and field deployability. Existing deep learning models for plant disease detection are predominantly trained on the real time database, which inadequately represents Indian fruit crop species. Furthermore, published systems focus on binary disease presence detection and lack disease severity grading — a critical requirement for treatment decision-making. The absence of expert-verified datasets and the visual overlap among disease classes (such as Phytophthora vs. Scab in Guava, and Powdery Mildew vs. Ring-spot in Papaya) present significant classification challenges. Limited training data for rare classes such as Stylerandroot (Guava, 310 samples) and Bacterial Blight (Pomegranate, 304 samples) further compounds model generalization. This study presents an end-to-end deep learning framework using VGG-16 and VGG-19 architectures trained on a novel, expert-verified dataset of 7,372 approved images spanning 22 disease/healthy classes across 5 fruit types, after on filed validation. The pipeline includes image preprocessing (CLAHE, Gaussian denoising, normalization), hybrid multi-feature extraction (CNN features, GLCM, LBP, Color Histograms), transfer learning with progressive fine-tuning, and a cascaded severity estimation module. Three algorithms are designed: (1) a Transfer Learning Classification Algorithm using VGG-16/VGG-19 backbone with softmax multi-class head, (2) a Hybrid Feature Fusion Algorithm combining CNN deep features with handcrafted descriptors for improved minority-class performance, and (3) a Cascaded Rule-CNN Severity Estimation Algorithm classifying disease progression into Healthy, Mild, Moderate, and Severe categories. VGG-19 achieved 96.1% overall accuracy, 95.1% precision, 94.5% recall, and a macro F1-score of 0.942, significantly outperforming VGG-16 (94.5% accuracy, F1: 0.918). Mango classification achieved the highest accuracy at 97.8%, while severity estimation reached 91.2% overall accuracy with the Mild category being the most challenging at 86.4%.The proposed system demonstrates that expert-verified, domain-specific datasets combined with transfer learning and hybrid feature fusion significantly advance the state of fruit disease detection for Indian agriculture. This framework provides a scalable, interpretable, and practically deployable solution for precision horticulture.</p>','["fruit disease classification, VGG-16, VGG-19, severity detection, transfer learning, transfer learning"]',NULL,16,1,'788 - 802','10.59543/jidmis.v3.469','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',41);
INSERT INTO publications (id,submission_id,version,title,abstract,keywords,references_text,issue_id,section_id,pages,doi,copyright_holder,copyright_year,license_url,status,date_published,seq) VALUES
(470,470,1,'A Deep Learning Pixel Coordination Transformer Approach with Multi-Head Attention for Brain Tumor Segmentation','<p>Brain tumor segmentation using multi-modal Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) scans is important for diagnosis, planning treatment, and prognosis, yet remains challenging because of heterogeneous appearance, irregular shapes, and varying intensity profiles of tumors. Existing deep learning approaches, often struggle in capturing fine-grained spatial details while maintaining global contextual awareness, leading to suboptimal boundary delineation and inconsistent performance across tumor subregions. To address this gap, this work proposes a novel Pixel Coordination Transformer Multi-Head Attention (PCT-MHA) model, designed to accurately segment Tumor Core (TC), Whole Tumor (WT) and Enhanced Tumor (ET) regions. The PCT-MHA integrates a pixel-level decoder with coordinated convolutional embeddings and multi-head attention, enabling iterative refinement of queries while preserving both local and global features. Experimental evaluation on the BraTS 2021 dataset show that PCT-MHA achieves Dice Similarity Coefficients (DSC) of 90.5%, 92.3%, and 89.7% for ET, WT, and TC, respectively. These results highlight the PCT-MHA ability to precisely delineate tumor boundaries with high reliability.</p>','["Brain tumor segmentation, MRI, Deep learning, Transformer, Multi-head attention, Dice Similarity Coefficient"]',NULL,16,1,'803 - 820','10.59543/jidmis.v3.470','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',42),
(471,471,1,'An Explainable Multimodal Vision Transformer Framework with Agentic AI for Medicinal Leaf Analysis','<p>The Medicinal plant species play an important role in pharmacological research. According to research from World Health organization herbal medicine provides opportunity for biocompatible drugs. Medicinal plant analysis plays a critical role in sustainable development of bioactive compounds. Traditional black box methods of classification don’t give insights about the medicinal properties of plants providing limited transparency.  The proposed work has a framework with explainable capabilities and an agentic AI framework for medicinal plant leaf analysis. The medicinal plant leaf classification is driven by CLIP Transformer which uses morphological features of leaf and images for classification. The contrastive learning captures the embeddings from visual images of 171 species of plant with 64600 images along with morphological features. The explainability of model is achieved by Grad CAM for visual attribution, LIME for local Interpretations and SHAP for Feature importance. The unified XAI techniques add meaningful explanations for the model`s output. The agentic AI framework uses Lang Chain orchestration model, along with ReAct and Chain of Thought for dynamic decisions of model. The Agentic RAG is used to retrieve external knowledge for agents reasoning process. The information retrieved is used for contextual predictions with medical properties of plant. The agentic framework with XAI for multimodal medicinal plant leaf classification aids real time decision making for pharmacologists, botanists along with biodiversity documentation and digital herbarium platforms. The state of artwork highlights potential of transformers and agentic AI capabilities for reliable medicinal plant species classification</p>','["CLIP Transformer, Multi Modal Learning, Explainable AI(XAI), LIME SHAP, Grad CAM, Lang Chain Orchestration, Agentic RAG"]',NULL,16,1,'821 - 839','10.59543/jidmis.v3.471','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',43),
(472,472,1,'The Evolution of Human-AI Collaboration: A Data-Driven Systematic Literature Review and Multi-Level Maturity Framework','<p>The rapid rise of Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) is changing how humans and AI collaborate, moving beyond purely human-led decision-making toward more shared and, in some cases, delegated forms of control. Conversely, the existing research in this domain exhibits a degree of fragmentation. Much of the existing research on Human-AI interaction focuses on specific perspectives, often treating it as a fixed set of categories, rather than a process that changes over time. To address this, this study conducts a data-driven systematic review, encompassing 141 peer-reviewed articles. Employing bibliometric analysis and machine learning techniques, the study maps how the field has evolved and identifies its main areas of focus. The findings point to three dominant thematic clusters and a five-stage pattern, reflecting how Human-AI collaboration has gradually developed. Building on these results, the study introduces a Multi-Level Evolutionary Maturity Framework, which views Human-AI collaboration as an ongoing socio-technical process shaped by the gradual delegation of agency. In this framework, cognitive fit plays an important role at the individual level by shaping trust, which then influences the extent to which decision-making is delegated to AI. As delegation increases, changes are seen in organizational structures and, more broadly, in how institutions respond. The study also highlights some challenges, including algorithmic aversion and the likelihood of deskilling, that may slow or complicate this process. Overall, the study contributes to the Information Systems and organizational literature by shifting the focus from fixed classifications to the processes that shape the development of Human-AI collaboration. It also provides a practical perspective for organizations that are interested in better aligning their technological capabilities with their own level of preparedness in the context of GenAI.</p>','["Human-AI collaboration", "Generative AI", "Socio-technical systems", "Multi-level perspective", "Cognitive fit", "Delegation of agency", "Systematic literature review"]',NULL,16,1,'840 - 863','10.59543/jidmis.v3.472','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',44),
(473,473,1,'Smart Obstacle Detection and Fall Detection with IoT-GPS Safety and Caregiver Tracking','<p>The elderly often lose their mobility due to a loss of muscle control, leading to reduced independence and safety. The elderly and visually impaired often struggle to navigate their environments because of obstacles. Real-time location tracking, health monitoring, fall detection, and communication of alerts to caregivers all present design, integration, and misalignment challenges. Consequently, the existing systems do not cover all safety aspects and oscillate between false alerts and missing important safety alerts. This paper outlines the introduction of Safe Walk AI Navigator, a full-spectrum system based on navigation monitoring and tracking to provide safety coverage. Sensor data is gathered and analyzed based on thresholds and is collected by Arduino Uno to be the monitoring and tracking command center. Implementation will have thresholds-based fall detection and a combined rule based, fall detection classification algorithm. Additionally, data will be collected and stored on a MySQL database, and IoT protocols will be used to provide both buzz and email alerts. The results of the various experiments demonstrate an improvement in the accurate detection of falls and obstacles, and sensor fusion will reduce false detection. The system has the potential to monitor health and stays accurate even in emergency situations. The proposed system is everything needed to provide a cost-effective full spectrum monitoring safe guardian and has the potential to grow based on the receiver''s needs. This paper introduces a healthcare and safety monitoring system that uses a set of specialized sensors including an MPU6050, an ultrasonic sensor, a pulse sensor, a temperature sensor, and a GPS module. The sensors are aimed at detecting unfortunate events such as falls and obstacles as well as other potentially harmful situations. Decision-making and performance are improved through the application of AI Model and other reinforcement learning techniques. The system communicates with caregivers by creating alerts and sending fall sample data and other information. Testing of the system shows validity, performance, and reliability.</p>','["IoT, Fall Detection, Obstacle Detection, Arduino, GPS Tracking, Health Monitoring"]',NULL,16,1,'864 - 874','10.59543/jidmis.v3.473','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',45),
(474,474,1,'An Intelligent Sentiment Analysis Model Based on Fine-Tuned BERT Architecture for Context-Aware Opinion Mining and Emotional Polarity Classification','<p>The rapid expansion of user generated text through social media, online reviews, and feedback mechanisms has made consequent and rapid development of sentiment analysis as a key challenge in the field of Natural Language Processing (NLP) the social media text, online reviews and feedback. In the automated decision support systems, sentiment analysis, as a part of the system, brand/ product/ service monitoring systems, and public sentiment systems/analyst applications, picking positive/ negative emotion from the text is a must. Although a lot of research has been done, the earlier Machine Learning (ML) and Deep Learning (DL) models are still facing issues of contextual dependence, polysemy, and lack of resilience in various linguistic phenomena. To overcome the above research gaps, this paper presents an adaptive intelligent-aware framework for sentiment analysis using fine-tuned Transformers models/architectures whereby RoBERTa and DistilBERT are suggested as main/primary models. The proposed methodology will include data preprocessing, subword embedding tokenization, and sentiment polarity classification through supervised fine-tuning of the pre-trained language models. The use of RoBERTa will be since RoBERTa is known for its pre-training and contextual representation. DistilBERT will be used to provide the balance of other models, and to allow the framework to be designed for use in time-constrained and low-resource computing environments. The proposed system will be tested using the available datasets for sentiment analysis using the standard metrics, and to evaluate its performance, the accuracy, precision, recall, and the F1 score will be tested. The results show that the fine-tuned RoBERTa model can outperform the baseline BERT-based and traditional deep learning models, in terms of classification accuracy and managing context-dependent sentiment expressions. Even though DistilBERT is less accurate, it has a lower inference time and memory usage, creating a balance between performance and efficiency. The comparative analysis exhibits the proposed models’ scalability and robustness, despite differing lengths of text and sentiment distributions. This study advocates the proven utility of the transformer-based language models for sentiment classification and recognizes RoBERTa and DistilBERT as intelligent, efficient, and accurate sentiment analysis models. The proposed framework can be adapted for future work, such as sentiment analysis for multiple languages or opinion mining in specific domains</p>','["Sentiment Analysis, RoBERTa, DistilBERT, Context-Aware Opinion Mining, Emotional Polarity Classification, Transformer Models"]',NULL,16,1,'875 - 890','10.59543/jidmis.v3.474','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',46),
(475,475,1,'Sign Language Video Translation for Effective Communication using Hybrid Deep Learning Techniques','<p>Sign language is the communication medium for millions with speech and hearing disabilities. One of the biggest challenges with existing sign language systems is that there is minimal communication between the deaf and hearing communities, because there are no real time, accurate translation systems. Breaking this communication barrier involves advanced and precise computation. This includes interpreting complex gestures captured through video, with the least amount of lag possible. Challenges in fully automating sign language video translation include continuous gesture streams, variability of multiple signers, co-articulation, occlusions, and sign sequence modeling and their temporal dependencies. Many of the existing systems are highly dependent on controlled conditions and have a limited vocabulary, and therefore a limited scope of temporal dependability. For this reason, most of the systems are inadequate in real world scenarios. This paper proposes the Hybrid Deep Learning Framework for Sign Language Video Translation (HDLF-SLVT). This framework utilizes a Dual-Stream Spatiotemporal Convolutional Network, a Cross-Modal Transformer Encoder, and a CTC (Connectionist Temporal Classification) decoder. The framework uses MediaPipe Holistic to provide robust extraction of the skeletal keypoints. From there, a multi-branch feature fusion is used. The framework proposes a Temporal Context Attention Module (TCAM), which increases recognition accuracy in the presence of continuous signing. The author proposes a Keypoint-Guided Attention, a Temporal Sliding Window Alignment, and a Multi-Label CTC Sequence Decoder with a beam search optimization. The system was tested on the standard datasets, PHOENIX14, CSL-Daily, and an ISL (Indian Sign Language) dataset collected by the author. Experimental results indicate a Word Error Rate (WER) of 18.6% for PHOENIX14, 14.2% for CSL-Daily, and 16.8% for the ISL dataset, which is an improvement on the current state-of-the-art by 3.1%, 2.4%, and 4.7%, respectively. The system achieves a real-time inference of 28.4 frames per second using commodity hardware. It is evident that HDLF-SLVT defines a new, accurate, and efficient landscape of sign language translation, paving the way for new methodologies in the design of assistive technology in communication, education, and public service. The following research will be an advancement of the proposed framework by incorporating multiple sign languages, as well as limited sign language resources.</p>','["Sign Language Recognition, Video Translation, Deep Learning, Transformer Networks, Temporal Modeling, Multimodal Fusion"]',NULL,16,1,'891 - 908','10.59543/jidmis.v3.475','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',47),
(476,476,1,'A Hybrid Deep Learning Spectral–Spatial Graph Neural Network with Harris Hawk and Levy-Flight Optimization for Robust Hyperspectral Image Classification','<p>Hyperspectral image (HSI) classification is a crucial task in many remote sensing applications including environmental monitoring, precision agriculture, mineral exploration, and land-cover mapping. However, the high dimensionality of hyperspectral data, spectral redundancy, noise contamination and complex spatial variations often pose challenges to the classification process and can significantly reduce classification accuracy. To overcome these problems, this paper proposes a Hybrid Deep Learning Spectral- Spatial Graph Neural Network(HSS-GNN-HHLF) optimized by Harris Hawk and Levy Flight Strategy. The proposed framework integrates spectral feature reduction, intrinsic spatial reflectance extraction, spectral-spatial feature fusion, graph-based feature learning and adaptive metaheuristic optimization into a unified model. First, spectral redundancy is reduced through spectral fusion and intrinsic decomposition techniques. Then the spatial affinity modeling is adopted to preserve the texture continuity and relieve the illumination distortions. The improved spectral and spatial features are fused and represented as graph structures to capture the nonlinear neighborhood relationships. Finally, the Levy Flight search based Harris Hawk Optimization is employed to adaptively tune the graph-learning parameters, thereby enhancing the convergence and classification performance.</p>','["Deep Learning, Graph Neural Network, Harris Hawk Optimization, Hyperspectral Image Classification, Levy Flight Strategy, Spectral\\u2013Spatial Feature Fusion, Remote Sensing Analytics"]',NULL,16,1,'909 - 933','10.59543/jidmis.v3.476','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',48),
(477,477,1,'Automated Water Quality Prediction System for Aquaponics Farming Setup','<p>The need for effective, sustainable aquaponics farming has paved the way for the development of efficient aquaponics monitoring systems. Most available water-quality monitoring systems for aquaponics are primarily laboratory-based and require human intervention. Advancements in technology have enabled the use of IoT-based water quality monitoring systems for rapid model deployment. Previous researchers have used machine learning-based systems for multi-class water quality classification for aquaponics farming. Still, the accuracy of these systems is affected by various factors, such as unseen data, high dimensionality, and imbalanced datasets. This work focuses on designing and implementing an IoT-based automated water-quality prediction system-as-a-service that leverages the internet, sensors, advanced analytics, and computing for aquaponics farming. The proposed research aims to improve an IoT-based system by optimizing data augmentation and deep learning methods. The sensor data is first collected from various sensors. Then, faulty (Non-operational) nodes are identified using the Hybrid-Adaptive Neuro-fuzzy Inference System (H-ANFIS) method. The improved data augmentation method, the novel Modified Synthetic Minority Oversampling Technique (M-SMOTE) algorithm, is employed to address class imbalance. Then, we proposed an Adapted Deep Learning Modified Neural Network (A-DLMNN) classifier for a multi-class classification system to predict the suitability of water for aquaponics farming. The experimental results achieved an accuracy of 95.65%, with a validation loss of 0.07 for water quality classification. Thus, the system has improved accuracy by 21.89 percent compared to existing systems.</p>','["Aquaponics farming, Internet of Things, water quality prediction, sensor fault detection, H-ANFIS", "M-SMOTE, deep learning, class imbalance, multi-class classification."]',NULL,16,1,'934 - 958','10.59543/jidmis.v3.477','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',49),
(478,478,1,'Energy-Efficient FPGA-Based CNN Accelerator: Achieving Significant I/O Power Reduction through Hierarchical Interface Optimization and Architectural Redesign','<p>The Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) based AI inference on edge devices faces challenges like computing complexity, high power consumption and large memory requirements. The Proposed work introduces a custom CNN accelerator design that balances the computational performance and power. The CNN accelerator is designed to optimize the performance in terms of energy-efficient and hardware-feasible. The CNN accelerator is implemented on Xilinx Artix-7 XC7A100T Field Programmable Gate Array (FPGA), which significantly reduces exposed I/O signals from 1,283 to 42 ports, improving routing feasibility and lowering I/O utilization to nearly 20%. Power analysis shows total on-chip power consumption of only 0.381 W with nearly 72% I/O power reduction through interface optimization and controlled switching activity. The proposed framework reduces power density from approximately 2.8 W/mm² to 0.76 W/mm², thereby improving thermal efficiency, reducing routing congestion, and enhancing overall energy-efficient FPGA deployment.</p>','["AI,CNN Accelerator, FPGA, Hardware Acceleration, Energy Efficiency, Power Utilisation"]',NULL,16,1,'959 - 976','10.59543/jidmis.v3.478','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',50),
(479,479,1,'A Triangular Employability Digital Twin Framework for Explainable Graduate Career Readiness Prediction through Student–Faculty–Industry Intelligence.','<p>Graduate employability has become a critical challenge in higher education, driven by the growing mismatch between institutional outputs and evolving industry expectations. Existing prediction frameworks predominantly rely on student-centric datasets, neglecting the interconnected influence of faculty mentoring and industry collaboration on employability outcomes. This paper proposes a Triangular Employability Digital Twin Framework that simultaneously integrates Student, Faculty, and Industry data into a unified, multi-stakeholder intelligence system capable of predicting, explaining, and actively enhancing graduate employability. The framework is built upon data collected through structured online surveys administered across multiple engineering and arts and science colleges, consolidating 1,000 student records alongside corresponding faculty and industry entries across eight structured datasets. A weighted composite employability score is computed from twelve academic, soft skill, mentoring, and industry attributes, with percentile-based thresholding partitioning students into Low, Medium, and High employability categories. An XGBoost multiclass classifier trained on an 80/20 stratified split achieved an overall accuracy of 94.5%, with precision, recall, and F1-scores exceeding 0.91 across all three classes. SHAP explainability analysis identified Hiring Readiness and CGPA as the most influential employability predictors, with importance scores of 0.40 and 0.31 respectively. A typed heterogeneous knowledge graph comprising 1,150 nodes and 2,908 edges was constructed, with greedy modularity community detection identifying 12 distinct Student–Faculty–Industry clusters achieving a modularity score of 0.4442. Digital twin uplift simulations demonstrated that a combined multi-intervention strategy improved High Employability Probability from 0.9944 to 0.9975. The results collectively validate the framework as a robust, interpretable, and institutionally deployable employability intelligence system.</p>','["Career Readiness Prediction, Digital Twin Simulation, Employability Classification, Graduate Employability, Higher Education Analytics, Knowledge Graph Construction, SHAP Explainability, Triangular Ecosystem Modelling, XGBoost Classification."]',NULL,16,1,'977 - 1002','10.59543/jidmis.v3.479','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',51),
(480,480,1,'Density-Aware Explainable Hybrid CNN–Transformer Framework for Automated BI-RADS Breast Density Classification in Mammographic Images','<p>Breast density is a significant imaging biomarker associated with breast cancer risk and plays an important role in mammographic interpretation. Breast density classification helps radiologists to assess risk, make screening decisions, and manage breast cancers individually. While traditional CNN-based methods have been the main interest in recent years and have shown good performance by considering local textures, transformer-based methods have been prioritizing global contextual relationships. However, these methods are found to show a poor ability in learning the multi-scale density patterns jointly, and in delivering clinically meaningful explanations of their classification results. To overcome these limitations, in this study, a new Density-Aware Explainable Hybrid CNN-Transformer (DAEHCT) framework is proposed for automated breast density classification. We propose a Density-Guided Multi-Scale Feature Extraction (DMSFE) module to extract fine, medium and global density features, a Density Region Attention Map (DRAM) module to focus on dense tissue regions, a CNN–Transformer Cross Fusion Block (CTCF) module to foster bi-directional local-global interaction of features, and a Density Severity Embedding Layer (DSEL) module to learn BI-RADS specific features. The end-to-end learning system based on the framework not only learns from the mammographic images, but also produces evidence that explains the classification results. Experimental results show that it outperforms the current CNN, transformer and hybrid models. DAEHCT proposed in this study shows an accuracy of 97.86%, precision of 97.43% and an AUC of 99.12%. The findings suggest that density-aware feature learning and explainable decision support significantly aid in the assessment of breast density. The proposed framework presented an accurate and interpretable breast density classification solution in mammograms that can help in developing reliable computer-aided diagnostic systems for clinical breast imaging applications.</p>','["Breast Density Classification, Mammogram Analysis, CNN\\u2013Transformer Fusion, Explainable Artificial Intelligence, BI-RADS Density Assessment, Deep Learning"]',NULL,16,1,'1003 - 1027','10.59543/jidmis.v3.480','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',52),
(481,481,1,'Intelligent and Adaptive Prompt Management for Enhanced Generative AI Performance and Explainability','<p>Effective prompt engineering is crucial for optimizing Generative AI (GenAI) models across diverse applications. However, current methodologies often employ static or manual approaches that struggle with adaptability, personalization, and explainability, limiting the full potential of GenAI''s. This research paper show light on an intelligent adaptive prompt management framework using Hybrid Prompt Scoring Mechanism (HPSM), User-Intent–Context Modelling Module (UICM), Reinforcement Learning–Based Prompt Refinement (RLPR), Prompt Embedding Graph (PEG) and Explainable Prompt Decision Engine (XPDE). Where HPSM integrates semantic similarity, task alignment, domain relevance, and user intent to evaluate prompt effectiveness. UICM of this model utilizes transformer-based encoders to explicitly capture contextual nuances. RLPR for continuous improvement based on user feedback and PEG for achieving efficient and prompt information retrieval. At last XPDE of the model provides transparent rationales for prompt selection. The experiments show that our model gives better results than other methods. It produces more accurate, relevant, and personalized responses, and also explains its answers more clearly, across different datasets and test situations. Proposed system is with accuracy of 88%. This work improves automated prompt design by providing a clear and scalable solution that makes generative AI more effective and easier to use in both research and real-world applications.</p>','["Prompt Engineering, Generative AI, Intelligent Systems, Adaptive Prompts, Explainability, Reinforcement Learning, Context Modelin"]',NULL,16,1,'1028 - 1056','10.59543/jidmis.v3.481','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',53),
(482,482,1,'Computationally Efficient CKKS-Based Homomorphic Deep Learning for Privacy-Preserving Medical Image Classification','<p>The high rate of deep learning application in medical image analysis has made significant contribution to automated diagnosis, screening and clinical decision support. Nonetheless, sensitive patient information poses significant privacy, regulatory, and governance risks that tend to restrain widespread implementation of intelligent healthcare systems. Fully Homomorphic Encryption (FHE) is a highly effective solution because it provides the possibility to perform the computation with encrypted data without revealing the plaintext. This article introduces: a computationally efficient privacy preserving classification of medical images securely over chest X-ray images. The suggested system combines the Cheon-Kim-Kim-Song (CKKS) homomorphic encryption algorithm, realized with the help of the TenSEAL library, and a small convolutional neural network (CNN) that is used to diagnose three classes of patients with COVID-19, Normal, and Viral Pneumonia cases. The framework includes standardized preprocessing, class-balancing augmentation, encrypted data processing, trusted-zone inference, and implementation, which is deployment-oriented. Moreover, mathematical basis of CKKS and architecture of the classifier are developed systematically to be clear and reproducible methodologically. Experimental analysis on a balanced public chest X-ray dataset shows high prediction potential with accuracy 97.13%, precision 97.08%, recall 97.15%, F1-score 97.11%, specificity 98.57% and AUC 0.9968. Operationally, the system has an average secure prediction latency of 111.7 ms/sample and the entire secure pipeline is 296.3 ms/sample, which confirms that the system is practically feasible in the field. The findings determine that diagnostic performance and strict protection of privacy are not conflicting goals. This work provides a viable way to trustworthy artificial intelligence in healthcare by integrating cryptographic protection, computational effectiveness, and deployable deep learning.</p>','["Fully Homomorphic Encryption, CKKS Scheme, Deep Learning, Convolutional neural network, Medical Image Analysis, Privacy-preserving AI, TenSEAL, Secure computing, Chest X-ray Classification."]',NULL,16,1,'1057 - 1082','10.59543/jidmis.v3.482','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',54),
(483,483,1,'A Scalable Artificial Intelligence Framework for Autonomous Decision Making and Knowledge Representation in Complex Computational Systems','<p>The rapid digitization of enterprise, healthcare, and industrial processes has created an urgent demand for artificial intelligence systems capable of autonomous, transparent, and scalable decision making across highly complex computational environments. Conventional AI decision support systems suffer from three fundamental limitations: opacity of inference processes that undermines stakeholder trust, degraded performance under distributional shift when deployed at scale, and inadequate mechanisms for structured knowledge representation that would allow AI conclusions to be audited, corrected, and incrementally refined. These shortcomings prevent organizations from fully leveraging AI-driven automation in high-stakes domains where accountability is non-negotiable. This research proposes the Scalable Artificial Intelligence Framework (SAIF), a novel architecture that integrates autonomous decision making, dynamic knowledge representation, and built-in explainability into a unified computational system designed to operate reliably at organizational scale. SAIF is grounded in three theoretical pillars: (i) a hybrid neuro-symbolic reasoning engine that combines the pattern recognition strengths of deep neural networks with the structural clarity of symbolic logic, (ii) an adaptive ontology management system that continuously updates its knowledge graph using information extracted from operational decisions and environmental feedback, and (iii) a Transparency and Trust Engine that produces human-interpretable explanations calibrated to the cognitive needs of diverse stakeholders. Three algorithms are central to SAIF''s operation: the Adaptive Knowledge Graph Evolution Algorithm (AKGEA) for continuous ontology refinement, the Multi-Objective Autonomous Decision Algorithm (MOADA) for Pareto-optimal decision synthesis under conflicting constraints, and the Explainability-Aware Representation Learning algorithm (EARL) for producing compact, interpretable model representations. Experimental evaluation across benchmark datasets and simulated enterprise scenarios demonstrates that SAIF achieves 96.1% decision accuracy on large datasets, sustains 24.6 decisions per second under 5,000 concurrent users, and maintains 52.9% explainability at the highest complexity level tested, representing improvements of 9.7%, 203.7%, and 110.7% respectively over the strongest baseline. Memory utilization under knowledge base growth was reduced by 43.7% compared to competing frameworks. These results establish SAIF as a viable foundation for enterprise-grade autonomous decision systems that honor the transparency requirements of modern regulatory frameworks while delivering the performance demanded by large-scale deployment environments. The framework advances the state of the art in AI-driven decision support and opens new research directions in adaptive knowledge management and human-AI collaborative intelligence.</p>','["autonomous decision making, knowledge representation, explainable artificial intelligence, scalable AI framework, neuro-symbolic reasoning, decision support systems"]',NULL,16,1,'1083 - 1103','10.59543/jidmis.v3.483','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',55),
(488,488,1,'Decision-Oriented Analysis of Pediatric Dental Fear: A Systematic Review of Psychological and Behavioral Evidence','<p><strong>Background</strong>: Dental fear and anxiety are common psychological conditions among children. and they can  mess up cooperation during dental care, oral health, and overall quality of life. There are multiple psychological and behavioral contributors that help build up dental fear in pediatric groups, so it is rarely just one thing.</p>
