(6140,6140,1,'Impact of Mobile Mental Health Apps on Well-Being Among University Students in Qatar','<p>Mental health is one of the prevailing issues influencing students’ well-being, academic performance, and social life. This is especially pertinent for first-year university students who are struggling to adjust to a new environment. Considering this, the current study was designed to examine the impact of Mobile Mental Health Apps (MMHAs) on students’ social, emotional, and psychological well-being in Qatar. The research design of the current 15-week experimental study was longitudinal. The participants were first-year college students (boys only) at Qatar University. Sixty students were randomly assigned to experimental and control groups. Students in the experimental groups were taught through MMHAs based on Cognitive Behavioural Therapy (CBT). The data were collected using the Mental Health Continuum-Short Form (MHC-SF) four times at different stages of the study. The analysis was carried out using SPSS software. After deploying two-way repeated measures ANOVA, the analysis revealed that MMHAs had improved students’ social, emotional, and psychological well-being significantly in the experimental group compared to the control group. The results of Bonferroni-corrected post hoc comparisons revealed that there is a significant difference between the four stages of the intervention. Based on the results, the current study offers evidence-based implications to enhance the use of MMHAs to overcome Qatari students’ mental health issues at the university level. Moreover, the study had several practical, policy, and research implications for students and teachers at the university level.</p>','["mental health, mobile applications, well-being, cognitive behavioral theory, psychoeducation"]',NULL,15,1,'202 - 214',NULL,'The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-29',5),
