<p>Objectives: This study examined whether baseline psychosocial wellbeing predicts mental health outcomes across three waves and identified the most influential psychosocial dimensions.</p>
<p>Methods: Using a quasi-longitudinal design, 350 adult refugees in refugee camps across the West Bank, Palestine were assessed at three six-month intervals (T1–T3). Psychosocial wellbeing (social connectedness, purpose, cultural continuity, safety) was measured via the PWB-R, and mental health via the PCL-5, PHQ-9, and GAD-7. Analyses included hierarchical regression, autoregressive modeling, latent growth curves, and relative importance analysis with multiple imputation.</p>
<p>Results: Baseline psychosocial wellbeing predicted T3 mental health severity (β = −.39, p &lt; .001), remaining significant after controlling for baseline mental health (β = −.27, p &lt; .001). Both psychosocial wellbeing (d = −0.59) and mental health (d = +0.61) worsened over time. Social connectedness (34.7%) and perceived safety (29.8%) were the strongest predictors.</p>
<p>Conclusions: Psychosocial wellbeing is a robust upstream predictor of refugee mental health trajectories. Interventions targeting social connectedness and perceived safety should be prioritized in humanitarian responses.</p>','["refugee mental health", "psychosocial wellbeing", "COVID-19", "quasi-longitudinal", "PTSD", "humanitarian settings."]',NULL,24,1,'220-244','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1684','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',13),
(1685,1685,1,'The Impact of Digital Health Technology on the Quality of Patient Care in Nursing: A Study on the Use of Smart Applications and Remote Monitoring Devices','<p>Digital health technology has become a revolutionary phenomenon in the modern healthcare system, which offers a chance to improve the quality, safety, and efficiency of patient care. This paper examines how digital health technology, in this case smart applications and remote monitoring tools affect patient care in nursing at Al Karak Government Hospital, in southern Jordan. The research design used was a quantitative and descriptive-correlational research design to study 145 nurses who deal with patients and use digital health systems. Structured Likert-scale questionnaires were used to collect the data which were analyzed using descriptive statistics and simple linear regression. The results showed that digital health technologies are viewed favorably on the part of nurses, in general, especially concerning the communication networks, governance, availability of devices and software, and clinical decision support. Regression analysis revealed a significant positive correlation to exist between the digital health technology adoption and the quality of patient care ( = 0.652, p &lt; 0.001), and the digital health technology explains 42.5% of the perceived care quality (R 2 = 0.425). These findings highlight the importance of effective digital tools in improving patient security, precision and accuracy in documentation, prompt service delivery, and patient confidence and support and do not displace the human side of nursing care. Issues of employee education and the best use of technology point towards the areas of improvement. The research adds empirical knowledge regarding the advantages of digital health adoption in nursing and offers practical recommendations that healthcare administrators and policymakers can use to maximize technology application in a hospital.</p>','["Digital Health Technology", "Nursing practice", "Smart Applications", "Remote monitoring devices", "Quality of patient care", "Jordan."]',NULL,24,1,'245-262','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1685','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',14),
(1686,1686,1,'Institutionalizing Cybersecurity within the Operational Framework of National Security','<p>The world is heading toward more complex and potentially more dangerous developments than those currently observed, driven by the rapid evolution of global cyber technologies that now span virtually unlimited digital spaces. Digital technologies and advanced electronic communications have become the central pillar of this environment, serving as essential tools for all human activities and transactions. Public and private data, in all their detailed forms, are now stored, processed, and transmitted through cyber technologies and intelligent platforms. These technologies have come to dominate critical domains, including industrial security, financial markets, transportation systems (air, land, and sea), social records, security and military operations, and even entertainment applications and children’s programs.</p>
<p>It is neither surprising nor unexpected that this cyber revolution, which has transformed various aspects of daily life, has also produced negative impacts on societal behavior, resulting in new forms of crime associated with modern technologies, commonly referred to as cybercrime. Keeping pace with these technological transformations, which now shape human life and transactions, along with their future implications—both positive and negative—requires serious examination of security protection mechanisms and the mitigation of risks and threats that might hinder the effective use of cyber technologies. This necessitates continuous research and study in cybersecurity as an essential discipline. Ongoing research and learning are critical to avoiding cyber risks and threats, which have evolved into powerful tools in international conflicts.</p>
<p>This research is structured into an introductory chapter and five main chapters:</p>
<p>Chapter One: Concept of cybersecurity, its types, and threats</p>
<p>Chapter Two: Cybersecurity planning</p>
<p>Chapter Three: Cybersecurity management</p>
<p>Chapter Four: Cybersecurity applications</p>
<p>Chapter Five: Structuring cybersecurity within the national security system</p>
<p>This research has examined cybersecurity from a comprehensive perspective, highlighting its conceptual foundations, strategic planning requirements, governance structures, legal dimensions, and its critical role within national security systems. It has addressed cybersecurity in its broad sense, including its fundamental components and its close relationship with national security and considering that cybersecurity investment is no longer optional; it has become a necessity. Investment in cybersecurity has moved from “nice to have” to “must have.”</p>
<p>The findings emphasize that cybersecurity is no longer a technical necessity alone but a strategic imperative that influences all aspects of modern life, including economic stability, social development, and national sovereignty. The research concluded with a summary of findings and a set of recommendations aimed at strengthening the effectiveness of cybersecurity systems in the frame-work of national security operations.</p>','["Cybersecurity, national security, data monetization, cybercrime, cyber criminology."]',NULL,24,1,'263-284','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1686','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',15),
(1687,1687,1,'Artificial Intelligence in Supporting Strategic Decision-Making and Its Impact on Organizational Performance','<p>AI capability is a new strategic capability in the organization that goes beyond operational efficiency and can support the quality strategic decision-making, sustainable performance of an organization, and high decision quality. Though AI capability is evolving, current research remains disparate in how to transform an AI capability to a organizational value with the role of governance, leadership, and organizations capability. To solve this, in this study, a integrated conceptual framework grounded in the theory of resource-based view(RBV), dynamic capabilities theory(DCT) and the AI Governance literature is developed and empirically tested. In the model, the sequential relation between AI capability, AI governance, strategic decision quality, organizational agility, and organizational performance was proposed and the moderating role of digital leadership was examined. An explanatory sequential mixed-methods research design was used. The empirical analysis includes two phases. In the first phase, a cross-sectional survey of 446 senior executives and strategic decision makers of public and private organizations was conducted to empirically test the proposed integrated model using Partial Least Squares Structural Equation Modeling (PLS-SEM). In the second phase, qualitative data from 30 semi-structured interviews with senior executives was collected to gain a deep understanding of AI governance, digital leadership and organizational agility practices. Multi-group analysis further revealed differences in the proposed relationships for public and private organizations. Findings revealed that AI capability not only significantly strengthens the AI governance, and consequently the strategic decision quality, but it also improve the organizational agility, resulting in improved performance. Furthermore, digital leadership has a positive effect on reinforcing the association between AI governance and the strategic decision quality. Overall, this study integrates the technology capability, the organizational capability and the leadership capability to establish an AI-enabled strategic decision-making and performance management framework, and provides strategic insights for organizations that aim to realize greater value from their AI investments.</p>','["Artificial intelligence Capability", "AI Governance", "Digital Leadership", "Strategic decision Quality", "Organizational agility", "Organizational performance."]',NULL,24,1,'285-304','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1687','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',16),
(1689,1689,1,'ArCompose: Compositional Text to Image Synthesis for Modern Standard and Iraqi Arabic via Token Supervised Diffusion and NMT Driven Prompt Planning','<p>The latest diffusion-based text-to-image (T2I) models are capable of generating very realistic images, but they are easily destabilized by the compositional prompts involving multiple objects, attributes, and relations. Meanwhile, the use of multiple languages is on the increase, yet Arabic (particularly the dialectal ones) is underrepresented in the generative framework and in the evaluation metrics. In this paper, ArCompose is introduced, a compositional T2I system, which fills these two gaps through an integrative approach based on token-level supervision, a compositional curriculum, and a linguistically informed Arabic prompt planner. Beginning with Stable Diffusion version 1.5, auxiliary losses are added that both match noun tokens to pseudo segmentation masks at selected cross attention layers and enhance token to region grounding with no change in inference cost. A COCO-only three-stage curriculum is developed, which introduces the model to single-object, multi-object, and full complexity scenes gradually in order to stabilize training. An OPUS MT Arabic-English model is adapted on the language side, training it on the Modern Standard Arabic style and a small Iraqi dialect corpus, and uses it with spaCy dependency parsing to generate weighted, Compel-style English prompts on an Arabic input. The experiment on the T2I-CompBench demonstrates that ArCompose achieves a higher score (0.62) on the Attribute Binding (against 0.39 for the baseline) and 0.44 Relationship score (against 0.29). Moreover, using the ArCompose system on the VISOR benchmark, the object Accuracy is increased from 30.2% to 54.2%, which eliminates the difference between the system and state-of-the-art English-only systems such as TokenCompose. The NMT-based planner was more accurate, with BLEU scores on MSA captions of 44.6, and the token-monitored cost is almost zero at 3.3%. By using an experimental with controlled single-dataset curriculum on COCO, the structural guidance and curriculum learning (without massive pre-training on huge amounts of multilingual data) were proven to be more than adequate to overcome intricate dialectal and compositional ambiguities in generative AI.</p>','["Text to Image Synthesis", "Diffusion Models", "Compositionality", "Arabic Neural Machine Translation", "Curriculum Learning."]',NULL,24,1,'305-332','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1689','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',17),
(1690,1690,1,'The Effect of Social Media on the Rise of Cybersecurity Crimes among Generation Alpha','<p>Social media has become an essential part of children’s daily lives, particularly for Generation Alpha, who are exposed to digital platforms from early childhood. While social media offers educational and social opportunities, it also increases children’s exposure to cybersecurity crimes. This study aims to examine the effect of social media use on the rise of cybersecurity crimes among Generation Alpha.</p>
<p>A descriptive–analytical research design was adopted, using a structured questionnaire to collect data on social media usage patterns, exposure to cyber threats, digital awareness, and parental supervision. The findings reveal a significant relationship between frequent social media use and increased exposure to cybersecurity risks, including cyberbullying, online harassment, phishing attempts, and inappropriate content.</p>
<p>Low levels of cybersecurity awareness and limited parental supervision were identified as key factors increasing vulnerability. The study contributes to the literature by focusing on Generation Alpha, a group that remains underrepresented in cybersecurity research, and emphasizes the importance of early digital safety education and effective parental mediation to reduce online risks.</p>','["Generation Alpha", "Social Media", "Cybersecurity Crimes", "Digital Literacy", "Child Online Safety."]',NULL,24,1,'333-358','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1690','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',18),
(1691,1691,1,'Evaluating the Clinical Utility of Preoperative 2D Ultrasound and Color Doppler in High-Risk Placenta Accreta Spectrum: A Clinicopathologic Correlation','<p>Background: Placenta Accreta Spectrum (PAS) disorders are associated with significant maternal morbidity and mortality, primarily due to severe hemorrhagic complications. Antenatal detection using ultrasound and Doppler imaging can improve surgical outcomes and reduce complications in high-risk cases.</p>
<p>Objective: To evaluate the role of preoperative 2D ultrasound and Color Doppler in predicting surgical outcomes and diagnostic performance in patients at high risk for PAS disorders.</p>
<p>Methods: This multi-centric cohort study was conducted at Al Galaa University Hospital and Al Zahraa University Hospital, Faculty of Medicine, Al Azhar University Egypt, involving 82 pregnant women identified as high-risk for PAS. Patients underwent preoperative ultrasound and Color Doppler imaging to assess features predictive of PAS. The findings were compared with intraoperative outcomes and histopathological diagnoses. Statistical analyses were performed to determine diagnostic accuracy and associations with surgical outcomes.</p>
<p>Results: The results revealed significant insights into the demographic and clinical characteristics of the study cohort. The mean age of patients was 33 years, and 29.3% had a history of prior uterine surgeries other than cesarean sections. Ultrasound findings indicated that the loss of retroplacental sonolucent zone (90.2%) and thinning of the serosa-bladder interface (81.7%) were the most frequently observed abnormalities. Histopathological analysis confirmed that morbidly adherent placenta was present in 63.4% of cases, with Placenta accreta being the most common subtype (41.5%), followed by increta (18.3%) and percreta (3.7%). Surgical outcomes showed that 42.7% of patients required hysterectomy, 15.9% experienced bladder injuries, and 91.5% needed blood transfusions. The number of ultrasound abnormalities (≥5) demonstrated a high diagnostic accuracy (AUC = 0.947) for predicting morbidly adherent placenta.</p>
<p>Conclusions: Preoperative ultrasound and Color Doppler imaging significantly enhance the prediction of PAS severity and associated surgical outcomes. The number of ultrasound abnormalities serves as a reliable diagnostic marker, aiding preoperative planning and management in high-risk cases.</p>','["Placenta Accreta Spectrum, Histopathology, Ultrasound, Color Doppler, Surgical Outcomes, Antenatal Diagnosis, Morbidly Adherent Placenta, PAS Disorders."]',NULL,24,1,'359-383','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1691','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',19),
(1692,1692,1,'Mediated Perception of Architecture: Time, Place, and Design Dimensions as Perceptual Factors','<p>In the 21st century, the way architecture is encountered has been transformed by technological development. Without the need for direct engagement, Architectural experience has been mediated through technologies. Based on phenomenological and postphenomenological perspectives. The study hypothesises that media technologies have a dual effect on architectural perception, as they may both enhance and limit the experience of architecture through time, place, and design. Empirically, a comparative research design is applied based on four paired cases structured under two analytical dimensions: temporal accessibility and spatial reachability. The first dimension includes Hardly Accessed Architecture (the Round City of Baghdad and Rusafa’s Baghdad) and Easily Accessed Architecture (the former and current Unknown Soldier Monuments in Baghdad). The second dimension includes Easily Reached Architecture (Hagia Sophia and Blue Mosque in Istanbul) and Hardly Reached Architecture (Dome of the Rock and Al-Aqsa Mosque in Jerusalem). These pairs were selected because they are misidentified in the media. The analysis is based on widely circulated media, enabling the study. The findings show that media transforms perception into a distributed process, in ambiguity of name dominance, symbolic overlap, stylistic similarity, spatial adjacency, and inaccurate labelling. The paper concludes that the media is the third entity that reconstructs the perceiver-perceived relationship, reshaping through time, place, and design dimensions.</p>','["Architectural Perception", "Media", "Mediated Perception"]',NULL,24,1,'384-397','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1692','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',20),
(1693,1693,1,'Modular Functionalism and AGIL-Digital: A Theoretical Reconstruction of Social Systems in the Information Era Through a Study of the Maiyah Community','<p>This study reconstructs Talcott Parsons’ structural functionalism paradigm in the context of a digital network-based Islamic social movement in 21st-century Indonesia. Using the Maiyah community led by Emha Ainun Nadjib (Cak Nun) as the object of analysis, this study argues that the classic AGIL (Adaptation, Goal-Attainment, Integration, Latency) framework needs to be updated into what the author calls “AGIL-Digital” (AGIL-D) and “Modular Functionalism.” Qualitative data collected through participant observation, in-depth interviews, and document analysis show that the Maiyah community has succeeded in maintaining the continuity of its value system throughout three historical phases from traditional social movements (1980s), new social movements (1990s–2000s), to contemporary social movements (2010s to present) through a modular and decentralized cultural-spiritual adaptation mechanism. The theoretical synthesis between Parsons’ AGIL framework, Manuel Castells’ network society theory, and Anthony Wallace’s revitalization theory produces a new analytical model capable of explaining how social systems maintain their essential functions outside of formal hierarchical structures. The study identifies four key theoretical propositions constituting the AGIL-Digital model: (P1) digital modularity the capacity of autonomous nodes to replicate core functions independently; (P2) asynchronous latency the temporal unbounding of value transmission through digital archives; (P3) spiritual goal-attainment the prioritization of individual subjective sovereignty over collective political outcomes; and (P4) structural immunity the cultivation of institutional resilience against algorithmic manipulation and platform fragility. These propositions advance a post-Parsonian sociological framework with direct relevance to the study of non-Western, spiritually-oriented social movements in the digital age.</p>','["Modular Functionalism", "AGIL-Digital", "Maiyah Community", "Islamic Social Movement", "Network Society", "Social Systems Theory"]',NULL,24,1,'398-419','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1693','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',21),
(1694,1694,1,'The Impacts of Problem-Based Online Learning and Online Self-Efficacy on Students’ Ability in Writing Problem Solution Essays','<p>This research aims to determine the impact of applying problem-based online learning strategy and online self-efficacy on the EFL students’ ability in writing a problem-solution essay. This research used quasi-experimental design. It was conducted at a reputable university in Kediri City of East Java, Indonesia, involving 81 first semester students of Pharmacy study program of Health Sciences Faculty. The students were divided into 2 groups: experimental group consisting of 41 students and control group consisting of 40 students. This research showed that the experimental group can improve their ability in writing a problem-solution essay better than the control group who did not gain online self-efficacy. There is an interaction between the impact of implementing PBOL and OSE on students'' ability to write essays, which is reflected in the post-test in the experimental class. In the experimental class, students had essay writing that was more coherent and persuasive than in the control class. Thus, having been taught how to apply a problem-based online strategy and applied online self-efficacy, students can improve their ability in writing a problem-solution essay.</p>','["Problem-based online learning (PBOL), online self-efficacy (OSE), English Writing Ability"]',NULL,24,1,'420-442','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1694','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',22),
(1695,1695,1,'Political Marketing and its Impact on Gaining Political Consumer Satisfaction (the Iraqi Elections as a Model)','<p>Marketing is a common term; when we hear about marketing, the market that is, economic activity immediately comes to mind. However, recently, many terms related to marketing have emerged, such as political marketing or digital marketing, among others. Marketing is a set of processes or activities that work to discover customer desires and develop a range of products or services that satisfy those needs. Therefore, political marketing is not a multifaceted hybrid, as it is a marketing activity that uses modern technologies to influence individuals within society It is a political activity because it is concerned with political action, which is working in the public sphere, in the way that a political organization can exercise power that includes strategic activity because it depends in its contents on strategic thinking in planning, setting plans and formulating strategies, including different policies and programs for the political marketing activities that are to be achieved according to the general goals of the political organization. It is a behavioral activity because it is concerned with knowing the aspirations, needs, issues and concerns of each segment of the social segments within society towards the activities of political organizations.</p>','["Political marketing, political marketing mix, political consumer satisfaction."]',NULL,24,1,'443-460','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1695','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',23),
(1697,1697,1,'The Concept of Bilingualism Reviewed.','<p>Bilingualism has a complex sociolinguistic nature that could be seen from different perspectives. There are some current reasons that affect the phenomenon of Bilingualism. The spread of war and displacement, and migration are all among these reasons. The researchers studied various aspects of bilingualism i.e. bilingualism and gender, bilingualism and diglossia, bilingualism and code switching, Neurological and psycholinguistics aspects of bilingualism, bilingualism and education, bilingualism and second language acquisition. The researcher discusses definition of bilingualism, reasons, types, advantages and disadvantages, and factors affecting bilingualism. Some consideration is given to countries where the phenomenon of bilingualism is increasing recently i.e. Saudi Arabia, USA, .. This paper is useful to, language planners, educationists, linguistists and other researchers.The phenomenon of bilingualism deserves further and more future investigations because of its spread and great influence in many linguistic areas.</p>','["Bilingualism, proficiency, acquisition, simultaneous, consecutive, balanced, dominant, cognitive, code-switching, diglossia."]',NULL,24,1,'461-465','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1697','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',24),
(1698,1698,1,'Understanding Lived Experiences of Workplace Stress Among Bedside Emergency Nurses in King Abdul-Aziz Hospital at Makkah City','<p>The Emergency Department (ED) represents a highly demanding work environment for Registered Nurses (RNs), characterized by significant levels of occupational stress. This stress is attributed to multiple factors, including interactions with interdisciplinary team members and the inherently complex nature of the ED setting, which involves exposure to trauma, death, emotional distress, moments of relief and joy, and continuous unpredictability. While existing literature has examined stress within general healthcare settings and its impact on staff, there remains a notable gap in research specifically addressing stress within the ED context. Moreover, few studies have explored stressors from the perspective of ED nurses themselves.</p>
<p>This dissertation adopts an interpretive phenomenological approach to explore the lived experiences of ED RNs, with a particular focus on their perceptions of stress within this unique clinical environment. Data were collected through in-depth, semi-structured interviews with practicing nurses, allowing for thematic analysis and interpretation. Participants’ narratives revealed both negatively perceived stressors (distress) and positively experienced stress (eustress), reflecting the dual nature of stress within emergency care practice.</p>
<p>Findings from this study highlight the critical importance of supportive workplace conditions in mitigating distress among ED nurses. Specifically, an optimal ED environment is characterized by alignment with individual professional goals, the availability of adequate resources, and the promotion of effective communication within interdisciplinary teams. Based on these findings, recommendations are proposed to enhance resource allocation and strengthen interdisciplinary collaboration, ultimately improving both nurse well-being and quality of care.</p>','["distress, emergency department (ED), eustress, nurse, registered nurse (RN), stress, stressors."]',NULL,24,1,'466-487','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1698','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',25),
(1699,1699,1,'Can Strategic Management Increase Organizational Preparedness to Face Crises: Evidence from Jordanian SMEs','<p>Current study aimed to examine the effect of strategic management in increasing preparedness of Jordanian SMEs to face sudden and unpredicted crises in terms of (strategic planning, risk management, resource-based strategies, leadership and decision-making and strategic networking and alliances). Quantitative methodology was adopted, and a questionnaire was self-administered by (177) administrators from public and private hospitals, the Royal Medical Services, and numerous other health centers operating in Jordan during 2024-2025. SPSS was used to screen and analyze collected primary data. Results of analysis indicated the acceptance of main hypothesis and confirmed that ‘strategic management is able to increase preparedness of Jordanian SMEs to face crises’ scoring a r = 0.857. Regarding the adopted sub-variables, it was seen that strategic planning scored the highest influence with b= .280 confirming that with strategic planning, SMEs can review their internal and external conditions, spot possible risks and set up clear, workable plans in case of problems. Current study enabled us to reach conclusion that because of strategic management, organizations become better prepared for unforeseen situations by identifying hazards, making effective use of resources and developing thorough contingency plans. Study recommended that small and medium enterprises should help their managers and leaders develop strong leadership abilities.</p>','["Strategic management", "strategic planning", "risk management", "resource-based strategies", "leadership and decision-making", "strategic networking and alliances"]',NULL,24,1,'488-503','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1699','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',26),
(1700,1700,1,'Challenges Faced by Nurses in Providing Breastfeeding Counseling: A Descriptive Survey Study','<p>The research investigated nursing barriers to breastfeeding counseling assistance through evaluation of knowledge limitations and training deficits and institutional barriers and workplace constraints alongside social influences and psychological effects on nursing staff. Researchers used survey data collected from 100 nurses based at public hospitals, private hospitals and primary healthcare facilities as part of their descriptive study. Nurses evaluated different challenges through a structured questionnaire featuring a five-point Likert scale for response. The research demonstrated that nursing staff perceived their training was insufficient regarding breastfeeding counseling yet strongly requested additional workshops directed to this subject. The institution''s barriers along with insufficient funding and inadequate workplace backing and time-consuming responsibilities limited nurses from delivering proper counseling to patients. The challenges grew worse because of cultural beliefs as well as maternal resistance and language barriers between nurses and patients. The analysis revealed that inadequate breastfeeding counseling leads to psychological distress among nursing practitioners thus indicating the importance of institutional backing to improve counseling quality.</p>','["Challenges, Nurses, Breastfeeding Counseling."]',NULL,24,1,'504-516','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1700','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',27),
(1701,1701,1,'Professionalism and Expertise in Media Practice: Defining the Journalist','<p>The rapid evolution of media tools, formats, and research areas within the field of communication has positioned the communicator most notably the journalist as a central figure in the communication process. To fulfil this role, journalists must be scientifically qualified, professionally experienced, skilled in media practice, and adhere to rigorous ethical standards. A growing challenge in the field is the influx of individuals presenting themselves as media professionals, often without formal qualifications, turning media specialization into an added value primarily for those who possess innate skills and talent. This paper seeks to address the question: Who is a journalist? It aims to define the term considering contemporary media practice, including the roles occupied by those on social media platforms. Given the ongoing transformation in media landscapes, this paper will contribute to a clearer understanding of the qualifications and standards expected of journalists. To achieve this, the paper employs two methodological approaches: (1) a theoretical review of the existing literature and (2) an empirical survey. The theoretical review is complemented by a survey conducted by the researcher in September 2024, involving 125 media professionals. The findings demonstrate significant alignment between the literature and the survey results, confirming the researcher’s hypothesis: the three essential qualifications for a journalist are Practical Skills (97.6%), Academic Credentials (88%), and Affiliation with Professional Organizations (80.8%). This paper concludes by emphasizing the necessity of adhering to professional standards and ethical guidelines, supporting efforts to establish a more structured and accountable media environment. By identifying the qualifications of a true journalist, this paper aims to provide a framework for more rigorous professional standards in media practice.</p>','["Journalism, Media practice, Professionalism, Expertise"]',NULL,24,1,'517-527','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1701','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',28),
(1703,1703,1,'Enhancing Government Cybersecurity through the Utilization of Automated and AI-Driven Techniques to Fortify Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) Systems','<p>Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) systems are a key part of modern cybersecurity operations, especially in government settings where they are responsible for protecting sensitive data and making sure that important national services keep running. Even though traditional SIEM platforms are used a lot, they mostly use rule-based detection methods and manual incident response workflows. This makes it take longer to contain threats and keeps the false positive rate high. This paper systematically analyzes the operational difficulties encountered during SIEM implementations at the General Administration of Government Computer (GAGC) and the Palestinian Computer Emergency Response Team (PALCERT), both functioning under the Ministry of Telecommunications and Information Technology in Palestine. A mixed-methods research methodology—incorporating semi-structured expert interviews, focus group discussions, structured surveys, and quantitative log analysis—is utilized to assess current operational constraints and system limitations. Based on these results, we suggest a better AI-driven SIEM framework that combines machine learning-based threat detection with an automated incident response layer that follows security playbooks that have already been set up. Experimental assessment utilizing simulated cyberattack scenarios indicates statistically significant enhancements: detection accuracy rose from 79.1% to 91.5%, the false positive rate diminished from 32.4% to 20.2%, and the average incident response time decreased from 18.7 to 11.0 minutes, reflecting a 41% reduction. These results show that adding AI and automation to SIEM operations can make national cybersecurity much stronger, make analysts less tired, and make government digital infrastructure more resilient overall.</p>','["SIEM systems, government cybersecurity, automated incident response, machine learning, artificial intelligence, threat detection, and reducing false positives are all important."]',NULL,24,1,'528-541','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1703','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',29),
(1704,1704,1,'Evaluating and Comparing the Performance of Statistical and Machine Learning Algorithms to Achieve Successful Load Forecasting in Saudi Arabia','<p>Electricity is a vital resource that powers modern society, and reliable forecasting of electricity demand and supply is essential for the effective operation of power systems. Accurate forecasts allow power system operators to make informed decisions about generation, transmission, and distribution, which can help to prevent blackouts and other disruptions to the electricity supply. Various methods have been developed for forecasting electricity, including statistical methods such as ARIMA and Prophet and artificial intelligence (AI) algorithms such as recurrent neural network (RNN) and support vector machines (SVM). Recent advances in deep learning, particularly Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks, have demonstrated superior performance for time-series forecasting tasks, especially with high-frequency datasets.  In this paper, we compared the performance of six methods covering two statistical and four AI algorithms for forecasting electricity demand. They were applied to four different datasets: 1) A monthly KAPSARC, which stands for The King Abdullah Petroleum Studies and Research Center, Dataset in Saudi Arabia with limited historical data, 2) A generated hourly KAPSARC Dataset in Saudi Arabia, 3) An hourly PJM Dataset in USA with a large amount of data, and 4) A generated monthly PJM Dataset in USA. The performance of the approaches was different with each dataset. Overall, the results confirm that data richness particularly hourly granularity is a decisive factor in forecasting accuracy, and that deep learning models require substantial data volumes to outperform statistical baselines. These findings have direct implications for electricity infrastructure planning in Saudi Arabia under Vision 2030.</p>','["Electricity Load Forecasting", "Machine Learning", "Deep Learning", "LSTM", "ARIMA", "Prophet"]',NULL,24,1,'542-568','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1704','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',30),
(1705,1705,1,'Deep Learning Techniques for Crop Health Monitoring and Disease Detection','<p>This paper explores how deep learning methods can be used to monitor the health of crops and identify diseases, particularly for the apple crop. As the need for food security and sustainable farming methods increases, there is a strong demand for early detection of crop diseases. We have used a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN), based on the model of VGG16 architecture, since the model is known to be effective in image classification. The dataset contained 7771 training images for to enhance machines deep learning regarding plant diseases. Following that, validation image collection of 1747 images divided into four health conditions of the apple crops. In addition, the model was evaluated using 196 new images as a final test. To enhance the model capacity for recognizing diseases of real leaves rather than just memorize exact training pictures, data augmentation was used with ImageDataGenerator of TensorFlow. This means the training images were zoomed, rotated, and shifted to enable the machine detects more variations. Ten epochs of training were performed to measure the model accuracy. The results indicated that the model obtained significant improvement in training and validation accuracy from 56.43% to 78.12% and 92.94 to 96.93%, respectively. Most impressively, the final test dataset, which contained completely new images, scored an accuracy rate of 98%. The results indicate that in the architecture field the application of deep learning methodologies is effective, suggesting that automated detection of diseases by using sophisticated image analysis manages crop diseases identification efficiently. Combination of these methods successfully creates avenues for novel research to built real-time systems of crop disease monitoring, which help farmers increase their productions and farm their lands sustainably.</p>','["Deep Learning, Crop Disease Detection ,Disease Classification ,CNN ,VGG16 Architecture ,Data Augmentation"]',NULL,24,1,'569-584','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1705','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',31),
(1707,1707,1,'Predictive Priority-Based Wireless Charging for Critical Node Reliability in Wireless Rechargeable Sensor Networks: A Systematic Literature Review and Research Framework','<p>Power constraints pose a significant challenge to rechargeable wireless sensor networks, particularly in applications requiring high reliability and stable connectivity. This is despite substantial advancements in Wireless Power Transfer (WPT) technology thanks to the use of portable chargers and drones. Most current studies have focused primarily on improving charging efficiency or extending network lifespan. This systematic review aims to analyze recent studies on several criteria, including charging methods, AI and prediction techniques, charging decision-making mechanisms, the level of critical node consideration, and the limitations of each study. This systematic review study presents a systematic literature examines the recent studies on power management and wireless charging in Wireless Rechargeable Sensor Networks (WRSNs) published between 2016 and 2026. It focused on charging scheduling techniques, power prediction, AI-driven charging, and critical node analysis. The analysis revealed that most current studies rely on traditional indicators such as remaining power level or charger path optimization, while the integration of Critical Node Awareness, Predictive Energy Modeling, and Priority-based Charging remains limited. The results also highlighted a significant research gap: the absence of a comprehensive framework linking node importance analysis within the network, early power depletion prediction, and priority-based smart charging mechanisms to enhance network reliability. Therefore, this systematic review proposes an integrated conceptual framework based on Topology-aware Critical Node Identification, Predictive Energy Modeling, and Priority-based Wireless Charging to achieve Selective Reliability in WRSNs. The proposed framework aims to improve the continuity of critical nodes and reduce the likelihood of network collapse due to high-impact node failures, making it a promising approach for developing smarter and more reliable power management systems in future wireless sensor networks.</p>','["Wireless Rechargeable Sensor Networks, Wireless Power Transfer, Mobile Charging, Predictive Energy Modeling, Critical Node Identification, Priority-based Charging, Deep Reinforcement Learning, Energy Management, Selective Reliability, Internet of Things (IoT)."]',NULL,24,1,'585-601','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1707','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',32),
(1708,1708,1,'From Accuracy to Deployability: A Layer-Wise Computational Complexity and Edge-Feasibility Analysis of the CNN-BiGRU-Attention Federated IDS for Resource-Constrained IoV ECUs','<p>Federated intrusion detection systems (IDS) for Internet of Vehicles (IoV) CAN-bus networks are typically evaluated on detection accuracy and adversarial robustness alone, leaving their practical deployability on resource-constrained Electronic Control Units (ECUs) unexamined. This paper extends the CNN-BiGRU-Attention federated IDS architecture and the adversarially robust Adaptive Weighted Input (AWI) aggregation mechanism established in prior work [1]–[3] with a dedicated, layer-wise computational complexity and edge-feasibility analysis. Using standard parameter-count and multiply-accumulate (MAC) formulas applied directly to the architecture specification validated in [1] and [3], we derive new layer-by-layer parameter and computational-cost breakdowns, quantify the memory footprint of the full model and of each architectural branch under FP32, FP16, and INT8 precision, and estimate inference latency on representative automotive-grade microcontroller platforms. The full architecture totals 116,097 trainable parameters and approximately 0.93 million floating-point operations (MFLOPs) per inference, occupying 453.5 KB at FP32 precision and 113.4 KB under INT8 quantization — well within the SRAM budget of common automotive-grade microcontrollers. Cross-referencing these computed costs against the accuracy figures independently reported in [1] and [3] shows that the convolutional branch delivers the highest accuracy-per-parameter efficiency of the three architectural components, and that the dual-stage adversarial defense mechanism improves robustness without adding any inference-time parameters, since it operates exclusively at the server-side aggregation stage. These findings corroborate and extend the design choices validated in [1]–[3], providing the deployment-oriented evidence needed to support real-vehicle ECU integration of the proposed framework.</p>','["Edge AI, Model Compression, Computational Complexity, CNN-BiGRU-Attention, Internet of Vehicles (IoV), CAN-Bus Security, Embedded ECU Deployment, Federated Learning."]',NULL,24,1,'602-614','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1708','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',33),
(1710,1710,1,'Decoding Recruitment Quality: Artificial Intelligence’s Role in Shaping Hiring Intentions','<p>Artificial intelligence (AI) is revolutionising recruitment by empowering data-driven decision-making, and automating candidate screening and evaluation. Despite the growing prevalence of AI recruitment systems, scant empirical research has examined how perceived AI benefits affect the quality of recruitment decisions and the intention to adopt AI technologies. This study examines the perceived AI benefits of time efficiency, improved candidate–job matching, reduced bias, cost-effectiveness, and perceived fairness and desirability of AI adoption on recruitment decision quality and intention to recruitment AI. Data were collected via a survey administered to employees who participated in the recruitment process in the Eastern Province of Saudi Arabia. A total of 100 valid samples were analysed using Partial least squares structural equation modelling (PLS-SEM). The findings suggest that improved matching, cost effectiveness, and perceived fairness enhance recruitment decision quality, whereas time efficiency and reduced bias are not significant predictors. Recruitment decision quality emerged as a significant predictor of intention to adopt AI, and acts as a mediator between perceived AI benefits and AI adoption.</p>','["Artificial intelligence", "Recruitment decision quality", "AI adoption", "HR analytics", "Algorithmic recruitment", "Human resource management"]',NULL,24,1,'615-630','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1710','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',34);
<p><strong>Patients</strong> and Methods: In this clinical trial, 135 women who were having abdominal hysterectomy done for noncancerous uterine tumors were part of the randomization process. Each participant received an equal amount of vasopressin, TXA, or a placebo. Approximate blood loss during surgery was the main result. Postoperative blood loss, hemoglobin changes, transfusion needs, operation time, and hospital stay were all considered secondary outcomes.</p>
<p><strong>Results</strong>: There was a significant decrease in intraoperative, postoperative, and total blood loss (p &lt; 0.001) when TXA and vasopressin were administered, as opposed to the control group. It was possible to determine which of the vasopressin-treated groups had a significant reduction in overall blood loss. The two intervention groups showed a significantly reduced decrease in hemoglobin levels compared to the control group (p &lt; 0.001). Blood transfusions were much less common in the groups that received the interventions. Although both therapy groups had shorter hospital stays, there was no discernible difference in the total amount of time spent in the hospital by both group.</p>
<p><strong>Conclusion</strong>: Vasopressin and TXA, when administered topically, significantly diminished the need for transfusions and the degree of perioperative blood loss throughout benign abdominal hysterectomy. There was a slightly higher decrease in total blood loss with vasopressin. Both agents represent safe and effective adjuncts for blood conservation in abdominal hysterectomy.</p>','["Abdominal hysterectomy, blood loss, tranexamic acid, vasopressin."]',NULL,24,1,'671-681','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1715','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',37),
(1764,1764,1,'Artificial Intelligence-Driven Economic Decision Making: A Conceptual Framework and Policy Implications','<p>Modern economies are undergoing a profound digital transformation in which economic decision-making processes are being reshaped under conditions of increasing data intensity, algorithmic complexity, and uncertainty. Although artificial intelligence (AI) has significantly enhanced the accuracy, speed, and analytical capacity of economic decision-making, the existing literature largely addresses decision support systems, predictive models, human-AI interaction, and AI governance as separate research streams. Studies that integrate these components into a comprehensive conceptual architecture for economic decision-making remain limited. This study aims to develop an integrated conceptual framework that explains AI-driven economic decision-making from technical, managerial, and ethical perspectives. Adopting a conceptual synthesis approach, the study systematically reviews and integrates the interdisciplinary literature on economics, decision sciences, artificial intelligence, and public policy. Based on this synthesis, the AI-EDG Framework (Artificial Intelligence-Driven Economic Decision and Governance Framework) is developed. The proposed framework conceptualizes economic decision-making as an integrated system comprising a data ecosystem, AI analytics, human-AI collaborative reasoning, ethical governance, decision implementation, and continuous learning through feedback loops. Building upon this framework, policy implications are systematically examined for the public sector, private enterprises, and the labor market. The findings highlight trustworthy AI governance, explainability, human oversight, and institutional compliance as essential determinants of sustainable AI-driven economic decision-making. By integrating fragmented streams of literature into a unified theoretical framework, the study introduces a novel conceptual model that bridges AI-based decision support systems and ethical governance approaches, providing a testable theoretical foundation for future empirical research and evidence-based policy design.</p>','["Artificial intelligence, economic decision-making, AI-EDG Framework, decision support systems, AI governance, human-AI collaboration"]',NULL,24,1,'682-699','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1764','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',38),
(1765,1765,1,'Adaptive Behavioral Validation for Cloud Security Posture Management with AI-Driven Alert Prioritization','<p>Cloud computing has become a fundamental com-ponent of modern IT infrastructure due to its scalability, flexi-bility, and cost-efficiency. However, as organizations increasingly rely on cloud platforms, security challenges have grown more complex. Misconfigurations in cloud services such as storage, compute instances, and identity management systems can lead to serious vulnerabilities and data breaches. Cloud Security Posture Management (CSPM) tools are commonly used to monitor cloud environments and detect configuration risks, but traditional CSPM solutions rely primarily on static rule-based checks and often generate a large number of security alerts, many of which are false positives.</p>
<p>This paper proposes an Adaptive Behavioral Validation frame-work combined with AI-Driven Alert Prioritization to improve the effectiveness of CSPM systems. The proposed system integrates behavioral analysis with machine learning techniques to analyze the contextual exploitability of detected alerts and classify them based on risk level. Cloud resources including Amazon S3, EC2 instances, Identity and Access Management (IAM), and network configurations are scanned for security misconfigurations. A Random Forest machine learning model is then applied to clas-sify alerts into high-risk, medium-risk, and low-risk categories, enabling security teams to focus on critical vulnerabilities. Exper-imental results demonstrate a 97.96% reduction in false positive alerts (from 196 to 4), while correctly identifying 45 true positive threats. The proposed system significantly reduces alert fatigue, improves cloud security monitoring efficiency, and advances the state of the art in intelligent, context-aware CSPM [1], [4], [6].</p>','["Cloud Security Posture Management (CSPM), Behavioral Validation, Adaptive Threat Detection, False Positive Reduction, Cloud Misconfigurations, AWS Security, AI-Based Alert Prioritization, Random Forest, Security Automation, Cloud Risk Assessment."]',NULL,24,1,'700-710','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1765','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',39),
(1767,1767,1,'The Dark Side of Fast-Food Influencer Marketing: A Systematic Literature Review of Social Media Influence on Generation Z Consumer Behaviour','<p>Social media influencer marketing has emerged as a dominant promotional strategy in the food industry, yet the academic literature on its effects on Generation Z (Gen Z) consumers remains fragmented across disciplines. This review systematically synthesizes existing empirical and theoretical evidence on how social media influencer marketing shapes Gen Z consumer behaviour in the food industry, with a focus on identifying key mechanisms of influence, boundary conditions, and gaps in current knowledge. A systematic literature review following PRISMA 2020 guidelines was conducted across seven academic databases covering the period 2015-2025. After screening 253 initial records and removing duplicates, 20 peer-reviewed studies meeting all inclusion criteria were retained for full synthesis. Three thematic dimensions emerged: (1) consumer attitudes, purchase intentions, and food choices; (2) influencer credibility, trust, and parasocial relationships; and (3) content, platform, and endorser characteristics. Findings consistently indicate that influencer effects on food behaviour are predominantly indirect, mediated by parasocial relationships, trustworthiness, and consumer attitude. Micro-influencers are comparably effective to macro-influencers for informational content, and food type moderates engagement. Platform diversity remains under-researched, with Instagram dominating 70% of included studies. Influencer marketing exerts a significant but context-dependent influence on Gen Z food behaviour. Food brands should prioritise endorser trustworthiness over follower scale. Critical gaps persist around TikTok, male Gen Z consumers, cross-cultural contexts, longitudinal designs, and ethical dimensions of marketing unhealthy food to young audiences.</p>','["Social media influencer marketing", "Generation Z", "food consumer behaviour", "parasocial relationship", "purchase intention", "systematic literature review", "influencer credibility"]',NULL,24,1,'711-729','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1767','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',40),
(1768,1768,1,'Information, Externalities, and the Limits of the Market: A Shannon-Theoretic Reading of the Coase Theorem','<p>This article proposes a formal correspondence between Shannon''s mathematical theory of communication and Coase''s theorem on social cost. By interpreting the vendor as a sender, the buyer as a receiver, the transaction as transmitted information, and externalities as channel noise, we argue that the Coase theorem can be recovered as a limiting case of the Noisy Channel Coding Theorem, obtained when channel capacity is unbounded. The mapping yields two observable consequences. First, property rights operate as channel codes whose redundancy structure determines how efficiently externalities are internalized. This predicts that markets with finer-grained rights should exhibit lower and less persistent externalities than markets where the same physical good is described by a coarser code. Second, transaction costs determine an upper bound on the volume of welfare-relevant exchanges a market can sustain beyond which no institutional refinement can keep externalities internalized, predicting threshold behavior in environmental markets at high volumes of activity. The framework recasts the Pigou-Coase debate as a choice between modifying the source distribution and modifying the channel code and offers a candidate metric for institutional quality that, unlike most welfare measures in environmental economics, is in principle estimable from data on intended and realized transactions.</p>','["Coase theorem", "Shannon entropy", "Externalities", "Property rights", "Transaction costs", "Information theory"]',NULL,24,1,'730-737','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1768','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',41),
(1769,1769,1,'Prohibited Names of Pre-Freudian and Early Psychoanalysis in Eastern Europe (Late 19th – Early 20th Century)','<p>This paper will look at the development and evolution of pre-Freudian and early psychoanalytic ideas in Eastern Europe during the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries, through the work of intellectual figures whose work was later relegated, suppressed, or disregarded even in canonical historiography. The object of the research is to discover these so-called prohibited names, reconstitute their theoretical location, and evaluate their place in the wider genealogy of psychoanalysis. The qualitative historical research and comparative intellectual analysis form the basis of the methodology. The research has relied on secondary databases, archival literature of 1880–1935, early scholarly journals, reprinted monographs, and dissertation documents, including examination of Ukrainian philosophy on psychoanalytic problematics. Hermeneutic textual analysis, structural-comparative approaches, and contextual political analysis were used to trace conceptual similarities and oppression means. The results indicate that psychoanalysis in Eastern Europe was developed before and contemporarily with institutional Freudianism. Models of unconscious processes and symbolic mediation and creative transformation, which predicted prominent psychoanalytic ideas, were expressed through philosophical linguistics, aesthetic theory, psychobiography, and ethno-psychological studies. This ideological censorship was, however, fed upon by the later political regimes, especially the Soviet Marxism-Leninism and nationalist restructuring of Central Europe, as well as historiographical omission. The paper redefines Eastern Europe as not a peripheral recipient of psychoanalysis but rather an active epistemological space in its constructions. More than merely bringing the marginalized thinkers back into the historical record, the study provides a polycentric and critically informed perspective on psychoanalytic historiography.</p>','["psychoanalysis, unconscious, conscious, creative, cognition, language."]',NULL,24,1,'738-753','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1769','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',42),
(1770,1770,1,'AI Recommendation Systems and Consumer Trust: A Conceptual Model of Personalisation, Explainability, and Privacy Risk','<p>Artificial intelligence (AI) based recommendation systems now mediate most consumer discovery journeys in e-commerce, yet the literature linking recommendation quality, personalisation, and explainability to consumer trust and purchase intention remains scattered across disconnected empirical streams. This conceptual paper synthesises theory from recommendation-agent research, trust theory, the Stimulus-Organism-Response paradigm, and explainable-AI scholarship to build a single, internally consistent, testable model, integrating propositions from 92 peer-reviewed sources indexed in Scopus and/or Web of Science, with a deliberate concentration on 2020-2026 literature spanning e-commerce trust research, algorithm aversion/appreciation psychology, hybrid Structural Equation Modelling-Artificial Neural Network (SEM-ANN) methodology, and privacy-calculus research; no primary data or student theses were used. The synthesis yields a six-construct integrative model - AI Recommendation Quality, Personalisation, Consumer Trust, Perceived Value, Perceived Privacy Risk, and Purchase Intention - in which trust operates as the central mediating mechanism, explainability acts as a positive boundary condition, and perceived privacy risk acts as a negative boundary condition on the recommendation-to-trust pathway, alongside a recommended two-stage hybrid SEM-ANN validation procedure. Rather than merely cataloguing prior findings, the paper derives a novel integrative structure, articulates eight falsifiable propositions, and proposes a concrete methodological agenda - including sample sizes, measurement sources, and analysis sequence - for scholars testing the model empirically, particularly in under-studied markets such as India.</p>','["Artificial Intelligence", "Recommendation Systems", "Consumer Trust", "Explainable AI", "Purchase Intention", "Structural Equation Modelling", "Artificial Neural Networks", "Conceptual Model", "E-Commerce"]',NULL,24,1,'754-777','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1770','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',43),
(1771,1771,1,'Beyond Authentication: A Three-Pillar Evidentiary Framework for Deepfake Evidence in Court of law','<p>As Artificial Intelligence—generated deepfakes become forensically indistinguishable from authentic media, existing rules of evidence including the United States Federal Rules of Evidence 1975, the United Kingdom''s Police and Criminal Evidence Act 1984, and India''s Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023 are proving inadequate to ensure the reliability of audiovisual evidence in criminal proceedings. This article conducts a comparative doctrinal analysis of evidentiary authentication frameworks across these three jurisdictions, demonstrating that current standards were designed for an era in which media manipulation left detectable forensic artefacts. We identify a dual epistemic threat: the risk of admitting fabricated deepfake evidence (the "fabrication threat") and the risk of excluding authentic evidence through spurious deepfake claims (the "liar''s dividend"). To address this doctrinal gap, we propose a three-pillar jurisprudential model comprising (i) a structured burden-shifting authentication protocol, (ii) judicial notice of AI generative capability, and (iii) a provenance-first evidentiary doctrine grounded in cryptographic content credentials. The model is evaluated against the constitutional and procedural constraints of each jurisdiction, with particular attention to the Indian evidentiary landscape under the newly enacted Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam. We conclude with concrete legislative and procedural reform recommendations for courts operating in the post-authentication era.</p>','["Deepfakes", "Evidence Law", "Authentication", "Artificial Intelligence", "Admissibility", "Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam", "Federal Rules of Evidence", "Digital Forensics"]',NULL,24,1,'778-808','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1771','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',44),
(1772,1772,1,'Linguistic Strategies of Military Communication in Ukrainian and European Media Discourse','<p>The article is devoted to a comparative analysis of linguistic strategies for the representation of the Russian-Ukrainian war in English-language media discourse. The study is based on the corpus approach and combines the methods of corpus linguistics, critical discourse analysis and quantitative framing analysis. The empirical base is a corpus of 197 English-language news headlines for the period January–May 2026, divided into two subcorporas: Ukrainian and Western European. With the help of the Voyant Tools tool, a frequency analysis of vocabulary was carried out, as well as an annotation of each headline by type of discursive strategy (neutral reporting, threat/framing, diplomatic framing, analytical framing, attribution/hedging, revelation/exclusivity framing) and level of intensity. The results demonstrate the dominance of the two main strategies: threat/framing (44.2%) and neutral reporting (33.5%). An almost deterministic relationship between the level of discursive intensity and the type of strategy was found (Contingency Coefficient = 0.816; p &lt; 0.001). No statistically significant differences were found between the Ukrainian and European subcorporas (p = 0.733), which indicates a discursive convergence of the English-language media space regarding the war in Ukraine. The results obtained confirm the theoretical provisions of strategic narratives, framing theory and the concept of "diffused war", as well as indicate the stabilization of certain linguistic models of representation of long-term conflict.</p>','["Military Communication", "Linguistic Strategies", "Media Discourse", "Framing", "Critical Discourse Analysis", "Corpus Analysis", "Russian-Ukrainian War."]',NULL,24,1,'809-825','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1772','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',45),
(1773,1773,1,'Artificial Intelligence Capabilities, Fintech Innovation, and Islamic Bank Performance: A Cross-Country Panel Analysis of Indonesia, Malaysia, and GCC','<p>This study investigates the impact of fintech innovation, dynamic capabilities, and artificial intelligence (AI) capabilities on the performance of Islamic banks in Indonesia, Malaysia, and the Gulf Cooperation Council (GCC). From 2015 to 2023, a balanced panel dataset of 28 Islamic institutions contains 252 observations. AI sentiment, governance metrics, and digital adoption are normalized; compliance variables are employed to guarantee accuracy through robustness checks and panel linear models. The research reveals that the random effects estimator is the optimal choice for evaluating return on assets (ROA), whereas fixed effects are required for return on equity, non-performing financing, and technical efficiency. In the banking sector, ROA is significantly predicted by digital adoption and dynamic capabilities, with GCC banks outperforming Indonesian banks by approximately 1.96 percentage points. Furthermore, the relationship between AAOIFI compliance and governance quality is complex, with partial alignment demonstrating benefits that are not statistically significant. The study employs a synthetic dataset that is in accordance with benchmarks, emphasizing the necessity of data validation against supervisory data. It also recommends future research on primary data, the impact of generative AI, and modeling approaches. These results collectively provide valuable empirical insights into the ways in which digitalization affects financial performance in the Islamic finance industry, regardless of the regulatory framework or sustainability outcomes.</p>','["Islamic finance", "FinTech", "artificial intelligence", "Islamic banking", "financial performance"]',NULL,24,1,'826-839','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1773','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',46),
(1774,1774,1,'Adoption of Central Bank Digital Currency in India: Task Technology Fit and Viability Perspective from Banking Institutions','<p>India''s first pilot project to adopt a new central bank digital currency (CBDC), the digital rupee by the Reserve Bank of India (RBI), was initiated in 2022. This will be a unique opportunity for India, as it can help it run its business more efficiently and strengthen the security and resilience of the overall payment ecosystem. Also, CBDCs are reshaping global payment ecosystems, yet institutional adoption-readiness remains underexplored in emerging economies. Task-technology fit (TTF) and viability are crucial in determining the adoption of the digital rupee within banking institutions. The objective of this research is to explore the importance of task technology fit and viability aspects of CBDC adoption in India from banker’s perspectives. Task-Technology Fit refers to the degree to which the functional capabilities of digital rupee applications align with the operational requirements of banking tasks. This study applied fit viability model (FVM) to evaluate bankers'' readiness for digital rupee adoption. Data collected from 195 banking managers across 13 Indian banks were analyzed using PLS-SEM (SmartPLS4). The findings showed that IT infrastructure, organizational readiness, and task-technology fit significantly influence adoption performance; economic feasibility is insignificant during the pilot phase deployment. The findings of this study support RBI policy design and banking infrastructure planning.</p>','["Task Technology Fit", "Economic Feasibility", "IT Infrastructure", "Organization Readiness", "Fit-Viability Framework"]',NULL,24,1,'840-863','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1774','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',47),
(1775,1775,1,'Business Models and Organizational Strategies in a Climate of Global Uncertainty and Risk','<p>The purpose of the research is to determine the role of organizational strategies and innovative business models in ensuring the resilience of enterprises in the face of global risks and uncertainty, as well as to substantiate approaches to the integration of strategic management, risk management systems and adaptive mechanisms for the functioning of organizations in a dynamic economic environment. The empirical study was conducted in 2023–2025 based on the analysis of corporate strategic documents and annual reports of international companies operating in the technology, industrial production, digital services and financial services sectors. The sample included 36 companies, for which 84 strategic and management documents were analyzed for 2021–2024. The results showed that 72% of companies actively integrate digital transformation into strategic development models, 64% use business model diversification as a mechanism for reducing risks, and 58% systematically use innovative management practices to increase organizational adaptability. Statistical comparison of enterprises made it possible to identify differences between companies with a high level of strategic flexibility and organizations with traditional strategic models, which confirms the importance of adaptive management mechanisms in ensuring business sustainability. It has been established that the integration of digital technologies, diversified business models and risk management systems forms a comprehensive model of strategic adaptation of enterprises to global economic instability. The results obtained indicate that strategic flexibility is a key factor in the long-term competitiveness of enterprises and can be used as an analytical indicator of the effectiveness of organizational strategies in the modern economic environment..</p>','["Insurance", "Risk Management", "Business Model Transformation", "Strategic Adaptation Of Enterprises", "Uncertainty Management."]',NULL,24,1,'864 - 879','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1775','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',48),
(1777,1777,1,'A Negotiation Case Study: Exploring the Enron Case in India','<p>This study aims to analyze the negotiations between Enron Corporation and the Indian government regarding the Dabhol power project, focusing on the factors that led to the project''s failure. Using qualitative case study methodology, data from government reports, media coverage, and academic literature were analyzed to examine negotiation dynamics, strategies, and stakeholder influences. The findings reveal that political interference, financial constraints, cultural differences, and ethical issues significantly affected outcomes, resulting in Enron’s bankruptcy and financial losses for India. Key recommendations include prioritizing transparency, trust-building, stakeholder engagement, and adapting negotiation strategies to local regulatory and social contexts.</p>','["Negotiation, International Business, Stakeholder Management, Cross-Cultural"]',NULL,24,1,'880-893','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1777','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',49),
(1779,1779,1,'Cybersecurity for the Internet of Things: An AI-Based Adaptive Model and Landscape Analysis — A Systematic Literature','<p>The Taj Trapezium Zone (TTZ), which includes the industrial areas of Firozabad and Mathura, was established to shield the Taj Mahal from the harmful effects of declining air quality. Accurate Air Quality Index (AQI) forecasting is crucial for executing the Graded Response Action Plan (GRAP) of the Commission for Air Quality Management, as it necessitates early warnings of pollution episodes. This study develops machine learning–based AQI forecasting models using real-time monitoring data from the Central Pollution Control Board, which includes 18 environmental parameters collected between February 2023 and October 2025. Four ensemble algorithms—AdaBoost, XGBoost, LightGBM, and CatBoost—were employed with TimeSeriesSplit validation, Optuna-based hyperparameter tuning, and extensive preprocessing. The findings show that the tuned CatBoost model delivered the best predictive performance, with R² values of 0.995 in Firozabad and 0.951 in Mathura. SHAP analysis highlighted site-specific pollutant influences, revealing that both PM2.5 and PM10 significantly impacted AQI variations in Firozabad, while PM10 was the primary factor affecting AQI levels in Mathura. The CPCB breakpoint methodology confirmed that the predicted AQI categories align with observed classifications, ensuring reliable GRAP decision support. By enabling 72-hour advance forecasts, the proposed models offer a practical early-warning framework for proactive pollution control, targeted emission management, and enhanced air quality governance within the TTZ region.</p>','["GRAP, LightGBM, CatBoost, XGBoost, AdaBoost, SHAP analysis, Taj Trapezium Zone, Air Quality Index"]',NULL,24,1,'894-905','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1779','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',50),
(1780,1780,1,'Explainable Machine Learning-Based Decision Support for Diabetes Risk Screening Using Public Health Indicator Data','<p>Diabetes remains a major public-health challenge because many individuals at elevated risk are identified only after avoidable metabolic deterioration or complication pathways have already begun. This study develops and evaluates an explainable machine-learning decision-support framework for diabetes risk screening using the Kaggle Diabetes Health Indicators Dataset derived from the 2015 Behavioral Risk Factor Surveillance System (BRFSS). The framework is designed for screening support and population-level risk stratification rather than clinical diagnosis. It combines data quality assessment, class-imbalance-aware modelling, clinically meaningful feature engineering, stratified splitting, supervised learning, threshold analysis, calibration assessment, subgroup reliability analysis, and explainability using SHAP and LIME. The main modelling protocol focuses on Logistic Regression, Decision Tree, Random Forest, and XGBoost, while additional benchmark outputs from AdaBoost, Naive Bayes, and K-Nearest Neighbors are retained as exploratory comparators from the supplied notebook. The notebook audit showed an apparent high KNN hold-out performance; however, cross-validation and balanced-file sensitivity analysis indicated that Random Forest and XGBoost provided more stable and methodologically defensible behaviour for screening-oriented deployment. SHAP analysis identified general health, comorbidity burden, age, BMI, high blood pressure, sex, income, and high cholesterol as leading model contributors. Threshold analysis showed that lowering the decision threshold increased sensitivity and reduced false negatives, which is important in screening applications. The contribution of this work is an interpretable and reproducible public-health decision-support pipeline that links predictive performance with explanation, calibration, threshold selection, and subgroup reliability.</p>','["Diabetes screening", "Explainable AI", "Clinical decision support", "Public health data", "BRFSS", "Machine learning", "SHAP", "LIME", "XGBoost", "Calibration."]',NULL,24,1,'906-932','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1780','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',51),
(1781,1781,1,'Oryza YOLOCNN-X: Real-time Rice Disease Detection and Classification on Raspberry Pi','<p>Rice is significantly impacted by its disease leading to loss of rice field yield impacting global food security and thereby effective real-time monitoring solutions are required. This proposed study created a deep learning-based framework for rice disease detection. The proposed framework integrates a patch-based disease extraction and classification using deep learning model which can be used for drone-captured imagery. A YOLO-based model for disease patch extraction and texture based-CustomCNN classification for disease identification. The framework is deployed on real-time edge-device i.e raspberry pi. The training of model is done on dataset of image count 5,181 belonging to 9 disease classes captured in Hyderabad and then testing was done on self-captured images from the rice field captured in Palghar, thus demonstrating cross-region validation. The experimental results demonstrate that model training, validation and testing gave an accuracy of 96.67,98.07,94.46 respectively, thus when deployed on edge devices gave 52 ms latency and 18.5 fps.</p>','["rice, disease, customCNN , YOLO11 ,edge device, imagery"]',NULL,24,1,'933-956','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1781','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',52),
(1782,1782,1,'Policy Integration and Implementation Gaps in China’s Dual-Carbon Strategy: A Multi-Dimensional Analysis of Carbon Trading System, New Energy Vehicle Incentives, and Air Quality Policies','<p>Achieving global climate targets relies heavily on the effective execution of China’s dual-carbon strategy. However, translating national decarbonisation ambitions into local outcomes presents profound governance challenges. Ultimately, this study is oriented towards assessing whether these governance arrangements position China to fulfil its 2060 net-zero commitment, as codified in the 2021 White Paper on China’s Policies and Actions to Address Climate Change (State Council Information Office, 2021). This study evaluates the policy integration and implementation dynamics of China’s three core mitigation pillars: the carbon emissions trading system (ETS), new energy vehicle (NEV) incentives, and the Blue Sky Protection Campaign, to assess their contribution to the 2060 net-zero pathway and identify institutional barriers hindering progress towards the dual-carbon goals. The primary evidence base is confined to the 2015–2020 foundational period; references to the July 2021 national ETS launch and the 2060 carbon-neutrality pathway serve as contextual backdrop drawn from retrospective stakeholder interviews and post-period literature, not as primary analytical data. Employing a qualitative methodology, we conducted thematic coding of 156 national policy documents alongside 15 semi-structured stakeholder interviews. The findings reveal that while central policy texts exhibit high integration, operational execution is hindered by systemic misalignments. We identify three distinct implementation phenomena: superficial compliance in the ETS driven by weak price signals, subsidy dependence in the NEV sector that crowds out breakthrough innovation, and administrative rigidity in air quality governance that exacerbates regional development disparities. Viewed through an institutional lens, these implementation gaps are not mere technical or local capacity failures, but rational adaptations to conflicting incentive structures within China’s governance architecture. To enhance mitigation efficacy, the study recommends institutionalizing cross-sectoral coordination and implementing differentiated regional mandates. Ultimately, these insights offer critical lessons for global climate governance by highlighting how institutional constraints mediate mitigation strategies in developing economies navigating the tension between decarbonisation and economic growth.</p>','["dual carbon goals", "policy integration", "carbon trading system", "new energy vehicles", "air quality governance", "China"]',NULL,24,1,'957-987','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1782','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',53),
(1783,1783,1,'A Multi-Model Machine Learning Approach for Predictive Analytics and Intelligent Decision Making','<p>Disease risk can be predicted and clinical decisions can be made early based on wearable sensor data of physiological and behavioral parameters. However, patterns in wearable data of disease development are mostly non-linear, time-dependent, and influenced by individual health variations. Therefore, it is challenging to get reliable results with a single machine learning model for disease risk prediction. This study introduces a multi-model framework using wearable sensor data to improve early disease risk prediction and support timely clinical decisions. First, the data is processed using a data cleaning, normalization, missing value handling, feature extraction and class balancing. Then, a variety of predictors such as Support Vector Machine, Random Forest, Extreme Gradient Boosting and Long Short-Term Memory networks are trained and tested. The prediction probabilities of the single models are integrated in a stacking-approach. An intelligent decision making component classifies users into different disease risk categories (low, moderate, high) based on predicted probabilities and on clinical decision making thresholds. The framework is complemented by Explainable AI to identify the most influential physiological parameters for individual user risk scores. Performance is measured by accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, area under ROC curve, sensitivity, specificity and by calibration. In comparison to individual machine learning models, the proposed multi-model framework enables more robust and interpretable disease risk predictions and supports health monitoring, early clinical interventions as well as evidence-based clinical and healthcare decisions.</p>','["Multi-model learning, predictive analytics, machine learning, ensemble learning, intelligent decision making, explainable artificial intelligence, data-driven systems."]',NULL,24,1,'988-1003','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1783','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',54),
(1784,1784,1,'Child in Daycare, Mother at Work? An Analysis of Female Employability IN the Amazon Region – Rio Branco, Acre','<p>Low female participation in the labor market in contexts of social vulnerability is associated with insufficient maternity support policies. The limited availability of public daycare spots constitutes a structural barrier to women''s labor insertion, especially mothers. This study investigates the effect of daycare availability on female employability in Rio Branco, Acre, by applying questionnaires to mothers and estimating a logit model combined with machine learning techniques. The results indicate that access to daycare, participation in professional training courses, household responsibility, and marital status significantly increase employment chances, while receiving Bolsa Família benefits and absence of childcare support reduce this probability. With 73.75% accuracy, the model guides the formulation of public policies to promote gender equity in the labor market.</p>','["Women. Employability. Daycare Centers. Logit Model. Machine Learning. Rio Branco - AC"]',NULL,24,1,'1004-1022','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1784','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',55),
(1785,1785,1,'AI-Driven Customer Churn Prediction and Retention Intelligence from Behavioral Signals','<p>Customer retention represents a strategic priority for organizations seeking to reduce revenue loss and strengthen long term customer relationships in increasingly competitive markets. This study utilizes two openly available customer analytics datasets drawn from the telecommunications and retail banking sectors to examine engagement patterns, transaction history, service usage, and account activity. Data preprocessing, behavioral feature engineering, exploratory analysis, and class imbalance treatment prepare a combined sample of 17,032 customer records for predictive modeling. Six behavioral indicators, recency, frequency, monetary value, engagement decline, complaint intensity, and inactivity duration, are engineered from the raw fields available in each dataset and capture early signs of customer disengagement across sectors. The study benchmarks five predictive models, Logistic Regression, Random Forest, Gradient Boosting, XGBoost, and LightGBM, for churn classification performance under a unified evaluation protocol. Model interpretability analysis relies on SHAP values to identify the primary behavioral drivers of customer departure, and k-means clustering profiles at-risk customer segments to support targeted retention strategies. Results show that Gradient Boosting achieves the strongest overall balance of performance, reaching an F1 score of 0.55 and an area under the curve of 0.80, while XGBoost records the highest raw accuracy at 74.2 percent, with every benchmarked model falling in a modest 66 to 74 percent accuracy range. Frequency and engagement decline emerge as the dominant behavioral predictors of churn, ahead of monetary value, inactivity duration, complaint intensity, and recency. Clustering analysis identifies two behaviorally distinct customer segments, a large lower-risk Stable Loyalists group and a smaller higher-risk At-Risk Decliners group, each warranting a differentiated retention approach. The novelty of the study lies in combining real, openly accessible cross sector behavioral data with interpretable ensemble modeling and segment level retention profiling within a single transparent analytical framework.</p>','["Customer Churn Prediction", "Explainable Machine Learning (SHAP)", "Behavioral Segmentation"]',NULL,24,1,'1023-1041','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1785','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',56),
(1786,1786,1,'Blockchain-Assisted Trust-Evidence Retrieval for Secure IoT/WSN Routing in Edge-Assisted Communication Systems','<p>Secure routing is a critical requirement for Internet of Things (IoT) and wireless sensor networks (WSNs) that form the sensing layer of future internet and edge-assisted communication systems. Shortest-path routing alone cannot provide reliable delivery when intermediate nodes exhibit selective forwarding, malicious dropping, or inconsistent behavior. This paper presents HBTV-SR, a permissioned blockchain-assisted trust-evidence retrieval method for secure IoT/WSN routing. The proposed design keeps ordinary sensor nodes lightweight by allowing them to generate signed trust observations, while miner/gateway edge nodes verify signatures, freshness, the absence of duplicate hashes, evidence completeness, and trust-value ranges. Storage nodes maintain Merkle-rooted trust records for tamper-evident retrieval. A reproducible packet-level simulator evaluates HBTV-SR against AODV-like routing, TARF-style trust routing, a direct She et al. blockchain trust baseline, and other baselines surveyed in the literature for the same scenario. Across 30 fixed-seed Monte Carlo runs, HBTV-SR achieved 83.12% packet delivery ratio in the representative 100-node, 20% malicious-node scenario, compared with 78.21% for the She et al. baseline. The results indicate that edge-assisted ledger-backed trust retrieval improves secure routing reliability while avoiding excessive blockchain workload on constrained IoT/WSN nodes.</p>','["Blockchain", "Future Internet", "Internet of Things", "edge-assisted secure routing", "wireless sensor networks", "trust management", "permissioned ledger", "malicious node detection."]',NULL,24,1,'1042-1062','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1786','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',57),
(1788,1788,1,'Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning Approaches to Identifying Non-Standard Lexical Units in English and Russian Digital Media: A Comparative Corpus-Based Study','<p>The rapid development of artificial intelligence (AI) and machine learning (ML) has significantly transformed the field of computational linguistics, enabling researchers to analyze increasingly complex linguistic phenomena across multilingual digital environments. Among these phenomena, non-standard lexical units—including slang, internet abbreviations, neologisms, emojis, hybrid lexical forms, and code-mixed expressions—have become a defining feature of communication in English and Russian digital media. Traditional linguistic methods often struggle to identify and classify these dynamic lexical innovations due to their context-dependent nature, rapid evolution, and semantic variability. Consequently, AI-driven natural language processing (NLP) techniques provide promising opportunities for improving the automatic recognition and interpretation of such lexical units.</p>
<p>This study proposes a comparative corpus-based framework integrating artificial intelligence and machine learning techniques to identify non-standard lexical units in English and Russian digital media. The research combines corpus linguistics with advanced NLP models, including transformer-based architectures, to examine lexical innovation across multilingual online communication. The proposed framework aims to improve linguistic annotation, semantic classification, and automatic detection of emerging lexical forms while revealing similarities and differences between English and Russian digital discourse. The findings are expected to contribute to computational linguistics, corpus-based lexical studies, digital discourse analysis, and AI-assisted language processing by providing an effective methodology for multilingual lexical analysis.</p>','["Artificial Intelligence", "Machine Learning", "Natural Language Processing", "Corpus Linguistics", "Digital Media", "English Language", "Russian Language", "Non-standard Lexical Units", "Computational Linguistics", "Lexical Innovation."]',NULL,24,1,'1063-1087','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1788','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',58),
(1790,1790,1,'Federated Deep Learning for Privacy-Preserving Cryptocurrency Fraud Detection Using Heterogeneous Financial Data','<p>Cryptocurrency fraud encompassing pump-and-dump schemes, Ponzi contracts, phishing, ransomware laundering, and exchange manipulation inflicted estimated losses of USD 9.9 billion globally in 2023. Existing centralised fraud detection systems require pooling sensitive transactional data across financial institutions and blockchain analytics firms, posing severe privacy, regulatory, and competitive risks. This paper introduces FedHDL (Federated Heterogeneous Deep Learning), a novel privacy-preserving framework for cryptocurrency fraud detection that enables collaborative model training across five heterogeneous institutional nodes without raw data exchange. FedHDL integrates a Graph Attention Network (GAT) for transaction graph encoding, a Bidirectional LSTM (BiLSTM) for temporal behavioural modelling, and a Transformer-based attention fusion layer to reconcile divergent feature spaces across nodes with non-IID (non-independent and identically distributed) data distributions. Model aggregation employs a novel Reputation-Weighted Federated Averaging (RW-FedAvg) algorithm resistant to Byzantine gradient attacks. Privacy is enforced through the synergistic application of Rényi Differential Privacy (RDP) with a Gaussian mechanism (ε = 2.0, δ = 10⁻⁵) and additive homomorphic encryption of gradient updates. Evaluated on 276.23 million real-world and synthetic cryptocurrency transactions spanning five institutional nodes, FedHDL achieves an F1-score of 94.4%, AUC-ROC of 0.981, and accuracy of 96.4% surpassing the best competing federated baseline (FedProx+LSTM) by 7.7 percentage points in F1 and approaching centralised oracle performance (F1 = 95.1%) within 0.7 percentage points, while preserving strict data locality. Communication overhead is reduced by 89.7% relative to uncompressed FedAvg through gradient sparsification and top-k compression. These results demonstrate that FedHDL constitutes a practical, privacy-compliant, and high-fidelity solution for cross-institutional cryptocurrency fraud detection.</p>','["Federated Learning, Cryptocurrency Fraud Detection, LSTM, Heterogeneous Data, Blockchain Analytics, Financial AI"]',NULL,24,1,'1088-1098','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1790','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',59);
<p>The results of the study show a positive impact on the quality of media research by facilitating researchers’ access to information, improving the accuracy of analysis, and enabling researchers to produce innovative media content. The study also reflects the impact of modern technologies on the variables of media theories, and shows new directions for research that are consistent with technical developments. In the end, the field study emphasizes the importance of developing media theories that are effectively compatible with modern technologies to achieve sustainable development in the field of media research.</p>','["Scientific Variables, Technical developments, media research, quality, media theories, media content."]',NULL,24,1,'1422-1443','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1838','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',79),
(1839,1839,1,'Myriad Effect of Organizational Life: Mitigation Through Entrepreneur’s Strategic Styles, Emotional Contagion and Performance','<p>Theoretical Framework:</p>
<p>The myriad effects of organizational life on entrepreneurial performance in the service industry focusing on the interplay between emotions, strategic styles, and organizational outcomes. Three distinct entrepreneurial emotional profiles were identified: Balanced Emotionality, High Positive Affect, and Emotionally Volatile. These profiles correlate with four strategic styles. The four archetypes of business leaders include innovative risk-takers, analytical planners, adaptive collaborators, and intuitive decision-makers. Entrepreneurs guide the organization by fostering a highly ethical and moral environment and culture in a realistic manner. Entrepreneurs prioritize the well-being of employees to optimize both their satisfaction and the company''s profit.</p>
<p> </p>
<p>Design / Methodology/approach:</p>
<p>The study employed a mixed-methods approach, analyzing data from one hundred and three service industry companies using structural equation modeling, descriptive statistics and correlation analysis.  Employed SmartPLS, Version 4.1.0.0, to evaluate the data. This article examines various emotional effects, strategic approaches, and emotional contagion in organizational contexts to enhance entrepreneurial success in the service sector.</p>
<p>Findings:</p>
<p>The findings conclusively shaped the seminal role that emotional intelligence plays in the performance of entrepreneurs in the services industry. The structure of the flow of emotions top-down, peer-to-peer, and bottom-up determines the organizational climate and performance. High Positive Affect entrepreneurs independently managed enterprises performed better compared to others in the areas of customer satisfaction and employee turnover.</p>
<p>Research, Practical &amp; Social Implications:</p>
<p>The government and MSMEs adopt mental health education and workplace training to mitigate both positive and negative effects, thereby ensuring organizational unity and fulfilling corporate objectives. Some of the measures are Emotional intelligence training, equal representation of men and women at leadership level, and frequent climate surveys. The research offers a clear, systematic conceptualization of emotions, strategies, and organizational performance.</p>','["Emotional Intelligence, Strategic Styles, Emotional Contagion, Organizational Climate, Service Industry Performance."]',NULL,24,1,'1444-1458','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1839','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',80),
(1841,1841,1,'A Hybrid GA-KNN Framework For Cardiovascular Disease Prediction Using Optimized Clinical Feature Selection','<p>Background Study: Background Study: Cardiovascular disease (CVD) causes millions of fatalities each year and places a heavy financial strain on healthcare systems. Better patient outcomes, prompt clinical intervention, and lower healthcare costs all depend on early and precise cardiovascular disease prediction. Through the analysis of massive amounts of clinical data, machine learning algorithms have considerable potential in helping doctors identify diseases.</p>
<p>Problem Statement: High-dimensional clinical datasets, repetitive and irrelevant features, and the difficulty to consistently identify the most discriminative risk factors are common problems for current machine learning-based techniques for cardiovascular disease prediction. These problems limit the robustness and generalizability of prediction models, raise computing costs, and decrease classification accuracy. This is particularly true for distance-based classifiers, such as K-Nearest Neighbor (KNN). Developing an efficient approach that combines accurate classification with suitable clinical feature selection remains a critical research problem for improving early cardiovascular disease prediction and enabling reliable clinical decision-making.</p>
<p>Purpose: In a medical decision support system, the prediction of cardiovascular disease is an important task, as early detection can help in minimizing the risk of mortality, delay in treatment, and cost of healthcare.</p>
<p>Methods: In this study, an optimized hybrid approach of the genetic algorithm and K-nearest neighbor method for cardiovascular disease prediction is proposed. The clinical attributes are selected using the genetic algorithm, and the final classifier is KNN. Four datasets, the Cleveland Processed Heart Dataset, the CRPF Ranchi Clinical Heart Dataset, the Cleveland Hungarian Statlog Dataset, and the Heart Failure Clinical Record Dataset, were used for evaluating the model. Initial experiments were conducted with k-fold values of 5, 10, 15, 20, and 25 folds, and then an optimized 10-fold GA-KNN approach with feature selection, normalization, binary target conversion, and hyperparameter tuning of KNN was executed.</p>
<p>Results: The optimized model achieved accuracies of 78.19%, 75.71%, 92.10%, and 81.98%, respectively, with ROC-AUC values of 0.8612, 0.7603, 0.9665, and 0.8313.</p>
<p>Conclusion: It is demonstrated that optimized GA-KNN can achieve both  feature dimensions for the initial screening of cardiovascular diseases. The proposed GA-KNN framework is simple, interpretable, and computationally efficient for preliminary cardiovascular disease screening.</p>','["Cardiovascular disease prediction, genetic algorithm, K-nearest neighbor, feature selection, medical decision support system"]',NULL,24,1,'1459-1495','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1841','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',81),
(1842,1842,1,'Artificial Intelligence-Driven Workforce Optimization: An Operational Research Framework for Strategic Human Resource Management and Organizational Decision-Making','<p>The increasing adoption of Artificial Intelligence (AI) has transformed strategic human resource management by enabling organizations to predict workforce outcomes more accurately. However, most existing studies focus primarily on employee attrition prediction and provide limited guidance for translating predictive insights into actionable workforce planning decisions. This study proposes an integrated Artificial Intelligence–Operational Research (AI–OR) framework for workforce optimization that combines machine learning, explainable AI, and mathematical optimization to support strategic human resource management and organizational decision-making. Two publicly available employee datasets comprising 16,189 employee records were analyzed using Logistic Regression, Decision Tree, Random Forest, XGBoost, and LightGBM algorithms. Model performance was evaluated using Accuracy, Precision, Recall, F1-score, ROC-AUC, PR-AUC, and Matthews Correlation Coefficient, with Random Forest demonstrating superior predictive performance across both datasets. SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) were employed to identify the key determinants of employee attrition, enhancing model transparency and interpretability. Subsequently, a Mixed Integer Linear Programming (MILP) model was developed to optimize workforce intervention strategies under resource constraints. The optimization results demonstrated that targeted interventions directed toward a relatively small proportion of high-risk employees could mitigate a substantial share of organizational attrition risk while efficiently utilizing limited organizational resources. Sensitivity analysis further confirmed the robustness of the proposed framework under varying intervention capacities and effectiveness scenarios.</p>','["Artificial Intelligence, Workforce Optimization, Human Resource Analytics, Operational Research, Strategic Workforce Planning"]',NULL,24,1,'1496-1509','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1842','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',82),
(1846,1846,1,'Backbone-Preserving Physics-Guided Digital Twin Modelling for Reliable State and Molecular-Quality Forecasting in Batch Polymerization','<p>Batch polymerization is a highly nonlinear process and is very sensitive to temperature, thus it is important to be able to reliably forecast the reactor temperature, conversion, and molecular-quality indicators for process monitoring. Mechanistic models can be physically interpreted, but are subject to parameter uncertainty, actuator dynamics, and model–plant mismatch, while purely data-driven models can violate physical constraints and are prone to generalization failure when faults and/or distribution shift occur. A physics-guided digital twin for batch styrene polymerization was developed, preserving the backbone of the reactor, based on a verified 12-state kinetic–thermal model of the reactor and a high-fidelity virtual plant. This resulted in 60 full datasets and 84,873 observations of normal, faulty, combined fault and out of distribution. Leakage-safe batch partitions were tested for five predictive baselines and a bounded physics-guided residual model. Measured-state correction was able to reduce TEST_ID NRMSE by 21.49% at the 60 s horizon compared to the fixed mechanistic model. Learned residual correction reduced the TEST_OOD NRMSE by 19.79% at 300 s. Further error reduction in the mechanistic-model error was achieved by using physically realized actuator trajectories; the error reduction ranged from 7.10% to 19.16% across the operating regimes. All three physics guided neural candidates, however, degraded validation NRMSE by 3.38-4.40% and did not pass the physics-residual acceptance gate and so the verified mechanistic backbone was kept. The chosen trajectories were mass closed, initial condition, positive, temperature, conversion and molecular-quality constrained. The results show that trustworthy digital twins should be open to neural corrections, and not presume that more complex models will always lead to better forecasting.</p>','["Batch polymerization", "digital twin", "physics-guided learning", "backbone preservation."]',NULL,24,1,'1510-1533','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1846','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',83),
(1847,1847,1,'Student Engagement and Ethical Awareness in Generative Artificial Intelligence Use Among University Students','<p>This study examined university students’ use of generative artificial intelligence (AI) in relation to engagement, confidence, ethical awareness, and perceived academic value. A descriptive-quantitative, cross-sectional design was employed, involving 380 students from 11 colleges of a Philippine state university. Data were collected using a structured questionnaire and analyzed using descriptive statistics. Students reported using AI-assisted applications primarily for research, summarization, writing, and editing, with ChatGPT, QuillBot, and Grammarly among the most frequently reported tools. Confidence in independent AI use was concentrated in the moderate and lower categories, while perceptions of AI-supported engagement were neutral overall. Ethical awareness was uneven: students showed stronger recognition of familiar concerns involving copyright and unethical academic use but greater uncertainty regarding the broader meaning of ethical AI use in education. Taken together, the findings indicate that access to and use of AI tools do not necessarily develop alongside confidence, meaningful engagement, and ethical understanding. The study highlights the need for AI literacy initiatives that combine practical skills with critical evaluation, appropriate disclosure, verification of generated information, and student accountability in AI-assisted academic work.</p>','["QuillBot", "verification of generated information , overreliance"]',NULL,24,1,'1534-1558','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1847','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',84);
<p>This study addresses the research gap in designing an integrated, AI-driven clinical trial intelligence framework capable of time-aware data harmonization, proactive anomaly detection, and context-aware decision support. We propose a unified architecture that combines multimodal data ingestion, advanced analytics, and Agentic AI to dynamic risk adoption trial operations and enhance data quality oversight. The proposed methodology leverages artificial intelligence-based data quality assessment, predictive operational analytics, and AI agents to generate actionable insights and recommendations throughout the trial lifecycle. We utilized historical clinical trial records from the Aggregate Analysis of ClinicalTrials.gov (AACT) database, including enrolment ratio, recruitment status, study phase, sponsor category, protocol amendment frequency, study duration, and site characteristics, to construct a multi-dimensional trial risk representation. The proposed framework has integrated with multiple agents such as Risk Assessment Agent, Trial Management Agent, Prioritization Agent, and Recommendation Agent collectively used for risk identification and intervention planning.  </p>
<p>The proposed Agentic-AI framework mechanisms to enhance early risk identification and operational intelligence. The framework was validated using multi-fold rolling time-series cross-validation and trained on historical observations to prevent temporal leakage.  The performance was evaluated using several metrics, and the proposed framework was further compared across Statistical Process Control (SPC), Logistic Regression, and Gradient Boosting-based monitoring approaches.</p>
<p>The Experimental results show that the proposed framework illustrated optimised performance against baseline models across various predictive and operational metrics achieving accuracy (≈88), F1-score (≈88.5), and AU-ROC (≈96.1) with enhanced risk prioritisation accuracy, trial delay detection capability, and early risk identification performance. The proposed work contributes an adaptive and scalable framework for intelligent clinical trial monitoring by integrating predictive analytics with agentic decision mechanisms, time-aware validation strategies, and an intelligent fusion layer. The study advances existing clinical trial intelligence systems from reactive and static monitoring strategies towards stable, operationally optimised AI-assisted trial management.</p>','["Agentic AI, Risk Monitoring, AACT Intelligent Framework, Explainable AI, Predictive Analytics"]',NULL,24,1,'1669-1690','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1855','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',91),
(1856,1856,1,'Cluster-Based Data Traffic Monitoring and Trusted Neighbors Prediction for Secure Data Transmission in Wireless Sensor Network','<p>Wireless Sensor Networks (WSNs) are now vital components in environmental monitoring systems, healthcare, industrial automation, and military missions. Unfortunately, the resource-limited features of sensor nodes, along with a dynamic network environment and malicious attacks, lead to a number of difficulties associated with secure and energy-efficient data transmission. Most of the trust-based routing schemes have low efficiency in terms of ineffective cluster formation, high energy utilization, increased communication delay, and inaccurate malicious node detection. This, in turn, leads to poor network performance. Therefore, the present research develops an Archer Fish Hopfield Forecasting System (AFHFS) for cluster-based data traffic monitoring and prediction of trusted neighbors in WSNs. First, the Archer Fish Optimization (AHO) approach is utilized to find out optimal clusters and cluster formation is enabled. After that, network traffic is constantly monitored to analyze node behavior and determine trustworthiness based on a number of communication factors. Finally, the obtained trust information is fed to the Hopfield Neural Network (HNN) for the classification of neighboring nodes in terms of trustworthiness. Experimental analysis has revealed that the suggested AFHFS performs better. The results have shown that the suggested framework provides improved network reliability, energy efficiency, and secure communication in WSN.</p>','["Wireless sensor network, archer fish, Hopfield, cluster head, trust node, secure transmission."]',NULL,24,1,'1691-1712','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1856','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',92),
(1858,1858,1,'Use of Problem-Based Learning as an Instructional Strategy in Online Learning','<p>This present study aims to elaborate on the use of the problem-based method as an instructional strategy to produce an engaging online program that can facilitate the student’s learning achievement. Since the students must interact actively with lecturers, other students, and course content, it is necessary that online learning has to involve the use of proper instructional strategy. Contextual practice and learning feedback must be applied to facilitate students’ learning process. This study applied a research and development model of Borg, Gall, and Gall (2007) that adopts the systematic design of instruction of Dick, Carey, and Carey (2005). The online learning course on academic writing was designed and developed by implementing the problem-based method as an instructional strategy. The study involved three systematic and systemic phases of research and development that include: the design phase, development phase, and evaluation phase. This present study involved a subject matter expert, an instructional designer, and a computer programmer. Besides, 36 online learning students participated as respondents in the study. A formative evaluation process was employed to try out and revise the program to ensure that the developed program was used by the intended target audience effectively. At the end of the study, the developed program was evaluated by conducting pre and post-test sessions to get information regarding the impact of the program on students’ learning achievement. The result of the study indicated that implementing a problem-based learning approach significantly impacted students’ learning achievement in academic writing skills.</p>','["problem-based learning, instructional strategy, online learning."]',NULL,24,1,'1713-1721','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1858','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',93),
(1859,1859,1,'Impact of Vitamin D Supplementation on Sinonasal Mucosal Integrity: Modulation of Inflammatory Cytokines and Matrix Metalloproteinases','<p>Background: Chronic rhinosinusitis (CRS) involves persistent sinonasal inflammation and tissue remodeling. Vitamin D is an essential micronutrient with potent immunomodulatory properties; however, its specific impact on molecular markers of remodeling and inflammation in uncontrolled CRS is not fully established.</p>
<p>Aim: To evaluate the efficacy of oral Vitamin D as an adjuvant therapy in patients with uncontrolled CRS and to investigate its effect on MMP-9 and TNF-$\\alpha$ expression.</p>
<p> Method: Forty patients with uncontrolled CRS (per EPOS criteria and CT findings) and OH-VitD deficiency were randomized into two groups: Group A received oral OH-VitD (50,000 IU weekly) via a gradual replenishment regimen for 3–6 months, and Group B received a placebo (5 mL distilled water) for the same duration. Twenty healthy subjects served as controls. Sinus mucosal specimens were examined using hematoxylin and eosin staining, immunohistochemistry for MMP-9, and real-time PCR for inflammatory cytokines.</p>
<p> Results: OH-VitD supplementation in Group A resulted in significant improvements in EPOS scores and CT findings compared to Group B. Histopathology revealed nearly normal nasal mucosa with intact respiratory epithelium in Group A, whereas Group B showed epithelial damage. Vitamin D-treated patients and healthy controls demonstrated significantly lower MMP-9 expression and TNFα levels compared to the placebo group.</p>
<p> Conclusion: Vitamin D supplementation may serve as a promising adjuvant therapy for CRS by modulating MMP-9 and TNFα pathways, thereby reducing chronic inflammation and tissue remodeling.</p>','["chronic rhinosinusitis, vitamin D, MMP-9, EPOS, nasal mucosa."]',NULL,24,1,'1722-1737','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1859','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',94),
(1867,1867,1,'Victor Malarek’s The Johns: Sex for Sale, the Men Who Buy it An Analysis in the light of Sigmund Freud’s Psychoanalytic Theory','<p>This article examines the portrayal of female trafficking in Victor Malarek’s The Johns: Sex for Sale and the Men Who Buy It through the lens of Freud’s psychoanalytic theory. It explores the psychological effects of sexual exploitation on female victims, focusing on how patriarchal domination and trafficking destroy personal identity and reduce women to objects of exploitation. Using a qualitative content analysis, the study applies Freudian concepts, including the id, ego, superego, repression, anxiety, and defense mechanisms, to analyze the characters’ psychological conflicts and behavioral responses. The findings reveal that trafficking inflicts profound psychological as well as physical harm, leading to repression, fear, denial, and identity fragmentation. Despite these traumatic experiences, some characters demonstrate resilience and a desire for freedom and self-recovery. The study concludes that Freud’s psychoanalytic theory provides an effective framework for understanding the hidden psychological dimensions of trafficking victims and highlights the human and psychological consequences of sexual exploitation within patriarchal power structures.</p>','["Victor Malarek, Sigmund Freud, Psychoanalytic Theory, Trafficking, Psychological Trauma."]',NULL,24,1,'1738 - 1747','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1867','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',95),
(1868,1868,1,'Bridging Visual and Textual Cues: Cross-Attention Fusion for Multi-Modal Sentiment Analysis','<p>Uni-modal studies on sentiment analysis (SA) has made significant strides, emotions in the real world, which are typically multi-modal, encompassing audio, images, video and other contents more than text. The several modes contribute to mutual development. The accuracy of sentiment evaluation will be enhanced even more if it is possible to mine the connections between different modalities. This work presents a hybrid Deep Auto-Encoding with Convolutional Neural Networks (DAE+CNN), a multi-modal technique based on cross-attention based multi-modal fusion model for text and visuals. Text features are first extracted using the pre-training model; text context features are then extracted CNN; image characteristics are extracted using the network model; and specific emotion-related areas in images are extracted using DAE+CNN. The retrieved characteristics of the text and image are then fused using multi-modal cross-attention, and the output is classified to ascertain the emotional polarity. The model presented in this research outperforms the baseline model in the comparison study of the Fashion dataset and Deep Fashion datasets with accuracy of 86.5 % and 85.5% and F1-scores of 75.3% and 76.7%, respectively,. Furthermore, authors carried out ablation studies, which verified that multi-modal fusion sentiment evaluation outperforms single-modal sentiment analysis.</p>','["prompt engineering, deep learning, feature learning, multi-modal analysis, prediction"]',NULL,24,1,'1748 - 1764','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1868','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',96),
(1869,1869,1,'The Role of Financial Structure in Enhancing Financial Performance According to the DuPont Model','<p>This study aims to examine the impact of the financial structure on financial performance using the five-component DuPont model for industrial companies listed on the Iraq Stock Exchange, with the Bursa of Amman serving as a comparative case. The research population consists of industrial joint-stock companies listed on the Iraq Stock Exchange and the Bursa of Amman. The study sample includes ten (10) Iraqi industrial companies and ten (10) Jordanian industrial companies drawn from the population. The research relies on financial data for panel time series using EViews 13 as the statistical analysis tool. For descriptive purposes, the study employs measures of central tendency and dispersion—specifically the arithmetic mean and standard deviation—to describe the study variables. The findings indicate the presence of statistically significant effects of financial structure indicators across various measures of financial performance. In particular, short-term debt, long-term debt, equity, and the debt-to-equity ratio contribute to explaining variations in operating profit margin, asset utilization efficiency, financial leverage, tax burden, and interest burden. The results further show that increases in short-term debt negatively affect operating profitability. Conversely, long-term debt and equity contribute to improving financial performance and enhancing stability. Additionally, the study reveals that all financial structure indicators positively affect asset turnover efficiency, with long-term debt demonstrating the strongest influence in this regard. The findings also indicate that greater reliance on debt increases financial leverage, tax burden, and interest burden. In contrast, higher equity reduces financial leverage, increases tax burden, and decreases interest burden. Based on these results, the study recommends achieving a balanced financial structure, reducing excessive reliance on short-term debt, and promoting the use of long-term debt in a deliberate and well-planned manner, alongside improving asset management efficiency. The study also calls for strengthening equity financing, developing debt management policies, and periodically reviewing the funding mix to control costs and financial risks. Finally, the recommendations emphasize the importance of strengthening internal corporate policies and improving firms’ efficiency in utilizing resources to ensure sustainability and maximize market value.</p>','["Financial Structure- Financial Performance - DuPont Model."]',NULL,24,1,'1765 - 1786','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1869','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',97),
(1870,1870,1,'The Impact of Sustainable Development Concepts on Urban Structure: A Comparative Analysis of Global Urban Experiences)','<p>Background: The urban structure of cities plays a vital role in determining the paths and directions of urban development. With the accelerating pace of contemporary urban sprawl, sustainability has become an urgent necessity to achieve a balance between economic growth, social justice, and environmental protection. Over the past fifty years, several planning concepts—such as Transit-Oriented Development (TOD), neo-urbanization, and smart growth—have emerged with the aim of reducing reliance on private vehicles and transforming cities into human-centered environments. However, a gap remains in evaluating the urban outcomes of these concepts on a global comparative scale.</p>
<p>Objective: This study aims to research and apply the concepts of sustainable urban development and determine their structural, functional, and visual impact on the urban structure and fabric of contemporary cities.</p>
<p>Methodology: The study employs dual research methodology. First, it includes a literature review of the theories and approaches related to forms of sustainable cities (compact cities, eco-cities, neo-traditional development, and urban sprawl control). Secondly, it includes a comparative inductive analysis of five leading global urban experiences: Barcelona (Spain), Amsterdam (Netherlands), Curitiba (Brazil), Kuala Lumpur (Malaysia), and Melbourne (Australia), to identify urban threats and the planning policies implemented in each.</p>
<p>Results: The results showed that successful sustainable urban transformation is fundamentally based on shifting the design philosophy from car-centric infrastructure to human-centered spaces. The comparative analysis demonstrated that integrating smart and sustainable public transportation systems (such as Bus Rapid Transit and tram networks) with mixed land uses directly contributes to containing unplanned sprawl and increasing green spaces and pedestrian walkways. Conversely, the research revealed significant challenges to this implementation, most notably: the high financial costs associated with establishing and maintaining sustainable infrastructure, the continuous need for data updates, and the extensive reliance on government spending.</p>
<p>Conclusion: The study concludes that urban sustainability is determined by the complex interaction of three key elements: land, population, and urban activity. To ensure quality of life, urban administrations must adopt integrated, multidisciplinary planning frameworks that balance natural and built elements, achieving high architectural and environmental standards that support the health and well-being of residents.</p>','["Sustainable urban development", "urban structure", "human-centered design", "comparative urbanism", "spatial transformations", "smart growth."]',NULL,24,1,'1787 - 1806','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1870','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',98),
(1896,1896,1,'Digital Transformation and Sustainable Innovation: Emerging Trends in Engineering, Technology, and Management','<p>In recent years increasing concern about climate change, resource depletion, environmental degradation, industrial competitiveness etc. has made sustainability one of the most important priorities in engineering, technology and management. Technology has evolved from Industry 4.0 to Industry 5.0, changing the way engineering and management is done: from traditional to intelligent and sustainable systems, with the support of many new technology solutions, such as Artificial Intelligence (AI), Internet of Things (IoT), Industry 5.0, blockchain, digital twins, cloud computing and big data analytics. Such technologies enable the efficient use of resources, lower carbon footprint, increase manufacturing productivity and help in taking decisions with environmental responsibility. At the same time, sustainable organizational growth has been put at the heart of management concepts like Environmental, Social and Governance (ESG) and the principles of the green supply chain management, lean manufacturing and circular economy. In this research paper, the recent advances of engineering, technology and management are discussed to understand emerging trends in sustainable innovation. It also discusses industrial applications, implementation issues, future research directions and strategic advice for organizations striving for sustainable. The study suggests that the effective implementation of digital technologies in the context of sustainable engineering practices and responsible management strategies will be crucial for realizing the United Nations Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) and sustainable industrial resilience, at least for the future.</p>','["Sustainable Innovation, Engineering, Industry 5.0, Artificial Intelligence, Circular Economy, Digital Transformation, ESG, Green Manufacturing, Smart Industry."]',NULL,24,1,'1807 - 1821','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1896','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',99),
(1897,1897,1,'Artificial Intelligence–Driven Strategic Decision Frameworks for Engineering and Technology Enterprises','<p>The concept of Artificial Intelligence (AI) is rapidly changing the way strategic decision-making processes in engineering and technology-driven enterprises are conducted, by facilitating data-driven insights and predictive analysis. This paper suggests an artificial intelligence-based strategic decision model with the Random Forest model, which is supported by statistical validation methods, such as ANOVA, correlation analysis, regression analysis, cross-validation, and feature importance analysis. The findings indicate that the model will have a high predictive accuracy and reliability, with ANOVA showing statistically significant differences among models (p &lt; 0.05). Both correlation and regression results show that the risk level and technological uncertainty have a negative effect on the results of decision making, and team experience has the most significant positive impact. The results of cross-validation also confirm the stability and generalizability of the model to various data subsets. The feature importance analysis identifies the most important variables that determine the strategic decisions. The suggested framework will improve the quality of decisions made, facilitate risk management, and enhance resource allocation. The current study is an addition to AI-based decision systems that incorporates predictive modeling in conjunction with statistical validation of enterprise applications.</p>','["Artificial Intelligence, Strategic Decision-Making, Random Forest, Machine Learning, ANOVA, Regression Analysis, Engineering Management."]',NULL,24,1,'1822 - 1830','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1897','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',100),
(1898,1898,1,'Intelligent AI Systems and Advanced Machine Learning: Recent Advances and Real-World Applications','<p>AI and Machine Learning (ML) are powerful and rapidly evolving technologies, reshaping intelligent decision-making, automation, and data-driven problem-solving in various industries. In recent years, the potential of intelligent systems to manage complex data, to learn adaptive patterns, and to assist in autonomous decision-making has been greatly improved by the emergence of new technologies, such as Deep Learning, Transformer-based Models, Generative AI, Large Language Models (LLMs), Explainable AI (XAI), Federated Learning, Edge AI, and Digital Twin. These advancements have empowered the healthcare, manufacturing, agriculture, finance, transportation, education, cybersecurity, and smart city sectors with enhanced efficiency, productivity, and service quality, driving faster AI adoption across these industries. The innovations have contributed to improved efficiency, productivity, and service quality, leading to increased adoption of AI across the healthcare, manufacturing, agriculture, finance, transportation, education, cybersecurity, and smart city sectors. The fundamentals of intelligent AI systems, key learning paradigms, notable technological advances, and applications are discussed in this chapter, providing a comprehensive review of intelligent AI systems and advanced ML. It also explores the potential of AI to solve real-world problems and discusses some of the critical challenges associated with data privacy, model interpretability, computational complexity, algorithmic bias, and ethical considerations. Finally, the chapter proposes new research directions towards the development of trustworthy, explainable, sustainable, and human-centric AI systems. This review offers a brief overview of current research progress and prospects on the development of intelligent AI systems and advanced machine learning, which will facilitate the future generation of trustworthy and ethical AI-based solutions.</p>','["Artificial Intelligence, Machine Learning, Deep Learning, Intelligent AI Systems, Generative AI, Large Language Models, Explainable AI, Federated Learning, Smart Applications."]',NULL,24,1,'1831 - 1845','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1898','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',101),
(1901,1901,1,'Smart Healthcare, Digital Technologies, and Sustainable Healthcare Systems: Advances and Future Directions','<p><strong>Background:</strong> The rapid advancement of smart healthcare technologies, including artificial intelligence (AI), telemedicine, wearable devices, and electronic health records (EHRs has transformed healthcare delivery worldwide. The successful implementation of sustainable digital healthcare systems depends on the awareness, acceptance, and readiness of healthcare professionals to adopt these emerging technologies.<br/><strong>Objective:</strong> This study aimed to assess the awareness, adoption, perceptions, barriers, and readiness of healthcare professionals toward smart healthcare technologies and sustainable digital healthcare systems in India.<br/><strong>Methods:</strong> A multicenter cross-sectional survey was conducted among 500 healthcare professionals, including physicians, pharmacists, nurses, physiotherapists, and healthcare students. Data were collected using a validated self-administered questionnaire comprising demographic characteristics, awareness, technology adoption, perceptions, barriers, and readiness domains. Statistical analyses were performed using SPSS version 26, employing descriptive statistics, Chi-square tests, ANOVA, Pearson correlation, and logistic regression analysis.<br/><strong>Results:</strong> Among the 500 participants, awareness of telemedicine, AI, and EHRs was reported by 94%, 88%, and 90% of respondents, respectively. The adoption rates of telemedicine, EHRs, and clinical mobile applications were 74%, 82%, and 85%, respectively. Approximately 89% of participants believed that smart healthcare technologies improve patient outcomes, while 86% reported enhanced healthcare efficiency. Major barriers identified included lack of training (84%), data privacy concerns (79%), and inadequate infrastructure (70%). Furthermore, 91% of participants expressed willingness to receive training in AI-based healthcare systems, and 93% considered AI to be the future of healthcare delivery.<br/><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Healthcare professionals demonstrated high awareness and positive attitudes toward smart healthcare technologies and sustainable healthcare systems. However, significant barriers related to training, infrastructure, and data security remain. Strategic investments in education, digital infrastructure, and policy development are essential to facilitate the successful implementation of smart and sustainable healthcare systems.</p>','["Smart healthcare, artificial intelligence, digital health, telemedicine, sustainable healthcare systems, healthcare professionals, healthcare technology."]',NULL,24,1,'1846 - 1854','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1901','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',102),
(1902,1902,1,'An Explainable AI-Driven Framework for Integrating Diverse Health Data to Enhance Predictive Accuracy and Clinical Interpretability','<p>The potential benefits of artificial intelligence (AI) in healthcare cannot be overstated, and there is a lot of potential to ensure that patients can receive optimal care through early disease detection, personalized treatment options, and more. Nonetheless, the used advanced AI models have remained largely hindered by their black-box nature, which is why they are usually called the black-box problem. Such a lack of transparency undermines trust amongst clinicians and regulatory agencies so as to restrict the effective utilization of such powerful tools practically. Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) appears as a decisive way out in such an essential challenge. Increasing clarity and explanation of complex machine learning models have the direct benefits of fostering more trust, as well as enhancing diagnostic accuracy and the successful overall results of patients. The effectiveness with which XAI can provide transparent and decipherable information toward AI-derived predictions is not only a technical contribution, but a necessary match with demands of ethical and clinical concerns in an area where decisions have significant implications. This means that the XAI capacity will become a rising need in a regulatory and ethical Trias handled and measured in an environment where the outcomes of decisions have great implications on the outcomes of patients. This report is an account of a framework through XAI to incorporate various health data sources such as electronic health records, medical imaging, laboratory reports, and wearable sensor information, which can be integrated in the context of achieving higher predictive performance in disease prediction and treatment stratification, as well as decision-making transparency.</p>','["Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI)", "Healthcare Analytics", "Clinical Decision Support", "Transparency and Trust", "Disease Prediction", "Personalized Medicine", "Medical Data Integration", "Ethical and Regulatory AI"]',NULL,24,1,'1855 - 1880','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1902','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',103),
(1903,1903,1,'SugarNet: A Training-Free Multi-Method Consensus Algorithm for Sugarcane Disease Severity Classification','<p>One of the major requirements for automated sugarcane disease severity assessment is need of robust method capable of handling diverse stages of symptoms and provide reliable confidence estimates for field deployment. A common and most frequent constraint in agricultural research is the unavailability of ground truth severity labels. To address these challenges, this paper presents SugarNet, a training-free multi-method consensus algorithm for sugarcane disease severity classification. The algorithm integrates three complementary computational approaches: (1) HSV color thresholding with disease-specific calibration, (2) ResNet50 feature extraction with six augmentation types and consensus scoring, and (3) self-supervised learning feature representation. A confidence-weighted ensemble dynamically combines these methods with weights of 0.30, 0.50, and 0.20 respectively. Evaluated on 1,998 sugarcane leaf images across four disease classes (RedRot, Rust, Mosaic, Yellow), SugarNet achieves a method agreement score of 0.792 and average consensus confidence of 0.832. The system produces high-confidence decisions (≥0.8) for 77.3% of test cases, demonstrating robust internal consistency. Statistical validation confirms significant differences between individual methods (Friedman test, χ2= 1370.20, p &lt; 0.001), justifying the ensemble architecture. The training-free nature and CPU-only inference enable immediate deployment for emerging disease outbreaks without requiring labeled training data.</p>','["Sugarcane disease classification", "Computer vision, Ensemble learning", "Confidence estimation", "Selfsupervised learning", "Training-free method."]',NULL,24,1,'1881 - 1912','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1903','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',104),
(1904,1904,1,'A Unified Discrete Mathematical Framework for Validation and Feature Attribution of Trust in Hybrid AI-Driven Network Architectures','<p>This paper introduces a discrete mathematical framework that renders the trustworthiness of automated nodes in hybrid AI-driven networks both cryptographically verifiable and mathematically provable. The framework is built on a bounded lattice of admissible node states, normalized feature attributions, and trust valuations, enabling the definition of performance metrics and composite trust functionals. Formal proofs establish boundedness, monotonicity, and stability, ensuring that node rankings form a well-defined total preorder resilient to input perturbations. Cryptographic validation is achieved through a SHA 256 attestation pipeline, providing digest generation, hash chaining, and tamper-resistance under collision-resistance assumptions. Interpretability is integrated via Shapley-value feature attribution, formulated as an additive set function on the Boolean lattice of feature coalitions, linking explainable AI directly to discrete algebra. A hybrid BiLSTM–BiGRU sequence model with attention serves as the predictive engine, improving forecasting accuracy by 2–4% over baselines, with statistical significance confirmed by Wilcoxon signed-rank testing. The contribution is a unified, mathematically rigorous pipeline that combines discrete trust modeling, cryptographic attestation, and explainable AI, supported by complete theorems and proofs for secure, interpretable, and adaptive network management.</p>','["Discrete Mathematical Modeling", "Cryptographic Attestation", "Trust Frameworks", "Applied Discrete Algebra", "Partially Ordered Sets and Lattices", "Hash-based Verification", "Information Security", "Feature Attribution."]',NULL,24,1,'1913 - 1931','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1904','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',105),
(1905,1905,1,'Deep Learning-Based Segmentation, Classification, and Explainable Diagnosis of Breast Cancer Lesions','<p>Breast cancer remains one of the most prevalent malignancies affecting women worldwide, and early, accurate detection through medical imaging is central to improving survival outcomes. This paper presents an end-to-end computer-aided diagnosis (CAD) framework that performs lesion segmentation, region-of-interest (ROI) extraction, tumor stage estimation, benign/malignant classification, and visual explainability across two complementary imaging modalities: digital mammography, represented by the Curated Breast Imaging Subset of the Digital Database for Screening Mammography (CBIS-DDSM), and breast ultrasound, represented by the Breast Ultrasound Images (BUSI) dataset. For each modality, a modality-specific U-Net architecture was trained to generate binary lesion masks, from which tumor coverage percentage and a rule-based disease stage were derived. Three classifier backbones were benchmarked per modality — ResNet50, VGG16, and a custom convolutional neural network (CNN) for CBIS-DDSM (benign vs. malignant), and ResNet50, EfficientNet-B0, and a custom CNN for BUSI (benign vs. malignant vs. normal) — with Gradient-weighted Class Activation Mapping (Grad-CAM) applied to the strongest classifiers for interpretability. The trained pipelines were deployed in a Django-based web application allowing end users to upload an image and receive segmentation, ROI, stage, classification, confidence, and Grad-CAM outputs in real time. Results show a pronounced modality gap: the U-Net segmentation network achieved a validation Dice coefficient of 0.462 on BUSI versus 0.247 on CBIS-DDSM, and the best classifier reached 84.07% test accuracy on BUSI (ResNet50) compared with 61.86% on CBIS-DDSM (ResNet50), with markedly lower malignant-class recall on mammography (37%) than on ultrasound (76%). These findings quantify how imaging modality, lesion conspicuity, and dataset scale materially affect both segmentation and diagnostic performance, and they underscore the necessity of transparent, modality-aware reporting — including recall/sensitivity for the malignant class, not just aggregate accuracy — when evaluating deep learning systems intended to support breast cancer screening.</p>','["breast cancer detection", "CBIS-DDSM", "BUSI", "U-Net segmentation", "ResNet50", "VGG16", "EfficientNet", "Grad-CAM", "explainable AI", "computer-aided diagnosis"]',NULL,24,1,'1932 - 1950','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1905','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',106),
(1906,1906,1,'The Impact of Employee Empowerment-Based Dynamic Capabilities on the Sustainable Performance of SMEs in Shanghai, China','<p>Small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs) increasingly depend on employee initiative and operational execution to maintain sustainable performance under conditions of resource scarcity, digital transformation, and market uncertainty. This study examines the direct effect of employee empowerment-based dynamic capabilities (EEBDC) on SME sustainable performance and the moderating role of operational capability. EEBDC is conceptualised as an empowerment-centred organisational capability through which employees’ meaning, competence, autonomy, participation, and perceived impact support organisational adaptation and improvements in work. A quantitative cross-sectional survey was conducted among managers and employees from 50 SMEs in Shanghai, China. Of 400 questionnaires distributed, 384 valid responses were retained. Data were analysed using SPSS and AMOS, including reliability testing, exploratory and confirmatory factor analyses, correlation analyses, and moderation analyses. The measurement results demonstrated satisfactory internal consistency and convergent validity for EEBDC, operational capability, and sustainable performance. EEBDC had a significant positive effect on sustainable performance (B = 0.364, β = 0.361, p &lt; .001). Operational capability also had a positive effect (B = 0.255, β = 0.277, p &lt; .001), while the EEBDC × operational capability interaction was positive and significant (B = 0.224, β = 0.221, p &lt; .001). The interaction explained an additional 4.7% of the variance in sustainable performance. The study extends Dynamic Capability Theory by providing an employee-centred explanation of organisational adaptation and identifies operational capability as an execution-related boundary condition that strengthens the performance value of empowered employees.</p>','["Employee empowerment-based dynamic capabilities", "Sustainable performance", "Operational capability", "SMEs", "Dynamic Capability Theory."]',NULL,24,1,'1951 - 1965','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1906','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',107),
(1907,1907,1,'Artificial Intelligence-Driven Omnichannel Retail Management: A Systematic Literature Review and Integrated Decision Intelligence Framework','<p>Artificial intelligence (AI) transforms omnichannel retailing and consumer services beyond basic coordination into real-time decision orchestration. However, customer analytics and operational efficiency continue to be treated as discrete, parallel streams. This paper reviews AI and its contribution to integrated omnichannel retail management and how it connects consumer intelligence, operational intelligence, and responsible decision-making. The author applied a PRISMA 2020-aligned approach to records available in Scopus, Web of Science, ScienceDirect, Emerald, SpringerLink, Taylor &amp; Francis, and IEEE Xplore, and citation chasing. From 1,348 records, 35 studies pertaining to the time period 2010 to June 2026 were selected for qualitative review. The author notes that AI heightens the understanding of the customer through segmentation and recommendations, and enhances the operational side of the business through demand forecasting and the optimization of inventory and order fulfillment. Evidence is still quite fragmented. Many applications of AI focus on optimizing customer engagement, yet do not take into consideration the availability of inventory, the cost of fulfillment, return risk, governance, trust, and the operational capacity. To close this gap, the author suggests a new integrated decision intelligence framework for omnichannel retail. This framework integrates omnichannel data, predictive and prescriptive models, governance, and learning. The main contributions of this study include consolidating AI research in retailing, proposing a decision-oriented framework for omnichannel ecosystems, and establishing a future research agenda for explainable AI within consumer services....</p>','["Artificial intelligence", "Omnichannel retailing", "Consumer services", "Decision intelligence", "Customer analytics", "Operational efficiency", "Retail supply chain..."]',NULL,24,1,'1966 - 1980','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1907','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',108),
(1909,1909,1,'Integrating Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Fuzzy Multi - Criteria Decision-Making for Strategic Organisational Performance Optimization','<p>The application of Artificial Intelligence (AI) in decision-making processes is changing the way organizations approach and make decisions.Organizations are increasingly turning to Artificial Intelligence (AI) to analyze complex data, detect trends, predict outcomes, and consider various options in their decision-making process. Organisation performance assessment, however, encompasses numerous both qualitative and quantitative criteria, uncertainty, conflicting priorities of management and imprecise expert opinions – which cannot be fully captured by conventional decision making techniques. This study presents a new AI assisted Fuzzy Multi-Criteria Decision-Making (F-MCDM) model to assess the performance of an organization and to assist in making strategic management decisions. The proposed framework is based on the use of AI-based analytical capabilities and fuzzy decision-making techniques that can handle the vagueness of managerial assessments and prioritize strategic options in a systematic manner. The relative importance of organisational performance criteria are determined by using the fuzzy Analytic Hierarchy Process (FAHP), and the fuzzy Technique for Order Preference by Similarity to Ideal Solution (FTOPSIS) is used to rank the strategic alternatives. It looks at aspects such as financial performance, operational efficiencies, innovation capability, customer orientation, human-resource effectiveness, and strategic adaptability as well as digital capability. The aim of the proposed approach is to enhance the decision consistency, transparency of decision making, strategic alignment and managerial responsiveness. The study illustrates that AI and fuzzy MCDM are able to offer an intelligent decision-support mechanism to organisations in an uncertain and competitive environment.</p>','["Artificial Intelligence, Fuzzy Multi-Criteria Decision Making, Strategic Management, Organisational Performance, Fuzzy AHP, Fuzzy TOPSIS"]',NULL,24,1,'1981 - 2010','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1909','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',109);
<p>For students to be admitted in these universities, they are required to submit an application for each university. On the other hand, universities set up admission boards that go through each application before choosing which applicants to admit. This process is quite tedious and time consuming both for the applicants and the universities. The objective of this paper is to design and implement a Centralised Admission System (CAS) using the current technology to facilitate and ease the admission process. With CAS, applicants will not have to repeat the application process for each university. Rather, they will only submit one application. The system will rank the students and allocate them to the universities depending on their scores. Universities will not have to go through the tedious process of choosing qualified students, since the system will use a verified algorithm in determining the qualified applicants. With this system, there will be transparency in the admission process and students will not have to spend a lot of time in the process. The system will make work easier for all the stakeholders. My recommendation is that the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia, Ministry of Higher Learning will embrace this new technology and encourage efficiency and effectiveness in the university admission process.</p>','["CAS, KSA, process, Universities, efficiency, transparency, Applicants"]',NULL,24,1,'2081-2094','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1927','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',113),
(1928,1928,1,'Social Acceptance and Human Rights Challenges Perceived by Ecuadorian Citizens Regarding the Use of Artificial Intelligence in Ecuador''s Judicial System','<p>The problem addressed in this study concerns understanding how Ecuadorian citizens accept the use of artificial intelligence in the administration of justice while simultaneously perceiving risks to human rights, amid challenges related to legitimacy, institutional trust, and guarantees of transparency, oversight, and accountability. This article aims to analyze the social acceptance and human rights challenges perceived by Ecuadorian citizens regarding the use of artificial intelligence in Ecuador''s judicial system. The study adopted a quantitative, non-experimental, cross-sectional approach, using a structured citizen survey. Dimensional indices were constructed, and descriptive analyses, normality tests, reliability tests, and Spearman correlation tests were applied in SPSS 27, ensuring statistical rigor and validity. The results show no significant correlations among knowledge, social acceptance, human rights challenges, and conditions of legitimacy, revealing that citizen perceptions operate independently of one another. Expectations of judicial efficiency coexist with persistent concerns about transparency, bias, due process, and data protection. Consequently, the Ecuadorian population regards artificial intelligence in the field of justice as a two-dimensional phenomenon, in which social support does not depend on technological literacy but rather on the degree of institutional trust and the presence of robust safeguards for the protection of human rights, as well as on the principles of transparency and democratic oversight.</p>','["Social acceptance, human rights challenges, judicial system, Ecuadorian citizens, Ecuador."]',NULL,24,1,'2095-2106','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1928','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',114),
(1929,1929,1,'Innovative Event Management for Environmental Sustainability: Challenges and Opportunities A Case Study of the Panchatatva EcoPride event Organized by Pallavi''s Management for Youth, Children & Cultural Awareness','<p>Growing environmental concerns require innovative approaches that move beyond passive awareness and encourage public participation and sustainable behavioural change. This research presents Panchatatva EcoPride, a community-based environmental awareness initiative organized by Madhuri Deshpande and Pallavi Deshpande, Founder of Pallavi''s Management, on 5 June 2026 at Kanhaiya Park, Azamgarh, Uttar Pradesh, India, to commemorate World Environment Day. Based on the Indian ecological philosophy of Panchatatva (Earth, Water, Air, Fire, and Sky), the initiative demonstrates how event management can support environmental communication, experiential learning, and sustainability education.</p>
<p>The initiative transformed a public park into an interactive environmental learning space through participatory activities. Environmental awareness rounds communicated sustainability messages, while the Pass the Kulhad and Tirth Water Round highlighted water conservation. The use of earthen kulhads for serving tea and lassi promoted sustainable alternatives to disposable plastic products. Tulsi sapling distribution encouraged household-level greening, while eco-friendly stoles symbolized the protective relationship between humans and the Earth. Madhuri Deshpande introduced the EcoPride concept, Adwita communicated environmental responsibility, Vivan led the Eco Walk, and Pallavi Deshpande coordinated the event.</p>
<p>The initiative revealed challenges including limited public awareness, communication barriers, and the need for continuous participant engagement. These challenges created opportunities for environmental education and community interaction. The study concludes that culturally rooted and participatory environmental events can contribute to sustainability education and behavioural transformation. The Panchatatva EcoPride model offers practical implications for event managers, educators, policymakers, and community organizations seeking environmentally responsible events that promote collective action and support the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs).</p>','["Sustainable Event Management", "Cultural Sustainability", "Community Participation", "Innovative Events", "Environmental Awareness", "Panchatatva EcoPride."]',NULL,24,1,'2107-2121','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1929','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',115),
(1930,1930,1,'Multiscale and Texture-Aware Deep Learning for Brain Tumor Segmentation in MRI: A Systematic Review','<p>The development of aberrant brain cells, some of which may be malignant, is known as a brain tumor. For patients to survive, brain tumors must be identified and diagnosed early. The tumor segmentation stage in the analysis of brain tumor images is essential to the further processing of MRI images. The tumors designated for segmentation are anatomical structures that are often characterized by flexibility and complex morphology, exhibiting significant variability in their location and structure, and differing from patient to patient. Accurate brain tumor segmentation faces challenges such as location uncertainty, morphological uncertainty, low contrast imaging, annotation bias and data imbalance. The aim of this systematic literature review is to determine which deep learning segmentation technique employing multiscale texture analysis is suitable for segmenting the uneven, diffuse tumor boundary from the adjacent brain matter. We conducted a systematic review of 970 brain tumor segmentation studies which employed multiscale features, texture features and deep learning techniques. Also, segmentation techniques using T1-weighted, T2-weighted, gadolinium-enhanced T1-weighted, fluid-attenuated inversion recovery, diffusion-weighted and perfusion-weighted magnetic resonance imaging sequences were reviewed. We synthesized each method as per the deep learning method used, strengths and weaknesses of the proposed technique, dataset used, types of brain tumors analyzed, feature types employed, and the performance measures achieved. It was observed that, T1-weighted, T2-weighted, gadolinium-enhanced T1-weighted, and fluid-attenuated inversion recovery MRI are the most used in the different segmentation algorithms. We found that multiscale and texture features have emerged as critical components in improving the accuracy and robustness of brain tumor segmentation from MRI images. Incorporating these features addresses the inherent challenges posed by the complex nature of brain tumors, including variations in size, shape, location and blurred edges.</p>','["MRI, Deep Learning, Brain Tumor Segmentation, Multiscale Feature Analysis, Texture-Based Feature Analysis"]',NULL,24,1,'2122-2154','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1930','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',116),
(1931,1931,1,'Intelligent Cybersecurity for the Internet of Things: An AI-Based Adaptive Model and Landscape Analysis — A Systematic Literature Review','<p>Rapidly increasing numbers of devices connected to the Internet of Things have greatly changed how modern digital ecosystems operate by facilitating interoperability among industries such as healthcare, intelligent transportation systems, smart city technology and industrial automation. The increasing number of Internet connected devices has created new vulnerabilities for cyber-attacks on these devices, such as DDoS, Botnets, unauthorized access to devices and data breaches. Traditional IoT protection approaches generally fail to address these types of threats due to their limited resources, heterogeneity, and constant changes. In this regard, artificial intelligence has been proposed as a viable method to enhance cybersecurity for IoTs via AI-based systems capable of intelligent threat detection, adaptive learning, and real-time responses.</p>
<p>To provide a clear understanding of AI-based cybersecurity methods for IoTs, this paper presents a systematic literature review (SLR) using a structured review process that follows the PRISMA guidelines. A total of 3072 original research papers on AI-based cybersecurity methods for IoTs published in top indexing databases were obtained. Each selected paper was analysed using a systematic screening process. The studies were grouped into six distinct theme areas, including machine learning-based intrusion detection, deep learning-based anomaly detection, hybrid/ensemble security models, federated learning frameworks, explainable AI, and blockchain-enabled IoT security mechanisms.</p>
<p>Machine learning algorithms demonstrated high computational efficiency in detecting intrusions but lacked the flexibility to adapt to new threats. On the other hand, deep learning algorithms demonstrated a high intrusion detection capability but at a high computational cost. Hybrid/federated learning algorithms represent an emerging class that can deliver both the required accuracy and scalability while preserving user privacy. While several important advancements exist in AI-based cybersecurity methods for IoTs, many additional areas will require significant work before widespread adoption. Some examples include continued reliance on outdated benchmarks to evaluate algorithmic performance; the absence of validation testing for algorithms running in real-time environments; difficulty interpreting results generated by black-box AI-based algorithms; and a lack of collaboration among researchers studying different layers of the edge-cloud architecture.</p>
<p>As a result of synthesising the current state of the art in AI-based cybersecurity methods for IoTs, this paper proposes a conceptual model of an AI-based adaptive cybersecurity system for IoTs, designed to utilise edge intelligence, federated learning, and continuous feedback mechanisms to detect and respond dynamically to potential threats. This paper makes contributions to the current body of knowledge regarding the rapidly changing landscape of IoT cybersecurity by providing an organised, analytical summary of the current state of the art in AI-based cybersecurity methods for IoTs; defining specific research gaps; and providing recommendations for future research to develop scalable, understandable, and adaptable security systems for IoT environments.</p>','["Internet of Things (IoT), IoT Security", "Cybersecurity", "Artificial Intelligence, Machine Learning", "Deep Learning, Intrusion Detection Systems, Anomaly Detection, Adaptive Security, Federated Learning, Edge Computing, Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI)"]',NULL,24,1,'2155-2184','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1931','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',117),
(1932,1932,1,'Teacher Readiness for Artificial Intelligence Integration in Higher Education','<p>Background: Artificial intelligence (AI) is entering school teaching through lesson planning, resource creation, differentiated support, feedback, and assessment. Responsible use depends on teachers who can check outputs, protect data, maintain student thinking, and follow clear school rules. Purpose: This study develops a school-focused model of teacher readiness and tests whether a clear 28-item questionnaire and a simple analysis plan work together. Methodology: A reproducible synthetic dataset of 500 fictional primary- and secondary-school teacher profiles was generated with a fixed random seed. No human participants or real schools were involved. Six five-point Likert scales measured AI-related digital competence, perceived usefulness, school support, ethical concern, teacher readiness, and responsible classroom AI integration. Reliability, basic construct validity, correlations, and two multiple regression models were examined. Key findings: All six scales showed satisfactory reliability (Cronbach''s alpha = .872-.907) and met the stated basic validity checks. Digital competence (standardized beta = .302), perceived usefulness (.276), and school support (.241) were positively related to readiness, while ethical concern was negatively related (-.155). Readiness was the strongest predictor of responsible classroom integration (.431). Usefulness and school support also had positive direct relationships, while ethical concern had a negative relationship. The models explained 41.9% of readiness and 49.8% of integration in the generated dataset. Practical implications: Schools should combine subject-based AI training, approved tools, clear policy, teaching support, and practical safeguards. The numerical results are a reproducible benchmark, not estimates of real teachers.</p>','["Artificial intelligence", "school teachers", "teacher readiness", "classroom teaching", "school support", "ethical AI", "synthetic data"]',NULL,24,1,'2185-2215','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1932','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',118),
(1933,1933,1,'Leadership, Organizational Climate, and Member Value in Philippine Multipurpose Cooperatives: Evidence for Organizational Decision-Making','<p>Cooperatives are expected to balance economic viability with social responsibility, yet sustaining these organizations has become increasingly challenging due to changing market conditions, technological advancements, and evolving member expectations. Although previous studies have established relationships among organizational climate, creativity, innovation, and organizational performance, limited qualitative evidence explains how these processes contribute to long-term cooperative sustainability. This study explored how cooperative leaders and members perceive creativity and innovation as organizational processes that support the sustainability of multipurpose cooperatives in the Philippines. A qualitative descriptive design was employed using semi-structured interviews with five cooperative leaders and five focus group discussions involving thirty cooperative members from selected multipurpose cooperatives in Bukidnon, Philippines. Participants were purposively selected based on leadership experience and active organizational involvement. Data were analyzed using Braun and Clarke''s reflexive thematic analysis, with member checking, triangulation, and an audit trail employed to ensure trustworthiness. Five interrelated themes emerged: (1) organizational challenges stimulate innovation; (2) leadership creates an enabling climate for creativity; (3) innovation strengthens member value and organizational commitment; (4) organizational learning supports adaptive decision-making; and (5) sustainability is reinforced through collective responsibility and shared commitment. Participants viewed innovation as a leadership-driven organizational process that enhances member welfare, organizational resilience, and community relevance rather than merely technological advancement. The findings suggest that cooperative sustainability is a dynamic capability shaped by the interaction of adaptive leadership, a supportive organizational climate, creativity, organizational learning, and active member participation. The study proposes a process-oriented framework illustrating how innovation creates member value that reinforces long-term cooperative sustainability while offering practical implications for cooperative leaders, policymakers, and development institutions seeking to strengthen organizational resilience.</p>','["leadership", "sustainability", "cooperative member value", "organizational climate", "innovation"]',NULL,24,1,'2216-2229','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1933','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',119),
(1934,1934,1,'Development and Evaluation of an Explainable AI-Driven Decision Support System for Clinical and Operational Healthcare Management','<p>Healthcare organizations need to have an integrated decision-support system that can enhance patient risk assessment and optimize their use of resources. But the lack of transparency and separation between the use of AI in clinical use and administrative applications puts adoption challenges in the way.Conceive and evaluate a healthcare management explainable AI-based decision support system integrating clinical-risk prediction, resource-demand prediction and operational healthcare management. A synthetic multicentre dataset of 1,800 anonymized patient encounters was divided into a development set (n=1,260), a validation set (n=270) and an independent test set (n=270). The following models were tested: logistic regression, random forest, support vector machine, extreme gradient boosting, artificial neural network and the proposed explainable AI system. Evaluation of performance was made using the classification, discrimination, calibration, decision-utility, operational, subgroup and professional-acceptance measures. SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) was used for the interpretation of predictions. The proposed system achieved an accuracy of 0.900, sensitivity of 0.730, specificity of 0.952, F1-score of 0.773, ROC–AUC of 0.951, and Brier score of 0.085. Clinical-risk probability, oxygen saturation, comorbidity burden, C-reactive protein, respiratory rate, age and bed occupancy were important predictors. The efficiency of the work process increased by 16.0%, waiting time was reduced by 21.2%, length of stay by 10.9%, and the accuracy of the resource allocation''s handling by 14.4% after using it in management. The explainability, trust, usefulness and acceptance were positively reported by healthcare professionals. Explainable AI can enhance collaborative clinical and operational decision making. However, prospective multicenter validation in the real world is needed before clinical implementation.</p>','["artificial intelligence", "clinical decision support", "explainable AI", "healthcare management", "resource optimization", "machine learning."]',NULL,24,1,'2230-2244','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1934','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',120),
(1935,1935,1,'Blockchain Enabled Cybersecurity Frameworks for Intelligent Transportation System','<p>The rapid evolution of Intelligent Transportation Systems (ITS) has resulted in highly connected transportation ecosystems involving vehicles, roadside infrastructure, traffic management centres, cloud platforms, edge devices, and users. Although connectivity improves traffic efficiency, road safety, and intelligent mobility, it simultaneously increases exposure to cyber threats such as data manipulation, identity spoofing, Sybil attacks, denial-of-service attacks, replay attacks, malicious node behaviour, and privacy violations. Blockchain technology provides a promising cybersecurity foundation for ITS through decentralised trust management, immutable data storage, cryptographic authentication, transparent transaction verification, and smart-contract-based access control. This paper proposes a blockchain-enabled cybersecurity framework for ITS that integrates distributed ledger technology with secure vehicle-to-vehicle, vehicle-to-infrastructure, and vehicle-to-everything communication. The framework incorporates identity management, consensus-based validation, trust evaluation, secure data sharing, intrusion detection, and privacy-preserving mechanisms. Particular emphasis is placed on lightweight consensus mechanisms and edge-assisted blockchain architectures to address the latency, computational, storage, and scalability constraints of vehicular environments. The proposed framework provides a systematic architecture for improving data integrity, authentication, accountability, privacy, and resilience against cyberattacks while supporting intelligent and safety-critical transportation services.</p>','["Blockchain, Intelligent Transportation System, Cybersecurity, Vehicular Ad Hoc Network, Vehicle-to-Everything, Trust Management"]',NULL,24,1,'2245-2277','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1935','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',121),
(1936,1936,1,'Integrating Strategic Human Resource Practices and Organizational Culture Through Crisis Response in Oman Healthcare Institutions','<p>This study examined the influence of strategic human resource management on Organizational culture through the mediating role of crisis-management capability in private healthcare institutions in Muscat, Oman. A quantitative, cross-sectional survey was conducted among 137 HR specialists, managers, supervisors, and administrative employees from 11 private hospitals. Data were analyzed using partial least squares structural equation modelling. The findings showed that strategic human resource management had a significant positive effect on crisis-management capability, while Crisis Management significantly influenced Organizational culture. However, the direct relationship between strategic human resource management and Organizational culture was not statistically significant. The mediation analysis indicated that Crisis Management fully mediated this relationship, suggesting that strategic Human resource practices contribute to cultural outcomes mainly by strengthening preparedness, response, recovery, coordination, and organizational learning. The study extends the Resource-Based View and Contingency Theory by demonstrating that human resources generate organisational value when they are mobilized through crisis-responsive capabilities. Practically, private healthcare institutions should integrate workforce planning, leadership development, crisis training, performance systems, flexible staffing, and post-crisis learning into a unified resilience framework. The study provides context-specific evidence from Oman’s private healthcare sector and highlights the importance of aligning Human resource strategy, crisis preparedness, and cultural development.</p>','["Strategic human resource management, Crisis Management, Organizational culture, organizational resilience, private healthcare"]',NULL,24,1,'2278-2296','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1936','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',122),
(1937,1937,1,'Educational Transformation Through Technological Innovation: Perceived Effects on Learning Among Life Sciences Students at the ULEAM Pedernales Extension','<p>Objective: This study evaluated students’ perceptions of technological and active teaching strategies and examined their association with learning outcomes among Life Sciences students at the ULEAM Pedernales Extension.</p>
<p>Background: Technological and active learning strategies, including the flipped classroom, gamification, and experimental project-based learning, are increasingly used to improve student participation, motivation, and academic performance in higher education. However, evidence regarding their application in geographically and institutionally specific contexts in Ecuador remains limited.</p>
<p>Methods: A quantitative, exploratory study was conducted among Life Sciences students at the ULEAM Pedernales Extension. From a population of 339 students, a sample of 180 was calculated using the finite-population formula at a 95% confidence</p>
<p>level and a 5% margin of error. Data were collected through a 30-item Likert-type questionnaire addressing students’ knowledge, attitudes, and perceptions of innovative and traditional teaching strategies. Descriptive statistics, contingency analysis, a Shapiro–Wilk normality test, and cluster analysis were used to examine response patterns and similarities among teaching strategies.</p>
<p>Results: Sixty percent of the students selected “agree” or “strongly agree” regarding the contribution of technological innovation to learning. The reported score distribution departed significantly from normality. The cluster analysis grouped the flipped classroom, gamification, and experimental project-based learning relatively closely, whereas the traditional strategy formed a more distinct group. The flipped classroom produced the highest reported mean score of 9.00, while the traditional strategy produced the lowest mean score of 6.31.</p>
<p>Conclusion: Active and technology-supported strategies were associated with more favourable student perceptions and higher reported academic performance than traditional teaching. Nevertheless, the findings should be interpreted as associations rather than definitive causal effects. Further validation using the original student-level dataset is required.</p>
<p>Originality/value: The study provides context-specific evidence on the use of active and technology-supported teaching strategies in Life Sciences education at a regional Ecuadorian university extension.</p>','["educational technology", "flipped classroom", "gamification", "project-based learning", "Life Sciences education"]',NULL,24,1,'2297-2317','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1937','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',123),
(1938,1938,1,'The Impact of Artificial Intelligence on English Language Learning, Communication, and Linguistic Development: Opportunities and Challenges','<p>Artificial Intelligence (AI) has emerged as a transformative force in education, significantly influencing English Language Learning, communication, and linguistic development. AI-powered technologies, including natural language processing, conversational agents, speech recognition systems, generative AI, and adaptive learning platforms, have introduced innovative approaches to language instruction and learning. These technologies offer opportunities for personalized learning, continuous feedback, and enhanced learner engagement while simultaneously raising concerns regarding ethical use, reliability, and educational integrity. This review synthesizes and critically examines existing literature on the application of AI in English language education. The review explores major AI technologies, their impact on language learning outcomes, communication practices, and linguistic development, while also evaluating the opportunities, challenges, and future directions associated with their integration into educational contexts. The findings indicate that AI contributes positively to vocabulary acquisition, grammar learning, writing development, reading comprehension, listening proficiency, speaking skills, and learner autonomy. AI also enhances academic, professional, and intercultural communication while supporting lexical, syntactic, semantic, pragmatic, and communicative competence. However, challenges related to AI hallucinations, overreliance on technology, ethical and privacy concerns, cultural bias, academic integrity, and digital inequality continue to limit its effective implementation. AI possesses substantial potential to enhance English language education through personalized, accessible, and learner-centered learning experiences. Nevertheless, its successful integration requires a balanced approach that combines technological innovation with ethical governance, pedagogical expertise, and human oversight. Future developments should focus on human–AI collaboration, intelligent tutoring systems, multimodal learning environments, and sustainable educational practices to maximize learning outcomes and support meaningful linguistic development.</p>','["Artificial Intelligence, English Language Learning, Language Education, Linguistic Development, Communication Competence."]',NULL,24,1,'2318-2338','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1938','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',124),
(1939,1939,1,'Artificial Intelligence and Intelligent Decision Support Systems: Emerging Trends and Applications in Computer Science','<p>Artificial Intelligence (AI) has become a transformative technology for enhancing Intelligent Decision Support Systems (IDSS) by enabling accurate, adaptive, and data-driven decision-making across diverse computer science applications. This review examines the fundamental concepts of AI, its major techniques, including machine learning, deep learning, expert systems, fuzzy logic, reinforcement learning, explainable AI, and generative AI, and their roles in modern decision support systems. It further discusses emerging trends such as human-centered AI, edge AI, federated learning, digital twins, hybrid AI models, and responsible AI that are reshaping intelligent decision-making. The review also highlights the applications of AI-enabled IDSS in cybersecurity, software engineering, cloud computing, the Internet of Things, healthcare informatics, robotics, big data analytics, smart manufacturing, and education technologies. In addition, key challenges related to data quality, explainability, scalability, privacy, ethics, computational complexity, and user trust are critically discussed, followed by future research directions emphasizing foundation models, neuro-symbolic AI, Green AI, Quantum AI, AI governance, and autonomous decision intelligence. Overall, the review provides a comprehensive overview of recent advancements and identifies promising opportunities for developing trustworthy and efficient AI-driven intelligent decision support systems.</p>','["Artificial Intelligence", "Intelligent Decision Support Systems", "Machine Learning", "Explainable Artificial Intelligence", "Computer Science."]',NULL,24,1,'2339-2355','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1939','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',125),
(1948,1948,1,'Artificial Intelligence and Predictive Policing in the Indian Criminal Justice System: Methods, Applications and Constitutional Concerns','<p>What was speculative a decade ago is now routine, as the algorithms have entered the everyday machinery of policing. In India, as elsewhere, law-enforcement agencies increasingly rely on computational tools for crime mapping, facial recognition, forensic analysis and the allocation of manpower. This paper focus on the critical study by unpacking what predictive policing actually means, then places of Indian experience against the older trajectories of the United States, the United Kingdom and China – jurisdictions from which India has borrowed its technologies and less consciously, its problems. The Indian scenario is surveyed in detail: Delhi’s crime-mapping platform, the state-level experience of Uttar Pradesh, Telangana, Maharashtra, Punjab and Kerala, the Bureau of Police Research and Development’s women’s-safety project, and the quieter adoption of AI by forensic laboratories and the judiciary. These deployments are measured against the criminal procedure framework and constitutional guarantees of privacy, equality and fair trial as enshrined in various judgments of the Hon’ble Supreme Court. In the absence of a dedicated statute, algorithmic policing in India threatens to harden caste and communal prejudice, hollow out due process and shelter behind statutory opacity. The article intends to suggest by proposing legislative, institutional and technical safeguards for an accountable regime of algorithmic law enforcement.</p>','["Artificial Intelligence", "Predictive Policing", "Criminal Justice System", "Right to Privacy", "Algorithmic Accountability."]',NULL,24,1,'2356-2372','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1948','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',126),
(1949,1949,1,'Semiotic Representations for Learning Function Analysis Through Khan Academy','<p>The main objective of this article was to examine the semiotic representations of function analysis used by engineering students mediated by Khan Academy. To do this, a mixed methodology was used. This was based on a descriptive-explanatory type, with a transversal field design. The population of the study was composed by 315 students and the sample was focused on 30 students belonging to the Universidad Particular de Loja in Ecuador. For data gathering, knowledge test, rubric of relationship between cognitive activities and learning and the reports of the platform were the instruments applied. The results indicated that the students of Univariate Mathematical Analysis correctly used the three cognitive activities with a high domain, highlighting the training activity. In addition, the platform reported that students reached an average level in terms of mastery of the content studied. It was concluded that students, through the use of platforms such as Khan Academy, can approach more easily the various semiotic representations of function analysis. This is possible by using of different semiotic registers to enhance levels of understanding and academic performance.</p>','["Theory of Semiotic Representations, Khan Academy, Learning, Function Analysis, Cognitive Activities"]',NULL,24,1,'2373-2391','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1949','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',127),
(1950,1950,1,'Artificial Intelligence for Sustainable Human Development: Integrating Humanities, Education, and Intelligent Information Systems','<p>Artificial Intelligence is transforming the landscape of education, research, healthcare, public decision making and sustainability practice at a fast pace, but the impact of AI on human development cannot be measured solely by technical outcomes. This article discusses how humanities, education and intelligent information systems can be incorporated into a human-centred AI to make a difference in sustainable human development. It reviews and amalgamates research from the last few years on cultural values, fairness, trust, AI literacy, generative AI, assessment reform, digital sustainability, interpretable systems, climate action, and healthcare applications. Contingency of AI''s capacity and human judgment, along with cultural meaning and pedagogical intent, institutional accountability and environmental preferences, are important factors in creating social value, the analysis argues. The value-oriented and interpretation-based approach of Humanities is the basis for understanding the meaning and agency, ethical, and cultural diversity aspects of using AI; the critical literacy and adaptive capabilities developed in the process of education are the basis for using AI responsibly; and the intelligent information systems unite data and computational resources into practical support for learning, governance, health, research, sustainability. The article proposes a multi-faceted understanding of AI as a socio-technical system that needs to be inclusive, accountable, transparent, monitored by humans and have a long-term public purpose. Development of AI should therefore complement and extend the capacities of humanity and ensure equitable, resilient and environmentally-friendly social progress in diverse learning, institutional, cultural and developmental environments in the world.</p>','["Artificial intelligence", "Human development", "Education", "Information systems", "Sustainability"]',NULL,24,1,'2392-2408','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1950','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',128),
(1951,1951,1,'A Data-Driven Intelligent Decision Framework Integrating Digital Marketing, Supply Chain Management, Operations Management, and Business Management for Sustainable Operational and Financial Performance','<p>Digital transformation increasingly requires organizations to connect customer-facing digital activity with supply-chain execution, operational efficiency, and financial performance. This study has provided an intelligent decision framework based on data as a fusion of Digital Marketing, Supply Chain Management, Operations Management and Business Management. The DataCo Smart Supply Chain dataset was used to clean, precisely match and aggregate 180,519 transaction-item records and 466,728 web-log records into a lifecycle-valid product-week panel. Two digital indices were built, regression modelling was adjusted, robustness analysis was performed, a 17-indicator product decision framework was created, and leakage-safe predictive modelling was done using chronological train, validation, and reserved-test splits. While a 12.2% decline in the chances of conditional late delivery was a significant result, seven out of nine outcomes were significant, such as a 151.5% increase in transaction items, a 203.2% increase in units sold, a 198.5% increase in net revenue, and a 360.9% increase in total loss magnitude, with the profit margin remaining unchanged. The framework identified 5 strategic product groups consisting of 36 products. The late-delivery model obtained the best results with ROC-AUC 0.773 and average precision 0.854, with no support for negative-profit prediction. The results reveal that taking a broader holistic view of digital growth and fulfilment reliability, alongside the operational constraints and financial risk are required to achieve sustainable performance and return on investment, and that this is far more important than relying on individual marketing or sales metrics.</p>','["Digital marketing", "Supply chain management", "Intelligent decision framework", "Operational performance", "Predictive analytics"]',NULL,24,1,'2409-2425','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1951','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',129),
(1952,1952,1,'Classification of Mangrove Ecosystem and Prediction by Mapping the Drone Based Imagery using Machine Learning Techniques','<p>Usually, mangrove species raise in close proximity, resulting in mixed pixels in remote sensing data. Yet, no prevailing works concerted on numerous overlapping species identification in mangrove species classification.Thus, mixed pixel-based mangrove species classification is introduced by employing the GP-CMM along with SLC-tDNN approaches. Firstly, the drone images are collected; further, pre-processed via radiometric correction, shadow removal, color correction, along with Contrast Enhancement (CE). As of the pre-processed image, mosaicking is done, as well as mosaic images’ 3D construction is created. A topography map is created as of the mosaicked image. Yet, by employing CGCLP, the mosaicked image endures gradient blending. Afterward, by employing GP-CMM, spectral unmixing is done on gradient blending outcome. Next, from the spectral unmixing outcome, the distinct regions are segmented employing SNWHS. Also, by utilizing RRFSD, derivative analysis is done as of the segmented image. Then, from a constructed topography map, distinct region segmentation, together with derivative analysis, features are extracted. Also, by employing SLC-tDNN, the species classification is performed from the extracted features. Lastly, by using STFM-KMC, the classified species are mapped. The proposed model’s overall performance is appraised by collecting images as of the Coringa Mangroves across a huge area of about 150 acres in the Andhra Pradesh (Kakinada district). By employing drone-centric imaging, Coringa Mangroves (1834 images) are captured. As per the experimental analysis, the proposed model achieved 98.33% accuracy along with a precision of 98.32%.</p>','["Mangrove Species, Contextual Gradient Clipped Laplacian Pyramid (CGCLP), Gaussian Penalty on Coefficient Variability Mixture Model (GP-CMM), Spatial Neighborhood Watershed Histogram Segmentation (SNWHS), Spatial-Temporal Frequency Mapped K-Means Clustering (STFM-KMC), Student''s Log Convolutional t-Distribution Neural Network (SLC-tDNN), and Intensity Thresholding Poisson Equation (ITPE)."]',NULL,24,1,'2426-2446','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1952','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',130),
(1953,1953,1,'IMPACT OF CORPORATE SOCIAL RESPONSIBILITY ON INVESTMENT EFFICIENCY OF DIGITAL ECONOMY ENTERPRISES IN CHINA','<p>The digital economy has become a major engine driving global economic growth, and the investment efficiency of digital economy enterprises has gradually attracted attention. This study takes listed companies in China''s A-share market''s core digital economy industries from 2015 to 2023 as samples to explore the relationship between corporate social responsibility and investment efficiency. The study reveals that corporate social responsibility has a significant positive impact on the investment efficiency of digital economy enterprises, while financing constraints play a partial mediating role. Notably, employee responsibility benefits demonstrate stronger promotional effects on enhancing investment efficiency, followed by supplier, customer and consumer rights responsibility. Comprehensive analysis indicates that proactive fulfillment of corporate social responsibilities plays a pivotal role in improving investment efficiency among digital economy enterprises.</p>','["Corporate social responsibility, Investment efficiency, Digital economy enterprises"]',NULL,24,1,'2447-2467','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1953','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',131),
(1954,1954,1,'An Artificial Intelligence Based Air Pollution Prediction Model for Smart Cities','<p>Air pollution is an escalating concern driven by urbanization and population growth, leading to significant health problems. Accurate information on air quality and associated health risks is essential for effective environmental management. In this study, the implementation of Artificial Neural Network (ANN) techniques for hourly, daily, &amp; monthly air pollution prediction is investigated. The study highlights that ANN methods offer superior accuracy in air pollution forecasting compared to traditional approaches. It emphasizes that the accuracy of ANN-based models is influenced by input parameters and the type of architecture algorithms employed. Due to their ability to process a diverse range of meteorological data, ANN models prove to be more reliable and precise than other empirical models. The study also emphasizes the present research gaps pertaining to the utilize of neural networks in air pollution prediction. This analysis aims to stimulate further research and encourage advancements in artificial intelligence for improving air quality forecasting. The proposed system integrates heterogeneous real-time data streams — including ground-level electrochemical sensor readings, satellite-derived atmospheric measurements, vehicular flow indices, and localized meteorological parameters — into a unified analytical pipeline.Spatial interpolation modules further enhance prediction granularity across city zones with uneven sensor coverage.</p>
<p>The system delivers pollutant concentration forecasts for particulate matter (PM2.5, PM10), nitrogen dioxide (NO₂), carbon monoxide (CO), and ozone (O₃) at hourly and 48-hour prediction horizons.Beyond technical performance, the framework is engineered for operational integration with Smart City command infrastructure, enabling automated public advisory dissemination, dynamic traffic rerouting triggers, and evidence-based environmental policy support. This study establishes a replicable, data-driven blueprint for intelligent air quality governance in the era of connected urban environments.</p>','["Air Pollution, Artificial Neural Network, Environmental Management, Forecasting Intervals, Accuracy, Meteorological Parameters, Empirical Models, Research Gaps, Air Quality, Artificial Intelligence."]',NULL,24,1,'2468-2479','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1954','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',132),
(1955,1955,1,'Multimodal Cancer Classification Using a SwinR Transformer Cross-Attention and Contrastive Learning','<p>Deep learning models are designed to process complex medical images, allowing physicians to more accurately identify and classify cancerous cells. There precision and speed, are both crucial in real clinical settings. This paper presents an enhanced SwinR Transformer based framework that incorporates hierarchical feature extraction with adaptive attention to maintain a higher degree of "awareness" of what is important. Instead of just one signal, we do multimodal fusion, meaning that we combine genetic data, radiology images, and histopathology slides into one and coherent view, albeit it''s a bit different in each modality. We also add differential learning + self-supervised learning, which helps with generalization and resilience, in essence by enabling more efficient feature representation in the event that labelled datasets are limited. But to make it even more accurate we employ cross attention mechanisms, which we enable complementary information to be integrated dynamically across modalities. In addition, we use adaptive patch-based processing for local feature extraction as well as global – some patterns are subtle. End to end pipeline. The first is a modality-specific encoder that deals with images and genomics, and the next is “cross attention fusion.” Then we do a contrastive pretraining step and at the end a classification head yields the result. Comparing to other state-of-the-art designs such as Vision Transformers (ViT) and ConvNeXt and hybrid CNN transformer method, our accuracy, recall and interpretability give positive results. From the experimental results, the SwinR Transformer is clearly better than the CNN based models with the multimodal fusion and advanced learning techniques. That implies it may be a viable alternative for actual diagnostic uses in the fight against cancer. Compared with the existing methods, our method is quantitative with an accuracy of 91.3%, recall of 88.1%, and F1 score of 89.7% which is superior. Besides the modality specific evaluations, we also conduct ablation studies to quantify the contribution of each modality and each architectural component.</p>','["SwinR Transformer", "multimodal fusion", "hierarchical feature extraction", "adaptive attention", "cross-attention", "patch-based processing"]',NULL,24,1,'2480-2499','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1955','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',133),
(1956,1956,1,'Conflict Mitigation Modelling To Mitigate Human-Elephant Conflicts Using Machine Learning Approaches','<p>Data accumulated from sensors are utilized for tracking endangered species. Nonetheless, when it comes to animals that survive in complex environments like areas where human wandering is recognized. Conventional approaches like remote monitoring turn to be a more complex system and highly inefficient. This work anticipates characterization of random animal movement with a random walk model to choose detection range approximation and to ensure target detection probability. For this, Pyroelectric Infrared Sensors (PIR), a superior device, is considered here to recognize the animal presence in complex environmental design and offer cost-effective surveillance. The presence of elephants is detected with sensors placed on land that detects elephant movement, temperature and sound. The movement of the dataset is acquired from the online available elephant dataset, where the dataset has 1635 elephant images for training and 439 images for testing. With natural elephant movement, this sensor is used to activate intruder alarm and activate elephants upon the presence of any hazardous environment. The analogue output from this sensor is proportional to temporal and spatial relationships among objects covered under sensor views like features, sensitivity and environmental heat conditions. The construction of this model relies on successful detection probability and lifetime of sensors for active data streaming. Finally, this virtual framework is modelled with a Markov chain for numerical computation of animal''s modes to the next state, either alerted or progression to a dangerous condition. The outcome has to show that trajectories are modelled based on certain phase-type sensor distributions. This model is suited for Polar Regions, where sensor signal processing and Machine learning approaches are obvious to estimate distance, direction and presence of animal movement in living regions. A simplified IVF model uses one sensor to analyze animal movement and various performance metrics like accuracy, precision, F-measure, recall, and cost function evaluated.</p>','["Polar Regions", "detection probability", "Pyroelectric Infrared Sensors", "random movement", "random walk"]',NULL,24,1,'2500-2518','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1956','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',134);
<p>Objective: To analyze dengue-related deaths in Colombia during the 2024 outbreak.</p>
<p>Methodology: An observational, descriptive, and retrospective study. Secondary sources provided by Instituto Nacional de Salud de Colombia, were used. Absolute and relative frequencies with 95% confidence intervals were calculated for categorical variables, and measures of central tendency and dispersion were computed for quantitative variables.</p>
<p>Results: In 2024, 284 confirmed dengue-related deaths were recorded in Colombia, with a higher predominance in females during the adult stage; the department with the highest number of deaths was Valle del Cauca. The departments of Guainía (0.35), La Guajira, and Putumayo (0.26) showing the highest case fatality rates. Differences were identified among age groups: individuals aged 50 to 59 years showed the greatest delay in seeking care, with an average of 4.93 days (SD: 1.73), while children aged 1 to 4 years sought care more promptly, with an average of 2.9 days (SD: 2.5). Additionally, children under 1 year of age had the shortest mean interval between hospitalization and death, with only 1.8 days (SD: 0.7) .</p>
<p>Conclusion: These findings highlight the need to strengthen surveillance actions, timely diagnosis, and the problem-solving capacity of health services, especially in vulnerable contexts and during periods of higher transmission.</p>','["Dengue, Mortality, Colombia, Health Services, Health Services Accessibility."]',NULL,24,1,'2619-2632','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1964','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',140),
(1965,1965,1,'Intersectionality and the Experiences of Women in Stem in Colombia','<p>This paper examines the experiences of women in science, technology, engineering, and mathematics (STEM) in Colombia through an intersectional lens. Drawing on focus groups with undergraduate students, graduates, and women in leadership positions, the study analyzes how gender interacts with class, ethnicity, territory, age, nationality, and family roles to shape unequal trajectories in academic and professional settings. The findings show that exclusion in STEM cannot be explained by gender alone. Instead, it is organized through a matrix of domination in which some women enter and remain in STEM under significantly harsher conditions than others. The analysis highlights five central dynamics: the unequal weight of class-based and territorial disadvantages, the political invisibility of Afro-descendant and Indigenous women, the penalization of motherhood, the normalization of symbolic and communicative violence, and the transfer of institutional care to informal support networks. At the same time, the study identifies forms of agency and perseverance developed by participants, although these often come at the cost of emotional hardening, chronic exhaustion, and self-surveillance. Rather than celebrating resilience as a sufficient answer, the article argues for a shift toward institutional responsibility, epistemic justice, and more habitable educational and professional environments for women in STEM.</p>','["intersectionality", "women", "STEM", "higher education", "symbolic violence", "motherhood", "participation", "Colombia."]',NULL,24,1,'2633-2646','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1965','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',141),
(2115,2115,1,'Stakeholder Technology Readiness and Acceptance of a Virtual Tourism Platform: Evidence from the Tabuk 360 Initiative','<p>Digital tourism platforms can improve destination promotion, information access, and heritage interpretation, but their sustainability depends on stakeholder readiness, usability, and institutional support. This study evaluated stakeholder technology readiness and acceptance of the Tabuk 360 Virtual Reality Initiative in Tabuk City, Kalinga, Philippines. A descriptive-correlational design supplemented by semi-structured interviews was used with 77 purposively selected respondents from the HEARTS sector, institutional stakeholders, and community representatives. Technology readiness was assessed through the four dimensions of the Technology Readiness Index 2.0, while platform acceptance was examined through perceived usefulness and perceived ease of use. Stakeholders reported high optimism (M = 4.45), moderate innovativeness (M = 3.83), and low discomfort (M = 2.60) and insecurity (M = 2.47). The platform received very high ratings for perceived usefulness (M = 4.66) and perceived ease of use (M = 4.57). The relationship between technology readiness and perceived usefulness was very weak and not statistically significant (r = 0.169, p = 0.141). Qualitative responses identified technical skills, funding, connectivity, content maintenance, and culturally accurate representation as key implementation concerns. The findings indicate that strong perceived usefulness can coexist with only moderate technology readiness, emphasizing the importance of usability, accessibility, maintenance governance, and community-validated cultural content in decisions about sustaining local digital tourism systems.</p>','["technology readiness", "technology acceptance", "virtual reality", "smart tourism", "information systems", "cultural heritage"]',NULL,24,1,'2647 - 2655','10.59543/jidmis.v3.2115','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',142),
(2120,2120,1,'Algorithmic Ethnomathematics: Formulating an Acehnese CultureBased Coding-RME Model for 4C Competencies and Post-Disaster Psychosocial Resilience','<p>This study aims to develop a Coding–Realistic Mathematics Education learning model based on Acehnese culture that is valid, practical, and effective in improving elementary school students'' numeracy literacy, 21st-century competencies (4Cs), and psychosocial recovery in post-disaster settings. The study employed the ADDIE development model, consisting of the Analysis, Design, Development, Implementation, and Evaluation stages. During the analysis stage, learning needs, students'' characteristics, postdisaster psychosocial conditions, and the potential of Acehnese culture as a contextual basis for mathematics learning were identified. The design stage produced the syntax of the Coding-RME learning model by integrating the principles of Realistic Mathematics Education, simple coding activities, and Acehnese ethnomathematics. The development stage resulted in teaching modules and student worksheets that were validated by subject matter experts, media experts, Acehnese culture experts, and educational psychology experts. The implementation stage involved a limited trial conducted with students at UPTD SD Negeri 1 Bireuen. Data were collected using validation sheets, observation forms, numeracy literacy tests, psychosocial questionnaires, and 4C competency assessment instruments. The findings indicate that the Coding-RME model achieved a validity level of 91.2%, categorized as highly valid, and a practicality level of 88.4%, categorized as practical. The evaluation results showed an improvement in students'' numeracy literacy with an N-Gain score of 0.52 (moderate category), while the average score for 4C competencies reached 4.35 (good category), and the psychosocial aspect achieved an average score of 4.45 (high category). These findings demonstrate that integrating coding, Realistic Mathematics Education, and Acehnese culture creates a contextual, collaborative, and meaningful mathematics learning environment while supporting students'' psychosocial recovery after disasters. Therefore, the Acehnese culture-based Coding-RME model can serve as an innovative alternative for developing 21st-century mathematics learning that is adaptive to students'' social, cultural, and psychological contexts.</p>','["Coding-RME", "Acehnese Culture", "21st-Century Competencies", "Psychosocial Resilience", "Post-Disaster"]',NULL,24,1,'2656 - 2679','10.59543/jidmis.v3.2120','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-01',143),
(1370,1370,1,'The Effect of Lean Manufacturing on Customer Satisfaction through Service Quality','<p>Purpose – The aim of current research is to identify service quality as a mediator variable between lean manufacturing and customer satisfaction of the state company of Textile &amp; Leather Industries in Iraq.</p>
<p>Design/methodology/approach – The sample group for the research consist of individuals from the state company for Textile &amp; Leather Industries in Iraq. The sample involved 160 individuals and was designed depending on a stratified and proportional distribution. The scale of Lean manufacturing presented in this study was found to be applicable and well-matched. The linking between the quality service dimensions found on the real perception of the state company for Textile &amp; Leather Industries in Iraq, through the individual, with the individuals’ satisfaction to their organizations, was investigated using SEM.</p>
<p>Findings – The plethora of originality value statements recognized in the works are analytically presented. Moreover, significant topics of the originality value of the LM studies are formulated.</p>
<p>Research limitations/implications.</p>
<p>Practical implications – the originality value of the existing LM, QS, and CS studies and presenting respective meaningful themes can help researchers and practitioners design their future research and implementation plans, respectively.</p>
<p>Originality value – The relationships among the variables studied are a subject that has not been reviewed in the literature previously.</p>','["Lean manufacturing, Service Quality, customer loyalty, Textile & Leather Industries."]',NULL,23,1,'01 - 11','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1370','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',1),
(1371,1371,1,'The Influence of Green Supply Chain Management Practices on Sustainability Performance in the Sultanate of Oman Industries: The Mediating Role of Environmental Collaboration','<p>Green supply chain management (GSCM) has become a strategic requirement for industrial firms seeking to reduce environmental harm while improving long-term sustainability performance. Despite growing interest in green procurement, eco-design, green manufacturing, and green distribution, limited empirical evidence explains how these practices are converted into sustainability performance through collaborative environmental action in the Sultanate of Oman. Guided by the Natural Resource-Based View, this study examines the effects of four GSCM practices on environmental collaboration and investigates the mediating role of environmental collaboration in the relationship between GSCM practices and sustainability performance. A quantitative cross-sectional survey was conducted among procurement, manufacturing, logistics, and supply chain professionals working in Omani industries. From 520 questionnaires distributed, 342 valid responses were retained for analysis. The data were analyzed using Smart PLS 4.0 and partial least squares structural equation modelling. The measurement model demonstrated acceptable reliability, convergent validity, and discriminant validity. The structural results show that eco-design, green procurement, green manufacturing, and green distribution have significant positive effects on environmental collaboration. Environmental collaboration, in turn, has a strong positive effect on sustainability performance. The indirect effects also support the mediation of the relationships between the GSCM practices and the sustainability performance, confirming the mediation chain. The results indicated that green practices are not enough; they have to be integrated with the collaboration of suppliers, customers, logistics and other functions within the company to make green practices produce results in terms of performance. The study adds to GSCM and sustainability literature by providing a relational mechanism that determines the contribution of green practices to the industrial sustainability of the Oman context.</p>','["Green Supply Chain Management, Environmental Collaboration, Sustainability Performance, Eco-design, Green procurement, Green manufacturing, Green distribution, Oman."]',NULL,23,1,'12 - 31','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1371','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',2),
(1372,1372,1,'Validation of a Policy Framework for Support Programs of Iraqi Sports Organizations to Promote the Marketing of Olympic Sports: A Case Study of Swimming','<p>Introduction: The sports industry, particularly Olympic sports, requires systematic support programs for the development of marketing activities. This study aimed to design a policy model for supportive programs of sports organizations in Iraq regarding marketing activities in Olympic sports, with a case study of swimming.</p>
<p>Methodology: This study employed a sequential exploratory mixed-method approach. In the qualitative phase, the systematic grounded theory method was utilized, and data were collected through semi-structured interviews with 22 experts. In the quantitative phase, the extracted model was tested using a researcher-made questionnaire and structural equation modeling with Smart-PLS software on a sample of 384 participants.</p>
<p>Findings: The final model indicated that supportive policymaking (the core phenomenon) comprises two main categories: "supportive programs" and "marketing activities." This phenomenon is influenced by causal conditions (managerial, economic, socio-cultural, and educational factors). The extracted strategies are shaped by contextual and intervening conditions, leading to outcomes such as swimming development, strengthening international relations, and socio-economic transformations. The quantitative results also confirmed the model''s fit (GOF=0.433).</p>
<p>Conclusion: The proposed model can serve as a guiding framework for Iraqi sports policymakers towards systematic development of marketing and attracting effective support in Olympic sports.</p>','["Supportive Programs, Sports Marketing, Swimming, Policymaking, Grounded Theory, Iraq."]',NULL,23,1,'32 - 39','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1372','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',3),
(1373,1373,1,'Digital Leadership and Organizational Resilience in Iraqi Organizations: The Mediating Role of Knowledge-Sharing Behavior','<p>The context of Iraq is very unique and so I believe that it would be a great setting to study these phenomena. These phenomena include digital leadership, knowledge sharing and organizational resilience. Apparently, the terrain is rather contradictory as it still is a conflict but in some parts also a post-conflict area. Since around 2003, it can be classified as a post-conflict economy. Most importantly, it is both a developing economy and a conflict economy at the same time. According to UNDP 2003 classification, however, it can be considered as least developed economy. Furthermore, it is at once a resource-rich economy and institutionally weak economy, at the same time. In addition, the economy is simultaneously complex due to culture as well as an institution. The nexus of chaos, refuge, and business exists. The aim of this study is to tease out how through ensuring knowledge sharing, digital leadership can contribute to the resilience of an organisation, from the perspective of employees and managers working within Iraqi organizations.</p>
<p>An analysis of the descriptive statistics reliability and validity checks are carried out using SPSS 28. Based on the research findings, employees'' knowledge-sharing behaviour is affected positively and significantly by digital leadership. In addition, Digital leadership has a significantly indirect effect on resilience through knowledge sharing. So, we can see that knowledge sharing could be a possible mediator.</p>
<p> The final model explains 48 per cent of the variance of the organisational resilience. Research into how digitally savvy leaders are capable of stimulating knowledge sharing that leads to organization adaptability, capacity and continuity has significance on the theory and practice of digital leadership. Based on the theoretical and empirical conclusion, the paper presents ramifications for Iraqi leaders and policy-makers who wish to address their digital leadership development and knowledge management practices.</p>','["Digital Leadership", "Organizational Resilience", "Knowledge-Sharing Behavior", "Organizational Performance", "Knowledge Management", "Digital Transformation", "Iraqi Organizations", "Organizational Agility."]',NULL,23,1,'40 - 53','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1373','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',4),
(1374,1374,1,'Lie Symmetry Analysis and Conservation Laws of the Topp–Leone Exponential Distribution','<p>This study investigates the Lie symmetry analysis of the Topp–Leone exponential (TLE) distribution in the context of Caputo fractional derivatives. Then, the TLE distribution is used to perform a description of the dynamic behavior of viscoelastic materials including memory effects. Utilizing the Lie symmetry method, we obtain the associated symmetry generators, implement symmetry reductions and analyze the TLE distribution invariant features and conservation laws. The symmetry group underlying (1) may be found using a fractional modification of the prolongation formula for integer-order derivatives, which enables the construction of precise similarity solutions. It also presents illustrative figures for selected explicit solutions. In addition, conservation laws are formed using Ibragimov’s theorem (even in the absence of a classical Lagrangian). These results confirm the power of Lie symmetry methods for fractional differential equations as well contribute to a deeper understanding of the structural and solution behaviour of the TLE model as described by its probability density function.</p>','["Lie symmetry analysis, Conservation laws, Topp\\u2013Leone exponential distribution, Ibragimov\\u2019s theorem, Probability density function (PDF), Differential equations in statistics, Reliability modeling"]',NULL,23,1,'54 - 62','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1374','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',5),
(1375,1375,1,'EquiAI: A Vision Foundation Model for Fair and Robust Cross-Demographic Remote Identity Verification','<p>Remote identity verification has become a critical component of autonomous agents deployed in smart healthcare, intelligent transportation, digital finance, and secure access control systems. Recent advances in Vision Foundation Models (VFMs) have significantly improved facial representation learning; however, their deployment remains challenged by demographic bias, which leads to inconsistent verification performance across different age groups, genders, ethnicities, and skin tones. Such disparities reduce the reliability, fairness, and trustworthiness of AI-enabled biometric systems, particularly in safety-critical applications where accurate identity verification is essential. This study proposes EquiAI, a robust fairness-aware remote identity verification framework that integrates a pretrained Vision Foundation Model (DINOv2), fairness-aware representation learning, adaptive feature alignment, presentation attack detection, and explainable artificial intelligence into a unified architecture. The proposed framework employs transformer-based feature extraction to learn generalized facial embeddings while minimizing demographic disparities and enhancing resistance against spoofing attacks. Experimental evaluation demonstrates that EquiAI achieves a verification accuracy of 98.21%, an F1-score of 97.94%, an ROC-AUC of 0.991, and an Equal Error Rate of 1.82%, while maintaining balanced performance across diverse demographic groups and supporting real-time inference. These findings demonstrate that integrating Vision Foundation Models with fairness-aware optimization and explainable AI substantially improves the robustness, equity, and practical deployment of remote identity verification systems, providing a scalable foundation for trustworthy autonomous agent authentication in real-world environments.</p>','["Vision Foundation Models (VFMs), Remote Identity Verification, Cross-Demographic Fairness, Face Recognition, Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI), Autonomous Agents."]',NULL,23,1,'63 - 80','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1375','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',6),
(1376,1376,1,'High-Gain Compact Terahertz Fractal Antenna Design for Next-Generation Wireless Applications','<p>The structure of a University-affiliated hotel, reflecting its dual commercial–pedagogical mandate, makes prioritising improvements to the hotel''s operational processes, based on discipline and scientific evidence, more complex and significant than in other types of hotels. The study builds on this work to create an integrated decision analytic framework to turn evidence from multiple stakeholder surveys into a defensible prioritization of operational improvements and applies this framework at the Pangasinian State University (PSU) Golden Lion Hotel in the Philippines. Eight dimensions of operation were rated by 400 respondents who were managers, staff/interns, and guests. The performance gap, derived importance (with respect to overall guest satisfaction) and the percentage of dissatisfied evaluations are used to fill a decision matrix, which is ranked objectively using Shannon entropy and classified objectively using TOPSIS, and complemented by the importance–performance analysis and a CART classification model. The dominant improvement priority (closeness coefficient 0.866) is service quality, and the CART model achieves a ten-fold cross-validated accuracy of 91.0% while using only about 98.0% of the predictive information for this priority, as compared to the modal baseline of 52.5%. The weakest performing dimensions are sustainability practices (weighted mean 1.89) and technology integration (2.17), and they are both ranked second and third, respectively, based on equal criterion weights. It provides a transparent framework for hotel decision makers with a replicable pipeline from stakeholder perceptions to an operational investment ordering, and it adds an application of objective-weight multi-criteria decision-making and machine learning in the relatively under-studied university-hotel field.</p>','["multi-criteria decision-making", "TOPSIS", "entropy weighting", "decision tree", "hotel operations management", "service quality."]',NULL,23,1,'81 - 95','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1376','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',7),
(1377,1377,1,'Exploring Innovation and Change Management as Drivers of Employee Performance and Adaptability','<p>Higher education institutions are increasingly experiencing rapid technological and organizational transformation that is continuously reshaping employee roles and institutional performance. Organizations are facing challenges in maintaining employee performance while implementing innovation and organizational change. This issue is becoming critical because ineffective change implementation and limited employee adaptability are reducing organizational effectiveness and slowing institutional development. This study is examining the influence of innovation practices on employee performance while investigating the mediating roles of organizational change management and employee adaptability among employees of Indian public universities. A quantitative, cross-sectional approach is being adopted, and data are being collected from 402 employees using a structured questionnaire. Hierarchical regression and PROCESS mediation analysis are being applied to evaluate the proposed relationships. The findings indicate that innovation practices are significantly improving employee performance through organizational change management and employee adaptability. These findings extend existing knowledge by demonstrating the combined mediating effects of organizational change management and employee adaptability and are providing practical evidence for strengthening innovation-driven performance in higher education institutions.</p>','["Innovation Practices", "Organizational Change Management", "Employee Adaptability", "Employee Performance", "Higher Education Institutions"]',NULL,23,1,'96 - 107','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1377','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',8),
(1378,1378,1,'An Explainable AI Framework for Enhancing Pedagogical Decision-Making Through Learning Analytics','<p>Learning analytics, as well as Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI), have emerged as key approaches for improving educational decision-making by analyzing student interaction data and providing interpretable insights. The current study focuses on developing an explainable AI framework to enhance pedagogical decision-making through learning analytics using the EdNet-KT3/KT4 dataset. The proposed method integrates machine learning models with SHAP and LIME techniques to predict student performance and generate transparent explanations of learner behavior. The framework is assessed using a variety of performance indicators to guarantee reliability and interpretability. The main result demonstrates that the suggested model attains an accuracy of 96.12%, outperforming traditional models while maintaining full explainability. The findings demonstrate that the integration of XAI improves both prediction performance and pedagogical usability by enabling instructors to identify at-risk learners and support data-driven interventions. The study concludes that explainable learning analytics provides an effective and trustworthy approach for modern educational environments and adaptive learning systems.</p>','["Explainable AI, Learning Analytics, Pedagogical Decision-Making, EdNet Dataset, Student Performance Prediction, SHAP, LIME"]',NULL,23,1,'108 - 119','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1378','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',9),
(1379,1379,1,'Modeling Student–Project–Skill Interactions Using Graph Neural Networks for Enhanced Learning Analytics','<p>Learning analytics is increasingly used to understand learner behavior, predict academic performance, and support personalized interventions. In contrast, traditional methods of machine learning algorithms assume that the learner is an isolated observation without considering the relational structure of educational scenarios. To overcome this problem, this paper presents an innovative solution in the form of a Student-Activity-Skill Graph Neural Network (SAS-GNN) model that captures the relationship between the learner, activity, and skill by representing them with a heterogeneous graph. SAS-GNN combines graph convolution, relation-aware message passing, graph attention mechanism, and the GNNExplainer technique to provide solutions to the task of predicting the outcomes of learning and interventions. When evaluated on the Open University Learning Analytics Data Set (OULAD) with more than 10 million interactions of learners, SAS-GNN attained 92.4%, 0.915, and 0.946 accuracies, F1-scores, and ROC-AUC scores, respectively. The results highlight the effectiveness of graph-based learning analytics in delivering accurate, interpretable, and actionable educational insights.</p>','["learning analytics", "graph neural networks", "educational data mining", "student performance prediction", "heterogeneous graphs", "explainable artificial intelligence", "OULAD", "personalized learning"]',NULL,23,1,'120 - 130','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1379','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',10),
(1380,1380,1,'The Influence of AI-Generated Health Information on Medical Consultation Decision-Making Among Adults: A Systematic Review','<p>Background: The emergence of large language models (LLMs) such as ChatGPT has transformed how adults access and interpret health information, with approximately 48% of consumers now using generative AI for health-related inquiries. However, the influence of AI-generated health information on medical consultation decision-making remains poorly synthesized. Objective: This systematic review synthesizes evidence on how AI-generated health information influences medical consultation decision-making among adults, examining demographic characteristics of AI users, types of AI tools used, outcomes on consultation behaviour, and moderating factors. Methods: A comprehensive search of PubMed/MEDLINE, Scopus, Web of Science, Cochrane CENTRAL, PsycINFO, IEEE Xplore, and ACM Digital Library was conducted for studies published between 2021 and 2026. Two independent reviewers screened 2,604 records, with 13 studies meeting inclusion criteria. Data extraction and quality assessment were performed using JBI, AXIS, ROBINS-I, and MMAT tools. A narrative synthesis approach was adopted. Results: AI tools significantly influence consultation decisions, with 47.4% of users employing AI to determine consultation necessity, 35.6% requesting referrals, and 31% modifying medications. AI demonstrates potential benefits including improved consultation efficiency (40% reduction in waiting times) and high-quality information provision, yet safety concerns persist with fewer than 40% of AI responses deemed harmless. Patients consistently prefer physician-led care, with AI serving as a complementary tool. Digital health literacy and younger age predict AI engagement, raising equity concerns. Conclusions: AI-generated health information significantly shapes medical consultation behaviour, necessitating risk-stratified deployment strategies, clinician guidance frameworks, and interventions to ensure equitable access and preserve the physician-patient relationship.</p>','["artificial intelligence", "large language models", "ChatGPT", "medical consultation", "health information seeking", "patient decision-making"]',NULL,23,1,'131 - 149','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1380','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',11),
(1383,1383,1,'Intelligent Web Localization: Enhancing Information Accessibility and Digital Inclusion Through Akeanon Translation','<p>As digital ecosystems expand globally, intelligent web localization is increasingly critical for ensuring information accessibility and digital inclusion among low-resource language communities. While dominant languages often dictate internet infrastructure, native dialects remain vital for localized information processing and cultural identity. This study investigates the strategic need for and potential impact of developing an online Akeanon translation system designed to bridge the digital divide for both Aklanon and non-Aklanon speakers in the Philippines. Employing a user-centric descriptive-survey design, empirical data were gathered from 100 respondents—encompassing students and non-native speakers—to evaluate user requirements for localized digital tools. Analysis revealed a significant system gap, with 71% of participants recognizing the critical necessity of an additional, dedicated application to supplement existing Akeanon translation frameworks. Both quantitative and qualitative findings demonstrated that intelligent web localization enhances user engagement in digital content creation, while providing essential infrastructural support for the education and tourism sectors. Furthermore, qualitative responses highlighted the technology''s crucial role in facilitating daily communicative decision-making. The findings establish that deploying localized translation technology is a vital mechanism for sustaining linguistic heritage while fostering inclusive digital spaces. Consequently, the study recommends the architectural development of robust mobile and web-based Akeanon translators, alongside their strategic integration into educational curricula and regional digital frameworks, ensuring equitable information access and the continued relevance of the Akeanon language in the digital age.</p>','["Intelligent Web Localization, Digital Inclusion, Information Accessibility, Low-Resource Languages, Machine Translation, Akeanon Language."]',NULL,23,1,'150-155','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1383','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',12),
(1384,1384,1,'Determinants of Tourist Satisfaction and Revisit Intention: An Integrated Service Quality Perspective','<p>Touristcontentment and return propensity have been demonstrated to have a major impact on destination competitiveness and sustainable tourism development. Few studies have combined service quality, perceived worth, experience quality, destination image, and destination credibility into a single conceptual model to examine how these factors affect overall visitor satisfaction and desire to come back, even though these concepts have all been thoroughly studied separately. As part of the ongoing study effort to look into the combined An Integrated Service Quality Perspective is suggested to fill this vacuum in the literature about the effects on tourist satisfaction and intention to return of service quality, destination image, experience quality, perceived value, and destination trust. Indicator reliability, The Fornell-Larcker criterion, Cronbach''s alpha, composite reliability, average variance extracted, and HTMT were utilized to evaluate the measurement model''s validity; bootstrapping,  structural model was assessed using path coefficient, coefficient of determination (R2), predictive relevance (Q2), effect size (f2), and importance-performance map analysis (IPMA). The overall impact of high-quality services is determined to be crucial in raising visitor satisfaction, which positively influences their propensity to visit, according to the research findings.The current paradigm offers a strong theoretical foundation for destination loyalty and can be regarded as a good explanation of tourist conduct.</p>','["Tourist Satisfaction, Destination Image,Sustainable Tourism,Perceived Value,Destination Image,PLS-SEM,Integrated Service Quality."]',NULL,23,1,'156-167','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1384','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',13),
(1385,1385,1,'Influence of Experiential Tourism on Destination Brand Loyalty: Evidence from Emerging Markets','<p>Experiential tourism has emerged as a central strategy through which destinations in emerging markets differentiate themselves and cultivate long-term visitor commitment. Although destination image, tourist engagement, and destination trust have each been studied individually as antecedents of loyalty, comparatively little empirical work has examined how these mechanisms jointly translate multi-dimensional tourist experiences into destination brand loyalty within the specific context of emerging-market destinations. To close this gap, the present study develops and tests an Experiential Tourism-Brand Loyalty framework that integrates five experiential dimensions (sense, feel, think, act, relate) with destination brand image (DBI), tourist engagement (TE) and destination brand trust (DBT) as mediating mechanisms leading to destination brand loyalty (DBL). Data is gathered through structured questionnaire administered to tourists visiting destinations across emerging economies and examined utilizing Partial Least Squares Structural Equation Modelling (PLS-SEM). Reliability of the measurement approach confirmed by indicator loadings, Cronbach''s alpha, composite reliability, average variance extracted, the Fornell-Larcker criterion, and heterotrait-monotrait ratio, while the structural model is assessed under path coefficients, the coefficient of determination (R²), predictive relevance (Q²), effect size (f²), and importance-performance map analysis (IPMA). The results show that the experiential tourism exerts a significant positive influence on DBI, TE and DBT, each of which, in turn, positively predicts destination brand loyalty; tourist engagement emerges as the strongest mediating pathway. This work contributes to the body of experiential marketing literature by expanding it to the emerging markets tourism scenario and provides practical insight for destination management organizations on creating distinctive tourist experiences.</p>','["Experiential Tourism, Destination Brand Loyalty, Tourist Engagement, PLS-SEM, Emerging Markets."]',NULL,23,1,'168-179','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1385','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',14),
(1386,1386,1,'Mobility-Robust Beamforming for Multi-Cell Integrated Sensing and Communication Using Temporal Federated Learning','<p>The development of next-generation wireless technologies heavily relies on Integrated sensing and communication (ISAC) for its ability to handle both data transfer and environmental awareness using a singular infrastructure, leveraging shared spectrum or hardware. However, multi-cell ISAC systems face issues with inter-cell interference, heterogeneous statistics from cell to cell, and user mobility on the time variance of the channel itself. Coordination of beamforming has reached a deadlock as federated learning (FL) and personalized-FL techniques only apply to the instantaneous channel state or snapshot of the channel. As a result of this process, FL techniques fail to account for what has been experienced through the channel over a long period of time. Due to this, FL and personalized FL techniques are unable to be applied to high-mobility regimes. In this paper, we propose a mobility aware Beamforming Temporal Federated Learning (TFL) framework for a Integrated Sensing and Communication (ISAC) multi-cell system. We introduce a novel method to leverage temporal sequence of prior communication/sensing channels as input to the federated learning framework allowing for adaptive and robust beamforming to the user''s mobility and movement of sensing targets and Doppler effect. Experimental findings indicate that TFL achieves quicker convergence and delivers a superior weighted ISAC utility for users, alongside improved communication and sensing performance, and a more potent blend of communication-sensing advantages compared to per-cell learning, standard federated learning, privacy-compromising federated learning, and mobility-aware federated learning approaches.</p>','["Beamforming, ISAC, Federated Leaning, Doppler effect, Temporal learning, Inter-cell Interference.."]',NULL,23,1,'180-192','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1386','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',15),
(1387,1387,1,'Development of Efficient Wavelet Scheme in DWT, FFT & DTCWT based OFDM Model for Underwater Communication','<p>The multipath propagation, Doppler spread, and channel distortion have negative impacts on the reliability of Underwater acoustic communication channels. Therefore, OFDM communication becomes unreliable because of these impairments. In this paper, a comparison will be made among FFT-OFDM, DWT OFDM, and a newly introduced DTCWT OFDM methods with regard to their performance for underwater acoustic communication on WATERMARK (NOF1). The simulations for the schemes have been modelled through MATLAB for various channel depths (&lt;10 m and 10 – 50 m) and values of Eb/No varying from 0 dB to 20 dB. Performance analysis of BER for the OFDM methods will be made in the presence of channel effect and in the absence of channel effect. It is evident from simulation results that the performances of all the three OFDM schemes are almost equal at low Eb/No ratio; however, there are marked differences at higher Eb/No ratio. For an Eb/No of 20 dB and less than 10 m water depth, the proposed DTCWT-OFDM gives a BER of 0.0188 whereas for FFT-OFDM and DWT-OFDM the BER is 0.0554 and 0.0491, respectively. If water depths are in between 10 m and 50 m, then the DTCWT-OFDM scheme gives a BER of 0.0261 which performs better than FFT-OFDM (BER= 0.0410) and DWT-OFDM (BER= 0.0490). This novel scheme results in 66% and 62% reduction in the BER when compared to FFT-OFDM and DWT-OFDM schemes, respectively.</p>','["BER, DTCWT-OFDM model ,Watermark underwater channel, OFDM model, underwater communication."]',NULL,23,1,'193-207','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1387','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',16);
<p>Three findings organize the results. First, the strongest evidence concerns market activity rather than broad repricing: firms that were more liquid before the reform experienced a statistically significant relative decline in turnover and trade counts during the reform window, with the sharpest adjustment concentrated in the mid-cap segment. Second, the relation between prior liquidity and subsequent returns is heterogeneous across the conditional return distribution rather than monotonic in the mean. Third, placebo, falsification, treatment-definition, and clustering exercises all support the interpretation that the reform window coincided with a redistribution of trading attention away from previously dominant names and toward a broader cross-section of firms.</p>
<p>The paper contributes to the literature on capital markets, market microstructure, and market-access reforms in emerging economies by showing that liberalization may first appear in the allocation of trading activity before it appears in average-return differentials. The evidence is therefore most informative about trading-activity reallocation, while causal interpretation remains cautious because treatment is not exogenous, the transition begins before full implementation, and the post-reform horizon is short.</p>','["market access liberalization, trading activity, liquidity reallocation, Tadawul, market microstructure, emerging markets"]',NULL,23,1,'346-368','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1397','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',25),
(1405,1405,1,'Integrating Accounting Governance, Cybersecurity Governance and Digital Trust for Digital Banking Risk Reduction in Jordanian Banks: A Conceptual Framework for Digital Banking Risk Governance','<p>Beginning with this paper''s overall objective is to develop a comprehensive theoretical framework that illustrates how Jordanian banks reduce digital banking risk (DBR) through two distinct but complementary paths: digital trust (DT) and operational resilience (OR). As such, this paper will expand upon the traditional focus on channel-specific risks associated with e-banking by providing an alternative ecosystem-based perspective on digital banking risk governance. This paper uses a structured conceptual synthesis methodology. In doing so, it synthesizes four separate literature streams including those related to cybersecurity governance; digital trust-risk logic in digital financial services; API security and third-party risk management; and operational resilience. To facilitate this process, studies were selected based on relevance for developing theory, clarity regarding constructs, applicability to banking or financial service institutions, and the potential to inform development of specific propositions. Additionally, two institutional sources located in Jordan were relied upon to provide additional contextualization of the proposed conceptual model in terms of the Jordanian banking environment. The paper presents a multi-layered conceptual model illustrating the role that cybersecurity governance plays in supporting both DT and OR, and ultimately reducing DBR. Specifically, four governance capability domains (API security, customer digital awareness, third-party risk management, and incident response capability) are identified as critical domains of practice that link high-level cybersecurity governance practices to tangible reductions in digital banking risk. The paper provides value by integrating previously separate concepts (cybersecurity governance, DT, TPRM, API security, CDA, IRC, and OR) into a cohesive conceptual architecture designed to facilitate understanding of factors that contribute to reductions in DBR. Further, the paper shifts the emphasis from viewing DBR as being primarily attributable to narrow technological or consumer behavioral issues (e.g., Internet banking channels) to viewing DBR as an issue of broader ecosystem governance. From an accounting information systems and internal control lens, the framework also positions digital banking risk as a problem of transaction authorization, auditability, control monitoring, exception reporting, and assurance over digitally processed banking activities.</p>','["Cybersecurity governance", "Digital trust", "Digital banking risk reduction", "Operational resilience", "API security", "Third-party risk management"]',NULL,23,1,'369-390','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1405','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',26),
(1406,1406,1,'Human Resource Strategies as Governance-Enabling Mechanisms for SME Financing Outcomes: Evidence from Jordanian SMEs','<p>This study examines how human resource strategies contribute to financing outcomes among small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs) in Jordan. Drawing on the resource-based view, agency theory, information asymmetry theory, and SME financing literature, the study conceptualizes human resource strategies as internal organizational capabilities that may strengthen governance-related capabilities and enhance the credibility of SMEs in external financing relationships. Data were collected through a structured survey administered to 159 SME managers and decision-makers in Jordan. The study applies partial least squares structural equation modeling (PLS-SEM) and supplementary robustness diagnostics based on construct scores. The findings indicate that human resource strategies are positively associated with SME financing outcomes and with the development of strategic HR capabilities, governance knowledge, and governance leadership. The originally tested moderation effect of governance capabilities is not supported in the PLS-SEM results. Additional mediation-oriented diagnostics suggest that governance-related capabilities and strategic HR capabilities provide a more theoretically plausible explanation of how human resource strategies are linked to financing outcomes. The study contributes to SME finance and governance research by clarifying how internal organizational capabilities may reduce information asymmetry and support financing access in an emerging economy context. The findings should be interpreted cautiously because the study is cross-sectional and relies on self-reported survey data.</p>','["Human resource strategies", "strategic HR capabilities", "governance knowledge", "governance leadership", "SME financing", "Jordan", "PLS-SEM", "emerging economies"]',NULL,23,1,'391-407','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1406','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',27),
(1407,1407,1,'From Artificial Intelligence Capabilities to Financial Innovation in FinTech-Oriented Digital Ecosystems: The Mediating Role of Digital Entrepreneurship','<p>Artificial intelligence (AI) has become a strategic capability for enhancing innovation within FinTech-oriented digital ecosystems. However, the mechanisms through which AI capabilities are associated with financial innovation remain insufficiently understood. This study examines the relationship between AI capabilities and financial innovation by investigating the mediating role of digital entrepreneurship. Rather than if AI adoption automatically leads to financial innovation, the study proposes that digital entrepreneurship represents an important organizational mechanism through which AI-enabled analytics, automation, prediction, and intelligent decision-support capabilities are translated into innovation outcomes. A quantitative cross-sectional research design was employed, and data were collected from 159 respondents involved in technology-enabled business, entrepreneurial, and financial activities in Jordan. Partial least squares structural equation modeling (PLS-SEM) was used to evaluate both the measurement and structural models. The findings indicate that AI capabilities are positively associated with digital entrepreneurship, while digital entrepreneurship is positively associated with financial innovation. AI capabilities also demonstrate a smaller but statistically significant direct association with financial innovation. Furthermore, digital entrepreneurship partially mediates this relationship, highlighting its role in strengthening the translation of AI-enabled capabilities into innovation outcomes. These findings suggest that organizations achieve stronger financial innovation not merely through AI adoption but through their ability to combine AI capabilities with entrepreneurial opportunity recognition, digital experimentation, and commercialization activities. The study contributes to FinTech, artificial intelligence, and digital entrepreneurship literature by providing empirical evidence from an emerging digital economy and by demonstrating how entrepreneurial processes help explain the relationship between AI capabilities and financial innovation.</p>','["artificial intelligence", "FinTech", "financial technology", "digital entrepreneurship", "financial innovation", "AI for financial services", "digital ecosystems", "emerging economies", "Jordan", "PLS-SEM"]',NULL,23,1,'408-431','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1407','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',28),
(1408,1408,1,'A Memory-Allocated Variable-Order Stochastic Volterra Model for Financial Time Series','<p>A causal variable-order stochastic Volterra model is proposed for financial time series. Three bounded indicators–volatility, directional persistence, and trend intensity–are combined into a market-information score. The score generates a preferred kernel exponent, while a strictly convex allocation rule regularizes that exponent toward a reference value and its previous allocation. The finite-grid indicator–allocation map is proved globally Lipschitz, and the continuous allocation history is represented by an adapted causal functional. Under bounded globally Lipschitz coefficients, local mean-square existence, uniqueness, a moment bound, and continuous dependence on the initial value are established. A causal product-integration method with common Brownian increments is developed and verified through constant-order, positivity, subgrid-refinement, and grid-consistency experiments. The empirical procedure uses profile Gaussian estimation after triangular Volterra inversion, a prespecified 32-candidate validation grid, and rolling conditional forecasts. An illustrative application to 249 daily NIFTY 50 observations from 2025 shows that the model is not competitive at one day. At five days it improves on the random walk and geometric Brownian motion and attains the best probabilistic score, although a constant-order model has slightly lower RMSE. At ten days it has the smallest RMSE and CRPS among the evaluated models, with RMSE reductions of 14.08% relative to the random walk and 21.16% relative to geometric Brownian motion. Diebold–Mariano tests are not significant, so the financial evidence is preliminary rather than conclusive.</p>','["stochastic Volterra equation", "variable order", "financial memory", "product integration", "rolling forecast", "NIFTY 50."]',NULL,23,1,'432-463','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1408','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',29),
(1409,1409,1,'Pathological Voice Classification Using Empirical Mode Decomposition and Ensemble Empirical Mode Decomposition with Extreme Learning Machine','<p>Pathological voice analysis has been proved as an important computer aided diagnostic approach for early identification of laryngeal disorders using sustained phonation recordings. Due to the highly non-linear and non-stationary nature of speech signals, traditional spectral features often fail to capture subtle changes related to pathological vocal fold vibration. In this paper, we propose a hybrid feature extraction framework by combining Empirical Mode Decomposition (EMD) and Ensemble Empirical Mode Decomposition (EEMD) to extract discriminative time-frequency representations from sustained vowel (/a/) recordings of the VOICED database. The decomposed intrinsic mode functions are further characterized by instantaneous energy and instantaneous frequency obtained from Hilbert spectral analysis and statistical descriptors that preserve temporal and spectral characteristics of healthy and pathological voices. The resulting feature vectors are classified using an Extreme Learning Machine (ELM) due to its fast learning, good generalization performance and low computational complexity. The experimental evaluation is carried out using standard performance measures such as accuracy, sensitivity, specificity, precision and F1-score. The hybrid EMD–EEMD feature set can provide complementary information to the individual decomposition methods and can mitigate mode-mixing effects, which demonstrates better performance than each individual decomposition method. The proposed EMD+EEMD-ELM framework obtained the overall classification accuracy of 98.74%, sensitivity, specificity, precision and F1-score of 98.42%, 99.05%, 98.63% and 98.52% respectively on the VOICED dataset under the adopted experimental protocol.</p>','["Pathological voice analysis", "Empirical Mode Decomposition (EMD)", "Ensemble Empirical Mode Decomposition (EEMD)", "Hybrid feature extraction", "Hilbert spectral analysis", "Extreme Learning Machine (ELM)", "Laryngeal disorder detection", "VOICED database."]',NULL,23,1,'464-485','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1409','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',30),
(1410,1410,1,'Digital Investment Participation and Wealth Creation: A Bibliometric Analysis of Long-Term Financial Resilience Research','<p>This paper presents a bibliometric analysis of research at the intersection of digital investment platforms (trading apps, Robo-advisors, digital brokerages, crypto exchanges) and app-based wealth management and their relationship to long-term wealth creation and financial resilience, in an attempt to map the intellectual structure and future trajectory of the field. The study adopts the four-step bibliometric approach of Donthu et al. (2021) to establish the scope of the field, identify performance analysis and science-mapping techniques (co-citation analysis, bibliographic coupling and co-word analysis), and to propose a reproducible data-collection protocol using Scopus and Web of Science Core Collection for 2010–2026, recommending VOSviewer, Bibliometrix/Biblioshiny and CiteSpace for implementation. The quantitative bibliometric run has not been completed, so citation counts, network statistics and algorithmically derived clusters are not reported. A qualitative reading of 2020–2026 literature suggests the field may provisionally organise around five recurring themes – digital financial inclusion, Robo-advisory platforms, financial and electronic literacy as antecedents of resilient investment behaviour, household-level financial resilience and the behavioural and regulatory consequences of gamified trading-app design – offered as a plausibility check to be tested against future quantitative analysis rather than as a conventional finding. The originality of the paper rests on its being one of the first to situate digital investment participation within the wealth-creation and financial-resilience literatures, rather than as a standalone fintech silo. It offers both a fully reproducible methodological template and a provisional narrative synthesis to serve as an a priori hypothesis for empirical testing in subsequent research.</p>','["Digital investment participation", "Wealth creation", "Financial resilience", "Bibliometric analysis", "Robo-advisors", "Fintech", "Digital financial inclusion."]',NULL,23,1,'486-506','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1410','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',31),
(1411,1411,1,'Qualitative Insights into Retail Investors'' Perception of the Robo-Advisory Landscape in India','<p>Purpose – This study intends to discuss the attitude of the retail investors towards the robo-advisory services in India. The research focuses on the factors related to investors'' awareness, trust perception, perceived benefits, perceived risks, and behavioral barriers that affect the adoption of AI-powered financial consulting platforms.</p>
<p>Methodology – In terms of method, the current research was qualitative research in which semi-structured interview method was used. The data collected were transcribed and analysed by content analysis. Coding processes were also used to identify key themes of investors'' attitudes towards robo-advisory services</p>
<p>Findings– The findings suggest that investors may find robo-advisory to be convenient, easy to use, and affordable but concerns remain regarding trust, data privacy, technology risk, uncertain returns, and lack of human connection have an impact on the adoption behaviour of investors. There were two standout issues among some investors: familiarity bias and lack of willingness to embrace technology innovation.</p>
<p>Practical implications – The results indicate that the following measures need to be taken to promote the adoption of robo-advisory services in India: (i) enhancing investor awareness, (ii) transparency, (iii) boosting cybersecurity protection and (iv) hybrid advising.</p>
<p>Originality/value – The present study forms a part of the few qualitative studies that have looked at the perception of retail investors on the concept of robo-advisory services in the Indian context. This study expands on a large volume of research on the adoption of finance technology, behavioural finance and AI-driven financial advisory services.</p>','["Robo-advisory services, FinTech, Retail investors, Artificial intelligence, Qualitative study, India"]',NULL,23,1,'507-527','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1411','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',32),
(1412,1412,1,'Self-Attention Based Progressive Generative Adversarial Network optimized with Aquila Optimization Algorithm for Sentimental Analysis of Fake News Detection','<p>Fake news on social media is increased in recent years. This has led to the requirement for efficient algorithms for detecting fake news. This research proposes SAPGAN-AOA-FND, a novel framework combining a self-attention-based progressive generative adversarial network (SAPGAN) enhanced using the Aquila optimization algorithm (AOA) tailored specifically for fake news identification. The proposed methodology encompasses four key steps: data collection, data pre-processing, feature extraction, and detection. Initially, a fake news dataset is sourced from Kaggle fake news dataset, followed by a rigorous pre-processing phase involving tokenization, stop-word removal, filtering, lemmatization, hashtag removal, lowercasing, stemming, and multi-word grouping. Subsequently, a lexicon hybrid N-gram2vector model is employed for comprehensive feature extraction, encompassing content level, user level, social level, local, and global features. The extracted features are provided to the self-attention-based progressive generative adversarial network (SAPGAN) model, which typically lacks explicit optimization techniques for ensuring accurate fake news detection. To address this gap, the AOA is introduced to enhnce the weight parameters of the SAPGAN technique, enhancing its discriminative capabilities. Furthermore, the proposed approach conducts sentiment analysis to discern positive and negative sentiments within real along fake news. It observes a prevalence of positive sentiment in real news and a greater occurrence of negative sentiment in fake news. The efficiency of SAPGAN-AOA-FND is rigorously evaluated utilizing performance metrics including sensitivity, precision, recall, f-measure, specificity, accuracy, and computational complexity. The proposed technique offers 20.67%, 33.89% and 24.67% higher accuracy and 28.76%, 30.67% and 34.89% higher specificity comparing with existing techniques like Fake news detection using cross modal attention residual with multichannel convolutional neural networks (CARMN-FND), Fake news detection using capsule neural networks (CapNN-FND) and Convolutional neural network utilizing margin loss for fake news detection (CNN-FND), respectively.</p>','["avoid too general and too specific keywords", "CDF Letters (Please use semicolon as separator)"]',NULL,23,1,'528-542','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1412','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',33),
(1413,1413,1,'Artificial Intelligence for Learning: Bridging Students’ Digital Competence and Literacy in Universities','<p>Introduction: The rapid diffusion of artificial intelligence (AI) tools into higher education has reshaped how students search, process, and produce knowledge, yet universities in decentralized regions of Indonesia continue to debate whether these tools strengthen or erode the digital competence and digital literacy that underpin responsible learning. Objective: This conceptual paper examines how AI-mediated learning can be understood as a bridge, rather than a substitute, between students’ digital competence and their digital literacy, and proposes an integrative framework for higher education contexts such as South Sulawesi, Indonesia. Methodology: Adopting a narrative and conceptual review approach, the paper synthesizes peer-reviewed literature published between 2020 and 2026 on AI in learning, digital competence frameworks (including DigCompEdu and related models), and digital literacy scholarship, triangulating these strands with recent Indonesian empirical studies on AI adoption in higher education. Results: The synthesis indicates that AI functions as a bridging mechanism when its use is scaffolded by explicit competence-building and critical literacy practices, but functions as a displacement mechanism when adopted without pedagogical structure, producing convenience without comprehension. Four bridging pathways are identified: procedural scaffolding, critical verification, reflective self-regulation, and ethical co-authorship. Discussion and Conclusions: The paper argues that Indonesian public universities, illustrated through the higher education landscape of South Sulawesi, are well positioned to operationalize this bridge through curriculum-embedded AI literacy modules, lecturer digital-competence development, and institutional policy anchored in Pancasila values of responsible, collective knowledge use. The proposed AI-Bridged Digital Competence and Literacy (ABDCL) framework offers a conceptual tool for future empirical validation rather than a report of field data. </p>','["Artificial intelligence", "digital competence", "digital literacy", "higher education", "AI literacy", "Indonesia.."]',NULL,23,1,'543-559','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1413','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',34),
(1414,1414,1,'Artificial Intelligence for Health Citizenship Education: Bridging Civic Competence and Physical Health Literacy in Universities','<p>The growing use of artificial intelligence (AI) in information environments requires higher education to equip students with the ability to critically evaluate AI-mediated health information and apply it to healthy behaviors, yet civic education and health education are rarely treated as interdependent despite sharing core capacities such as critical thinking, self-regulation, and responsible decision-making. This study examined whether participation in an AI-Assisted Health Citizenship Learning (AIHCL) program predicts university students'' civic competence and physical health literacy, and whether civic competence bridges the relationship between AI-assisted learning and health literacy. A sequential explanatory mixed-methods design was conducted with 210 undergraduate students from four universities in South Sulawesi, Indonesia. Quantitative data were analyzed using Structural Equation Modeling-Partial Least Squares (SEM-PLS), while qualitative data were collected through 20 semi-structured interviews and analyzed thematically. AIHCL engagement significantly predicted civic competence (beta = 0.59, p &lt; .001) and physical health literacy (beta = 0.47, p &lt; .001). Civic competence also significantly predicted physical health literacy (beta = 0.42, p &lt; .001), confirming a partial bridging effect. Four themes emerged from the qualitative data: AI as a Civic Information Filter, Digital Deliberation as Civic Practice, From Personal Data to Collective Health Responsibility, and Algorithmic Trust Under Critical Scrutiny. The findings indicate that AI-assisted instruction can simultaneously strengthen civic competence and physical health literacy, with civic competence serving as a critical bridge linking AI engagement to health outcomes. The study provides a validated model and a practical framework for AI-based health citizenship education in higher education. </p>','["Artificial intelligence", "civic competence", "health citizenship", "higher education", "physical health literacy."]',NULL,23,1,'560-575','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1414','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',35),
(1416,1416,1,'The Lived Experiences of Digital Learning Management in Homeschooling-Based Nonformal Education: A Phenomenological Study in Community Learning Centers','<p>The use of digital technology in nonformal education continues to evolve, particularly in supporting learning processes within homeschooling-based Community Learning Centers. However, learning activities are often managed through multiple non-integrated platforms, resulting in fragmented information, limited monitoring, and difficulties in making timely pedagogical and managerial decisions. This study aims to explore the lived experiences of tutors, administrators, and learners in managing digital learning and to examine how fragmented learning information influences decision-making within homeschooling-based nonformal education. A qualitative approach with a descriptive phenomenological design was employed. Thirty informants consisting of tutors, administrators, and learners were selected through purposive sampling based on their active involvement in digital learning. Data were collected through in-depth interviews, non-participant observation, and documentation and were analyzed through coding, theme development, and interpretation of the essence of participants’ experiences. The findings revealed four interconnected themes: fragmentation of digital learning platforms, difficulties in monitoring and evaluation, unstructured learning documentation, and the emerging need for an integrated digital learning information system. Fragmented information prevented tutors and administrators from obtaining complete and timely evidence regarding attendance, participation, assignment completion, and learning progress. Consequently, decisions concerning learner support, instructional adjustment, evaluation, and program management were frequently based on manually collected and incomplete information. The study concludes that digital integration should not be understood merely as a technical solution but as an information-management and pedagogical requirement for supporting evidence-informed decision-making. This study contributes a user-experience-based perspective for developing integrated digital learning information systems in homeschooling-based nonformal education.</p>','["digital learning management", "information fragmentation", "pedagogical decision-making", "integrated information system", "user experience", "nonformal education."]',NULL,23,1,'576-596','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1416','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',36),
(1417,1417,1,'Conceptual Design of a Project-Based Learning Model Based on Green Economy Based Entrepreneurship (GEbE) to Strengthen Students'' Environmental Literacy: An ADDIE Analyze–Design Study','<p>This article reports the Analyze and Design stages of a Research and Development (R&amp;D) study developing a Project-Based Learning model integrated with Green Economy Based Entrepreneurship (PjBL-GEbE) intended to strengthen the environmental literacy of undergraduate students. The background of this study is that entrepreneurship education in Indonesian higher education has not been sufficiently connected to environmental issues, so that graduates'' environmental literacy and green entrepreneurial readiness remain low, while environmental degradation and the green-economy transition demand a generation of environmentally responsive entrepreneurs. The problem addressed is how to describe and design a PjBL-GEbE model, together with its supporting learning devices and evaluation instruments, using the ADDIE framework (Analysis, Design, Development, Implementation, Evaluation). Following a literature review and a needs assessment conducted among Biology Education students, the Design stage produced a six-phase instructional syntax (Preparation, Project Launch, Exploration and Planning, Project Implementation, Presentation and Reflection, and Follow-Up), supported by a four-aspect assessment rubric (environmental literacy, creativity and innovation, collaboration and communication, and sustainability impact) and five authentic green-economy project themes. The model, its supporting devices, and an evaluation-instrument framework (content-validity, practicality, and effectiveness measures, including Gregory''s index and normalized gain) are proposed for a subsequent empirical study covering the Develop, Implement, and Evaluate stages of ADDIE. No empirical data on validity, practicality, or effectiveness were collected for this article, and no such figures are reported here. It is concluded that the PjBL-GEbE model constitutes a theoretically grounded conceptual design that offers a plausible instructional pathway for strengthening students'' environmental literacy in entrepreneurship-related courses, pending future empirical content-validation, practicality, and effectiveness testing.</p>','["Green Economy Based Entrepreneurship", "Project-Based Learning", "Environmental Literacy", "ADDIE Analyze\\u2013Design", "Higher Education", "Conceptual Model Development."]',NULL,23,1,'597-610','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1417','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',37),
(1418,1418,1,'Adaptive Graph Neural Network for Cross-Subject EEG Emotion Recognition Using Functional Connectivity','<p>Electroencephalography (EEG)-based emotion recognition has gained significant attention for applications in mental health monitoring and brain–computer interfaces; however, achieving ro- bust cross-subject generalization remains a major challenge due to high inter-subject variability. This study proposes an adaptive Graph Neural Network (GNN) framework for EEG-based emo- tion recognition, where EEG channels are modeled as graph nodes and functional connectivity is dynamically constructed using correlation-based relationships. Unlike conventional approaches that rely on fixed or fully connected graph structures, the proposed method generates window- wise adaptive graphs, enabling the model to capture time-varying and subject-invariant neural interactions.</p>
<p>EEG signals are segmented into overlapping temporal windows, and frequency-domain features are extracted using power spectral density across standard EEG bands. A Graph Convolutional Network (GCN) is employed to learn spatial dependencies, and the model is evaluated under a strict subject-independent protocol using Leave-One-Subject-Out (LOSO) cross-validation on the DEAP dataset.</p>
<p>Experimental results demonstrate a mean accuracy of 58.95% and a macro-F1 score of 0.438, highlighting the inherent difficulty of cross-subject EEG emotion recognition. Despite moderate accuracy, the proposed framework provides a more realistic evaluation compared to subject- dependent studies and establishes a robust baseline for generalizable emotion recognition. The findings confirm that adaptive graph construction improves functional brain connectivity representation while emphasizing the need for advanced generalization techniques such as domain adaptation and temporal modeling for future research.</p>','["Electroencephalography (EEG), Emo- tion Recognition, Graph Neural Net- works (GNN), Functional Connec- tivity, Cross-Subject Generalization, Leave-One-Subject-Out (LOSO), Deep Learning, Affective Computing."]',NULL,23,1,'611-625','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1418','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',38),
(1419,1419,1,'Servant Leadership, Human Capital, and Structural Capital in Improving Innovative Work Behavior of Elementary School Principals','<p>This research examines the link between servants'' leadership abilities and primary school leaders'' innovation behaviours. 214 headmasters in primary schools throughout the region were selected using stratified cluster sampling, and the survey instrument was administered via Google Forms. The data was analysed using Partial Least Squares–Structural Equation Modelling (PLS-SEM). The measurement model demonstrated strong reliability and validity, with composite reliability ranging from 0.83 to 0.91 and average variance extracted (AVE) exceeding 0.50. The structural model results showed that servant leadership had a significant direct effect on innovative work behaviour (β = 0.32, p &lt; 0.001). Furthermore, it significantly affected human capital (β = 0.58, p &lt; 0.001) and structural capital (β = 0.49, p &lt; 0.001). Human capital (β = 0.29, p &lt; 0.01) and structural capital (β = 0.27, p &lt; 0.01) were also found to have significant positive effects on innovative work behaviour. Bootstrapping was used to test mediation in the relationship between servant leadership and innovative behavior, and both human and structural capital were partially verified as mediators. The model explained 62% of the variance in school principals'' innovative work behaviors (R² = 0.62). This demonstrates an excellent predictive capability. These results offer theoretical benefits by integrating servant leadership and intellectual capital approaches in educational leadership studies. Furthermore, the practical outcomes of principal development programs, with particular emphasis on capacity building and a supportive organisational structure, are also discussed.</p>','["Servant Leadership", "Innovative Work Behavior", "Human Capital", "Structural Capital", "Educational Leadership."]',NULL,23,1,'626-638','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1419','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',39),
(1421,1421,1,'Populism: Excavating Meaning and Probing Depths','<p>This study examines populism as a complex political and sociological phenomenon that reflects deep crises within democratic representation, political mediation, and the relationship between state and society. It aims to deconstruct the conceptual and historical dimensions of populism, analyze the structural conditions contributing to its emergence, and explore its implications for democratic institutions, political participation, and social cohesion across different contexts.</p>
<p>The study adopts a qualitative sociological and political approach grounded in critical discourse analysis and historical-structural interpretation. It relies on interpretive and comparative analysis of academic literature in political sociology, political theory, critical social thought, and cultural studies. Particular attention is given to the theoretical contributions of Pierre Rosanvallon, whose work is used as a central analytical framework for understanding populism as a symptom of a crisis in democratic representation rather than a mere ideological deviation. The study also engages with the works of Pierre Bourdieu, Antonio Gramsci, and Cornelius Castoriadis to construct a multidimensional interpretation of the phenomenon.</p>
<p>The findings indicate that populism emerges in contexts characterized by weakened intermediary institutions, declining public trust, increasing inequality, and the expansion of emotionally driven media environments. Populist discourse typically simplifies social complexity into binary oppositions such as “the pure people” versus “the corrupt elite,” thereby undermining democratic pluralism and institutional accountability. The analysis further shows that populism is not confined to a single ideological orientation but appears in diverse forms across both Western and non-Western contexts.</p>
<p>The significance of this study lies in its attempt to provide a comprehensive theoretical framework for understanding populism beyond media-driven or reductionist interpretations. It contributes to contemporary debates on democracy, representation, and political legitimacy by linking populism to broader structural transformations in modern societies.</p>','["Populism", "Democratic Representation", "Political Sociology", "Political Discourse", "Democracy Crisis", "Political Mediation", "Arab World.."]',NULL,23,1,'639-653','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1421','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',40),
(1422,1422,1,'A Multi-Modal Cloud Architectures for Healthcare and Predictive Clinical Analytics','<p>The data in healthcare is highly heterogeneous and is continuously increasing from Electronic Health Records (EHRs), medical imaging, wearable Internet of Things (IoT) devices, laboratory systems, and genomic sequencing, which makes it difficult to support intelligent clinical decision making. Current cloud-based healthcare systems may lack sufficient interoperability, scalability, privacy protection, and integration with multiple modalities, due to various factors. The paper proposes a novel multi-modal cloud architecture, which combines edge computing, cloud data lakes, multimodal feature fusion, explainable artificial intelligence (XAI) and federated learning technologies to achieve secure and scalable predictive clinical analytics. The architecture uses deep learning techniques, such as Convolutional Neural Networks, Vision Transformers, Long Short-Term Memory networks, Graph Neural Networks, and multimodal Transformers, to process and analyse diverse healthcare data types. The superior disease prediction accuracy (99.21%), lower latency (42 ms), greater privacy preservation and greater clinical interpretability over existing healthcare frameworks are confirmed by the experimental evaluation performed on benchmark datasets, including MIMIC-IV, eICU, CheXpert, MIMIC-CXR, PhysioNet, BRATS, NIH Chest X-ray and UK Biobank.</p>','["Artificial Intelligence, AI-Driven Analytics, IoT, Predictive Analytics, Blockchain, Decision-Making, Cloud Computing, Healthcare Technology, Data Security, Telemedicine."]',NULL,23,1,'654-684','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1422','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',41);
<p>Design/methodology/approach – A survey was completed by 300 teenage students between aged 12 to 18 years. Structural Equation Modelling was used to test the research hypotheses.</p>
<p><strong>Findings</strong>: research shows a positive relationship between fashion innovativeness on Internet, attitude toward the website, fashion involvement, on the one hand, and fashion leadership, on the other hand. However, Internet familiarity was not associated with fashion leadership. Tunisian teen girls become fashion leaders when they are fashion involved and interested in websites novelty. Indeed, the latter provide them with the latest information about fashion. A positive attitude toward fashion websites and clothing products online enhances their fashion leadership.</p>
<p><strong>Practical implications:</strong> the article concludes with some suggestions that can be used by marketers to attract fashion leaders. More specifically, it recommends that companies can target fashion leaders by focusing their efforts on fashion innovativeness on Internet and on fashion involvement.</p>
<p><strong>Originality</strong>: fashion motivations in the Internet context are identified. Moreover, a direct relationship between Individual motivations and fashion leadership is examined.</p>','["Fashion leadership, Internet familiarity, Fashion innovativeness on Internet, Attitude toward the website, Fashion involvement."]',NULL,23,1,'716-742','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1427','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',44),
(1428,1428,1,'Coerced Transformation: Vision 2030 as an Institutional Pressure Field and the Organisational Dynamics of Imposed Digital Change in Saudi Public Sector','<p><strong>Purpose</strong>: This paper examines Vision 2030''s National Transformation Program as a coercive institutional pressure field that imposes digital transformation on Saudi public sector organisations regardless of their internal readiness, capacity, or willingness. Drawing on a qualitative case study of a government organisation participating in the NTP, it analyses how coercively imposed institutional pressure shapes organisational responses to digital transformation, and proposes a typology of organisational responses to coerced institutional change.</p>
<p><strong>Design/Methodology/Approach</strong>: An interpretive, qualitative case study was conducted using 24 semi-structured interviews with change agents and government employees. Data were analysed using grounded theory procedures following Strauss and Corbin (1990, 1994). The analysis draws on institutional theory — particularly coercive isomorphism (DiMaggio and Powell, 1983), institutional complexity (Greenwood et al., 2011), and organisational responses to institutional pressure (Oliver, 1991) — to examine how the NTP''s coercive architecture shapes organisational dynamics and individual actor responses.</p>
<p><strong>Findings:</strong> The study identifies four organisational response modes to coerced digital transformation: compliance — surface-level conformity to NTP requirements without substantive institutional change; adaptation — selective integration of market logic practices while preserving core state logic structures; resistance — active contestation of NTP mandates through legitimate and illegitimate channels; and creative accommodation — the development of hybrid practices that satisfy external accountability demands while maintaining internal institutional coherence. The study further demonstrates that the coercive character of the NTP generates distinctive organisational dynamics — including accountability anxiety, strategic gaming, and institutional fatigue — that are not adequately theorised in existing accounts of voluntary digital transformation.</p>
<p><strong>Originality/Value:</strong> This paper makes three contributions. First, it extends the coercive isomorphism literature by demonstrating that coercive institutional pressure generates a more differentiated range of organisational responses than existing theory predicts. Second, it advances understanding of imposed digital transformation in non-western public sector contexts by identifying the distinctive organisational dynamics generated by coercive transformation architectures. Third, it contributes a typology of organisational responses to coerced institutional change that provides a theoretical framework for comparative research on imposed digital transformation across diverse national and cultural contexts.</p>','["coercive isomorphism, Vision 2030, digital transformation, public sector, institutional pressure, Saudi Arabia, organisational responses, imposed change, NTP"]',NULL,23,1,'743-759','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1428','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',45),
(1429,1429,1,'Modeling the Psychological and Technical Factors Influencing the Use of Artificial Intelligence Tools among Non-Native Arabic Learners: A Comparative Study in Egypt, Saudi Arabia, and Jordan.','<p>This study aimed to develop a predictive longitudinal model of the psychological and technical factors influencing the use of artificial intelligence tools among non-native Arabic learners (international students) in three Arab countries: Egypt, the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia, and Jordan. The study adopted an extended Technology Acceptance Model (TAM) incorporating two psychological variables: trust in artificial intelligence and artificial intelligence anxiety. A quantitative longitudinal design with two time waves (T1 and T2) over a full academic semester was employed using Hierarchical Multiple Regression Analysis and PROCESS Macro for mediation. The sample consisted of 812 international students from public universities in the three countries. Six validated instruments were administered at T1 (Perceived Usefulness, Perceived Ease of Use, Trust in AI, AI Anxiety, Attitude toward Use) and T2 (Actual Use, including a behavioral frequency item). The results indicated that T1 perceived usefulness (β = 0.37), perceived ease of use (β = 0.26), and trust in AI (β = 0.28) significantly and positively predicted T1 attitudes, while T1 AI anxiety (β = -0.20) demonstrated a significant negative predictive effect. T1 attitude significantly predicted T2 actual use (β = 0.50). Bootstrapped mediation analysis (PROCESS) confirmed the significant indirect effects of trust and anxiety on T2 actual use through T1 attitudes. Little''s MCAR test indicated that missing data were completely random, and no significant attrition bias was found. Hierarchical regression with dummy variables revealed significant cross-country differences. The study recommends enhancing early trust, reducing initial anxiety, and developing Arabic curricula to systematically integrate AI tools.</p>','["Artificial Intelligence, Technology Acceptance Model, Arabic Language Learning, International Students, Trust, Technological Anxiety, Longitudinal Design, PROCESS Macro, Bootstrapping, Multiple Regression Analysis, Comparative Study."]',NULL,23,1,'760-780','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1429','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',46),
(1430,1430,1,'The Impact of International Marketing on International Competitiveness: A Case Study of the Mellitah Oil & Gas Complex Libyan Branch','<p>The study aims to identify the impact of international marketing on international competitiveness applied to the Mellitah Oil &amp; Gas Complex Libyan Branch; the study adopted a descriptive-analytical approach and used a random sample to collect data. The sample size was 384 individuals. The study concluded that there is a statistically significant positive impact of international marketing on the international competitiveness of the Mellitah Oil &amp; Gas Complex Libyan Branch. The study also found a positive impact of international marketing dimensions (pricing, distribution, and promotion) on international competitiveness.</p>','["International Marketing \\u2013 International Competitiveness \\u2013 Mellitah Oil & Gas Complex"]',NULL,23,1,'781-802','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1430','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',47),
(1431,1431,1,'Strategies and Impacts of Diversity, Equity, and Inclusion (DEI) in the Workplace: Evidence from the Private Sector of Saudi Arabia','<p>This paper investigates the impact of Diversity, Equity and Inclusion (DEI) on employee engagement in Saudi private sector using Social Identity Theory and Inclusive Leadership as theoretical lenses. The analysis was done based on survey data of 701 employees with structural equation modeling. Findings indicate that DEI activities have a substantial impact on employees’ feeling of inclusiveness/being treated fairly, and the perception of leadership support to its long timeemployees around the world. The comparative results suggest that cultural value systems are a stronger moderator of the relationships than policies for Saudization; this reflects the greater influence of social norms in comparison to regulatory-based measures. While the research is located in the context of Saudi Arabia’s transformations, it adds to international (DEI) literature by applying theory-building concepts to a non-Western hierarchical policy-driven backdrop. The resultsalso provide pragmatic implications for organizations and policymakers, specifically in the integration of DEI initiatives with Vision 2030 towards increasing female workforce involvement, fostering diversity at leadership positions and promoting sustainable localization.</p>','["Diversity, Equity, Inclusion, Workforce, Saudi Arabia Vision 2030, Private Sector Leadership."]',NULL,23,1,'803-821','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1431','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',48),
(1432,1432,1,'The Employment of Strategic Monitoring Dimensions and Their Impact on Reducing Organizational Inertia the Mediating Role of Dynamic Capabilities','<p>The study aims to test the impact of the dimensions of strategic monitoring in reducing organizational inertia through the intermediate role of Dynamic capabilities in a number of government hospitals in Dhi Qar Governorate. The strategic monitoring variable includes three dimensions (strategic thinking, strategic intelligence, strategic vigilance). As for the dimensions of the organizational inertia variable (vision inertia, Action inertia, psychological inertia), while the dimensions of Dynamic capabilities were adopted as an intermediate variable (sensing capability, seizing capability, and reconfiguring capability). The problem of the study was a set of questions that diagnose the levels of variables on the ground and determine appropriate actions. The idea revolves around providing the dimensions of strategic monitoring to reduce organizational inertia, and the extent to which Dynamic capabilities as an intermediate variable contribute to enhancing this impact. The study adopted the descriptive analytical approach on a random sample of leaders in government hospitals using the questionnaire as a main tool for data collection, where a community of (260) leaders was targeted and distributed to them (155) questionnaires and retrieved (153) valid questionnaires for analysis. The data were processed through statistical programs (SPSS V.28,SmartPLS 4) using methods: arithmetic mean, standard deviation, and coefficient of variation. The results proved the validity of the study''s hypotheses, perhaps the most prominent of which is the presence of a direct moral impact of strategic monitoring in reducing organizational inertia, as well as the presence of a moral impact of strategic monitoring in Dynamic capabilities. The results of the mediation test showed that Dynamic capabilities play the role of partial mediator in the relationship between the variables of the study, which means that an important part of the impact of strategic monitoring is achieved through the development of Dynamic capabilities.</p>','["strategic monitoring, Dynamic capabilities, organizational inertia, government hospitals"]',NULL,23,1,'822-845','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1432','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',49),
(1469,1469,1,'Multi-Agent Collaborative Code Generator','<p>Developments in artificial intelligence have made it possible for autonomous systems to compose, test, and refine code, but all current methods are based on single-agent designs or weak multi-agent systems with little cooperation and poor validation procedures. Single-agent designs lack internal diversity and poor reasoning, whereas small multi-agent designs are mostly competitive rather than cooperative and lack strong role specialization to facilitate complicated iterative improvement. The work presented here provides a five-agent collaborative architecture to provide continuous and verifiable code optimization by controlled specialization and iterative refinement. The architecture consists of five specialized agents, including the planner agent, the coder agent, the tester agent, the reviewer agent, and the explainer agent. Each agent plays a differentiated role in an integrated process of feedback where code is conceived, developed, tested, examined, and explained in an iterative process until optimality is reached. The improvement process continues until an internally optimal solution is obtained, and the reviewer agent then checks its validity individually and passes it on to the explainer agent for explainability and documentation, else the system goes through the improvement cycle again. This composition unifies independent thinking, responsibility, and transparency in an interdependent environment with lesser bias and more consistency of choice. The five-agent architecture realizes maximum heterogeneous viewpoints and distributed knowledge, producing better-quality, understandable, and verifiably optimal code than current single-agent or smaller multiagent approaches. Experiments show that continuous validation and specialization-based cooperation result in better results in automatically generated code and provides a scalable platform for intelligent, self-adjusting development environments with a 92</p>','["Program Synthesis , AI Pair Programming ,Multi-Agent Collaboration ,Intelligent Code Generation ,Autonomous Coding Agents , Bug Detection."]',NULL,23,1,'846-857','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1469','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',50),
(1471,1471,1,'Analyzing the Impact of FinTech Adoption on Internal Audit Efficiency: Evidence from Saudi Banks Using Partial Least Squares Structural Equation Modeling (PLS-SEM)','<p>Our study aims to explore the impact of internal auditors'' adoption of fintech technologies within Saudi banks on the quality and efficiency of the internal audit process.  This is achieved by examining auditors'' opinions regarding this technology, its acceptance, performance, and ease of use, as well as the challenges they face in its successful implementation. The study collected data from 230 internal auditors working in Saudi banks. The questionnaire was distributed electronically. The results indicated that internal auditors show significant interest in fintech technology, and their opinions regarding its ease of use in audit processes were not uniform. The significance of this study lies in the lack of previous research that has addressed the benefits and ease of use of fintech in the banking sector. Therefore, this study contributes new, practice-based information by evaluating the opinions of internal auditors regarding the use of fintech in Saudi Arabia. It addresses the urgent need to study the extent to which these auditors support the broader adoption and use of this technology, and the potential benefits it can offer, not only at the local level, but also in terms of added value to the internal audit process, sustainability, and ultimately, enhancing competitive advantage. </p>','["FinTech adoption, digital transformation, competitive advantage, added value, internal audit efficiency."]',NULL,23,1,'858-873','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1471','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',51),
(1472,1472,1,'Study of Digital Asset Auditing and Future Perspectives','<p>This study examines the current state of digital asset auditing and proposes a clearer future vision through a systematic review of relevant literature and prior studies. It highlights the fundamental differences between digital and traditional assets, explains the classification of digital assets and their close association with blockchain technology, and analyzes the existing accounting and auditing frameworks considering international standards and provides a brief overview of the status of Egyptian legislation. The study also discusses the evolving role of auditors and the main stages of the audit process in the digital environment. The findings indicate that rapid digital transformation requires the development of advanced auditing standards and methodologies, and that the adoption of data analytics, smart contracts, and continuous auditing, together with enhancing auditors’ technical and professional competencies, contributes to improving audit quality, transparency, and risk management related to digital assets.</p>','["Digital Asset Auditing", "Blockchain", "Continuous Auditing", "Audit Quality."]',NULL,23,1,'874-900','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1472','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',52),
(1473,1473,1,'Computer-Based Systems And Artificial Intelligence In Healthcare: A Review Of Applications, Challenges, And Future Directions','<p>In the healthcare industry, AI and computer-based systems promise to advance patient care, improve diagnostic precision, and facilitate improved clinical decision-making. In this review, the use of cutting-edge AI tools like machine learning, deep learning, and natural language processing, along with telemedicine systems, clinical decision support systems, electronic health records, and lab automation systems, is explored. Early identification of disease, optimized treatment, evidence-based processes, real-time analytics, and efficient data management can be performed using artificial intelligence techniques. Currently, medical imaging, early diagnosis, drug development, personalized medicine, robotic-assisted surgery, and predictive analytics are important applications. Combining the technologies of Artificial Intelligence, Cloud Computing, and Internet of Medical Things allows for intelligent service in healthcare and constant monitoring of patients. It still, however, has challenges to address, such as the lack of skilled professionals, the cost of implementation, privacy and security issues, connectivity, and data quality. There''s also an ethical concern because there''s a lack of transparency and algorithmic prejudice. These challenges must be tackled by bridging the gap between disciplines, implementing robust regulations, and continually advancing technology to ensure the successful application of AI in healthcare. The technology could revolutionize the industry.</p>','["Artificial Intelligence, Healthcare Systems, Machine Learning, Clinical Decision Support, Digital Health."]',NULL,23,1,'901-913','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1473','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',53),
(1474,1474,1,'Generative Artificial Intelligence as a Metacognitive Partner: a Theoretical Model of Shared Regulation of Learning in Higher Education','<p>The rapid adoption of generative artificial intelligence in higher education has shifted the debate from academic integrity toward a deeper question: how this technology transforms the processes through which students regulate their own learning. Recent evidence shows ambivalent results, since artificial intelligence assistance improves immediate performance but can induce metacognitive laziness, cognitive offloading and dependence. This conceptual article, designed as a theory synthesis, integrates three research streams: regulation of learning and metacognition, social modes of regulation, and human interaction with generative artificial intelligence. As a result, the article proposes the Model of Metacognitive Coregulation with Generative Artificial Intelligence, which explains under what conditions this technology can operate as an external agent of shared regulation that scaffolds student planning, monitoring and evaluation instead of replacing them. The model is formalized in eight testable propositions and a concept map, and concludes with an empirical research agenda for higher education.</p>','["generative artificial intelligence", "metacognition", "regulation of learning", "shared regulation", "higher education", "theory synthesis."]',NULL,23,1,'914-927','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1474','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',54),
(1476,1476,1,'Why Older Consumers Resist Voice Commerce: An Extended UTAUT2 Model of Techno-Anxiety, Privacy Calculus, and Trust - SHAP Evidence from Urban India','<p>Purpose: Voice commerce is proliferating at a higher rate than most other retail product categories, although it remains significantly under-adhered among older consumers in India. The current research explores adoption barriers to the use of voice commerce among Indian adult citizens aged 55 and above, who reside in six metro cities. An extended UTAUT2 model is applied to the data collected, so that techno-anxiety, voice accuracy perception, privacy concern, and trust in voice AI are included in the framework as additional predictors of adoption intention and usage behavior.<br/>Design/Methodology/Approach: The cross-sectional survey of the 412 respondents with a mean age of 61.4 was analysed in two successive stages. The first stage used SmartPLS 4.0 software to perform partial least squares structural equation modelling analysis. The second stage applied Random Forest and XGBoost classification algorithms with the SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) interpretation to identify the SEM variables that were misestimated (non-linear effect) and to cross-validate the results of the SEM analysis.<br/>Findings: Techno-anxiety is the dominant adoption barrier (β = −0.298, p &lt; 0.001, f² = 0.148), exceeding privacy concern (β = −0.224) and all four UTAUT2 base construct effects. Voice accuracy perception is the strongest positive predictor beyond the UTAUT2 core (β = 0.241, p &lt; 0.001). XGBoost achieves 86.1% classification accuracy (AUC-ROC = 0.907). SHAP analysis ranks voice accuracy perception second in feature importance - above its PLS-SEM β rank - revealing a threshold effect that the linear model does not capture. Age cohort (55–64 versus 65 and above) significantly moderates the adoption intention to actual use path.<br/>Originality/Value: This study is the first to treat voice-interface techno-anxiety as a distinct construct in an UTAUT2 voice adoption framework and to validate voice accuracy perception as a novel predictor. The PLS-SEM and SHAP dual design identifies where non-linear predictive contributions diverge from linear path coefficients, offering a replicable methodological approach for adoption researchers. The findings shift design priority for voice commerce platforms from feature development toward anxiety reduction and speech recognition accuracy for older populations.</p>',NULL,NULL,23,1,'928-948','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1476','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',55),
(1477,1477,1,'Integrating Comprehensive Sexuality Education into Educational Systems: A Holistic Approach to Policy, Pedagogy, and Student Development - A Systematic Literature Review','<p>The integration of Comprehensive Sexuality Education (CSE) into educational systems has become a major issue in public health, social development, and educational equity. In many countries, sexuality education programs remain limited, fragmented, or heavily influenced by cultural, religious, and political constraints. However, international research demonstrates that comprehensive, inclusive, and culturally responsive programs contribute to improving adolescents’ knowledge, attitudes, and behaviors regarding sexual and reproductive health. This systematic literature review, conducted in accordance with the PRISMA 2020 guidelines, aims to analyze the main factors influencing the integration of CSE into educational systems. The literature search was conducted using peer-reviewed scientific publications published between 2015 and 2025. The selected studies addressed the pedagogical, political, sociocultural, and institutional dimensions of integrating sexuality education across diverse educational contexts. The findings reveal that the main barriers to the integration of Comprehensive Sexuality Education (CSE) are related to cultural taboos, religious resistance, the absence of coherent policy frameworks, insufficient teacher training, and a lack of adequate educational resources. The analyzed studies also highlight that the quality and effectiveness of CSE programs depend heavily on teacher training, community involvement, the cultural adaptation of educational content, and the existence of effective monitoring and evaluation systems. The most successful programs are those that adopt an inclusive approach incorporating gender perspectives, human rights, consent, violence prevention, and reproductive health. This review highlights the need to develop integrated educational policies that are tailored to local sociocultural contexts, particularly in Arab-speaking and African countries where debates surrounding sexuality education remain sensitive. It also emphasizes the importance of participatory approaches involving families, schools, healthcare professionals, and community stakeholders.</p>','["Comprehensive Sexuality Education (CSE), educational systems, adolescents, sexual and reproductive health, pedagogy, PRISMA, educational policies, inclusion."]',NULL,23,1,'949-969','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1477','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',56),
(1478,1478,1,'Bodies, Symbols, and Gender in the Military Cadet School (1907)','<p>This article analyzes a set of photographs taken in 1907 and preserved in the Department of Military History of the General José María Córdova Military Cadet School. It examines in particular those that record the presence of women interacting with the military field, applying a historical methodology with an emphasis on primary sources and supported by Pierre Bourdieu''s concepts of habitus, field, and symbolic capital, as well as Peter Burke''s perspective on the image as historical testimony. The analysis reveals that these photographs not only document, but also construct a visual narrative that legitimizes the military institution, reproduces gender hierarchies, and articulates the military with the social and political in the modernizing context of the early 20th century.</p>','["Military photography", "Visual history", "Gender and the military", "Pierre Bourdieu", "Symbolic capital", "Historical methodology", "Military modernization", "Colombia."]',NULL,23,1,'970-988','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1478','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',57),
(1479,1479,1,'Sustainable Human Resource Strategies for Heritage Organisations: Exploring Ethical Leadership and Workforce Engagement','<p>Organisations with a duty to preserve cultural heritage, the heritage organisations, should adjust to the principles of sustainability in economic, environmental and social aspects. Sustainable human resource management (HRM) strategies and ethical leadership are becoming imperative in enhancing the engagement and performance of the workforce in such organisations. This study attempts to examine the way ethical leadership influences workforce engagement and in what way the concept of sustainable HR practices can be applied to achieve social and economic sustainability in heritage organisations. A descriptive and exploratory research design was used and targeted 15 heritage organisations. The data were gathered using surveys, interviews and focus groups among the HR managers, team leaders, and employees. Regression analysis was used in analysing quantitative data, and thematic analysis was used in analysing qualitative data. The researchers discovered that ethical leadership is an important factor in improving the engagement of the workforce. Engagement and job satisfaction were reported in organisations that practised green HRM and sustainability. The larger heritage organisations showed more formal ethical leadership practices, but the small institutions also experienced the benefit of ethical leadership adoption, albeit having fewer resources. Sustainable HR practices and ethical leadership are critical in enhancing engagement of the workforce and performance of the organisation in heritage organisations. By involving sustainability practices in HRM, staff satisfaction and organisational performance may be increased to make cultural institutions profitable in the long run.</p>','["Ethical Leadership, Sustainable HRM, Workforce Engagement, Heritage Organisations, Green HRM."]',NULL,23,1,'989-999','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1479','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',58),
(1480,1480,1,'Machine Learning-Based Predictive Maintenance for Mechanical Equipment in Smart Manufacturing','<p>In computer numerical control (CNC) systems, the remaining useful life (RUL) of cutting tools is an important factor to be considered to prevent unplanned tool stoppages, premature tool replacement, and to ensure machining quality. This research suggests a group-aware and explainable machine learning-based approach to normalized RUL prediction using vibration, current and operating condition features. In contrast to the traditional CNC RUL studies performed by observation level random splitting, the proposed method ensures complete separation of tools between training, validation, and independent test partitions to avoid any information leakage between partitions at the level of the tools. Seven stable predictors were identified: five vibration variables, one current variable and the hardness of the workpiece, by using the stability-based feature selection. Four methods of repeated group validation, formal validation, macro-tool error and worst-tool performance were used to compare baseline and nonlinear regression models. Random Forest was chosen as the final model, with the validation RMSE value of 0.1676 and test RMSE value of 0.2377 and test R^2of 0.3417. The framework showed that unseen-tool generalization could be measured, but there was a variation between test tools that showed the difficulty of cross-tool degradation variability. The features related to vibration accounted for 66.29% of the total model importance, while the workpiece hardness and current variability accounted for 26.72% and 6.99% respectively. When the dominant predictive information was extracted from the vibration, the best performance was achieved by sensor-group ablation, and the complete feature configuration gave the best performance in terms of validation.</p>','["CNC machining", "remaining useful life", "Random Forest", "SHAP", "predictive maintenance", "explainable machine learning."]',NULL,23,1,'1000-1019','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1480','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',59),
(1481,1481,1,'Design of an Iterative Model for Contextual Structuring of Unstructured NoSQL Data Using Hybrid Topological, Probabilistic, and Relational Learning Models','<p>Unstructured exponential growth of NoSQL data is causing an effective challenge for data processing in general, semantic querying, and integration into the traditional analytics pipeline for efficient data processing. Current solutions depend at least in part on rule-based static transformation or partial machine learning models, often lacking any preservation of semantic integrity, schema heterogeneity, or alignment with real-world usage patterns. These constraints severely cripple downstream applications like query optimization, relational mapping, and ML pipeline integration in process. This work proposes a thorough Contextual Structuring Pipeline that automatically converts unstructured NoSQL data into structured, schema-consistent, and optimized relational representations in the queries. The pipeline includes five new learning models designed to contribute to a specific subtask in the transformation. First, CADA-Net (Context-Aware Document Attention Network) uses transformer-based hierarchical encoding to segment raw NoSQL records into semantically meaningful key Value structures. Second, TopoGraph-X constructs an entity-type graph with multi-level topologies from latent document hierarchies through topological learning. Third, HarmoField employs domain-specific BERT embeddings along with the Gaussian mixture model to normalize field representation across heterogeneous sources. Fourth, MetaRel-Frame constructs meta-relational abstractions on logical entity-role patterns to discover relational table blueprints. Finally, QueryStruct transforms the blueprint into a use-optimized dataset through restructuring, indexing, reordering, and compressing its components, taking into account historic query logs. The structural accuracy achieved by this framework is 92.3%, schema normalized is 91.2%, and query latency is decreased by 39.4%. The entire process of structuring unstructured NoSQL data forms a fully automated method, semantically grounded and performance-aware, towards compatibility with SQL-ready, graph analytics, and downstream workflows in machine learning process.</p>','["Unstructured Data, NoSQL Structuring, Semantic Schema Mapping, Graph-Based Learning, Query Optimizations, Process"]',NULL,23,1,'1020-1038','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1481','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',60),
(1482,1482,1,'Saving Pattern Among Salaried Women: Case of Delhi NCR','<p>Increased women participation in economic growth has led to need of understanding financial behaviour of salaried women. The study investigated the factors affecting saving behaviour among salaried women in Delhi NCR. Data collected from 803 salaried women through structured questionnaire was analysed using chi square test to explore relationship between demographic, socio economic variables and saving behaviour of salaried women. Annual Savings and Percentage of Income Saved has been used to understand the saving behaviour and tested with variables like: age, marital status, educational qualification, occupation domain, working city and Spouse Earning Status.</p>
<p>As a result, it was found that saving behaviour is positively influenced by age, marital status, educational qualification, occupation domain, and spouse earning status whereas, working city remained ineffective in influencing saving behaviour of Salaried women in Delhi NCR.</p>
<p>The study offers valuable insights that can strengthen financial inclusion schemes and financial education plans to bring about effective change, which would finally lead to positive economic situations for working women and broaden the understanding of saving behaviour of women in socio economic contexts.</p>','["Saving Behaviour, Salaried Women, Demographic factors, Annual Savings, Percentage of Income Saved."]',NULL,23,1,'1039-1057','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1482','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',61),
(1484,1484,1,'Hybrid Working and the Future of Work: Examining Work–Life Balance, Employee Well-Being, and Mental Health Outcomes in the Indian IT Sector','<p>The rapid adoption of hybrid working models has fundamentally transformed organizational work practices, particularly within knowledge-intensive industries. While hybrid work is widely associated with greater flexibility and autonomy, existing research reports inconsistent findings regarding its influence on employee performance, with limited evidence explaining the underlying mechanisms in emerging economies such as India. This study investigates the relationships among hybrid working, work–life balance, employee well-being, and employee performance within the Indian IT sector using the Job Demands–Resources (JD–R) framework.</p>
<p>A quantitative research design was adopted, and data were collected from 523 professionals employed in hybrid work arrangements across the Indian IT industry. The proposed hypotheses were examined using regression-based statistical analysis, complemented by thematic analysis of qualitative responses to provide additional contextual insights.</p>
<p>The findings reveal that hybrid working does not directly enhance employee performance. Instead, its influence operates through a sequential mechanism whereby improved work–life balance enhances employee well-being, which subsequently contributes to higher employee performance. The empirical results support the proposed sequential mediation model and demonstrate significant positive relationships among the study variables.</p>
<p>The study extends the Job Demands–Resources framework by conceptualizing hybrid working as a structural job resource that indirectly influences employee performance through psychological and behavioral pathways. The findings contribute to the growing body of literature on hybrid work by providing empirical evidence from an emerging economy and offer practical insights for organizations seeking to design sustainable hybrid work policies that promote employee well-being while enhancing organizational performance.</p>','["Hybrid Working", "Work\\u2013Life Balance", "Employee Well-Being", "Employee Performance", "Job Demands\\u2013Resources Framework", "Indian IT Sector"]',NULL,23,1,'1058-1085','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1484','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',62),
(1485,1485,1,'Quantum-Resistant Blockchain Framework for Remote eVoting Via Integration of Adaptive Governance, Homomorphic Encryption, and Audit for Secure Digital Democracy Scenarios','<p>Online voting platforms that rely on classical cryptography and centralized trust anchors face escalating challenges as the demand for secure and transparent digital elections grows. Such systems remain exposed to quantum-era threats, insider manipulation, and delayed audit mechanisms, which together can undermine public confidence and electoral legitimacy. To counter these risks, a quantum-resistant, multi-layer blockchain architecture has been developed to enable remote voting with continuous verifiability and resilience. This architecture resolves key weaknesses through five integrated layers. Quantum-Resistant Distributed Ledger Initialization (QR-DLI) embeds lattice-based cryptography, specifically Kyber and Dilithium variants, directly within the genesis block, ensuring the ledger is tamper-proof from inception and immune to quantum brute-force attacks. The Self-Adaptive Smart Contract Governance Engine (SASCG) introduces dynamic, participation-aware rule adjustments, allowing principled governance without manual overrides and ensuring that voting periods and eligibility rules adapt securely in real time. Homomorphic Vote Encryption with Multi-Authority Shard Key Distribution (HVE-MASKD) guarantees ballot confidentiality and authenticity by combining fully homomorphic encryption with distributed key shares, eliminating single points of trust. The Zero-Knowledge Proof–Based Real-Time Audit Layer (ZKP-RTAL) continuously validates ballot integrity while concealing vote content, creating a public and immutable audit trail. Finally, the Federated Performance &amp; Threat Intelligence Optimizer (FPTIO) aggregates live telemetry and historical attack data to proactively tune consensus parameters and predict potential intrusions without interrupting the election process. Collectively, these layers achieve sub-second cryptographic operations, transaction throughput exceeding 1,500 TPS, over 99 % fraud detection accuracy, and strong scalability. The model provides a future-ready, auditable replacement for current e Voting infrastructures, strengthening digital democracy through post-quantum security, adaptive governance, and intelligent, continuous optimizations.</p>','["Blockchain-Based E-Voting, Post-Quantum Cryptography, Smart Contract Governance, Homomorphic Encryption, Zero-Knowledge Proofs."]',NULL,23,1,'1086-1105','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1485','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',63),
(1486,1486,1,'Blockchain- LSTM Integration for Securing and Enhancing Real-Time Healthcare Analytics','<p>This paper presents a comprehensive study on integrating Deep Learning (DL) modelling Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM)-based models with blockchain technology to deal with the most critical problems in healthcare data management, security and analytics. Escalating the size of healthcare data exponentially due to the development of e-HRs (electronic health records), wearables, and real-time monitoring systems pushed traditional data storage and processing practices into the limelight as their most significant weaknesses. LSTM networks are perfect for analyzing time-series data in health care, such as disease classification, anomaly detection, and patient outcome prediction over the long run. Nevertheless, these models require sound data protection techniques and privacy measures to be followed per the regulations while maintaining trust. Blockchain technology fills in the gaps beyond LSTM by offering a decentralized, tamper-proof platform to safely store and share data, keeping confidentiality, integrity, and availability simultaneously. This paper surveys the available literature on hybrid models by flushing out the topic with the help of LSTM and blockchain. It explores their potential use in real-time healthcare analytics applications, along with the challenges of scalability and interoperability. By presenting a model through the use of these technologies, the research centres on sharpening health information systems such as accuracy, security, and transparency, which in turn intensify the trust of both the patients and the providers of care, thus enabling the development of a patient care solution that is more reliable and efficient.</p>','["LSTM, Blockchain strategy, Healthcare Data Analytics process, Real-Time Data mitigates, Anomaly identification, Disease Classification, Data Security model, Decentralized network Systems, Patient results Prediction, Hybrid supervisor Models."]',NULL,23,1,'1106-1129','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1486','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',64),
(1487,1487,1,'NLP-Driven Extraction of Key Features from Legal Texts: Court Opinions, Briefs, Statutes, and Case Law','<p>This paper outlines a unique method of legal text processing using Natural Language Processing (NLP) technology to extract the information from the legal texts meaningfully and naturally. The proposed system is designed in a data pipeline architecture by integrating the NLP functionalities such as tokenization, part-of-speech tagging, named entity recognition (NER), and dependency parsing to systematize the processing of typologies of legal text hubs, including legal briefs, statutes, and case law. The methodology presented concerns the importance of pre-processing legal texts that address domain-specific challenges. The texts may contain ambiguities, while the legal language itself is a very intricate kind of language. The system uses advanced methods like syntactic parsing and semantic role labeling to parse and find relevant entities, relationships, and context, ensuring the automation of the large amount of raw legal data for review and analysis. Besides that, the first is leveraging machine learning models to optimize the data extraction process and to ensure high efficiency and scalability. This methodology guarantees that accurate and reliable information is extracted and reduces the time and costs that conventionally come with manual legal analysis. The focal point of the offered system is overcoming legal workflow issues and bringing model texts to widespread use. Therefore, the proposed system aims to facilitate decision-making processes in legal practice and even the accuracy of the proposed model.</p>','["Automation, Court Opinions, Legal Document Computing, Legal Information, Syntactic Parsing Events, Semantic Role Labelling Framework, Relevant Feature Extraction, Legal Brief, Legal Text Analysis, Legal Text Automation, Named Entity Recognition System, Natural Language Processing Model, Supervisor Learning Models, Text Separation, Text Pre-processing.."]',NULL,23,1,'1130-1151','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1487','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',65),
(1488,1488,1,'A Comparative Analysis of Deep Autoencoder, Graph-Based, and Temporal Contrastive Multi-Criteria Recommender Systems: Performance, Explainability, and Preference Adaptation','<p>Recommender Systems (RS) have been progressing from traditional collaborative filtering approaches through deep learning and graph-based learning towards more advanced architectures based on preference modeling adaptability. Multi-Criteria Recommender Systems (MCRS) further improve the personalization level by considering various aspects of user preferences. In this paper, we provide a comparative analysis of three recommendation architectures designed progressively: Deep Autoencoder-Based Multi-Criteria Recommender System (DAE-MCRS), Graph-Enhanced Deep Autoencoder-Based Multi-Criteria Recommender System (GDAE-MCRS), and Temporal Graph Contrastive Deep Autoencoder-Based Multi-Criteria Recommender System (TC²-GDAE-MCRS). We compare the predictive power, ranking effectiveness, robustness, explainability, and adaptability of all three models using Yahoo! Movies and TripAdvisor datasets. Experiments show the gradual increase of the performance from DAE-MCRS to GDAE-MCRS and TC²-GDAE-MCRS. The latter provides the best recommendation performance with RMSE of 0.824, MAE of 0.701, NDCG@10 of 0.812, Precision@10 of 0.423, and Recall@10 of 0.371. Additionally, the use of graph learning, contrastive self-supervised learning, temporal preference modeling, reinforcement learning, and SHAP explainability greatly improves the recommendation efficiency and interpretability. The paper gives the insights on the contribution of each architectural component and shows the way forward for adaptive and explainable recommender systems.</p>','["Multi-Criteria Recommender Systems", "Deep Autoencoder", "Graph Neural Networks", "Contrastive Learning", "Temporal Recommendation", "Reinforcement Learning", "SHAP Explainability", "Causal AI", "Personalized Recommendation", "Deep Learning."]',NULL,23,1,'1152-1161','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1488','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',66);
<p>We evaluate BP-ASTGN on five medically critical actions from the NTU RGB+D 120 dataset: falling, yawning, nausea, fanning self, and chest pain, comprising 4,764 skeleton sequences. Our model achieves 88.89% top-1 accuracy and 88.51% macro F1-score with only 4.82M parameters, representing a 16.4 percentage point improvement over the ST-GCN baseline. Extensive ablation studies confirm the contribution of each architectural component.</p>','["Skeleton-based action recognition", "Graph neural network", "ICU patient monitoring", "Spatial-temporal modeling", "Body-part decomposition", "Deep learning", "Medical action recognition"]',NULL,23,1,'1362-1376','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1504','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',78),
(1506,1506,1,'Smart Biosensors for Food Quality Control: Current Challenges, Emerging Innovations, and Commercial Potential','<p>Meeting the global demand for fresh, minimally processed food requires us to rethink how we monitor food safety. Traditional laboratory methods are often too slow, labor-intensive, and impractical for real-time applications. To overcome these delays, biosensors have emerged as a rapid, highly sensitive, and cost-effective alternative. This study explores how biosensing technology accurately detects pathogens, chemical contaminants like heavy metals and pesticides, and spoilage indicators across dairy, meat, produce, and packaged foods. What makes these tools truly transformative is their seamless integration with modern digital infrastructure. By combining biosensors with the Internet of Things (IoT), artificial intelligence (AI), nanotechnology, edge computing, and blockchain, we can create intelligent, continuous monitoring systems. These interconnected frameworks allow for real-time, farm-to-fork traceability, enabling early hazard detection, extending shelf life, and significantly reducing food waste through data-driven decisions. Despite this immense potential, bringing smart biosensors to the commercial market involves overcoming distinct practical hurdles. We examine current technical barriers, including biofouling, long-term sensor stability, power management, and high manufacturing costs. More importantly, we highlight the emerging innovations actively solving these bottlenecks, such as biodegradable materials, battery-free platforms, advanced printed electronics, and smart packaging technologies.</p>','["Biosensors, Internet of Things (IoT), Food safety, Nanotechnology, Real-time monitoring, Smart sensors, Food quality, Pathogen detection, Food preservation, Environmental monitoring."]',NULL,23,1,'1377-1396','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1506','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',79),
(1508,1508,1,'Centrality-Based Identification of Critical Nodes in Institutional Information Technology Systems','<p>Institutional information technology systems depend on routers, switches, authentication services, databases, cloud gateways and learning platforms whose failure may disrupt academic and administrative operations. This paper proposes a compact graph-theoretic procedure for identifying structurally critical IT assets through degree, betweenness, closeness, eigenvector centrality and articulation-point status. An institutional IT system is represented as a weighted graph, and a normalized criticality index is used to combine local connectivity, brokerage, reachability, influence and disconnection risk. Two exact results quantify weight-robust dominance and reachability loss under articulation-node deletion, giving a transparent basis for prioritizing vulnerable IT assets.</p>','["Centrality measures", "critical nodes", "institutional information technology", "graph theory", "network resilience", "cyber-physical systems. AMS (MOS) Subject Classifications: Primary 05C82", "Secondary 05C90, 68M10, 90B15."]',NULL,23,1,'1397-1400','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1508','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',80),
(1509,1509,1,'AI-Assisted Salesforce Metadata Reconciliation Using TF-IDF Similarity: A Case Study on Multi-Org Enterprise CRM Consolidation','<p>Purpose: Large companies often maintain more than one Salesforce organization. Over time, acquisitions, regional changes, and separate release schedules cause those organizations to diverge. The same business concept may appear under different names, while similarly named components may no longer serve the same purpose. This study examines that reconciliation problem.</p>
<p>Methods: We built a pipeline that retrieves metadata from two Salesforce organizations, converts the XML into comparable text records, and uses term frequency-inverse document frequency (TF-IDF) with cosine similarity to suggest matches. The pipeline also produces review queues and risk reports. The evaluation covered 4,152 comparison records, mainly from Account, Case, Contact, Lead, and Opportunity metadata, together with a smaller set of non-object artifacts.</p>
<p>Results: Of the 4,152 records, 2,943 were classified as high-confidence matches (70.9%), 233 required review (5.6%), and 976 had no suitable match (23.5%). The process also found 240 possible renames and 34 possible drift cases. We manually reviewed a stratified sample of 300 records. On the binary subset, TF-IDF produced 94.5% precision, 95.9% recall, and a 95.2% F1 score, which was higher than the exact-name and Levenshtein baselines.</p>
<p>Conclusion: The findings indicate that a relatively simple and explainable text-similarity method can be useful for Salesforce consolidation. It reduces the number of items that require detailed manual inspection and keeps the reasoning behind each candidate match visible to reviewers.</p>','["Salesforce Metadata API", "schema matching", "metadata reconciliation", "TF-IDF", "cosine similarity", "enterprise DevOps"]',NULL,23,1,'1401-1409','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1509','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',81),
(1510,1510,1,'Predictive Maintenance Using Artificial Intelligence for Enhancing Operational Efficiency in Industry 4.0','<p>Predictive maintenance has emerged as a transformative strategy within Industry 4.0, enabling organizations to transition from reactive and preventive maintenance approaches toward intelligent, data-driven asset management. The convergence of artificial intelligence, Industrial Internet of Things (IIoT), cyber-physical systems, cloud computing, digital twins, and edge computing facilitates continuous monitoring of equipment health, early fault diagnosis, and accurate prediction of component failures. These capabilities significantly reduce unexpected machine downtime, optimize maintenance scheduling, extend equipment lifespan, minimize operational costs, and improve production quality. Artificial intelligence techniques, including machine learning, deep learning, reinforcement learning, and hybrid predictive analytics, enhance the ability to process large-scale industrial data and generate reliable maintenance decisions in real time. Furthermore, predictive maintenance supports sustainability objectives through improved resource utilization, energy efficiency, and reduced material waste while strengthening organizational competitiveness. Despite implementation challenges related to data quality, interoperability, cybersecurity, and model interpretability, continuous technological advancements are accelerating industrial adoption across manufacturing, energy, transportation, healthcare, and process industries. Consequently, artificial intelligence-driven predictive maintenance represents a critical enabler of operational excellence, resilient manufacturing systems, and sustainable industrial transformation in the era of Industry 4.0.</p>','["Predictive Maintenance, Artificial Intelligence, Industry 4.0, Machine Learning, Industrial Internet of Things, Operational Efficiency"]',NULL,23,1,'1410-1441','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1510','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',82),
(1511,1511,1,'Sustainable Marketing Practices and Their Influence on Financial Performance','<p>In an era when businesses are increasingly concerned with sustainable marketing, organisations have recognized the crucial link between the economic development and environmental and social concerns. There is clearly growing value to sustainability for consumers, investors and regulators, and sustainability is beginning to play a role in product development, pricing, promotion, distribution and stakeholder engagement. The current research aims to explore the relationship between sustainable marketing strategies and the main financial results of the business, such as profitability, revenue growth, market share, customer retention and brand value, and to analyze the impact of these strategies on the financial performance of companies. The research is quantitative in nature and the primary data has been collected by using a structured questionnaire method with the respondents being marketing professionals, business managers and consumers from different industries. The sustainable marketing initiatives effect is tested using descriptive statistics, correlation analysis, and regression analysis. The results have shown that companies that apply sustainable marketing are more efficient in their operations, have more trustworthy customers, are more loyal to their brand, and have a more favourable corporate image, all of which have a positive impact on the financial aspects. The study also shows that communication of sustainability programs is a key factor in making the program successful and that long-term commitment is a key factor in successful implementation of sustainability programs. Moreover, the study shows that companies that pursue sustainable marketing will be more likely to deal with risk, to gain access to the market of eco-conscious consumers, and to be competitive in dynamic markets. The findings of the study showed that sustainable marketing is not a CSR project alone but also an investment that will benefit in the long term with the financial performance and resistance of the organization. The results will be informative for business leaders, marketing professionals, policymakers and researchers who want to learn how to incorporate sustainability into marketing strategies without losing out financially and how to gain sustainable competitive edge.</p>','["Sustainable Marketing, Financial Performance, Corporate Sustainability, Green Marketing, Brand Loyalty, Customer Trust, Competitive Advantage, ESG, Business Performance."]',NULL,23,1,'1442-1457','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1511','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',83),
(1512,1512,1,'Artificial Intelligence in Mental Health Care: A Systematic Review of Benefits and Risks for Clinical and Institutional Decision-Making','<p>Stunting is a critical public health issue, particularly in developing countries, with long-term impacts on physical and cognitive development due to chronic malnutrition. In the digital era, public sentiment, and opinion on stunting, as reflected on social media platforms, can provide valuable insights for policymakers and healthcare providers. However, there has been limited research combining multiple machine-learning algorithms for sentiment analysis on this topic. This study aims to conduct a comparative analysis of user sentiment on X regarding public opinion on stunting using three widely utilized text classification algorithms: K-Nearest Neighbour (KNN), Naïve Bayes, and Decision Tree. The research involves collecting and annotating sentiment data (positive, negative, neutral) from social media platforms like Twitter and X, followed by model training and evaluation using metrics such as accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score. Results from the analysis of 1227 data points (2023-2024) with an 80:20 train-test split revealed that Naïve Bayes achieved the highest accuracy at 87.18%, followed by Decision Tree at 79.83%, and KNN at 57.56%. Meanwhile, a comparison using a 70:30 train-test split yielded similar results, with Naïve Bayes achieving 86.41%, Decision Tree 78.41%, and KNN 54.48%. The study demonstrates the effectiveness of the Naïve Bayes algorithm for sentiment analysis in public health contexts, offering a novel approach to understanding public opinion on stunting. By leveraging social media data, this research provides real-time insights that can guide policy and public health interventions. Furthermore, the comparative analysis of algorithms provides valuable knowledge on the most effective models for sentiment analysis in complex, sentiment-driven public health issues. The study emphasizes the role of machine learning in addressing malnutrition and stunting through data-driven policy decisions.</p>','["artificial intelligence, mental health, clinical decision-making, decision support, psychotherapy, ethics, systematic review"]',NULL,23,1,'1458-1472','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1512','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',84),
(1513,1513,1,'Histopathological and Molecular Characterization of Potentially Malignant Oral Disorders for Early Diagnosis and Prognostic Assessment','<p>Potentially malignant oral disorders (PMODs) represent a critical group of lesions with a well-documented risk of transformation into oral squamous cell carcinoma, necessitating early and precise diagnostic approaches to improve patient outcomes. This study aims to integrate histopathological evaluation with molecular characterization to enhance the early detection and prognostic assessment of PMODs. A cohort of clinically diagnosed cases, including leukoplakia, erythroplakia, and oral submucous fibrosis, was systematically examined using conventional histopathological techniques alongside advanced molecular assays. Histological grading of epithelial dysplasia was performed to assess architectural and cytological alterations, while molecular analysis focused on the expression patterns of key biomarkers associated with cell proliferation, apoptosis, and genetic instability, such as p53, Ki-67, and cyclin D1. The findings reveal a significant correlation between the severity of dysplasia and the upregulation of these molecular markers, indicating their potential utility in identifying high-risk lesions at an early stage. Furthermore, molecular profiling provided additional insights into the biological behavior of lesions that appeared histologically ambiguous, thereby improving diagnostic accuracy and risk stratification. The combined approach demonstrated enhanced sensitivity in predicting malignant transformation compared to histopathology alone, emphasizing the importance of incorporating molecular diagnostics into routine clinical practice. This study also highlights the heterogeneity of PMODs and underscores the need for personalized diagnostic frameworks that consider both morphological and molecular parameters. The integration of these methodologies not only facilitates early intervention but also aids clinicians in tailoring appropriate therapeutic strategies, ultimately contributing to better prognostic outcomes. The results support the growing paradigm shift toward precision medicine in oral pathology, where multidisciplinary diagnostic tools are employed to refine disease characterization and management. Overall, this research provides a comprehensive evaluation of PMODs, reinforcing the clinical significance of combined histopathological and molecular analysis in improving early diagnosis and prognostic prediction.</p>','["Potentially Malignant Oral Disorders, Histopathology, Molecular Biomarkers, Early Diagnosis, Prognostic Assessment"]',NULL,23,1,'1473-1482','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1513','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',85),
(1531,1531,1,'AI aware softwares - Help AI serve better & How to make software more AI aware','<p>The current study presents a new approach for constructing AI-aware software systems that augment traditional service responses with rich contextual information, which allows AI agents to perform with increased accuracy and transparency. In particular, the new Intent Graph Context Engine (IGCE) uses graph neural networks to link user intentions with API endpoints, business objects, and past interactions to create context-enriched responses. On the other hand, the Recursive Reasoning Augmentation Engine (RRAE) creates explanation traces, dependency chains, and reasoning metadata that serve as an additional level of transparency that provides information about the reasons why certain data exists and how it can be used for recommendations. In order to deal with the uncertainty that may occur during the process of user queries, the Entropy-Adaptive Response Optimizer (EARO) adjusts the payload of responses by increasing the response size in case of high uncertainty and reducing it when there is clarity about the user intention. Overall, the presented set of modules forms a pipeline that converts user/agent queries into AI-aware service responses with an accuracy of 97.4%. The suggested model places great importance on developing software systems that can actively engage with the AI and not just serve the requests made to them. In doing so, the inclusion of contextual awareness, reasoning enhancement, and adaptation of optimization provides the assurance that the response provided to the AI agents is imbued with meaning and clarity. In this way, the level of interpretability is increased, and at the same time, trust in decision making by AI systems is improved. The use of IGCE, RRAE, and EARO indicates how layered intelligence can make an ordinary service AI-aware.</p>','["AI aware software, Intent Graph Context Engine, Recursive Reasoning Augmentation Engine, Entropy Adaptive Response Optimizer, graph neural networks, recursive reasoning frameworks, entropy based optimization, context enriched responses, reasoning metadata, adaptive payload generation."]',NULL,23,1,'1483-1504','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1531','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',86),
(1533,1533,1,'A Biomathematical Model for Predicting the Impact of Biophilic Interior Design on Psychological Responses for Private University Students','<p>People have an inherent relationship with nature, and exposure to natural elements has been shown to improve mood, support cognitive and practical performance, and reduce stress. Hence, the foundation of biophilic design was established to link interior design with nature. This scoping review addresses by using a Biomathematical model for predicting the Impact of Biophilic Interior Design (BID) on Human Physiological and Psychological Responses. According to the importance of biophilic design in educational places, including indoor plants, window views, natural light, and natural materials inspired by nature and examines their influence on Cognitive Responses (CR), Affective Responses (AR), and Perceptual Responses (PR). Cross-category analyzes of college students identified design characteristics most consistently associated with restorative outcomes and human cognitive and physiological responses, and results in terms of the impact of Biophilic design indicated that emotional responses were the highest and both CR and Perceptual Responses (PR) were also demonstrative positive outcomes. The results confirm the role of biophilic design as a foundation for improving learning spaces, student well-being, and productivity, as well as for directing future design studies and strategies.</p>','["Biophilic Interior Design", "Psychological Responses", "Affective Responses", "Cognitive Responses", "Perceptual Responses."]',NULL,23,1,'1505-1519','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1533','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',87),
(1534,1534,1,'ERP Workflow Surveillance and Ebitda Sensitivity Analysis for Funding Related Risk Control in Enterprise Systems','<p>Contemporary executive management now revolves upon enterprise resource planning (ERP) expertise, which incorporate indispensable communal manoeuvres and deliver real-time statistics perceptibility. There are several benefits to using ERP-generated data for financial decision-making, such as increased precision, predictive insights, and strategic flexibility. Despite these advantages, ERP systems'' centralized architecture exposes companies to business risks such insider manipulation, illegal contracts, and irregular processing pipelines that can have a detrimental effect on an organization''s profitability. This paper outlines a progressive ERP-based commercial surveillance architecture, which scrutinizes organized commercial processing pipelines to find abnormalities in typical contract patterns. Precarious business accomplishments like proof of purchase processing, procurement endorsements, expense approval, and record book changes are characterized by the foundation as consecutive functioning measures to find unusual movement. To achieve this, it is essential to forecast business constraints like EBITDA (Earnings Before Interest, Taxes, Depreciation, and Amortization) and inventory levels precisely. The experimental assessment shows that the suggested context offers expedient evidence concerning functioning threats that disrupt an organization''s EBITDA concert and enhances the credentials of viable abnormalities.</p>','["Commercial threat, EBITDA, ERP, sensitivity analysis, processing pipeline Surveillance."]',NULL,23,1,'1520-1536','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1534','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',88),
(1535,1535,1,'Leadership Style, Perceived Fairness, Perceived Competence, and Employee Trust as Sequential Drivers of Acceptance of AI-Driven Decisions: Evidence from Higher Education in Lebanon','<p>The rapid integration of artificial intelligence (AI) into organizational decision-making has generated an urgent need to understand the human conditions that enable or inhibit employee acceptance of AI-driven decisions. Drawing on Mayer, Davis, and Schoorman''s (1995) Integrative Trust Model, Social Exchange Theory (SET), and the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM), this study proposes and empirically tests a novel sequential dual-mediation model in which Leadership Style (Transformational vs. Transactional) influences employee Acceptance of AI-Driven Decisions through two parallel first-stage mediators — Perceived Fairness and Perceived Competence — and one sequential second-stage mediator — Employee Trust. Data were collected from 400 academic faculty and administrative staff across higher education institutions in Lebanon using a cross-sectional survey. Partial Least Squares Structural Equation Modeling (PLS-SEM) via SmartPLS 4 was employed for analysis. The measurement model demonstrated strong reliability (Cronbach''s α: 0.843–0.901) and validity (AVE: 0.571–0.634; HTMT &lt; 0.85). Structural results indicate that transformational leadership exerts significantly stronger positive effects on perceived fairness (β = 0.423, p &lt; 0.001) and perceived competence (β = 0.391, p &lt; 0.001) than transactional leadership. Both mediators significantly predicted employee trust, which in turn significantly enhanced acceptance of AI-driven decisions (β = 0.447, p &lt; 0.001). Full sequential mediation was confirmed for all leadership pathways. The model explained 54.3% of the variance in employee trust and 49.8% of the variance in AI-driven decision acceptance. Findings advance theory by providing the first empirical validation of a sequential dual-mediation model connecting leadership style to AI acceptance, and offer practical guidance for higher education administrators navigating AI adoption.</p>','["transformational leadership", "transactional leadership", "perceived fairness", "perceived competence", "employee trust", "AI-driven decisions", "technology acceptance", "higher education", "PLS-SEM", "Lebanon"]',NULL,23,1,'1537-1553','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1535','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',89),
(1537,1537,1,'A Real-Time Edge-Aware Neural Enhancement Algorithm Empowering Autonomous Visual Systems','<p>Latest developments in image processing and artificial intelligence (AI) are used to handle real-time applications, particularly in autonomous approaches. Consequently, recent studies have focused on the related challenges, such as computational latency and suboptimal edge detection in terms of accuracy. This article is motivated by these challenges and aims to evolve an unprecedented edge-aware deep neural network (DNN) approach based on high-speed image processing over dynamic qualifications. This approach combines convolutional filters with the activation layer(s) using gradient-descent optimization, sigmoid activation, mean squared error loss, and backpropagation with weight updates. A proposed work consists of main analysis of sample data achieved through MATLAB-enabled hardware utilizing a gradient-descent optimization method. For illustrating robust feature improvements, noise minimization, and real-time processing speed enhancements compared to traditional methods. This study is successfully addressing previous limitations and proposes a masterful approach for real-time, autonomous image improvements in dynamic situations.</p>','["Edge-aware enhancement, deep neural network, image processing, real-time optimization, autonomous systems."]',NULL,23,1,'1554-1568','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1537','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',90),
(1538,1538,1,'The Impact of Public External Debt on Capital Formation in Sub-Saharan Africa: A Panel GMM Analysis','<p>This study investigates the impact of public external debt on capital formation in five selected Sub-Saharan African (SSA) countries using panel data from 1990 to 2025. Employing the Generalized Method of Moments (GMM) technique, the analysis reveals a statistically significant and positive relationship between external debt and capital formation. The results indicate that external debt (EXTD), savings rate (SAVR), exchange rate (EXCR), and corruption control (CORR) positively influence capital formation, while trade openness (TROP) and population growth (POPG) have a negative impact. The findings suggest that rising public external debt in SSA countries can drive capital formation and economic growth when channeled towards productive investments. To leverage this opportunity, SSA countries should prioritize effective debt management and direct borrowed funds towards investments that foster economic growth and development. Effective management of public debt and governance reforms are crucial to addressing these challenges and promoting economic growth in SSA.</p>','["Public External Debt, Capital Formation, Sub-Saharan Africa, Generalized Method of Moments (GMM), Panel Data, Economic Growth, Debt Management, Productive Investment, Savings Rate, Exchange Rate, Trade Openness, Population Growth, Corruption Control, Governance Reforms."]',NULL,23,1,'1569-1584','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1538','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',91);
<p>Design/methodology/approach, This study employs a quantitative approach applied to 124 IPO companies that issued warrants on the Indonesia Stock Exchange during the 2020 2025 period. Stock return volatility over the one-year period following the IPO was estimated using the Exponential Generalized Autoregressive Conditional Heteroskedasticity (EGARCH) model, while the relationships among variables were tested using Moderated Regression Analysis (MRA).</p>
<p>Findings, The results show that firm value and free float have a negative effect on IPO stock return volatility, whereas risk-taking has a positive effect. In addition, warrants are found to strengthen the relationship between firm value, free float, and risk-taking and IPO stock return volatility, indicating that firms'' fundamental characteristics, ownership structure, and risk level are important determinants of stock return volatility among IPO companies.</p>
<p>Research limitations/implications, This study is limited to IPO companies that issued warrants on the Indonesia Stock Exchange, so generalizing the findings to other capital markets should be done with caution. The findings extend the application of Signaling Theory and Information Asymmetry Theory in explaining the stock return volatility of IPO companies.</p>
<p>Practical implications, The findings offer implications for companies planning an IPO, investors, underwriters, and regulators in improving firms'' fundamental quality, managing free float structure, and utilizing warrants as part of risk-management and capital-market development strategies.</p>
<p>Originality/value, This study develops an empirical model that integrates firm value, free float, and risk-taking as determinants of IPO stock return volatility, positioning warrants as a strategic moderating mechanism. These findings extend the IPO literature by showing that warrants not only affect IPO performance but also strengthen the relationship between firm characteristics and stock return volatility a relationship that remains very limited in prior research.</p>','["Initial Public Offering (IPO), Stock Return Volatility, Warrant, Firm Value, Free Float, Risk-Taking, EGARCH."]',NULL,23,1,'1670-1686','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1546','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',97),
(1547,1547,1,'Integrating Distance-Based and Density-Based Clustering to Enhance Forecasting Accuracy in Heart Disease Diagnosis a Hybrid Machine Learning Approach','<p>The diagnosis of heart-related diseases with time accuracy is a pivotal factor needed in reducing fatality and also possibly improving patient conditions. Traditional machine learning models become inadequate with such data, often high dimensional with so much noise, overlapping and outliers. These attributes can then thrash the prediction accuracy. This study aims to demonstrate an ensemble-modeling mechanic-comprising distance-based (K-means) along with density-based (DBSCAN) clustering and hierarchical clustering with OPTICS, in order to improve patient stratification and forecasting. Such models are harbingers in the world of clustering because such models collaborate as soon as synergy demands. To define and have quantitative content to our supposed improvements, we experimentally validated UCI Cleveland Heart Disease and Statlog Heart Disease datasets and found in totality quite an improvement in performance. For the K-means + DBSCAN models, we were able to attain accuracy of 91.0 and 93.0% on UCI and Statlog datasets, respectively; correspondingly, they have AUC values of 0.95 and 0.97. The Hierarchical Clustering + OPTICS model proved even better, with accuracies of 92.0 and 94.0%, and their corresponding AUC values of 0.96 and 0.98. This strongly supports that deploying a hybrid system using distance-based and density-based clustering can significantly improve the accuracy and robustness of heart disease diagnosis and risk prognosis systems so as to make clinical trial policies more trustworthy.</p>','["Hybrid clustering, Hierarchical clustering, Risk forecasting.Heart disease diagnosis, K-means, DBSCAN, OPTICS."]',NULL,23,1,'1687-1706','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1547','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',98),
(1548,1548,1,'Elevating Educators: The Impact of Job Satisfaction on Teacher Performance in Anhui’s Private Universities, China','<p>This empirical study examines the relationship between job satisfaction and job performance among teachers in private universities in Anhui Province, China. Given the critical role that job satisfaction plays in enhancing educational quality and institutional effectiveness, understanding its dynamics within the higher education sector is essential. Utilizing a mixed-methods approach, the research collected quantitative data through surveys administered to a representative sample of teachers across various private institutions, alongside qualitative insights obtained through focus group discussions. The findings reveal a strong positive correlation between job satisfaction and job performance, indicating that higher levels of satisfaction significantly contribute to enhanced teaching effectiveness and student outcomes. Key factors influencing job satisfaction include working conditions, institutional support, workload management, and interpersonal relationships among colleagues. This study highlights the necessity for university administrators and policymakers to prioritize strategies that foster a supportive and engaging work environment. Recommendations for practice include implementing policies for equitable workload distribution, enhancing physical workspaces, and promoting professional development opportunities tailored to teachers’ needs. By focusing on these areas, private universities can not only improve job satisfaction among educators but also drive institutional performance, ultimately benefiting students and the broader educational phenomenon in Anhui. This research contributes to the existing literature by providing empirical evidence of the link between job satisfaction and performance, offering valuable insights for institutional leaders aiming to enhance teacher welfare and effectiveness in the evolving phenomenon of higher education.</p>','["Job satisfaction, Job performance, Private universities, Higher education, Anhui Province."]',NULL,23,1,'1707-1734','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1548','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',99),
(1549,1549,1,'Explainable Machine Learning for Credit Risk Management and Intelligent Lending Decisions in Nepalese Cooperative Banks: A Mathematical Review','<p>The cooperative banking sector in Nepal constitutes a foundational pillar of financial inclusion, serving approximately 7.4 million members across 31,450 primary cooperatives and disbursing loans exceeding NPR 453 billion. Yet this sector is hemorrhaging credibility. The National Cooperative Bank Limited reported a non-performing loan ratio of 33.01 percent as of mid-July 2025, with its capital adequacy ratio collapsing to 0.82 percent — a figure that would trigger immediate regulatory intervention in any conventional banking jurisdiction. Against this backdrop, the question is no longer whether cooperative banks in Nepal need better risk assessment tools, but whether they can afford to continue without them.</p>
<p>This paper develops a comprehensive theoretical framework for integrating explainable machine learning into credit risk management systems of Nepalese cooperative banks. We derive the complete mathematical architecture of ensemble gradient boosting — specifically XGBoost and LightGBM — alongside post-hoc interpretability mechanisms grounded in cooperative game theory, namely SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) and LIME (Local Interpretable Model-agnostic Explanations). The framework incorporates information-theoretic feature selection, SMOTE-based class imbalance correction, fairness constraints calibrated to Nepal’s socio-economic heterogeneity, and a rigorous convergence analysis of the boosting iteration. We prove consistency of the mutual-information feature selector, derive the bias-variance decomposition for gradient-boosted ensembles, establish generalization bounds via Rademacher complexity, and verify the four Shapley axioms for the TreeSHAP algorithm. Drawing upon Nepal Rastra Bank’s Financial Stability Report FY 2024/25, the National Cooperative Federation of Nepal’s sectoral statistics, and the Government of Nepal’s Economic Survey 2025/26, we situate the technical apparatus within Nepal’s regulatory architecture — the Cooperative Act 2017, the Financial Sector Development Strategy 2022–2026, and the National Financial Inclusion Roadmap. The paper argues that predictive accuracy and regulatory transparency are not competing objectives but complementary necessities for institutional survival in Nepal’s cooperative sector.</p>','["Explainable Artificial Intelligence, Credit Risk Management, Cooperative Banks, Nepal, SHAP, LightGBM, Gradient Boosting, Financial Inclusion, Shapley Values, Class Imbalance, Rademacher Complexity, Bias-Variance Decomposition."]',NULL,23,1,'1735-1772','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1549','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',100),
(1550,1550,1,'An Ensemble Machine Learning Framework with Zero Trust Architecture and Blockchain-Anchored Audit Logging for Multi-Cloud Intrusion Detection','<p>Multi-cloud adoption has widened the enterprise attack surface to a degree that perimeter-based defence can no longer address. Traffic is now flowing continuously across AWS, Azure, and GCP, and the majority of deployed Zero Trust Architecture (ZTA) systems are still using static rule tables, with no ability to provide an audit trail of the reasoning behind decisions, and with logs stored in datastores that can be modified by an insider without detection. This paper proposes ZT-ChainGuard, a framework that overcomes these three limitations in one architecture that integrates an ensemble machine learning trust-scoring engine, ZTA policy enforcement and a blockchain-based audit trail. The trust-scoring engine is a two-layer stacking ensemble, with XGBoost and Random Forest as base learners, and Logistic Regression as a meta-learner, and it returns a continuous trust score, P(Attack | flow), for each network flow, which is then used to trigger the ZT policy decision at a threshold of 0.5. The explanation of each decision is provided by SHAP values at both the global and per-flow level, and each decision is stored as an immutable, SHA-256 hash-chained block. On CICIDS2017 (2.83 million flows, 14 attack classes) the framework achieves 99.90% accuracy, 99.71% F1-score, and 99.99% ROC-AUC; on ToN-IoT (2.23 million IoT records, 9 attack types) it achieves 99.81% accuracy, 99.88% F1-score, and 100% ROC-AUC. The latency of inferences is 0.006ms per sample, and the overhead of auditing the blockchain is 0.019ms per block. This performance is not just a quirk of a particular split, as it is shown to be stable across the three folds of three-fold cross validation.</p>','["Zero Trust Architecture", "Intrusion Detection", "Ensemble Learning", "XGBoost", "SHAP Explainability", "Blockchain Audit", "Multi-Cloud Security", "CICIDS2017", "ToN-IoT", "Trust Scoring."]',NULL,23,1,'1773-1788','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1550','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',101),
(1551,1551,1,'Assessment of Signature Feature Integrity Across Successive Generations of Document Reproduction Assisted by Digital Image Processing','<p>Handwritten signatures remain one of the most widely accepted methods of personal authentication in legal, financial, administrative, and commercial transactions. In contemporary document environments, original documents frequently undergo photocopying, scanning, printing, and digital transmission, resulting in the widespread use of reproduced signatures. In forensic document examination the forensic reliability of such signatures depends upon the extent to which diagnostically significant handwriting characteristics are preserved during successive reproduction processes. This study evaluates the integrity of selected signature characteristics across five generations of document reproduction using laser multifunction printers (LMFPs), single-function photocopiers (SFPs), and inkjet multifunction printers (IMFPs). A total of 330 reproduced signature samples were generated and examined using a combination of conventional forensic examination and digital image processing employing Fiji (ImageJ). Five diagnostically significant characteristics, namely pen lift (with space), pen lift (without space), pen pause, curvature, and writing slant, were evaluated across successive reproduction generations. Statistical analysis was performed using Pearson''s Chi-square test to determine the significance of observed feature alterations. The findings indicate that first-generation (G1) reproductions preserve most diagnostically significant signature characteristics, whereas progressive degradation becomes increasingly evident in higher generations (G2 - G5), particularly within micro-structural features such as pen lifts, pen pauses, and curvature. Writing slant remained comparatively stable throughout the reproduction sequence. Digital image processing based examination improved feature visualization and enabled rapid, standardized examination of subtle degradation patterns. The study demonstrates that the forensic suitability of reproduced signatures is generation-dependent and highlights the value of digital image processing based examination in contemporary forensic document authentication.</p>','["Digital Image Processing", "Forensic Document Examination", "Reproduced Signature", "Fiji (ImageJ)", "Document Authentication."]',NULL,23,1,'1789-1797','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1551','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',102),
(1555,1555,1,'Sustainable Biodiesel Production from Waste Cooking Oil via Acid Catalysis: Process Optimization and Industrial Management Perspectives','<p>The swift exhaustion of fossil fuel reserves and escalating environmental issues have heightened the quest for sustainable and renewable energy alternatives. Biodiesel has emerged as a viable alternative to conventional diesel due to its biodegradability, low toxicity, and diminished greenhouse gas emissions. This research examines the generation of biodiesel from waste cooking oil (WCO) by acid-catalysed transesterification, a method especially appropriate for feedstocks with elevated free fatty acid levels. Acid catalysis concurrently promotes esterification and transesterification, reducing soap generation and increasing fatty acid methyl ester (FAME) yield. The generated biodiesel was analyzed based on essential fuel quality criteria, such as acid value, density, viscosity, calorific value, flash point, pour point, and fatty acid content. FT-IR and GC–MS were utilized to verify the chemical composition and quality of the biodiesel in compliance with international fuel standards. Furthermore, engine performance, combustion properties, and exhaust pollutants were analyzed to determine its viability as an alternative diesel fuel. The findings demonstrate that acid-catalyzed biodiesel possesses physicochemical characteristics akin to petroleum diesel, while markedly decreasing emissions of carbon dioxide (CO₂), sulfur oxides (SOₓ), and particulate matter (PM). The findings indicate that biodiesel production from WCO is an environmentally sustainable, economically viable.</p>','["Trans-esterification", "biodiesel", "acid-catalysed", "methyl ester of fatty acid", "sustainable fuels."]',NULL,23,1,'1798-1808','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1555','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',103),
(1575,1575,1,'Examining the Impact of FinTech Adoption on Operational and Insolvency Risks in Jordanian Banks: A Panel Data Approach','<p>The purpose of this study is to explore the effect of financial technology (FinTech) on banking risk in the Jordanian commercial banks from two perspectives: Solvency risk and operational risk. The three primary elements of FinTech that are proxied are: Internet and mobile banking (IBMB); Automated Teller Machines (ATMs); and Point-of-Sale (POS) systems. The study utilizes panel data of the 13 listed commercial banks in Jordan''s Amman Stock Exchange during the period of 2019–2025, and applies a fixed effects regression model to analyze the relationship between digital banking technologies and bank risk. The empirical results reveal a heterogeneous impact of FinTech on banking risk. In the case of insolvency risk, the results show that digital financial services with IBMB, ATMs, and POS systems have a significant positive influence on the banking''s insolvency risk, thus improving the banking''s stability through increased efficiency, financial liquidity management, and operational performance. In contrast, operational risk results show that ATMs significantly reduce operational risk, internet and mobile banking has a significantly and negative relationship with operational risk, while POS systems have an insignificant effect. From the overall results, it can be concluded that FinTech has a generally risk reducing effect on banks in Jordan, but with the exception of some cases, each depending on the type of technology and the risk dimension. This research adds to a growing body of literature on FinTech and banking stability, as it draws on a context of an emerging economy. It points out the positive contribution of digital transformation in terms of financial stability, but not uniformity of the effects on technologies, which calls for the need to have differentiated regulatory and risk management approaches. The findings have significant consequences for policy makers and bank managers on the safe and efficient use of FinTech in banking industry.</p>','["FinTech, Internet and Mobile Banking, ATMs, POS Systems, Insolvency Risk, Operational Risk, Jordanian Banks, Panel Data."]',NULL,23,1,'1809-1821','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1575','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',104),
(1576,1576,1,'AI-Driven Predictive Maintenance for Reliable Underwater Data Center Infrastructure','<p>The fast increase in the cloud services has intensified the fear of energy efficiency, dependability in operations and complexity of maintenance in large scale data centers. Undersea data centers that apply natural ocean cooling have become a viable alternative; however, the sealed and physically inaccessible deployment environment presents great difficulties in inspection, corrective maintenance and ability to recover faults. Therefore, self-directed and anticipatory maintenance systems cannot be ignored in ensuring reliability in the long run.</p>
<p>This paper suggests a software-defined, AI-based predictive maintenance system with specific applications to underwater data center infrastructures. The system combines multi-modal IoT-sensing with edge-level intelligence to make continuous measurements of the state of environmental and system-level parameters, such as thermal and humidity, power stability, storage health indication, and water ingress detection. We present the AquaSense Predictive Intelligence Algorithm (ASPIA) which is a hybrid model of both anomaly detection and failure prediction that integrates supervised learning with time-series analysis to identify early-stage degradation trends before failure occurs on a critical basis.</p>
<p>The suggested architecture is based on edge computing to perform real-time inference along with redundant storage co-ordination and cloud-synchronized logging, which means fault resilience and continuity of data. Control fault-injection experimental tests show accuracy in early failure prediction of up to 87 percent to 92 percent, automated storage failover can recover in 3 to 5 seconds and does not suffer any data loss.</p>
<p>The findings suggest that the suggested framework can be used to improve operational resilience, minimize unplanned downtime, and manage (autonomously) the infrastructure in physically inaccessible data center settings. This research paper finds a predictable, scalable and extendable scheme of predictive reliability which informs the roll out of next generation sustainable data centers.</p>','["Predictive Maintenance, Underwater Data Centers, Artificial Intelligence, Internet of Things, Edge Computing, Fault Detection, Autonomous Infrastructure Monitoring."]',NULL,23,1,'1822-1840','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1576','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',105),
(1577,1577,1,'A CNN–LSTM Deep Learning Framework for Multivariate Temperature Forecasting and Extreme Weather Event Analysis','<p>Throughout history weather predication has been a powerful and necessary tool in a number of activities that have had a major influence on human development and survival such as climate monitoring, agricultural planning and disaster management. Unlike other variables, temperature fluctuations are a major challenge for prediction due to the fact that they are nonlinear and extremely dynamic. This research paper introduces deep learning (DL) architectures for multivariate temperature forecasting using past weather data of single cities in India as samples. The DL methods such-as LSTM, GRU, Hybrid CNN-LSTM and Attention-based model were first outlined and then experimented. The meteorological variables that were considered include humidity, precipitation, wind speed and cloud cover. The quality of forecasting is quantified by the means of MAE, RMSE, MAPE and R2. Experimental results reveal that the DL methods achieve significantly better forecasting of temperature as compared to the conventional ARIMA model. Out of the deep learning models which were experimented with the CNN-LSTM model gave the best performance and has the following results: MAE (287.37), RMSE (401.60), MAPE (7.35%) and R2 (0.893). Besides that, CNN-LSTM model not only performed better in normal conditions but also in extreme temperatures and was able to achieve R2 of 0.923, which underscores its capacities to capture complex weather changes. This paper provides strong evidence of the value of combining DL methods for weather prediction. In the future, efforts will be made to enhance the accuracy of prediction by using transformer-based time-series models and incorporating larger spatiotemporal climate data.</p>','["Weather Forecasting, Deep Learning (DL), LSTM, GRU, Time-Series Forecasting."]',NULL,23,1,'1841-1861','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1577','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',106),
(1578,1578,1,'IoT and Recent Sensor Technologies in Smart Farming: A Review of Innovations and Enhancements','<p>The field of agriculture is encountering considerable difficulties in fulfilling the rising stresses in order to produce food while maintaining sustainability and efficient source utilization. Numerous technologies currently employed in agricultural practices for monitoring crop growth, soil efficiency, and nutrient levels have faced challenges. Some of these technologies have proven inadequate due to variations in frequency and distance range within smart farming applications. To tackle these issues, this review highlights an integration of biosensors, bioelectronics and Internet of Things (IoT) technology into agricultural performs, which has become a hopeful answer. The paper discusses how these sensing elements are integrated with IoT architectures, microcontroller-based nodes, wireless sensor networks, and cloud-enabled analytics to support precision irrigation, nutrient management, disease indication, and yield-oriented crop supervision. In addition, it compares major IoT communication technologies, reviews current applications and limitations, identifies research gaps in field deployment and system integration, and outlines future directions for robust, scalable, and intelligent agricultural biosensing systems.</p>','["Smart farming, IoT, Biosensors, Bioelectronics, wireless, embedded, Image processing, soil sensing."]',NULL,23,1,'1862-1878','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1578','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',107),
(1579,1579,1,'Voluntary Social Media Disengagement and Student Well-Being: A Mixed-Methods Study Among Youth in Tamil Nadu, India','<p>The omnipresence of social media in the lives of young people is raising concern among communication researchers, educators, and mental health practitioners. Though researchers are keen to explore usage patterns and impact of social media on the lives of young people, very little of this is Indian. There is very little region-specific evidence on the impact of social media use or the effects of social media detoxification which happens when young users voluntarily step away from these platforms. This study conducted in a Southern state in India explores the behavioural and psychological correlates of voluntary social media disengagement among college students. Conducted in Tamil Nadu, the study uses a sequential mixed-methods design. A preliminary survey of 1,062 undergraduate students (aged 17–25) assessed social media addiction and engagement using the Bergen Social Media Addiction Scale (SMAS) and the Social Media Engagement Scale (SMES). Students who scored at or above a clinical threshold (n ≈ 200) were invited to undertake a voluntary 14-day social media disengagement challenge; 31 enrolled, and 7 completed the full protocol, contributing daily diaries, mobile-usage screenshots, and post-challenge in-depth interviews. Quantitative analysis revealed a significant, moderate positive correlation between addiction and engagement scores (r = .350, p &lt; .001), no significant gender or age differences in addiction levels, and significant associations between addiction status and both willingness to disengage and field of study. Thematic analysis of the disengagement narratives showed a marked, if non-linear, reduction in screen time, improvements in sleep, mood, and academic focus, and partially sustained behaviour change at one-month follow-up. The findings suggest that structured voluntary disengagement is a feasible and psychologically beneficial short-term intervention for reducing problematic social media use among Indian youth, while also highlighting the difficulty of sustaining change without external support. Implications for digital-wellness programming in higher education are discussed.</p>','["Social media disengagement, digital well-being, social media addiction, youth, digital detox"]',NULL,23,1,'1879-1888','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1579','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',108),
(1580,1580,1,'Self-Advocacy Strategies Predict Psychological Immune System in UAE Adolescents: A Hierarchical Regression Study','<p>Adolescents in the United Arab Emirates (UAE) face increasing academic and social demands that may challenge regulatory capacities. The Psychological Immune System (PIS) operationalized as containment, adaptive confrontation, and self-regulation, captures coordinated protective subsystems supporting recovery and balance under stress. This study tested whether self-advocacy strategies predict PIS functioning in a general school-based adolescent sample.</p>
<p>A cross-sectional predictive correlational design was applied to 204 adolescents (16–18 years; 44.6% male) from private secondary schools in Sharjah and Ajman, UAE. Participants completed a researcher-developed Self-Advocacy Strategies Scale, and the UAE-validated short Psychological Immune System Scale. Analyses included Pearson correlations (two-tailed) and hierarchical regression with gender entered in Step 1 and self-advocacy dimensions in Step 2.</p>
<p> PIS functioning resulted a moderate (M = 35.16, SD = 5.32; range = 18–48), with adaptive confrontation showing the highest mean (M = 12.66, SD = 2.14). Self-advocacy was strongly correlated with PIS (r = .675, p &lt; .001). Gender was not a significant predictor in Step 1 (R² = .012, p = .117). Adding self-advocacy dimensions explained substantial incremental variance (ΔR² = .521, p &lt; .001), and the final model accounted for 53.3% of PIS variance (R² = .533, Adj. R² = .521), F(5, 198) = 45.155, p &lt; .001. Self-awareness (β = .471) and communication/expression (β = .283) were the strongest unique predictors, followed by negotiation/problem solving and support seeking.</p>
<p>In conclusion, Self-advocacy strategies are robust correlates and predictors of PIS functioning in UAE adolescents, whereas gender contributes little once skills are considered.</p>','["self-advocacy", "psychological immune system", "PIS functioning", "adolescents", "UAE"]',NULL,23,1,'1889-1898','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1580','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',109),
(1584,1584,1,'AI-Augmented Data Governance Framework for Data Management Offices','<p>The exponential growth of organizational data assets has rendered traditional manual data governance approaches inadequate for modern Data Management Offices (DMOs). Existing frameworks lack the scalability, adaptability, and intelligence required to address the complexities of heterogeneous, high-velocity data environments. This paper proposes the AI-Augmented Intelligent Data Management Office (AI-iDMO) framework, a comprehensive six-layer architecture that integrates cutting-edge artificial intelligence including natural language processing (NLP), deep learning, federated learning, knowledge graphs, and reinforcement learning to automate and optimize core data governance functions. The AI-iDMO framework encompasses automated metadata management, real-time data quality monitoring, policy compliance enforcement, intelligent data lineage tracking, and adaptive access control, all orchestrated through a unified AI governance engine. We formally define each framework component using mathematical notations and present a reference implementation evaluated on benchmark enterprise datasets. Experimental results demonstrate that AI-iDMO achieves a classification accuracy of 96.8%, an F1-score of 0.967, a precision of 0.971, and a recall of 0.963, outperforming five state-of-the-art baselines. The framework offers a scalable, explainable, and privacy-preserving approach to intelligent data governance, providing practical guidance for DMOs navigating increasingly stringent regulatory requirements such as GDPR, CCPA, and HIPAA.</p>','["Data Governance, Data Management Office, Artificial Intelligence, Deep Learning, Natural Language Processing, Federated Learning, Knowledge Graph, Reinforcement Learning, GDPR, AI-iDMO."]',NULL,23,1,'1899-1915','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1584','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',110),
(1585,1585,1,'An Artificial Intelligence-Driven Decision Support Framework for Intelligent Information Systems Management and Operational Optimization','<p>The intelligent information systems produce huge volumes of heterogeneous data related to their operations, which poses challenges to the accuracy and timeliness of decision-making processes in organizations. In this study, an Artificial Intelligence-Driven Decision Support Framework for intelligent information systems management and optimization that involves data preprocessing, feature selection, machine learning, and intelligent decision support engine is suggested. The efficacy of the suggested framework is tested via the use of the DataCo SMART Supply Chain dataset with more than 180,000 operational instances and 53 features. Preprocessing of the data includes missing value imputation, encoding of categorical features, Min-Max normalization, detection of outliers, and 80:20 partitioning of the data set, while feature selection is done through the use of Information Gain. The results of the experiment have shown that the LightGBM algorithm works better with 97.1% accuracy, 96.6% precision, 96.0% recall, 96.3% F1-score, 0.99 ROC-AUC, 0.11 MAE, 0.20 RMSE, and a training time of 15 seconds. Moreover, the suggested framework increases the efficiency of decision support from 70% to 94%, and increases the efficiency of resources utilization by 22%, inventory management by 24%, delivery efficiency by 19%, cost-saving by 18%, and customer satisfaction by 17%. The above-mentioned results confirm the effectiveness of the suggested AI-based framework.</p>','["Artificial Intelligence (AI), Decision Support Systems (DSS), Intelligent Information Systems, Machine Learning, Operational Optimization."]',NULL,23,1,'1916-1931','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1585','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',111),
(1586,1586,1,'Digital Precarity and the Transformation of Labor Exploitation in Latin America: Rethinking Modern Slavery in Platform Economies','<p>This article examines the transformation of labor exploitation in Latin America within the context of digital platform economies. Moving beyond traditional conceptions of modern slavery centered on physical coercion, the study introduces the concept of digital precarity to capture emerging forms of structurally mediated exploitation. Drawing on a mixed methodological approach that combines legal analysis, institutional reports, and critical socio-economic theory, the paper identifies a growing misalignment between existing labor regulations and contemporary mechanisms of control, including algorithmic management and data-driven dependency. The findings demonstrate that exploitation increasingly operates through indirect coercion, economic vulnerability, and fragmented employment relations rather than overt force. This shift challenges the adequacy of international legal frameworks grounded in industrial labor paradigms. The article proposes the Digital Precarity Matrix as an analytical tool to assess varying degrees of labor exploitation across physical, economic, and digital dimensions. By focusing on Latin America as a region marked by informality and rapid digitalization, this research contributes to ongoing debates on labor transformation, human rights, and platform governance, offering policy-relevant insights for adapting labor protections to the digital age.</p>','["Modern slavery, labor exploitation, digital precarity, Latin America, forced labor, human trafficking, labor law transformation, digital labor platforms, platforms work, precarious work, gig economy."]',NULL,23,1,'1932-1945','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1586','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',112),
(1587,1587,1,'Dehumanization in Investigative Journalism: Ethical Challenges of AI, Automation, and Digitalization in the Newsroom','<p>The accelerating integration of artificial intelligence (AI), automation, and digitalization has transformed the practice of investigative journalism, reshaping both its workflow and ethical foundations. While these technologies enhance efficiency, precision, and access to information, they also present profound ethical challenges that risk eroding the humanistic core of journalism. This study examines the phenomenon of dehumanization in investigative journalism by analyzing how technological dependence diminishes empathy, moral discretion, and interpersonal trust between journalists and sources. Adopting a qualitative library-based approach, the study synthesizes academic literature published between 2019 and 2025 to explore the dual impact of technology, enhancing productivity but weakening ethical agency. The findings indicate that AI-driven automation improves operational performance but simultaneously undermines human judgment, narrative depth, and moral accountability. Consequently, the research highlights the urgent need for adaptive ethical frameworks and AI literacy programs that reinforce transparency, emotional intelligence, and human oversight in newsroom environments. By addressing the tension between technological innovation and ethical responsibility, this study contributes to ongoing scholarly debates on sustaining human-centered values in digital-era journalism.</p>','["Automation, Dehumanization, Digitalization, Environments, Investigative Journalism, Technological."]',NULL,23,1,'1946-1965','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1587','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',113),
(1588,1588,1,'Flood Exposure, Water and Sanitation Disruptions, and Household Health Outcomes: A Cross-Sectional Survey in Sudan','<p>This household field survey assessed flood exposure, displacement, property damage, water, sanitation and hygiene conditions, health outcomes, environmental exposure, healthcare access, assistance, and warning coverage among 409 households. It also examined statistical associations between selected household exposures and reported health outcomes. Mean household size was 6.5, and 42.8% contained more than six members. Overall, 71.1% of houses were flooded in 2024. Among flooded houses, mean maximum water depth was 81.08 cm and mean flood duration was 10.03 days. Nearly half of respondents (48.7%) were displaced, with a mean displacement duration of 20 days. Surface water was used alone or with another source by 78.0% of households, while 82.4% reported that water sources became contaminated or inaccessible during or after flooding; 70.9% used no water-treatment method. During the preceding four weeks, households reported acute watery diarrhea (45.7%), fever with chills (62.6%), skin infections or rash (33.0%), respiratory symptoms (33.3%), and flood- or cleaning-related injuries (14.9%). Among 2,660 household members, the corresponding prevalence rates were 20.7%, 29.8%, 8.7%, 8.2%, and 3.0%, respectively. Severe anxiety or insomnia was reported by 83% of adults, and 90.2% of respondents reported a high increase in mosquitoes, flies, or rodents. Although 86.3% could access essential healthcare, only 40.1% received post-flood assistance and 24.7% received a flood warning. Contaminated or inaccessible water was associated with acute watery diarrhea (p = .039). The findings demonstrate extensive household exposure alongside substantial WASH, physical-health, mental-health, vector, assistance, and warning challenges following flooding. These results identify priority areas for post-flood public health response planning.</p>','["flood exposure", "household health", "water, sanitation, and hygiene", "displacement", "communicable disease", "healthcare access", "disaster response."]',NULL,23,1,'1966-1991','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1588','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',114),
(1589,1589,1,'Green Product in Sustainable Business Development Future Research Agenda: Bibliometric Analysis','<p>Purpose – Over the past few decades, many studies have been conducted on green products. However, research that explores the factors that influence the adoption of green products by consumers is still relatively limited. Given the increasingly pressing issues of climate change and environmental sustainability, the importance of implementing and developing green products is becoming increasingly critical. Therefore, this study aims to conduct a bibliometric analysis of green products, with a focus on published empirical research, in order to encourage the acceleration of adoption and innovation in green products.</p>
<p>Design/methodology/approach – The approach in this study uses a systematic literature review (SLR) with bibliometric analysis using VOSviewer related to green products as a reference in “Article Title, Abstract and Keywords” sourced from the Scopus database from 1958 to 2024. The analysis was conducted on September 20, 2024. </p>
<p>Findings – The results showed that research related to green products is still very relevant to be studied in order to provide guidelines for practitioners and academics in developing sustainable green products in the future, especially in developing countries. The interesting finding is that China is very dominant in conducting studies related to the issue of green products compared to the United States which is very in recent decades.</p>
<p>Research limitations/implications – This study relies on research literature on green products that is only sourced from the Scopus database. Future studies can combine with the Web of Science database to expand generalization to be more comprehensive in explaining the research phenomenon.</p>
<p>Practical implications – The results of this study include implications for the management and development of sustainable green products in the future for practitioners and academics.</p>
<p>Originality/value – Research related to green products is starting to become an interesting topic for practitioners and academics in various countries, both in developed and developing countries. To support the sustainable development of green products, research related to this is important to provide important guidance for practitioners and academics in developing green products in the future. </p>','["Green Products, Literature Review, Bibliometric Analysis, VOSviewer."]',NULL,23,1,'1992-2007','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1589','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',115),
(1590,1590,1,'AI-Driven Public Relations and Relational Outcomes in Health Communication: Public Trust and Engagement in India and Indonesia','<p>Artificial intelligence has increasingly transformed health communication by expanding the speed, scale, and personalization of institutional messaging. However, its strategic value cannot be understood solely in technical terms. This study examines how AI-driven public relations influences relational outcomes in health communication, with particular attention to public trust and public engagement in India and Indonesia. Using a quantitative design, data were collected from 320 respondents who had prior exposure to AI-supported health communication through digital platforms. The proposed model was tested using Partial Least Squares Structural Equation Modeling. The findings show that AI-driven public relations has a positive and significant effect on public trust and public engagement. Public trust also has a positive and significant effect on public engagement and mediates the relationship between AI-driven public relations and public engagement. These results suggest that the effectiveness of AI in health communication depends not only on communicative efficiency, but also on whether AI-supported interaction is perceived as credible, responsive, and institutionally reliable. By situating the analysis in two developing-country contexts, this study extends the relational perspective of public relations into AI-mediated health communication and highlights trust as a key mechanism through which technological communication becomes meaningfully engaging. The study also offers practical implications for health institutions seeking to integrate AI into communication strategies without weakening public confidence or relational quality.</p>','["Artificial intelligence", "Public relations", "Health communication", "Public trust", "Public engagement."]',NULL,23,1,'2008-2025','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1590','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',116);
<p>In connection to the averages stated previously, this research determined a total average of 4.21 with a standard deviation of 0.62 on all considered attributes; a high, reliable perception level on all issues being observed. Because this path from externals toward easy-use yielded at least a five percent chance of being related (below, .001), teachers’ opinions regarding “externals” appear to impact the assumed ease of using new tech technologies directly. There appeared to be no positive relationship with how valuable a new tech tool appears and an externality, suggesting a clear line of inquiry in terms of usage but no direct relevance for assuming usefulness. Perceived use value influences a teacher’s motivation toward using the new tech tools but easy-to-use aspect does not have a significant contribution to an instructor’s’ will to use these, though it might predict some likelihood that the teacher actually adopts technologies. For our purposes, this suggest we focus on in-depth teaching for teachers on the topics and further enhancing their proficiency and certainty regarding these tools to bring them into successful classroom use. The goal of our research was to contribute to education policy regarding technology adoption, while proposing several interventions to promote successful incorporation of technology for lessons with children.</p>','["Educational Tools, Technology Acceptance Model (TAM), Teacher Awareness, Technology Utilization, Contemporary Classrooms."]',NULL,23,1,'2048-2061','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1592','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',118),
(1593,1593,1,'Intelligent Decision-Making Frameworks for Gender Integration in Peacekeeping Missions: Evidence from Indonesian Air Force Female Personnel','<p>This study examines how political will shapes the gender integration of WARA (Women of the Indonesian Air Force) in United Nations peacekeeping missions. It applies a constructivist qualitative case study design based on interviews with thirteen informants and supported by document analysis. Political will is conceptualized as a multidimensional configuration consisting of individual, organizational, relational, socio-cultural, and policy dimensions. The findings show that individual commitment to meritocracy and gender inclusion is present but operates within organizational arrangements that distribute authority and rely on discretionary interpretation. Relational dynamics influence role allocation, with female personnel often positioned in coordination and community engagement functions. Socio-cultural norms continue to inform institutional practices, particularly in relation to domestic expectations and role suitability. At the policy level, commitments to gender integration exist, while procedural alignment remains in development. Theoretically, the study extends political will literature by showing that commitment-to-implementation gaps in military institutions are produced through the interaction of multiple institutional dimensions, not by leadership intent alone. It also demonstrates that gender integration should be understood as an organizational process of norm translation across hierarchical and culturally embedded settings. Effective integration emerges when alignment is achieved across these dimensions, enabling international gender norms to be translated into consistent deployment practices.</p>','["political will", "gender integration", "peacekeeping", "Indonesian Air Force."]',NULL,23,1,'2062-2077','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1593','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',119),
(6418,6418,1,'Determinants of Plastic Waste Generation in Delhi: A Time-Series Econometric Framework with Data Validation','<p>This study examines demographic and economic variables associated with plastic waste generation (PWG) in Delhi within a time-series econometric framework that explicitly retains the five independent variables and the dependent variable: PWG, population density (PD), urbanization rate (UR), GDP per Capita (GDP per Capita (GDPPC)), industrial output (IO), and plastic consumption per capita (PCPC). Annual observations for 2006–2025 are analysed as a supplied diagnostic record because a complete, homogeneous Delhi-specific annual source series could not be independently established for every variable. The analysis therefore does not treat the numerical record as fully validated administrative data. Descriptive statistics, Pearson correlations for the five independent variables and the dependent variable, ADF unit-root tests, five-predictor VIF diagnostics, a full five-predictor level OLS equation, residual tests, Cook''s distance, and first-difference diagnostics are reported. The level series for PWG, PD, GDP per Capita (GDPPC), IO and PCPC are non-stationary at the 5% level, whereas UR is stationary. The full five-predictor model produces R² = 0.918, but extreme multicollinearity is present across the predictors, with VIF values ranging from 116.605 for IO to 103,541.807 for PCPC. The level model also shows a low Durbin–Watson statistic (1.305) and evidence of heteroskedasticity (Breusch–Pagan p = .049), while the Breusch–Godfrey test is not significant at one lag (p = .108) and RESET is not significant (p = .775). First differences make PWG, GDP per Capita (GDPPC) and IO stationary, but PD, UR and PCPC remain non-stationary under the reported specifications. The evidence therefore supports diagnostic rather than causal interpretation and shows that a final Delhi-specific determinant model requires a fully harmonised annual dataset and a time-series specification appropriate to the integration properties of the five independent variables and the dependent variable.</p>','["Plastic Waste Generation", "Delhi", "Population Density", "Urbanization Rate", "GDP per Capita", "Industrial Output", "Plastic Consumption per Capita", "Time-Series Econometrics", "Unit Root", "Multicollinearity", "Data Validation"]',NULL,23,1,'2078 - 2102',NULL,'The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-01',120),
(1320,1320,1,'Hybrid Decision-Support Framework for Prioritising PMMVY Awareness Outreach among Marginalised Women in Rural Bharatpur','<p>Programmes for maternal welfare can be widely enrolling and yet failing to reach those who are still aware and able to access but who have not yet done so. This study is about the lack of an operational mechanism for the identification and prioritisation of such women in the context of Pradhan Mantri Matru Vandana Yojana (PMMVY). A hybrid explainable decision-support framework was proposed, combining the Gower-distance k-medoids clustering, entropy weighting, TOPSIS ranking and a shallow decision-tree surrogate. Primary survey data was collected from 300 PMMVY beneficiaries in a rural area of Bharatpur, Rajasthan, based on their demographic characteristics, awareness, access, digital literacy and institutional dependence. The analysis resulted in six stable clusters of beneficiaries, Gower silhouette = 0.585 and bootstrap agreement = 0.959. The 62 women were ranked at high priority, 101 as moderate priority and 137 as routine priority by TOPSIS. Access friction and awareness deficit were the most significant factors in prioritisation, with several high-need groups being distinguished by educational vulnerability. The decision tree successfully replicated the priority categories at high fidelity and macro-F1&gt;0.91, while the sensitivity analysis demonstrated good ranking stability. The study finds that the success of the aggregate programme may mask the need for targeted outreach and that it provides a clear and documented mechanism for equitably distributing resources for PMMVY outreach.</p>','["PMMVY, beneficiary segmentation, entropy-TOPSIS, explainable decision support, maternal welfare."]',NULL,22,1,'01 - 18','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1320','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',1),
(1321,1321,1,'The Role of Bonding Social Capital and Nature-Based Water Management in Enhancing Ecotourism Resilience: A Case Study of Loksado in the Meratus Mountains','<p>This study investigates how bonding social capital and nature-based water management jointly enhance the resilience of Loksado ecotourism in the Meratus Mountains, South Kalimantan. The Amandit River Basin faces hydrological degradation, and the Dayak Loksado community’s indigenous social capital remains poorly integrated into formal water governance. We hypothesize that bonding capital directly and indirectly strengthens ecotourism resilience through its influence on nature-based solutions (NbS) for water management, with bridging capital acting as a partial mediator. A quantitative survey was conducted among 168 respondents across four villages in Loksado District. Data were analyzed using Structural Equation Modeling-Partial Least Squares (SEM-PLS). Our results show that bonding capital positively and significantly predicts NbS-based water management (β=0.421, p&lt;0.001), which in turn significantly predicts ecotourism resilience (β=0.387, p&lt;0.001). Bridging capital partially mediates the relationship between bonding capital and resilience. The model explains 64.3% of the variance in ecotourism resilience (R^2=0.643), with all structural paths exhibiting large effect sizes (f^2&gt;0.35). The total effect of bonding capital on resilience is 0.359, while the total effect of NbS-based water management is 0.554. Hence, NbS-based water management serves as a partial mediator. This research contributes empirical evidence that indigenous social cohesion and traditional ecological knowledge are foundational, not merely ancillary, to sustaining ecotourism in hydrologically sensitive tropical mountain regions. The findings underscore the necessity of integrating community-level social capital with nature-based water strategies to bolster ecotourism resilience against environmental threats.</p>','["ecotourism", "indigenous knowledge", "nature", "social capital", "water management"]',NULL,22,1,'19 - 39','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1321','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',2),
(1322,1322,1,'Religious Moderation, Digital Literacy in Social Cohesion Interaction among the University Students in Indonesia','<p>This paper investigates the interplay between digital literacy, religious moderation, and social cohesion among university students in Indonesia, a context marked by increasing digital religious consumption and concerns about polarization. We propose and test a mediation model wherein digital literacy—particularly algorithmic awareness and critical source evaluation—predicts religious moderation, which in turn predicts social cohesion. The study employs a multi-method design integrating a cross-sectional survey of 1,200 students across STEM, social sciences, and Islamic studies, semi-structured interviews with 48 participants, content analysis of 200 student-curated religious items on WhatsApp and Instagram, and simulated platform analysis of TikTok and YouTube recommendation systems over 14 days. Our results demonstrate that the relationship between digital literacy and religious moderation varies significantly across academic disciplines due to distinct epistemic cultures; for instance, STEM students emphasize procedural verification, while Islamic studies students combine textual authority with interpretive tradition. Furthermore, peer-to-peer digital sharing networks function as informal curation systems that can either strengthen or weaken institutional moderation efforts. The evidence supports a mutually reinforcing pathway rather than a unidirectional causal chain: digital literacy enables moderation indirectly through source criticism and resistance to algorithmic reinforcement, while moderation is associated with stronger social cohesion when supported by dialogue and institutional framing. The strongest protective factor across disciplines is the combination of epistemic culture, digital literacy, and institutional support for moderation. This research contributes a multi-level framework that integrates individual competencies with structural factors, offering actionable insights for designing targeted interventions in Indonesian higher education that strengthen both digital competencies and social harmony.</p>','["digital literacy, religious moderation, social cohesion, university students."]',NULL,22,1,'40 - 66','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1322','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',3),
(1323,1323,1,'Impact of Metaverse-Based Immersive Learning on Multiliteracy Skills and Character Development in Primary Education','<p>The growing integration of immersive technologies into education has highlighted the need for empirical evidence on their effectiveness, particularly at the primary school level where research remains limited. This study investigates the impact of a Metaverse-Based Multiliteracy Virtual Laboratory (MVL) on students’ multiliteracy competencies and character values. Employing a quasi-experimental non-equivalent control group design, the study involved two intact classes, with the experimental group receiving instruction through the MVL and the control group learning via conventional methods. Pre-test and post-test assessments were administered to evaluate changes in multiliteracy and character outcomes. The findings reveal significant improvements for the experimental group compared to the control group. Post-test multiliteracy scores were considerably higher for students using the MVL (M = 82.60, SD = 6.40) than for those in the control class (M = 69.20, SD = 7.10), with a large effect size (t(48) = 6.87, p &lt; 0.001, d = 1.95). Similarly, character values showed meaningful gains in the experimental group (M = 85.10, SD = 5.90) relative to the control group (M = 72.40, SD = 6.20), supported by strong statistical significance (t(48) = 7.12, p &lt; 0.001, d = 2.04). These results indicate that immersive, scenario-based learning embedded in the metaverse substantially enhances multiliteracy development and character formation. This study contributes empirical validation to an area dominated by conceptual and development-oriented research, offering robust evidence that metaverse learning environments can produce measurable academic and socio-emotional gains. The findings underscore the potential of immersive digital media to strengthen literacy instruction and character education in primary schools while informing future efforts to integrate metaverse-based learning into broader educational ecosystems.</p>','["Character Values", "Metaverse", "Multiliteracy", "Virtual Laboratory"]',NULL,22,1,'67 - 80','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1323','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',4),
(1324,1324,1,'Multilingual Visitor Information as a Planning Instrument for Sustainable Rural Tourism: Evidence from Flores Island, Indonesia','<p>Sustainable tourism development in rural and less-favoured areas requires planning instruments that not only regulate spatial use but also guide visitor behavior in environmentally and culturally sensitive contexts. Flores Island, Indonesia, represents a rural tourism destination experiencing growing international visitation alongside challenges related to environmental protection, cultural heritage preservation, and limited destination management capacity. This study aims to develop and evaluate a multilingual visitor information booklet as a planning-support instrument for sustainable rural tourism management. Using a research and development approach based on the ADDIE framework, a multilingual booklet in Indonesian, English, and Japanese was designed to integrate sustainability-oriented information, cultural interpretation, and guidance on appropriate visitor behavior. The evaluation involved expert validation focusing on content relevance, linguistic clarity, and visual design, as well as an exploratory assessment of visitor responses to the booklet’s clarity, attractiveness, and perceived usefulness. The results indicate that the developed booklet effectively embeds sustainability-related messages and is positively perceived by both experts and visitors as a source of destination information. The findings suggest that multilingual visitor information can function as a soft planning instrument by reducing information asymmetry, supporting visitor self-regulation, and complementing formal planning and management mechanisms in rural tourism destinations. This study contributes to the literature on sustainable tourism planning by highlighting the role of multilingual information-based interventions as practical and low-cost tools within destination governance frameworks. The findings offer relevant insights for planners and destination managers seeking to align tourism development with environmental sustainability and cultural heritage protection in rural and less-favoured areas.</p>','["destination governance, flores island, multilingual information, rural tourism, sustainable tourism planning, visitor management."]',NULL,22,1,'81 - 93','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1324','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',5),
(1325,1325,1,'From Fragmented Routes to Integrated Governance: Balancing Affordability and Operator Viability in Bogor’s Urban Public Transport','<p>Urban public transport in rapidly growing cities of the Global South faces a persistent tension between passenger affordability and operator financial viability. This paper addresses this challenge through a mixed-methods study conducted in Bogor, Indonesia, where fragmented route networks, individually owned fleets, and aging vehicles have produced unreliable services and uncertain revenues. We adopt a sequential explanatory design that integrates quantitative transport modeling with qualitative institutional analysis. The quantitative phase examines travel patterns, vehicle operating costs, passengers’ ability to pay (ATP), and willingness to pay (WTP) through household and on-board surveys, origin-destination matrices, and PTV VISUM simulations. The qualitative phase draws on semi-structured interviews with transport officials, operators, and users to explore regulatory and governance constraints. Our results reveal a structural imbalance: the vehicle-operating-cost-based fare for conventional minibuses is IDR 5,094 per passenger trip, while average ATP is IDR 4,072 and average WTP is IDR 3,742. This gap indicates that passengers possess greater financial capacity than their stated willingness to pay, suggesting perceived service utility remains insufficient. Furthermore, aggregate seven-year fare revenue covers only 4.3% of estimated long-term operating and fleet-replacement costs. Fleet rationalization analysis shows that an optimized network would require an 80.4% reduction in vehicles, improving the cost-recovery ratio from 0.043 to 0.219. However, even under optimized conditions, fare revenue covers only 21.9% of costs. We therefore propose an integrated four-pillar framework combining demand-based route planning, professional operator management, performance-based financing with transparent subsidies, and enforceable regulatory policies. The study’s primary contribution lies in demonstrating that technical optimization alone cannot resolve the affordability-viability dilemma without corresponding governance reform and sustained fiscal commitment. These findings offer actionable insights for policymakers in Bogor and comparable emerging cities worldwide.</p>','["balancing affordability", "fragmented routes", "integrated governance", "operator viability", "urban public transport."]',NULL,22,1,'94 - 107','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1325','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',6),
(1433,1433,1,'Provisioning An Adaptive Model to Analyze Uncertainty and Large Language Patterns for Enhanced Document Re-Ranking','<p>A fundamental change in information retrieving (IR) has been brought about by the quick development of large language patterns (LLMs), which go beyond standard keyword inquiries and ranked outcome lists. Retrieving-Augmented Generation that followed, a more interactive and lively regaining process that incorporates different facets of Accessibility to data into the conversation amongst an individual and the internet engines for searching and exploring, is one of the new interaction forms introduced by LLMs, which are now crucial to the development of IR technologies. We examine the complex effects of LLMs on IR, focusing on three different layers from which they have become essential to the retrieving process: the interaction layer, the structure for obtaining information and a computation pipeline functionality that can leverage a richer meaning representation through sophisticated language patterns, as well as the larger IR ecology. This work introduces a trust based adaptive reranking model- ATM (Adaptive Trust Model)that allocates computational resources according to file level uncertainty. Instead of assigning a fixed number of reranker calls per query, ATM focuses computation only where ranking confidence is low. This concentrate on prejudice, fairness, and ethical considerations in addition to evaluation challenges for the latter. The model gives 15–30% reduction in floating point operations (FLOPs) and up to 20% lower latency while maintaining or improving retrieval precision. To illustrate the influence on one area of study, we point to a few current examples of LLMs being employed in the medical field</p>','["Information retrieval, Language pattern, Adaptive model, Re-ranking, Computational resource."]',NULL,22,1,'108-126','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1433','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',7),
(1436,1436,1,'A Blockchain-Auditable Cross-Modal Trust Intelligence Model for Crowdfunding Fraud Detection','<p>Persuasive textual narratives, bogus visual evidence, disreputable update patterns and absent accountability systems are being increasingly used to deceive backers in fraudulent crowdfunding campaigns. Current fraud detection techniques are primarily based on static information, on text-only indicators, or on very shallow fusion of multimodal information, and they are not able to detect deceptive information that evolves over time or is inconsistent across different modalities. This study presents a Temporal Cross-Modal Trust Intelligence Framework to mitigate reward-based crowdfunding fraud that is explainable. The framework combines Hidden Method-of-Moments Markov modelling for latent temporal behaviour analysis, Polynomial Expansion Canonical Correlation Analysis for nonlinear text–image consistency evaluation and a Frequency-Gated GRU classifier to distinguish subtle drift in behaviour from sudden suspicious behaviour anomalies. Local Outlier Factor-based risk refinement is also added to detect rare and locally abnormal fraud patterns, and a blockchain-auditable layer ensures prediction outcomes are transparent and tamper-proof, enhancing the decision-making process. Experimental results on a multimodal crowdfunding dataset created from the Kickstarter platform show that the proposed model achieves better accuracy, recall, F1-score, ROC-AUC, PR-AUC, and calibration reliability than conventional multimodal, transformer-based, and recurrent neural network models and classical machine learning. The results validate the proposed solution, which is built on the four temporal dynamics, cross-modal consistency, anomaly refinement and auditability, to be a comprehensive and interpretable solution for detecting early crowdfunding fraud.</p>','["Crowdfunding fraud detection", "multimodal learning", "temporal behaviour modelling", "cross-modal consistency", "frequency-gated GRU", "explainable artificial intelligence", "blockchain auditability", "trust intelligence."]',NULL,22,1,'127-145','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1436','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',8),
(1438,1438,1,'A Model-Based System Engineering and Blockchain-IPFS Framework for Enhancing Decision-Making in National Engineering Accreditation Systems','<p>Indonesia''s national engineering accreditation system faces systemic inefficiencies, governance limitations, and data integrity challenges that hinder international recognition under the Washington Accord and impede the global mobility of engineering graduates. These shortcomings are compounded by fragmented audit trails, opaque decision-making processes, and vulnerability to credential fraud, which undermine trust in accreditation outcomes. This study aims to design a model-based system engineering framework for a blockchain-enabled accreditation system that enhances data integrity, transparency, and traceability to support evidence-based decision-making and Washington Accord compliance. A Systematic Literature Review (SLR) guided by the PRISMA protocol was conducted, synthesizing insights from 109 selected studies on blockchain implementation in accreditation and credential verification systems. The study adopts a Model-Based System Engineering (MBSE) approach using the Requirement-Functional-Logical-Physical framework to translate requirements into a structured system architecture. The proposed framework integrates Hyperledger Fabric as a permissioned blockchain network for immutable record-keeping and the InterPlanetary File System for decentralized off-chain document storage, creating a hybrid on-chain/off-chain architecture. The resulting five-layer framework comprises Participants, Digitalized Access Points, Communication, Distributed Ledgers, and Existing IT Systems layers. Key findings demonstrate that the proposed architecture significantly enhances data integrity through cryptographic verification, provides transparent and tamper-proof audit trails for all accreditation activities, enables real-time verification of document authenticity, and supports interoperability among diverse stakeholders. The framework strengthens decision-making by providing verifiable evidence for informed judgments, ensuring accountability through transparent record-keeping, and enabling cross-border trust in accreditation decisions. This research concludes that the blockchain-IPFS integrated framework offers a scalable and trustworthy pathway for transforming Indonesia''s engineering accreditation system, addressing both domestic governance challenges and international compliance requirements under the Washington Accord.</p>','["Blockchain framework", "data integrity", "decision-making", "engineering accreditation", "model-based system engineering"]',NULL,22,1,'146-161','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1438','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',9),
(1440,1440,1,'A Hybrid Deep Learning Framework for Arecanut Disease Classification Using Efficientnet-B0 And Swin Transformer','<p>Arecanut is an important plantation crop in India, and diseases affecting the crop reduce yield and quality. Timely identification of these diseases can help farmers take appropriate management measures. This study presents a hybrid deep learning model for the classification of five arecanut conditions: Healthy, Rot (Mahali), Split Rot, Bud Rot, and Inflorescence Dieback. The model combines EfficientNet-B0 and the Swin Transformer for image classification. The dataset consists of 3030 field images collected from arecanut plantations in Karnataka, India. Before training, the images were preprocessed using Gaussian denoising, Contrast Limited Adaptive Histogram Equalization (CLAHE), normalization, and data augmentation. The proposed model was compared with standalone EfficientNet-B0 and Swin Transformer models using the same training and testing protocol. The model achieved an overall accuracy of 94.78%, with a macro-average precision of 94.81%, recall of 94.78%, F1-score of 94.79%, and a macro-average AUC of 0.9951. The confusion matrices and ROC curves showed fewer classification errors than the individual baseline models, particularly for the Rot and Split Rot classes. The results show that combining EfficientNet-B0 and the Swin Transformer improves classification performance for arecanut disease identification.</p>','["Arecanut disease classification", "EfficientNet-B0", "Swin Transformer", "Deep learning", "Image classification", "Precision agriculture."]',NULL,22,1,'162-179','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1440','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',10),
(1441,1441,1,'An Explainable and Scalable EEG Framework for Major Depressive Disorder Detection and Severity Staging Through Signal Reconstruction, Channel Optimization, and Neuro-Fuzzy Intelligence','<p>In the modern world, Major Depressive Disorder (MDD) remains a serious global issue affecting mental health. Therefore, a reliable, non-invasive and scalable method for MDD diagnostics should be found. Analysis of EEG recordings shows that it can become the basis for automatic depression diagnosis and severity rating. Unfortunately, performance of models used for solving this problem is hindered by high dimensionality of data, noise, lack of clinical samples and channel redundancy. In this work, we propose a comprehensive method based on signal reconstruction, channel selection, data driven models, neuro-fuzzy systems and data augmentation techniques for identification and severity rating of MDD with EEG. First of all, several channel selection methods were applied to find the best features. Among them were Asymmetric Variance Ratio (AVR), Amplitude Asymmetry Ratio (AAR), Entropy-based Probability Mass Function (PMF) and Recursive Feature Elimination (RFE). The latter proved to be more efficient and selected 11 important channels minimizing computational costs. With the help of those channels, a Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) showed excellent performance achieving 98.7% accuracy, precision of 1.00, recall of 0.966 and F1-score of 0.983. It is significantly better than most common classifiers. Furthermore, to extend our approach to more than two classes, the classification of depression severity (Mild, Moderate, Severe) was conducted using CNN, Adaptive Neuro-Fuzzy Inference Systems (ANFIS) and GAN augmentation technique. The ANFIS proved to be robust and consistent providing 98.34% accuracy with balanced precision-recall values, while GAN allowed us to increase accuracy of severe-stage classification by 15%. In addition, a novel EEG reconstruction framework using Convolutional Autoencoders (CAE), MLP and Variational Autoencoders (VAE) was proposed in order to advance the eminence of the signal and scale up. CAE-MLP combination reached 96% of accuracy for MDD diagnosis, while VAE-based severity rating model showed the accuracy of 97.3% on original dataset and 97.84% on augmented one. Altogether, we managed to show that channel selection, explainable AI, neuro-fuzzy modelling and autoencoder-based signal reconstruction allow developing efficient and scalable approach to automatic diagnosis of depression severity using EEG.</p>','["MLP, Convolutional Autoencoder, ANFIS, CAE-MLP-VAE, MDD, Depression, EEG"]',NULL,22,1,'180-209','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1441','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',11),
(1442,1442,1,'Explainable Temporal Intelligence: Bi-LSTM With SHAP for Early Prediction of Student Academic Performance','<p>The paper presents a student performance prediction model with a Bi-LSTM deep learning model and SHAP to enhance the prediction precision and the timely identification of the academic risk among the students. Bi-LSTM model consists of 1,254 student samples based on the academic and behavioral data. Bi-LSTM with SHAP model consists of 1,300 samples based on the GPA trends, attendance, LMS activity, and behavioral scores. The proposed Bi-LSTM with SHAP achieved an excellent performance in predicting things as opposed to the baseline models. The statistical analysis with a t-test of p = 0.001. The suggested Bi-LSTM with SHAP model enhances the ability to predict the performance of students and early recognize academic risks, which can be used to make improved decisions in the educational process.</p>','["Bi-LSTM", "Student Performance, Risk Detection", "Artificial Intelligence", "Time-Series Modeling", "Educational Analytics", "Learning Analytics", "Deep Learning."]',NULL,22,1,'210-220','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1442','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',12),
(1443,1443,1,'A Self-Calibrating Closed-Loop Framework for Robust Revisit Positioning in Astronomical Imaging','<p>Accurate target repositioning is essential in automated astronomical imaging because previously detected sources must be reliably centred during subsequent observations. Although source detection may be accurate during initial acquisition, revisit operations can still produce substantial positioning errors because of coordinate offsets, scale mismatch, cross-axis coupling, calibration drift, and inconsistencies between image and control-coordinate systems. This study aimed to develop and evaluate a self-calibrating closed-loop framework for correcting such revisit-positioning errors. A publicly available Sloan Digital Sky Survey r-band astronomical image was analysed, and 78 compact sources were detected using DAOStarFinder. A controlled revisit fault combining translation, anisotropic scaling, and horizontal–vertical coordinate coupling was then introduced. Three correction strategies were evaluated: affine coordinate calibration, closed-loop preview correction, and online residual learning. The simulated fault produced a mean centring error of 60.89 pixels, with none of the sources positioned within the predefined 10-pixel tolerance. Affine calibration achieved the best performance, reducing the mean error to 1.25 pixels, representing a 97.9% reduction, and positioning all sources within tolerance. Closed-loop preview correction reduced the mean error to 3.13 pixels and also achieved complete tolerance compliance. Online residual learning reduced the mean error to 18.49 pixels but was less effective because the implemented global-bias model could not represent spatially varying distortions. The findings demonstrate that severe revisit-positioning errors can arise from coordinate-calibration and software-integration faults even when source detection is accurate. The proposed framework provides a reproducible basis for combining geometric calibration, preview-based feedback, adaptive correction, and drift monitoring in automated astronomical revisit-imaging systems.</p>','["Astronomical image analysis", "affine calibration", "closed-loop control", "adaptive residual learning", "coordinate error correction."]',NULL,22,1,'221-233','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1443','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',13),
(1444,1444,1,'A Hybrid Lesion Segmentation and Deep Learning Framework for Intelligent Grape Leaf Disease Classification','<p>Early and accurate detection of grape leaf diseases is crucial for enhancing productivity and precision agriculture. But the traditional deep learning approach tends to classify a whole leaf image, and is easy to be interfered by the background and has poor performance under real-field conditions. The authors suggest a hybrid architecture combining improved SegNet-based lesion segmentation and an Attention-Guided GhostNet architecture with the proposed LGTrp feature learning strategy for classification of grape leaf diseases (AGh-ISegN-DL). The framework first localizes regions of interest in the image with the disease in order to block any irrelevant background information to include in the classification process, and then extracts the discriminative disease specific features for classification. Performance was tested with standard grape leaf disease datasets and also tested with cross-dataset validation with real field AgroVision data set (collected independently). The proposed framework performed better than the evaluated baseline models, such as the previously published Py-ICNN framework, with an accuracy of 96.07%, sensitivity of 96.08%, a precision of 96.05%, a specificity of 99.35% and an F1-score of 96.05%. Ablation analysis showed that the overall classification performance was improved by the synergistic effect of combining lesion segmentation and attention-guided feature learning. Across a new dataset of real-field images acquired independently, with natural environmental variability and class imbalance, the accuracy of 93.10% and the specificity of 97.60%, showed high robustness and generalisation capacity in practical conditions of vineyard. The proposed approach is overall efficient for grape leaf disease diagnosis with intelligence and it has strong potential use in precision agriculture and smart monitoring systems in the vineyard.</p>','["Grape leaf disease classification, disease lesion segmentation, GhostNet, lightweight deep learning, agricultural image analysis, plant disease diagnosis."]',NULL,22,1,'234-261','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1444','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',14),
(1445,1445,1,'A Hybrid Deep Neural Network Approach for Robust Multi-Scale Object Detection in SAR Images','<p>Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) images always provide high-resolution data in all weather and lighting circum-stances. However, speckle noise, clutter backgrounds and scale variation remain as significant challenges for accurate target detection on SAR images. This paper proposes a hybrid deep learning method, which combines Convolutional Neural Networks (CNN), STDNet model and CFAR-based detection, with the objective to enhance performance of multi-scale object detection. The outputs from both the CNN and STDNet branches are fused using an Intersection over Union (IoU)-based fusion strategy helps to achieve better detection accuracy. The experimental results show that the proposed hybrid model reaches better precision, recall, and F1-score than separate classifiers. This proposed approach is a cost-effective and practical strategy for tracking different real-world SAR targets.</p>','["Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR), Target Detec-tion, Global CFAR, Local CFAR, Convolutional Neural Networks (CNN), STDNet, Multi-Resolution Images."]',NULL,22,1,'262-272','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1445','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',15),
(1446,1446,1,'Cardiovascular Disease Classification Using Machine Learning: A Comparative Study with SMOTE Class-Imbalance Correction','<p>Cardiovascular disease (CVD) is the major cause of global mortality, accounting for approximately 17.9 million annual deaths and 32% of all global fatalities. The dataset revealed a real class imbalance of 58% CVD-positive cases (580) and 42% CVD-negative cases (420), with an imbalance ratio of 1.381. Without correction, classifiers trained on this data are biased toward the majority class, inflating accuracy while suppressing sensitivity on the minority class. To correct class imbalance using the Synthetic Minority Over-sampling Technique (SMOTE) on the training set, compare six supervised machine learning classifiers for binary CVD detection, and evaluate their performance on the original test set.  Six classifiers Decision Tree, KNN, SVM, Gradient Boosting, Random Forest, and Logistic Regression were evaluated. A stratified 80:20 train-test split was applied, followed by SMOTE only on the training set (336 → 464 samples per class). To prevent data leakage, SMOTE was embedded within an imbalanced-learn Pipeline. Performance on the original test distribution was evaluated using Accuracy, Precision, Recall, F1 Score, MCC, and AUC-ROC. After SMOTE balancing, Logistic Regression and Random Forest achieved the highest F1 score (0.9871), with AUC-ROC values of 0.9982 and 0.9990, respectively. Gradient Boosting achieved the highest AUC-ROC (0.9993) and was the most stable model in cross-validation (99.20% ± 0.98%). Feature importance analysis identified maxheartrate, oldpeak, and noofmajorvessels as the top three discriminative predictors. The pipeline approach ensured no data leakage in cross-validation, and the findings support ensemble ML models with SMOTE as a preprocessing step for imbalanced CVD datasets.</p>','["cardiovascular disease", "machine learning", "supervised classification", "random forest", "gradient boosting", "AUC-ROC", "clinical decision support", "feature importance", "Matthew\\u2019s correlation coefficient."]',NULL,22,1,'273-294','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1446','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',16),
(1449,1449,1,'Investor Decision-Making in Robo-Advisory Services: A Trust-Mediated Perspective','<p>Robo-advisory services offer automated, algorithm-based investment advice and portfolio management, enabling cost efficiency and wider access to financial services. Despite these advantages, adoption in emerging economies remains limited due to psychological, cognitive, and social barriers. Trust is particularly critical in financial decision-making, as it reduces perceived risk and uncertainty associated with algorithm-driven systems. Trust is an important component, since adoption depends on people''s faith in technological advances and the safety of their investments. The study purposes to contribute to the larger in the field of financial technology analysing the factors which impact on the adoption of Robo-advisory services and provide new insights into the psychological and social dynamics. Using a quantitative research design, primary data were collected from 285 investors through a structured questionnaire. Partial Least Squares-Structural Equation Modeling (PLS-SEM) was employed to test the proposed relationships among financial awareness, behavioral biases, innovativeness, subjective norms, trust, and intention to use robo-advisory services. The results indicate that trust is the strongest predictor of adoption intention and significantly mediates the effects of key antecedent variables. The findings could help policy-makers and financial institutions to enhance the features of robo-advisory services with targeted interventions and marketing strategies.</p>','["Trust, Behavioral biases, Financial awareness, Intention to adopt, Robo-advisory services.."]',NULL,22,1,'295-310','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1449','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',17),
(1451,1451,1,'XAI-Enhanced Hybrid CNN–Transformer Framework For Multi-Class Lung Disease Classification','<p>Chest radiograph images have become a critical research area for applying deep learning in radiological interpretation for the classification of pulmonary diseases. But, to achieve both high accuracy and good interpretability continues to be a major hurdle for many researchers. In this research, we offer a hybrid architecture that incorporates CNNs and Transformer techniques for classifying different respiratory diseases using chest radiograph images. The CNN component provides a mechanism to capture many of the fine, local details found in an image, while the Transformer provides a self-attentive mechanism to capture the overall context of an X-ray image. In addition, a range of approaches exist to improve overall performance of the CNN and Transformer architecture, including structured preprocessing, data augmentation and class balancing. All of these techniques will improve model learning performance and help to effectively manage class imbalance when dealing with imbalanced datasets. To make our model more transparent to users and clinically useful, we employed explainability methods like Grad-CAM and Attention Visualizations to provide users with evidence of the specific area in an X-ray where the model is basing its prediction, thereby providing a greater amount of trust on the part of radiologists in interpreting the model''s output. Based on our findings from testing the 6 Classes Chest Xray dataset, the proposed system proved to achieve a very impressive final testing accuracy of 94.42%. It classifies tuberculosis and healthy patients particularly well, with precision, recall, and F1-scores of 0.99 and 0.97, respectively, but provides good performance across the other disease types too. Furthermore, confidence analysis of predicted labels exhibited that when there was an accurate prediction, the assigned probability score was usually much higher than the assigned probability score for an incorrect prediction. Thus, these results suggest that the hybrid CNN-Transformer model provides a strong level of diagnostic accuracy and meaningfully understood visual rationale so it can serve as an excellent decision support mechanism for hospitals and radiologists in their daily operations.</p>','["Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI), Chest Radiograph Analysis", "Hybrid CNN\\u2013Vision Transformer", "Multi-class Classification", "Deep Learning."]',NULL,22,1,'311-328','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1451','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',18),
(1452,1452,1,'Influence of Alkali Treatment on the Mechanical Properties of COIR Fiber Polymer Composites','<p>This study presents the design, fabrication, and characterization of sustainable coir fiber–reinforced polymer composites based on cashew nut shell liquid (CNSL)–modified epoxy resin, with potential relevance to tribological and structural applications. Three composite configurations—interlocked untreated coir fibers, randomly oriented untreated fibers, and NaOH-treated coir fibers—were manufactured using the vacuum bagging technique with an epoxy-to-hardener ratio of 1:1.25 and CNSL content ranging from 15 to 20 wt.%. The composites were evaluated through tensile, flexural, and impact testing to assess their mechanical performance, while Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR), thermogravimetric analysis (TGA), and scanning electron microscopy (SEM) were employed to analyze chemical structure, thermal stability, and fracture morphology, respectively. The results indicate that interlocked coir fiber composites exhibit superior mechanical strength and structural integrity compared to randomly oriented systems, attributed to improved load transfer and fiber interlocking. The incorporation of CNSL enhanced impact resistance and thermal stability, which are critical for durability under service conditions involving repeated loading and surface interactions. However, excessive NaOH treatment was found to reduce impact toughness due to fiber degradation. The outcomes highlight the potential of CNSL-modified coir fiber composites as eco-friendly materials for tribology-related components in automotive, construction, and light engineering applications. Future research should focus on optimizing fiber surface treatments, fiber loading, and wear behavior to further enhance tribological performance.</p>','["Coir fiber composite, CNSL, epoxy resin, sun light dried mercerization, mechanical properties, FTIR, SEM, TGA."]',NULL,22,1,'329-345','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1452','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',19),
(1453,1453,1,'Functional MRI–Based Deep Learning Framework for PTSD Classification Using Independent Components','<p>Post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD) is a prevalent mental health condition resulting from exposure to traumatic events and affects millions of people. This study leverages advanced neuroimaging techniques, including functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI), to investigate the dominant independent components (ICs) using Independent Components Analysis (ICA) and IC-based activation maps. Additionally, it introduces an automated ICA-based three-dimensional convolutional neural network (3D-CNN) using the most dominant ICs to diagnose PTSD. The study analyzes fMRI data from 32 PTSD patients and 36 healthy controls (HCs) to identify key regions of interest. The ICA-based 3D-CNN model classifies PTSD from HCs using the most dominant ICs derived from ICA. The training, testing partition with data augmentation was also adopted. Performance metrics, including accuracy, sensitivity, specificity, precision, F-1 score, loss rate, false-positive rate (FPR), false-negative rate (FNR), and the Receiver operating characteristic curve (ROC) with Area under the curve (AUC), are used to evaluate the model. Results highlight the top 5 Middle temporal gyrus Right, Left Occipital Superior, calcarine Left, Frontal Medial Orbital, and Right Frontal Superior Gyrus were obtained as most activated in PTSDs. For HCs, regions such as the Anterior Cingulate_Left, Right Superior Parietal Gyrus, Cerebellum 9 Left, Inferior Frontal Gyrus, Orbital Part_Left, and Right Lateral Occipital were prominent. The top ICs for PTSD were IC-3, IC-7, IC-9, IC-10, IC-12, IC-13, IC-15, IC-17, IC-20, and IC-24) and for HCs were (IC-4, IC-5, IC-6, IC-12, IC-13, IC-15, IC-17, IC-19, IC-21, and IC-22). The proposed models outperform baseline architectures, achieving 98.10% accuracy on the training data and 98% on the test data. The study underscores the potential of integrating functional imaging with machine learning to yield robust, sensitive diagnostic models. These findings provide valuable insights for future research and clinical applications in PTSD classification.</p>','["Post-traumatic stress disorder", "Magnetic Resonance Imaging", "independent component analysis", "Healthcare."]',NULL,22,1,'346-365','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1453','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',20),
(1454,1454,1,'Case-Based Explainable Generative AI as a Framework for Trustworthy Digital Innovation','<p>The rapid adoption of Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) has accelerated digital innovation across diverse sectors; however, challenges such as hallucination, limited transparency, and insufficient reasoning traceability continue to restrict its deployment in high-stakes applications. This study proposes a Case-Based Explainable Generative Artificial Intelligence (CB-XGenAI) framework that integrates Case-Based Reasoning (CBR), Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI), and Large Language Models (LLMs) to establish an evidence-driven and trustworthy reasoning architecture. The proposed framework consists of six interconnected modules: input processing, semantic case retrieval, case adaptation, explainable reasoning generation, trustworthiness validation, and continuous case learning. Semantic retrieval is implemented using BAAI/bge-large-en-v1.5 embeddings and a FAISS vector database, while Llama 3.1-8B-Instruct performs adaptive generative reasoning. The framework is evaluated on five benchmark datasets, namely Natural Questions, SQuAD 2.0, CNN/DailyMail, MMLU, and Alpaca, using retrieval accuracy, explanation quality, factual consistency, user trust, and overall performance as evaluation metrics. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed framework achieves an overall performance of 93.4%, consistently outperforming conventional LLMs, Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), Explainable AI-enhanced LLMs, and traditional CBR approaches. Ablation analysis further confirms that semantic case retrieval and explainable reasoning are the primary contributors to system effectiveness, while statistical validation using paired t-tests, one-way ANOVA, and confidence intervals verifies that the observed improvements are statistically significant (p &lt; 0.001). The findings demonstrate that integrating case-based reasoning with explainable generative intelligence substantially enhances transparency, factual reliability, adaptability, and user trust, providing a scalable foundation for trustworthy AI in digital innovation.</p>','["Case-Based Reasoning (CBR)", "Generative Artificial Intelligence", "Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI)", "Trustworthy Digital Innovation."]',NULL,22,1,'366-400','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1454','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',21);
<p>Methods: A quasi-experimental design was employed with 102 healthcare professionals from four hospitals in Jazan Health Cluster. Participants were assigned to control groups (n=52) using paper-based surgical time-out checklists or experimental groups (n=50) implementing the Vida App. Data were collected at baseline (T1), post-intervention (T2), and three-month follow-up (T3) using the TeamSTEPPS Teamwork Perceptions Questionnaire (T-TPQ). Repeated-measures ANOVA, t-tests, and Pearson correlations analyzed changes across six constructs: Team Structure, Leadership, Communication, Mutual Support, Situation Monitoring, and Patient Safety.</p>
<p>Results: Experimental groups showed large to massive within-group improvements (partial η² = 0.282–0.719, p &lt; .001), with mean scores rising from 2.41–2.82 at T1 to 4.04–4.95 at T3. Net gains versus controls were 1.58–1.80 points (Cohen''s d = 1.58–1.87). Post-intervention correlations with Patient Safety were strong (r = 0.717–0.867). Controls exhibited trivial changes (η² = 0.002–0.079).</p>
<p>Conclusion: The Vida App significantly enhances teamwork and patient safety perceptions, offering a scalable digital solution for perioperative care in developing healthcare systems.</p>
<p>Implications for Nursing Management: Nurse managers in resource-limited settings, including Saudi public hospitals, can adopt mobile EMR tools like the Vida App to address documentation inefficiencies, reduce communication breakdowns, and foster high-reliability teams. This supports Vision 2030 healthcare digitization goals by overcoming barriers such as digital literacy gaps and infrastructure constraints through user-friendly, offline-capable technology. Managers should prioritize targeted training, policy integration for EMR adoption, ongoing monitoring of teamwork metrics, and leadership support to sustain gains in patient safety and collaborative practice.</p>','["Vida Mobile Application", "electronic medical record", "perioperative nursing", "TeamSTEPPS", "teamwork effectiveness", "patient safety", "quasi-experimental study."]',NULL,22,1,'489-503','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1718','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',26),
(1719,1719,1,'An Explainable Transformer-Based Machine Learning Framework for Bilirubin-Derived Severity Classification of Hepatitis B: A Cross-Domain Validation Study','<p>Background: Accurate and comprehensible severity evaluation is necessary for chronic hepatitis B (HBV), which continues to be a significant worldwide health concern. Complex nonlinear interactions among clinical biomarkers are frequently missed by conventional clinical ratings.</p>
<p>Objective: Using a novel TabTransformer model, create and assess an explainable artificial intelligence (XAI) framework for four-class bilirubin-based hepatitis severity categorization.</p>
<p>Methods: Patients were categorized into Low, Mild, Moderate, and Severe bilirubin-based groups using the UCI Hepatitis dataset (N = 155). MICE was used to impute missing values. Using stratified five-fold cross-validation, the suggested TabTransformer was contrasted with Logistic Regression, SVM, Random Forest, Gradient Boosting, AdaBoost, and MLP. The Indian Liver Patient Dataset (ILPD, N = 583) was used for external validation. SHAP and permutation feature significance were used to assess the model''s interpretability.</p>
<p>Results: The TabTransformer outperformed all baseline models, achieving 91.2% accuracy, 0.908 weighted F1-score, 0.961 macro-AUC-ROC, and a Brier score of 0.089. The Severe class has an AUC of 0.983, suggesting great discrimination despite the class imbalance. External validation showed good generality, and SHAP identified ascites, prothrombin time, and albumin as the most influential predictors, which is congruent with current clinical knowledge.</p>
<p>Conclusions: The proposed explainable TabTransformer paradigm enhances hepatitis severity classification while making clinically meaningful predictions. These findings demonstrate the possibility of transformer-based tabular learning for liver disease risk classification; however, prospective multicenter validation is required before clinical use.</p>','["hepatitis B", "severity classification", "TabTransformer", "explainable AI", "SHAP", "bilirubin", "MICE", "machine learning."]',NULL,22,1,'504-527','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1719','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',27),
(1721,1721,1,'Dynamic Decision-Making on Trade Receivables under COVID-19: Moderating Roles of Firm Characteristics in an Emerging Market','<p>Trade credit management is a critical financial decision that firms must continuously recalibrate in response to systemic risk. This study examines how the COVID-19 pandemic altered this decision process by analyzing its impact on the speed at which firms adjust trade receivables toward their target level. Using the Dynamic Panel Fractional estimator on an unbalanced panel of 583 non-financial firms listed on the Vietnamese stock market over 2015–2024, the study documents that the COVID-19 pandemic significantly increases the speed of trade receivables adjustment. This acceleration reflects stronger credit risk avoidance incentives and intensified liquidity pressures during periods of severe macroeconomic uncertainty. Furthermore, the analysis shows that financial leverage and state ownership mitigate the COVID-19-induced increase in adjustment speed, whereas board size amplifies it. These findings provide evidence that capital structure and ownership act as structural buffers that dampen crisis period adjustment while board governance serves as an enabling mechanism that strengthens firms’ capacity to respond to heightened credit risk. Overall, the results extend the dynamic trade-off theory of trade receivables to the context of systemic shocks and offer practical implications for designing more flexible and risk-aware trade credit policies in emerging markets.</p>','["Corporate trade credit decisions", "COVID-19 pandemic", "dynamic trade-off theory", "financial leverage", "state ownership", "board governance", "emerging market."]',NULL,22,1,'528-558','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1721','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',28),
(1722,1722,1,'Postgraduate Academic Difficulties: Multidimensional Perception Gaps between Students and Coordinators at an Ecuadorian Public University','<p>This study examines the academic difficulties perceived by postgraduate students and the perception gaps between students and program coordinators at an Ecuadorian public university. Most available studies rely on a single informant, typically the students themselves, which limits the possibility of contrasting self-perceptions with the institution''s own reading of the problem. To address this gap, a cross-sectional, non-experimental, descriptive-comparative design was employed, drawing on 809 cleaned student records from 56 programs and responses from 57 coordinators. Variables were organized into three dimensions: knowledge, time management, and the teaching-learning process. Internal consistency, dimensional structure via confirmatory factor analysis, gaps on a 0-100 scale, Cohen''s d effect sizes, and bootstrap confidence intervals were calculated. All three dimensions showed adequate to high internal consistency. Students reported greater difficulty with teacher demands, multiple assignments, and lack of time, whereas coordinators consistently perceived greater intensity, particularly in reading, research, technology, and argumentation; the widest gap was observed for the knowledge dimension (d = 0.59). A robustness analysis by disciplinary area showed homogeneous difficulty levels across the seven areas but heterogeneous student-coordinator gaps, larger in Law and Social Sciences than in Business Administration. The study concludes that institutional support strategies should triangulate student self-perception with the institutional reading of the problem, prioritizing the areas with the largest gaps.</p>','["Postgraduate education", "academic difficulties", "student perception", "program coordinators", "higher education", "Latin America", "multi-informant assessment."]',NULL,22,1,'559-578','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1722','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',29),
(1723,1723,1,'Cultural Certainty and Tax Compliance: An Analysis of Uncertainty Avoidance and Trust in Tax Authorities Among SME Taxpayers','<p>This study aims to examine the influence of uncertainty avoidance on the tax compliance of small and medium-sized enterprise (SME) taxpayers in Indonesia, with trust in tax authorities serving as a mediating variable. Tax compliance is not only understood as a response to legal obligations but also as a behavior influenced by cultural orientation and taxpayers’ perceptions of tax authorities. This research employs a quantitative approach using a survey method involving 283 SME taxpayers in Makassar City. The data were analyzed using Partial Least Squares Structural Equation Modeling (PLS-SEM) to test both direct and indirect relationships among the variables.</p>
<p>The results indicate that uncertainty avoidance has a positive and significant effect on taxpayer compliance. Furthermore, uncertainty avoidance positively influences trust in tax authorities. Trust in tax authorities is also found to have a positive effect on taxpayer compliance and partially mediates the relationship between uncertainty avoidance and taxpayer compliance. These findings suggest that taxpayers’ need for certainty, procedural clarity, and rule predictability can enhance compliance, particularly when tax authorities are perceived as fair, transparent, consistent, and trustworthy. This study contributes to the tax compliance literature by proposing a cultural-institutional model to explain the compliance behavior of SME taxpayers.</p>','["uncertainty avoidance", "trust in tax authorities", "taxpayer compliance", "SMEs", "PLS-SEM."]',NULL,22,1,'579-591','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1723','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',30),
(1724,1724,1,'Computer Vision Approaches For Smart Farming: Yield Prediction and Monitoring','<p>Smart farming is a combination of sophisticated technologies that are used to maximize agricultural output and sustainability. Computer vision is one of these technologies that have become a force to be used in automating crop monitoring and yield prediction. This paper discusses the different computer vision techniques applied in agricultural yield forecasting, and primarily looks at deep learning-based techniques applied to both aerial and ground images. The research focuses on what the recent literature indicates as the effectiveness of the convolutional neural networks (CNNs), vision transformers (ViTs), and object detectors as a form of estimation of yield in large crops through the analysis of secondary data and model performance. The methodology entails a comparative study of the techniques of counting the images of the plant, estimating the canopy cover as well as the technique of biomass modeling. It has been shown that CNN-based methods have an average accuracy in yield predictions of 88 percent and ViT models outperform by up to 93 percent on the same metric as they are able to capture long-range correlations in aerial images. UAV imagery used along with computer vision methods will greatly minimize the time spent in data collection using manual means and enhance farm management decisions. The paper concludes that precision agriculture can be assisted by the adoption of computer vision approaches since it facilitates early, scalable, and accurate yield prediction, which eventually will improve productivity and sustainability.</p>','["Computer Vision, Smart farming, Yield forecasting, UAV imagery, Deep learning, Precision agriculture."]',NULL,22,1,'592-598','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1724','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',31),
(1725,1725,1,'Context-Adaptive Multimodal Inception Network With Aquila Optimization For Automated Rheumatic Heart Disease Classification','<p>Rheumatic Heart Disease (RHD) continues to be a leading contributor to cardiovascular illness and death, especially in low- and middle-income regions where access to specialist consultation and advanced diagnostics is limited. Although deep learning has advanced automated detection of cardiac conditions, most current methods depend on single-modality inputs or employ fixed multimodal fusion schemes that do not fully leverage complementary structural and acoustic cues. In addition, traditional hyperparameter search methods frequently fail to find optimal configurations for complex multimodal networks, resulting in diminished diagnostic accuracy, poor probability calibration, and limited clinical transparency. To overcome these shortcomings, this work introduces a Context-Adaptive Multimodal Inception Network coupled with an Aquila-guided bilevel hyperparameter optimization approach for automated RHD classification using echocardiographic images and phonocardiographic recordings. The architecture uses multiscale Inception modules to learn salient features across diverse spatial scales, followed by a context-adaptive cross-modal attention module that dynamically allocates emphasis to structural versus acoustic representations based on their diagnostic importance. A bilevel optimization scheme steered by the Aquila Optimization Algorithm concurrently tunes hyperparameters while model weights are updated via gradient-based learning. To bolster performance on imbalanced clinical datasets, focal loss is adopted, and Youden Index-driven threshold selection, together with temperature scaling, enhances sensitivity and probability calibration. The proposed system was validated through baseline comparisons, ablation experiments, optimizer analyses, calibration assessments, statistical significance testing, and Grad-CAM-driven interpretability studies. Results indicate improved performance over representative convolutional and transformer-based baselines, reaching an overall accuracy of 91% and an AUC of 0.91, while markedly enhancing calibration and diagnostic dependability. Quantitative explainability assessments also reveal strong concordance between model attention maps and expert-annotated cardiac regions. The presented framework delivers an accurate, robust, and interpretable multimodal decision-support tool for automated RHD screening, with potential to facilitate earlier detection and AI-assisted clinical decisions, particularly in resource-limited healthcare environments.</p>','["Rheumatic Heart Disease", "Deep Learning", "Multimodal Learning", "Cross-Modal Attention", "Aquila Optimization Algorithm", "Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI)", "Echocardiography", "Phonocardiography."]',NULL,22,1,'599-626','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1725','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',32),
(1726,1726,1,'Fractal-Aware Masked Vascular Transformer for OCTA-Based Retinal Disease Classification via Graph-Guided Self-Supervised Learning','<p>Optical Coherence Tomography Angiography (OCTA) is a high-resolution imaging tool that captures the lens-less imaging of microvasculature from the retina, and has become a key imaging tool for automated diagnosis of retinal diseases. But current methods are primarily based on the transformers trained on ImageNet, and the OCTA images are regarded as natural images, ignoring the vascular topology and structural complexity of retinal pathologies. In this paper, we propose a two-stage framework called Fractal-Aware Masked Vascular Transformer (FAMV-T) which consists of a graph-guided Masked Vascular Modeling (MVM) for topology-aware self-supervised representation learning and a Fractal Attention Bias (FAB) module for fractal-guided attention refinement in supervised fine-tuning. The proposed framework leverages the anatomical meaning of retinal vessels connectivity and the local fractal complexity to learn anatomically meaningful and disease-sensitive representations for retinal disease classification using transformer learning. The experimental results on the OCTA-500 dataset show that FAMV-T always performs better than the representative CNN and transformer-based methods. Qualitative visualization also suggests the proposed framework can effectively localize clinically relevant vascular abnormalities, which enhances the model interpretability. The results highlight the importance of adding vascular topology and fractal morphology to transformer learning for more meaningful and discriminative representations of automated classification of retinal diseases using OCTA.</p>','["Optical Coherence Tomography Angiography (OCTA)", "Retinal Disease Classification", "Self-Supervised Learning", "Swin Transformer", "Vascular Topology", "Fractal Attention Bias", "Graph-Based Masked Vascular Modeling."]',NULL,22,1,'627-644','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1726','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',33),
(1727,1727,1,'Policy Implementation and Institutional Gaps: Evidence from Certified Palm Oil Seed Governance Based on Early Detection Technology in Indonesia','<p>The Smallholder Oil Palm Replanting (PSR) program is a national strategic initiative in Indonesia that aims to address productivity and legality issues in the palm oil industry. These issues include the ongoing widespread use of illegal oil palm seeds by smallholders in Riau Province. To prevent future declines in oil palm productivity, this research aims to review the implementation of oil palm seed certification service policies and the variables influencing the concept, in an effort to achieve sustainable oil palm growth in Riau Province through early detection technology. A qualitative, descriptive method was adopted to systematically explain the inaccurate implementation of oil palm seed certification service policies and the factors influencing the process. Furthermore, NVivo 12 Plus software was used to analyze the data and answer the proposed questions. The results showed that the current policy implementation failed due to various weaknesses and complexities. This emphasized the importance of more specific regulations and a stronger role for local actors in the oil palm industry to ensure compliance with seed certification service policies, promoting sustainable oil palm growth in Riau Province. Local governments served as the main actors in overcoming obstacles, with a more specific policy and regulatory framework. The private sector supported farmers and the government in strengthening implementation and supervision through early-detection technology. This research also made a theoretical contribution by expanding the policy implementation model in the agribusiness sector, strengthening the digital-based seed supervision model. Meanwhile, the empirical and practical contribution was to strengthen a more applicable governance model for the Smallholder Oil Palm Replanting (PSR) program in Indonesia. This study has limitations because the data and research area only covered a few areas in Riau Province. The novelty of this research lies in the context of oil palm seed certification governance, the illegal seed market, and the gap in implementing agricultural certification, which provides new insights on emphasizing the need for more specific and detailed policies since certification has not been effectively implemented due to the widespread illegal seed market and implementation gap.</p>','["Certification Services", "Early Detection Technology", "Oil Palm Seeds", "Policy", "Sustainable Development."]',NULL,22,1,'645-663','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1727','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',34),
(1729,1729,1,'Multimodal Deep Learning for Data-Driven Delirium Prediction in ICU: A Comprehensive Review using MIMIC-IV Dataset','<p>Delirium is a prevalent neurocognitive disorder among critically ill patients and is associated with increased morbidity, mortality, and prolonged intensive care unit (ICU) stays. Given that delirium frequently remains undetected, its identification is essential for safeguarding the health and well-being of affected patients. This review introduces the Medical Information Mart for Intensive Care IV (MIMIC-IV) dataset, which is accessible via PhysioNet, to investigate its potential for detecting and predicting life-threatening diseases. Electronic health records (EHRs) function as repositories of patient information pertinent to the development of predictive models for healthcare solutions. MIMIC-IV encompasses a comprehensive collection of patient databases within three modules: hosp, icu, and note facilitating multimodal analysis for advanced clinical modeling. Moreover, this review delineates classical statistical models, such as logistic and Cox regression, as benchmarks and discusses their limitations. Furthermore, it reveals the evolution of supervised machine learning and deep learning techniques for delirium prediction, emphasizing their advantages in handling multi-modal time-series data. A comprehensive study conducted from 2014 to 2025 identified research gaps and addressed challenges, including data class imbalance, label noise, missing data, and time-series data issues, and proposed solutions. Future research should focus on federated learning approaches for privacy-centric prediction models and the integration of real-time ICU tools for clinical decision support systems. In conclusion, this review used MIMIC-IV as a critical resource for advancing intelligent data-driven delirium prediction models and bridging the gap between research and clinical deployment in critical care environments.</p>','["Delirium Prediction, Electronic Health Records, Traditional Statistical Models, Supervised Machine Learning Models, Deep Learning Models"]',NULL,22,1,'664-692','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1729','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',35),
(1730,1730,1,'A Case-Based Verification Framework for Detecting and Reducing Hallucinations in Generative AI','<p>Generative artificial intelligence has become an important technology for knowledge creation, education, healthcare, finance, and organizational decision-making. However, its practical adoption is limited by hallucinations, where generated responses contain fabricated, unsupported, outdated, contextually inappropriate, logically inconsistent, or incorrectly cited information. Existing verification approaches frequently assess responses independently, rely on imperfect evidence retrieval, and lack mechanisms for reusing previously verified knowledge, resulting in limited adaptability and explainability. This study proposes a Case-Based Verification Framework (CBVF) to improve the reliability of generated responses through experience-driven verification. The framework employs the four stages of Case-Based Reasoning—Retrieve, Reuse, Revise, and Retain—and integrates atomic-claim decomposition, semantic case retrieval, evidence entailment, source-reliability weighting, semantic-entropy estimation, hallucination-risk calibration, selective verification, explainable correction, and incremental case-base maintenance. The framework is evaluated using approximately 2,000 prompt–response instances constructed from six publicly available benchmark datasets spanning general knowledge, multi-hop reasoning, scientific verification, citation verification, temporal reasoning, and hallucination evaluation. Performance is compared with unverified generation, self-consistency verification, retrieval-augmented generation, and retrieval-augmented generation combined with rule-based fact-checking using claim groundedness, evidence support, calibration quality, hallucination reduction, selective verification, correction safety, retrieval effectiveness, and Bayesian multilevel analysis. The experimental evaluation demonstrates that the proposed framework consistently outperforms the baseline approaches by improving factual grounding, evidence alignment, calibration accuracy, and retrieval quality while substantially reducing hallucinations and preserving response relevance and semantic meaning. The Bayesian analysis further confirms statistically robust improvements across multiple benchmark domains. The findings indicate that integrating Case-Based Reasoning with evidence-driven verification provides an adaptive, explainable, and continuously improving mechanism for enhancing the trustworthiness of generative artificial intelligence in applications requiring reliable and evidence-supported information.</p>','["Case-Based Reasoning (CBR)", "Generative Artificial Intelligence", "Hallucination Detection", "Evidence-Based Verification", "Large Language Models (LLMs)"]',NULL,22,1,'693-720','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1730','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',36),
(1731,1731,1,'Virtual Reality for Environmental Education in Higher Education Institutions: A Systematic Review','<p>This systematic review examines the application of Virtual Reality (VR) in environmental education within higher education institutions, framed by the United Nations Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) and the demand for innovative teaching methods that promote environmental awareness. Using the PRISMA framework, 17 empirical studies published between 2017 and 2024 were analysed, covering the implementation, usage and impacts of VR adoption, particularly from the perspective of SDGs such as environmental protection, climate change, and pollution. The findings reveal that VR is primarily implemented through two methods, interactive virtual environments and 360-degree videos, with both modalities were generally associated with improvements in environmental knowledge, emotional engagement, risk perception, and pro-environmental behavioural intentions. Virtual field trips were identified as a particularly effective usage for increasing intrinsic motivation while reducing the carbon footprint associated with traditional fieldwork. Although challenges remain - including limited theoretical grounding, varied sample sizes, high costs, and potential physical discomfort - VR demonstrates promising potential as a pedagogical tool to foster environmental responsibility, sustainable behaviours, and critical awareness of ecological issues in higher education.</p>','["Virtual reality, environmental education, higher education, sustainability, climate change, immersive learning, pro-environmental behaviour."]',NULL,22,1,'721-744','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1731','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',37),
(1732,1732,1,'Hybrid CNN-LSTM Channel Estimation for 5G Massive MIMO Systems using Sparse Pilot Reconstruction and Time-Varying 3GPP Channels','<p>Massive multiple-input multiple-output (MIMO) technology underpins the spectral efficiency targets of fifth-generation (5G) New Radio (NR) networks, and its performance depends critically on accurate channel state information at the receiver. Classical pilot-based channel estimators face three fundamental constraints: pilot overhead that scales with the antenna count, matrix inversion complexity that grows cubically with the array size, and severe noise sensitivity at low signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) or under high-mobility conditions. The objective of this study is to develop a channel estimator that accurately reconstructs the full time-frequency channel response from a sparse pilot grid while remaining computationally feasible for real-time operation. A two-stage hybrid deep learning estimator is proposed in which least-squares estimates at pilots placed at every twelfth subcarrier are first expanded by two-dimensional bilinear interpolation and then refined by a time-distributed convolutional neural network (CNN) coupled with a long short-term memory (LSTM) recurrent stage; the model is trained and evaluated on time-varying 3GPP TR 38.901 tapped delay line channels with Jakes Doppler fading at speeds of up to 120 km/h. Evaluated on a 4×4 MIMO-OFDM link with 624 subcarriers over an SNR range of 0-20 dB, the proposed estimator reduces the normalized mean-squared error by up to 88%, approximately halves the bit-error rate at mid-range SNRs, and raises the spectral efficiency from approximately 0.13 to 4.3-5.0 bits/s/Hz relative to a conventional two-dimensional interpolation baseline, while consuming only half the pilot overhead of a dense-pilot configuration; inference latency on a graphics processing unit is below 1 ms per frame. These results indicate that hybrid CNN-LSTM processing offers a practical route to accurate, low-overhead, and latency-compliant channel estimation for 5G massive MIMO deployments.</p>','["Channel estimation", "Convolutional neural network", "Long short-term memory", "Massive MIMO", "Sparse pilot", "Deep learning", "5G New Radio"]',NULL,22,1,'745-757','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1732','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',38),
(1733,1733,1,'Wearable-Supported Stroke Telerehabilitation in Saudi Health Centres: A 12-Week Single-Arm Feasibility and Implementation Pilot','<p><strong>Introduction:</strong> Post-discharge stroke rehabilitation often depends on intermittent clinic visits and patient or caregiver self-report, limiting clinicians’ ability to monitor home exercise participation, physiological response, and early warning signs. This study evaluated the feasibility, safety, and implementation value of a wearable digital wristband-enabled telerehabilitation model for stroke survivors managed through Saudi health centres.</p>
<p><strong>Material and methods:</strong> A 12-week single-arm feasibility and implementation pilot was conducted with 27 adult stroke survivors medically cleared for home exercise and able to use a smartphone and wristband independently or with minimal assistance. The intervention combined wearable monitoring of heart rate, activity, steps, active minutes, and wear time with a structured home rehabilitation programme, clinician dashboard review, feedback, and escalation for abnormal alerts or poor exercise tolerance. Outcomes included recruitment, retention, adherence, wearable use, monitoring completeness, data quality, safety, exploratory functional outcomes, and clinician workflow indicators.</p>
<p><strong>Results</strong>: Recruitment yield was 79.4%, week-12 retention was 92.6%, and 84.0% of scheduled home rehabilitation sessions were completed. Mean valid wearable use reached 5.4 days per week, and monitoring-complete participant-weeks reached 88.1%. No intervention-related serious adverse events were recorded. Paired exploratory outcomes improved across mobility, walking endurance, functional independence, balance, and stroke-specific quality of life. Clinician review time decreased by 47.8%, dashboard data completeness increased from 58.2% to 89.4%, and alert response time decreased by 55.1%.</p>
<p><strong>Conclusions</strong>: Wearable-supported telerehabilitation was feasible, operationally safe, and potentially useful for structured home-based follow-up after stroke. Larger controlled multicenter studies are required to confirm clinical effectiveness, cost-effectiveness, long-term adherence, and scale-up readiness.</p>','["Remote Consultation", "Feasibility Studies", "Treatment Adherence and Compliance", "Activities of Daily Living", "Quality of Life"]',NULL,22,1,'758-791','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1733','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',39),
(1734,1734,1,'Financial Data Analytics Adoption in Indian MSMEs and SMEs: A Systematic Review, Theoretical Integration, and Future Research Agenda','<p>This paper presents a PRISMA-compliant systematic literature review of the adoption, implementation, and measurable impact of Financial Data Analytics (FDA) in India''s Micro, Small, and Medium Enterprises (MSMEs) and Small and Medium Enterprises (SMEs). Drawing on 61 peer-reviewed studies published between 2010 and 2025, identified through searches of Scopus, Web of Science, and Google Scholar, the review synthesises findings across six thematic clusters: adoption drivers, adoption barriers, fintech and AI/ML integration, firm performance effects, regulatory context, and sectoral variation.</p>
<p>The theoretical contribution is an integrated three-stage adoption model sequencing the Technology Acceptance Model and Diffusion of Innovation theory at the initiation stage, UTAUT and the Resource-Based View at the institutionalisation stage, and Dynamic Capabilities Theory at the capability-building stage. This sequencing resolves a persistent problem: the tendency to apply a single framework to a process that evolves significantly as firms mature analytically.</p>
<p>Core findings: perceived usefulness driven by GST reconciliation pain points and affordable cloud delivery is the dominant predictor of initial adoption; the cost of skilled analysts — not software licensing — is the primary barrier to deeper capability development; fintech-enabled ML credit scoring is improving loan access for MSMEs with digital footprints, but field-trial evidence in Indian contexts remains absent; and manufacturing firms in industrial clusters show a 7-14% ROA premium among analytics adopters, though the performance literature is hampered by reverse-causality and non-standardised outcome measures. The Account Aggregator framework and GST data ecosystem represent institutional infrastructure changes that the reviewed literature has barely begun to theorise.</p>
<p>Methodological gaps dominate: cross-sectional surveys, urban sampling bias, and concentration in four Indian states limit generalisability. Future priorities include longitudinal panel studies, AI/ML lending field trials, ESG analytics integration studies, and algorithmic fairness audits of fintech credit models serving MSME populations.</p>','["financial data analytics, MSMEs, SMEs, India, fintech adoption, technology acceptance, digital transformation, PRISMA"]',NULL,22,1,'792-808','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1734','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',40),
(1735,1735,1,'Beyond Adoption: Behavioural Validation of an Integrated FinTech Engagement Framework in India A PLS-SEM Investigation of Emotional Engagement, Personalization, and Loyalty','<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> This work aims to resolve this research fragmentation around the concept of adoption in FinTech and to present an empirically validated novel quadripartite integrated framework that integrates constructs from Technology Adoption (UTAUT2), Service Quality (DBSQual/SERVQUAL), Information Systems Success (DeLone &amp; McLean) and Customer Experience (CX) theory. The framework and its validation are based on objective behavioural data, distinguishing it from the current literature that has been limited to self-report measures.</p>
<p><strong>Design/Methodology/Approach:</strong> We surveyed 450 active FinTech users across India from mobile payment applications, neobanks and traditional banks digital platforms, a cross-sectional survey. To analyse the data we applied PLS-SEM, drawing inferences from 5,000 resampling iterations. Convergent validity was established with three different objective measures of behaviour based on the platform usage logs (transaction intensity, adoption of a feature, and a Behavioural Engagement Index).</p>
<p><strong>Results:</strong> All except two of the eight hypothesised pathways are statistically significant. The highest Predictors of Emotional Engagement are Personalisation (β = 0.148, p &lt; 0.001), Performance Expectancy (β = 0.105, p &lt; 0.05) and Hedonic Motivation (β = 0.092, p = 0.051 marginal). There is a strong association between Emotional Engagement and User Satisfaction (β = 0.109, p &lt; 0.05) and Behavioural Loyalty (β = 0.230, p &lt; 0.05). Information Quality is the strongest direct driver for User Satisfaction (β = 0.155 and p &lt; 0.01). Trust—rather than Security alone—drives Habit (β = 0.148, p &lt; 0.001). Convergent validity is confirmed with behavioural validation: self-reported usage is correlated with objective transaction logs (r = 0.895); and self-reported satisfaction with the Behavioural Engagement Index (r = 0.862).</p>
<p><strong>Practical Implications:</strong> FinTechs need to build emotional connection with the users by first figuring out how to enhance trust, then the next most effective way to keep users (i.e. data-driven personal experiences and better information quality). It has been shown that investing in trust results in the greatest return on investment with regards to user satisfaction. Therefore, trust is not simply a security measure, it''s a ground level expectation prior to users'' ongoing interaction with platforms.</p>
<p> </p>
<p><strong>Originality/Value:</strong> This is the first study to empirically establish an integrated FinTech adoption framework in the Indian setting using real-time behavioural data, while resolving long-standing measurement concerns and thereby offering a robust methodological template for subsequent work.</p>','["FinTech adoption", "PLS-SEM", "Emotional engagement", "Personalisation", "Behavioural loyalty", "Customer experience"]',NULL,22,1,'809-832','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1735','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',41),
(1736,1736,1,'Stability-Aware Genetic Algorithm Feature Selection and a Calibrated Hybrid Ensemble for Imbalanced Smart-Meter Loss Detection','<p>Reliable screening of electrical loss cases from smart-meter records is difficult because positive cases are rare, raw operational exports contain identifier and timestamp fields that can cause information leakage, and single-run wrapper selection can be unstable. This study develops a stability-aware genetic algorithm and calibrated hybrid ensemble (GA-CIHE) for leakage-controlled loss-status classification. The supplied workbook contains 74,570 records and 22 raw columns. Data auditing removed unique identifiers, timestamps, and constant administrative fields, leaving 15 three-phase voltage, current, and total harmonic distortion measurements. Ten independent genetic-algorithm runs optimized an imbalance-sensitive fitness combining precision-recall area under the curve, receiver-operating-characteristic area under the curve, balanced accuracy, Matthews correlation coefficient, and a sparsity penalty. A 50% consensus rule retained six measurements (V1–V3 and A1–A3), reducing the usable feature space by 60%. Logistic regression, random forest, extra trees, histogram gradient boosting, LightGBM, and XGBoost were evaluated on a stratified untouched test set. A validation-optimized ensemble combining random forest (0.15), histogram gradient boosting (0.45), and LightGBM (0.40) was isotonic-calibrated and operated at a threshold of 0.401. GA-CIHE achieved 0.9903 accuracy, 0.9562 PR-AUC, 0.9957 ROC-AUC, 0.9038 precision, 0.8842 recall, 0.8939 F1, 0.8889 MCC, and a Brier score of 0.0069, with 65 false positives among 14,223 normal test records. Exact McNemar tests showed significantly fewer errors than the corresponding random forest, histogram gradient boosting, and LightGBM baselines. The results demonstrate that stable feature consensus, class-aware evaluation, probability calibration, and validation-only threshold selection can yield a compact and operationally useful decision-support model. External and temporal validation remain necessary before deployment.</p>','["feature selection", "imbalanced classification", "smart meter", "ensemble learning", "probability calibration", "decision support"]',NULL,22,1,'833-854','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1736','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',42),
(1737,1737,1,'A Mathematical Approach by Weighted Fermatean Uncertainty Parameterized Set for Modeling and Assessing Decision Making Issues via Root Mean Square Threshold','<p>In recent times, the integration of fermatean fuzzy sets with multi-criteria decision-making (MCDM) methodologies has emerged as a powerful tool for resolving decision-making challenges. In this paper, we investigate the concept of root mean square threshold for the fermatean uncertainty parameterized set. In light of this new innovation, we analyze the root mean square level soft set in order to provide additional rationale for decision making using fermatean uncertainty parameterized information. Moreover, we propose the concept of a weighted fermatean uncertainty set with two tuple weights and investigate about its utility for decision making problems. Finally, we frame a new algorithm for weighted fermatean fuzzy parameterization set of medical diagnosis problem with suitable example</p>','["Uncertainty set", "Intuitionistic fuzzy set", "Pythagorean fuzzy set", "Fermatean fuzzy set", "Parameterized set", "Root mean square threshold", "Weighted value", "Observation value."]',NULL,22,1,'855-867','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1737','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',43),
(1738,1738,1,'Algebraic Analysis of Quantum Fuzzy Subgroups of Some Special Cyclic Groups','<p>In this paper, we introduced the concept of quantum fuzzy sub group and its level properties and how it is varying from classical fuzzy subgroups. Union and intersection of two Quantum fuzzy subgroups explained clearly with suitable examples. Cartesian fuzzy product and power fuzzy set based on Quantum theory investigated in this. We propose order of quantum fuzzy abelian subgroup and its properties. Finally, we propose finite Quantum cyclic fuzzy subgroup family and its characterization with interesting proofs.</p>','["Fuzzy set, Quantum fuzzy set, Classical fuzzy group, Quantum fuzzy abelian subgroup, Order, Cyclic group, Union, Power fuzzy set.."]',NULL,22,1,'868-881','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1738','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',44),
(1739,1739,1,'Unlocking Sustainable Talent: Implementing Circular Human Resource Management Practices for Human Capital Regeneration','<p>Ubiquitous resource shortages and growing environmental concerns are significant elements altering the dynamic Indian economy, emphasizing the key role of sustainability. Service sector organizational firms are progressively embracing Circular Economy ideas, a notion that offers human capital management through Circular Human Resource Management (CHRM). The smooth operation of Circular Human Resource Management practice is necessary for service sector organizations focusing to attain their sustainable expansion goals. </p>
<p>The central focus of this research is to assess the current performance level of Circular Human Resource Management practices across service sector organizations. Moreover, it explores certain challenges that the organizations run into during the process of execution. For this research, data was gathered by using a Mixed Methods approach, integrating quantitative surveys with in-depth interviews to get reliable outcomes and deeper comprehension from workers in service (non-manufacturing) sectors.</p>
<p>This research uncovers a low to moderate extent of implementation of Circular Human Resource Management practices, mainly attributable to identified obstacles. The most commonly followed practices covering the service sector organizations that were interviewed, incorporate activites linked to Employee Participation in Circularity, Learning and Development for Circular Skills and Career Development discussion for Circular End-Results. The major obstacles obstructing fruitful implementation of Circular Human Resource Management are: Insufficient Incentives for attaining circular targets, Unawareness regarding the Circular Human Resource Management conception and inadequacy of Pre-planning to execute circularity practices within the bounds of human resource function.</p>','["Circular human resource practices, human capital, quantitative surveys, indepth interviews, service sector."]',NULL,22,1,'882-895','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1739','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',45),
(1740,1740,1,'An Efficient Digital Body Coupled Communication (BCC) Based Transceiver For wireless Body-Area-Network Applications','<p>Wireless Body Area Networks (WBANs) require communication systems that simultaneously provide high data reliability, low power consumption, and efficient hardware implementation for continuous physiological monitoring. Although conventional radio frequency (RF)-based Body Area Networks (BANs) offer acceptable communication performance, they are constrained by electromagnetic interference, limited battery lifetime, security concerns, and increased power consumption. Body-Coupled Communication (BCC) has emerged as an effective alternative by utilizing the human body as the transmission medium, thereby enabling secure and energy-efficient short-range communication. This paper presents an energy-efficient digital BCC Transceiver (TR) architecture incorporating convolutional codes and configurable modulation schemes to improve communication reliability, hardware efficiency, and data throughput for WBAN applications. The proposed architecture supports three configurable modulation techniques, namely Binary Amplitude Shift Keying (BASK), Binary Frequency Shift Keying (BFSK), and Binary Phase Shift Keying (BPSK), which are implemented using a compact Direct Digital Synthesizer (DDS)-based modulation framework to reduce hardware complexity and chip area. To enhance transmission reliability over noisy body channels, a convolutional encoder and a Viterbi decoder are integrated into the transmitter and receiver, respectively. The proposed BCC TR is implemented in Verilog-HDL and synthesized on Spartan-6, Virtex-6, and Artix-7 FPGA platforms to evaluate hardware resource utilization, operating frequency, power consumption, throughput, hardware efficiency, and Bit Error Rate (BER). Experimental results demonstrate that the BFSK-based implementation achieves the highest throughput and hardware efficiency with only a marginal increase in hardware resources, whereas the BPSK-based implementation provides the lowest BER under Additive White Gaussian Noise (AWGN) channel conditions. The architecture achieves BER values of 10⁻⁷ using BFSK and 10⁻⁸ using BPSK while maintaining efficient FPGA resource utilization. Comparative evaluation with existing BCC transceivers further demonstrates that the proposed architecture provides an improved balance between throughput, communication reliability, hardware efficiency, and implementation complexity. The presented digital BCC TR offers a flexible and scalable communication platform for next-generation WBAN systems by combining configurable modulation, convolutional coding, and efficient FPGA implementation within a unified hardware architecture.</p>','["Body-Coupled Communication (BCC)", "Configurable Modulation", "Bit Error Rate (BER)", "Data Throughput", "Hardware Efficiency."]',NULL,22,1,'896-919','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1740','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',46);
<p>Using the PICO framework, data were collected from 760 elderly respondents across rural and urban regions. The Technology Engagement Questionnaire for Seniors (TEQS), based on the Big Five Inventory, assessed personality dimensions and technology usage. Statistical tools including Pearson Correlation and Multiple Linear Regression were used to examine relationships between traits, perceived barriers, motivators, and digital comfort.</p>
<p>Findings reveal that personality traits have limited influence, with only conscientiousness showing a weak positive correlation. In contrast, digital exclusion is more strongly driven by low digital literacy, financial limitations, and inadequate infrastructure. These findings have direct implications for Sustainable Development Goal 3 (Good Health and Well-being) and Goal 4 (Quality Education), both of which emphasize equitable access to digital health and lifelong learning.</p>
<p>The study contributes by shifting attention from individual-level explanations to broader systemic factors, providing evidence to guide inclusive policy design for digital adoption among older adults. It advocates for integrated strategies that prioritize infrastructural and educational support to bridge the digital divide in aging populations</p>','["Senior citizens, Technology adoption, SDG 3, SDG 4, Digital literacy, Structural barriers, Personality traits."]',NULL,22,1,'1151-1175','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1756','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',61),
(1758,1758,1,'Optimized Non-Linear DNA Cryptosystem with Quantum-Secured Key Synthesis','<p>Medical imaging systems are moving quickly to cloud-based picture archiving and communication systems (PACS), while rapidly shifting to cloud-based PACS networks requires encryption standards that offer cryptographic strength, clinical fidelity and clinical workflow compatibility. The existing approaches cater to some of these requirements, but there is no published system that meets all requirements simultaneously. This paper presents the Efficient Data Masking (EDM) framework, a three-phase chaotic-DNA encryption pipeline that addresses this gap through three integrated components. First, a Nested Chaotic System (NCS) coupling a logistic map with a sine map through bidirectional parameter exchange yields a maximum Lyapunov exponent λ = 0.87, guaranteeing exponential sensitivity to initial conditions. Second, a SHA-256-based key derivation module (termed quantum-inspired because key material is augmented with NCS-derived entropy to resist Grover-class exhaustive search) generates a 256-bit session key with effective entropy exceeding 2²⁵⁶. Third, an eight-rule dynamic DNA encoding layer with XOR diffusion implemented over GF(4) provides invertible nucleotide-level confusion and diffusion.</p>
<p>Formal security analysis is conducted under the IND-CPA game, with a structured argument linking ciphertext indistinguishability to the computational hardness of distinguishing NCS output from a truly random sequence. We explicitly acknowledge that the NCS pseudorandomness assumption is non-standard and identify reduction to an established hard problem as future work. Experiments on six medical imaging modalities demonstrate that all fifteen NIST SP 800-22 randomness tests pass (minimum p-value 0.213; minimum pass rate 98/100), Shannon entropy reaches 7.942–7.999 bits, pixel correlation coefficients fall within ±0.003 of zero, NPCR spans 98.36–99.27%, and UACI spans 33.42–33.62% (both within theoretically optimal bounds). Zero recoverable information was obtained under known-plaintext (up to 1,000 pairs) and chosen-plaintext (seven structured patterns) attacks. Throughput ranges from 39 MB/s (ARM Cortex-A72) to 264 MB/s (Xilinx Artix-7 FPGA), with ROI-selective encryption reducing overhead to 2–5% relative to AES-256-CTR. Bit-perfect lossless decryption (PSNR = ∞, SSIM = 1.000) is verified for all tested modalities, and a Hadoop/Spark pipeline achieves 100% reconstruction accuracy from 1 KB to 1 TB.</p>','["medical image encryption", "chaotic systems", "DNA encoding", "SHA-256 key derivation", "post-quantum security", "DICOM", "differential cryptanalysis", "NIST SP 800-22", "IND-CPA", "GF(4) arithmetic"]',NULL,22,1,'1176-1196','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1758','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',62),
(1759,1759,1,'The Future of Work and Finance: A Study of the Intersections between Neo Banking and the Gig Economy','<p>The convergence of neo-banking and the gig economy represents a significant shift in the future of work and finance, reshaping how individuals manage their financial lives in an increasingly digital and decentralized economy. This research article investigates the intersections between neo-banking, a digital-only banking model, and the gig economy, focusing on how neo-banks are addressing the unique financial challenges faced by gig workers. Unlike traditional employment, gig work often involves irregular income, lack of employer-provided benefits, and limited access to financial services. Neo-banks, with their technology-driven, customer-centric approach, are emerging as key players in providing tailored financial solutions such as flexible accounts, real-time payments, income-smoothing tools, and low-cost international transfers. These innovations are particularly relevant for gig workers, who require greater financial flexibility and accessibility. The research highlights the economic and social implications of neo-banking for the gig economy, including its role in promoting financial inclusion, reducing barriers to financial access, and empowering workers to better manage their finances. However, the study also identifies potential risks, such as over-reliance on digital platforms, cybersecurity vulnerabilities, and regulatory challenges that could hinder the sustainable growth of neo-banking in this context. Furthermore, the article explores the broader implications of this intersection for the future of work and finance. It argues that neo-banks are not only transforming how gig workers interact with financial systems but also influencing the broader financial ecosystem by driving innovation and competition. The findings suggest that while neo-banks are well-positioned to support the evolving needs of the gig economy, collaboration among regulators, traditional banks, and fintech companies is crucial to address systemic risks and ensure equitable access to financial services. This research contributes to the growing body of literature on digital finance and labor economics, offering actionable insights for policymakers, financial institutions, and gig workers. By examining the synergies and challenges at the intersection of neo-banking and the gig economy, the article provides a comprehensive understanding of how digital financial solutions can support the future of work in an increasingly fragmented and dynamic labor market. </p>','["Neo-banking, gig economy, financial inclusion, digital finance, future of work, financial innovation."]',NULL,22,1,'1197-1209','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1759','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',63),
(1760,1760,1,'An Adaptive CBR–Agentic AI Model for Detecting Fraudulent User Behavior in Digital Marketplace Platforms','<p>The rapid growth of digital marketplace platforms has led to increased exposure to sophisticated fraudulent activities, necessitating advanced detection mechanisms. This study proposes an Adaptive Case-Based Reasoning (CBR)–Agentic Artificial Intelligence (AI) model for detecting fraudulent user behavior in dynamic digital environments. The model integrates experiential learning through the CBR 4R cycle (Retrieve, Reuse, Revise, Retain) with autonomous multi-agent coordination to enable adaptive, real-time fraud detection. Beyond conventional hybrid models, the proposed framework introduces a novel paradigm of experience-driven autonomous intelligence, where historical reasoning (CBR) is not only reused but actively orchestrated by agentic systems capable of contextual adaptation and self-directed learning. This represents a shift from static predictive systems to continuously evolving cognitive architectures that mimic human-like reasoning under uncertainty. Experiments were conducted using a multi-source dataset comprising transactional, behavioral, and network data with over 1.27 million instances. The proposed model achieved superior performance compared to traditional models, including Logistic Regression, Random Forest, and Support Vector Machine, with an Accuracy of 0.962, Precision of 0.938, Recall of 0.927, F1-score of 0.932, and AUC of 0.979. Importantly, the model demonstrates a new level of robustness under concept drift, highlighting its ability to sustain intelligence in non-stationary environments—an essential requirement for next-generation fraud detection systems.</p>','["Case-Based Reasoning (CBR)", "Agentic Artificial Intelligence", "Fraud Detection", "Digital Marketplace Platforms", "Adaptive Learning Systems"]',NULL,22,1,'1210-1236','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1760','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',64),
(1761,1761,1,'The Role of Data Mining in Modern Healthcare: A Review of Predictive Models, Descriptive Techniques, and Emerging Trends','<p>The advancements of technology and the wide implementation of Electronic Health Records (EHRs) have resulted in an unprecedented data size in the healthcare industry. While these massive, complex, and heterogenous datasets hold significant potential for improving clinical decision-making, extracting meaningful knowledge from high-dimensional and heterogeneous medical data remains a significant obstacle for data-handling mechanisms. Data mining has emerged as an essential methodology within Knowledge Discovery in Databases (KDD) to uncover hidden clinical patterns, anomalies, and correlations. This paper provides a comprehensive survey and deep methodological overview of the role of data mining in medicine. 21 recent studies conducted between 2019 and 2025 critically evaluated a diverse range of single and hybrid data mining architectures, including classification, clustering, and deep learning algorithms applied across various medical subfields (such as oncology, neurology, and chronic disease prediction).  This analysis revealed that while hybrid data mining models and ensemble techniques demonstrate superior predictive performance, constantly achieving classification accuracies exceeding 95%, the existing literature shows critical gaps. Many current frameworks depend on theoretical or un-validated models that fail to address real-world implementation challenges, i.e., parameter-tuning sensitivities, high computational complexities, and potential overfitting due to small or imbalanced datasets.  The significance of this work lies in identifying these systemic architectural and data-structural bottlenecks to facilitate a steady transition from theoretical data mining models to practical, deployable systems in clinical settings. Finally, this paper illustrated upcoming trends and Artificial Intelligence (AI) integration, providing a roadmap for future research aimed at enhancing the robustness and scalability of healthcare analytics.</p>','["Data mining", "Electrical Health Records", "Knowledge Discovery in Databases", "Clinical Decision Making", "Machine Learning", "Deep Learning", "Predictive Analytics."]',NULL,22,1,'1237-1257','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1761','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',65),
(1810,1810,1,'Graph-Based Adaptive Multimodal Transformer with Temporal Emotion Memory for Robust Conversational Emotion Recognition','<p>Conversational emotion recognition has become an essential research area for developing intelligent human–computer interaction systems capable of understanding contextual emotional dynamics from multimodal information. Existing transformer-based approaches mainly focus on local contextual dependencies while often overlooking long-range conversational relationships and historical emotional evolution, resulting in reduced recognition accuracy under noisy and heterogeneous environments. To overcome these limitations, this paper proposes a Graph-Based Adaptive Multimodal Transformer with Temporal Emotion Memory (GAMT-TEM) framework that integrates adaptive conversational graph learning, graph transformer encoding, temporal emotion memory modeling, confidence-aware graph fusion, and end-to-end multi-objective optimization. The proposed framework simultaneously exploits textual, acoustic, and visual modalities collected from the CMU-MOSEI and IEMOCAP benchmark datasets to capture both contextual and temporal emotional dependencies. Experimental evaluation demonstrates that the proposed framework significantly outperforms existing conversational emotion recognition methods. On the CMU-MOSEI dataset, GAMT-TEM achieves 92.84% accuracy, 92.17% F1-score, and 97.23% AUC, while obtaining 85.73% accuracy and 85.22% weighted F1-score on IEMOCAP. The proposed model further exhibits superior robustness under missing modalities, efficient computational complexity with only 24.1 million parameters, and stable convergence during optimization. These results demonstrate that adaptive graph reasoning combined with temporal emotion memory substantially improves multimodal conversational emotion recognition under realistic conversational environments.</p>','["Conversational Emotion Recognition", "Multimodal Learning", "Graph Transformer Network", "Temporal Emotion Memory", "Adaptive Graph Learning", "Context-Aware Emotion Analysis", "Human\\u2013Computer Interaction."]',NULL,22,1,'1258-1284','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1810','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',66),
(1831,1831,1,'An Explainable Risk-Calibrated Stacked-Ensemble Framework for Anticipatory Multimodal Fall Analytics in the Elderly Using Wearable IMU and IoMT Assistive-Stick Sensing','<p>Falls in geriatric patients continue to pose a significant source of harm, hospitalization, worsening of function, and impaired autonomy, prompting the development of smart technologies that no longer rely on reactive fall detection methods to move towards proactive predictions and prevention. A new application-oriented multimodal fall analytics framework that utilizes two supplementary sets of data, namely, IMU-based wearable data to detect binary falls and smart cStick IoMT data to distinguish between no fall, fall predicted, and definite fall is introduced in the paper. The suggested system incorporates motion-derived statistical measures like the maximum acceleration, the maximum gyroscopes, kurtosis, skew and after-impact with contextual and physiological data, including grip pressure, variations in heart rates, blood oxygen saturation levels, sugar level, and distance of proximity. This combination allows an event-based detection of risks and in-the-field anticipatory risk analysis in real time. Rather than selecting a single classifier, the framework introduces a heterogeneous stacked-generalization ensemble that combines Random Forest, Extremely Randomized Trees, and gradient boosting as base learners with a regularized logistic-regression meta-learner trained on out-of-fold predictions. The ensemble outputs are probability-calibrated (isotonic regression) so that a subject-specific, risk-aware adaptive decision threshold operates on trustworthy probabilities, and model behaviour is made transparent through SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) for both global and local interpretability. A salient preprocessing, feature-engineering, and evaluation pipeline supports the comparison against conventional baselines including Logistic Regression, Naive Bayes, K-Nearest Neighbors, Support Vector Machine, and Decision Tree. Experimental analysis of IMU data shows that discrimination is very reliable and the proposed calibrated stacked ensemble attains a high value of 97.472% test accuracy, matching or marginally exceeding its strongest base learner, with good macro-F1 performance and good generalization. Multimodal characteristics are highly applicable in the practice on the cStick dataset since pressure, distance and HRV turned out to be the central predictors of instability and fall progression. The main innovation of the work is that it is risk-conscientious, multimodal and anticipatory constructed in such a manner that it can be applied in real-time to smart assistive devices, elderly care monitoring, home safety system, and remote healthcare applications. The developed framework provides a scalable base on the next-generation AI-empowered elderly fall prevention and intervention technologies</p>','["Elderly Fall Detection, Fall Prediction, Multimodal Sensing, Wearable IMU, IoMT Assistive Stick, Machine Learning, Stacked Ensemble, Gradient Boosting, Probability Calibration, Explainable AI, SHAP, Risk-Aware Analytics, Real-Time Healthcare Monitoring, Smart Assistive Devices, Elderly Safety, Fall Prevention"]',NULL,22,1,'1285-1313','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1831','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',67),
(1981,1981,1,'An Optimized Deep Learning Framework for Robust Epileptic Seizure Detection Using EEG Signals','<p>Epilepsy affects a person''s behavior and is characterized by seizures. Epileptic seizures are infamously difficult to treat because of how unpredictable they are. Automatic electroencephalogram (EEG) seizure detection allows medical professionals to better predict when patients may have seizures and adjust treatment plans appropriately. The signals only show one side of the brain, making it impossible to accurately identify epileptic seizures. When done manually, feature extraction also consumes a significant amount of time. To build seizure detection systems reliant on multi-view feature learning via automatic means is not feasible in their absence. A proposed method for detecting epileptic seizures from electroencephalogram data involves creating an optimal deep learning architecture. Using sophisticated hyperparameter tuning techniques, the system incorporates deep learning architectures, feature optimisation, and wavelet-based preprocessing. Results show that compared to previous approaches, ours are much more accurate, robust, and computationally efficient, according to extensive trials conducted on several benchmark datasets. Clinical and real-time deployment of the suggested method is confirmed to be effective by statistical validation.</p>','["Electroencephalogram, sophisticated hyperparameter, wavelet transform, convolutional layers."]',NULL,22,1,'1314-1324','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1981','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',68),
(2090,2090,1,'AI-Enabled IoT Wearable System for Real-Time Fall Detection and Emergency Alerting in Older Adults','<p>Falls are a significant health issue for older people, and can result in injury, disability, hospitalisation and loss of independence. Continuous monitoring and quick caregiver notification can be enabled using an AI-powered wearable IoT system. Objective: The purpose of this study was to design and test an edge-based wearable IoT framework for real-time fall detection based on the accelerometer and gyroscope signals. Methodology: A synthetic dataset was created by using the features of SisFall and MobiAct, including 270 falls and 630 activities of daily living from 30 simulated participants. Various temporal, statistical and frequency domain features were extracted from the sensor signals and these were filtered, normalized, segmented. Participant-wise Training and Testing were performed on the Random Forest and Support Vector Machine Models. The architecture of ESP32–IMU and the alert mechanism using MQTT was designed. On the synthetic test data, both models obtained a 100% accuracy, sensitivity, specificity, precision and F1-score, while the AUC is 1.000 for both models. Discussion: The results show technical feasibility, and probably represent good synthetic class separation, and should not be used as a measure of clinical performance. Discussion: The framework provides a good base for the private, rapid fall detection but still needs to be validated in the real world, tested with hardware, put through with a variety of elderly participants and evaluated for usability.</p>','["Fall detection", "artificial intelligence", "Internet of Things", "wearable sensors", "elderly care", "edge computing", "MQTT."]',NULL,22,1,'1325 - 1334','10.59543/jidmis.v3.2090','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',69),
(2131,2131,1,'AI-Driven Decision Intelligence for Enhancing Professional Identity Development Among Elderly-Care Vocational Students','<p>Background: Vocational programmes preparing students for elderly-care work confront a persistent identity problem: students may acquire occupational skills while remaining uncertain about whether older-adult care constitutes a desirable, respected, and sustainable professional future. Artificial intelligence (AI) is increasingly used in education, yet most applications optimise information delivery rather than the higher-order decisions through which learners interpret evidence, compare career pathways, and construct professional commitments.</p>
<p>Objective: This study develops an AI-driven decision intelligence (DI) framework designed to strengthen professional identity development among elderly-care vocational students by combining explainable recommendation, career self-efficacy scaffolding, structured reflection, and human mentor oversight.</p>
<p>Methods: Because no human-participant dataset was supplied for the present manuscript, the empirical section is explicitly presented as a simulation-based proof of concept rather than as an observed trial. A reproducible study sample of 320 students (160 standard-guidance; 160 AI-DI) was modelled across four measurement waves. The simulated intervention contained five functions: learner profiling, scenario-based career exploration, explainable pathway comparison, reflective prompts, and mentor review. Professional identity, career self-efficacy, reflective agency, and intention to work with older adults were represented on five-point scales. Longitudinal mixed-effects modelling and change-score mediation were used to test the proposed mechanism.</p>
<p>Results: In the study dataset, professional identity was essentially equivalent at baseline (M = 3.18 in both groups) but reached 3.77 in the AI-DI group versus 3.31 under standard guidance at Week 12. The adjusted between-group difference in identity change was 0.468 points (95% CI 0.423–0.513, p &lt; .001). The simulated effect was partly transmitted through career self-efficacy (indirect effect = 0.182) and reflective agency (indirect effect = 0.040). Older-adult-care career intention increased from 3.00 to 3.59 in the AI-DI condition compared with 3.00 to 3.13 in the control condition.</p>
<p>Conclusion: The proof of concept suggests that the educational value of AI may lie less in automated career prediction than in creating an explainable, revisable decision environment in which students can test possible professional selves. The framework requires empirical validation through multi-site vocational trials and should be implemented with safeguards against algorithmic labelling, automation bias, privacy intrusion, and premature identity foreclosure.</p>','["Artificial intelligence", "decision intelligence", "professional identity", "vocational education", "older-adult care", "career self-efficacy", "nursing education"]',NULL,22,1,'1335 - 1353','10.59543/jidmis.v3.2131','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',70),
(2209,2209,1,'Data-Driven Decision Analytics for Poverty Reduction: Green Investment, Digitalisation, and Economic Freedom in Emerging Economies','<p>This study aims to assess the role of green investment, digitalization, and economic freedom in poverty reduction, focusing on 25 emerging economies in the context of overcoming SDG 1 Zero Poverty. The study analyzed the panel data for the period 2000 to 2022 using advanced econometric analyses such as CS-ARDL, NARDL, DH Causality, Westerlund Cointegration test, and the Unit Root test. The main results indicate that all three factors are important in mitigating poverty in the long term: Green investment adds value for employment and the environment; digitalization contributes to financial inclusion and access to services; and economic freedom boosts entrepreneurship and market access. The short-run effect is conditional upon whether it is the reduction of digitalization and/or economic freedom that has an immediate negative impact or whether the increase of both has an impact as well. In this study, a new framework of nonlinear and asymmetric poverty analysis is proposed to provide deeper insights into poverty dynamics in the context of emerging economies and to show the differential impacts of changes in policy settings. Differentiated rural investments, institutional measures, and improved digital infrastructure are recommended to enable economic freedom. These concerted actions can contribute to speeding up the poverty reduction process in the country.</p>','["Green Investment, Digitalisation, Economic Freedom, Poverty Reduction, Emerging Economies."]',NULL,22,1,'1354 - 1386','10.59543/jidmis.v3.2209','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',71);
<p>- Multi-Objective Trust-Energy Fitness Function: A mathematically focused fitness function that integrates the concept of node energy residuals with a dynamic trust value, allowing for a controlled and secure selection of Cluster Head (CH).</p>
<p>- Adaptive Hybrid Switching: A new method in which the algorithm adapts and switches between global (PSO) and local (FF/EHO) methods of solution searching based on the swarm convergence behavior of the node population.</p>
<p>- Velocity-Infused Elephant Separation: A speculative adjustment to the EHO separation operator, which posits that instead of random movement, a PSO-based velocity vector should be used to eliminate the potential solution(s) that may be lost in the search space.</p>
<p>- With a simulation of 3,000 nodes, Trust FF-EHO model out of all the approved models provided the highest level of benchmarks. The model in particular showed a loss of packets in relation to the standard FF algorithms of Trust up to 31.14% at a density of 2000 nodes and 14.88% at 3000 nodes. Trust FF-EHO sustained 45% of the nodes alive for 600 rounds. This is a significant improvement of 24% compared to Trust PSO-EHO and 23% compared to Trust PSO-FF. The model also demonstrates a significant improvement in scalability with a reduction in end-to-end latency of 8.35% to 25.98% and an improvement in delay of 8.92% at higher node densities. The hybridization of Trust FF-EHO model explores and enhances the overall energy utilization of the wireless sensor network while maintaining the remaining directed nodes in a functional manner for the highest possible.</p>','[": WSN, CH selection, Optimization, PSO, FF, EHO"]',NULL,22,1,'2064 - 2088','10.17762/jidmis.v3.3475','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-15',105),
(3484,3484,1,'Red-Deer-Inspired Feature Selection and a Lightweight Deep Classifier for Efficient Three-Class Brain Tumor Recognition','<p>Accurate brain-tumor classification from magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) must balance predictive performance with computational cost and transparent evaluation. This study presents a three-class pipeline comprising a fine-tuned ResNet50V2 feature extractor, a Red-Deer-inspired binary feature-search (RDI-FS) procedure, and a lightweight fully connected classifier. The reported implementation uses 2,475 images from the glioma, meningioma, and pituitary folders of the public Bhuvaji brain-tumor MRI training repository. An image-level stratified 85:15 split produced 2,103 development images and 372 held-out test images; the held-out set was excluded from feature search and model selection. RDI-FS reduced the 2,048-dimensional ResNet50V2 representation to 1,026 features (49.9% reduction). The displayed single-view confusion matrix contains 357 correct predictions from 372 test images, corresponding to 95.97% accuracy; weighted precision, recall, F1-score, and one-vs-rest AUC were 0.9600, 0.9597, 0.9596, and 0.9949, respectively. The separately reported soft test-time augmentation result was 97.04% accuracy, corresponding to 361/372 correct predictions after averaging original and horizontally flipped views. For the documented classifier head, reducing the input from 2,048 to 1,026 dimensions theoretically reduces trainable head parameters from 1.183 million to 0.659 million and dense-layer multiply-accumulate operations from 1.180 million to 0.657 million, excluding the ResNet50V2 backbone and test-time augmentation. The results support a compact classification pipeline on this image-level hold-out split; they do not establish patient-level generalization, clinical reliability, or measured real-time deployment performance.</p>','["Brain tumor classification, magnetic resonance imaging, feature selection, Red-Deer-inspired search, ResNet50V2, dimensionality reduction, lightweight classification"]',NULL,22,1,'2089 - 2104','10.17762/jidmis.v3.3484','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-15',106),
(3537,3537,1,'Deployment Topology and Load Balancing for IoT Networks in Smart Agriculture','<p>The management of the massive amounts of data generated from the transition of devices requires astute management like the deployment of nodes. The deployment process is conducted efficiently to guarantee the network''s operability, with a strong emphasis on two critical factors: connectivity and coverage. This significantly impacts network connectivity, as it determines the physical topology of the network, whereas coverage relates to the network''s accessibility. However, identifying the optimal strategy for the deployment, which reduces costs, alleviates computational and communication demands and improves network connectivity coverage, presents a considerable challenge. To address these issues, a novel approach called the Multi-Objective Nutcracker Carpet Weaver Optimization Algorithm (MONCWOA) is tailored for Internet of Things (IoT) deployment, which guarantees comprehensive coverage of IoT devices, including various IoT devices, while effectively managing irregular boundaries. Furthermore, the research introduces the Convoluted Dual Attention Generative Adversarial Network (CDAGAN) aimed at improving job scheduling within IoT systems, thereby increasing both scheduling efficiency and accuracy. The proposed method has demonstrated outstanding performance, achieving a convergence rate of 96 %, a connectivity ratio of 95.16 %, a coverage ratio of 95.16 %, transmission rate of 270.86 m, transmission delay of 11.6 ms, a scheduling time of 366.02 sec, runtime of 1.29 sec and energy consumption of 314. 78 J and makespan of 131.77 ms score in comparison to current techniques. Overall, CDAGAN establishes a robust basis for enhancing IoT implementation, increasing network functionality and guaranteeing superior performance in IoT settings.</p>','["Network Coverage, Job Scheduling, IoT Devices, Node Deployment, Network Connectivity."]',NULL,22,1,'2105 - 2128',NULL,'The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-15',107),
(3638,3638,1,'Inhabitants Perception of Sustainable Tourism Development- A Study Based on Empirical Evidence','<p>Sustainable tourism has emerged as an important approach for achieving a balance between environmental conservation, economic development, community well-being and visitor satisfaction. The present study examines the factors influencing residents'' attitudes towards the concept of sustainable tourism. The study is based on the responses of 121 residents, in the tourism places of Virudhunagar District, Tamilnadu, India. The research involves multi-stage random sampling method to identify the respondents and direct survey method was adopted to collect data from the respondents. A multiple regression analysis was employed to identify the factors influencing residents'' attitudes towards sustainable tourism. The results indicate a strong relationship between the selected factors and residents'' attitudes (R = 0.723), with the model explaining 52.3% of the variance in the dependent variable (R² = 0.523; Adjusted R² = 0.492). Environmental sustainability emerged as the most influential positive factor (β = 0.595, p &lt; 0.001), followed by community participation (β = 0.321, p = 0.035). The study concludes that residents'' support for sustainable tourism depends on an integrated approach that prioritises environmental sustainability, meaningful community participation, equitable economic benefits and long-term tourism planning while minimising social costs. The findings provide useful implications for policymakers and destination managers in developing socially inclusive, environmentally responsible and economically sustainable tourism strategies.</p>','["Sustainable Tourism, Residents'' Attitude, Environmental Sustainability, Community Participation, Economic Benefits, Social Cost, Tourism Planning."]',NULL,22,1,'2129 - 2138',NULL,'The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-15',108),
(3758,3758,1,'Intelligence in Today’s World: Theoretical Failures, Technological Gaps, and Methodological Tools for Decision-Making under Uncertainty','<p>Intelligence, understood as the discipline devoted to producing actionable knowledge for security decision-making, is undergoing a profound transformation. Classic challenges rooted in intelligence failures are now compounded by technological gaps that constrain the operational capacity of law enforcement, by the need to incorporate methodological tools capable of functioning under uncertainty, and by the often-underestimated role of auxiliary forensic techniques such as handwriting examination in investigative processes. This article proposes an integrative reading of these four dimensions—theoretical, technological, methodological, and forensic—drawing on recent literature on intelligence failures, technological gaps and security, fuzzy decision-making frameworks (IRA-FIS), and grapho-corporal inconsistencies in handwriting sample collection. It argues that contemporary intelligence can no longer be understood as a linear collection-analysis-dissemination process, but rather as a socio-technical system in which cognitive error, technological gaps, and methodological uncertainty interact and reinforce one another. The article closes with a proposed research and intervention agenda aimed at strengthening the robustness, interpretability, and adaptability of intelligence systems.</p>','["intelligence", "intelligence failures", "technological gaps", "fuzzy logic", "handwriting examination", "decision-making under uncertainty."]',NULL,22,1,'2139 - 2147',NULL,'The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-15',109),
(3976,3976,1,'Collaborative Learning: Developing Professional Capacity to Strengthen Collaborative Governance in Village-Owned Enterprises in Bulukumba Regency','<p>Village-Owned Enterprises (BUMDes) in Indonesia face challenges in building the professional capacity of actors to support sustainable collaborative governance. This research aims to analyze the role of collaborative learning in developing professional capacity and strengthening collaborative governance of BUMDes. The research uses a qualitative approach with a constructivist paradigm that goes through an in-depth interview, observation, and documentation process of actors involved in the governance of BUMDes in Ujung Loe District, Bulukumba Regency. The analysis was carried out through open coding, axial coding, and selective coding, as well as data triangulation. The results show that professional capacity is not primarily formed through formal training, but through experiential learning, knowledge exchange, mentoring, peer learning, joint problem-solving, and reflection. The effectiveness of learning is influenced by facilitative leadership, institutional clarity, knowledge documentation, knowledge transfer, and sustainability of inter-stakeholder interactions. Key findings show a circular relationship between collaborative governance, collaborative learning, and professional capacity: collaboration creates learning spaces, learning builds capacity, and stronger capacity improves the quality and sustainability of collaboration. In addition, this research contributes by placing collaborative learning as a capacity building mechanism in collaborative governance and recommending the sustainable development of the BUMDes learning ecosystem.</p>','["collaborative learning, kapasitas professional, collaborative governance, BUMDes, learning ecosystem"]',NULL,22,1,'2148 - 2163',NULL,'The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-15',110),
(3980,3980,1,'Borrowing Authenticity in the Creator Economy: How Creator-Voiced Sustainability Claims Shape Consumer Scepticism toward Luxury Brands','<p><strong>Purpose</strong>: Luxury brands increasingly delegate their sustainability claims to content creators rather than voicing them directly. This article theorises when such creator-voiced claims reduce, and when they intensify, consumer scepticism toward luxury brands.</p>
<p><strong>Design/methodology/approach:</strong> The article is conceptual. Drawing on an integrative review of 60 sources spanning consumer scepticism, greenwashing, the luxury–sustainability tension, source credibility, persuasion knowledge, signalling, parasocial relationships and authenticity, it evaluates two competing explanations and develops a set of propositions and an integrative model.</p>
<p><strong>Findings</strong>: A transfer account, which treats authenticity as a property that moves from creator to brand, explains credibility gains but not the reversals produced by disclosure, reach and creator–brand incongruence. A relational account, which treats borrowed authenticity as a triadic inference about creator, brand and claim, explains these reversals through three tests: indexical anchoring, dual motive attribution and narrative ownership. Six propositions and three boundary conditions follow. The relational account is more adequate under routine disclosure, yet it explains perceived rather than warranted credibility; authenticity can therefore be borrowed only against verifiable evidence.</p>
<p><strong>Originality/value:</strong> The article reconceptualises authenticity transfer as a triadic inference, introduces the constructs of the selection signal and bidirectional depletion, and connects research on luxury responsibility attributions with research on influencer persuasion knowledge.</p>
<p><strong>Practical implications</strong>: Luxury managers should anchor claims in the brand’s native sustainable register of craft, durability and repair, grant creators access rather than scripts, and secure evidence before borrowing credibility—an imperative reinforced by the EU Empowering Consumers Directive, applicable from 27 September 2026.</p>','["creator economy", "influencer marketing", "luxury brands", "sustainability communication", "consumer scepticism", "greenwashing", "authenticity", "persuasion knowledge", "signalling theory"]',NULL,22,1,'2164 - 2187','10.17762/jidmis.v3.3980','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-09-21',111),
(4030,4030,1,'Hybrid Unimodular Hill Cipher (UHC) and Rivest-Shamir-Adleman (RSA) Encryption with Chaotic Key Generation for Secure Binary Files','<p>This study introduces an intelligent adaptive hybrid cryptographic framework that integrates the Unimodular Hill Cipher (UHC), RSA-OAEP, and Logistic Chaotic Map-based key generation to provide comprehensive security for heterogeneous binary files. Unlike conventional cryptographic approaches that generally apply a predetermined encryption mechanism regardless of the characteristics of the input data, the proposed framework incorporates an AI-based adaptive decision mechanism to identify an appropriate encryption configuration based on the statistical properties of the target file. The decision mechanism evaluates multiple file-level characteristics, including Shannon entropy, byte-frequency distribution, file size, standard deviation, and the number of unique byte values. Based on these characteristics, the framework assigns most of the data to the computationally lightweight UHC mechanism, whereas selected sensitive segments are protected using RSA-OAEP to introduce an additional layer of asymmetric cryptographic security. The generation of dynamic unimodular matrices is supported by Logistic Chaotic Maps, whose sensitivity to initial conditions contributes to enhanced key unpredictability and randomness. The proposed framework was experimentally assessed using a collection of binary PDF files with different matrix dimensions and password configurations. The experimental evaluation produced a ciphertext entropy of 7.999891 and a plaintext–ciphertext correlation coefficient of −0.000152, indicating an almost negligible statistical relationship between the original and encrypted data. In addition, the Chi-Square statistic decreased substantially from 8159.84 for the plaintext to 269.85 after encryption, demonstrating improved statistical uniformity and diffusion. Data integrity and lossless recovery were further confirmed by obtaining identical SHA-256 hash values for the original and decrypted files. These results indicate that the proposed adaptive hybrid cryptographic framework can simultaneously provide strong statistical security, computational efficiency, adaptive encryption behavior, and compatibility with heterogeneous binary data, making it a promising approach for secure digital file protection across diverse computing and transmission environments.</p>','["Adaptive Hybrid Cryptography", "Unimodular Hill Cipher", "RSA-OAEP", "Logistic Chaotic Maps", "Binary Data Protection", "Universal Digital File Security"]',NULL,22,1,'2188 - 2213','10.17762/jidmis.v3.4030','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-09-23',112),
(4070,4070,1,'Modeling Academic Performance in Higher Education: A Multilevel Mediation Analysis of Transformational Leadership and Organizational Culture via Academic Competence and Satisfaction','<p>Background: The rapid digital transformation of higher education institutions has increased the importance of leadership capability, digital organizational culture, and academic competencies in sustaining academic performance within technology-enabled learning environments. Objective: This study examines the influence of transformational leadership and organizational culture on academic performance, focusing on the mediating roles of academic competence and academic satisfaction. Methods: A quantitative, cross-sectional survey collected data from 120 lecturers at private higher education institutions in Indonesia. Structural Equation Modeling with AMOS tested direct and indirect relationships. Confirmatory Factor Analysis evaluated the measurement model, while reliability, validity, and model fit were assessed using standard indices. Results: Transformational leadership and organizational culture significantly influenced academic competence and academic satisfaction. Both mediators positively and significantly affected academic performance, with academic competence showing the stronger effect. Conclusion: Leadership and organizational culture influence academic performance through a structured mediation mechanism rather than direct effects alone. The validated multilevel model integrates leadership, organizational culture, and capability development and contributes evidence from Indonesian private higher education.</p>','["Digital transformation", "Academic performance", "Academic competence", "Academic satisfaction", "Organizational culture"]',NULL,22,1,'2214 - 2235',NULL,'The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-15',113),
(4071,4071,1,'Development of Creative Thinking Skills Test (CTST) for Prospective Physics Teacher','<p>This research aims to analyze 1) the content validity of the creative thinking skills test (CTST), 2) the validity of the CTST items, and 3) the reliability of the CTST for prospective physics teacher. Development of CTST was carried out using the ADDIE model. The research procedure consists of five stages including analyze, design, development, implementation, and evaluation. The CTST consists of 16 essay items especially in the topic of electricity and magnetism. The test results show that: 1) the content validity of the CTST by five experts in the field of physics education from three different universities is 0.95 in the very high category; 2) a total of 12 questions were declared valid and represented all indicators in the dimensions of creative thinking; and 3) the reliability of the CTST is 0.846 in the very high category. Based on the test results, the developed CTST is very valid and very reliable. The final CTST consists of 8 essay questions. The CTST can be used for further research purposes.</p>','["competencies", "practicum process", "Prospective, elaboration"]',NULL,22,1,'2236 - 2245',NULL,'The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-09-24',114),
(4072,4072,1,'The Teen Health Application as a Digital Intervention Medium for Adolescent Reproductive Health: Development, Validation, and Effectiveness within Integrated Primary Care in Garut Regency Indonesia.','<p>Background: Adolescents face multiple reproductive health risks, while conventional educational media are widely regarded as inadequate for reaching adolescents given their preference for interactive, digitally based information. This study aimed to develop, validate, and evaluate the effectiveness of the Teen Health application as a digital intervention medium for adolescent reproductive health within the Integrated Primary Care (Integrasi Layanan Primer, ILP) framework in Garut Regency, Indonesia. Methods: Development of the Teen Health application was informed by qualitative exploratory findings (in-depth interviews and focus group discussions) that identified adolescents'' need for more interactive educational media. The application provides adolescent health education materials and videos alongside four self-assessment features: a nutritional status (body mass index, BMI) calculator, general health screening, reproductive health screening, and HEEADSSS psychosocial screening. The validity and reliability of the technology acceptance-use instrument (Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology, UTAUT), the perceived system quality questionnaire (Shilbayeh), and the Mobile App Rating Scale (MARS) were assessed using Rasch modelling among 150 adolescents and 30 health workers. Effectiveness was evaluated using a three-month quasi-experimental pretest-posttest control-group design involving 70 adolescents in the intervention group (application users) and 70 adolescents in the control group (routine primary health centre education without the application). Results: The UTAUT, Shilbayeh, and MARS instruments demonstrated good internal consistency based on Rasch modelling, indicating their suitability as measurement tools. In the intervention group, increases in knowledge, attitude, and practice scores were 4.36, 4.00, and 3.97, respectively, significantly higher than in the control group (0.86, 0.91, and 0.56, respectively; all p&lt;0.001). Application dashboard data showed a decline in mean reproductive health risk scores from 10.6 in January to 6.6 in May, and in HEEADSSS psychosocial risk scores from 75.2 to 66.0 over the same period, with mental health identified as the most prevalent concern. Conclusion: The Teen Health application was valid, acceptable, and effective in improving adolescent reproductive health knowledge, attitudes, and practices, and may serve as a scalable digital intervention medium supporting the continuity of adolescent health services within Integrated Primary Care.</p>','["Teen Health application", "adolescent mHealth", "adolescent reproductive health", "knowledge attitude practice", "Integrated Primary Care"]',NULL,22,1,'2246 - 2255',NULL,'The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-09-24',115),
(4123,4123,1,'From Integration to Decoupling: Empirical Evidence on Socio-Technical Misalignment in Responsible AI Adoption for Software Testing','<p>The swift embrace of Artificial Intelligence (AI) by software testers has created many new socio-technical difficulties that are not solely the result of a technical implementation. The most Responsible AI frameworks typically address ''ethics of design'' principles, thus offering little assistance in judging an organization''s readiness and ability to adopt AI in actual testing scenarios. This research proposes the iRAIT framework, which is built upon relevant empirical data and presents a socio-technical model for evaluating and supporting the responsible adoption of AI across five key areas that include: technical, ethical, human, organizational, and continuous monitoring. To empirically assess socio-technical adoption trends, a quantitative analysis is performed using the iRAIT analytical framework based on survey responses (n=350). The empirical results show that significant differences exist between the five areas of a tester’s readiness to adopt AI (p &lt; .001). Further analysis indicates that the degree of readiness is not constant across different areas of readiness with the most immature of the five areas being the ''Human'' element (i.e., trust, usability, and Human-AI interaction). In addition, the lack of alignment between areas indicates that the adoption of AI for software testing is primarily fragmented rather than integrated. The results provide evidence that current Responsible AI frameworks make the implicit assumption that there is socio-technical alignment in their recommendations. Therefore, these findings indicate a need for coordination when aligning these two dimensions in the responsible adoption of AI through software testing, and not just through isolated ethical or technical interventions. In addition, this proposed framework provides a systematic method for organizations to assess their readiness for AI adoption in software testing environments and implement the appropriate actions to improve that readiness.</p>','["Responsible AI, Software Testing, AI Adoption Readiness, Socio-Technical Framework, Human-AI Interaction"]',NULL,22,1,'2256 - 2274',NULL,'The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-09-25',116),
(4171,4171,1,'The Role of Brand Value Co-Creation Behavior as Mediator Between Motivation and Brand Equity in the Virtual Communities of New Consumer Brands','<p><strong>Objectives</strong>: With the rapid development of virtual brand communities in the new consumer brand ecosystem, customer interaction and value co-creation in the digital environment have increasingly become the key path for the formation of brand assets. Based on this, this study takes the virtual brand community of new consumer brands in China as the research context, and constructs and verifies the structural model of “customer motivation - value co-creation behavior - brand equity”. The research first classifies customer participation motivations into five categories based on the use and satisfaction theory: cognitive needs, personal integration needs, social integration needs, hedonic needs and economic benefit needs. Value co-creation behavior can be divided into participation behavior and civic behavior. Brand equity encompasses two dimensions: brand awareness and brand image.</p>
<p><strong>Methods</strong>: A total of 426 valid samples were obtained through questionnaire surveys, and the partial least squares structural equation model (PLS-SEM) was used to test the model.</p>
<p><strong>Results</strong>: The research results show that all five types of motivations can significantly promote customers'' participation behavior and civic behavior in virtual brand communities. Both types of value co-creation behaviors can significantly enhance brand awareness and brand image, and the degree of effect varies. Meanwhile, participation behavior and civic behavior play a partial mediating role between motivation and brand equity, indicating that customer value co-creation is an important mechanism for new consumer brands to build brand value.</p>
<p><strong>Conclusions</strong>: This research not only expands the application of the use and satisfaction theory in the field of marketing, but also provides empirical evidence for new consumer brands to enhance customer engagement, stimulate value co-creation behavior, and strengthen brand equity.</p>','["Brand equity", "Customer behavior", "Customer motivation", "New consumer brands", "PLS-SEM", "Value co-creation"]',NULL,22,1,'2275 - 2301','10.17762/jidmis.v3.4171','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-15',117),
(4172,4172,1,'Forecasting Beijing''s Indemnificatory Housing Supply Scale Based on Explainable Machine Learning Models','<p>Accurate prediction of the supply scale of indemnificatory housing is essential for housing policy decision-making and the sustainable governance of urban housing. This study takes Beijing as the research object and constructs an explainable machine learning framework that integrates principal component analysis (PCA), univariate polynomial regression (UPR), and three deep learning models: Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM), Back Propagation Neural Network (BPNN), and Convolutional Neural Network (CNN). Based on annual data from 2010 to 2023, six key influencing factors are identified across three dimensions: fiscal supply, residents'' demand, and the housing market. UPR is used to extrapolate the future trends of these key factors, and three deep learning models are systematically compared under different lag structures. The results show that the CNN model with a two-year lag achieves the best predictive performance (MAPE = 10.08%, R² = 0.9763). The SHAP and MIV interpretability methods further reveal that fiscal supply-side variables are the dominant drivers of the supply scale, housing market variables exert significant linkage effects, while residents'' demand variables play a consistent but moderate positive role. Forecast results indicate that Beijing''s indemnificatory housing supply scale will follow an inverted-U-shaped trajectory over 2024-2028, transitioning from rapid expansion to structural optimization.</p>','["Indemnificatory housing", "Supply scale forecasting", "Explainable machine learning", "Convolutional Neural Network", "SHAP"]',NULL,22,1,'2302 - 2317','10.17762/jidmis.v3.4172','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-15',118),
(4252,4252,1,'Improving Stability and Scalability in Edge Computing Task Scheduling Using an Adaptive Multi-Objective Evolutionary Algorithm','<p>The rapid spread of latency-critical and computationally heavy applications in IoT, 5G, or the upcoming 6G has turned edge computing into more than just a buzzword; it is now a foundational piece of the infrastructure. That said, the very features that make edge appealing (its distributed nature, dynamic behavior, and tight resource limits) also create serious headaches when it comes to scheduling tasks. Two pain points stand out: keeping performance stable when workloads fluctuate, and making sure the system can scale up without breaking as the network grows. Conventional scheduling approaches, along with static multi‑objective optimizers, tend to buckle under these pressures. Their performance often wavers unpredictably, and when you push them to larger setups, the computational cost skyrockets. To get a handle on this, we have designed an Adaptive Multi‑Objective Evolutionary Algorithm (AMOEA) that builds on a beefed‑up version of NSGA‑II. The core idea is to make the algorithm smart enough to adjust itself on the fly. Specifically, we have added three mechanisms: first, a dynamic tuning scheme for mutation and crossover rates so that exploration and exploitation stay balanced; second, a continuous monitoring loop that watches workload changes and triggers adaptive relearning whenever needed, which helps maintain stability even when conditions shift; and third, a diversity preservation trick that stops the population from converging too early, which in turn improves scalability. We ran a wide range of experiments, covering different workload patterns and system sizes, to see how well this approach holds up. The numbers speak for themselves: compared to classic NSGA‑II, MOPSO, and common heuristic methods, our AMOEA consistently comes out ahead. Performance variance dropped by as much as 46%, which is a strong sign of improved stability. More importantly, as the number of tasks increases, the latency growth curve is noticeably flatter so scaling really does work better. On top of that, we recorded meaningful gains across other metrics too: energy consumption fell by 8.15%, average response time shortened by 13.1%, and CPU utilization settled at a healthy 0.86 rate. The Hypervolume indicator, at 0.84, further confirms that the trade‑offs among objectives are well balanced. All in all, these results suggest that adding adaptive intelligence to evolutionary scheduling is a promising path forward especially for heterogeneous edge environments where conditions rarely stay the same for long. The framework we have put together appears robust, scalable, and efficient enough to handle the kind of dynamic resource management that real‑world deployments actually demand.</p>','["Edge computing, Task scheduling, Adaptive evolutionary algorithm, Stability, Scalability, Multi\\u2011objective optimization, NSGA\\u2011II, Dynamic workload."]',NULL,22,1,'2318 - 2329',NULL,'The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-15',119),
(4276,4276,1,'Crime Characteristics and Psychosocial Experiences of Women Prisoners in Andhra Pradesh: A Prison-Based Empirical Study','<p>Criminological research in India continues to be shaped by male-centred frameworks, resulting in limited empirical understanding of women’s pathways into crime and their psychosocial experiences within prison institutions. Existing studies frequently examine crime patterns, socio-demographic characteristics, or prison conditions in isolation, creating a critical gap in integrated analyses that link women’s social backgrounds with their emotional experiences and coping capacities during incarceration, particularly in Andhra Pradesh. The present study addresses this gap by examining the types and causes of crime among women prisoners, analysing their association with selected socio-demographic variables, and exploring women’s emotional experiences at the time of arrest, during imprisonment, and in relation to emotional resilience. The study adopted a mixed-method research design using a census approach and included all 82 women prisoners housed in selected women’s prisons of Andhra Pradesh. Primary data were collected through a structured interview schedule documenting socio-demographic profiles, crime characteristics, mental state at arrest, current emotional experiences, and emotional resilience. Data were analysed using SPSS, employing descriptive statistics and Chi-square tests. The results revealed that most socio-demographic variables did not show statistically significant associations with types or causes of crime, emotional states, or resilience levels. However, caste was significantly associated with types of crime, and occupation showed a significant relationship with causes of crime. Arrest was experienced as a profound emotional rupture, while imprisonment was characterised by guilt, remorse, loneliness, and emotional distress. Most women demonstrated moderate emotional resilience developed within the prison environment. The study concludes that women’s incarceration experiences are shaped primarily by structural vulnerabilities and institutional realities, underscoring the need for gender-responsive and trauma-informed criminal justice interventions.</p>','["Women prisoners, Gender and crime, Psychosocial experiences, Emotional resilience, Socio-demographic factors, Structural inequality."]',NULL,22,1,'2330 - 2352',NULL,'The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-15',120),
(4280,4280,1,'AgriTwinAI: An IoT-Enabled Digital Twin and Deep Learning Framework for Potato Disease Monitoring and Precision Crop Management','<p>Crop diseases, environmental fluctuations, inefficient use of crop resources and late diagnosis of crop diseases are more and more influencing potato production. This research suggests a Digital Twin and deep learning-based framework, named AgriTwinAI, which aims to detect potato diseases intelligently by utilizing IoT technology and to manage the crops precisely. The framework combines image analysis of potato leaves using an IoT-based Hybrid DenseNet–Vision Transformer architecture with monitoring the environmental conditions, such as temperature, relative humidity, soil moisture, soil temperature, leaf wetness, rainfall etc. The Multimodal data fusion integrates disease probabilities, real-time sensor readings and past crop data in a Digital Twin environment. Crop areas are then classified into disease-risk zones and prioritized recommendations are created for field inspection and disease risk assessment. Enhanced understanding of disease predictions with the use of explainable AI using the Grad-CAM technique. The framework also examines the effectiveness of classification, computational efficiency, sensor reliability, communication latency, Digital Twin synchronization, and multimodal contributions by ablation analysis. Despite the quantitative outcomes shown, the presented results are yet to be validated with actual experimental and field data, AgriTwinAI offers a systematic architecture for integrating the fields of AI, IoT, Digital Twins, and precision agriculture in support of timely and location-specific potato crop-health management.</p>','["Potato Disease Detection", "AgriTwinAI", "Digital Twin", "Internet of Things (IoT)", "Deep Learning", "Hybrid DenseNet\\u2013Vision Transformer"]',NULL,22,1,'2353 - 2373','10.17762/jidmis.v3.4280','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-15',121);
<p>The findings reveal a substantial increase in scientific production, particularly after 2015, demonstrating the rapid expansion of this interdisciplinary research domain. China, the United States, and several European and Asian countries emerged as the leading contributors to scientific productivity and international collaboration, while Marine Pollution Bulletin and Science of the Total Environment ranked among the most productive publication sources. Author keyword co-occurrence analysis identified microplastics, plastic waste, circular economy, recycling, artificial intelligence, machine learning, and remote sensing as dominant research themes. Thematic evolution further revealed a transition from broad marine pollution research toward technology-oriented investigations emphasizing plastic waste management and microplastics. Overall, the study provides a comprehensive knowledge map of the field, highlighting the growing integration of digital technologies and sustainable waste management strategies. The findings offer valuable insights for researchers, policymakers, and environmental practitioners in identifying research priorities, strengthening international collaboration, and advancing technology-driven solutions for sustainable ocean plastic waste management.</p>','["Artificial intelligence", "Bibliometric analysis", "Marine plastic pollution", "Ocean plastic waste management", "Remote sensing", "Technological innovation"]',NULL,21,1,'614 - 650','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1213','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',32),
(1214,1214,1,'Digital Technologies for Sustainable Electronic Waste Management: A Global Bibliometric Analysis of Emerging Innovations and Future Research Directions','<p>The rapid increase in electronic waste (e-waste) generation has intensified the need for innovative technologies that improve waste monitoring, resource recovery, recycling efficiency, and circular resource management. Digital technologies have emerged as important enablers of these transformations; however, a comprehensive understanding of the global evolution of this research domain remains limited. This study presents a global bibliometric analysis of digital technologies for sustainable electronic waste management to examine research productivity, influential contributors, collaboration networks, thematic development, and emerging technological directions. Bibliographic records were retrieved from the Scopus database on 11 July 2026 using a structured TITLE-ABS-KEY search strategy. Following document type and language screening, 2,553 publications were analyzed using Bibliometrix in RStudio and VOSviewer. The findings reveal a substantial increase in scientific production after 2018, with publication growth following a logistic trajectory (R² = 0.942) that reflects the rapid expansion of the research field. India emerged as the leading contributor in publication output, whereas China demonstrated the highest citation impact. International collaboration formed six major country clusters, highlighting extensive cross-border scientific cooperation. Keyword co-occurrence and thematic analyses identified artificial intelligence, machine learning, Internet of Things, computer vision, recycling, and circular economy as the dominant conceptual areas driving current research. The thematic evolution further indicates a transition from conventional electronic waste management toward intelligent, data-driven, and circular approaches. By mapping the intellectual landscape and knowledge structure of the field, this study provides a valuable reference for researchers, practitioners, and policymakers seeking to advance digitally enabled solutions for sustainable electronic waste management and circular resource systems.</p>','["Artificial intelligence", "Bibliometric analysis", "Circular economy", "Digital technologies", "Electronic waste management", "Sustainability."]',NULL,21,1,'651 - 686','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1214','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',33),
(1215,1215,1,'Smart Technologies for Aquaculture: A Global Bibliometric Analysis of Digital Innovation, Knowledge Structures, and Future Directions','<p>The rapid integration of digital technologies has transformed aquaculture into a data-driven industry, yet the expanding body of research on smart technologies remains fragmented across multiple scientific disciplines. Despite increasing interest in intelligent aquaculture systems, a comprehensive understanding of the field''s global development, knowledge structure, and emerging research directions remains limited. This study addresses this gap through a bibliometric analysis of 7,197 English-language publications indexed in the Scopus database from 1976 to 2026. Bibliographic data were analyzed using the Bibliometrix package in RStudio and VOSviewer to evaluate scientific growth, research productivity, collaboration networks, conceptual structure, thematic evolution, and future research directions. The results reveal accelerated scientific expansion over the past decade, driven by a relatively concentrated group of influential journals, researchers, institutions, and countries, with China emerging as the leading contributor to both scientific production and international collaboration. Collaboration network analysis demonstrates an increasingly interconnected global research community, while keyword co-occurrence and thematic evolution analyses reveal a clear transition from sensor-based environmental monitoring toward integrated applications of the Internet of Things, artificial intelligence, machine learning, computer vision, and remote sensing for intelligent aquaculture management. Emerging themes further indicate growing attention to sustainability, predictive analytics, and autonomous decision-support systems. By synthesizing nearly five decades of scientific development, this study provides a comprehensive knowledge map of smart aquaculture research, identifies strategic research opportunities, and offers evidence-based guidance for researchers, policymakers, and technology developers advancing sustainable digital transformation within the global aquaculture sector.</p>','["Artificial intelligence", "Bibliometric analysis", "Digital innovation", "Internet of Things", "Smart aquaculture", "Sustainable aquaculture."]',NULL,21,1,'687 - 723','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1215','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',34),
(1216,1216,1,'Technology-Mediated Instructional Supervision for Arabic Language Education: Experiences of Muslim Educators','<p>Technology-mediated instructional supervision has emerged as an important approach for enhancing instructional quality, teacher professional development, and educational effectiveness across diverse learning environments. Within Islamic educational settings, however, limited evidence exists on how Muslim educators experience technology-mediated instructional supervision in supporting Arabic language education while preserving the linguistic, cultural, and religious foundations of Muslim learning communities. This study explored the experiences of Muslim educators regarding technology-mediated instructional supervision for Arabic language education in Bongao, Tawi-Tawi. Specifically, it examined educators'' experiences, identified the pedagogical, institutional, and technological challenges influencing supervisory practices, explored strategies for strengthening technology-mediated instructional supervision, and developed a contextually grounded supervisory framework. Employing a qualitative phenomenological research design, the study utilized purposive sampling to recruit ten Muslim educators actively involved in instructional supervision within schools implementing Arabic language instruction. Data were collected through semi-structured Key Informant Interviews and analyzed using Braun and Clarke''s thematic analysis. The findings revealed that technology-mediated instructional supervision was influenced by limited supervisory preparation, varying levels of Arabic language proficiency, inadequate institutional resources, organizational constraints, and culturally responsive supervisory relationships. Despite these challenges, participants identified continuous professional development, contextualized curriculum enhancement, and sustained institutional and community advocacy as essential strategies for strengthening supervisory practices and advancing Arabic language education. The study concludes that effective technology-mediated instructional supervision in Islamic educational settings requires context-responsive approaches that integrate digital innovation with pedagogical competence, linguistic preparedness, and the cultural and religious values underpinning Arabic language education. The findings contribute to the growing discourse on technology-mediated instructional supervision and educational information systems while offering practical insights for educational leaders, policymakers, and Islamic educational institutions seeking to design supervisory practices that are technologically informed, culturally responsive, and pedagogically meaningful.</p>','["technology-mediated instruction, instructional supervision, Arabic language, Islamic education, Muslim educators, BARMM."]',NULL,21,1,'724 - 740','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1216','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',35),
(1217,1217,1,'Intelligent Technologies for Blue Carbon Ecosystem Monitoring and Management: A Global Bibliometric Analysis of Emerging Digital Technologies and Future Research Directions','<p>This bibliometric study analyzed the global development of intelligent technologies for blue carbon ecosystem monitoring and management by examining research growth, scientific impact, collaboration patterns, conceptual structures, and emerging research directions. A total of 7,054 Scopus-indexed publications (1971–2026) were retrieved from the Scopus database and analyzed using Bibliometrix (RStudio) and VOSviewer. The findings revealed sustained growth in scientific production, particularly after 2010. Remote Sensing emerged as the leading publication source, while Universitas Gadjah Mada and East China Normal University were the most productive affiliations. The United States and China dominated publication output and citation impact, supported by collaboration among 57 countries. Science mapping identified six major keyword clusters centered on remote sensing, mangroves, blue carbon, geographic information systems, machine learning, LiDAR, and ecosystem monitoring. Thematic evolution indicated a shift toward artificial intelligence, deep learning, and data-driven approaches, providing a valuable reference for future blue carbon research and sustainable coastal ecosystem management.</p>','["Artificial intelligence", "Blue carbon", "Bibliometric analysis", "Geographic information systems", "Remote sensing", "Sustainable coastal management."]',NULL,21,1,'741 - 762','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1217','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',36),
(1218,1218,1,'Digitalization-Enhanced Teaching Approaches for Building Students'' Mental Fortitude in Highly Technical Courses in ICT, Science, and Mathematics','<p>The rapid digitalization of higher education has transformed instructional practices in highly technical disciplines by integrating digital learning environments, technology-enabled instructional tools, and interactive pedagogical strategies that support both academic achievement and students'' psychological development. In Information and Communication Technology (ICT), Science, and Mathematics education, where learners frequently encounter complex concepts, cognitive overload, and persistent academic challenges, digitalization-enhanced teaching approaches have emerged as valuable mechanisms for fostering resilience, perseverance, confidence, and adaptive coping. Despite the increasing adoption of digital technologies in higher education, limited research has explored how instructors intentionally utilize digitalization-enhanced teaching approaches to build students'' mental fortitude in highly technical courses. This exploratory qualitative study investigated how instructors implement digitalization-enhanced teaching approaches to strengthen students'' mental fortitude in ICT, Science, and Mathematics courses. Semi-structured interviews were conducted with eighteen instructors from higher education institutions, and the data were analyzed using reflexive thematic analysis. The findings revealed three major themes: technology-enabled feedback and digital scaffolding, digital simulations and interactive learning environments, and digital learning communities for collaborative resilience. These digitalization-enhanced approaches enabled instructors to provide immediate feedback, scaffold complex learning tasks, promote collaborative problem-solving, and create psychologically supportive digital learning environments that encouraged students to persist despite academic setbacks. Participants further observed that students became more resilient, confident, self-regulated, and motivated as they engaged with digitally supported learning experiences. The study demonstrates that digitalization functions not only as a catalyst for instructional innovation but also as a pedagogical mechanism for strengthening students'' mental fortitude in highly technical education. The findings provide practical implications for educators, curriculum developers, and higher education institutions seeking to develop digitally enriched learning environments that simultaneously promote academic excellence and students'' psychological well-being.</p>','["digitalization-enhanced teaching, digital pedagogy, digital learning environments, mental fortitude, academic resilience, ICT education, science education, mathematics education, higher education."]',NULL,21,1,'763 - 773','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1218','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',37),
(1219,1219,1,'AI-Assisted Physical Education: Promoting Positive Mindsets for Non-Enthusiast Learners','<p>This study investigates how inclusive, non-competitive physical education (PE) activities can enhance mental health and motivation among college students identified as non-enthusiasts, aiming to establish foundational user requirements for AI-assisted educational systems. Despite the recognized benefits of physical activity, traditional competitive frameworks often alienate students, leading to disengagement and stress. Anchored in Self-Determination Theory (SDT), this research explores how autonomy, competence, and relatedness can be fostered to inform the design of Information and Communication Technology (ICT) in educational settings. A qualitative descriptive approach was employed, utilizing semi-structured interviews with 15 college students aged 18–23 classified as non-PE enthusiasts. Thematic analysis revealed that when activities prioritize enjoyment and individual participation over peer competition, students experience enhanced self-worth, emotional relief, and reduced academic stress. These empirical human experiences provide the critical psychological baseline required to engineer empathetic smart systems. The findings demonstrate that future AI fitness platforms and gamified learning applications must be intentionally designed to emphasize personalized progress and cooperative engagement. Ultimately, this study bridges physical education and computer information systems, offering a data-driven framework for developing inclusive, technology-supported well-being interventions.</p>','["Artificial Intelligence, Information Systems, Physical Education, Mental Health, Motivation"]',NULL,21,1,'774 - 786','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1219','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',38),
(1220,1220,1,'Digitalization of Extracurricular Management Systems through AI and Behavioral Psychology','<p>Despite the rapid growth of educational technologies, smart campus systems often lack empirical validation regarding user motivation. This study assessed student engagement in extracurricular activities to establish quantitative user requirements for designing AI-integrated management platforms. A descriptive-quantitative research design was employed in a public senior high school involving 89 student respondents. Utilizing a survey method with a modified standardized questionnaire, baseline data was collected on how the psychological needs for autonomy, competence, and relatedness influence participation. The findings reveal that these fundamental psychological needs significantly impact engagement and serve as vital parameters for technology acceptance. Furthermore, students demonstrated minimal engagement in administrative leadership roles, highlighting an operational friction gap that artificial intelligence could effectively automate. The results emphasize that to sustain long-term involvement, software developers must engineer customizable user interfaces and gamified skill-tracking mechanisms that actively support independence, user capability, and digital social connection. Ultimately, educational institutions should deploy these empirically designed management ecosystems to foster user adoption, promote holistic student development, and ensure sustained participation in school organizations.</p>','["extracurricular activities, student engagement, smart campus technologies, artificial intelligence, self-determination theory"]',NULL,21,1,'787 - 795','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1220','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',39),
(1221,1221,1,'Technology Acceptance towards the Integration of Audio-Visual Conversational Videos in Teaching Filipino Drama and Language','<p>The Filipino language instruction faces challenges that affect the quality and effectiveness of language learning. The increasing influence of globalization, the dominance of English, the expansion of digital media, and the growing preference for foreign languages have contributed to the declining use of Filipino, raising concerns about its continued promotion and preservation. This descriptive study explored the application of audio-visual conversational videos in teaching Filipino drama and language among students and beyond. There were 27 education students (Filipino majors) who participated in the study. The study instrument was used to measure technology acceptance based on clarity, usefulness, language, style, and format, illustration, presentation, appropriateness, adequacy, and timeliness. In addition, their performance was measured before and after the audio-visual instructional method designed by the teachers. The findings revealed that students had a very high acceptance level towards the application of audio-visual conversational videos in teaching Filipino drama and language. Students’ performance improved by an average of approximately 2.33 points following the implementation. Students believed that conversational videos have significant implications for Filipino language education because it provides an innovative, technology-mediated approach that promotes learner engagement, improves academic performance, and strengthens students’ appreciation of Filipino drama, literature, and culture. Nevertheless, there is a potential for conversational videos to support learner-centered pedagogy, expand access to quality Filipino language instruction, and contribute to the long-term promotion, preservation, and wider dissemination of the Filipino language through meaningful and technology-assisted educational practices.</p>','["audio-visual learning, conversational videos, Filipino drama, Filipino language, technology acceptance"]',NULL,21,1,'796 - 806','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1221','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',40),
(1222,1222,1,'Smart Technology and Innovation in Seaweed Aquaculture: A Global Bibliometric and Scientometric Review','<p>Smart technology and innovation have become increasingly important in transforming seaweed aquaculture into a more sustainable, efficient, and data-driven industry. This study conducted a global bibliometric and scientometric review to examine research trends, technological developments, collaboration networks, and emerging scientific directions related to smart seaweed aquaculture. Bibliographic data were retrieved from the Scopus database using a structured search string covering technologies such as Internet of Things (IoT), artificial intelligence (AI), automation, remote sensing, blockchain, machine learning, and digital monitoring systems. The collected datasets were analyzed using RStudio through the Bibliometrix package and visualized using VOSviewer for network mapping and thematic analysis. Findings revealed a continuous increase in scientific publications, particularly after 2015, indicating growing global interest in precision aquaculture and sustainable marine resource management. The results identified China, India, Indonesia, and the United States as leading contributors in terms of research productivity and international collaboration. Keyword co-occurrence and thematic evolution analyses demonstrated the increasing integration of AI, IoT, automation, environmental monitoring, and sustainability-focused technologies within seaweed farming systems. Scientometric clustering further highlighted the interdisciplinary nature of the field, combining marine science, environmental studies, biotechnology, and digital innovation. The study also identified research gaps associated with technological accessibility, collaboration disparities, and sustainable implementation in developing regions. Overall, the findings confirm that smart technologies are playing a transformative role in advancing seaweed aquaculture toward more intelligent, climate-resilient, and sustainable production systems globally.</p>','["seaweed aquaculture, smart technology, bibliometric analysis, scientometric review, artificial intelligence, Internet of Things (IoT), automation, remote sensing, preci-sion aquaculture, sustainability, digital innovation, marine resource management."]',NULL,21,1,'807 - 826','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1222','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',41),
(1223,1223,1,'AI-Driven Modernization of Medicare and Medicaid Enterprise Systems: Interoperability, Claims Analytics, and Fraud Detection Frameworks','<p>The Centers for Medicare &amp; Medicaid Services (CMS) is responsible for the provision of healthcare coverage to more than 150 million beneficiaries; however, the enterprise systems of CMS suffer from various issues, namely data interoperability, inefficiency, and fraud, waste, and abuse. Rule-based mechanisms have proven to be inadequate for fraud prevention in the changing environment, whereas the fragmentation of datasets of CMS limits the effectiveness of any analysis. Therefore, this paper aims to propose an integrated AI-based framework for healthcare analytics, which includes interoperability, predictive analytics, anomaly detection, and explainable artificial intelligence for large-scale fraud detection. In particular, the suggested framework includes a novel FHIR-like interoperability module that would allow to align heterogeneous datasets within CMS into a single patient-provider-focused data lake. A hybrid approach to the fraud detection algorithm implementation is introduced based on supervised machine learning methods (Logistic Regression, Random Forest, XGBoost, LightGBM, CatBoost) with the use of imbalance aware training. Moreover, the framework incorporates unsupervised anomaly detection algorithms (Isolation Forest, Local Outlier Factor) and graph-based network analysis of providers to detect relational fraud. Experimental results on a dataset with over 1.1 million samples show that XGBoost outperforms other classifiers, producing the highest accuracy at 99.64%, the highest ROC-AUC at 0.9998, and the highest F1-score at 0.96. Unsupervised models continue to detect anomalous providers at a rate of 2%, whereas graph-based analysis detects suspicious communities among providers at 58% of the total set. Claim frequency and Medicare payment amounts appear as features used most frequently by feature attribution in detecting fraudulent activities.</p>','["Healthcare Fraud Detection, Artificial Intelligence (AI), Machine Learning, Healthcare Analytics, Data Interoperability, FHIR, Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI), Predictive Analytics, XGBoost, Random Forest, LightGBM, CatBoost, Logistic Regression, Anomaly Detection, Isolation Forest."]',NULL,21,1,'827 - 866','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1223','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',42),
(1224,1224,1,'Assess the Effectiveness and Robustness of the Developed Framework Through Rigorous Testing and Real-World Deployment Scenarios to Ensure its Efficacy in Bolstering Blockchain Security','<p>Validation of a secure blockchain architecture''s effectiveness and robustness may be achieved via a methodical process that involves comprehensive testing and implementation in real-world environments. The testing process includes analyzing requirements, setting up the environment, and conducting detailed evaluations. All aspects of the smart contract, from its logic and functionality to its defences against common attack vectors like Sybil and re-entrancy vulnerabilities, are evaluated in these reports. Performance testing under different loads and realistic network conditions is essential for assessing scalability, latency, and throughput, in addition to API and integration testing, which ensure that system components operate well together. System resilience to defects and hostile events is tracked, critical test cases are automated, and large-scale peer-to-peer networks are modelled to evaluate the framework''s robustness further. Supply chain verification and clinical trial administration are two examples of real-world applications of blockchain technology that demonstrate its ability to secure sensitive activities on a large scale. These use cases also give light on the system''s efficacy, data integrity, and anomaly detection capabilities. Verifying the scalability, security, and reliability of a blockchain architecture against real business objectives via integrated deployments and complicated testing methods is essential for a safe blockchain.</p>','["blockchain, security testing, robustness, real-world deployment, smart contracts."]',NULL,21,1,'867 - 891','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1224','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',43),
(1225,1225,1,'Technology Acceptance and the Perceived Effectiveness of Social Networking Sites in Predicting Consumer Behavior in Clothing Purchases','<p>The increasing integration of social networking sites into online commerce has transformed these platforms into important channels for consumer purchasing, particularly in the fashion retail sector. As social networking sites continue to evolve into digital marketplaces, understanding how technology acceptance shapes consumers'' perceptions of their effectiveness has become increasingly important. This study examined the relationship between technology acceptance and the perceived effectiveness of social networking sites for clothing purchases using the Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (UTAUT) framework. A descriptive-correlational research design was employed involving 159 randomly selected students, faculty members, and administrative staff from North Eastern Mindanao State University–Cantilan Campus. Descriptive statistics and Pearson product–moment correlation analysis were used to analyze the data. The findings revealed that respondents perceived social networking sites as highly effective platforms for clothing purchases, particularly in terms of shopping convenience, customer reviews and feedback, and user engagement. Likewise, respondents demonstrated a very high level of technology acceptance, with performance expectancy, effort expectancy, and social influence emerging as the most prominent dimensions. Correlation analysis further showed significant positive relationships between the UTAUT constructs and all dimensions of perceived effectiveness, indicating that consumers who perceive social networking sites as useful, easy to use, and socially supported are more likely to regard them as effective environments for product discovery, purchase evaluation, and online transactions. The findings highlight the importance of fostering technology acceptance to strengthen consumers'' confidence in social commerce platforms and enhance their overall shopping experiences. Businesses may benefit from developing customer-centered digital strategies that prioritize platform usability, meaningful user engagement, and trustworthy consumer interactions to sustain long-term participation in the evolving e-commerce landscape.</p>','["consumer behavior", "perceived effectiveness", "social networking sites", "technology acceptance", "Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (UTAUT)"]',NULL,21,1,'892 - 904','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1225','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',44),
(1226,1226,1,'A Hybrid Graph and Transformer-Based Deep Learning Model for Multi-Omics Drug Response Prediction','<p>Background:<br/>Predicting drug response is critical for precision medicine, enabling personalized therapies and minimizing adverse effects. However, multi-omics data pose challenges such as high dimensionality, missing values, noise, and class imbalance.<br/>Methods:<br/>This study proposes a hybrid graph-aware framework, GraphTrans-Omics, integrating multi-stage preprocessing and learning. Data are normalized using log₂ transformation and z-score scaling, followed by imputation via Singular Value Thresholding (SVT). Feature selection combines Mutual Information, Recursive Feature Elimination, and LASSO. To address class imbalance, a graph-based augmentation method (GSMOTE-GMC) is introduced. Random Forest and Multi-Layer Perceptron models are trained on the processed features with graph-based enhancement.<br/>Results:<br/>The framework achieves competitive performance with accuracy of 0.42–0.46, precision of 0.44, recall of 0.43, AUC of 0.64, and RMSE of 0.69. Improvements are observed over baseline models in terms of stability and minority-class representation.<br/>Conclusion:<br/>The proposed approach provides a robust and interpretable solution for multi-omics drug response prediction. It demonstrates potential for pharmacogenomics applications and offers a foundation for future graph-based deep learning models.</p>','["Data augmentation, drug response prediction, drug molecules, gene interaction, multi-omics data"]',NULL,21,1,'905 - 939','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1226','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',45),
(1228,1228,1,'Cyber Algorithmic Governance: AI as a new normative agent in contemporary law','<p>Just a decade ago, artificial intelligence (AI) was still a vague promise in the legal field; today it is an unavoidable actor. Their algorithms are involved—sometimes without us realizing it—in the way laws are written, precedents are interpreted, and administrative decisions are executed. Far from being a simple technical support, AI has become a new normative power link that alters the classic triangle formed by Law, State and citizenship (Hildebrandt, 2015). This phenomenon, which some authors call algorithmic governance, means that AI systems go from being "regulated objects" to "normative agents" capable of setting, de facto, standards of conduct with very real legal consequences (Yeung, 2017). The question is no longer whether or not to use algorithms, but how to ensure that their use respects the transparency, equality, and accountability demanded by contemporary democracies (Crawford, 2021). To understand the scope of this transformation, we took a mixed methodological approach. First, we conducted a systematic review of the literature covering the last decade of academic research. We then conduct a comparative analysis of regulatory frameworks – with a particular focus on the European Union''s AI Regulation – and finally, we examine case studies that reveal both the benefits (efficiency, consistency) and risks (opacity, bias) of delegating legal functions to automated systems. With this three-pronged approach, we identify opportunities for innovation, but we also warn of the dangers of eroding basic principles such as legal certainty and equality before the law.</p>','["algorithmic governance", "artificial intelligence", "regulation", "biases", "explainability", "responsibility."]',NULL,21,1,'940 - 956','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1228','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',46),
(1229,1229,1,'ARDA-GATE: Auditable Recovery Decision Architecture for Unsafe-Promotion Prevention in Hybrid Oracle Database Failover','<p>Hybrid Oracle database failover can return a successful command while still being unsafe: telemetry may be stale, redo apply lag may exceed policy, the old primary may remain write-capable, routes may leak active writers, or the promoted target may not yet accept durable application writes. This paper presents Auditable Recovery Decision Architecture with Guarded Admission and Transition Evidence (ARDA-GATE), an auditable admission-control layer for Oracle hybrid failover. ARDA-GATE does not replace Data Guard, broker-managed role transition, Fast-Start Failover, or infrastructure orchestration. Instead, it sits between failure detection and write release: it collects Oracle, broker, host, route, fencing, and write-probe observations; validates source freshness; applies non-compensatory safety gates; ranks only already admitted candidates; and preserves a replayable evidence bundle. A 30-run Oracle Database 19c pre-production fault-injection campaign compares immediate promotion, threshold-only promotion, pause-and-reconcile recovery, and ARDA-GATE under Maximum Availability and Maximum Performance conditions. The results distinguish command-time recovery from safety-credited recovery using validated recovery time, System Change Number (SCN) gap evidence, route and fencing proof, write-probe validation, rejected-target checks, manual intervention, and evidence completeness. Maximum Availability zero-gap outcomes are attributed to synchronized standby protection, whereas Maximum Performance tests show ARDA-GATE rejecting or holding unsafe high-lag candidates and recording bounded exposure when policy permits release. Six scheduled production disaster-recovery exercises validate operational feasibility under change controls with confidential identifiers redacted. The result is a practical admission-control framework for releasing writes only after one safe write authority is proven.</p>','["Auditable failover, database reliability engineering, Data Guard, disaster recovery, failover validation, guarded admission, high availability, Oracle database, recovery decision architecture, unsafe promotion."]',NULL,21,1,'957 - 992','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1229','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',47),
(1230,1230,1,'Key Performance Indicators for Assessing Strategic Procurement and Supply Chain Effectiveness in the FMCG Industry','<p>The fast-moving consumer goods industry requires efficient procurement and supply chain systems to maintain product availability, control costs, manage suppliers, and respond rapidly to changing market demand. This study evaluated the effectiveness of key performance indicators in assessing strategic procurement and supply chain performance in FMCG organisations. A quantitative explanatory design was employed by integrating cross-sectional survey responses with retrospective company performance records. Data were collected from 248 procurement, supply chain, logistics, inventory, production, quality-control, and finance professionals representing 38 FMCG firms, while 214 responses were matched with objective organisational records. Strategic procurement effectiveness was assessed through cost management, strategic sourcing, supplier relationships, process efficiency, risk and resilience, sustainability and compliance, and digital procurement. <br/>Descriptive statistics, reliability and validity tests, regression analysis, importance–performance analysis, and structural equation modelling were applied. The results showed that KPI effectiveness was the strongest predictor of supply chain effectiveness, followed by strategic sourcing, procurement-process efficiency, and cost management. Strategic procurement significantly improved supplier performance, process efficiency, and KPI effectiveness, while supplier performance and process efficiency partially mediated its relationship with overall supply chain outcomes. KPI effectiveness also strengthened the contribution of process efficiency to supply chain performance. The comparison between survey assessments and company records demonstrated strong agreement, confirming the practical relevance of the proposed KPI framework. Although cost management and strategic sourcing were comparatively well developed, implementation gaps remained in digital procurement, risk management, sustainability, compliance, and process automation. The study concludes that FMCG firms should adopt an integrated performance-measurement framework combining financial, operational, supplier-related, digital, risk, sustainability, inventory, logistics, and customer-service indicators to improve decision-making, resilience, and long-term competitiveness.</p>','["strategic procurement", "key performance indicators", "supply chain effectiveness", "supplier performance", "process efficiency", "FMCG industry"]',NULL,21,1,'993 - 1007','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1230','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',48),
(1251,1251,1,'Utilizing Vegetation Indices to Assess Brown Rust Disease Infection on Leaves of Sugarcane Crops in Maharashtra State of India','<p>Sugarcane is a key cash crop in the Maharashtra state and a substantial contributor to its agricultural economy. But also, sugarcane yield is put to great risk by brown rust disease which is a fungal disease caused by the pathogen Puccinia Melanocephala. The main purpose of this study is to utilize vegetation indices from multispectral imagery to quantify the amount of brown rust disease spread in sugarcane plantations. A survey of a 2-acre sugarcane field which is located in Maharashtra, India is carried out using a multispectral drone to produce an orthomosaic. NDVI, NDRE, GNDVI, GLI, and SAVI such vegetation indices are calculated. Further, these indices are used to assess disease severity. The disease severity map demonstrates that these vegetation indices precisely detect and quantify the spread of brown rust disease infection in sugarcane plantations. These indices offer a scalable and trustworthy way for monitoring disease in precision agriculture.</p>','["Vegetation Index", "NDVI", "GNDVI", "NDRE", "GLI", "SAVI"]',NULL,21,1,'1008 - 1025','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1251','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',49);
<p>This research delves into the realm of cross-domain recommendation systems, which leverage insights from one domain to enhance recommendations in another. Specifically, we explore the integration of user behavior and interests with source domain item data to offer precise suggestions within the target domain. By analyzing user responses to these recommendations, we iteratively refine future suggestions. This approach, known as cross domain recommendation making, capitalizes on richer information from the source domain to bolster recommendation accuracy in the target domain.</p>
<p>To realize this innovative recommender system, a dataset encompassing user profiles and interactions with items in the source domain is imperative. However, such datasets are scarce and challenging to obtain for practical applications. To address this limitation, we advocate the initial development of an in-domain recommender that considers user demographic information to generate recommendations within the source domain. User feedback on these recommendations is collected and integrated with user profiles to inform recommendations within the target domain.</p>
<p>This methodology ensures that recommendations from the source domain not only rely on user profile information but also incorporate interactions within that domain, thereby elevating the quality of generated suggestions.</p>
<p>While specific results from the proposed system are yet to be obtained, we anticipate that our approach will significantly improve recommendation accuracy across domains. By iteratively refining suggestions based on user responses, we aim to demonstrate the effectiveness of cross-domain recommendation making in enhancing user experience and engagement.</p>
<p>Overall, this research contributes to the advancement of recommender systems by proposing a novel approach that leverages deep learning techniques to bridge the gap between different domains and provide more personalized recommendations to users.</p>','["Cross Domain Recommender Systems, Transfer Learning, Topic Modelling"]',NULL,21,1,'1026 - 1037','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1252','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',50),
(1253,1253,1,'A Deep Learning-Based Feature Fusion Framework for Automated Thalassemia','<p>Thalassemia is a blood disorder which restricts the body from producing enough hemoglobin, the protein containing iron. People with thalassemia must get frequent blood transfusions to maintain the hemoglobin levels in. In a thalassemic patient the blood smear images show microcytosis, hypochromia, anisocytosis, poikilocytosis, and codocytes due to defective hemoglobin synthesis. Early detection helps in the rapid and accurate identification of abnormal red blood cell morphology. Traditional analysis is prone to misinterpretation and time-intensive which leads to delayed treatment. In this approach a hybrid framework is built for automated thalassemia detection using deep learning approach. The EfficientNet algorithm extracts the local features and Vision Transformer extracts the global features. The combined features are utilized for binary classification of Thalassemia and Non-Thalassemia. This approach has provided an accuracy of 95%, 94% sensitivity, 96% specificity, 94% positive predicted value, 94% recall. This approach effectively automates thalassemia detection from blood smear images with balanced performance metrics and high accuracy.</p>','["Thalassemia Detection", "Peripheral Blood Smear", "Deep Learning", "EfficientNet", "Vision Transformer", "Binary Classification", "Feature Fusion", "Medical Image Analysis"]',NULL,21,1,'1038 - 1057','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1253','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',51),
(1254,1254,1,'Remote Work Flexibility, Technological Infrastructure, and Managerial Support as Drivers of Productivity: The Moderating Role of Job Satisfaction','<p>The increasing adoption of remote and hybrid work has reshaped organizational practices and heightened the importance of maintaining high employee productivity. However, empirical studies examining the combined impact of remote work flexibility, technological infrastructure, and administrative support on productivity in Saudi Arabian work environments remain very limited. This study examines these relationships while assessing the moderating role of job satisfaction. A quantitative cross-sectional approach was employed, with participants selected from a stratified random sample of employees experienced in either remote or hybrid work. The findings revealed that remote work flexibility (β = 0.45, p &lt; 0.001), technological infrastructure (β = 0.42, p &lt; 0.001), and managerial support (β = 0.33, p &lt; 0.001) significantly enhanced employee productivity, with remote work flexibility emerging as the strongest predictor. The analysis further showed that job satisfaction strengthened the positive association between remote work flexibility and employee productivity (β = 0.13, p = 0.002). The findings demonstrate that integrating flexible work policies, advanced technological infrastructure, supportive leadership, and initiatives to enhance job satisfaction can boost employee productivity. The results also indicate that employees with higher job satisfaction achieve greater productivity gains through flexible work arrangements. This study offers practical applications for organizational leaders seeking to accelerate digital transformation in line with Saudi Vision 2030 and contributes to the literature by broadening the application of the Job Demands–Resources (JD-R) model to remote and hybrid work settings within Saudi organizations.</p>','["Remote work flexibility, technological infrastructure, managerial support, employee productivity, job satisfaction, Saudi Arabia, Saudi organizations."]',NULL,21,1,'1058 - 1077','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1254','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',52),
(1255,1255,1,'The Effect of Training and Development Programs on Employee Performance in the Saudi Retail Industry','<p>This study investigates how organizational training and long-term development initiatives affect employee performance among retail sector employees working in Saudi Arabia. Despite the growing importance of human capital development within the framework of Saudi Vision 2030, empirical evidence regarding the efficiency and effectiveness of training and development programs in the Saudi retail sector. The study used a quantitative approach based on a structured questionnaire distributed to employees in the Saudi retail sector. The collected data were examined using SPSS, including descriptive statistics, reliability assessment, EFA, Pearson correlation, multiple regression, and ANOVA. The findings reveal that both training programs and development initiatives exert significant positive effects on employee performance. Regression analysis demonstrates that training programs (β = 0.412, p &lt; 0.001) and development initiatives (β = 0.397, p &lt; 0.001) significantly predict employee performance, while the overall model explains 47.2% of the variance in employee performance (R² = 0.472). Correlation analysis further confirms strong positive associations between training, development, and employee performance. In addition, ANOVA results indicate significant differences in employee performance according to the frequency and type of training received, with employees participating in regular, job-related training exhibiting superior performance. Systematic investment in long-term employee training and development enhances workforce efficiency and productivity, as well as long-term organizational effectiveness, thus supporting human capital theory. This study highlights the strategic role of structured employee training and development in supporting the long-term success of organizations. The findings encourage human resources managers to implement continuous training and development initiatives that align with strategic business priorities and Saudi Vision 2030. Furthermore, this study extends existing research on training and development within the Saudi retail sector, offering evidence-based recommendations to enhance organizational efficiency, effectiveness, and sustainable performance.</p>','["Training programs", "Development initiatives", "Employee performance", "Human resource development", "Retail industry", "Saudi Arabia."]',NULL,21,1,'1078 - 1103','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1255','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',53),
(1256,1256,1,'Investigation of Thermal Performance of Solar Air Heater with parabolic Absorber Shapes Using Experimentation & ANSYS Simulations','<p>The increasing global demand for clean and sustainable energy has accelerated research on high-performance solar thermal systems. Among these, Solar Air Heaters (SAHs) offer a simple and cost-effective solution; however, their thermal efficiency is strongly influenced by absorber plate geometry. This study investigates the thermal performance of a Solar Air Heater employing parabolic absorber plates with four different configurations: regular inward, regular outward, zigzag inward, and zigzag outward. The performance evaluation was carried out through both experimental investigations and Computational Fluid Dynamics (CFD) simulations using ANSYS Workbench R15.0. The analysis considered inlet and outlet air temperatures, solar irradiance, heat transfer rate, Reynolds number, Nusselt number, and thermal efficiency under operating conditions from 11:00 AM to 3:00 PM. Experimental results demonstrate that the parabolic zigzag (outward) configuration provides the best thermal performance throughout the day due to enhanced airflow turbulence, improved heat transfer, and greater solar energy absorption. At peak solar irradiance (1350 W/m² at 2:00 PM), this configuration achieved the highest experimental thermal efficiency of 28.70%, a heat transfer rate of 75 W/m², and a CFD-predicted efficiency of 31.10%. The CFD results closely agreed with the experimental observations, confirming the reliability of the numerical model with only minor deviations. Comparative analysis further revealed that outward configurations consistently outperform inward configurations, while zigzag profiles provide superior heat transfer characteristics compared to regular profiles. Overall, the parabolic zigzag (outward) absorber geometry proved to be the most effective design for enhancing thermal efficiency and solar energy utilization in solar air heaters. The findings provide valuable guidance for the optimization of absorber plate geometries and support the development of efficient, sustainable, and large-scale solar thermal energy systems.</p>','["Solar Air Heater, Parabolic Absorber Plate, Regular and Zigzag Configurations, Thermal Efficiency, CFD Simulation, ANSYS Workbench, Experimental Analysis, Heat Transfer, Renewable Energy."]',NULL,21,1,'1104 - 1127','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1256','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',54),
(1257,1257,1,'Linguistic Features of AI-Generated Academic Texts and the Role of Human Editing','<p>The rapid spread of large language models (LLMs) has significantly transformed academic writing practices and actualized discussions about authorship, language quality, and academic integrity. At the same time, diachronic changes in academic discourse during the active implementation of generative artificial intelligence remain insufficiently studied. The study combines a systematic literature review with a corpus diachronic analysis of authentic academic annotations, allowing us to trace long-term trends in the development of academic discourse. The aim of the work is to identify linguistic changes in academic writing during 2015–2025 and determine the role of human editing in quality assurance of AI-assisted scientific texts. The research material was a corpus of 870 English-language annotations of scientific articles indexed in the Scopus database in the field of arts and humanities. Quantitative linguistic analysis was carried out using Python tools. Indicators of lexical density, lexical diversity, syntactic complexity, average sentence length and frequency of cohesive markers were analyzed. A statistically significant increase in lexical density and frequency of cohesive markers has been revealed, indicating an increase in information compression and explicit discursive organization of texts. Indicators of traditional lexical diversity and syntactic complexity remained relatively stable. The observed trends are consistent with characteristics described in AI-assisted writing studies; however, the study design does not allow them to be directly related to the use of large language models. Human editing remains a key factor in ensuring factual accuracy, discursive coherence, lexical enrichment, and academic integrity. The results can be used to develop practices for the responsible use of generative AI in academic communication.</p>','["Large Language Models", "Academic Discourse", "Human-AI Collaboration", "Linguistic Features", "Human Editing."]',NULL,21,1,'1128 - 1141','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1257','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',55),
(1258,1258,1,'Optimized Chaotic DNA Encryption Model with Quantum Key Generation','<p>Rapidly growing speed of quantum computing introduces unprecedented danger to modern cryptographic solutions, so security beyond typical encryption is imperative. Here, we present a hybrid cryptosystem consisting of chaotic map-based keystream generation, DNA-inspired quaternary encoding, and quantum-enhanced key generation. This architecture is constructed to address post-quantum security needs, while preserving computational efficiency for encryption of high-value information. Experimentation results confirm that hybrid key generation has a near-maximum entropy of 7.97 bits per byte, and passes benchmarks that are applied in the modern dataset. Multilayer encryption cascade, with deterministic chaos and DNA encoding, achieves a 96.8% avalanche effect, which means it possesses strong diffusion characteristics. Comparing AES-256-GCM and ChaCha20-Poly1305, we see competitive security–throughput trade-offs. Moreover, the machine learning–based analytics layer on encrypted metadata shows 93.8% accuracy on CICIDS2017 dataset, without impacting the core cryptographic security, maintaining formal security integrity and reproducibility.</p>','["chaotic cryptography, DNA encoding, quantum key distribution, post-quantum security, hybrid encryption, and intrusion detection."]',NULL,21,1,'1142 - 1161','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1258','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',56),
(1260,1260,1,'SARG-QLoRA: Syntax-Aware Retrieval-Guided Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning for Dependency Parsing with Autoregressive Large Language Models','<p>Dependency Parsing forms one of the key building blocks of natural language processing tasks such as semantic role labelling, machine translation, and information extraction since it furnishes the syntactic structure on which those high-level tasks depend. While transformer-based architectures can model highly expressive representations, there exists a tough trade-off between the two existing methods: specialized parsers can reach high benchmark scores but need special parsing heads and specific training processes, while generative large language models have flexible architecture but do not have any structural guarantee of forming dependency trees as outputs. This paper introduces SARG-QLoRA, a method which solves this trade-off problem by merging Syntax-Aware Retrieval Guidance (SARG) with Quantized Low-Rank Adaptation (QLoRA), allowing for the generation of dependency structures on a consumer-grade hardware setup. In contrast to conventional retrieval-augmented generation approaches where context selection is performed based on semantic similarity, the proposed approach generates a FAISS index based on multilingual-e5-base embedding of training examples and performs syntactically similar retrieval of context. In addition to a Tree Validity Checker (TVC) that validates four well-formedness formal criteria after generation, a structural validity dimension is introduced that is orthogonal to traditional attachment scores metrics. After training solely on the Universal Dependencies English Web Treebank and fine-tuning just 0.75% of the parameters (24.3 million out of 3.24 billion), SARG-QLoRA reaches 81.35% Unlabelled Attachment Score (UAS) and 78.28% Labelled Attachment Score (LAS) — just 1.07 UAS away from the specialized Stanza parser — while preserving zero-shot transfer capability to Spanish (62.81% UAS), German (61.51%), and French (59.11%). TVC analysis shows structural validity for 87.14% of the test set, balanced brackets for 97.35%, and one root per tree for 99.95%.</p>','["Dependency Parsing", "Large Language Models", "Retrieval-Augmented Generation", "QLoRA", "Universal Dependencies", "Structural Validation", "Cross-Lingual Transfer", "Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning"]',NULL,21,1,'1162 - 1198','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1260','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',57),
(1261,1261,1,'XSentiFusionNet: An Explainable Cross-Modal Attention Framework For Audio-Visual Sentiment Analysis Using Hybrid Deep Learning','<p>The exponential growth of audio-visual content created through social media, communication platforms as well as human-computer interaction has led to a need for effective multimodal sentiment analysis. Most of the multimodal frameworks have limitations in cross-modal interaction modeling, fusion strategy adaptability, interpretability, and robustness to noise. This paper introduces XSentiFusionNet, an end-to-end explainable multimodal framework for audio-visual sentiment analysis that incorporates cross-modal transformer-attention, reliability-aware adaptive fusion, and XAI. Our approach uses CNNs, BiLSTMs, and ViTs to capture emotional features while adaptively combining acoustic and visual modalities based on their reliability levels. Model transparency is achieved by integrating SHAP, LIME, Grad-CAM, and attention mechanisms. Comprehensive experiments were performed on CMU-MOSEI, MELD, and RAVDESS benchmark datasets using comparative analysis, ablation studies, cross-dataset generalization experiments, confusion matrix analysis, explainability, and robustness to noise experiments. Our framework achieved an accuracy of 94.82%, F1-score of 94.11% and a ROC-AUC score of 96.04% on the benchmark datasets performing better than existing approaches such as CNN-LSTM, Transformer Fusion, and Multimodal BERT. Additionally, the framework showed higher robustness to noise and generalization ability on different multimodal datasets while the XAI module demonstrated interpretability by highlighting key speech and facial features used for predictions. These results show that XSentiFusionNet is a reliable and efficient framework for audio-visual sentiment analysis and can be used in real-world multimodal processing and affective computing scenarios.</p>','["Multimodal Sentiment Analysis", "Cross-Modal Attention", "Audio-Visual Emotion Recognition", "Explainable AI, Deep Learning", "Transformer", "Hybrid Fusion Network."]',NULL,21,1,'1199 - 1223','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1261','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',58),
(1262,1262,1,'Intelligent Coordination of Collision Avoidance and Composite Anti-Lock Braking Systems for Stability Enhancement and Regenerative Energy Optimization in Electric Vehicles','<p>This study introduces an intelligent coordination framework which combines a Collision Avoidance System (CAS), Composite Anti-Lock Braking System (CABS), Vehicle Stability Control (VSC), and Regenerative Braking System (RBS) to improve the safety, stability and energy efficiency of an Electric Vehicle. The proposed system utilizes the multi-sensor fusion, adaptive braking control, and intelligent decision making to achieve optimal braking performance in both normal and emergency driving conditions. The results of simulations indicated that the ABS controller kept the wheel slip ratio in the optimum range (0.15–0.18), which was beneficial for preventing wheel lock and ensuring stable braking. The vehicle slowed down from 25 m/s to 0 m/s in about 6.6 s and had a safe clearance of 8 m from obstacles. Regenerative braking raised the battery SoC from 0.7000 to 0.70152, recovering almost 116 kJ of energy. The Time-to-Collision after intervention was approximately 74 s, which signified good collision avoidance. The results show that the proposed integrated braking strategy enhances the braking safety, vehicle stability, collision avoidance and rejuvenation of the regenerative energy in modern electric vehicles.</p>','["Collision Avoidance System (CAS)", "Composite Anti-Lock Braking System (CABS)", "Regenerative Braking, Vehicle Stability Control", "Electric Vehicles (EVs)"]',NULL,21,1,'1224 - 1245','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1262','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',59),
(1263,1263,1,'NAS Design with SYNOLOGY as an Alternative to Cloud Storage for Game Development','<p>Cloud storage has become a critical digital infrastructure in supporting academic activities, particularly in the game development laboratory at the Faculty of Information Technology, Universitas Tarumanagara (FTI Untar). This laboratory requires large and well-managed storage capacity to accommodate various types of data, including students’ thesis documents, game development reference books, and digital assets obtained from platforms such as the Unity Asset Store. However, reliance on third-party cloud services such as Google Drive and Microsoft OneDrive has introduced limitations due to restricted storage capacity, which affects the efficiency of managing and distributing digital resources. Previous research proposed a Network Attached Storage (NAS) solution based on a Single Board Computer (SBC) Raspberry Pi as a private cloud alternative. While functional, the solution requires considerable effort in system configuration and maintenance. In alignment with the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs), particularly Goal 9: Industry, Innovation, and Infrastructure, this study aims to design and develop a NAS system using Synology NAS, offering a more reliable, manageable, and integrated storage solution for institutional use. The research methodology adopts the Network Development Life Cycle (NDLC), which includes analysis, design, simulation, implementation, monitoring, and management stages. The system is further evaluated through alpha and beta testing using questionnaires distributed to students, alumni, and lecturers within FTI Untar, particularly in the game development laboratory environment. The results indicate positive user responses, highlighting ease of file downloading, user-friendly interface, and accessible system usage. These findings demonstrate that the proposed system provides an effective, scalable, and user-friendly private cloud storage solution to support academic and research activities.</p>','["Network attached storage", "innovation Synology", "network development", "life cycle", "cloud storage"]',NULL,21,1,'1246 - 1255','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1263','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',60),
(1264,1264,1,'Artificial Intelligence Literacy and Responsible Use In Higher Education: A Structural Model Of Behavioral Intention Among Ecuadorian University Students','<p>Artificial intelligence literacy is often assumed to be a uniform antecedent of its adoption, without empirically contrasting the relative weight of its distinct dimensions on behavioral intention to use. This study examined, through partial least squares structural equation modeling (PLS-SEM), the relationships between AI awareness, critical evaluation, ethical perception, self-efficacy, and prior experience, and behavioral intention to use artificial intelligence tools, in a sample of 300 Ecuadorian university students. The measurement model showed adequate reliability and validity across the six constructs assessed (AVE between 0.560 and 0.757; maximum HTMT of 0.634). The structural model explained 45.3 percent of the variance in behavioral intention, with eleven of the twelve hypothesized paths reaching statistical significance. The central finding of the study is that ethical perception toward artificial intelligence did not significantly predict behavioral intention to use it (beta = 0.042; p = 0.365), unlike awareness, critical evaluation, self-efficacy, and prior experience, all of which did. This result evidences a disconnect between ethical sensitization and declared technological behavior, with relevant implications for the design of training strategies on responsible artificial intelligence use in higher education.</p>','["Artificial intelligence literacy", "behavioral intention to use", "ethical perception", "self-efficacy", "PLS-SEM"]',NULL,21,1,'1256 - 1272','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1264','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',61),
(1265,1265,1,'Algebraic Structures in Modern Information Technology','<p>Modern information technology is implemented in hardware, networks, and software, but many of its correctness guarantees are algebraic. Boolean algebras describe switching logic; monoids and semigroups describe sequential composition; finite fields and vector spaces support cryptography and error-correcting codes; and lattices model information ordering. This paper provides a concise account of these structures and proves elementary results that demonstrate how closure, associativity, distance, homomorphism, order, and inverses yield digital representation, reliable transmission, structure-preserving encoding, and public-key inversion.</p>','["algebraic structures, Boolean algebra, finite fields, coding theory, cryptography, information technology."]',NULL,21,1,'1273 - 1276','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1265','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',62),
(1267,1267,1,'Management of Sports Programs in Higher Education Institutions: Insights from Cordillera Administrative Region (CAR) in the Philippines','<p>The management of sports programs among State Universities and Colleges (SUCs) in the Cordillera Administrative Region (CAR) was assessed using a descriptive-quantitative survey design involving 111 coach respondents. Data were collected through a researcher-developed, expert-validated questionnaire rated on a 4-point Likert scale. Descriptive statistics summarized coach profiles and domain ratings, and a one-way analysis of variance examined differences in perceptions across coach profile variables. The results indicated that most coaches were early-career practitioners: 64.60% reported 1 to 3 years of coaching experience, and 66.37% participated in only 1 to 5 competitions. More than half lacked formal coach training (58.41%) and accreditation (58.41%). Overall, sports program management was perceived as evident (M = 3.04). The strongest domains were organizational structure and leadership (M = 3.31) and community and stakeholder engagement (M = 3.29). Conversely, athlete development and support (M = 2.78) and coaching and staff development (M = 2.84) were rated lowest. ANOVA revealed no significant difference by years of coaching experience (p = .200), but significant differences were found by competition exposure (p = .003), awards received (p = .017), training level (p = .001), and accreditation level (p = .016). These findings suggest that while CARASUC sports programs demonstrate visible governance, leadership, and partnership foundations, there is a need for greater investment in athlete support, coach development, facility modernization, and systematic monitoring. A regional sports development framework is recommended to enhance policy alignment, funding, athlete welfare, coach accreditation, stakeholder collaboration, and continuous improvement across member institutions.</p>','["sports program management", "coach development", "athlete support", "CARASUC", "state universities and colleges", "sports leadership."]',NULL,21,1,'1277 - 1283','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1267','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',63),
(1268,1268,1,'Citizen-Centered Governance and Public Service Values: Towards an Inclusive Urban Governance Framework in a Philippine City','<p>Citizen-centered governance emphasizes understanding citizens'' perceptions to improve local governance and public service delivery. This study examined residents'' perceptions of public service values in the urban barangays of Tabuk City and explored the historical, political, linguistic, and contextual factors influencing these perceptions. An explanatory sequential mixed-methods design was employed. The quantitative phase involved 400 residents selected through proportionate stratified random sampling, while the qualitative phase included 35 purposively selected participants in semi-structured interviews. Survey data were analyzed using descriptive statistics, and interview data were examined through thematic analysis. Findings showed that residents generally held positive perceptions of public service values. Responsiveness and trust received the highest ratings, whereas accountability, transparency, inclusivity, and participation, although positively perceived, require further strengthening. Qualitative findings revealed that citizens'' perceptions are shaped by historical experiences, community memory, political culture, traditional leadership values, socioeconomic conditions, service experiences, education, political exposure, cultural values, and access to information. Integrating the quantitative and qualitative findings showed that citizens evaluate governance through both institutional performance and lived experiences, with public service values functioning as interconnected dimensions of effective governance. These meta-inferences informed the proposed Citizen-Centered Inclusive Urban Governance Framework, which highlights the integration of responsive service delivery, ethical leadership, transparency, accountability, inclusivity, and meaningful citizen participation to strengthen public trust and promote inclusive urban governance.</p>','["citizen-centered governance, public service values, local governance, public trust, accountability, transparency, participation."]',NULL,21,1,'1284 - 1304','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1268','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',64),
(1271,1271,1,'Superstitious Rituals and Perceived Performance Effects Among Top-Ranked Collegiate Team Sport Athletes: A Phenomenological Study','<p>Superstitious rituals are repetitive, symbolically meaningful behaviors that athletes believe may influence performance, despite the absence of a direct causal mechanism. In competitive sport, these rituals often serve as coping mechanisms for uncertainty, pressure, and fear of failure. This study explored the superstitious rituals practiced by top-ranked team sport athletes in the Cordillera Administrative Region Association of State Universities and Colleges (CARASUC), the purposes athletes attributed to those rituals, and their perceived effects on performance. Using a qualitative phenomenological design, 15 top-ranked team-sport athletes who self-identified as highly superstitious in a preliminary screening were interviewed individually during the 2019 CARASUC Athletic Meet. Interview transcripts and field notes were analyzed thematically. Findings showed that all participants practiced at least one ritual before, during, or after competition. Reported rituals included carrying religious items, wearing “lucky” clothing, performing repeated bodily gestures, reciting prayers, and following rigid routines tied to play situations. Five themes emerged regarding the perceived purpose of these rituals: comfort and relaxation, avoidance of negative consequences, mental preparation, injury prevention or protection, and beliefs related to luck. Although participants generally acknowledged that the rituals did not objectively determine game outcomes, they described them as psychologically beneficial because they reduced tension, strengthened confidence, and created a sense of control in uncertain competitive settings. The findings suggest that superstitious rituals among collegiate athletes operate less as irrational oddities than as psychologically meaningful coping practices embedded in performance culture.</p>','["superstition, rituals, collegiate athletes, team sports, phenomenology, sport psychology."]',NULL,21,1,'1305 - 1311','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1271','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',65),
(1272,1272,1,'Stock Market Reactions To Financial Statement Announcement: An Empirical Investigation Based On Chinese A-Shares','<p>Since the discovery of post earnings announcement drift, there has been a lot of research dedicated to this stock market anomaly. With Chinese A-share stock performance data from 2017 to 2024, we found a market-adjusted abnormal return of about 4.5% in the 120-trading day period following financial statement announcement, ranging from -10.0% to 11.8% depending on years. Using a panel model with fixed effects for both individual firm and fiscal year to identify the driving factors of this post-announcement drift, we found that the security financing facility has mitigated the positive announcement drift by reducing the average abnormal daily returns by 0.044, 0.059 and 0.063 percentage points for the 30, 60, and 90-day period. Our final model can explain 19.0%, 23.4% and 25.3% of the variation in post announcement average abnormal returns for the 30, 60, and 90-day period, respectively. Relevant market factors such as investor divergence of opinion and liquidity, information quality corresponding to the financial statement, investor inattention, and corporate fundamentals also play a role in explaining the post announcement abnormal returns.</p>','["post-announcement drift, security financing, divergence of opinion, information uncertainty, investor inattention, liquidity."]',NULL,21,1,'1312 - 1343','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1272','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',66),
(1273,1273,1,'Host Training Entity Assessment of the Workplace Performance and Employability Competencies of Bachelor of Arts in English Language Student Trainees','<p>This study evaluates the work performance of student trainees of the Bachelor of Arts in English Language Program through the Host Training Entities (HTEs) assessment for the school year 2024-2025. Specifically, this study aims at determining how the HTE supervisors assess the performance of the student trainees, and analyze the validity and reliability of the OJT (on-the-job training) rating tool, and identify the improvements for the performance of the trainees. The study used a mixed-methods approach and a survey structured with questions and incorporated the qualitative comments of the HTE supervisors in Tabuk City, Kalinga. It was found that the performance of the student trainees was assessed as very satisfactory, and that the majority of the student trainees met or exceeded the requirements of the demands of the workplace. It was found that the OJT rating tool was valid, as it measured the workplace skills and competencies of trainees, and the evaluation of the rating tool showed trainees'' effectiveness, professionalism, engagement in teamwork, initiative, and flexibility. It was remarked that the OJT evaluation tool had a limitation of gathering insufficient qualitative comments. It is significant to underscore training and evaluation to meet and prepare students for the challenges of the labor market. It can be concluded from this study, that the OJT programs, have been effective in promoting skills for employability, as this incorporates the preparation of the students for the actual working environment, but the programs need to be improved to fill the gaps of the competencies.</p>','["On-the-Job Training, student trainees, job performance, host training entities, performance evaluation, employability skills, workplace competencies, higher education."]',NULL,21,1,'1344 - 1355','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1273','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',67),
(1274,1274,1,'Effects of a Hale-Based Speed, Agility, and Quickness (SAQ) Training Program on Agility Performance in Male High School Futsal Players','<p>Futsal is characterized by frequent high-intensity accelerations, decelerations, multidirectional changes of direction, short sprints, and rapid perceptual–motor responses. Consequently, speed, agility, and quickness (SAQ) are essential physical qualities underpinning successful performance, particularly during adolescence when neuromuscular adaptations are highly responsive to training. Purpose: This study examined the effects of an eight-week Hale-based speed, agility, and quickness (SAQ) training programme on agility performance in adolescent male futsal players. Methods: Forty male futsal players aged 14–18 years from Matthayom Puranawas School participated in this study. Participants were allocated to an experimental group (n = 20) and a control group (n = 20) using a matched-pair procedure based on baseline Illinois Agility Test performance. The experimental group completed a structured Hale-based SAQ programme in addition to regular futsal training, whereas the control group continued regular futsal training only. Agility performance was assessed before and after the eight-week intervention using the Illinois Agility Test. Data were analysed using descriptive statistics, paired- and independent-samples t-tests, percentage change, and standardized effect size estimates, with statistical significance set at p &lt; .05. Results: Both groups demonstrated significant improvements in agility following the intervention (p &lt; .05); however, the experimental group achieved significantly greater gains. Illinois Agility Test performance improved from 18.09 ± 0.50 s to 16.93 ± 0.40 s (6.41%) in the experimental group, compared with 18.06 ± 0.47 s to 17.21 ± 0.46 s (4.71%) in the control group. Between-group comparisons at post-test indicated superior agility performance in the experimental group, accompanied by a moderate standardized effect size, suggesting meaningful practical benefits beyond conventional futsal training alone. Conclusion: An eight-week Hale-based SAQ training programme is an effective supplementary conditioning strategy for improving agility in adolescent male futsal players. The observed performance enhancements are likely associated with improved neuromuscular coordination, greater eccentric braking capacity, faster transitions from deceleration to acceleration, enhanced intermuscular coordination, and more efficient multidirectional movement mechanics.</p>','["speed, agility and quickness", "SAQ training", "futsal", "adolescent athletes", "change of direction", "Illinois Agility Test", "neuromuscular adaptation"]',NULL,21,1,'1356 - 1367','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1274','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',68),
(1275,1275,1,'Quality Assurance in Teacher Education: An Evaluation of the Bachelor of Elementary Education Program','<p>This study evaluated the Bachelor of Elementary Education (BEEd) program of J.H. Cerilles State College against the academic accreditation standards prescribed in Commission on Higher Education (CHED) Memorandum Order No. 74, series of 2017, using Provus'' Discrepancy Evaluation Model (DEM). Specifically, the evaluation examined the program across four stages of the model: design, installation, process, and product to determine the extent of alignment between institutional practices and prescribed quality standards. A concurrent triangulation mixed-methods design was employed, integrating quantitative data gathered through a researcher-developed evaluation instrument with qualitative evidence from semi-structured interviews and documentary analysis. Findings indicate that the BEEd program substantially complies with the minimum accreditation standards; however, several significant discrepancies remain. These include curriculum congestion, administrative assignments inconsistent with qualification requirements, excessive faculty workloads, insufficient library holdings, inadequacies in laboratory and physical facilities, inconsistent implementation of student retention policies, and below-target licensure examination performance. Despite these identified gaps, the institution demonstrates a sustained commitment to continuous quality improvement through strategic initiatives designed to strengthen program implementation and accreditation compliance. The study concludes that addressing the identified discrepancies through curriculum rationalization, faculty capability enhancement, infrastructure and learning resource development, and strengthened policy implementation will further improve program quality and graduate outcomes. The findings provide empirical evidence to guide institutional planning, quality assurance initiatives, and continuous improvement efforts in teacher education programs.</p>','["discrepancy evaluation model", "program evaluation", "teacher education", "accreditation standards", "quality assurance", "Bachelor of Elementary Education", "higher education."]',NULL,21,1,'1368 - 1388','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1275','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',69),
(1276,1276,1,'Evaluating a Reading Intervention Program for Elementary Learners: The Bawat Bata Makababasa Program (BBMP)','<p>This study evaluated the effectiveness of the Bawat Bata Makababasa Program (BBMP) in addressing reading learning gaps among elementary learners in Sominot District during the School Year 2025–2026. A mixed-methods research design combining quantitative and qualitative approaches was employed to provide a comprehensive evaluation of the program. Quantitative data were gathered from 34 teachers, 22 school heads, and the Comprehensive Rapid Literacy Assessment (CRLA) scores of 295 pupil-participants from 19 public elementary schools. Qualitative data were obtained through semi-structured interviews with teachers and school heads directly involved in the implementation of the program.<br/>Findings revealed that the BBMP was perceived as highly implemented in terms of program objectives, reading strategies, instructional materials, teacher and learner participation, and administrative support. Results further showed notable improvements in learners’ reading fluency and comprehension after participation in the intervention program. However, challenges such as learner absenteeism, limited parental involvement, and inconsistent remedial reading sessions affected program implementation.<br/>The study concludes that structured reading interventions, sustained administrative support, and active teacher participation significantly contribute to improving learners’ reading performance and reducing literacy-related learning gaps. The findings provide valuable implications for strengthening school-based literacy intervention programs in public elementary schools.</p>','["reading intervention", "literacy development", "learning gaps", "elementary learners", "BBMP."]',NULL,21,1,'1389 - 1401','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1276','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',70),
(1277,1277,1,'Toward Responsible AI Governance in Higher Education: AI Literacy, Use, Perceived Risks, and Policy Needs Among Students at Kalinga State University','<p>This cross-sectional study examined four student-level foundations of responsible artificial intelligence (AI) governance at Kalinga State University: AI literacy, patterns of academic use, perceived risks, and expectations for institutional policy. A structured 42-item questionnaire was completed by 667 students. The analysis used frequency, percentage, weighted mean, standard deviation, and Pearson correlation. Respondents were concentrated in the College of Engineering and Information Technology (48.43%), and 48.28% described their digital proficiency as basic. AI literacy was high overall (M = 4.08, SD = 0.60), with the strongest rating for familiarity with AI (M = 4.26). Using the interpretation bands stated in the method, overall AI use was high by tool category (M = 3.51, SD = 0.71) and academic purpose (M = 3.50, SD = 0.76), although the use of AI for data analysis and research remained moderate (M = 3.38). Students also reported high perceived risk (M = 3.99, SD = 0.64) and high policy need (M = 3.91, SD = 0.69). Every pair of major variables was positively associated (p &lt; .001); the strongest relationship linked perceived risks and policy needs (r = 0.771). The results support a governance approach that combines differentiated AI-literacy training, explicit rules for acceptable academic use, privacy and fairness safeguards, and regular consultation with students and faculty.</p>','["AI governance", "AI literacy", "higher education", "perceived risks", "policy needs", "student AI use."]',NULL,21,1,'1402 - 1414','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1277','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',71),
(1278,1278,1,'Teachers’ Mental Toughness and Emotional Flexibility Amid AI-Driven Teaching Demands','<p>Teachers’ mental toughness and emotional flexibility have become increasingly essential for sustaining effective instruction amid Artificial Intelligence (AI)-driven teaching demands and the growing complexity of learners’ academic and personal needs. This study explored how teachers develop these psychological capacities and utilize them alongside AI to support challenged learners, manage classroom complexities, and foster supportive learning environments. Employing an exploratory qualitative design, semi-structured interviews were conducted with eighteen purposively selected teachers, and the data were analyzed using reflexive thematic analysis. The findings revealed that professional and personal experiences strengthened teachers’ resilience, emotional regulation, and instructional adaptability, enabling them to integrate AI responsibly for differentiated instruction, personalized learning, instructional decision-making, and responsive learner support. Teachers consistently emphasized that AI enhanced, rather than replaced, professional judgment, empathy, and authentic teacher–learner relationships. The findings further indicate that mental toughness, emotional flexibility, and responsible AI integration function as interconnected adaptive capacities that strengthen teachers’ responsiveness to instructional complexity and learner diversity. These insights extend Psychological Flexibility Theory and Resilience Theory while providing practical implications for teacher education, professional development, and AI-supported educational practice.</p>','["Mental toughness", "Emotional flexibility", "Artificial Intelligence (AI)", "Teacher resilience", "Challenged learners"]',NULL,21,1,'1415 - 1428','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1278','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',72),
(1279,1279,1,'AI-Informed Upcycled Textile Products: Consumer Perceptions of Sustainability and Market Readiness','<p>Artificial Intelligence (AI)-informed textile upcycling strengthens sustainable product innovation by enhancing commercial readiness while preserving the environmental value of reclaimed materials. This qualitative descriptive inquiry examined consumer perceptions of AI-informed upcycled household products developed from secondhand (ukay-ukay) textiles and explored their commercial viability and sustainable innovation potential. Twelve (12) purposively selected product evaluators assessed an upcycled tote bag and a weather-protective hat through semi-structured interviews, with the data analyzed using thematic analysis. The findings revealed that consumer acceptance was principally influenced by functionality, durability, affordability, aesthetic quality, and climatic adaptability, while market readiness depended on continuous product refinement, branding, and consumer-oriented design. Participants further perceived AI as a complementary mechanism for design optimization, material enhancement, and consumer preference analysis rather than a substitute for human craftsmanship. Collectively, the findings indicate that integrating AI with sustainable craftsmanship reinforces consumer confidence, market acceptance, and environmentally responsible entrepreneurship.</p>','["Artificial Intelligence (AI), Textile Upcycling, Consumer Perceptions, Sustainable Product Innovation, Market Readiness, Circular Economy, Sustainable Entrepreneurship"]',NULL,21,1,'1429 - 1442','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1279','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',73),
(1280,1280,1,'The Social State Governed by the Rule of Law (Estado Social de Derecho): Limitations on the Fundamental Right to Indigenous Ethnoeducation','<p>The 1991 Political Constitution of Colombia established the Social State Governed by the Rule of Law (Estado Social de Derecho) as a new model of political, legal, and administrative organization, introducing into the Colombian legal system the right of Indigenous peoples to ethnoeducation and to own education (educación propia). Objective: To analyze how the Constitution, its implementing legal provisions, and the jurisprudence of the Constitutional Court have contributed to advancing the rights of Indigenous peoples through ethnoeducation. Methodology: The study employed a methodological design grounded in ethnographic research with the Murui-Muina people, adopting a qualitative socio-legal and educational approach, complemented by Participatory Action Research (PAR). Results: The findings highlight how the Social State Governed by the Rule of Law, as constitutionally established in 1991, has enabled Indigenous peoples in Colombia to receive an education that respects their language and worldview. Nevertheless, considerable challenges remain before the full realization of their ancestral rights can be achieved. Conclusions: The ethnographic dialogues and fieldwork revealed that the legal framework governing ethnoeducation does not fully guarantee the autonomy of Indigenous communities, as their ethnoepistemology remains subordinated to a standardized Western educational model. This situation, together with legislative provisions enacted by the Congress of the Republic of Colombia that restrict the recognition of Indigenous cultural diversity, contributes to the risk of disappearance of Indigenous peoples'' worldview, native languages, ethnophilosophy, and ancestral territories.</p>','["Social State Governed by the Rule of Law", "Education", "Ethnoeducation", "Ethnic Diversity", "Indigenous Peoples", "Worldview."]',NULL,21,1,'1443 - 1475','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1280','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',74);
<p>Aim: The study aimed to identify the relationship between nurse managers'' leadership skills and staff retention among registered nurses working in a tertiary healthcare hospital in Saudi Arabia.</p>
<p>Methods: A quantitative cross-sectional correlational study was conducted among 506 registered nurses recruited through convenience sampling. Data were obtained using a self-administered questionnaire that included demographic characteristics, the 12-item Nurse Managerial Leadership Scale, and the 6-item Nurse Retention Instrument. Data were analyzed using IBM SPSS Statistics version 29. Descriptive statistics summarized participant characteristics and study variables, while Pearson''s correlation and simple linear regression were used to examine the association between nurse managers'' leadership skills and staff retention. Statistical significance was set at p &lt; 0.05.</p>
<p>Results: Participants perceived nurse managers'' leadership skills (M = 2.899, SD = 1.076) and staff retention (M = 2.873, SD = 1.111) at moderate levels. Nurse managers'' leadership skills were positively and statistically significantly related to staff retention (r = 0.648, p &lt; 0.001). Linear regression analysis demonstrated that leadership skills significantly predicted staff retention (β = 0.648, B = 0.661, SE = 0.039, t = 16.949, p &lt; 0.001), explaining 42.0% of the variance in retention (R² = 0.420).</p>
<p>Conclusion: The findings indicate that stronger nurse managerial leadership is associated with greater staff retention among registered nurses. Enhancing leadership competencies among nurse managers may improve nurse retention, strengthen workforce stability, and support the long-term sustainability of tertiary care healthcare institutions.</p>','["nurse managerial leadership", "leadership skills", "staff retention", "nurse retention", "registered nurses", "Saudi Arabia."]',NULL,21,1,'1863 - 1875','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1332','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',97),
(1333,1333,1,'Impact of Staff Patient Ratio and Perceived Workload on Quality of Care Among Acute Care Nurses: A Cross-Sectional Study at a Tertiary Hospital in Saudi Arabia','<p>Background: Ensuring high-quality nursing care requires effective workforce management, particularly in acute care environments where nurses provide continuous monitoring and manage patients with complex healthcare needs. Staffing conditions and workload demands may influence nurses’ ability to deliver safe, efficient, and patient-centered care. However, limited evidence is available regarding the combined influence of nursing staff-to-patient ratios and perceived workload on nursing care quality in tertiary healthcare settings in Saudi Arabia.</p>
<p>Aim: This study aimed to investigate the relationship between nursing staff-to-patient ratios, perceived workload, and quality of nursing care among acute care nurses in a tertiary healthcare hospital in Saudi Arabia.</p>
<p>Methods: A quantitative cross-sectional study was conducted among 512 registered nurses at King Fahad Medical City, Riyadh, Saudi Arabia. Participants were recruited through proportionate stratified random sampling from different acute care units. Data were collected using structured questionnaires assessing nurse staffing ratio, perceived workload, and quality of nursing care. Statistical analysis was performed using IBM SPSS Statistics version 29.0. Descriptive statistics, Pearson’s correlation analysis, and multiple linear regression analysis were applied, with statistical significance established at p &lt; 0.05.</p>
<p>Results: The majority of participants were female (78.7%), aged 26–50 years (74.6%), and held a bachelor’s degree in nursing (92.6%). Participants reported moderate levels of nursing staff-to-patient ratio (M = 2.95, SD = 1.160), perceived workload (M = 2.91, SD = 1.074), and quality of nursing care (M = 2.90, SD = 1.112). Multiple linear regression analysis showed that nursing staff-to-patient ratio and perceived workload significantly predicted quality of nursing care, explaining 42.0% of the variance in care quality (R² = 0.420; Adjusted R² = 0.418; F(2,509) = 183.91, p &lt; 0.001). Perceived workload demonstrated the strongest predictive effect (β = 0.547, p &lt; 0.001), followed by nursing staff-to-patient ratio (β = 0.338, p &lt; 0.001).</p>
<p>Conclusion: The findings demonstrate that workforce-related factors, particularly workload demands and staffing conditions, play an important role in influencing nursing care quality. While maintaining appropriate staffing levels is essential, healthcare organizations should also implement strategies to monitor workload distribution, strengthen managerial support, and optimize nursing resources. These approaches may contribute to improved patient safety, enhanced care outcomes, and sustainable nursing workforce development.</p>','["Nurse staffing", "staff-to-patient ratio", "perceived workload", "quality of nursing care", "acute care nurses", "patient safety", "workforce management."]',NULL,21,1,'1876 - 1892','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1333','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',98),
(1334,1334,1,'Role of Nurses'' Engagement in Organizational Effectiveness: A Cross-Sectional Study Among Nurses in Jazan Hospitals, Saudi Arabia','<p>Background: Healthcare organizations increasingly recognize that an engaged nursing workforce is fundamental to achieving high-quality patient care and sustainable organizational performance. Nurses'' engagement has been associated with improved productivity, workforce retention, and patient outcomes; however, evidence regarding its contribution to organizational effectiveness within Saudi Arabian hospitals remains limited.</p>
<p>Aim: This study aimed to assess the level of nurses'' engagement and organizational effectiveness, examine the relationship between these variables, determine whether nurses'' engagement predicts organizational effectiveness, and explore their associations with selected demographic and professional characteristics among registered nurses working in public hospitals in Jazan, Saudi Arabia.</p>
<p>Methods: A quantitative cross-sectional correlational study was conducted between January and March 2026 among 458 registered nurses employed at three Ministry of Health hospitals in the Jazan region. Participants were selected using proportionate stratified random sampling. Data were collected using standardized questionnaires measuring nurses'' engagement and organizational effectiveness. Descriptive statistics were used to summarize participants'' characteristics and study variables. Pearson''s correlation analysis examined the association between nurses'' engagement and organizational effectiveness, while multiple linear regression identified predictors of organizational effectiveness after controlling for demographic and professional variables. Statistical analyses were performed using IBM SPSS Statistics version 29, with statistical significance set at p &lt; 0.05.</p>
<p>Results: Nurses reported a high level of work engagement (Mean = 3.70, SD = 0.623) and perceived organizational effectiveness (Mean = 3.50, SD = 0.465). Correlation analysis demonstrated a statistically significant positive association between nurses'' engagement and organizational effectiveness (r = 0.684, p &lt; 0.001). Multiple linear regression analysis showed that the overall model was statistically significant (F(7,450) = 62.81, p &lt; 0.001), explaining 49.4% of the variance in organizational effectiveness (Adjusted R² = 0.486). Nurses'' engagement emerged as the strongest independent predictor of organizational effectiveness (β = 0.674, p &lt; 0.001), while hospital affiliation also showed a significant positive association (p = 0.010). Gender, age, educational level, years of experience, and current clinical unit were not significant predictors.</p>
<p>Conclusion: The findings indicate that nurses'' engagement is a major contributor to organizational effectiveness in public hospitals. Strengthening work engagement through supportive leadership, professional development, recognition, effective communication, and participatory management may enhance organizational performance, workforce sustainability, and the quality of patient care. Healthcare administrators should prioritize engagement-focused strategies as part of organizational improvement initiatives and workforce planning.</p>','["Nurses'' engagement", "Work engagement", "Organizational effectiveness", "Registered nurses", "Hospitals", "Saudi Arabia", "Nursing management."]',NULL,21,1,'1893 - 1908','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1334','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',99);
<p> </p>
<p>Abstract<br/>Active learning methodologies are now firmly established as an effective way to improve learning in higher education, yet the evidence shows that their effects on metacognition and, in <br/>particular, on the transfer of learning are heterogeneous and seldom spontaneous. This article proposes a conceptual model, named CAMT after its four Spanish-language components (contexto, andamiaje, metacognición, transferencia, that is, context, scaffolding, metacognition and transfer), that integrates four research streams usually treated in isolation: active methodologies as the enabling context, metacognitive scaffolding as the mechanism, metacognition and self-regulation as the process, and transfer of learning as the outcome. Following a theory synthesis design, the model reconciles these literatures around a central thesis: transfer is produced neither by the active context nor by scaffolding on their own, but by the contingent fading of metacognitive scaffolding, that is, the gradual handover of regulatory responsibility to the learner, provided that regulation is coupled to concrete strategies and practised across varied contexts. The model yields eight testable propositions, including an inverted U relationship between scaffolding persistence and transfer and a reinforcing loop between achieved transfer and metacognition. The work offers a framework that links the <br/>practice of active methodologies to the cognitive mechanisms that sustain durable learning, together with implications for instructional design and a research agenda.</p>','["metacognitive scaffolding", "active learning methodologies", "metacognition", "self-regulated learning", "transfer of learning", "higher education."]',NULL,20,1,'558 - 574','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1043','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',30),
(1052,1052,1,'A Different Perspective on the Assessment of the Impact of Religiosity on Economic Development in Modern Conditions','<p>This study explores the impact of religiosity on global economic development, highlighting how religious restrictions can impede progress. Using data from 145 countries, categorised into Christian, Islamic, and Other Religious groups (including Buddhism, Hinduism, Shintoism, and Judaism), we conducted extensive analyses employing Python and EViews software. The study applied non-linear regression and correlation analysis to examine the relationships between religiosity levels, GDP per capita, freedom index, IQ index, corruption index, and average life expectancy.<br/>Key findings indicate that higher levels of religiosity are inversely correlated with positive economic indicators and directly correlated with negative ones. The detrimental effect of increased religiosity on economic development is mediated by intermediate factors such as the freedom index, IQ index, and education level. Furthermore, economic development is closely tied to the freedom index, with countries under "Other Religions" showing the highest freedom and IQ scores alongside the lowest religiosity levels. Christian-majority countries rank second, while Islamic-majority countries rank last.<br/>The model predicts that a reduction in religiosity levels corresponds with increases in the freedom index, anti-corruption index, and IQ index, underscoring the potential benefits of secularisation for economic development.</p>','["Religiosity, economic development, correlation, freedom, democracy."]',NULL,20,1,'575 - 601','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1052','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',31),
(1053,1053,1,'Gamification and Environmental Education: Impact on Solid Waste Management in Basic Education','<p>Solid waste management represents a critical socio-environmental challenge that requires the implementation of innovative pedagogical strategies from the early stages of schooling. This study analyzed the impact of a gamified didactic intervention on the ecological behavior of fifth-grade students in an Ecuadorian educational institution. The research followed a quantitative approach and a quasi-experimental design with repeated measures, utilizing a structured questionnaire that assessed cognitive, attitudinal, and procedural dimensions. The intervention integrated game mechanics and dynamics, such as challenges, immediate feedback, and reward systems, aimed at transforming waste management into a habitual, daily, and meaningful practice. Results revealed statistically significant differences when comparing ecological performance levels between the initial diagnosis, traditional instruction, and the gamified intervention phase. These findings confirm that gamification acts as an effective catalyst for cognitive and behavioral transfer, significantly outperforming traditional expository models by promoting a superior internalization of sustainable habits. It is concluded that the implementation of such gamified environments as cross-cutting themes in school environmental projects is essential to strengthen an early ecological culture, effectively closing the gap between theoretical knowledge and pro-environmental behavior. This research provides empirical evidence regarding the efficacy of techno-pedagogical tools for fostering citizenship education in sustainability within the scope of Basic Education.</p>','["Gamification", "environmental education", "waste management", "pro-environmental behavior", "educational innovation."]',NULL,20,1,'602 - 617','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1053','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',32),
(1054,1054,1,'Generative Artificial Intelligence in Education: Bridging the Gap between Opportunity and Teacher Readiness via the Extended UTAUT Framework','<p>The world of education is undergoing a transformative paradigm shift, as generative artificial intelligence is being integrated into the learning environment. The rapid development in Generative AI recently have revolutionized the classroom and teaching methodologies. In that regard, this study focuses on how teachers perceive generative AI under the formulation of an Extended UTAUT (Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology) model, the advantages and challenges related with its application. A descriptive method was adopted for this study and teachers'' perceptions in regard to Performance Expectancy (pedagogical effectiveness) and Effort Expectancy (AI literacy) was explored. Data were obtained from 146 teachers through use of survey methodology. Descriptive and inferential statistics were used in the analysis of the collected data. The result has a crucial difference, because the perceived pedagogical utility and the technical expertise may amend the teacher''s confidence for using the system. However, lack of indispensable facilitating conditions (deficient training, unclear governance etc) and ethical concerns may hinder implementation. <br/>These findings emphasize the urgent demand for professional development and clear institutional policy, announced by the UNESCO (2023) and Floridi et al. (2018) normative anchors. By isolating the crucial components of fairness, accountability, and agency which underpin the factors most predictive of teacher readiness, this study lays a framework for leaders and policy makers searching to translate teacher doubt into enthusiastic and responsible carrying out of AI tools. Clearly defining governing rules, strengthening facilitating conditions, will help address the fears that shortly impede the adoption of generative AI in schools.</p>','["Generative AI", "artificial intelligence in education", "educator perceptions", "teacher attitudes", "AI literacy", "pedagogical integration", "technology acceptance", "UTAUT", "ethical considerations", "professional development", "mixed-methods research."]',NULL,20,1,'618 - 636','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1054','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',33),
(1055,1055,1,'Advancing Sustainable Food Systems Through Urban Agriculture: Enhancing Food Security in Indonesia’s METR OPOLITAN Areas','<p>Urban agriculture is emerging as a pivotal strategy in addressing global food security challenges in line with the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs), particularly SDG 2 (Zero Hunger) and SDG 11 (Sustainable Cities and Communities). This study examines key determinants influencing the success of urban farming in enhancing local food availability in Indonesia’s metropolitan areas. Using direct surveys, interviews, and statistical analyses via SEM-WarpPLS, the research reveals that urban agriculture significantly improves the accessibility of nutritious, affordable food while promoting socio-economic benefits. The findings stress the importance of integrating urban farming into national food policies and urban development strategies to foster food security and community well-being. Variables such as age, education, innovation in human resources, agricultural technology, and community empowerment are central to the sustainable implementation of urban farming. This paper advocates for enhanced education programs, institutional support, and better access to modern agricultural technologies to ensure urban agriculture''s long-term success as a viable solution for sustainable urban food systems.</p>','["Food Availability, Urban Agriculture, Sustainable Development, Urban Areas, Food Security, SDGs"]',NULL,20,1,'637 - 657','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1055','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',34),
(1056,1056,1,'Sustainable Human Resource Practices and Their Impact on SDG 8: Decent Work and Economic Growth','<p>“Sustainable human resources management”, or SHRM, is regarded as a novel and successful strategy that emphasises people management, long-term HRM, rejuvenation, and regeneration. The researcher used both the inductive method and the positivist mindset in this investigation. The researcher can gather factual information about incorporating sustainability principles into firm HR operations thanks to the positivist attitude. The Google Form was used as the data-gathering tool for this article, and the researcher produced ten questions based on business HR procedures and practices linked to economic growth and decision-making (SDG 8). The assessment of the primary numerical findings has confirmed the greater and more progressive linkage between sustainable activities of HR and the facility of decent conditions of working as the coefficient for correlation is 0.509. On the other hand, the impression of sustainable activities of HR on the facility of decent conditions of working is vital as the regressive coefficient is not greater than 5 %. It is identified that integrating sustainability goals within the HR policies positively influences organisational performance in terms of practising inclusivity, diversity, flexible working hours, remote working, and many more. As a result, it has enhanced the overall productivity of the organisation, which ensures to achievement of economic performance.</p>','["Economic Growth, Decision-Making, SDG 8, Decent working states, Sustainability HR practices, Employee well-being, Diversity and Inclusion, Green HR, Fair labour practices."]',NULL,20,1,'658 - 676','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1056','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',35),
(1057,1057,1,'Towards the Professionalization of Military Teachers: Strengthening Military Education Through Educational Practices, Formative Research and The Use of Technology','<p>This study examines the need to professionalize military teachers in the Colombian Army through the creation of an undergraduate program in Military Pedagogy. Military instructors, including officers, non-commissioned officers, and civilian personnel, currently perform teaching functions without a consolidated career structure or sufficient preparation in educational sciences. Existing programs, such as the Instructor Preparation Course and the Military Teacher Course, provide short-term training, but remain limited in pedagogy, curriculum, assessment, formative research, and responsible educational technology. Using a grounded theory approach, the study analyzed surveys, interviews, current institutional training courses, and international experiences in military education. The findings reveal limited knowledge of educational principles, reliance on traditional teaching approaches, lack of bachelor-level teacher preparation, and absence of clear professional standards, certification, and career pathways. The study also highlights the distinctive nature of military education, where structured environments, operational objectives, discipline, leadership, and varied learner backgrounds require specialized pedagogical preparation. In response, the article proposes a bachelor’s degree in Military Pedagogy integrating pedagogy, didactics, curriculum, assessment, formative research, military sciences, ethics, leadership, communication skills, and artificial intelligence as a complementary pedagogical competence. AI is included to support lesson planning, controlled training scenarios, formative feedback, assessment, and differentiated instruction, while preserving instructor judgment, doctrinal accuracy, ethical responsibility, and information security. The study concludes that undergraduate training is necessary and feasible for strengthening military education.</p>','["Military Pedagogy, Teacher Professionalization, Technology in Education, Curriculum Development, Formative Research, Military Teacher Training"]',NULL,20,1,'677 - 698','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1057','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',36),
(1058,1058,1,'Sustainable Risk Governance and Insurance Market Resilience in Saudi Arabia under Vision 2030: A Review of ESG, Regulation, and Reinsurance Capacity','<p>In the Vision 2030 national transformation agenda, the role of sustainable risk governance is becoming more critical to enhance the resilience of the insurance market in Saudi Arabia. Economy diversification, giga projects, infrastructure development, tourism development, health care development, investments in renewable energies and digital transformation have brought new and complex risk exposures which demand a more robust and future-oriented insurance ecosystem. This article takes a narrative review approach to integrate conceptual, regulatory, policy and market-based views on the relationship between integration of environmental, social and governance (ESG) principles, regulatory modernization, reinsurance capacity and insurance-sector resilience. The focus is on the impact environmental, social and governance principles have on sustainable underwriting, responsible investment, awareness of climate-risk, consumer protection, ethical governance, transparency and long-term institutional trust. A key stabilizing factor is explored through the prism of regulatory transformation, in particular in the areas of solvency supervision, capital adequacy, market conduct, claims governance, digital insurance supervision and policyholder protection. Reinsurance capacity is also considered to be an important tool for capital relief, protection from cat losses, mega project risk transfer, claims paying capacity and systemic shock absorption. The results of the narrative synthesis indicate that ESG, regulation and reinsurance are not three distinct insurance development areas, but are instead interconnected. Overall, the review provides a strategic overview of the insurance industry and its role as a risk infrastructure in Saudi Arabia''s sustainable economic transformation and financial stability, which will help achieve the Vision 2030 goals.</p>','["Saudi Arabia, Vision 2030, sustainable risk governance, insurance market resilience, reinsurance capacity."]',NULL,20,1,'699 - 715','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1058','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',37),
(1059,1059,1,'A Holistic Approach for Designing Sustainable Supply Chains: A Conceptual Framework','<p>The increasing complexity of business process management arises from globalization, digital transformation, and economic disruptions such as pandemics and sanctions. Addressing these challenges requires a neutrosophic-driven, holistic approach that integrates uncertainty modeling, multi-criteria decision-making (MCDM), and sustainability constraints into supply chain design. This study proposes a multidimensional, systematic framework that incorporates neutrosophic logic and fuzzy decision-making methods to enhance the resilience and adaptability of modern supply chains. Organizations must re-engineer supply chain structures to optimize strategic objectives, such as cost efficiency and customer retention, while ensuring environmental and financial sustainability. The research introduces an adjusted neutrosophic tabular framework, supported by advanced analytical models and uncertainty-aware logistics methods, to enhance supply chain optimization. Key findings include a generalized uncertainty-driven algorithm for structuring supply chains, supplemented by real-world simulation-based validation. While the study acknowledges limitations such as data generalization, its reliance on aggregated enterprise data covering an entire manufacturing cycle enhances applicability. This research provides a decision-support tool for managers and policymakers, enabling them to navigate uncertainty, optimize investments, and implement sustainable business practices. By incorporating neutrosophic reasoning and ecological strategies, businesses can improve sustainability performance while maintaining operational efficiency. The proposed framework offers a scalable, intelligent model for supply chain design, serving as a strategic instrument for economic policy formulation in logistics and business resilience planning.</p>','["Neutrosophic Logic, Supply Chain Resilience, Uncertainty Modeling, Sustainable Development, Fuzzy Decision-Making, Conceptual Framework"]',NULL,20,1,'716 - 738','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1059','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',38),
(1060,1060,1,'Logistical, Financial, and Service N-D Structures of Networks on the Basis of the Concept of Metaverse','<p>This study investigates the physical and informational interaction processes driven by data flows within complexly structured domains typical of modern commercial and economic systems. Material and information flows are conceptualized as a network-like, layered topology that reflects a continuum of physical and virtual objects across three primary dimensions: a physical layer (commodity and transportation flows, warehousing, and supply chains), an information layer (cargo movement data, logistics, and documentation), and a financial layer (cash flows, investments, credit commitments, costs, and revenues). The authors demonstrate that highly complex overlay objects including interacting commercial networks, logistics operators, digital platforms, and supplier-consumer relations—form multidimensional, layered topological structures that interact dynamically across adjacency boundaries. The resulting model serves as a universal analytical framework for evaluating logistics, finance, and information flows within complex resource-interaction hypernetworks. By integrating this model into software architectures, the developed mathematical methods can be deployed in real-world commercial applications, optimizing operational efficiency and adaptability within the modern digital economy.</p>','["Multidimensional Network Structures, Hypernetworks of Resource Interaction, Continuum of Physical and Virtual Objects."]',NULL,20,1,'739 - 750','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1060','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',39),
(1061,1061,1,'Reconstructing Tax Consciousness through Transcendental Phenomenology: Exploring the Meaning of Tax and Accountability among Indonesian Academics','<p>This study aims to understand the meaning of taxes from the perspective of taxpayers. It is important to examine the concept of taxes from the perspective of taxpayers in order to gain an understanding of taxes from taxpayers. The results of this study are expected to increase public literacy regarding the meaning of taxes for social and state life. This study uses an interpretive paradigm with Husserl''s transcendental phenomenology as an analytical tool. The objects of this study are academics who are all taxpayers, with four people selected as research informants. Meanwhile, in the data collection process, the researcher used interviews, observation, and documentation. First, taxation can be examined from a narrow perspective, namely a perspective based on individualistic or limited collectivist interests. Second, taxation can also be examined using a more comprehensive, long-term, and broad-minded perspective. These two perspectives have different implications for attitudes and behaviors related to taxation.</p>','["Tax, Taxpayer. Husserl''s Transcendental Phenomenology"]',NULL,20,1,'751 - 762','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1061','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',40),
(1062,1062,1,'The Financial Inclusion - Literacy Gap in Indonesia: A Segment-Level Diagnostic from the 2025 National Survey (SNLIK)','<p>Indonesia has increased access to financial products at a rapid pace. However, there is still some question about whether households understand their use of such products which can have both direct consumer protection and financial stability implications. This paper utilizes the 2025 National Survey of Financial Literacy and Inclusion (SNLIK) that was conducted by the Financial Services Authority (OJK), with the assistance of Statistics Indonesia (BPS). Using this data we will construct a segment-level analysis of the financial inclusion/literacy gap (the number of percentage points the composite inclusion index is greater than the composite literacy index) across 26 segements defined by socio-demographic characteristics such as domicile, gender, age range, level of education, gross income, and occupation. The results show an overall gap of 14.05 percentage points at the national level (80.51% inclusion rate versus 66.46% literacy rate). The gap is not homogenous (ranging between 8.47 and 25.89 percentage points among the segments analyzed), and it is significantly negatively correlated with the level of literacy (Pearson r = -0.45, p = 0.022; OLS slope = -0.14, 95% CI [-0.26, -0.02]; R2 = 0.20): thus, those households with lower levels of financial literacy tend to be found in those households with the largest gaps. The lowest income (&lt; Rp500k: 22.50 pp), the youngest (15-17 years old: 22.32 pp), and the non-employed (15.46 pp) segments all have below median levels of literacy and above median gaps indicating they would be good candidates for financial education. The national literacy shortfall is 2.89 percentage points below the targeted rate of 69.35 percent in the RPJMN, and there is a gap of 12.49 percentage points with respect to the target rate of 93.0 percent for inclusion. Based on our findings, we assert that considering the gap between inclusion and literacy rather than the two indexes separately provides a more relevant framework for developing common financial capability interventions. The study is purely descriptive and uses published aggregate data; therefore, we present the limitations associated with inferential statistics in this regard.</p>','["financial literacy", "financial inclusion", "inclusion\\u2013literacy gap", "consumer protection", "Indonesia", "SNLIK JEL classification: G53", "G51", "D14", "O16"]',NULL,20,1,'763 - 773','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1062','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',41),
(1063,1063,1,'Digital Citizenship Education as a Preventive Framework for Cyberbullying among Students: A Critical Literature Review','<p>Cyberbullying has become a persistent educational and social problem as students increasingly participate in networked digital environments. Although many studies have examined cyberbullying as a psychological, behavioral, or technological risk, fewer studies synthesize how digital citizenship education can function as a preventive framework grounded in ethical communication, rights and responsibilities, digital law, privacy, empathy, and civic participation. This article critically reviews recent literature published between 2020 and 2026 to reconstruct digital citizenship education as a preventive framework for cyberbullying among students. A PRISMA-guided critical literature review was conducted by identifying, screening, and synthesizing peer-reviewed studies on cyberbullying, digital citizenship education, online safety education, civic education, digital literacy, and student wellbeing. From 386 records initially identified, 40 articles were included in the final synthesis. The findings indicate that cyberbullying prevention cannot rely solely on monitoring, sanctions, or reactive counseling. Instead, prevention requires the systematic cultivation of digital civic competencies: ethical awareness, responsible online communication, recognition of digital rights and responsibilities, privacy and security literacy, empathy-based bystander intervention, and critical participation in digital communities. The review contributes a conceptual framework that positions digital citizenship education as a multi-level preventive approach linking student competence, classroom pedagogy, school policy, family engagement, and democratic digital culture. The study recommends integrating digital citizenship education into civic education and broader school curricula through case-based learning, reflective dialogue, peer-led campaigns, reporting literacy, and assessment of digital behavior. Future empirical research is needed to test the effectiveness of this framework across educational levels and sociocultural contexts.</p>','["cyberbullying prevention", "digital citizenship education", "digital culture, civic education", "technological risk."]',NULL,20,1,'774 - 796','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1063','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',42),
(1064,1064,1,'Measuring Financial Management Climate from Government Audit Text: A Lexicon-Based Institutional Index and its Application to Fiscal Transfer Effectiveness in Philippine Local Governments','<p>Institutional-conditionality arguments in fiscal federalism hold that the developmental returns to intergovernmental transfers depend on the recipient organization''s administrative environment, yet testing such claims has been constrained by the absence of a financial-management measure resolved at the local-government-year level. This study''s primary contribution is methodological: we construct a continuous Financial Management Climate (FMC) index for 1,114 Philippine cities and municipalities from the textual content of Commission on Audit Annual Audit Report Executive Summaries, using lexicon-based sentiment analysis with a domain-specific severity dictionary. The index is validated against distributional, temporal-stability, and discriminant-validity criteria, exhibiting near-normal distribution, substantial within-unit variation suitable for fixed-effects identification (57.7% of total variance), and empirical distinctness from broader governance and fiscal measures. As a demonstration application, we deploy the index in an unbalanced panel of 8,227 local-government-year observations (fiscal years 2014–2024, excluding 2018), estimating the transfer–efficiency relationship and its conditioning by FMC through a graduated hierarchy of pooled, random-effects, and two-way fixed-effects specifications with cluster-robust standard errors, across contemporaneous, lagged, and per-capita transfer formulations. The application yields three substantive results: transfers relate negatively to within-unit economic efficiency; FMC carries a modest positive direct association but does not robustly moderate transfer effectiveness once unit and year heterogeneity are absorbed; and a broader public-administration-quality measure is the most stable within-unit predictor. The findings position audit-derived text as a tractable institutional-measurement instrument for governance analytics and indicate that resource expansion in the post-Mandanas setting is conditional on general institutional capacity.</p>','["Financial management climate", "sentiment analysis", "text-as-data", "institutional measurement", "fiscal decentralization", "intergovernmental transfers", "panel data", "fixed effects", "local government units", "Philippines"]',NULL,20,1,'797 - 827','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1064','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',43);
<p><strong>Objectives</strong>: This systematic review tried to analyze which psychological factors are related to dental fear in children and also to look at how children behave when pediatric dental anxiety is present.</p>
<p><strong>Methods</strong>: We did a systematic review using PRISMA- oriented type guidance. Relevant papers published from 2005 to 2025 were tracked in PubMed, Scopus, PsycINFO, Web of Science, and Google Scholar. Only studies about psychological determinants and the actual behavioral signs of dental fear among children were kept in. Studies that were not about pediatric populations, or that didn’t include a psychological analysis were left out.</p>
<p><strong>Results</strong>: Twenty studies met the eligibility criteria and were included in the qualitative synthesis. The results suggested. That dental fear in children is strongly connected to traumatic dental experiences, parental anxiety, cognitive distortions, behavioral conditioning and observational learning. In terms of what children do, common reactions included crying, avoidance, aggression, refusing treatment, and physiological stress reactions. The behavioral and cognitive frameworks seemed to give the best explanations for how dental anxiety starts and continues. There was also evidence that cognitive behavioral therapy, behavioral desensitization, positive reinforcement, and child-centered communication strategies can help lower dental fear and improve cooperation in treatment.</p>
<p><strong>Conclusion</strong>: Dental fear among children  a multifactorial psychological condition, shaped by emotional, cognitive, family related, and behavioral components. Putting both psychological assessment and practical behavioral management methods into pediatric dental care is important, for better treatment results and to reduce anxiety-based avoidance behaviors.</p>','["Clinical Decision-Making, Evidence-Based Dentistry, Pediatric Dental Anxiety, Psychological Factors, Behavioral Responses, Systematic Review"]',NULL,16,1,'1104 - 1122','10.59543/jidmis.v3.488','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',56),
(489,489,1,'Pancreatic Tumor Segmentation using Conditional Diffusion and Graph Regularization on Abdominal Computed Tomography Images','<p>The segmentation of pancreas and tumor regions is difficult in abdominal Computed Tomography (CT) images due to low tissue contrast, and anatomical variability and irregular boundaries of the organs. The traditional encoder–decoder segmentation methods are based on a deterministic prediction of each pixel, and thus cannot model uncertainty and maintain anatomical consistency. In this article, the problem of pancreas tumor segmentation is proposed as a conditional probabilistic inference and graph-regularized optimization problem. The proposed framework couples multi-scale representation learning, conditional latent diffusion modeling and geometric regularization via graphs in a single segmentation framework. A dual-stream encoded model is proposed, which includes a residual convolutional branch and a transformer branch, to extract local features and global contextual dependencies, respectively. The extracted representations are then merged together into a shared latent embedding space, in order to keep complementary anatomical information. A conditional latent diffusion module is trained by sequentially denoising latent variables to model the distribution of segmentation masks, and is used to infer from uncertainties in low-contrast regions. We propose a graph convolutional framework to refine the probabilistic segmentation mask based on image gradients and geometric priors, for better anatomical consistency. Further, smoothness and continuity of boundaries is preserved by graph Laplacian regularization. Experimental results on the publicly available datasets Kaggle and Pancreas-CT-Seg show excellent results with Dice scores of 0.781 and 0.778 respectively, higher than the existing state of the art segmentation methods.</p>','["Geometric Deep Learning, Pancreas Tumor Segmentation, Conditional Latent Diffusion, Graph Laplacian Regularization, Probabilistic Segmentation, Variational Optimization"]',NULL,16,1,'1123 - 1143','10.59543/jidmis.v3.489','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',57),
(491,491,1,'How Do Dividend Paths Affect Firm Value? — Evidence from Dividend Volatility Discount and Short- to Medium-term Pricing of Dividend Continuity','<p>Against the backdrop of increasingly normalized cash dividends, research on corporate dividend behavior should not only focus on "whether to pay dividends" or "how much to pay", but should further examine the capital market implications of the dividend path itself. This paper uses non-financial A-share listed companies in Shanghai, Shenzhen, and Beijing from 2005 to 2024 as the sample, constructs stable dividend and volatile dividend variables based on a three-year window, and employs firm fixed effects and year fixed effects models to systematically examine the immediate and lagged effects of the dividend path on firm value. The results show that dividend volatility has a significant negative impact on firm value in the current period, and this negative effect persists into subsequent periods; stable dividends do not generate a significant premium in the current period, but their positive value effect becomes significant after a one-period lag, and is significantly stronger than that of volatile dividends. Furthermore, the number of consecutive years of dividend payment has a significant positive effect on firm value in both the current and following periods, but this effect is no longer significant over a longer horizon. The above results suggest that the capital market''s pricing of dividend paths has obvious asymmetry and maturity differences: the market immediately penalizes dividend volatility, while the positive evaluation of stable dividends and dividend continuity is mainly realized gradually through time accumulation. This paper demonstrates that the dividend path itself is an important dimension of value information independent of the dividend amount, providing new evidence for understanding the pricing mechanism of dividend policies in emerging markets.</p>','["Cash Dividend", "Dividend Stability", "Dividend Volatility", "Dividend Continuity", "Firm Value"]',NULL,16,1,'1144 - 1161','10.59543/jidmis.v3.491','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-25',58),
(492,492,1,'Real-Time Automatic Video Summarization Through Multimodal Data Fusion and Transformer Architectures','<p>In recent years, the amount of video data on digital platforms has grown exponentially and automated real-time video summarization systems that can produce shorter and semantically coherent abstractions from long videos are in demand. The current methods mainly rely on visual frames and exhibit high latency, lack of context and poor salient event coverage. In this paper, we present a novel real-time video summarisation framework, called MFF-Trans, which combines Multimodal Feature Fusion (MFF) and a Transformer-based deep learning backbone. Proposed system involves spatial visual features derived using a Swin Transformer, temporal motion features derived using optical-flow guided convnets, acoustic features derived using the pre-trained VGGish audio model, and textual semantic features derived using BERT on automatic speech transcriptions. These feature streams are heterogeneous, which are unified by a cross-modal attention module and then passed to a hierarchical temporal Transformer, modeling the intra-segment dependencies and inter-segment narrative coherences. Then, an adaptive importance score mechanism is used to pick most relevant video segments in real-time. MFF-Trans outperforms state-of-the-art baselines consistently on all the experiments performed on different datasets, including TVSum50, SumMe, OVP and a subset of YouTube-8M, achieving the highest F1-Score of 0.634 and the highest ROUGE-L of 57.6% on TVSum50 and keeping the inference latency less than 100ms per frame. It has been showed in the ablation study that all the modalities are complementary and the fusion design is important. The presented results show MFF-Trans to be a solid and feasible approach to scalable real-time video summarization.</p>','["Video summarization, Multimodal feature fusion, Transformer, Real-time processing, Cross-modal attention, deep learning, Swin Transformer, BERT"]',NULL,16,1,'1162 - 1176','10.59543/jidmis.v3.492','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-25',59),
(497,497,1,'The Impact of Visual Appeal on Digital Hoarding Behavior and Online Consumer Decision-Making in E-commerce: A Structural Equation Modeling Approach','<p><strong>Background</strong>: E-commerce spaces depend on visual content because it helps businesses shape how customers shop. The research lacks sufficient evidence to show how digital visual stimuli create digital hoarding behaviors about their role in improving user engagement and their decision-making abilities.</p>
<p><strong>Objectives</strong>: The study investigated how digital visual stimuli affect consumer digital hoarding behavior in online shopping spaces while studying how different demographic groups exhibit digital hoarding through its multiple dimensional aspects.</p>
<p><strong>Methods</strong>: The study adopted a cross-sectional quantitative research design which utilized an online distribution method for its structured questionnaire. The research included a sample of (N = 250) participants who were chosen through convenience sampling methods. The researchers used descriptive statistics and Pearson correlation along with simple linear regression and multivariate analysis of variance (MANOVA) and structural equation modeling (SEM) to analyze the data.</p>
<p><strong>Results</strong>: Digital visual stimuli produce a major positive impact on digital hoarding according to the findings because their effect size reaches 0.50 and the p value remains below 0.001. The visual stimuli showed a positive correlation with all aspects of digital hoarding but the researchers found excessive acquisition to have the strongest relationship because it registered a correlation coefficient of 0.47. The SEM analysis demonstrated a strong direct relationship between variables because the direct effect size reached 0.52 and the p value remained below 0.001 while the model achieved good fit with CFI value of 0.95 and TLI value of 0.93 and RMSEA value of 0.05. The study identified statistically significant differences across gender, educational level, and income groups.</p>
<p><strong>Conclusion</strong>: The research shows that digital visual cues function as essential elements which lead to the development of digital hoarding tendencies among people who use online platforms. The research results show that organizations need to control visual content because it helps to prevent excessive digital storage while directing users toward better consumption patterns.</p>','["Digital Visual Stimuli", "Digital Hoarding", "E-commerce", "Consumer Behavior", "Structur-al Equation Modeling", "Online Shopping."]',NULL,16,1,'1177-1194','10.59543/jidmis.v3.497','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-25',60),
(533,533,1,'Application-Centric Survey of Semantic-Aware Frameworks in IoT Networks','<p>As Internet of Things (IoT) applications become more complex and data-driven, semantic techniques are increasingly becoming critical in extracting data contexts for understanding and supporting decision-making within specific application do- mains. Previous surveys on this topic focus on specific semantic model architec- tures or target a subset of IoT applications. On the contrary, this work presents a comprehensive review of existing semantic techniques covering a wide range of IoT applications. We perform a systematic, application-centric analysis and present a structured classification that maps semantic methods to relevant IoT applications. Furthermore, we highlight the major challenges of deploying such techniques in practice, such as specific application requirements or real-world im- plementation constraints. We also discuss areas to be addressed by future work, specifically on improving the scalability, interoperability, and interpretability of semantic techniques. Our work is intended to serve as a resource for researchers, practitioners, and experts seeking to improve semantic solutions across applica- tion domains and to examine the suitability of techniques for each application.</p>','["Internet of Things", "IoT applications", "semantic communication", "semantic extraction", "semantic segmentation", "semantic ontologies"]',NULL,16,1,'1195-1235','10.59543/jidmis.v3.533','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-25',61),
(6334,6334,1,'An Integrated Fuzzy AHP and Fuzzy TOPSIS Framework for Performance Evaluation and Ranking of Educational Institutions: A Case Study','<p>Evaluating performance in the education sector is a complicated Multi-Criteria Decision-Making (MCDM) challenge marked by qualitative ambiguities and quantitative differences. Conventional crisp evaluation techniques do not adequately address the inherent vagueness, subjectivity, and uncertainty present in human cognitive assessments during peer evaluations and academic reviews. To overcome this shortcoming, the paper introduces a comprehensive decision-making framework that integrates the Fuzzy Analytic Hierarchy Process (FAHP) with the Fuzzy Technique for Order of Preference by Similarity to Ideal Solution (FTOPSIS). Triangular Fuzzy Numbers (TFNs) are utilized alongside linguistic variables to model the subjective assessments of academic experts. FAHP based on Buckley''s geometric mean method is applied to derive robust, fuzzified weights for the evaluation criteria, while FTOPSIS is employed to rank the educational alternatives based on their fuzzy distance from the fuzzy positive and negative ideal solutions. The model’s efficacy is demonstrated through a practical case study benchmarking multiple educational institutions across key dimensional criteria, including academic excellence, infrastructural capabilities, and student outcomes. Finally, a sensitivity analysis is performed to validate the stability and reliability of the framework, proving its utility as a mathematically rigorous decision-support tool for educational administrators and policy makers.</p>','["Multi-Criteria Decision-Making (MCDM)", "Fuzzy AHP", "Fuzzy TOPSIS", "Triangular Fuzzy Numbers", "Educational Evaluation", "Institutional Benchmarking."]',NULL,16,1,'1236 - 1259',NULL,'The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-05-05',62),
(182,182,1,'A Comparative Analysis of Transformer Architectures for Automated Lung Cancer Detection in CT Images','<p>The imperative for early-stage lung cancer detection is widely recognized as a critical determinant of therapeutic efficacy and patient survival. Conventional diagnostic workflows, however, are frequently constrained by their labour-intensive nature and susceptibility to interpretive inaccuracies, positioning artificial intelligence (AI) as a transformative technology in medical imaging. This research conducts a rigorous comparative analysis of four prominent vision transformer (ViT) architectures; Swin-Base, ViT-Base, DeiT-Base, and BEiT-Base evaluating their performance in the automated classification of lung cancer from computed tomography (CT) scans. The empirical validation was performed on the open-access IQ-OTH/NCCD dataset, a corpus of 1,097 images distributed across benign (n=120), malignant (n=561), and normal (n=416) classes. Model proficiency was quantified using established metrics of accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score. The findings unequivocally establish the superiority of the Swin-Base model, which, by utilizing its innovative hierarchical design and shifted-window mechanism, attained a benchmark accuracy of 98.80% and an F1-score of 97.52%. While its counterparts achieved commendable accuracies ViT-Base (95.18%), DeiT-Base (96.39%), and BEiT-Base (95.78%) they did not match the performance of Swin-Base. Notably, this leading performance was achieved with greater computational efficiency, requiring a lower GFLOPS count than competing models.</p>
<p><strong> DOI:</strong> <a href="https://doi.org/10.59543/jidmis.v3i.17135">https://doi.org/10.59543/jidmis.v3i.17135</a></p>','["Lung Cancer Classificaiton", "Computer-Aided Diagnosis (CAD", "Deep Learning, Transformer Architectures", "Medical Image Analysis"]',NULL,15,1,'528-539','10.59543/jidmis.v3i.17135','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-01-15',1),
(183,183,1,'Assessing the Accuracy and Reliability of Artificial Intelligence in First Permanent Molar Extraction Decisions','<p>The first permanent molar (M1) plays a critical role in maintaining functional occlusion during mixed dentition. When this tooth is severely compromised by deep caries, structural weakness, or developmental enamel defects, extraction may be required. However, the decision to extract M1 is influenced by multiple prognostic factors, including the child’s age, dental development stage, second molar (M2) angulation, and the presence of third molars. In this context, artificial intelligence (AI)–based conversational systems can support clinical judgment and patient education by offering quicker access to relevant information. This descriptive-analytical study evaluated the performance of two AI-powered language models (ChatGPT-3.5 and ChatGPT-4) in providing clinical guidance on M1 extraction. Ten standardized questions were formulated based on established prognostic parameters associated with spontaneous space closure. Both AI systems produced coherent and clinically relevant content. However, ChatGPT-4 tended to produce longer responses with greater sentence complexity, resulting in lower readability and higher academic demand. By comparison, ChatGPT-3.5 produced shorter, more straightforward responses that may be more appropriate for communication with parents or patients. Future research incorporating varied question structures, such as case-based scenarios, radiographic-based questions, and real-user feedback, may help define how conversational AI can be safely and effectively integrated into pediatric dental workflows. Until then, AI-based tools should be used as supportive resources rather than as independent clinical decision-makers, and their outputs should be reviewed by qualified professionals to ensure patient safety.</p>','["Artificial intelligence", "Pediatric dentistry", "First permanent molar", "Extraction decisions", "Decision support"]',NULL,15,1,'540-553','10.59543/bcx2xp86%20','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-02-28',2),
(209,209,1,'AI Pair Programming and Knowledge Sharing in Developer Communities','<p>With the increasing availability of artificial intelligence-supported pair programming tools in professional software development, past research has largely concentrated on their impact on individual developers. Much less is known about their potential impact on developer communities. Specifically, we still lack empirical insight into whether the proliferation of artificial intelligence coding assistants is associated with observable changes in community participation, collaborative communication, and externalized knowledge creation across large-scale developer platforms. In this paper, we introduce the Loneliness Framework, a socio-technical process model comprising six mechanisms that link AI pair programming to community-level behavioral change, and examine whether statistically significant longitudinal shifts in platform-trace measures of developer community behavior are temporally correlated with the adoption of artificial intelligence-supported pair programming. We employ an observational, non-causal longitudinal design and analyze two real-world data sets: a Stack Overflow data set from the Stack Exchange Data Dump and a public GitHub events data set from GH Archive, spanning January 2018 to December 2024. We analyze nine monthly behavioral metrics through trend, period-based comparison, correlation, and effect-size analyses, using false discovery rate correction for the primary hypothesis tests. We find that all nine metrics exhibit statistically significant longitudinal changes. The largest changes include decreases in question volume, pull request discussion density, and documentation-related behaviors, together with increases in median time to first answer. Many of these changes became most pronounced after late 2022, when large language model-based coding assistants became widely available. We also find positive relationships among selected documentation-related metrics, indicating coordinated shifts in externalized knowledge behaviors. Overall, the results indicate statistically detectable changes in developer community behavior during the era of artificial intelligence-supported programming. However, because this is an observational study, the findings should be interpreted in a descriptive, non-causal manner.</p>
<p><strong> DOI:</strong> <a href="https://doi.org/10.59543/gk2qp136">https://doi.org/10.59543/gk2qp136</a></p>','["AI pair programming, developer communities, Loneliness Framework, knowledge externalization, collective intelligence, software engineering, longitudinal analysis"]',NULL,15,1,'554-571','10.59543/gk2qp136','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-03-19',3),
(192,192,1,'A System Dynamics Approach for Analyzing the Behavior and Control of COVID-19 in Iran: Focus on the Sixth (Omicron) Wave','<p>To analyze how vaccines can affect the highest number of infections per day, the highest mortality rates, and ultimately public health efforts to combat COVID-19, we use a System Dynamics approach to simulate the behavior of the virus over time focusing specifically on Iran’s 6th wave of the virus which occurred at the beginning of 2022 due to the Omicron variant. We are able to better understand the way in which diseases spread through their ability to classify the population into specific groups, as well as identify how interventions may impact the spread of disease. In order to simulate the COVID-19 virus using the System Dynamics method, we developed a full mathematical model with 7 different classifications of population ("Vulnerable", "Suspected", "Infected", "Hospitalized", "Quarantined", "Recovered" and "Deceased"). Our model simulated COVID-19 under three different scenarios: Current Conditions; 40% Initial Vaccination Rate; and 30% Reduction in Vulnerability due to Restrictions. We also included Quarantine and Intervention classifications in our model to simulate the effects of these methods on transmission. The research supports that the use of both increased levels of vaccinated individuals and reduced exposure to COVID-19 are necessary to decrease the severity of a pandemic; The findings of this study provide evidence-based recommendations for policies to minimize the amount of infections, hospitalizations and deaths in populations with limited resources, such as Iran, through the achievement of high (&gt; 60%) vaccination rates in addition to the implementation of other complementary measures during an omicron-like wave of COVID-19."</p>
<p><strong> DOI:</strong> <a href="https://doi.org/10.59543/r3z0m621">https://doi.org/10.59543/r3z0m621</a></p>','["system dynamics", "COVID-19", "Omicron wave", "vaccination thresholds", "non-pharmaceutical interventions", "scenario analysis", "Iran"]',NULL,15,1,'572-598','10.59543/r3z0m621','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-04-18',4);
<p><strong> DOI:</strong> <a href="https://doi.org/10.59543/jidmis.v2i.11737">https://doi.org/10.59543/jidmis.v2i.11737</a></p>','["Complex Pythagorean fuzzy sets, similarity measures, trigonometric function, decision-making"]',NULL,14,1,'156–173','10.59543/jidmis.v2i.11737','The Authors',2025,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2025-01-03',1),
(163,163,1,'Failure Mode and Effects Analysis (FMEA) using Neutrosophic Best Worst Method (BWM) : A Case Study of Helicopter Assembly Line for Military Aviation Area','<p>One of the most popular structured approaches in risk assessment is Failure Mode and Effects Analysis (FMEA) that helps in discovering potential failures existing within the design of a product or process. Determining the risk weights of failure types correctly is a critical part of the decision-making process of the analysis. In this study, the Best Worst Method (BWM) was integrated with the Neutrosophic Fuzzy Set and applied to the FMEA problem. The aim of the study is to determine the failure types encountered in the helicopter assembly line in the military aviation field and to offer solution suggestions. A case study was conducted for AH-64E Apache type helicopters for the effectiveness of the proposed method. The results show that gap and mismatch for structural elements, molds should not be used appropriately, unnecessary materials should be brought to the assembly line and attention should be paid to cabling problems.</p>
<p><strong> DOI:</strong> h<a>ttps://doi.org/10.59543/jidmis.v2i.12605</a></p>','["FMEA, BWM, MCDM, Neutrosophic Fuzzy Set, Helicopter"]',NULL,14,1,'174–185','10.59543/jidmis.v2i.12605','The Authors',2025,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2025-01-10',2),
(164,164,1,'A multi-criteria decision-making framework for addressing youth unemployment in developing countries: Pathways to sustainable solutions','<p>Youth unemployment is among Africa’s most urgent challenges, with over 60% of its population under 25, the largest youth demographic globally. This youthful population presents a significant opportunity to drive economic growth, innovation, and development. However, the lack of sufficient employment opportunities risks perpetuating poverty, inequality, and social instability. To address this issue and unlock Africa’s potential, this study evaluates and prioritizes six strategies identified through a comprehensive literature review and expert input. Using the Stepwise Weight Assessment Ratio Analysis (SWARA) method within an interval-valued spherical fuzzy (IVSF) framework, the research facilitates collective decision-making. It highlights three key strategies as the most appropriates: reforming education systems to align skills with labor market demands, fostering growth in sectors such as agriculture, manufacturing, and tourism, and promoting entrepreneurship to empower Africa’s young innovators. The study offers actionable insights for policymakers to effectively combat youth unemployment and maximize the continent’s demographic advantage.</p>
<p><strong> DOI:</strong> <a href="https://doi.org/10.59543/jidmis.v2i.11647">https://doi.org/10.59543/jidmis.v2i.11647</a></p>','["Youth unemployment, Strategy, IVSF, SWARA, Decision-making analysis, Africa"]',NULL,14,1,'186–196','10.59543/jidmis.v2i.11647','The Authors',2025,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2025-01-24',3),
(165,165,1,'Model Proposal for Ammunition Demand Forecasting and Ammunition Distribution Network Design: Military Unit Application','<p>During the war, the safe provision of the required ammunition at the desired place and time can affect the fate of the war and even the country. Determining the amount of ammunition to be needed in the war is as important as planning its distribution. Two problems are important research topics in the field of military logistics. A two-stage methodology is presented in the study to solve these problems. In the first stage, an analytical approach based on suitability tests is presented to estimate the amount of ammunition that military artillery units will need based on past general data, and ammunition requirements for possible battles are estimated. In the second stage, a distribution network design is carried out for the distribution of ammunition from the ammunition depot to the deployed artillery units. This distribution network also includes reverse logistics activities, as it is necessary to collect the waste left by the ammunition used by the artillery units. In order to achieve this in the study, a Multi-Period Multi Depot Simultaneous Pick Up&amp;Delivery Vehicle Routing Problem Model has been developed. The results obtained as a result of the solution of the model have been examined in detail and analyzed by comparing with the current situation.</p>','["Ammunition Demand Forecast, Ammunition Pick Up&Delivery Network Design, Simultaneously Pick Up&Delivery Vehicle Routing, Reverse Logistics"]',NULL,14,1,'197–217','10.59543/jidmis.v2i.12753','The Authors',2025,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2025-02-02',4),
(166,166,1,'Using Bayesian BWM to Analyze Elevator Performance Requirements for Collaborative Product Innovation Design','<p>This study addresses the critical need to evaluate elevator performance requirements within collaborative product innovation systematically. Leveraging insights from sales, design, and maintenance experts, the research identifies and prioritizes critical performance dimensions, including safety, design, service, and technological innovation. By employing the Bayesian Best-Worst Method (Bayesian BWM), the study overcomes the limitations of traditional decision-making approaches, offering robust and consistent results through probabilistic analysis. The findings highlight "mechanical and structural safety" as the paramount criterion, emphasizing its pivotal role in ensuring elevator reliability. Key factors, such as operational efficiency, economic performance, and maintenance, further inform actionable strategies to optimize elevator design and manufacturing processes. This research contributes a structured framework for performance evaluation and fosters collaboration among industry stakeholders, enhancing innovation and sustainability in the elevator industry.</p>','["Product Innovation, Safety and Reliability Prioritization , Bayesian BWM, Multiple Criteria Decision-Making"]',NULL,14,1,'218–232','10.59543/jidmis.v2i.11503','The Authors',2025,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2025-02-13',5),
(167,167,1,'Sustainable Urban Mobility Plan for Istanbul: A Critical Review','<p>The concept of a sustainable urban mobility plan (SUMP) for cities was first developed to address the shortcomings of conventional transport planning with its focus on accessibility, social equity, environmental quality, and an integrated development of all transport modes as well as an involvement of stakeholders and citizens through a transparent and participatory approach. Since its development in early 2010s, many European cities have adopted this concept for their mobility planning. The sustainable mobility literature already includes many examples and reviews of SUMPs which have been implemented at various cities across Europe. Having implemented the SUMP concept in 2022, the city of Istanbul, having a population of around 16 million people, offers a novel case in terms of the complexity of urban issues addressed. This paper reviews Istanbul’s sustainable urban mobility plan, discussing the benefits as well as shortcomings for achieving a sustainable mobility transition in Istanbul. The review provides many highlights that would be helpful for other cities going through a similar transition.</p>','["sustainable urban mobility plan, transport planning, Istanbul, sustainability, mobility transitions"]',NULL,14,1,'233–249','10.59543/jidmis.v2i.13481','The Authors',2025,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2025-02-25',6),
(168,168,1,'Artificial Intelligence and Medicine 2014-2024: Bibliometric Analysis and Global Impacts','<p>Artificial intelligence (AI) has radically transformed the field of medicine in the last decade, with a significant increase in academic publications. Based on 1783 English-language articles analyzed with Biblioshiny and VOSviewer tools, the findings highlight an annual growth rate of 30.38% and a significant increase from 2018 onwards. Each article received an average of 17.54 citations. The studies had contributions from 11678 authors and an international collaboration rate of 29%. There were single-author (118) and single-country (129) articles. Prominent contributing authors include Forestiero A, Mazzuca D and Zinno F. Harvard Medical School (104 papers) and the University of Toronto (83 papers) have played important roles in the advancement of AI applications in medicine. The USA stands out with both publication volume (3241 articles) and number of citations (9176). Journals such as Journal of Medical Internet Research (26 articles) and Frontiers in Medicine (25 articles) stand out as the leading publication venues in the field. The most cited articles were published in journals such as Jama, Radiology and Nature. This study highlights the wide-ranging applications of AI in areas such as machine learning, deep learning, natural language processing and computer vision, demonstrating its potential in medical imaging, genetic analysis and clinical decision support systems. Future research needs to focus on maintaining collaboration, increasing methodological rigor and finding solutions to emerging challenges.</p>
<p><strong> DOI:</strong> <a href="https://doi.org/10.59543/jidmis.v2i.13525">https://doi.org/10.59543/jidmis.v2i.13525</a></p>','["Medicine, Artificial Intelligence, Bibliometric Analysis, Biblioshiny, VOSviewer"]',NULL,14,1,'250–271','10.59543/jidmis.v2i.13525','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-05-18',7),
(169,169,1,'Designing and Analyzing Fermatean Fuzzy Decision Models: A Comprehensive Decision Making Approach','<p>In the framework of Multi-Criteria Decision Making (MCDM), this study explores the incorporation of fuzzy set theory with decision-making techniques. It starts by giving a summary of fuzzy set theory and the basic ideas that are necessary to comprehend how decisions are made. The paper presents the Technique for Order of Preference by Similarity to Ideal Solution (TOPSIS) and Logarithmic Percentage Change Driven Objective Weighting (LOPCOW), which are implemented using R programming. The paper illustrates the use of these approaches for alternative ranking and objective weight evaluation with a thorough numerical example. The outcomes demonstrate how well these strategies work together to handle challenging decision-making situations. The paper offers a precise and effective approach for resolving MCDM issues by utilising the computational power of R programming.</p>
<p><strong> DOI:</strong> <a href="https://doi.org/10.59543/jidmis.v2i.12601">https://doi.org/10.59543/jidmis.v2i.12601</a></p>','["Decision making, Fermatean fuzzy set, R-programming , LOPCOW, TOP- SIS"]',NULL,14,1,'272–286','10.59543/jidmis.v2i.12601','The Authors',2025,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2025-03-05',8),
(170,170,1,'Enhancing Cybersecurity Risk Assessment Techniques Based on Bipolar Complex Intuitionistic Fuzzy Soft Robust Aggregation Operators: A Comprehensive Approaches','<p>This article describes the cybersecurity risk assessment model’s accuracy, timely response, and actions against potential threats by providing the key areas in which the cybersecurity risk assessment model can be improved and enhanced. For this, we propose the model of robust aggregation operators based on bipolar complex intuitionistic fuzzy soft information, called the bipolar complex intuitionistic fuzzy soft weighted averaging operator, bipolar complex intuitionistic fuzzy soft order weighted averaging operator, bipolar complex intuitionistic fuzzy soft weighted geometric operators, and bipolar complex intuitionistic fuzzy soft order weighted geometric operators, with some practical properties, called idempotency, monotonicity, and boundedness. Further, we discuss the multi-attribute decision-making model based on proposed operators for evaluating the enhancement of the cybersecurity risk assessment techniques with a comprehensive approach. Finally, we illustrate numerical examples based on decision-making techniques for assessing the comparative analysis among the proposed ranking values with existing ranking values to show the worth and verity of the designed models.</p>','["Bipolar complex intuitionistic fuzzy sets, Cybersecurity risk assessments, Decision-making techniques,", "Robust aggregation operators"]',NULL,14,1,'287–317','10.59543/jidmis.v2i.13475','The Authors',2025,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2025-03-10',9),
(171,171,1,'Experience of Artificial Intelligence in Architectural Space Production within the Scope of Creating "Brand Identity" for a Local Brand','<p>Branding and brand identity emerged from the desire to stand out in line with consumption needs. Branding and corporate identity design are processes that must be carried out simultaneously. In this context, the design of the brand''s structure, interior spaces, and stores intersects with the discipline of architecture in the creation of a "brand identity." Spatial design, through the<br/>use of architectural elements and spatial configurations, differentiates the brand and aims to capture customer attention. Today, the role of artificial intelligence (AI) in the design process is being questioned. Text-to-image AI algorithms redefine architectural formation by generating unique and diverse design outputs. In this context, the question, "Can artificial intelligence produce architectural spaces for brand identity?" forms the basis of this research. The study aims to use text-to-image technology to rapidly generate diverse design alternatives for a local brand. The relationship between text to-image technology and decision-making is shaped by its ability to guide and optimize users'' creative processes. This technology allows users to provide text-based inputs to generate visual outputs, playing a critical role in visualizing both design options and possibilities in the decision-making<br/>process. By examining the contributions of the generated visuals to brand identity and architectural design, this method is proposed as a new approach to architectural design. While designers assume the role of decision-makers in this process, AI emerges as a significant design parameter.</p>
<p><strong> DOI:</strong> <a href="https://doi.org/10.59543/jidmis.v2i.13491">https://doi.org/10.59543/jidmis.v2i.13491</a></p>','["Brand Identity, Store Design, Artificial Intelligence, Text-to-Image Generation, Architectural Design"]',NULL,14,1,'318–331','10.59543/jidmis.v2i.13491','The Authors',2025,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2025-03-30',10),
(172,172,1,'Novel Hamy Mean Aggregation Operators Based on Advanced Operations for T-Spherical Fuzzy Group Decision-Making','<p>Some advanced operation laws (AdOLs) are put forward with the T-spherical fuzzy (TSF) information. A series of novel Hamy mean aggregation operators are developed, specifically TSF advanced Hamy mean (TSFAdHM), TSF advanced dual Hamy mean (TSFAdDHM), TSF advanced weighted Hamy mean (TSFAdWHM) and TSF advanced weighted dual Hamy mean (TSFAdWDHM) operators. These aggregation operators integrate the strengths of Hamy mean and AdOLs in that they not only reveal correlations among multi-input variables but also eliminate counter-intuitive problems, as well as reflect decision-maker risk preferences. Several reasonable natures and peculiar types of these aggregation operators are investigated. Moreover, a novel group decision-making framework for the type of TSF aggregation operator is constructed. The tractability and usefulness of the decision-making model are examined by some numerical examples. The sensitivity and method comparison analyses are used to demonstrate that the proposed model can remedy the drawbacks of existing methods, and this article offers a very useful method for the complicated group decision-making issues.</p>','["Advanced operational laws, Hamy mean, Aggregation operator, multi-attribute group decision-making, T-spherical fuzzy sets"]',NULL,14,1,'332–365','10.59543/jidmis.v2i.12603','The Authors',2025,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2025-04-16',11),
(173,173,1,'Evaluation of Banking Performance of the Balkan Countries in Type-2 Neutrosophic Fuzzy Environment','<p>This paper proposes the PSI &amp; CODAS integrated model based on the Type-2 Neutrosophic Fuzzy Numbers (T2fNNs) to solve the decision-making problems encountered in banking and make performance analyses of banking institutions. The proposed model is also an integrated MCDM approach that can overcome many complex situations and many misevaluations by decision-makers. Besides, it provides an applicable and stable assessment, as it is not sensitive to the number of criteria and decision alternatives. The T2NN MCDM approach was applied to evaluate the banking performances of banks in Balkan countries after the COVID-19 global pandemic and demonstrate the implementation of the suggested model. Next, a comprehensive robustness check consisting of two stages was performed to test the validation of the suggested MCDM model and its results. According to the study results, A13 Türkiye İş Bankası was the best option for applying the proposed model and has remained the best option for all scenarios. Besides, minor alterations have been surveyed that did not alter the overall conclusions in the ranking performance of a few options. The sensitivity analysis results prove that the proposed PSI &amp; CODAS approach is a powerful and applicable computational tool, and its results are reasonable, realistic, and substantially accurate.</p>
<p><strong> DOI:</strong> <a href="https://doi.org/10.59543/jidmis.v2i.13389">https://doi.org/10.59543/jidmis.v2i.13389</a></p>','["Bank performance analysis, Balkan banks, Preference Selection Index, Power-Heronian Function, CODAS"]',NULL,14,1,'366–385','10.59543/jidmis.v2i.13389','The Authors',2025,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2025-04-17',12),
(174,174,1,'Analysis of Factors Affecting the Vulnerability of the Hazardous Materials Transportation System','<p>A method for identifying key vulnerability factors in hazardous materials transportation systems is proposed, combining qualitative and quantitative analytical techniques. First, we establish a conceptual framework that Integrates definitions of hazardous materials, their transportation systems, and vulnerability (including supply chain vulnerability). Through qualitative analysis, we identify critical factors that contribute to systemic vulnerability. Subsequently, the interrelationships among these factors are quantified using the Decision-Making Trial and Evaluation Laboratory (DEMATEL) method. By applying DEMATEL, we model causal relationships and prioritize key factors based on centrality and causality indices. Finally, we proposed targeted mitigation measures aligned with these prioritized factors to enhance the resilience of hazardous materials transportation systems.</p>','["hazardous materials, transportation system, vulnerability, supply chain, DEMATEL"]',NULL,14,1,'386–399','10.59543/jidmis.v2i.14015','The Authors',2025,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2025-04-21',13),
(175,175,1,'Examing the impact of digital transformation on the resilience of food supply chains: a comprehensive analysis','<p>This study analyzes the relationship between digital transformation and food supply chain resilience(FSCR). First, it identifies 10 factors that affect structural changes in FSCR. These factors are selected from organizational, social, and technological aspects. The Analytic Hierarchy Process (AHP) is used to hierarchically classify and analyze the factors influencing FSCR and their mechanisms of action. Then, Interpretive Structural Modeling (ISM) is established to study the interactions among the factors of FSCR, with digital technology as an external manifestation of digital transformation. Through those methods, the study gets the identification of the interrelationship and intrinsic connections between digital transformation and FSCR, and helps us to have a better understanding of how digital transformation affects FSCR.</p>
<p>The study provides valuable insights for decision-makers in improving FSCR, emphasizing the importance of digital transformation. Digital technology plays an intermediate role in the factor system of FSCR. It acts like a bridge linking the causal factors, such as government macro-control, and result factors, such as environmental protection. While the study offers some guidance for the improvement of FSCR, the study has some limitations in methods and regions. Future research should focus on integrating advanced techniques and looking for more specific solutions.</p>
<p><strong> DOI:</strong> <a href="https://doi.org/10.59543/jidmis.v2i.13731">https://doi.org/10.59543/jidmis.v2i.13731</a></p>','["Food supply chain resilience, Digital transformation, Interaction relationship, AHP, ISM"]',NULL,14,1,'400–417','10.59543/jidmis.v2i.13731','The Authors',2025,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2025-04-27',14),
(176,176,1,'Investigation of Factors Driving Manufacturing Companies to Green Manufacturing Using Direct Rating Technique','<p>Environmental practices have been incorporated into business models and procedures in today''s competitive climate, and they are now significant challenges that require attention. Businesses have undergone several changes as a result of these practices, including concerns about preserving a habitable environment for future generations and guaranteeing energy efficiency with sustainable production. To the extent that green manufacturing practices have been included into business strategies and used to inform future planning. In this context, green production practices have emerged as a crucial factor that requires careful attention from businesses. Nowadays, green production is a crucial tool for manufacturing businesses. The study investigated what motivates manufacturing companies to engage in green production practices. The goal was to weight the characteristics that were identified as driving businesses to engage in green manufacturing operations. To do this, the identified criteria were weighted using one of the multi-criteria decision analysis techniques, direct rating. The results of the application showed that "support from top management," "increasing demand for green products," and "increasing public pressure" were the main drivers of manufacturing companies'' transition to green production practices.</p>
<p><strong> DOI:</strong> <a href="https://doi.org/10.59543/jidmis.v2i.14093">https://doi.org/10.59543/jidmis.v2i.14093</a></p>','["Green manufacturing, green manufacturing activities, multi-criteria decision analysis, direct rating technique"]',NULL,14,1,'418–428','10.59543/jidmis.v2i.14093','The Authors',2025,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2025-04-27',15),
(177,177,1,'The Synergy of Fuzzy Logic and Multi-Criteria Decision-Making: Application Areas and Global Trends','<p>This study conducts a bibliometric analysis to understand the evolution and application of Multi-Criteria Decision Making (MCDM)/Fuzzy MCDM methods in scientific literature. The literature review process, utilizing the Scopus database, analyzed 13278 articles out of 30144 published between 2014-2024. The analysis reveals substantial academic output in MCDM/Fuzzy MCDM fields with an annual growth rate of 13.38%. However, the declining citation-per-paper ratio suggests that increasing publication volume may negatively impact individual studies'' visibility. Journals like Sustainability, PLOS One, and IEEE Access emerge as top publishers, highlighting the rise of open-access platforms while cautioning that rapid acceptance processes may introduce methodological weaknesses. Leading authors such as Zeshui Xu and Dragan Pamučar have made significant theoretical and practical contributions. Xu''s operators for intuitionistic fuzzy sets and Pamučar''s novel MCDM methods for healthcare supplier selection represent key milestones. The University of California System demonstrates strong research infrastructure as the most prolific institution. China dominates as the most productive country (14545 articles, 122430 citations), followed by India, Pakistan, and Turkey, whose collaborations enhance scientific interaction. Yager''s (2013) IEEE Transactions on Fuzzy Systems paper, "Pythagorean Membership Grades in Multicriteria Decision Making," remains the most cited (2433 citations). Science mapping identifies key trends: fuzzy logic''s role in decision model evolution and integration with machine learning. Applications in energy efficiency, maritime safety, and sustainability are growing. Future integration with AI/ML is projected to enhance decision-making efficiency.</p>
<p><strong> DOI:</strong> <a href="https://doi.org/10.59543/jidmis.v2i.14157">https://doi.org/10.59543/jidmis.v2i.14157 </a></p>','["multi-criteria decision making, fuzzy logic, bibliometric analysis, VOSviewer, Biblioshiny, Python"]',NULL,14,1,'429–455','10.59543/jidmis.v2i.14157','The Authors',2025,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2025-05-10',16),
(178,178,1,'Comparative Analysis of Neural Network Architectures for Image Classification','<p>An important challenge in the growing domains of computer vision and machine learning is the precise categorisation of images, which drives the growth of innovative methodologies and methods. The proposed study will examine three distinct picture classification frameworks. The structures include a Single Layer Network (SLN), a Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP), and a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) based on LeCun''s technique. The study will employ the MNIST digit identification dataset, the Fashion-MNIST dataset, and the CIFAR-10 dataset. Precision, recall, and F1-score are the measures used for model evaluation. Compared to the SLN and CNN models, the MLP model exhibits superior precision and recall.</p>
<p><strong> DOI:</strong> <a href="https://doi.org/10.59543/jidmis.v2i.11481">https://doi.org/10.59543/jidmis.v2i.11481</a></p>','["Neural Networks, MLP , CNN, Image Classification"]',NULL,14,1,'456–471','10.59543/jidmis.v2i.11481','The Authors',2025,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2025-05-14',17),
(179,179,1,'Evaluation of Strategic Management and Growth Alternatives in Independent Auditing Firms Using Interval Type-2 Fuzzy ARAS Method','<p>Independent audit firms (IAFs) need to base their strategic management and growth decisions on more logical and rational foundations due to the technological and digital transformation, which has been increasing pressure in the recent period, the intensification of competition in the industry, and the rapid and frequently changing structure of legal regulations. In this context, this study aims to evaluate and prioritize strategic management and growth decisions systematically and analytically by identifying the strategic decision problems faced by IAFs. In this direction, six strategic alternatives determined by a comprehensive field study were analyzed with the help of the Interval Type-2 Fuzzy ARAS (IT2F–ARAS) method, considering thirteen criteria determined based on expert opinions. IT2F–ARAS technique has been preferred as it can effectively include differences in interpretation in specialists'' judgments and ambiguities in the model. This procedure enables the integrated and flexible evaluation of quantitative and qualitative data in decision-making processes. When the results obtained by applying the proposed model are examined, it is seen that benefit-oriented criteria such as expanding the customer base, providing a competitive advantage, and increasing brand value are more decisive. It has been observed that cost-side criteria, such as initial investment cost and technological incompatibility, limit the applicability of some strategies. In addition, "digital transformation," "expanding the range of services," and "market diversification" have emerged as priority strategies for IAFs in the context of strategic management and growth decisions. The study aims to contribute to developing more consistent and sustainable growth policies by providing independent audit firms with a multi-criteria and fuzz-based decision support perspective in their strategic orientations.</p>
<p><strong> DOI:</strong> <a href="https://doi.org/10.59543/jidmis.v2i.14421">https://doi.org/10.59543/jidmis.v2i.14421</a></p>','["Independent auditing, strategic management, growth strategies, fuzzy MCDM, IT2F-ARAS"]',NULL,14,1,'472–493','10.59543/jidmis.v2i.14421','The Authors',2025,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2025-05-23',18),
(180,180,1,'The Protective Effects of Ramadan Fasting against Cancer: Exploring Metabolic, Cellular, and Epigenetic Mechanisms','<p>Fasting, particularly the fasting during Ramadan, is becoming very common due to the potential health benefits of which it is inclusive of its anti-cancer properties as well as therapy. It is mentioned in a recent study that intermittent fasting, as during Ramadan, induces some alterations in the metabolism, physiology, and epigenetics, which could suppress cancer growth. The problem that this study seeks to address is the deficiency of scientific honesty and transparency in explaining the mechanisms by which Ramadan fasting affects cancer development and treatment response. The research seeks to systematically update the literature to evaluate the protective mechanisms of Ramadan fasting against cancer progression based on scientific transparency. The review was conducted according to the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analysis (PRISMA) guidelines for executing sound and transparent reporting. The key findings are that fasting controls metabolism towards increased production of ketone bodies, reduced availability of glucose, and reduced levels of insulin and insulin-like growth factor-1 (IGF-1)—vital modulators of cancer cell growth. Also, fasting increases the DNA damage response (DDR), stimulates cellular homeostasis through autophagy, and decreases oxidative stress, thereby inhibiting malignant transformation. Epigenetically, fasting regulates non-coding RNAs, DNA methylation, and histone modification, which act to activate tumor suppressor genes and silence oncogenes. The most significant finding is that Ramadan fasting is of immense potential as an adjuvant intervention in cancer therapy. These findings have crucial implications for integrative oncology practice, dietary intervention, and public health policy in the integrative oncology setting.</p>
<p><strong> DOI:</strong> <a href="https://doi.org/10.59543/jidmis.v2i.14363">https://doi.org/10.59543/jidmis.v2i.14363</a></p>','["Ramadan Fasting , Cancer Prevention , Metabolic Epigenetic Modification , Autophagy, Oxidative"]',NULL,14,1,'494–509','10.59543/jidmis.v2i.14363','The Authors',2025,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2025-05-26',19),
(181,181,1,'Transformer-based Mobile Traffic Prediction in Internet of Vehicular Networks','<p>This paper presents a Transformer-based mobile traffic prediction model for the Internet of Vehicular Things (IoVT), addressing the challenges of handling long-term dependencies and improving computational efficiency in mobile traffic forecasting. The proposed model, which integrates a gated residual attention unit (GRAU) and channel embedding (CE) technique, leverages the strengths of the Transformer architecture to enhance predictive accuracy and efficiency while maintaining recurrent dynamics. Through experiments on a real-world dataset, the model demonstrated superior performance over existing baselines in terms of root mean square error (RMSE) and mean absolute error (MAE), showcasing its effectiveness in capturing complex temporal patterns in mobile traffic data. The study contributes to the IoVT by providing a robust prediction tool that can optimize network resource allocation and support the development of intelligent transport systems within smart city frameworks.</p>
<p><strong> DOI:</strong> <a href="https://doi.org/10.59543/jidmis.v2i.14303">https://doi.org/10.59543/jidmis.v2i.14303</a></p>','["Internet of Vehicular Things, Mobile Traffic Prediction, Deep Learning, Transformer"]',NULL,14,1,'510–527','10.59543/jidmis.v2i.14303','The Authors',2025,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2025-06-04',20);
